(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","id":"c-m-01","n":1101,"icon":"👪","title":"Telling a mother her son has died by suspected suicide","moment":{"when":"Thursday 13:05","where":"Relatives' room, Emergency Department, acute hospital","text":"You are the CT2 in the community mental health team. Jamie Walsh, a 24-year-old man you have seen three times in clinic for depression and heavy drinking, was found unresponsive in his flat at 11:00 by the home treatment team on a planned visit, with empty medicine packets and a note. Paramedics brought him to A&E and he was pronounced dead at 12:10. His mother, Mrs Lorraine Walsh, 52, has been told only that he is 'very unwell'. Your consultant is 30 minutes away. The ED consultant asks you to tell her now, with Grace, the ED nurse who cared for Jamie."},"task":"Tell her in person, clearly and kindly, that Jamie has died; answer her questions honestly; explain the coroner, family liaison and what happens next; give support; then arrange the team's own debrief.","first60":["Check the facts: Jamie's full name and date of birth, time of death, who confirmed it, and that she is his mother.","Ask the ED team: have the police and coroner been informed, and can she see him now?","Hand your bleep to a colleague; take Grace with you.","Check the room: private, chairs at the same level, tissues, water, no interruptions.","Knock, sit down, introduce yourself and Grace, and ask who is with her."],"ask":["'What have you been told so far?'","'Is anyone with you, or on their way? Shall we wait for them?'","'Would you like to see him? Grace will come with you.'","'What would you like to know about what happened?'","'Who else in the family needs to be told, and would you like help to plan that?'","'Who will be with you tonight?'","'Sometimes after a death like this, people have thoughts of not wanting to go on. Have you had any thoughts like that?'"],"recognise":["Shock can look like silence, screaming, practical questions or calm; none of them is wrong.","Say 'died' or 'dead' early. 'Passed away', 'we lost him' or 'we did everything we could' leave room for false hope.","'How did nobody see this coming?' comes early. It is grief, not an invitation to defend the team's care.","This is a sudden, unexplained death: it must be reported to the coroner, and the police attend on the coroner's behalf.","People bereaved by suicide are themselves at raised risk of suicide; ask before she leaves.","Staff who knew him, including you, may be shaken; that needs planning, not ignoring."],"decide":[{"c":"Give a short warning, then say plainly that Jamie has died; pause, let her react, and answer her questions before explaining the next steps.","r":"Clear words prevent false hope, and following her lead lets her take in what she can.","best":true},{"c":"Wait for your consultant to arrive before telling her.","r":"She is frightened and asking now; 30 minutes of evasion destroys trust. Tell her, and let the consultant meet her afterwards.","best":false},{"c":"Explain the coroner, the inquest and the trust review first, so she understands the process before the news.","r":"She will take in nothing before the news, and nothing about process for some time after it.","best":false}],"law":["Notification of Deaths Regulations 2019: doctors must report to the coroner a death that is unnatural, violent, of unknown cause, or possibly due to self-harm. No medical certificate of cause of death is issued while the coroner investigates.","Coroners and Justice Act 2009: the coroner decides on a post-mortem and an inquest; an inquest establishes who died, and when, where and how.","A conclusion of suicide at inquest is reached on the balance of probabilities (R (Maughan) v HM Senior Coroner for Oxfordshire, Supreme Court, 2020).","GMC Confidentiality (2017): the duty continues after death, but you can usually share information with those close to him to help them understand, unless he asked you not to.","Statutory duty of candour (CQC Regulation 20) applies if the trust's review finds his death followed a patient safety incident; the family must then be told and written to.","PSIRF (NHS England, 2022) and the national Learning from Deaths guidance: the trust reviews his care and must offer the family a part in that review."],"say":[{"to":"Mrs Walsh","s":"I'm afraid I have some very bad news. When the team found Jamie this morning, he wasn't breathing. The doctors here tried for a long time to restart his heart. I'm so sorry. Jamie died at ten past twelve."},{"to":"Mrs Walsh, on how he died","s":"He was found with empty medicine packets and a note addressed to you. It looks as though he may have taken his own life. The coroner will confirm the cause."},{"to":"Mrs Walsh, on 'how did nobody see it?'","s":"You have every right to ask that, and I don't have the answer today. The trust will look carefully at his care, you will have a named contact, and you will be told what we find."},{"to":"Grace and the team","s":"Thank you. I've booked half an hour at 16:30 for everyone who looked after Jamie, here and in the community team. It's to look after each other, not to investigate."}],"write":"Notes entry (dated, timed):\nPresent: Dr [name] (CT2 psychiatry), Grace [surname] (ED staff nurse), Mrs Lorraine Walsh (mother).\nTold of Jamie's death at 12:10 (confirmed by Dr [ED consultant]); her questions and the answers given, in brief.\nShe saw Jamie at 13:40 with Grace.\nGiven: Help is at Hand booklet; Support After Suicide Partnership; Cruse helpline 0808 808 1677; Samaritans 116 123.\nHer own risk: fleeting wish 'not to wake up', no plan; staying with her sister; GP Dr Ahmed to be told today.\nCoroner informed by ED; police attended. Trust incident report made 13:55. CMHT consultant and GP informed. All appointments cancelled. Team debrief 16:30.","make":["Prepare: facts, room, people, bleep handed over.","Find out what she knows, then ask if she wants you to go on now or wait for her sister.","Warning shot, then 'Jamie died' within the first minute. Then stop talking.","Answer her questions as they come, one fact at a time; no speculation about why.","Offer to take her to see him; warn her that tubes stay in place for the coroner.","Next steps only when she is ready: coroner's officer, post-mortem, inquest, trust family liaison contact.","Give support in writing, ask about her own safety, cancel his appointments, report the death, book the debrief."],"next":"Mrs Walsh sees Jamie with Grace and goes home with her sister. The coroner's officer phones her the next morning. The trust's family liaison lead calls within two working days and invites her questions for the review. At 16:30 the ED and community teams meet for a short debrief, and your consultant meets you afterwards.","marks":["Prepared the facts and a private room before going in.","Found out what she knew before giving the news.","Said 'died' clearly and early, then allowed silence.","Answered honestly about how he died without speculating or defending care.","Explained the coroner, post-mortem, inquest and family liaison in plain words.","Gave written support details and asked about her own safety.","Arranged a team debrief and cancelled his appointments."],"pitfalls":["Euphemisms ('we lost him', 'he's gone') that leave her unsure.","Filling her silence with process and leaflets.","Defending the team's care, or promising that nobody could have prevented it.","Forgetting to cancel his appointments, so a clinic letter arrives next week.","Leaving without asking who will be with her tonight."],"refs":["1. National Suicide Prevention Alliance and NHS England. Help is at Hand: support after someone may have died by suicide (current edition; checked Nov 2026)","2. Ministry of Justice. Guide to coroner services for bereaved people (2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. The Notification of Deaths Regulations 2019 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England. Patient Safety Incident Response Framework, and Engaging and involving patients, families and staff following a patient safety incident (2022; checked Nov 2026)","5. National Quality Board. National guidance on learning from deaths (2017; checked Nov 2026)","6. GMC. Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK families expect plain words. Phrases that are kind elsewhere ('he has gone', 'God has taken him', 'we tried our best') can be heard here as hope. After you say 'died', silence is normal and kind; you do not need to fill it.","obj":["Told her within the first minute, in plain words, that Jamie has died","Answered what is known about how he died honestly, with no speculation or defence of the team","Explained the coroner's officer, post-mortem and inquest, and named a trust family liaison contact","Given Help is at Hand, Support After Suicide Partnership and Cruse details in writing, and asked about her own safety","Cancelled his appointments, reported the death and booked a same-day team debrief"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"No. He texted me last night. He can't be dead.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. I know this is impossible to take in. He did die, at ten past twelve. Take all the time you need. We're staying right here with you."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He saw you lot nine days ago. How did nobody see this coming?\"","r":"You have every right to ask that. I don't have the answer today. The trust will review his care, you'll have a named contact, and we'll tell you what we find."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Did he suffer?\"","r":"I don't know for certain, and I won't guess. The coroner's post-mortem will tell us more. I'll make sure the coroner's officer knows you want to ask about that."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have gone round. I knew he was low.\"","r":"It's natural to go over and over what you could have done. You were a loving mum who was in touch with him. His illness hid a lot, even from us."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I have the note? It's mine.\"","r":"Of course you want it. The police are holding it for the coroner because his death was sudden. It will come back to you, and the coroner's officer can tell you when."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I don't want to be here without him.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. That's a really important thing to say. Can I ask a bit more about those thoughts, so we can make sure you're safe tonight?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Ask her GP (with her consent) to see her within a week; on the day, avoid starting sleeping tablets in A&E","Tell Jamie's GP and community pharmacy today so repeat prescriptions and reminders stop"],"psych":["Help is at Hand booklet, printed, before she leaves","Support After Suicide Partnership (local groups bereaved by suicide) and Cruse Bereavement Support helpline 0808 808 1677","Referral to the local suicide bereavement support service, with her consent, by the care coordinator"],"social":["Coroner's officer phones her, usually within 1 to 2 working days, about the post-mortem and release of Jamie's body","Trust family liaison lead (patient safety team) is her single named contact for the review","Care coordinator cancels all appointments and letters today and phones her within 2 working days","Her sister takes her home; she should not drive today"],"safety":["Ask directly about her own suicidal thoughts; give Samaritans 116 123 and NHS 111 option 2","Plan with her how Jamie's brother will be told so he is not alone when he hears","Incident report on the trust system today; CMHT consultant and GP informed before you leave A&E","Team debrief at 16:30, facilitated by the CMHT psychologist; staff wellbeing service offered"]},"facts":[{"h":"Say 'died' in the first minute","x":"Warning shot, then the word 'died' or 'dead'. Euphemisms prolong hope and confusion. Then stop and wait; silence is part of the conversation."},{"h":"Sudden deaths go to the coroner","x":"Unnatural or unexplained deaths are reported to the coroner; police attend on the coroner's behalf. No death certificate until the coroner decides; a post-mortem is likely."},{"h":"Tubes stay in for the coroner","x":"She can usually see and touch him in A&E with a nurse present, but lines and tubes stay in place until the coroner allows removal. Tell her before she walks in."},{"h":"Ask about her own safety","x":"Close family bereaved by suicide have a raised risk of suicide. Ask kindly before she leaves, and give Samaritans 116 123 in writing."},{"h":"Stop the letters today","x":"Cancel appointments, texts and repeat prescriptions straight away. A clinic letter arriving for a dead son is a common, avoidable harm."},{"h":"Debrief is support, not investigation","x":"Offer a short team debrief the same day for everyone involved, including you, then signpost staff wellbeing support. The formal review is separate."}],"type":"Breaking bad news","do":"Tell his mother clearly that Jamie has died, answer her questions, and give coroner and support details.","sim":{"setup":"Thursday 13:05, A&E relatives' room. Jamie Walsh, 24, your community patient, died at 12:10 after a suspected overdose. His mother has been told only that he is 'very unwell'. With the ED nurse, tell her, answer her questions and plan next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Lorraine Walsh, 52, Jamie's mother","open":"Yes. Sit. Where is he? Nobody's telling me anything. Can I see him?","manner":"Frightened and pressured; numb, then sobbing after the news; angry at the team for a moment; quieter and practical once she has had time and a plan.","free":["Jamie texted her at 23:40 last night: 'Night Mum, love you'","Her sister is parking the car","Jamie saw the community team nine days ago","She thought he was doing better; he had cut down his drinking"],"ifasked":["Jamie's brother Connor, 20, is at university in Leeds and does not know yet","She will stay at her sister's tonight","She has fleeting thoughts of not wanting to wake up, with no plan","Her GP is Dr Ahmed","Jamie's father died when Jamie was 12","She wants to see Jamie today"],"worries":["How did the team not see this coming?","Can she have the note?","Will there be a post-mortem, and when can she plan the funeral?","Who will tell Connor?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses euphemisms or delays, she says, 'Is he dead? Just say it.'","Becomes angrier if the doctor defends the team's care; settles if her question is acknowledged and a named contact promised","Takes in practical information only after she has had time to cry","Mentions her own wish not to wake up only if asked directly and kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Walsh? I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors from Jamie's community mental health team. This is Grace, the nurse who's been looking after him. May we sit down with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. Sit. Where is he? Nobody's telling me anything. Can I see him?"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'm going to tell you everything I know. Is anyone with you, or on their way?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"My sister's parking the car. I couldn't wait."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"Would you like us to wait for her, or shall I go on now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"No. Just tell me. Please."},{"t":"0:38","band":"What she knows","w":"D","s":"What have you been told so far?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"That the crisis team found him at his flat and brought him here. That he's very unwell. Is he in intensive care?"},{"t":"0:50","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid I have some very bad news.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Oh God."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"When the team found Jamie this morning, he wasn't breathing. The paramedics and the doctors here tried for a long time to restart his heart. I'm so sorry, Mrs Walsh. Jamie died at ten past twelve.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No. No. He texted me last night. He can't be dead."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"He said, 'Night Mum, love you.' That's all. I thought he was getting better."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"That message must feel so precious right now. Take all the time you need. We're not going anywhere.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"What happened? Did he do something to himself?"},{"t":"1:35","band":"How he died","w":"D","s":"I'll tell you what we know. He was found in bed. There were empty medicine packets beside him, and a note addressed to you. It looks as though he may have taken his own life. The coroner will confirm the cause.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"A note. Where is it? I want it."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. The police are keeping it for the coroner for now, because his death was sudden. It will come back to you, and the coroner's officer can tell you when.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"He saw you lot nine days ago. How did nobody see this coming?"},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"You have every right to ask that. I saw him nine days ago, and I don't have the answer for you today. The trust will look carefully at his care, and you'll be involved and told what we find.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Don't fob me off."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I won't. You'll have a named person from the trust, and you can put every question to them, including that one. I'll make sure it happens.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Can I see him?"},{"t":"2:43","band":"Seeing him","w":"D","s":"Yes. Grace will take you whenever you're ready. Because the coroner is involved, the tubes the doctors used are still in place. He's covered up to his shoulders. There may be a tube in his mouth. You can hold his hand, talk to him, step out and go back in as often as you want.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"I want to see him. In a minute."},{"t":"3:07","band":"What happens next","w":"D","s":"When you feel able, can I explain what happens over the next few days? Or is that too much right now?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Tell me. I need to know what to do."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Because his death was sudden, it's been reported to the coroner. That's the normal process for any sudden death. A coroner's officer will phone you, usually in the next day or two.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Will they cut him open?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"The coroner will probably ask for a post-mortem to confirm the cause. The coroner's officer will explain it, and when he can go to a funeral director. You can choose a funeral director now, but not fix a date yet.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"And then a court case?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"There'll probably be an inquest. It isn't a trial. It's a hearing to record who he was, and when, where and how he died. You can attend and ask questions. It's usually some months away.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"I can't take all this in."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable. You don't need to remember any of it today. I'll write the key points down, and the coroner's officer will go through it again.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"Grace has a booklet called Help is at Hand. It's written for families after a suicide, and it covers all of this.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"I don't want a leaflet. I want my son."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. Nothing makes this better. Keep it for later, for when questions come at three in the morning.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"I've also written down two organisations. Support After Suicide Partnership can link you to people bereaved in the same way. Cruse Bereavement Support has a free helpline.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:53","band":"Her safety","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about you? Who'll be with you tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"My sister. I'll stay at hers. Oh God, Connor. His brother's at university in Leeds. Who tells Connor?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"We can help you plan it, so he isn't alone when he hears. Would you and your sister like to ring him together, with a friend of his close by?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. He shouldn't be on his own."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Mrs Walsh, sometimes after a loss like this, people have thoughts of not wanting to go on themselves. Have you had any thoughts like that?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Not like that. I just don't want to wake up tomorrow."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If those thoughts get stronger, ring Samaritans on 116 123, any time, or NHS 111 and choose option 2. Can I ask your GP to see you this week?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Yes. Dr Ahmed."},{"t":"5:51","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. What will you tell your sister happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"The coroner's person rings tomorrow. Probably a post-mortem. Someone from the trust contacts me. And the note comes back later."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Our family liaison lead will call you within two working days. I'll cancel all Jamie's appointments today, so no letters come to you.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I hadn't even thought of that. Thank you."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"My consultant, Dr Ross, will be here soon and would like to meet you, today or whenever you choose. Grace will take you to Jamie now, and I'll wait with your sister.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Later. I want to see my boy."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Grace, thank you. I've booked half past four for everyone who looked after Jamie, to talk about how we're all doing. Will you come?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll tell the resus team."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and nurse, sits at her level, checks who is with her and whether to wait","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she has been told before giving any news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then says 'died' clearly within the first minute","d":"Content"},{"c":"Pauses after the news and responds to grief and anger with silence and validation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains what is known about how he died honestly, without speculating or defending care","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers to see him, warning that tubes stay in place for the coroner","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains coroner's officer, post-mortem and inquest in plain words, when she is ready","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Promises a named trust family liaison contact and her involvement in the review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives Help is at Hand, Support After Suicide Partnership and Cruse details in writing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks who will be with her, helps plan telling her son, and asks about her own suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say back the next steps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with next contacts, cancels his appointments and books a team debrief","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Says 'died' early and plainly, stays with her grief, answers honestly, explains the coroner and support, checks her safety and arranges the debrief.","borderline":"Delivers the news clearly but rushes into process, or forgets her own risk, the appointments or the team debrief.","fail":"Uses euphemisms or delays the news, defends the team's care, speculates about cause, or leaves her without support or a safety check."}}},
{"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","id":"c-m-02","n":1102,"icon":"⚖","title":"Duty of candour: telling a patient he was given a double dose of clozapine","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30","where":"Male acute ward, adult inpatient unit","text":"You are the CT1 on the ward. Mr Derek Hollis, a 58-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia, takes clozapine 450 mg a day (150 mg in the morning, 300 mg at night). Last night his 300 mg night dose was given at 20:00 and not signed for; a second nurse gave it again at 21:30. By 23:00 he was very drowsy with BP 86/50 and was sent to the acute hospital for monitoring. His ECG and bloods there were normal, and he came back at 08:30. On advice from the medical team and pharmacist, this morning's dose was held. He is informal and has capacity. Your consultant asks you to tell him what happened, with Jo, the ward manager."},"task":"Tell him openly about the error: a clear apology, the facts known so far, what was done and what happens next; agree written follow-up and who else he wants told.","first60":["Get the facts straight: doses, times, obs, what the acute hospital found, what was held this morning and the plan for tonight.","Check the incident has been reported on the trust system (Datix) and graded; moderate harm makes this a notifiable safety incident.","Agree with Jo who says what, and that neither of you will name or blame the nurses.","Check he is awake enough to talk; if he is still very drowsy, wait, but not beyond today.","Find a private room and ask if he wants anyone with him."],"ask":["'How are you feeling this morning?'","'What do you remember about last night?'","'Would you like anyone with you while we talk?'","'What questions do you have about what happened?'","'What worries you most now?'","'Would you like us to tell your sister, and what may we share?'","'Can you tell me back what happened and what you'll look out for today?'"],"recognise":["Being sent to another hospital for monitoring is a moderate increase in treatment: this is moderate harm, so the statutory duty of candour applies.","A man with past paranoia may hear 'overdose' as 'poisoned'. Anger and suspicion are expected; validate before you inform.","His biggest fear may be losing clozapine, the only drug that has worked.","Risks after excess clozapine: sedation, low BP on standing, fast pulse, drooling with aspiration risk, seizures, delirium and constipation.","Saying sorry is not admitting legal liability.","Naming or blaming a nurse damages trust and pre-judges the review."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell him today in person: a warning, then plainly what happened; apologise; give the known facts, the monitoring plan and that clozapine continues; promise the review findings and a letter.","r":"Meets the statutory and professional duties and is the best way to keep his trust and his treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him he had 'a reaction to his medication' and leave the details to the investigation.","r":"Misleading. It breaches the duty of candour and will destroy trust when he finds out.","best":false},{"c":"Explain it was the agency nurse's fault so he does not lose trust in the ward.","r":"Blaming an individual is unfair, pre-judges the review and breaks the trust team's credibility.","best":false}],"law":["Statutory duty of candour, CQC Regulation 20 (Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014): for moderate harm, tell the person in person as soon as reasonably practicable, apologise, give the facts known, explain further enquiries, then follow up in writing and keep records.","NHS Standard Contract: the first candour notification is expected within 10 working days of the incident being reported.","GMC/NMC Openness and honesty when things go wrong (2022): every clinician has a personal professional duty to be open, apologise and explain.","Compensation Act 2006, section 2: an apology is not in itself an admission of negligence.","He has capacity: his consent is needed before telling his sister (GMC Confidentiality, 2017).","Incidents go on the local system, which feeds NHS England's Learn from Patient Safety Events service; the response follows PSIRF (2022)."],"say":[{"to":"Mr Hollis","s":"I'm afraid we made a mistake with your medicine last night. You were given your night dose of clozapine twice: 300 milligrams at eight, then another 300 at half past nine."},{"to":"Mr Hollis","s":"I'm truly sorry. This should not have happened. On behalf of the ward and the trust, I apologise."},{"to":"Mr Hollis, on stopping clozapine","s":"We're not stopping it. If your checks are fine today, you'll have your usual 300 milligrams tonight. One held dose doesn't mean starting again from the beginning."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"I've told Mr Hollis with Jo. He understands, he's agreed we phone his sister, and Jo will send the letter. He's on 4-hourly obs with lying and standing BP and an ECG this afternoon."}],"write":"Candour entry (dated, timed):\nPresent: Dr [name] (CT1), Jo [surname] (ward manager), Mr Derek Hollis.\nTold: night dose of clozapine 300 mg given twice (20:00 and 21:30), so 600 mg; low BP and drowsiness; acute hospital monitoring; ECG and bloods normal.\nApology given on behalf of the ward and trust.\nExplained: incident reported (ref no.), review to follow, findings to be shared.\nPlan: morning dose held; usual 300 mg tonight if obs stable; obs and ECG today.\nHe consents to his sister Carol being told. Letter to follow within 10 working days (Jo). Offered PALS and advocacy.\nHe repeated back the key facts accurately.","make":["Confirm the incident report exists and its harm grade; add the candour conversation to it.","Warning shot, then the error in one plain sentence with doses and times.","Apologise sincerely; then stop and let him react.","Facts known so far; 'the review will look at that' for anything unknown; no names, no blame.","What was done: acute hospital, tests, morning dose held.","Today's monitoring, the warning signs to report, and that clozapine continues tonight.","Written follow-up, sister told with consent, PALS and advocacy, teach-back, named contact."],"next":"His obs stay normal and he has his usual 300 mg at 20:00, watching the nurse check and sign. His sister rings Jo, angry, and is offered a meeting. The candour letter goes out within the week, and the review finds that the chart layout and an interrupted round led to the missed signature.","marks":["Told him the same day, in person, with the ward manager.","Stated the error plainly, with dose and times.","Gave a clear, sincere apology.","Explained what was done and today's monitoring.","Reassured him that clozapine continues, with the plan for tonight.","Did not speculate, name or blame staff.","Promised the review findings and written follow-up, and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Vague words like 'a medication issue' or 'a reaction'.","Blaming an individual nurse.","Forgetting the written follow-up, which the regulation requires.","Not saying whether clozapine continues, so he fears losing it.","Sharing with family without asking him first."],"refs":["1. CQC. Regulation 20: Duty of candour, guidance for providers (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC and NMC. Openness and honesty when things go wrong: the professional duty of candour (2015, updated 2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England. Patient Safety Incident Response Framework (2022; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: Clozapine (checked Nov 2026)","5. Compensation Act 2006, section 2 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in some health systems, errors are discussed only after legal advice. In the UK, telling the patient promptly and saying sorry is a legal duty for the organisation and a professional duty for you. It is expected, protected and never optional.","obj":["Told him in person, the same day, that he was given his 300 mg night dose of clozapine twice","Given a clear apology on behalf of the ward and the trust, without blaming any individual","Explained what was done, today's monitoring and the warning signs he should report","Confirmed clozapine continues tonight at his usual dose, if his checks are stable","Agreed the written follow-up, the review findings, and whether his sister is told"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you poisoned me. That's what you're saying.\"","r":"I can see why it feels like that, and it's frightening to hear. It was a mistake, not something done on purpose, and I'm truly sorry. Let me tell you exactly what happened."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Who was it? I want their name.\"","r":"I understand why you'd want that. I'm not going to name staff today. The review will look at how the checks failed, so it can't happen to anyone again, and you'll hear what it finds."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you stopping my clozapine now? It's the only thing that's worked.\"","r":"I can hear how much it matters to you. We're not stopping it. If your checks are fine today, you'll have your usual dose tonight."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Has it damaged my heart?\"","r":"Your heart tracing and blood tests last night were normal, and your blood pressure has recovered. We don't expect lasting harm, and we'll do another tracing this afternoon to be sure."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking anything from that trolley again.\"","r":"That's an understandable reaction after last night. Would it help to watch the nurse check your chart and sign tonight, with me there?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm going to sue you lot.\"","r":"That's your right, and it won't affect your care here. You'll get everything I've told you in writing. PALS or an advocate can explain your options."}],"bps":{"bio":["Morning clozapine 150 mg held today (consultant and pharmacist); usual 300 mg at 20:00 tonight if obs stable","NEWS2 with lying and standing BP 4-hourly for 24 h; doctor review if pulse over 120, systolic under 90 or new drowsiness","ECG this afternoon (QTc, rate); clozapine plasma level today; FBC as per his routine clozapine monitoring","Stool chart daily and continue his regular laxatives; constipation risk rises with higher clozapine exposure","Sit him upright if drooling with drowsiness (aspiration risk); seizure or temperature means urgent medical review"],"psych":["Consultant review tomorrow to answer new questions and rebuild trust","Offer him the chance to watch tonight's dose being checked and signed"],"social":["With his consent, Jo phones his sister Carol today with the same account","Ward manager sends the candour letter within 10 working days; findings shared when the review is complete","PALS and independent advocacy offered in writing"],"safety":["Incident on Datix graded moderate harm; candour conversation recorded on it","Interim ward change: clozapine signed immediately after each dose; second check of chart before any night dose","Day team handover: warning signs and escalation triggers written in his notes"]},"facts":[{"h":"Moderate harm triggers statutory candour","x":"Death, severe or moderate harm, or prolonged psychological harm: tell in person, apologise, give the facts, explain the review, then write. Transfer for monitoring counts as moderate."},{"h":"Sorry is not liability","x":"The Compensation Act 2006 says an apology is not, by itself, an admission of negligence. Say 'I'm sorry this happened' clearly and early."},{"h":"Missed for over 48 hours: re-titrate","x":"If clozapine has been missed for more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and re-titrate because of collapse risk. One held dose does not need that."},{"h":"What excess clozapine does","x":"Sedation, low BP on standing, fast pulse, drooling with aspiration risk, seizures (dose-related), delirium, constipation. Watch NEWS2, lying and standing BP, ECG and bowels."},{"h":"Facts, not fault","x":"Share only what is known; answer 'the review will look at that' about why. Do not name, blame or guess. Write down exactly what you said."}],"type":"Breaking bad news + Documentation and reporting","do":"Disclose the double dose to him honestly, apologise, explain actions taken, and agree written follow-up and support.","sim":{"setup":"Wednesday 10:30, male acute ward. Last night Mr Hollis, 58, was given his 300 mg clozapine night dose twice, became drowsy and hypotensive, and was monitored overnight at the acute hospital. With the ward manager, tell him what happened.","role":{"who":"Mr Derek Hollis, 58, informal inpatient with treatment-resistant schizophrenia","open":"Groggy. Like I've been hit by a bus. Why was I in the other hospital? Nobody's said.","manner":"Drowsy and wary at first; flares into suspicion ('poisoned') and anger; frightened about losing clozapine; calms and becomes grateful when spoken to plainly.","free":["He remembers his tablets at 20:00, being woken, then an ambulance","He feels groggy and a bit dizzy","Clozapine is the only drug that has helped him in 30 years","His sister Carol visits on Sundays"],"ifasked":["He felt dizzy getting up to the toilet at 07:00","His bowels last opened two days ago","He has no chest pain or cough","He wants Carol told and to see the letter","He used to believe staff were putting things in his food, years ago","He is thinking of complaining or suing"],"worries":["Was he poisoned on purpose?","Has his heart been damaged?","Will they stop his clozapine?","Who did it, and will it happen again tonight?"],"rules":["If the doctor is vague ('a medication issue'), he asks, 'What exactly did you give me?'","Becomes more suspicious if the doctor blames a named nurse or avoids the apology","Settles when his fear about clozapine is acknowledged and answered","Agrees to Carol being told only if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Hollis, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the ward doctors. This is Jo, the ward manager. How are you feeling this morning?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Groggy. Like I've been hit by a bus. Why was I in the other hospital? Nobody's said."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's what we've come to talk about. Is now OK, and would you like anyone with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"No. Just tell me."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"Before I start, what do you remember about last night?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"My tablets at eight. Then a nurse shaking me. Then an ambulance. That's it."},{"t":"0:44","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid we made a mistake with your medicine last night.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"What mistake?"},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"You were given your night dose of clozapine twice. You had 300 milligrams at eight o'clock, and another 300 at half past nine. So 600 instead of 300.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Twice? You poisoned me?"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like that. It's frightening to hear. I'm truly sorry. This should not have happened, and on behalf of the ward and the trust, I apologise.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sorry. Everyone's always sorry."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"You're right to be angry. Sorry isn't enough on its own, so I want to tell you exactly what happened and what we're doing about it.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Go on, then."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"What we know so far is this. The first dose wasn't signed on your chart. A second nurse saw no signature and gave it again.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Who was it? I want their name."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that. I'm not going to name staff today. What matters is how the checks failed, so it can't happen to anyone again. The review will look at exactly that.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Hmm."},{"t":"2:16","band":"What was done","w":"D","s":"By eleven you were very sleepy and your blood pressure was low. That's why the night doctor sent you to the general hospital, so you could be watched closely.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Am I damaged? My heart?"},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Your heart tracing and blood tests there were normal, and your blood pressure came back up overnight. We don't expect any lasting harm.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"You don't expect. That's not a no."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"That's a fair challenge. The risk was highest last night, and it's falling. That's why we're keeping a close eye on you today.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"The nurses will check your blood pressure lying down and then standing every four hours, and we'll do another heart tracing this afternoon and a blood test for your clozapine level.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"What am I looking out for?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Tell a nurse straight away if you feel dizzy when you stand, your heart races, you have any jerks or a fit, a cough or a temperature, or your bowels stop. Sit on the edge of the bed before you stand.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Dizzy, heart, fits, cough, bowels. Right."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"We held this morning's dose, on advice from the doctors there and our pharmacist.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Are you stopping it? It's the only thing that's ever worked. I'm not going back to the way I was."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much it matters to you. We're not stopping it. If your checks are fine today, you'll have your usual 300 milligrams tonight. Missing one dose doesn't mean starting again from scratch.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Right. Good."},{"t":"4:16","band":"Review and follow-up","w":"D","s":"Jo reported this on our incident system last night. Because it harmed you, there'll be a review of how it happened.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"Will I find out what they find?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Yes. We'll tell you what the review finds and what changes because of it. You can ask questions at any point.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"And what stops it happening again tonight?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"From last night, every clozapine dose on this ward is signed straight after it's given, and a second nurse checks the chart before any night dose.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Jo will also send you a letter within ten working days, with what I've told you, the apology and how the review will run.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I'll want that. My sister will want to read it. Carol."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Would you like us to phone Carol today and tell her what happened?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Yes. She'll go mad, mind."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. We'll be honest with her too. If either of you want to complain or get independent help, PALS or an advocate can support you, and it won't affect your care.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"I might."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've told you a lot. Can you tell me back what happened, and what you'll look out for?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Got my night tablets twice. Blood pressure dropped, went to the other hospital, tests fine. Watch for dizzy, racing heart, fits, cough, bowels. Tablets tonight if I'm OK."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. How are you feeling about it all now?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Shaken. I trusted you lot with my tablets. How do I trust it now?"},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"That's a completely fair thing to feel. Trust has to be earned back. Would it help to watch the nurse check and sign your dose tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"I will."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"I'll see you again after your heart tracing this afternoon, and Dr Varma will see you tomorrow. Jo and I are here all day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks for being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and ward manager, checks he is ready and offers someone to be with him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how he feels and what he remembers before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning, then states plainly he was given his night dose twice, with doses and times","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a clear, sincere apology on behalf of the ward and the trust","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the facts known so far without speculating, naming or blaming staff","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains what was done: acute hospital monitoring, normal tests, morning dose held","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains today's monitoring and the warning signs he should report","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms clozapine continues tonight at his usual dose if checks are stable","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the incident report, the review, immediate changes, and that findings will be shared","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates anger and fear before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees the written follow-up, offers to tell his sister with consent, offers PALS and advocacy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and closes with named contacts and review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the error plainly, apologises sincerely, explains what was done and what happens next, keeps clozapine on track, and promises review findings and a letter.","borderline":"Apologises and explains, but is vague on doses, monitoring or the written follow-up, or does not check understanding.","fail":"Hides or minimises the error, blames a nurse, speculates, or leaves him fearing his clozapine will stop."}}},
{"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","id":"c-m-03","n":1103,"icon":"⚖","title":"A wife's complaint: her husband waited 9 hours in A&E for a bed","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:10","where":"Corridor, then a quiet room, male acute ward","text":"You are the CT2 on the ward. Mr Brian Doyle, 64, was admitted informally overnight with severe depression and suicidal thoughts. The liaison team decided he needed admission at 21:30, but no bed was free until 06:30, so he waited 9 hours on a corridor chair in A&E. His wife, Mrs Pat Doyle, 61, stops you in the ward corridor: 'Are you a doctor? I want to make a complaint, and I want it done properly.' This morning Mr Doyle told the nurse he is happy for his wife to be told about his care."},"task":"Listen to the whole complaint, apologise appropriately, put right what you can today, explain PALS and the formal complaints process, and record and escalate it.","first60":["Move her somewhere private and sit down; offer a drink.","Check who she is and that Mr Doyle has agreed to information being shared (it is in this morning's notes).","Let her tell the whole story before you say anything about it.","Write down each concern in her words as she speaks.","Know where the PALS and complaints leaflets are on your ward."],"ask":["'Please tell me what happened, from the start.'","'Was there anything else?'","'Which of his tablets did he miss?'","'Was he on his own at any point while he waited?'","'What would you like to happen as a result of this?'","'How are you managing yourself?'","'Have I understood everything correctly?'"],"recognise":["Anger in a complaint usually sits on top of fear: she was at home all night afraid he would harm himself.","Separate the concerns: the wait, the conditions, missed medicines, no information for her, a hurtful remark, lost glasses.","Some can be fixed today; some need the formal process; one (missed medicines) is also a reportable incident.","Apologise for her experience and for what went wrong; do not blame A&E colleagues or invent reasons.","She is also his carer, with her own rights and needs."],"decide":[{"c":"Listen fully, summarise, apologise, fix what you can today, explain PALS and the formal route, then record, report and tell the ward manager.","r":"Shows her she was heard, resolves practical harm at once, and keeps her right to a full investigation.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her A&E is a different department and she should take it up with them.","r":"Deflecting is the commonest reason complaints escalate. The trust handles it as one complaint, and you can still help today.","best":false},{"c":"Explain at length the national bed shortage so she understands it is nobody's fault.","r":"Sounds like an excuse; she needs acknowledgement and action first, and a short honest explanation only after.","best":false}],"law":["Local Authority Social Services and NHS Complaints (England) Regulations 2009: complaints can be made orally or in writing, normally within 12 months; acknowledged within 3 working days; investigated, with a written response.","If unhappy with the trust's final response, she can go to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman.","A complaint about his care made on his behalf needs his consent while he has capacity; she can complain about her own experience in her own right.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): respond promptly, fully and honestly to complaints, apologise where appropriate, and do not let a complaint affect care.","NHS Constitution: the right to have a complaint dealt with efficiently and to be told the outcome.","Care Act 2014, section 10: carers can ask the council for their own carer's assessment own needs by the local authority."],"say":[{"to":"Mrs Doyle","s":"I am sorry. Brian should not have waited that long, in those conditions, and you should have been kept informed. I'm also sorry for what was said when he arrived here."},{"to":"Mrs Doyle, on why","s":"Last night there were no free beds in the trust until someone was discharged. That's an explanation, not an excuse, and it shouldn't fall on patients like Brian."},{"to":"Mrs Doyle, on the process","s":"PALS can get you quick answers, often within days. If you want a full investigation, the complaints team will acknowledge it within three working days and send a written response."},{"to":"Ward manager","s":"Mrs Doyle has raised a complaint about her husband's 9-hour wait, missed evening medicines, no updates, and a remark on arrival. I've given her PALS details and reported the wait and the missed doses."}],"write":"Notes entry (dated, timed):\nMet Mrs Pat Doyle (wife; Mr Doyle consented to sharing, 09:00 entry).\nConcerns in her words: 9-hour wait on a corridor chair after decision to admit (21:30 to 06:30); no food until 03:00; missed evening amlodipine and sertraline; she was sent home at midnight and not updated; remark on arrival 'you're lucky, some people wait two days'; glasses left in A&E.\nApology given. Actions: medicines chart checked; A&E asked to return glasses; named nurse to phone her daily at 18:00; consultant meeting at Wednesday ward round; carers' assessment offered.\nPALS and complaints leaflet given. Incident reported (long wait, missed medicines). Ward manager informed.","make":["Private room, seat, drink, your bleep on silent if safe.","Open question, then silence; 'Was there anything else?' until there is nothing else.","Summarise her concerns as a list and check you have them all.","Name the feeling; apologise for the specific failures, not 'if you were upset'.","One honest sentence on why, no excuses, no blaming colleagues.","Actions today with owners and times; then PALS, formal complaint, Ombudsman.","Write it up, report incidents, tell the ward manager, confirm the next contact."],"next":"His glasses arrive by 17:00 and his named nurse phones Mrs Doyle at 18:00. On Wednesday she meets the consultant with her husband. She chooses to make a formal complaint with his consent; the trust acknowledges it within three working days and includes the emergency department in the investigation.","marks":["Took her somewhere private and let her finish.","Checked consent to discuss her husband's care.","Summarised every concern back accurately.","Apologised for specific failures without blaming colleagues.","Fixed the practical problems the same day.","Explained PALS, the formal complaint route, timescales and the Ombudsman.","Recorded, reported and escalated, and offered a carer's assessment."],"pitfalls":["Arguing the facts or defending A&E before she has finished.","'I'm sorry you feel that way': an apology for her feelings, not for what happened.","Promising outcomes you cannot deliver, such as 'this will never happen again'.","Forgetting that missed medicines are a reportable incident.","Handing over a PALS leaflet instead of listening."],"refs":["1. The Local Authority Social Services and National Health Service Complaints (England) Regulations 2009 (checked Nov 2026)","2. Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman. NHS Complaint Standards (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC. Good medical practice (2024; checked Nov 2026)","4. Department of Health and Social Care. The NHS Constitution for England (checked Nov 2026)","5. Care Act 2014, section 10: assessment of a carer's needs (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients and families are encouraged to complain, and trusts must learn from complaints. A complaint is not a threat to your career; how you listen and respond is what people remember. Never discourage someone from complaining.","obj":["Heard her whole complaint without interrupting and summarised all six concerns back to her","Apologised for the specific failures without blaming A&E colleagues","Arranged today: medicines checked, glasses returned, a daily phone update and a consultant meeting","Explained PALS, the formal complaints process with its 3-working-day acknowledgement, and the Ombudsman","Reported the wait and the missed medicines, informed the ward manager and offered a carer's assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nine hours on a plastic chair! Is that how you treat people here?\"","r":"It's not how anyone should be treated, and I'm sorry it happened to Brian. Please tell me everything, from the start. I want to understand it properly."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I sat at home all night thinking he'd walk out and do something.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, being at home with no news while he was so unwell. Anyone would be frightened and angry. I'm sorry you were left like that."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Don't give me a leaflet and a pat on the head.\"","r":"I won't. I'll do some things today, and then I'll explain how to make sure your complaint is formally recorded and answered in writing."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why did it take nine hours?\"","r":"Honestly, there were no free beds until someone was discharged this morning. That explains it, but doesn't excuse it, and your complaint helps the trust see the effect."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I complain, will they treat him differently?\"","r":"No. Complaining won't affect his care in any way, and staff are expected to welcome it. I'd rather know when we've got things wrong."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"And what about that nurse who said he was lucky?\"","r":"That wasn't acceptable, and I'm sorry. I'll tell the ward manager today so it's dealt with properly."}],"bps":{"bio":["Check today's drug chart: morning sertraline and amlodipine given; missed evening doses recorded, next doses as normal, not doubled","Full obs with NEWS2 after a night with little sleep and food; meal and fluids offered now"],"psych":["Consultant meets Mr and Mrs Doyle at Wednesday ward round to agree the treatment plan","Named nurse phones Mrs Doyle every evening at 18:00 with an update"],"social":["Ask A&E to send his glasses up with the porter today","Carers' lead arranges a carer's assessment through the local authority (Care Act 2014)","PALS and complaints details given in writing; help offered to write the complaint if she wants it"],"safety":["Incident reports: the 9-hour wait after decision to admit, and the missed evening medicines","Ward manager told today, including the remark on arrival, for feedback to the nurse","His suicide risk reviewed today; she is given the ward number to ring any time with concerns"]},"facts":[{"h":"Complaints: 12 months, 3 days","x":"Complaints can be oral or written, usually within 12 months. The trust must acknowledge within 3 working days, investigate and reply in writing."},{"h":"PALS first, formal any time","x":"PALS resolves concerns informally and quickly. It does not replace the formal route; she can use both."},{"h":"The Ombudsman is the last step","x":"If she is unhappy with the trust's final response, she can ask the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman to review it."},{"h":"Apologise for what happened","x":"'I'm sorry he waited nine hours on a chair' works. 'I'm sorry you feel upset' makes people angrier."},{"h":"Consent: his care, her experience","x":"A complaint about his care needs his consent while he has capacity. Her own experience, such as no updates, she can complain about herself."},{"h":"Carers are entitled to assessment","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 any adult caring for another can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment, whatever the level of care."}],"type":"Handling a complaint","do":"Hear her complaint in full, apologise, fix what you can today, and explain PALS and formal complaints.","sim":{"setup":"Monday 15:10, male acute ward. Mr Brian Doyle, 64, waited 9 hours in A&E overnight for a bed. His wife stops you in the corridor wanting to complain. He has agreed she can be told about his care. Respond to her complaint.","role":{"who":"Mrs Pat Doyle, 61, Mr Doyle's wife","open":"Are you a doctor? I want to make a complaint, and I want it done properly.","manner":"Angry, clipped and determined; tearful once her fear is named; softens and becomes cooperative when she is heard and sees concrete action.","free":["Decision to admit at 21:30; he waited until 06:30 on a corridor chair","No food until 03:00","She was sent home at midnight and not told he had moved","A nurse on arrival said 'You're lucky, some people wait two days'"],"ifasked":["He missed his evening amlodipine and sertraline","A security guard sat with him most of the night","His glasses were left in A&E","They have been married 41 years","She feared he would leave and kill himself","She wants it on record and does not want it to happen to anyone else"],"worries":["Why did it take nine hours?","Will complaining affect his care?","Does Brian have to agree to the complaint?","What will happen about the nurse's remark?"],"rules":["If interrupted or contradicted early, she gets louder and says, 'You're not listening'","Becomes tearful when asked about her night at home; settles if validated","Rejects a leaflet offered before she has been heard","Calms noticeably once concrete actions with times are offered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Osei, one of the doctors on this ward. Can I help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Are you a doctor? I want to make a complaint, and I want it done properly."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear it properly. Let's not do it in the corridor. There's a quiet room here. Can I get you a drink?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Water. Fine."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"Can I check who you are?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Pat Doyle. Brian's wife. Brian Doyle. He came in last night."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Doyle. Brian told the team this morning he's happy for us to talk to you, so I can be open with you. Please tell me what happened, from the start.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"We got to A&E at seven. The mental health nurse saw him and said at half nine he needed a bed. Then nothing. Nine hours. On a plastic chair in a corridor, next to a drunk man shouting. Brian. Who's never been in trouble in his life."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Go on.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Nobody gave him anything to eat till three in the morning. He missed his evening tablets. And they sent me home at midnight. Nobody rang me. I didn't know he'd moved here till he texted at seven."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Was there anything else?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"When he got here, a nurse said, 'You're lucky, some people wait two days.' Lucky! And he left his glasses down there. He can't read a thing."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Which tablets did he miss, do you know?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"His blood pressure one and his antidepressant."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"And while he waited, was someone with him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"A security man, mostly. That's what upset him. Like he was a criminal."},{"t":"2:03","band":"Summarise","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it all. He waited nine hours on a corridor chair after the decision to admit him. No food till three. Missed evening tablets. You were sent home and not told when he moved. A hurtful remark when he arrived. And his glasses are still in A&E. Have I missed anything?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No. That's it. I sat at home all night thinking he'd walk out of there and... I thought I'd get a call to say he'd done something."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. He's very unwell, and you were at home all night with no news. Anyone would be frightened and angry.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Forty-one years we've been married. Sorry. I'm not a crier."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. Take your time.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:56","band":"Apologise and explain","w":"D","s":"I am sorry. Brian should not have waited that long, in those conditions, and you should have been kept informed. I'm also sorry for what was said when he arrived. That wasn't acceptable.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Why did it take nine hours?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you. There were no free beds in the trust last night until someone was discharged this morning. That's an explanation, not an excuse. It shouldn't fall on patients like Brian.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"So it'll happen to someone else tonight."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"It might. That's exactly why your complaint matters. Long waits are reported and reviewed, and complaints like yours show the trust what they mean for families.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:44","band":"Put things right","w":"D","s":"Let me tell you what I'll do today. As soon as we finish, I'll check his chart to make sure he's had this morning's tablets and that he carries on as normal tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"He said he'd had them."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"Good, I'll confirm it. Next, I'll ring A&E and ask them to send his glasses up today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"His consultant, Dr Hughes, will see you both at the ward round on Wednesday. And I can tell you his plan now, if you'd like.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Yes. Nobody's told me anything."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"He's settled and slept a few hours this morning. Today is a full assessment and blood tests. His named nurse, Mo, will ring you every evening at six.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"And the complaint? I want it on record. Not a pat on the head."},{"t":"4:46","band":"The process","w":"D","s":"It will be on record. There are two routes. PALS, our patient advice and Liaison Service (PALS) (PALS) (PALS), can take your concerns and get quick answers, often within days. Or you can make a formal complaint to the trust, by letter, email or phone.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"What happens with a formal one?"},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"They acknowledge it within three working days, agree with you what they'll look into, including A&E, and send you a written response. If you're not satisfied, you can go to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Does Brian have to agree?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"For anything about his care, yes, they'll ask him. Your own experience, being sent home and not told, you can complain about yourself.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Will it go against him? Will they treat him differently?"},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"No. Complaining won't affect his care in any way. I'd rather know when we get things wrong.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"You're also his carer. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, for support for you. Our carers' lead can arrange it.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"I've never thought of myself as a carer."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"Many people don't. Last night shows how much you carry.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So, I'll check his tablets and get his glasses up. Mo rings you at six. Dr Hughes sees you on Wednesday. Here are the PALS and complaints details in writing. Does that cover everything?","m":[12,5]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"And the nurse who said that?"},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"I'll tell the ward manager today. I'm also reporting the long wait and the missed tablets on our incident system, and I'll write what you've told me in his notes.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Right. Thank you for listening, at least."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If anything else worries you, ring the ward, day or night.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Moves her to a private room, introduces self and offers a seat and drink","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks her identity and the patient's consent before discussing his care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lets her tell the whole story without interrupting or defending","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks specific questions to clarify each concern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises all her concerns back and checks nothing is missed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Apologises for the specific failures without blaming colleagues","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and validates her fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a short, honest explanation for the wait without excuses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Takes concrete actions today: medicines, glasses, consultant meeting, daily update","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains PALS, formal complaint, 3-working-day acknowledgement and the Ombudsman","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reassures care is unaffected and offers a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Records, reports incidents, informs ward manager and confirms next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Listens fully, summarises, apologises specifically, fixes what can be fixed today, explains both complaint routes and records and escalates.","borderline":"Apologises and explains PALS, but interrupts, misses a concern or offers no same-day action.","fail":"Defends or blames A&E, argues with her, discourages a complaint, or hands over a leaflet without listening."}}},
{"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","id":"c-m-04","n":1104,"icon":"🏥","title":"Working with an interpreter: low mood in an Arabic-speaking woman","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in the CMHT. Mrs Rania Khalil, a 45-year-old woman from Syria who came to the UK four years ago, is referred by her GP with six months of low mood, poor sleep and weight loss. She speaks little English. A female Arabic interpreter, Ms Huda Saleh, has been booked in person. At reception, Mrs Khalil's husband says, 'We don't need the interpreter. Our son is sixteen and his English is perfect.'"},"task":"Brief the interpreter, assess Mrs Khalil's mood and risk speaking directly to her (seeing her without family), explain the plan and check she has understood it.","first60":["Thank the husband, explain that a professional interpreter is standard for every patient, and offer that family can join at the end.","Brief the interpreter in private before the consultation (2 to 3 minutes).","Check the dialect and that the interpreter does not know the family.","Arrange seats in a triangle so you face Mrs Khalil, with the interpreter beside you or slightly behind.","Allow extra time; plan short sentences and one question at a time."],"ask":["To the interpreter: 'Which Arabic do you speak, and will Syrian Arabic be comfortable?'","To the interpreter: 'Do you know her or her family?'","'Can you tell me what's been happening?'","'When you say your heart is squeezed, what is that like for you?'","'Have you had felt that life isn't worth living? Any thoughts about ending your life?'","'Do you feel safe at home? Has anyone hurt you or made you afraid?'","'Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?'"],"recognise":["Family interpreters filter, protect and omit, especially about suicide, sex, abuse or shame; a child should not carry their parent's distress.","Distress is often described in body terms ('my heart is squeezed', 'my chest is tight'); explore the meaning rather than assume a cardiac or a psychiatric cause.","Bereavement at a distance, unfinished grief, displacement and war trauma commonly sit behind depression in refugees.","Faith may protect against suicide, but never assume; ask everyone directly.","Stigma about mental illness may limit what she says in front of family or accepts as a diagnosis.","The interpreter may also be affected by the material; check on them afterwards."],"decide":[{"c":"Use the booked professional interpreter, brief her, see Mrs Khalil without family, speak to her directly, and invite her son in at the end with her agreement.","r":"Accurate, confidential and safe; the only way to ask about suicide and abuse properly.","best":true},{"c":"Let the son interpret since he is here and the family prefer it.","r":"Unsafe: a 16-year-old will filter, she cannot disclose freely, and he is burdened with her distress.","best":false},{"c":"Send the interpreter away and use a translation app on your phone.","r":"Not acceptable for a mental health assessment: inaccurate, no confidentiality, and no cultural context.","best":false}],"law":["Equality Act 2010 and the public sector equality duty: trusts must not leave people at a disadvantage in getting care because of race, which includes language barriers.","NHS England guidance on interpreting (2018): use professional interpreters; avoid family members, and never use children, except in an emergency.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): make sure arrangements meet patients' language needs; give information in a way they can understand.","GMC Decision making and consent (2020): she needs information she understands to make decisions about treatment.","Interpreters are bound by confidentiality through their professional code and contract.","Domestic abuse: ask when she is alone; if disclosed, follow local safeguarding adults procedures and offer a referral to an IDVA."],"say":[{"to":"Husband, at reception","s":"Thank you; your son sounds a great help. We use a trained interpreter for everyone, so your wife can speak freely and your son doesn't have to carry it. You're both welcome to join at the end."},{"to":"Interpreter","s":"Please interpret everything she and I say, in the first person, even asides. If a phrase needs a cultural explanation, tell us both that you're adding it."},{"to":"Mrs Khalil","s":"I think you have depression. It's a common illness, it's treatable, and it isn't weakness or your fault."},{"to":"Interpreter, afterwards","s":"Thank you. Some of that was hard to hear. How are you doing?"}],"write":"Clinic note (dated, timed):\nSeen with professional Arabic interpreter Ms Huda Saleh (in person, Levantine Arabic, booking ref); family not present for assessment; son joined at the end with her consent.\nHistory: six months low mood after mother's death in Syria; early waking, poor appetite, about 5 kg weight loss, anhedonia, guilt; describes 'heart squeezed' as sadness.\nRisk: passive death wishes, no intent or plan; protective: children, faith. Safe at home, no abuse disclosed.\nImpression: moderate to severe depressive episode.\nPlan: sertraline 50 mg mane; NHS Talking Therapies referral with interpreter; GP bloods; review in 2 weeks with interpreter booked; crisis information in Arabic.\nUnderstanding confirmed by teach-back. Interpreter debriefed.","make":["Book the interpreter in person, same sex if requested, the right dialect, and double time.","Brief: purpose, first person, everything interpreted, explain cultural terms openly, confidentiality, distressing topics.","Seat in a triangle; look at and speak to her, not the interpreter.","Short sentences, one question at a time, no jargon or idioms.","Full mood and risk assessment, including domestic abuse, while she is alone.","Teach-back for the plan; written information in Arabic.","Debrief the interpreter; book the next interpreter now; record the interpreter's details."],"next":"Mrs Khalil starts sertraline. At two weeks, with the same interpreter, she is sleeping a little better and has had one telephone triage with NHS Talking Therapies with an interpreter. She mentions nightmares about the war, and you screen for PTSD.","marks":["Declined the son as interpreter politely, with a reason.","Briefed the interpreter on role, first-person interpreting, confidentiality and distressing topics.","Spoke directly to the patient in short sentences.","Explored the meaning of bodily descriptions of distress.","Asked directly about suicide and safety at home.","Explained the diagnosis and plan simply and checked by teach-back.","Debriefed the interpreter and booked one for follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Saying 'tell her...' and 'ask her...' to the interpreter instead of speaking to the patient.","Long, compound questions that cannot be interpreted accurately.","Accepting a family member to save time.","Assuming faith rules out suicide and not asking.","Using idioms ('feeling blue', 'down in the dumps') that do not translate."],"refs":["1. NHS England. Guidance for commissioners: Interpreting and translation services in primary care (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022): treatment and management (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC. Good medical practice (2024) and Decision making and consent (2020) (checked Nov 2026)","5. Equality Act 2010 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: if you share a language with a patient, check your trust's policy before using it clinically, and still record it. Never ask a child or a relative to interpret; book a professional, even by phone or video if in person is not possible.","obj":["Briefed the interpreter on first-person, word-for-word interpreting, confidentiality and distressing topics","Declined the son as interpreter with a clear, kind reason and seen her without family","Assessed core depressive symptoms, the meaning of her bodily distress, and suicidal thoughts directly","Asked about safety at home while she was alone","Checked by teach-back that she understood the diagnosis, sertraline and talking therapy with an interpreter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My son can translate. He's outside. Why do we need a stranger?\"","r":"Your son sounds very caring. A trained interpreter means you can speak freely, and he doesn't have to carry your worries. He can join us at the end if you'd like."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My heart is squeezed all the time. Like a stone.\"","r":"That sounds very heavy to carry. Tell me more about that feeling, and when it started?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I left my mother there. I couldn't even go to her funeral.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. Not being able to say goodbye is one of the hardest things. It makes sense that you're grieving so deeply."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"In my family we don't have this. People will say I'm mad.\"","r":"I understand that worry. Depression isn't madness, and it's very common. What we talk about stays private."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I ask God to take me in my sleep.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's important. Have you ever thought of ending your life yourself, or made any plans?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to take tablets forever?\"","r":"No. Usually for at least six months after you feel well, then we reduce slowly together. Please don't stop suddenly."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning; range 50 to 200 mg; increase in 50 mg steps no sooner than weekly if needed","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache, more anxiety in the first 1 to 2 weeks; benefit after 2 to 4 weeks; do not stop suddenly","GP bloods: FBC, U&E (sodium), TFTs, B12, folate, ferritin, vitamin D, HbA1c; weight recorded today","Review in 2 weeks, earlier if suicidal thoughts increase"],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies referral: high-intensity CBT for depression with an Arabic interpreter at every session","Screen for PTSD at review if nightmares or flashbacks persist; trauma-focused CBT or EMDR if confirmed","Bereavement work about her mother's death as part of therapy"],"social":["Interpreter booked now for every future appointment; letters and crisis information in Arabic","With her consent, signpost a local Arabic-speaking women's or refugee community group","Son told only what she agrees to share"],"safety":["Passive death wishes, no plan: crisis line via NHS 111 option 2, asking for an Arabic interpreter","Safety at home asked privately; domestic abuse pathway and IDVA if ever disclosed","GP letter today with the plan, risk summary and the need for an interpreter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never a child, rarely family","x":"Professional interpreters only, except in a genuine emergency. Children must not interpret: they filter, and it harms them. Record the interpreter's name or ID."},{"h":"Brief before, debrief after","x":"Two minutes before: purpose, first person, everything interpreted, cultural terms explained openly, confidentiality, distressing topics. Afterwards ask how they are."},{"h":"Speak to the patient, not the interpreter","x":"Look at her and say 'you', not 'ask her'. Short sentences, one question, no idioms. Pause for each chunk."},{"h":"Check dialect and connection","x":"Arabic varies widely by region. Confirm the interpreter is comfortable with Syrian Arabic and does not know the family, especially in small communities."},{"h":"Sertraline start and review","x":"50 mg once daily; usual range 50 to 200 mg. Review within 2 weeks for adults (1 week if under 25 or higher risk). Check sodium if she becomes confused or unwell."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Brief the interpreter, assess her mood and suicide risk speaking directly to her, and confirm understanding by teach-back.","sim":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:00, CMHT clinic. Mrs Rania Khalil, 45, from Syria, is referred with six months of low mood. A professional Arabic interpreter is booked; her family want her 16-year-old son to interpret. Brief the interpreter, then assess her mood and risk.","role":{"who":"Mrs Rania Khalil, 45, Syrian, speaking through Ms Huda Saleh, a professional Arabic interpreter","open":"Thank you, doctor. My son is outside. His English is very good. He can help.","manner":"Polite, quiet and guarded; describes sadness as a squeezed heart; tears when her mother is mentioned; opens up when spoken to directly and kindly; worried about stigma.","free":["Her heart feels squeezed and she does not sleep","Her mother died in Syria six months ago; she could not go to the funeral","She has three children; her daughters now cook","She feels guilty for leaving her family behind"],"ifasked":["She wakes at 4 or 5 a.m. and cannot get back to sleep","Her clothes are loose; about 5 kg weight loss","She enjoys nothing, even time with her children","She sometimes asks God to take her in her sleep; no plan or intent","She has occasional nightmares about bombing","She feels safe at home; her husband worries about money"],"worries":["People will say she is mad","Will she have to take tablets forever?","What will her son be told?"],"rules":["Agrees to see the doctor without her son only if given a kind reason","Gives short answers if the doctor speaks to the interpreter in the third person ('ask her...')","Discloses passive death wishes only if asked directly","The interpreter adds one cultural explanation and responds if asked how she is afterwards"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Brief the interpreter","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ms Saleh, thank you for coming. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can we take two minutes before we go in?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Of course."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil is 45, from Syria, referred by her GP with low mood. Will Syrian Arabic be comfortable for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Yes, I'm from Damascus. No problem."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"Good. Do you know her or her family? It's a small community.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: I don't think so. I'll tell you if I recognise her."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Please interpret everything, as closely as you can, in the first person: 'I feel', not 'she says she feels'. Even things that seem off-topic.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Yes. Sometimes a phrase needs explaining."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Please do, and just tell us both you're adding an explanation. We may talk about painful things, including suicide. Is that OK for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: That's fine. Thank you for asking."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"I'll speak in short chunks and pause. Let's sit in a triangle so she and I face each other. Everything stays confidential.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil, my name is Dr Rahman. I'm a psychiatry doctor. This is Ms Saleh, a professional interpreter. She'll repeat everything we both say, and it's all confidential.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. My son is outside. His English is very good. He can help."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Your son sounds very caring. Today it's better for you to talk through Ms Saleh. You can say things privately, and he doesn't have to carry your worries. He can come in at the end, if you'd like.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Yes. Maybe that's better. He's only sixteen."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Doctor, my heart is squeezed. All the time. Here. And I don't sleep."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"When you say your heart is squeezed, what is it like for you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Like a heavy stone. The sadness sits here."},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Doctor, I'm adding something. This phrase is commonly used for deep sadness or worry, not only for pain."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's helpful. Mrs Khalil, how long have you felt this heaviness?","m":[7,6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"About six months. Since my mother died in Syria. I couldn't go to her funeral."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Not being able to say goodbye to your mother must be very painful.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Every day I think, I left her there. I left everyone."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Take your time. There's no rush.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"How is your sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"I wake at four or five and can't sleep again. I think and think."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And your appetite? Your energy?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"I'm not hungry. My clothes are loose. I'm tired all the time. My daughters cook now."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you still get pleasure from?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Even with my children, I'm there but not there. I feel guilty."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Do you have bad dreams or memories of the war?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Some nights I dream of the bombs. Less than before."},{"t":"3:36","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people feel this low, they feel life isn't worth living. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I ask God to take me. In my sleep."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you ever thought of ending your life yourself, or made any plans?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"No. Never. My children need me. And in my faith it is forbidden. I would not do it."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Your children and your faith matter a lot. If those thoughts ever got stronger, I'd want you to tell us straight away.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Do you feel safe at home? Has anyone hurt you, or made you afraid?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No, no. My husband is a good man. He is tired too. He worries about money."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If that ever changed, you could tell us or your GP privately, always with an interpreter, never family.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain the plan","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil, I think you have depression. It's a common illness, and it can be treated. It's not weakness, and it's not your fault.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Depression. In my family we don't talk of this. People will say I'm mad."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. Depression isn't madness, and many people have it. What we discuss stays private.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"What do I do?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Two things help. First, a tablet called sertraline. It's not addictive. One each morning. It takes two to four weeks to help. At first you may feel sick or more nervous; that usually settles.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Will I take it forever?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"No. Usually for at least six months after you feel better, then we reduce it slowly together. Please don't stop it suddenly.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Second, talking therapy, with an Arabic interpreter every time. I'll also ask your GP for blood tests, to check things like your thyroid and iron.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"To make sure I explained it well, can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I have depression. It's an illness. One tablet in the morning; it helps after some weeks; maybe I feel sick at first. Talking therapy with an interpreter. Blood tests with my GP."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. If those thoughts get stronger, call NHS 111 and press 2, and say 'Arabic' for an interpreter. I'll give you this in writing in Arabic. I'll see you in two weeks, with an interpreter.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Would you like your son to come in now? What would you like us to tell him?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Only that I have depression and will take medicine. Not about my mother. He's sad too."},{"t":"6:38","w":"D","s":"Of course. Ms Saleh, afterwards, thank you. Some of that was heavy. How are you doing?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: I'm fine. It reminded me of home, but I'm OK. Thank you for asking."}],"marks":[{"c":"Briefs the interpreter: purpose, first-person interpreting, everything interpreted, distressing topics","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks dialect, that the interpreter does not know the family, seating and confidentiality","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines the son as interpreter kindly with a reason, and offers him a part at the end","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Introduces self and interpreter and explains how the interpreting and confidentiality work","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Speaks directly to the patient in short, single questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about core depressive symptoms, duration, sleep, appetite, enjoyment and trauma","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the meaning of her bodily description of distress without assuming","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about safety at home and abuse while she is without family","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her grief, guilt and fear of stigma","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains depression, sertraline and interpreted talking therapy simply","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back, safety-nets, books follow-up and debriefs the interpreter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Briefs the interpreter, keeps family out of the assessment, speaks directly to her, covers mood and risk including home safety, and confirms understanding.","borderline":"Uses the interpreter well but misses the briefing, the home safety question or the teach-back.","fail":"Lets the son interpret, talks about her in the third person, or does not ask directly about suicide."}}},
{"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","id":"c-m-05","n":1105,"icon":"👪","title":"Explaining to a 10-year-old why her mum is on a psychiatric ward","moment":{"when":"Saturday 14:30","where":"Family room off the adult acute ward","text":"You are the CT1 covering the ward at the weekend. Mrs Sarah Bennett, 38, was admitted on Thursday under Section 2 with her first manic episode with psychotic features. Her daughter Lily, 10, saw the police and paramedics take her mum from home after an argument about bedtime. Her father, Mr Tom Bennett, 40, asks you to explain to Lily 'what's wrong with Mum', because Lily thinks it is her fault. Yesterday Mrs Bennett agreed that Lily should be told 'gently and honestly'."},"task":"Explain Mum's illness to Lily in words a 10-year-old understands, with her father present; tell her clearly it is not her fault; answer her questions honestly; and agree visiting, contact and Young Carers support.","first60":["Check with the nurse in charge that Mrs Bennett agreed to Lily being told, and what she is happy to share.","Ask Dad first, briefly and out of Lily's hearing: what has she seen, what has she been told, what words does the family use?","Use the family room, not the ward; sit at Lily's level; have paper and pens.","Plan three simple messages: Mum is ill and being looked after; it is not your fault; you can still see and talk to her.","Know the ward's child visiting arrangements before you promise anything."],"ask":["'What do you think has happened to Mum?'","'What was the scariest part?'","'What questions have you got? There are no silly ones.'","'How are you feeling about it all?'","'Who would you like to know at school?'","'Can you tell me what you'd say to your cat about Mum?'","To Dad: 'How are you managing?'"],"recognise":["Children often believe they caused a parent's illness; at 10, magical thinking about an argument is common and must be named and corrected directly.","Witnessing police involvement can make a child think the parent is a criminal or in prison.","Honest uncertainty ('I don't know when yet') is better than a broken promise.","A child doing more at home (making meals, looking after a parent) may be a young carer.","Silence or play during the talk is normal; check understanding rather than demanding eye contact.","Dad may be overwhelmed; his capacity to support Lily is part of her safety."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain in simple, honest words with Dad present: Mum's brain is ill, it is not your fault, you can't catch it, she is safe; then agree a phone call, a planned visit and Young Carers support.","r":"Reduces guilt and fear, keeps her connected to Mum and gives the family support.","best":true},{"c":"Tell Lily that Mum is 'very tired and having a rest' to protect her.","r":"She saw the police; a vague story increases fear and guilt, and she will stop trusting adults' explanations.","best":false},{"c":"Explain bipolar disorder, psychosis and the Mental Health Act in detail.","r":"Too much, too abstract; she needs three clear messages, not a lecture.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): share with family what the patient has agreed to; Mrs Bennett has consented to Lily being told gently and honestly.","MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 11: hospitals should support visits by children, in a suitable family room away from the ward, planned in advance and in the child's best interests.","Children and Families Act 2014: local authorities must assess a young carer's needs on request or where they appear to have needs.","Care Act 2014: Mr Bennett can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment.","Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023): adult services must 'think family', record children in the household and consider their needs."],"say":[{"to":"Lily","s":"Mum has an illness in her brain. It's making her thoughts go super fast, like a car without brakes, and she hasn't been able to sleep."},{"to":"Lily","s":"I want you to hear this really clearly. It is not your fault. Nothing you said or did made Mum ill."},{"to":"Lily, on the police","s":"Mum isn't in trouble. Sometimes the police help when someone is very poorly and needs to get to hospital safely. That's all they were doing."},{"to":"Mr Bennett","s":"There's a young carers service locally that supports children whose parent is unwell. Would you like me to refer Lily?"}],"write":"Notes entry (dated, timed):\nMet Lily Bennett (10) with her father Tom (40) in the family room, with Mrs Bennett's consent (recorded yesterday).\nExplained in age-appropriate words: Mum has a brain illness, is safe and being cared for, not Lily's fault, cannot be caught, police were helping not punishing.\nLily's questions answered; she repeated the key messages back.\nPlan: phone call Sunday with nurse support if Mum settled; first visit in family room early next week, decided by nurse in charge; Lily to bring a card.\nYoung Carers referral with father's consent; father to tell class teacher Monday; carer's assessment offered to father.\nThink Family section updated: Lily lives with father, who is caring for her; no safeguarding concerns.","make":["Prepare: consent from Mum, Dad's view of what Lily knows, the family room, paper and pens.","Start with her: name, something she likes, then 'What do you think has happened?'","Explain in three messages, concrete words, one idea at a time.","Correct the guilt directly and repeat it.","Answer questions honestly, including 'I don't know yet'.","Agree contact: phone call, visit in the family room, a card or drawing.","Young Carers referral, school informed with her say, carer's assessment for Dad, Think Family recorded."],"next":"On Sunday Lily speaks to Mum for five minutes with a nurse beside the phone. On Tuesday they meet in the family room for 20 minutes; Lily gives Mum a drawing of the cat. The young carers service contacts Mr Bennett, and Lily's teacher sets up a weekly check-in.","marks":["Asked what Lily already thought before explaining.","Used simple, concrete words with no jargon.","Said clearly, and more than once, that it was not her fault.","Explained the police role without frightening detail.","Was honest about uncertainty over when Mum comes home.","Agreed visiting and phone contact.","Arranged Young Carers support and checked on Dad."],"pitfalls":["Euphemisms ('Mum's tired') that do not match what she saw.","Promising a date for Mum coming home.","Talking to Dad over Lily's head.","Forgetting to address the guilt directly.","Telling her she 'won't get it', when the honest answer is that she cannot catch it."],"refs":["1. Department of Health. MHA Code of Practice, ch. 11 (2015; checked Nov 2026)","2. Children and Families Act 2014, section 96: young carers' needs assessments (checked Nov 2026)","3. HM Government. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Parental mental illness: the impact on children and adolescents, information for parents, carers and anyone who works with young people (checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, children are usually told the truth about a parent's mental illness, in simple words, and are encouraged to visit. Protecting a child by keeping it secret is not seen as kind; it tends to increase fear and guilt.","obj":["Explained Mum's illness in simple, concrete words a 10-year-old repeated back correctly","Told Lily clearly, more than once, that Mum's illness is not her fault","Answered her questions about the police, catching it and when Mum comes home honestly","Agreed a phone call and a planned visit in the family room","Arranged a Young Carers referral and school support with Dad's consent and Lily's say"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's my fault. I shouted at her and said I hated her.\"","r":"I'm really glad you told me. It is not your fault. Lots of children say that when they're cross. Nothing you said made Mum ill."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is Mum going to die?\"","r":"No. This illness doesn't make people die. Doctors know a lot about it, and most people get better with medicine and rest."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why did the police come? Is she in prison?\"","r":"She's not in trouble, and this is a hospital, not a prison. The police helped get her here safely because she was very poorly."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Can I catch it?\"","r":"You can't catch it, like you can't catch a broken leg. Being near Mum or hugging her is completely safe."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When is she coming home?\"","r":"I don't know yet, and I don't want to make a promise I can't keep. Usually it's a few weeks. You can talk to her and see her before then."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Will she be weird when I see her?\"","r":"She might talk fast or seem sleepy from her medicine. That's the illness. If it feels too much, you can stop the visit, and that's OK."}],"bps":{"bio":["Mum: antipsychotic at night to help sleep and slow her thoughts (for Lily: 'medicine to help her sleep and slow her thoughts down')","Visiting timed with nurses for when Mum is calmer, not straight after a PRN dose"],"psych":["Repeat the three messages in a few days; children need to hear them more than once","Lily makes a card or drawing to take to Mum; Dad to keep a short daily routine","If Lily has persistent sleep problems, school refusal or distress beyond a few weeks, GP to consider CAMHS"],"social":["Young Carers: refer with Dad's consent; young carer's needs assessment from children's services if needed","Dad tells Lily's class teacher on Monday (only the teacher, as Lily asked)","Carers' lead offers Mr Bennett a carer's assessment through the local authority","Named nurse phones Dad daily at an agreed time"],"safety":["Phone call Sunday with a nurse beside Mum, only if she is settled","First visit in the family room off the ward, Dad present, about 20 to 30 minutes, staff nearby; agreed by the nurse in charge","Think Family section of Mum's record updated: Lily lives with Dad, cared for safely; reviewed at ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three messages, simple words","x":"Mum is ill in her brain and being looked after. It is not your fault. You can still see and talk to her. Repeat them, and ask her to say them back."},{"h":"Name the guilt and correct it","x":"Children often link a parent's illness to something they did. Say 'It is not your fault' plainly, link it to the argument she mentioned, and say it again."},{"h":"Honest about uncertainty","x":"'I don't know yet' is safer than a date. A broken promise about coming home damages trust more than honest uncertainty."},{"h":"Children visit off the ward","x":"The MHA Code of Practice expects hospitals to support children's visits in a family room away from the ward, planned with staff and in the child's interests."},{"h":"Young carers have a right to assessment","x":"Under the Children and Families Act 2014, the local authority must assess a young carer's needs. Ask what she does at home for Mum."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Family meeting","do":"Explain Mum's illness to Lily in child-friendly words, address her guilt, and agree visiting and Young Carers support.","sim":{"setup":"Saturday 14:30, family room off the adult ward. Mrs Sarah Bennett, 38, was admitted on Thursday under Section 2 with mania. Her daughter Lily, 10, thinks it is her fault. Mum has agreed Lily should be told. With her father present, explain.","role":{"who":"Lily Bennett, 10, Mrs Bennett's daughter (with her father, Mr Tom Bennett, 40)","open":"OK.","manner":"Quiet and drawing at first; blurts out her guilt; cries when told it is not her fault; asks lots of direct questions once she feels safe; brightens at the idea of a card.","free":["She saw the police and paramedics take Mum","Mum was talking very fast and said the TV was talking to her","She thinks it is her fault because of a fight about bedtime","Her cat is called Biscuit"],"ifasked":["She told Mum she hated her that night","She has been making her own breakfast and helping Mum since the summer","She has not slept well since Thursday","Her teacher is Mrs Shah; she does not want the whole class to know","She wants to see Mum but is scared Mum will be 'weird'","Dad: he is exhausted, juggling work and hospital visits"],"worries":["Is Mum going to die?","Is Mum in prison or in trouble with the police?","Can she catch it?","When is Mum coming home?"],"rules":["Goes quiet and keeps drawing if spoken to with long or medical words","Cries when told clearly that it is not her fault; settles when reassured that Mum asked about her","Gets upset if promised a date and later told it might change","Dad speaks only if asked or to confirm what Mum said"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Lily, I'm Dr Ade. I'm one of the doctors looking after your mum. Hello, Mr Bennett. Is it OK if I sit down here with you both?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I like your drawing. Is that your cat?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"It's Biscuit. He's ginger."},{"t":"0:23","band":"What she knows","w":"D","s":"Lily, Dad told me you've got some questions about Mum. Before I explain, what do you think has happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Mum went mad and the police took her. Because I shouted at her."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That sounds like a really scary night.","m":[7]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"She was talking really fast and she wouldn't stop. And she said the TV was talking to her."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That must have been very confusing and frightening to see.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:01","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'm going to explain what's happening. Mum has an illness in her brain. You can't see it, like a cold or a cut. It changes how she thinks and feels.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Like what?"},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Right now her thoughts are going super fast, like a car without brakes. She hasn't been able to sleep. And sometimes the illness makes her believe things that aren't true, like the TV talking to her.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Is she going to die?"},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"No. This illness doesn't make people die. Doctors know a lot about it, and most people get better with medicine and rest.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"But it's my fault. We had a fight about bedtime and I said I hated her."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Not your fault","w":"D","s":"Lily, I want you to hear this really clearly. It is not your fault. Nothing you said or did made Mum ill. Not the shouting, not the fight about bedtime. This illness happens by itself.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"But I said I hated her."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Lots of children say that when they're cross. Mum knows you love her. She asked about you this morning.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Dad: She did, Lily. She asked if you'd fed Biscuit."},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Really?"},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Really. And it's OK to feel sad, or scared, or even a bit cross with Mum. All those feelings are allowed.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","band":"The police","w":"P","s":"Why did the police come? Is she in trouble?"},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"No, she's not in trouble. She hasn't done anything wrong. When someone is very poorly and doesn't realise it, the police sometimes help get them to hospital safely. That's all they were doing.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Is she in prison here?"},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"No, this is a hospital. She has her own bedroom. There are nurses here all day and all night. She's eating, and she's starting to sleep better.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Can I catch it?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"You can't catch it, like you can't catch a broken leg. Hugging Mum or being near her is completely safe.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"When's she coming home?"},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"I don't know yet, and I don't want to make a promise I can't keep. Usually it's a few weeks. When she's sleeping and her thoughts slow down, we'll start talking about home.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Weeks?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"I know that feels like forever. It's OK to be sad about that.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","band":"Seeing Mum","w":"D","s":"But you don't have to wait weeks to talk to her. If Mum's calm tomorrow, you could speak to her on the phone, with a nurse helping.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Can I see her?"},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Yes. There's a family room just outside the ward, with sofas and games. The nurses will plan a visit with Dad, maybe early next week, when she's a bit calmer. It might be short, about half an hour.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Will she be weird?"},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"She might still talk fast, or seem sleepy from her medicine. That's the illness, not Mum changing. If it feels too much, you can stop the visit, and that's fine.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"Can I bring her a card?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"She'd love that. You could draw Biscuit.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"Mr Bennett, there's a young carers service locally. They support children whose mum or dad is unwell, with clubs and someone to talk to. Would you like me to refer Lily?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Dad: She does a lot for her mum. Yes, please."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"It might also help to tell Lily's teacher on Monday, so school can look out for her. Lily, how would you feel about that?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"Only Mrs Shah. Not the whole class."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Dad can tell just Mrs Shah.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:41","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Lily, can you tell me what you'd say to Biscuit about Mum?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Mum's brain is poorly and her thoughts are too fast. It's not my fault. I can't catch it. She's in hospital, not prison. I can phone her tomorrow and make her a card."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"That's brilliant. You've got it all. If more questions pop up later, Dad can ask the nurses, or you can ask me when you visit.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Mr Bennett, how are you doing with all this?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Dad: Honestly, I'm shattered. Work, Lily, the hospital."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's a huge amount. Our carers' lead can arrange a carer's assessment for you too. I'll ask the nurses to ring you tonight about tomorrow's call. Any questions for me?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Dad: No. Thank you. That was more than I could explain."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. Bye, Lily. Say hi to Biscuit.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets Lily by name at her level, connects with her interest, and includes Dad","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Lily what she thinks has happened before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the illness in simple, concrete words with no jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Says clearly it is not her fault, linked to the bedtime argument","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains Mum is safe and cared for, and that she cannot catch it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the police role honestly without frightening detail","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear, sadness and anger, and says all feelings are allowed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Answers questions honestly, including uncertainty about when Mum comes home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees a phone call, a planned visit in the family room and a card","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers Young Carers support and school involvement with Lily's say","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks Lily's understanding by asking her to explain it in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks on Dad, offers a carer's assessment and closes with the next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses simple words, corrects the guilt clearly, answers honestly, keeps Lily connected to Mum, and arranges Young Carers and support for Dad.","borderline":"Explains kindly but uses some jargon, or forgets to check understanding or to offer support beyond the visit.","fail":"Uses vague euphemisms, does not address her guilt, promises a date, or talks to Dad over Lily's head."}}},
{"id":"c-m-06","n":1106,"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","icon":"🕊","title":"Faith and psychosis: a family who want to stop his antipsychotic and see a faith healer","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00","where":"Early intervention in psychosis (EIP) clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in the EIP team. Daniel Mensah, 22, was admitted informally 8 weeks ago with a first episode of psychosis (hearing voices, believing neighbours were poisoning him) and went home 3 weeks ago on aripiprazole 15 mg each morning. He is improving. His mother, Mrs Grace Mensah, 51, comes with him; his father is at work. The family believe the illness is spiritual. They want Daniel to stop his tablets and attend a deliverance service led by a visiting healer next Saturday. Daniel has agreed that you can speak openly with his mother."},"task":"Meet Daniel and his mother: respect their faith, find common ground, keep Daniel safe on treatment and offer a chaplaincy or faith link.","first60":["Greet Daniel first. He is the patient and an adult; check he is happy for his mother to stay.","Ask what the family believe has happened before you give any medical view.","Find out exactly what the service involves: prayer, fasting, oils, being held, travel abroad.","Check his mental state since discharge and any early signs of relapse.","Hold the goal: he keeps taking aripiprazole while the family keep their faith practices."],"ask":["'Daniel, what do you think has been happening to you, and what do you want to do?'","'Mrs Mensah, what do you understand is causing his illness?'","'What will happen at the service? Will he be asked to fast, or go without water, or be held?'","'Is there a pastor or elder whose advice you trust?'","'Since he came home, have you noticed poor sleep, suspiciousness or him talking to himself?'","'Daniel, any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else?'","'What would help you feel we respect your faith?'"],"recognise":["A spiritual explanation shared by the family's faith community is a cultural belief, not a delusion.","Stopping an antipsychotic in the first 1 to 2 years after a first episode sharply raises relapse risk.","Daniel is an adult: if he has capacity, the decision about tablets is his, not his parents'.","Harmful practices (fasting without fluids, restraint, beating, healing abroad against his will) are a safeguarding adults concern.","Families who feel dismissed often stop medication quietly and drop out of care; respect keeps him engaged.","Side effects such as restlessness (akathisia) often drive the wish to stop; always ask."],"decide":[{"c":"Acknowledge their faith, propose prayer and medication together, check the ritual's risks, offer chaplaincy and agree relapse signs.","r":"Keeps the alliance and the treatment; the family have a role and a voice.","best":true},{"c":"Tell the family the illness is a brain disorder and that faith healing is nonsense.","r":"Dismissing belief breaks trust; families often then stop the tablets in secret.","best":false},{"c":"Agree to stop the medication to keep the family on side.","r":"Unsafe: relapse risk is high, and your duty is to give an honest view and support Daniel's own choice.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: Daniel is presumed to have capacity to decide about medication; assess it for this decision if in doubt. Parents cannot consent or refuse for an adult.","He is informal: the Mental Health Act 1983 applies only if he relapses and meets the criteria for detention; it cannot be used to enforce tablets at home.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): share with his family only with his consent (he has agreed today). You can always listen to relatives.","Equality Act 2010: religion or belief is a protected characteristic; services must respect it and adapt where reasonable.","Care Act 2014, section 42: if he is at risk of abuse or neglect from a practice and cannot protect himself because of his illness, raise a safeguarding adults concern with the local authority."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"Your faith matters to you and to Daniel, and I am not asking you to give it up. Many families pray and use medicine together. I am asking that he keeps taking his tablet while you pray for him."},{"to":"Daniel","s":"This is your decision. What do you want to do about the tablets and the service?"},{"to":"Mother (relapse risk)","s":"The tablets protect him from the illness coming back. If he stops in the first year or two, it is much more likely to return, and each return can be harder to recover from."}],"write":"EIP clinic note:\nPresent: Daniel (22) and mother; Daniel consents to sharing.\nFamily view: illness spiritual; deliverance service next Saturday, planned 3-day fast without food or water.\nDaniel's view: wants to attend and to continue aripiprazole.\nMental state: no psychotic symptoms elicited; evening restlessness (akathisia); no thoughts of harm.\nCapacity for medication decision: present.\nAgreed: aripiprazole reduced 15 mg to 10 mg mane; attend service, keep drinking and take tablet daily; chaplaincy referral.\nRelapse signs and 111 option 2 given.\nReview: care coordinator visit Monday after service; my review 4 weeks.","make":["Refer to the trust spiritual and pastoral care (chaplaincy) team; with consent, they can talk with the family's own pastor.","Care coordinator to visit within 3 days of the service; relapse signature written into the care plan.","Offer family intervention (NICE: at least 10 planned sessions over 3 months to 1 year).","Offer CBT for psychosis (NICE: at least 16 planned sessions).","Offer the parents a Carer's assessment from the local authority.","Raise a safeguarding adults concern if restraint, fasting without fluids or travel abroad against his will is planned."],"next":"Two weeks later his mother rings: Daniel stopped his tablets after the service because the healer said he was healed, and he is up at night again. Thank her for telling you, arrange an EIP review within 24 hours, discuss restarting with Daniel, and involve the chaplain. If he loses capacity and risk rises, consider a Mental Health Act assessment.","marks":["Speaks to the patient first and respects his adult autonomy.","Explores the family's beliefs with curiosity and without judgement.","Finds a shared goal and a middle way: faith and medication together.","Checks what the ritual involves and addresses physical risk.","Explains relapse risk clearly and addresses side effects.","Offers a chaplaincy or faith link.","Leaves a relapse plan, crisis number and review date."],"pitfalls":["Calling the family's beliefs delusional or backward.","Talking only to the parent and not to the patient.","Agreeing to stop medication to avoid conflict.","Not asking what the healing ritual involves.","Forgetting a written relapse plan and crisis number."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Adult psychosis and schizophrenia guideline (2014, checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: aripiprazole (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","4. Equality Act 2010 (checked Nov 2026)","5. Care Act 2014, section 42 (checked Nov 2026)","6. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Spirituality and mental health (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, asking about faith is part of a good assessment, and every trust has a chaplaincy or spiritual care team serving all faiths and none. Refer to them as you would to any other team.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Family meeting","do":"Agree with Daniel and his mother a plan that combines faith practice with continued aripiprazole and a chaplaincy link.","obj":["Heard Daniel's own view first and confirmed his consent to share with his mother","Explored the family's spiritual explanation without dismissing it, and named one shared goal","Agreed that Daniel continues aripiprazole alongside prayer and the service, with akathisia addressed","Checked what the service involves and agreed he keeps drinking fluids and takes every dose","Made a chaplaincy referral and shared three relapse signs, the crisis line and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Doctors don't believe in God. You think we're backward.\"","r":"I'm sorry if it has felt like that. Your faith is important, and I want to understand it. Can you tell me what you believe is happening to Daniel?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's not mad. This is a spiritual attack on our family.\"","r":"I can hear how strongly you feel this, and I'm not here to argue with your faith. Whatever the cause, we've both seen him suffer. Can we agree that keeping him well comes first?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If we pray hard enough, why does he need tablets?\"","r":"That's a fair question. Many families see medicine as one of the ways God helps. Prayer and tablets can go together; the tablets keep the illness quiet while you pray."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He walks up and down all night. These tablets are poisoning him.\"","r":"That must be hard to watch. Thank you for telling me; that restlessness is a known side effect and we can usually fix it by changing the dose, without stopping altogether."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"The healer is booked. We are going, whatever you say.\"","r":"I respect that you'll go, and I'm not asking you to cancel. I'm asking that he keeps taking his tablet and keeps drinking water during it. Can we agree that?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The pastor says he must fast three days. No food, no water.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. Three days without water could make him seriously ill. Could he fast from food but keep drinking? Our chaplain can talk with the pastor if that helps."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe we brought this on him. Maybe we didn't pray enough.\"","r":"That sounds painful to carry. This is not your fault, and nothing you did caused it. You came today because you love him, and that is what will help him most."}],"bps":{"bio":["Aripiprazole: reduce from 15 mg to 10 mg each morning for akathisia (range 10-30 mg); review restlessness in 2 weeks.","Aripiprazole can be taken with or without food, so fasting from food needs no change; fluids and daily dose must continue.","NICE physical monitoring: weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks and 1 year; pulse, BP, HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks."],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP psychologist.","Family intervention, at least 10 sessions over 3 months to 1 year, with Daniel and both parents, EIP family worker.","Relapse signature written with Daniel and his mother: poor sleep, suspiciousness, withdrawing to his room."],"social":["Trust chaplaincy team meets the family within 2 weeks and, with consent, speaks with their pastor.","Care coordinator offers both parents a Carer's assessment by the local authority.","EIP employment worker (Individual Placement and Support) plans his return to college or work."],"safety":["Crisis plan: EIP duty line in hours; NHS 111, option 2 for mental health, out of hours.","Care coordinator visits within 3 days after the service.","Safeguarding adults concern if restraint, fasting without fluids or travel abroad against his will is planned."]},"facts":[{"h":"Shared belief is not delusion","x":"A belief held by the person's cultural or faith group is not a delusion. If unsure, ask the family and, with consent, a faith leader from their community."},{"h":"Relapse risk if stopped early","x":"After a first episode, stopping an antipsychotic within 1 to 2 years makes relapse much more likely. NICE advises discussing this risk before any planned stop."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: watch for restlessness","x":"Start 10-15 mg once daily, usual 15 mg, maximum 30 mg. Less weight gain and prolactin rise than many. Akathisia is common early and drives stopping; ask about it."},{"h":"Chaplaincy serves every faith","x":"NHS chaplaincy teams include multi-faith and non-religious staff. They can pray with families and talk with their own faith leaders, which often eases conflict over treatment."},{"h":"Adults decide for themselves","x":"Parents cannot consent or refuse for a 22-year-old. If he has capacity, his choice stands; your job is to inform him well and keep the door open."}],"sim":{"setup":"EIP clinic, Thursday afternoon. Daniel Mensah, 22, three weeks home after a first episode of psychosis, comes with his mother. She wants him to stop aripiprazole and see a faith healer. Agree a safe plan with them.","role":{"who":"Mrs Grace Mensah, 51, Daniel's mother (Daniel, 22, is with her and answers when asked directly)","open":"Doctor, I'll be honest. We don't think this is a sickness of the mind. We want him to stop the tablets and see a healer next Saturday.","manner":"Polite but guarded, expecting to be judged. Tearful about feeling judged at church. Warms and becomes flexible once her faith is respected and a middle way is offered.","free":["The family believe the illness is a spiritual attack that began after a party","A visiting healer will hold a deliverance service next Saturday","She wants Daniel to stop his tablets","She feels judged at church for using medicine"],"ifasked":["The service requires three days of fasting with no food or water beforehand","There is prayer, anointing with oil and laying on of hands; nobody will hold him down; no plans to travel abroad","Daniel paces every evening since starting the tablets","No voices for a month; he sleeps well","Her own mother took blood pressure tablets and prayed every day","The pastor may allow water for those who are unwell","Daniel's father agrees with her but will follow the pastor's advice"],"worries":["Do you think we are backward for believing this?","If God heals him, why does he need tablets?","What if the pastor says no water?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about judgement at church; settles if validated","Becomes defensive and closed if the belief is called a delusion or nonsense","Agrees to continued tablets only after her faith is respected and a middle way is offered","Daniel speaks only when asked directly; he wants to keep the tablets but avoids contradicting his mother"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors in the team. Daniel, good to see you. Are you happy for your mum to join us today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Yeah, it's fine. She wants to talk to you anyway."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Mrs Mensah, thanks for coming in. Daniel's happy for us to talk openly. What would you like to talk about?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Doctor, I'll be honest. We don't think this is a sickness of the mind. We want him to stop the tablets and see a healer next Saturday."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Their beliefs","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest with me. That really helps. Can you tell me what you believe has been happening to him?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"It started after he went to that party. Something followed him home. The voices, the fear. Our pastor says it is a spiritual attack on our family."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"So you see it as something spiritual that came into his life. What has your church told you would help?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Prayer. Deliverance. A healer is visiting from abroad and we have a place for Daniel."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"It sounds as if your faith has carried your family through this. I'm not going to argue with your beliefs. What worries you about the tablets?","m":[3,2]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"He walks up and down all evening, he can't sit still. And people at church say we are weak to give him medicine. (voice breaks) I just want my son back."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That's so hard: watching him struggle, and feeling judged at church as well. You've done everything you can for him. I think we want the same thing: Daniel well and back to himself.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. That is all I want."},{"t":"2:03","band":"Daniel's view","w":"D","s":"Daniel, what do you think has been going on, and what would you like to do?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Daniel: I don't know. The voices were real. The tablets helped, honestly. But the pacing does my head in. And I don't want to upset Mum."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Daniel. That's really useful. The pacing sounds like a side effect called akathisia, and it can usually be fixed. When is it worst?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Evenings. I can't sit through a film."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"We could lower the dose from 15 to 10 milligrams and see if that settles it, rather than stopping. Would you try that?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Yeah, I'd try that."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Since you came home, any voices, or feeling people are against you? How's your sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Daniel: No voices for a month. I sleep okay."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Daniel: No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:14","band":"The service","w":"D","s":"Mrs Mensah, tell me about next Saturday. What will happen at the service?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Prayer, anointing with oil. They lay hands on him. And he must fast for three days before, no food or water, to prepare."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Will anyone hold him down, or is there any plan for him to travel for more healing?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"No, no. Nobody will hurt him. Just prayer."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"I'm glad to hear it. Prayer and anointing sound peaceful. My worry is three days without water. That could make him seriously ill. Could he fast from food but keep drinking?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"I would have to ask the pastor. Maybe water is allowed for the sick."},{"t":"4:07","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain my worry about stopping the tablets?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"After a first episode, if someone stops their tablets in the first year or two, the illness is much more likely to come back. And each time it comes back, it can take longer to recover.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"But if God heals him, he will not need them."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I understand. Many families we work with see medicine as one of the ways God helps. You don't have to choose. He goes to the service, you all pray, and he takes his tablet each morning. Would that sit right with your faith?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"(pause) My own mother took tablets for her blood pressure, and she prayed every day. Maybe."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Daniel, how does that sound to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Daniel: That's what I want. Go with Mum, keep the tablets."},{"t":"5:12","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"We have a chaplaincy team here who work with all faiths. Would you like them to meet you, and, if you agree, speak with your pastor about the fasting?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"A chaplain from the hospital? Yes. The pastor might listen to a man of God."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Let's also agree what to watch for. If Daniel isn't sleeping, gets suspicious, or mentions voices, call us that day. Out of hours, ring 111 and choose option 2 for mental health.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping, suspicious, voices. I'll write it down."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"His care coordinator, Sam, will visit on the Monday after the service, and I'll see you both in four weeks after the dose change.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"And if the pastor says no water?"},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"Then please ring me before Saturday, and the chaplain and I will talk with him. Daniel's safety comes first, and I think the pastor will want that too.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"6:21","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it clearly? What will you tell Daniel's dad tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"That the doctor respects our faith. Daniel prays, drinks water, takes his tablet. The chaplain will call us."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Thank you both. It took courage to tell me what you really thought.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Thanks, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, greets Daniel first and confirms he agrees to his mother joining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks the mother's understanding of the illness with open questions before giving a view","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges her faith and distress without arguing, and names a shared goal","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Daniel directly what he thinks and wants about tablets and the service","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what the service involves: fasting, fluids, restraint, travel","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks for relapse signs and thoughts of harm to self or others since discharge","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains relapse risk of stopping in the first 1 to 2 years in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about side effects and offers a dose change rather than stopping","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees prayer and medication together, with fluids and daily doses continued","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a chaplaincy referral and a link with the family's own pastor","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives relapse signs, crisis line and a dated review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises and thanks them","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respects the family's faith, hears Daniel, checks the ritual's risks, keeps medication alongside prayer, links chaplaincy and leaves a relapse plan.","borderline":"Polite and safe but lectures about medication, or misses Daniel's own view or the fasting risk.","fail":"Dismisses the beliefs as delusional, talks only to the mother, or agrees to stop the antipsychotic."}}},
{"id":"c-m-07","n":1107,"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","icon":"📣","title":"Speaking up: you think the consultant is discharging a high-risk patient","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:45","where":"Acute adult inpatient ward","text":"You are a CT2, 29, on the ward. Mr Paul Hughes, 47, was admitted informally 6 days ago after a planned overdose of 40 co-codamol tablets, found by a neighbour. Last month his wife left and he lost his job; he lives alone. On this morning's ward round he said he 'feels much better' and wants to go home. Dr Martin Fraser, 58, the consultant, agreed discharge this afternoon with GP follow-up, as the bed is needed. Afterwards the healthcare assistant tells you Mr Hughes gave his dog to his brother yesterday and said, 'I've sorted everything now.'"},"task":"Raise your concern with the consultant using a graded assertiveness approach, agree a safer plan, document it and escalate if needed.","first60":["Check the new information yourself: speak to the healthcare assistant, then briefly to Mr Hughes.","Note the facts: what was said, by whom, when.","Ask the nurse in charge to pause the discharge paperwork until you have spoken to the consultant.","Find the consultant privately and soon, before the discharge happens.","Plan your opening: the topic, the new facts, your concern and one specific request."],"ask":["To the healthcare assistant: 'What exactly did he say, and when?'","To Mr Hughes: 'You said you feel better. What has changed? What are your plans for this weekend?'","To Mr Hughes: 'Is there anything at home you could use to harm yourself?'","To the consultant: 'Have you got two minutes, somewhere private? It's about Mr Hughes's discharge.'","'Can I share something new that came up after the ward round?'","'Could we review him together before he goes?'"],"recognise":["Sudden calm after a crisis, giving away a pet and 'sorting everything' can mean a decision to die, not recovery.","Static risk: middle-aged man, recent separation and job loss, lives alone, recent planned overdose.","Bed pressure is a known driver of unsafe discharge; it does not change the patient's risk.","Hierarchy and fear of seeming rude stop juniors speaking up; many incident reviews find someone had an unvoiced concern.","The consultant may know something you don't: go in curious, not accusing."],"decide":[{"c":"Speak to the consultant now, privately, with the new facts, a clear concern and a specific request; escalate if still unsafe.","r":"Gives the consultant the chance to change the plan, protects the patient and meets your GMC duty.","best":true},{"c":"Say nothing; discharge is the consultant's decision.","r":"GMC guidance says you must act on a patient safety concern, whatever your grade.","best":false},{"c":"Tell Mr Hughes you disagree with the consultant and that he should stay.","r":"Undermines trust in the team and puts the patient in the middle of a professional disagreement.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Good medical practice (2024): you must act promptly if patient safety is at risk and raise concerns through the right channels.","GMC Raising and acting on concerns about patient safety (2012): this duty overrides personal and professional loyalties.","Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998: protects workers who raise genuine concerns in the public interest.","Every NHS trust in England has a Freedom to Speak Up Guardian you can contact in confidence.","Mr Hughes is informal: if he tries to leave while at serious risk, the lawful routes are Section 5(4) (nurse, up to 6 hours) or Section 5(2) (RC or nominated deputy, up to 72 hours), not blocking the door."],"say":[{"to":"Consultant (probe)","s":"Dr Fraser, have you got two minutes, somewhere private? Something new has come up about Mr Hughes since the ward round."},{"to":"Consultant (challenge)","s":"I'm concerned. Yesterday he gave his dog to his brother and told staff he's sorted everything. I'm not comfortable with him going today. Could we see him together first?"},{"to":"Clinical director (escalation)","s":"I've raised a concern about a discharge with Dr Fraser and we still disagree. I think the patient is at high risk of suicide. Could you advise?"}],"write":"Ward note, 12:20, Dr [name], CT2:\nNew information: HCA [name] reports Mr Hughes gave his dog to his brother yesterday and said 'I've sorted everything now'.\nMy review 11:55: denies intent; guarded about weekend plans; lives alone; wife reports painkillers at home.\nDiscussed with Dr Fraser (consultant) 12:05: new information and my concern shared.\nAgreed: discharge paused; 15-minute observations; collateral from wife and brother; joint review 14:00; HTT alerted.\nSigned, bleep.","make":["Joint consultant and CT2 review before any discharge decision.","Collateral history from his wife and brother, with his consent (or without it if needed to prevent serious harm).","If discharged later: home treatment team, written safety plan, means removed, follow-up within 72 hours.","If disagreement persists: escalate the same day to the clinical director or another senior consultant, telling the consultant first.","Debrief with your educational supervisor; use the Freedom to Speak Up Guardian if normal routes fail."],"next":"At 14:00 you and the consultant see him together. He admits he planned to die at the weekend and agrees to stay informally. That evening he asks to leave. The nurse can use Section 5(4) for up to 6 hours, and the on-call doctor, as the RC's nominated deputy, can use Section 5(2) for up to 72 hours while a Mental Health Act assessment is arranged.","marks":["Checks the facts before raising the concern.","Raises it privately, early and clearly with the consultant.","Uses graded assertiveness, stepping up when not heard.","Stays respectful and acknowledges pressures without blame.","Makes a specific request and proposes interim safety steps.","Documents the concern and outcome factually.","Knows the escalation route if still unsafe."],"pitfalls":["Hinting rather than stating the concern ('I just wondered...').","Raising it in front of the patient or the whole ward round.","Accusing the consultant of being unsafe or driven by beds.","Dropping it after the first brush-off.","Not writing down the concern and the outcome."],"refs":["1. GMC Good medical practice (2024, checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC Raising and acting on concerns about patient safety (2012, checked Nov 2026)","3. National Guardian's Office: Freedom to Speak Up (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022, checked Nov 2026)","5. National Confidential Inquiry into Suicide and Safety in Mental Health: post-discharge risk (checked Nov 2026)","6. Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS teams expect juniors to voice safety concerns to consultants. Challenging a decision politely is professional, not disrespectful. 'I'm concerned' and 'I'm not comfortable' are recognised signals to stop and listen.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Handover and escalation","do":"Raise the discharge concern with the consultant using graded assertiveness, agree a safer plan, and document it.","obj":["Checked the new information first-hand before raising it, with times and who said what","Raised the concern privately with the consultant, stating the facts, your worry and a specific request","Stepped up from a question to a clear challenge when first reassured, without accusation","Agreed a joint review before any discharge, interim observations and collateral from his wife and brother","Documented the concern, the discussion and the outcome, and named who you would escalate to"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He told me he's fine. Are you saying I got it wrong?\"","r":"Not at all. You saw him this morning and he did seem better. Something new came up afterwards that I think changes the picture. Can I tell you what it is?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"We've got three people waiting for a bed. He goes.\"","r":"I know the bed pressure is real. I'm concerned this is a safety issue, and I'm not comfortable with him going today until one of us has seen him again."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What exactly makes you think he's high risk?\"","r":"Three things: he gave his dog away yesterday, he told staff he'd sorted everything, and he lives alone with painkillers at home. With last week's overdose, that worries me."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Fine. If you want to keep him, it's on you.\"","r":"I don't want to take over, and I'd rather we decide this together. Could we see him jointly at two? I'll record what we both decide."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I just want to get home. I've made my peace with things.\"","r":"I'm glad you feel more settled. When you say you've made your peace, what do you mean? I want to understand so we can plan the next few days properly."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You said I could go. Now you're keeping me here?\"","r":"I understand why that's frustrating, and I'm sorry the plan has changed. We had some new information and want to be sure you're safe. The consultant will see you at two."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue sertraline 50 mg each morning (started on admission); on discharge, 7 days' supply at a time via weekly pharmacy dispensing.","Ask the GP to stop repeat co-codamol and review analgesia; no stockpile of paracetamol-containing medicines at home."],"psych":["Joint consultant and CT2 reassessment at 14:00: intent, plans, reasons for living, what 'sorted everything' means.","If discharged later: written safety plan with him before he leaves (warning signs, coping, people, crisis numbers, means).","For you: debrief with your educational supervisor this week."],"social":["CT2 or named nurse takes collateral from wife and brother before 13:30.","Brother keeps the dog for now and removes medicines from the flat if discharge goes ahead.","Home treatment team alerted today; daily visits for the first week if discharged."],"safety":["Nurse in charge pauses discharge; intermittent observations every 15 minutes until review.","If he tries to leave before review: nurse Section 5(4) (6 hours) or doctor Section 5(2) (72 hours) while an MHA assessment is arranged.","If still unresolved: escalate the same day to the clinical director, telling the consultant first.","Any discharge: follow-up within 72 hours."]},"facts":[{"h":"Use graded assertiveness","x":"Probe ('Can I check something?'), Alert ('I'm concerned about...'), Challenge ('I'm not comfortable...'), Emergency ('Stop, this is unsafe'). Step up each time you are not heard."},{"h":"The two-challenge rule","x":"If you voice a safety concern clearly twice and it is not addressed, you are entitled to go to the next senior person. Say it plainly both times."},{"h":"Calm can mean a decision","x":"Sudden improvement, giving away pets or possessions and 'sorting affairs' can mean a decision to die has been made. Ask directly what has changed."},{"h":"The first days home are riskiest","x":"Suicide risk is highest in the first weeks after discharge from inpatient care, peaking in the first few days. Follow-up within 72 hours is the national standard."},{"h":"Write it down the same day","x":"Record the facts, who you told, what was agreed and when. Keep it factual and neutral; no comment on the consultant's judgement."},{"h":"Speak Up Guardians are there for you","x":"Every NHS trust in England has a Freedom to Speak Up Guardian. Contact them in confidence if the usual routes have not worked or feel unsafe."}],"sim":{"setup":"Wednesday 12:00, acute ward. Dr Fraser, 58, the consultant, has agreed discharge today for Mr Hughes, 47, admitted after a planned overdose. Since the ward round you have new worrying information. Raise your concern with him.","role":{"who":"Dr Martin Fraser, 58, male consultant psychiatrist and Mr Hughes's responsible clinician","open":"Make it quick, I've a meeting at quarter past. What's up?","manner":"Busy, kind but brusque; brushes off the first hint. Briefly defensive when challenged, then admits he is stretched and engages fully once given facts, a clear concern and a plan.","free":["Mr Hughes looked brighter on the ward round and asked to go home","The bed manager needs two beds by 4pm","The GP will follow him up","Mr Hughes denied suicidal thoughts this morning"],"ifasked":["He did not ask about weekend plans in detail on the ward round","He did not know about the dog or the 'sorted everything' remark","Home treatment team have not been asked to see him","He is free at two o'clock","The nurse in charge today is Ade","He would rather be told than read about it in an incident report"],"worries":["Are you saying I've got it wrong?","We've three people waiting; what do you suggest?","What if at two I still think he's fit to go?"],"rules":["Brushes off a vague hint ('he's fine, don't worry'); responds only when the concern is stated clearly","Becomes defensive if accused of being unsafe or bed-driven; softens if the bed pressure is acknowledged","Admits he is stretched after four overnight admissions; warms if this is acknowledged","Agrees a joint review if given specific facts and a specific request"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Dr Fraser, have you got two minutes, somewhere private? It's about Mr Hughes's discharge.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Make it quick, I've a meeting at quarter past. What's up?"},{"t":"0:17","band":"The facts","w":"D","s":"After the ward round, Jo, the healthcare assistant, told me he gave his dog to his brother yesterday. And he said to her, 'I've sorted everything now.'","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"People rehome pets when they're unwell. He was bright as a button this morning. He's fine, don't worry."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"He did look brighter, I agree, and I know we're under real pressure for beds. But I'm concerned. I went and saw him myself just now.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"And?"},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"He denied any plans, but he was guarded about the weekend. His wife told the nurses there are painkillers at home. He lives alone, he's lost his job and his marriage, and he took forty co-codamol a week ago.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"We've been through all that. Three people are waiting for beds. The GP will see him."},{"t":"1:32","band":"Challenge","w":"D","s":"I understand. But I'm not comfortable with him going home today. I think the calm, the dog and 'sorted everything' could mean he's made a decision, not that he's better.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"(pause) Are you saying I've got it wrong?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"No. You saw him this morning, and you've far more experience than me. This came up after the ward round, so you didn't have it. I'd just feel much safer if we looked again.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Sorry. It's been a hell of a week. Four admissions overnight and the bed manager on my back."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"That sounds relentless. Thank you for hearing me out when you're this stretched.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"So what do you want to do?"},{"t":"2:40","band":"The request","w":"D","s":"Could we see him together before he goes? I'll fit around you.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"I'm in the meeting till half one. Two o'clock?"},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Two works. Meanwhile, can I ask the nurse in charge to pause the discharge paperwork, and put him on fifteen-minute checks until we see him?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Yes, fine. Tell Ade."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"I'll also ring his wife and brother before two, with his agreement, to hear how he's been and what's at home.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"Good idea. What if he wants to go before then?"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"He's informal. If he tries to leave and we're worried, the nurse can use Section 5(4) for up to six hours, and you or I, as your nominated deputy, can use 5(2) while an assessment is arranged.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"Hopefully it won't come to that."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"And if we do decide he can go, I'd like home treatment to see him first, a written safety plan, his brother to clear the tablets, and follow-up within 72 hours.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"That's sensible. You've thought about it."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Recording it","w":"D","s":"I'll write a note now: what Jo told me, my review, our discussion and the plan for two o'clock. Facts only.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Fine. Do you need me to sign anything?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"No. I'll record that we discussed it and agreed the plan together. You can add your own review at two.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"And if at two I still think he's fit to go?"},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Then I'd respect that, as long as we've both seen him with the new information and a safety plan is in place. If I was still very worried, I'd want to talk it through with Dr Shah as clinical director, and I'd tell you first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"(pause) Fair enough. That's what you should do. I'd rather be told than read it in a report."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Honestly, I wasn't sure how to bring it up.","m":[3]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"You did it well. Next time, grab me straight away, even mid ward round."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"I will. I'll speak up as soon as something comes up.","m":[1]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"Right. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just to recap. I'll ask Ade to pause discharge and start fifteen-minute checks now. I'll call his wife and brother by half one. We see him together at two. I'll document it all.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"And home treatment?"},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"I'll give them a heads-up now, so they can see him today if we do discharge him. Is that everything?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"That's everything. Go."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Dr Fraser. See you at two.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"See you at two."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks for a private moment and states the topic clearly at the start","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the new facts concisely: what was said, by whom, when","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's view, experience and bed pressure without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names the concern explicitly, for example 'I'm concerned'","d":"Content"},{"c":"Steps up to a clear challenge ('I'm not comfortable') after the first brush-off","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the facts to suicide risk: recent attempt, losses, lives alone, means at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Makes one specific request: a joint review before discharge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Proposes interim safety steps: pause discharge, observations, collateral, legal holding powers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Proposes a safer discharge if it goes ahead: HTT, safety plan, means, 72-hour follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States they will document the facts, discussion and outcome factually","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names the next escalation step respectfully, telling the consultant first","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises actions with owners and times, and thanks the consultant","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the new facts and a clear concern, steps up calmly after a brush-off, secures a joint review with interim safety steps, documents it and knows the escalation route.","borderline":"Raises the concern but hints, accepts the first reassurance, or agrees a review without interim safety steps.","fail":"Says nothing, accuses the consultant, involves the patient in the disagreement, or does not document."}}},
{"id":"c-m-08","n":1108,"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","icon":"✋","title":"Saying no safely: a man demanding diazepam who threatens a complaint","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:20","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT1 in a CMHT clinic. Mr Lee Barker, 38, was referred by his GP with generalised anxiety disorder. In August an out-of-hours GP gave him 5 days of diazepam 2 mg three times a day. Since then he has been buying 'blues' online. Today he says only diazepam works and wants a month's supply. When you hesitate, he stands, raises his voice and says he will complain and 'go to the papers'. The clinic nurse is in the next room."},"task":"Say no to diazepam clearly and kindly, offer real alternatives, keep yourself and him safe, and respond to the complaint threat.","first60":["Stay calm; keep your voice low and slow. Know where the door and the alarm are.","Name his distress and frustration before saying anything about diazepam.","Ask about the 'blues': how many, how long, from where, last dose.","Screen for withdrawal and other substances: shakes, sweats, fits, alcohol, opioids, pregabalin.","Give one clear no with one reason, then move straight to what you can offer."],"ask":["'Tell me what the anxiety is like at its worst.'","'How many tablets have you been taking a day, and when was the last one?'","'What happens when you run out? Shakes, sweats, ever a fit?'","'Do you drink, or take anything else, like painkillers or pregabalin?'","'What have you tried before: talking therapy, other tablets?'","'When it's this bad, do you ever feel it's not worth going on?'"],"recognise":["Street 'diazepam' or 'blues' often contain other benzodiazepines (such as bromazolam or etizolam) of unknown strength, linked to UK drug deaths.","Daily use for a few weeks can cause dependence; sudden stopping risks rebound anxiety and seizures.","NICE does not recommend benzodiazepines for generalised anxiety disorder except short term in a crisis.","Standing, a raised voice and threats are early warning signs: de-escalate, and end the meeting if it escalates.","A complaint threat is not a reason to prescribe. He has a right to complain; you have a duty to prescribe safely."],"decide":[{"c":"Name his distress, say no clearly with a reason, offer sertraline, talking therapy and a drug service referral, and give PALS details calmly.","r":"Safe prescribing, a real plan and respect for his rights.","best":true},{"c":"Prescribe a week of diazepam to calm things down.","r":"Rewards escalation, deepens dependence on top of an unknown street supply, and is not good prescribing practice.","best":false},{"c":"Refuse and end the appointment as soon as he mentions a complaint.","r":"Leaves him with no plan and with withdrawal and overdose risk; end early only if safety requires it.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Good practice in prescribing and managing medicines and devices (2021): prescribe only when it serves the patient's needs; you need not prescribe what you believe is not in their interests.","Diazepam is a Schedule 4 (Part 1) controlled drug; prescriptions are valid for 28 days.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): a patient's complaint must not affect the care you give them.","Local Authority Social Services and NHS Complaints (England) Regulations 2009: complaints are normally made within 12 months; PALS can help resolve concerns informally.","Trust violence and lone-working policies allow you to end an appointment if you feel unsafe; report incidents."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how awful this anxiety is, and I want to help. I won't prescribe diazepam today, because in the long run it makes things worse. What I can do is start a treatment that works over weeks and get you help with the tablets you're buying."},{"to":"Patient (complaint)","s":"You have every right to complain, and I'll give you the details. It won't change how I look after you. I'd still like us to agree a plan today."},{"to":"Patient (escalation)","s":"I want to keep talking, but I need us both to sit down. If we can't, I'll end the appointment and we'll rebook."}],"write":"CMHT clinic note:\nRequest: diazepam, 1 month; declined. Reasons explained: dependence, NICE GAD guidance, street benzodiazepine use.\nStreet 'blues' 3-4/day for 8 weeks; last this morning; shakes and sweats when missed; no fits. Alcohol: 4 cans most nights.\nRisk: passive thoughts, no plan or intent. Stood and raised voice briefly; settled with de-escalation; no threats to harm.\nPlan: sertraline 25 mg mane for 7 days then 50 mg; NHS Talking Therapies self-referral; drug and alcohol service referral; harm reduction advice; crisis numbers.\nPALS details given. Consultant informed. Review 2 weeks.","make":["Start sertraline 25 mg each morning for 7 days, then 50 mg; review in 2 weeks.","Self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT or applied relaxation.","Refer to the local drug and alcohol service to assess benzodiazepine dependence and agree a safe reduction.","Letter to the GP: the declined request, the plan, and a recommendation not to prescribe benzodiazepines.","Give PALS details; tell your consultant today; complete an incident report if there was aggression."],"next":"Two days later the complaints team forwards a letter from Mr Barker saying you refused to help him. Do not reply to him directly. Give the complaints team a factual account from your notes, tell your consultant and educational supervisor, and reflect on it in your portfolio. Keep his follow-up as planned: the complaint does not change his care.","marks":["Validates distress before declining.","Gives one clear, kind no with a reason, and repeats it without arguing.","Explores street benzodiazepines, withdrawal, alcohol and suicide risk.","Offers concrete alternatives with doses and referrals.","Stays calm and safe when he escalates.","Responds to the complaint threat without defensiveness.","Gives harm reduction advice and a review date."],"pitfalls":["Giving 'just a few' to end the conflict.","Saying no before acknowledging distress.","Arguing back or matching his volume.","Not asking about street benzodiazepines, alcohol or withdrawal.","Discouraging a complaint or sounding defensive."],"refs":["1. NICE CG113 GAD and panic guideline (2011, checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: diazepam; sertraline; benzodiazepine withdrawal (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC Good practice in prescribing and managing medicines and devices (2021, checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Good medical practice (2024, checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE NG10 Violence and aggression guideline (2015, checked Nov 2026)","6. NHS England: Feedback and complaints, PALS (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients are routinely told how to complain, and a complaint is a normal process, not a verdict on you. Declining an unsafe prescription is good practice; record your reasons and tell your consultant.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Professional and ethical dilemma + Explain a treatment","do":"Decline diazepam with a clear reason, offer two concrete alternatives, and keep the consultation calm and safe.","obj":["Acknowledged his distress before giving one clear, kind no to diazepam with the reason","Found out his street benzodiazepine use, alcohol use, withdrawal symptoms and suicide risk","Offered sertraline with dose and side effects, NHS Talking Therapies and a drug service referral","Stayed calm when he stood up, set a limit on behaviour and kept safety in mind","Gave PALS details without defensiveness and confirmed his care would not change"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot never listen. Diazepam is the only thing that works!\"","r":"I can hear how desperate you are, and I am listening. Diazepam works fast, which is why it feels like the only thing. Let me explain why I won't prescribe it, and what I can offer."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'll put in a complaint. I'll go to the papers.\"","r":"That's your right, and I'll give you the PALS details. It won't change how I treat you. Right now, I'd like us to agree something that helps you."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me a week's worth. Then I'll do your therapy.\"","r":"I understand wanting something to tide you over. With what you're already taking, even a week adds to the dependence. Let's start sertraline today and get the drug service involved this week."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My heart pounds all day. I can't live like this.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting and frightening, and you've coped alone for months. When you say you can't live like this, have you had thoughts about ending your life?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's the difference? Blues are the same as diazepam.\"","r":"Good question. Many tablets sold as diazepam are something stronger, and there's no way to know the dose. People have died from them, especially with alcohol. That's why I want the drug service involved."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking antidepressants. They're for depressed people.\"","r":"Lots of people think that. Sertraline is one of the main treatments for anxiety too. It takes a few weeks and doesn't cause dependence. Would you be willing to try it?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If you won't give me any, I'll just buy more.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest; I'd rather know. If you do, don't take more than now, don't mix them with alcohol and don't use alone. The drug service can help you cut down safely."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 25 mg each morning for 7 days, then 50 mg (usual 50-200 mg; off-label for GAD, as NICE recommends). Warn: nausea, jitteriness for 1-2 weeks, sexual side effects.","No benzodiazepine prescription; avoid pregabalin (Schedule 3 controlled drug with misuse risk).","Drug service assesses dependence; if indicated, they lead a supervised, gradual diazepam reduction.","AUDIT-C at this visit; alcohol plus benzodiazepines raises overdose risk."],"psych":["NHS Talking Therapies: self-refer online today for CBT for GAD or applied relaxation.","Brief explanation of the anxiety cycle and avoidance; written NHS self-help information."],"social":["Drug and alcohol service key worker: harm reduction and support to reduce.","GP letter today: declined request, plan, recommendation of no benzodiazepine prescribing; copy to patient."],"safety":["Sit nearest the door, alarm on; clinic nurse aware; end the appointment if threats escalate.","Harm reduction: no increase, no alcohol or opioids with them, never use alone; 999 for a fit.","Crisis: NHS 111 option 2; Samaritans 116 123.","Tell consultant today; incident report if aggression; review in 2 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"NICE: no benzos for GAD","x":"Do not offer benzodiazepines for generalised anxiety disorder except short term during a crisis. Offer CBT or applied relaxation, or an SSRI: sertraline first (off-label for GAD)."},{"h":"Street blues are not diazepam","x":"Many 'diazepam' tablets bought online or on the street contain stronger benzodiazepines such as bromazolam or etizolam. Strength is unpredictable; deaths are linked to mixing with opioids or alcohol."},{"h":"Dependence within weeks","x":"The BNF limits benzodiazepines to 2-4 weeks for severe anxiety. Longer daily use causes dependence; stopping suddenly can cause rebound anxiety, insomnia and seizures."},{"h":"Validate, boundary, offer","x":"Name the feeling, give one clear no with one reason, then move to what you can do. If pushed, repeat the same calm no; don't add new reasons or argue."},{"h":"A complaint does not change care","x":"Give PALS details readily. GMC guidance says a complaint must not affect the care you provide. Tell your consultant and keep a factual record."},{"h":"SSRIs can worsen anxiety early","x":"Warn that jitteriness can rise in the first 1-2 weeks. Start low (sertraline 25 mg), review at 2 weeks, and increase gradually."}],"sim":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Mr Lee Barker, 38, with generalised anxiety, has been buying 'blues' online and wants a month of diazepam. The clinic nurse is next door. Respond to his request and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Lee Barker, 38, male patient with generalised anxiety disorder","open":"Look, I'll save us both time. Diazepam is the only thing that works. I need a month's worth.","manner":"Tense and restless; stands and shouts at the refusal and threatens a complaint. Sits and calms when his distress is named and a limit is set calmly; tearful about his child; then engages with the plan.","free":["Anxiety for years, much worse since redundancy in the spring","An out-of-hours GP gave him diazepam in August and it worked","He has been buying 'blues' online","His heart pounds and some days he can't leave the house"],"ifasked":["Takes 3 or 4 blues a day for about 8 weeks; last one this morning","Gets shaky and sweaty if he misses a day; no fits","Drinks 4 cans of lager most evenings; no other drugs","Never had talking therapy; tried citalopram 2 years ago for a week and stopped because of nausea","Fleeting thoughts of 'what's the point'; no plans or intent; his child keeps him going","Wants to be able to take his child to school"],"worries":["Why should I suffer when there's a tablet that works?","Antidepressants are for depressed people","Am I an addict now?"],"rules":["Stands and raises his voice at the first refusal; sits if the doctor stays calm, names his anger and asks him to sit","If the doctor argues or lectures, heads for the door threatening a complaint","Becomes tearful about not taking his child to school; settles if validated","Agrees to sertraline only after dependence is explained without judgement"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Barker. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors here. Your GP asked us to see you about anxiety. Where would you like to start?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Look, I'll save us both time. Diazepam is the only thing that works. I need a month's worth."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Okay. Before we get to that, tell me what the anxiety is like at the moment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"My heart pounds all day. Some days I can't get to the shop. The diazepam the out-of-hours doctor gave me in August was the first time I felt normal in years."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Living with that every day, then finding something that made you feel normal: I can see why you want it back.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"So you'll give it me?"},{"t":"1:05","band":"The no","w":"D","s":"I won't prescribe diazepam today. I know that's not what you want to hear. In the long run it makes anxiety worse and it's hard to come off. But I do want to help, and there are things I can do.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"(stands, louder) Unbelievable. You lot never listen! I'll put in a complaint. I'll go to the papers!"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"(calm, seated) I can hear how angry and let down you feel. I do want to listen. Would you sit back down with me so we can sort out a plan?","m":[4,2]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"(pause, sits) Go on then."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And you have every right to complain. I'll give you the PALS details before you go. It won't change how I look after you.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Right. Fine."},{"t":"2:11","band":"The blues","w":"D","s":"Your GP mentioned you've been getting tablets online. How many a day, and since when?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Three, four a day. Blues. About two months. Last one this morning."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"What happens if you miss a day? Any shakes, sweats, or ever a fit?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Shaky and sweaty. Never had a fit."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"And alcohol, or anything else, like painkillers or pregabalin?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Four cans most nights. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Many tablets sold online as diazepam are actually stronger drugs, and you can't tell the dose. With alcohol on top, people have stopped breathing.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"They're from the same seller every time."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Even so, the batches change. And the shakes when you miss a day suggest your body is already used to them. That's dependence, and more diazepam from me would deepen it.","m":[8,3]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"(quieter) So I'm an addict now?"},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"No. It's what happens to anyone's body after weeks of daily use. You haven't done anything wrong; you were trying to cope. It just means we help you come off safely.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I just want to be able to take my kid to school."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That really matters, and we'll work towards it. Can I ask: when things are this bad, do you ever feel it's not worth going on?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think, what's the point. I'd never do anything. My kid."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made any plans, or come close to acting on it?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"4:27","band":"What I can offer","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'd suggest. First, a tablet called sertraline. It's one of the main treatments for anxiety, and it doesn't cause dependence.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Antidepressants? I'm not depressed. And citalopram made me sick."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that way. It's used for anxiety too. We'd start low, 25 milligrams each morning, then 50 after a week. You might feel jittery or sick for the first week or two. It takes about four weeks to really help.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Four weeks. And in the meantime?"},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Two things. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies today for CBT. And I'll refer you to the drug and alcohol service this week, so they can help you cut down the blues safely.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Will they just tell me to stop?"},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"No. Stopping suddenly could be risky. They'll assess you and agree a safe plan, sometimes a prescribed reducing dose that they supervise.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's more than I thought."},{"t":"5:49","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Until then: don't take more than you do now, don't mix them with drink, and don't use alone. If you have a fit, call 999. If things feel desperate, ring 111 and choose option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've been clear. What's the plan?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Start sertraline, sign up for therapy, you refer me to the drug people. No diazepam."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly. No diazepam, but a real plan. I'll see you in two weeks to see how the sertraline's going, and I'll write to your GP. Here's the PALS leaflet too.","m":[12,3,11]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"Sorry for shouting."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying that. You've been under a lot of strain. See you in two weeks.","m":[2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, opens with an open question and hears his request without interrupting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and validates his distress before and after refusing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives one clear no to diazepam with a simple reason, and holds it calmly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Stays calm and seated when he stands, does not argue, and invites him to sit","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about street benzodiazepine use: amount, duration, last dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about withdrawal symptoms, fits, alcohol and other drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains street 'blues' and dependence risk in plain, non-judgemental words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers sertraline with start dose, early side effects and time to work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers NHS Talking Therapies and a drug and alcohol service referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to the complaint threat with PALS details, without defensiveness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives harm reduction and crisis advice, checks understanding and sets a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Validates, says one clear no with a reason, explores street use and risk, offers sertraline, therapy and drug service, and handles the complaint calmly.","borderline":"Safe refusal but little validation or exploration, or a vague plan without doses or referrals.","fail":"Prescribes to end the conflict, argues or matches his anger, misses street use or suicide risk, or discourages a complaint."}}},
{"id":"c-m-09","n":1109,"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","icon":"⚖","title":"His tribunal appeal has failed: the news, his rights and the risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:10","where":"Acute adult inpatient ward","text":"You are the CT1 on the ward. Mr Jordan Ellis, 34, has paranoid schizophrenia and has been detained under Section 3 for 10 weeks after stopping olanzapine and threatening a neighbour. The First-tier Tribunal heard his application on Wednesday and reserved its decision. The written decision arrived this afternoon: he stays detained. His solicitor is in court and will call on Monday. He keeps asking, and the nurse in charge asks you to tell him. Yesterday he told a nurse, 'If I lose, there's no point any more.'"},"task":"Tell him the tribunal decision honestly and kindly, explain his rights and options, respond to his anger and hopelessness, and assess and manage the risk.","first60":["Read the decision yourself first so you can answer questions.","Choose a quiet room; ask a nurse he trusts to join you; know the exits.","Check the risk history: self-harm, aggression, absconding, and yesterday's remark.","Warning shot, then the decision in one clear sentence.","Allow silence; respond to feelings before explaining rights."],"ask":["'What has your solicitor told you to expect?'","'How are you feeling, hearing this?'","'You told a nurse there'd be no point if you lost. What did you mean?'","'Are you having thoughts about ending your life? Any plans, or anything you've done?'","'Any thoughts of hurting anyone, or of leaving the ward without permission?'","'Who would you like to talk to tonight?'"],"recognise":["Hopelessness after a lost appeal can raise suicide risk in detained patients.","Anger is a normal response; separate it from threats or plans to harm others.","Absconding risk rises after bad news, especially before a weekend.","He may blame the team; an honest, consistent, non-defensive response keeps trust.","The tribunal decides independently, but the RC can still grant leave or discharge at any time."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell him today in private with a nurse present, respond to his feelings, explain his routes, assess risk and set a weekend plan.","r":"Honest, timely and safe; leaves him with options and some hope.","best":true},{"c":"Wait until Monday so his solicitor can tell him.","r":"He is asking now; delay erodes trust and leaves hopelessness unassessed over a weekend.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure him he'll be out soon anyway.","r":"False hope; trust collapses when it does not happen.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Health Act 1983, section 66: a Section 3 patient may apply to the First-tier Tribunal once in the first 6 months, once in the next 6 months, then once a year.","Hospital Managers can discharge him (section 23); he can ask for a Managers' hearing at any time.","Nearest relative: may order discharge with 72 hours' written notice; the RC can bar it if he would be likely to act dangerously (section 25); she can then apply to the tribunal within 28 days.","Challenging the decision itself is only on a point of law: his solicitor asks the First-tier Tribunal for permission to appeal to the Upper Tribunal, usually within 28 days of the written reasons.","Section 130A: right to an Independent Mental Health Advocate. Section 132: rights explained orally and in writing; the Code of Practice says repeat them at key moments such as this.","The RC can grant Section 17 leave or discharge him at any time if the criteria are no longer met."],"say":[{"to":"Patient (warning shot)","s":"Jordan, I've got the tribunal's decision. I'm afraid it isn't the news you were hoping for."},{"to":"Patient (the news)","s":"The tribunal has decided you should stay on Section 3 for now. I'm sorry."},{"to":"Patient (rights)","s":"This isn't the end of the road. You can ask the Hospital Managers to review your section at any time, you can apply to the tribunal again when your section is renewed, and an advocate can help you with both."}],"write":"Ward note, Fri 16:45, Dr [name], CT1, with RN Sam [surname]:\nInformed Mr Ellis of tribunal decision (detention upheld); written decision seen.\nResponse: angry, then tearful.\nRisk: thought of ligature last night if he lost; no act or preparation; no current plan or intent; protective: mother, cat. Thoughts of walking off leave; no thoughts of harm to others.\nRights re-explained (s132) with leaflet; Managers' hearing request supported; IMHA referral; solicitor to call Monday.\nPlan: 15-minute observations; ground leave paused pending review tomorrow; RC informed; weekend on-call handover.","make":["Re-explain Section 132 rights with the written leaflet; record that you did.","Help him request a Hospital Managers' hearing in writing today if he wants one; send it to the MHA office.","Refer to the IMHA service; ask for a visit on Monday.","Tell the RC today; ask for a Monday review setting out clear steps to leave and discharge.","Hand over to the weekend on-call doctor: risk, observation level, leave status and what he said.","Offer a phone call to his mother tonight."],"next":"On Monday he asks the RC, 'What do I actually have to do to get off this section?' The RC, you and his IMHA agree specific steps: taking olanzapine without prompting, building unescorted leave from 30 minutes, and a CPA meeting to plan discharge.","marks":["Tells the news promptly and honestly, with a warning shot.","Responds to anger and grief before giving information.","Explains his rights accurately: Managers' hearing, next tribunal, nearest relative, IMHA.","Assesses suicide, violence and absconding risk after the news.","Agrees a weekend plan with observations, leave review and support.","Documents and hands over clearly."],"pitfalls":["Giving the news in a corridor or in front of other patients.","Burying the result in legal detail.","False reassurance about when he will be discharged.","Missing the 'no point' remark and not asking about suicide.","Wrong information about when he can apply again."],"refs":["1. Mental Health Act 1983, sections 17, 23, 25, 66, 130A, 132 (checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015, checked Nov 2026)","3. First-tier Tribunal (Mental Health): HM Courts and Tribunals Service guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. Tribunal Procedure (First-tier Tribunal) (Health, Education and Social Care Chamber) Rules 2008 (checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE NG225 Self-harm (2022, checked Nov 2026)","6. NICE NG10 Violence and aggression (2015, checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England, detained patients have several ways to challenge detention. Get the routes and timings right: patients and advocates will check, and wrong information damages trust.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Breaking bad news + Mental Health Act process","do":"Tell him the tribunal outcome clearly, explain three routes to challenge detention, and assess suicide and absconding risk.","obj":["Told him the decision early, after a warning shot, in a private room with a nurse present","Allowed silence and named his anger and disappointment before giving information","Explained three routes accurately: Managers' hearing now, next tribunal on renewal, and IMHA support","Asked directly about suicide, harm to others and leaving the ward, and agreed a weekend plan","Documented the conversation and handed over risk to the weekend on-call doctor"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"It's a fix. You lot told them what to say!\"","r":"I can see how angry you are, and how unfair this feels. The tribunal is independent and makes its own decision. I know that doesn't make it easier. Can I tell you what you can do next?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"There's no point. I'm never getting out of here.\"","r":"It sounds like you feel trapped and hopeless right now. That's a heavy feeling. When you say there's no point, are you having thoughts about ending your life?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When can I appeal again?\"","r":"You can ask the Hospital Managers to review your section at any time, starting today. You can apply to the tribunal again once your section is renewed. An advocate can help with both."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My solicitor said I'd win.\"","r":"That must make this even harder to take in. She'll call you on Monday to go through the written reasons and whether there are grounds to challenge the decision."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I take the tablets, will you let me go next week?\"","r":"I can't promise a date, and I don't want to give you false hope. I'll ask your consultant to set out clearly what needs to happen for more leave and discharge, and go through it with you on Monday."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying in this room. I'm going out.\"","r":"You need some space, and that's fair. I need you to stay on the ward tonight. Could you take some time in the garden with Sam, and we talk again in half an hour?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Last night I thought about tying something round my neck.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. I want to keep you safe tonight. Did you do anything, or get anything ready? How strong is that thought now?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue olanzapine 15 mg at night; ask about sedation and weight gain, common reasons for wanting off the section.","If very distressed tonight, offer oral PRN as charted (for example promethazine 25 mg), with his agreement."],"psych":["Named nurse spends 1:1 time this evening and checks in again before the night shift.","RC reviews him on Monday: explains the tribunal's reasons and sets clear steps to leave and discharge.","Ward psychologist offered to work on hopelessness and recovery goals next week."],"social":["IMHA referral today; visit Monday.","Help him write a request for a Hospital Managers' hearing; MHA office logs it.","Phone call to his mother tonight if he wants; IMHA explains her nearest relative rights."],"safety":["Intermittent observations every 15 minutes overnight, agreed with the nurse in charge.","Ground leave paused this weekend until the nurse in charge and on-call doctor review it with him tomorrow; reasons recorded.","Remove obvious ligature items from his room per ward policy, explaining why.","RC told today; handover to weekend on-call doctor."]},"facts":[{"h":"When he can apply again","x":"Section 3: one tribunal application in the first 6 months, one in the next 6 months, then one a year. Losing this one uses up the chance for the current period."},{"h":"Managers' hearing: any time","x":"He can ask the Hospital Managers to review his detention at any time, in writing or by telling staff. A panel can discharge him, and asking does not use up his tribunal right."},{"h":"Appeal only on a point of law","x":"To challenge the decision itself, his solicitor asks the First-tier Tribunal for permission to appeal to the Upper Tribunal, usually within 28 days of the written reasons. Disagreement alone is not enough."},{"h":"The RC can still discharge him","x":"The tribunal decision does not tie the RC's hands. The RC can grant Section 17 leave or discharge him whenever the criteria are no longer met."},{"h":"The nearest relative has a power","x":"She can order discharge with 72 hours' written notice. The RC can bar it if he would be likely to act dangerously; she can then apply to the tribunal within 28 days."},{"h":"Bad news raises risk","x":"Hopelessness after a lost appeal can raise suicide and absconding risk. Ask directly, review observations and leave, and hand over before the weekend."}],"sim":{"setup":"Friday 16:10, acute ward. Mr Jordan Ellis, 34, detained under Section 3, has waited since Wednesday for his tribunal decision. It arrived today: he stays detained. His solicitor calls on Monday. Tell him, explain his options and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Ellis, 34, male patient detained under Section 3 (his named nurse, Sam, is present)","open":"Is it the tribunal? Just tell me. Am I going home?","manner":"Hopeful and tense, then shouts and stands at the news, then flat and tearful. Calms when his feelings are named; engages when he hears concrete options.","free":["He has waited since Wednesday","His solicitor told him he had a good chance","He feels well and wants to go home to his flat","He thinks the team 'told the tribunal what to say'"],"ifasked":["Last night he thought about tying something round his neck if he lost; did nothing and got nothing ready","No current plan or intent; his mother and his cat stop him","He has thought about walking off next time he is on ground leave","No thoughts of harming anyone, including the neighbour","His mother is feeding his cat and is his nearest relative","He takes olanzapine but hates the weight gain"],"worries":["When can I appeal again?","Is this going to go on forever?","So I'm locked in now as well?"],"rules":["Shouts and stands at the news; sits if the doctor stays calm and names his anger","If given legal detail before his feelings are acknowledged, says 'You're not listening'","Discloses last night's ligature thought only if asked directly about suicide","Agrees to stay on the ward and spend time with a nurse if offered concrete options and some control"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Jordan, it's Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. Sam's here with us. Can we sit in the quiet room for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Is it the tribunal? Just tell me. Am I going home?"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"It's about the tribunal. Can I check first what your solicitor told you to expect?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"She said I had a good chance. I've done everything they asked."},{"t":"0:35","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"Jordan, I'm afraid it isn't the news you were hoping for.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"The tribunal has decided you should stay on Section 3 for now. I'm sorry.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"(stands, shouting) That's a joke! You lot told them what to say! I've done everything!"},{"t":"1:02","band":"His reaction","w":"D","s":"(pause) I can see how angry you are. You've waited since Wednesday, and you've worked hard. This feels really unfair.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"It is unfair. Ten weeks. I'm not ill."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"I hear you. Would you sit with us? I want to hear how you're feeling, and then tell you what you can do next.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"(sits, quieter) What's the point."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"The tribunal is independent of the ward. They read the reports, heard from you, and made their own decision. I know that doesn't make it easier.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"My solicitor didn't even ring."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"She's in court today. She'll call you on Monday to go through their written reasons with you.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"And then what? Another six months stuck here?"},{"t":"2:07","band":"Your options","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. There are things you can do. First, you can ask the Hospital Managers to review your section. That's a separate panel, and you can ask today. They can discharge you.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Today?"},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Yes. I can help you write the request now, and Sam will pass it to the Mental Health Act office. An advocate, called an IMHA, can help you prepare. I'll ask them to see you on Monday.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"And the tribunal? Can I go again?"},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"You can apply again once your section is renewed, in about four months. And if your solicitor thinks the tribunal made a legal mistake, she can ask to appeal. She'll advise you on that.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Four months. Great."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Your consultant can also give you more leave, or discharge you, at any time if things keep going well. I'll ask Dr Clarke to set out on Monday exactly what needs to happen.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"(quietly, tearful) I just want to go home. My mum's feeding my cat. I miss my own bed."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. Ten weeks away from home is hard. It's okay to feel gutted about this.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"I told the nurses if I lost there'd be no point any more."},{"t":"3:54","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've said that. When you say no point, are you having thoughts about ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Last night I thought about tying something round my neck. If I lost."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. Did you do anything, or get anything ready?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"No. Just thought about it."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"And now, after the news? Any plan or intention to act on it?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"No. My mum would be in bits. And who'd have the cat."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Your mum and your cat matter a lot. Any thoughts of hurting anyone else, or of leaving the ward without permission?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"I did think about just walking off next time I'm on ground leave. But no. I'm not hurting anyone."},{"t":"5:03","band":"The weekend","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Here's what I suggest for this weekend. Sam and the night nurses will check on you every fifteen minutes and spend time with you. We'll pause ground leave and review it with you tomorrow. If the thoughts come back, tell a nurse straight away.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"So I'm locked in now as well."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels like that. It's for now, while things are raw, and we'll look at it again tomorrow. Would you like to ring your mum tonight?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She'll want to know."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Sam can help with that. Your mum is your nearest relative and she has rights of her own too. The advocate can explain those on Monday.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you'll take from this? What happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"I can ask the managers for a hearing, there's an advocate Monday, my solicitor rings Monday, and I can go to the tribunal again after the renewal."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll write it down for you with your rights leaflet. And I'm handing over to the weekend doctor so they know how you're feeling.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Thanks for telling me straight."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for talking with me, Jordan. I know this is hard. Sam will stay with you for a while.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and the nurse, and moves to a private room","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks what he knows and expects before giving the news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then the decision clearly in one sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Allows silence and names his anger and disappointment without defending the team","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the tribunal is independent, without blaming it or him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains a Hospital Managers' hearing can be requested at any time, and offers help","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains reapplication on renewal and appeal on a point of law via his solicitor","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers IMHA support and contact with solicitor and mother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, acts, plans and intent, including last night","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to others and about leaving the ward","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a weekend plan: observations, leave review, nurse support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives realistic hope: the RC can grant leave or discharge, with clear steps on Monday","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for tonight and closes kindly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gives the news early and clearly, sits with his anger, explains Managers' hearing, next tribunal and IMHA accurately, and assesses and manages suicide and absconding risk.","borderline":"Accurate news and rights but rushes past his feelings or does not ask directly about suicide.","fail":"Delays or hides the news, gives wrong appeal information, offers false hope, or misses the 'no point' risk."}}},
{"id":"c-m-10","n":1110,"prog":"Communication: conversations in every specialty","icon":"📞","title":"Urgent phone referral: a reluctant GP and possible lithium toxicity at home","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 13:30","where":"Community mental health team office","text":"You are the CT2 in a CMHT. Mrs Margaret Doyle, 72, has bipolar disorder and takes lithium carbonate (Priadel) 600 mg at night; her level 3 months ago was 0.7 mmol/L. She is housebound after a hip fracture. Her CPN rings from her home: for 3 days she has had diarrhoea and vomiting, a coarse tremor and unsteadiness, and she has been taking ibuprofen from the chemist for hip pain. She is alert, oriented and drinking a little. She refuses A&E, and the CPN thinks she has capacity for that decision, but she agrees to a blood test at home. You ring her GP, Dr Rachel Moss, 45, who says, 'Can't your team deal with it? We've no visits left.'"},"task":"Make an urgent SBAR referral to the GP, with a clear request, agree who does what, and document it.","first60":["Get the facts from the CPN: symptoms, duration, fluids, confusion, falls, last lithium dose time, other medicines.","Ask the CPN to tell her not to take tonight's lithium and to stop the ibuprofen.","Check the CPN's view of her capacity to refuse A&E, and that she knows the risks.","Write your SBAR in four lines before you dial.","Decide your one request: a visit today, by a set time, with an urgent lithium level and U&E."],"ask":["To the CPN: 'Is she confused, slurring, or too unsteady to stand? Can she keep fluids down?'","To the CPN: 'When did she last take lithium, and what else is she taking?'","To the GP: 'Is now okay for a quick urgent call about Mrs Doyle?'","To the GP: 'Could someone visit her today, by four, to assess her and take an urgent lithium level and U&E?'","To the GP: 'Who will see the result, and by when? What happens if it comes back after half six?'","To the GP: 'Can I read back what we've agreed?'"],"recognise":["Diarrhoea, vomiting, coarse tremor and unsteadiness on lithium are toxicity until proven otherwise.","NSAIDs and dehydration both raise lithium levels; older people become toxic quickly.","Worsening confusion, slurred speech, inability to walk, seizures or reduced consciousness mean 999.","Refusing A&E with capacity does not end your duty: find the safest alternative and safety-net.","GP reluctance is usually about workload, not indifference: acknowledge it and share the load."],"decide":[{"c":"Open with the concern, give SBAR, make one specific timed request, offer what your team will do, and agree results and red flags.","r":"Gets her a same-day assessment and level with clear owners, and keeps the GP on side.","best":true},{"c":"Accept a phlebotomy appointment tomorrow morning.","r":"Leaves a possibly toxic, dehydrated 72-year-old untested overnight while still vomiting.","best":false},{"c":"Argue that lithium monitoring is the GP's job under shared care.","r":"May be true locally, but arguing wastes time and loses goodwill; agree roles instead.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: her refusal of A&E stands if she has capacity for that decision; record the assessment. If she becomes confused and lacks capacity, act in her best interests.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): when you refer or hand over care, share all relevant information and make sure responsibility is clearly agreed.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): sharing information with her GP for her direct care is expected.","Shared care agreements set who monitors lithium; in many areas the GP does routine levels and the specialist advises on dose. Check your local agreement."],"say":[{"to":"GP (situation)","s":"I'm calling about Margaret Doyle, 72. I'm worried she may have lithium toxicity, and I need your help today."},{"to":"GP (request)","s":"I'm asking whether someone could visit her by four today to assess her and take an urgent lithium level and U&E."},{"to":"GP (reluctance)","s":"I know you're flat out, and I'm sorry to add to it. Here's what we'll do on our side, so this is shared."}],"write":"CMHT note, 13:50, Dr [name], CT2:\nSBAR call to Dr Moss (GP) re possible lithium toxicity: D&V 3 days, coarse tremor, unsteady, OTC ibuprofen; alert, oriented. Refuses A&E (capacity assessed by CPN [name]).\nAgreed: GP visits 16:00; urgent lithium (time of last dose 21:00 Mon), U&E/eGFR, calcium. Duty GP receives results until 18:30, then out of hours. Lithium 1.5 or above, or worse kidney function: admission.\nLithium held and ibuprofen stopped (CPN told patient and daughter). Red flags: 999.\nCMHT to phone her 09:00 Wed; consultant to review lithium. Summary emailed to practice 14:15.","make":["CPN tells her and her daughter: no lithium tonight, stop ibuprofen, sip fluids, red flags for 999.","CPN stays until the GP visit is confirmed.","Email a written summary of the agreed plan to the practice, marked urgent, within the hour.","Tell your consultant; consultant decides restart dose and target level.","Phone the patient or CPN at 09:00 tomorrow; confirm the result and the plan."],"next":"At 17:50 the duty GP rings: lithium 1.9 mmol/L and eGFR down from 58 to 34. Told the result, Mrs Doyle now agrees to hospital, and the GP arranges admission to the acute medical unit. You inform your consultant, the CPN and her daughter, and record that lithium stays stopped until psychiatry reviews her on the medical ward.","marks":["Opens with the concern in one sentence.","Uses SBAR with the relevant facts: dose, last level, symptoms, interacting drug.","Makes one specific request with a time.","Handles reluctance with empathy and an offer, not argument.","Agrees roles for lithium, bloods, results and red flags.","Reads back the plan and documents it the same day."],"pitfalls":["A long story before saying what you want.","Accepting 'tomorrow' without stating the risk.","Arguing about whose job it is.","Not agreeing who checks the result and when.","Forgetting to tell the patient to withhold lithium and stop ibuprofen."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: lithium carbonate, monitoring and interactions (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS lithium therapy patient information pack (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: SBAR communication tool (checked Nov 2026)","5. GMC Good medical practice (2024, checked Nov 2026)","6. Mental Capacity Act 2005 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK GPs are very busy and often say no first. A short SBAR call with one clear request, plus an offer of what you will do yourself, usually gets a yes. Always confirm the plan in writing the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","type":"Handover and escalation","do":"Use SBAR to secure a same-day GP visit and lithium level, agreeing owners, red flags and documentation.","obj":["Gave an SBAR referral that stated possible lithium toxicity in the first sentence","Made one clear request: a GP visit by 4pm with an urgent lithium level and U&E","Acknowledged the GP's workload and offered what your team will do in return","Agreed who withholds lithium, stops ibuprofen, checks results and acts on red flags tonight","Read back the plan, documented it and emailed a summary to the practice"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's your patient. Can't your team deal with it?\"","r":"I understand; you're stretched and this landed late. We'll do our part: the CPN stays, we stop the lithium and follow up. But she needs a medical assessment and bloods today, and you're best placed."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"We've no visits left. Send her to A&E.\"","r":"That would be my first choice too. She's refused, and she has capacity for that. A home visit is the only way she'll get bloods today. Could you or your paramedic practitioner go?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How sure are you it's lithium toxicity?\"","r":"I'm not sure, which is why I need the level. But three days of vomiting and diarrhoea, a coarse tremor, unsteadiness and ibuprofen on lithium is a classic picture. I'd rather we ruled it out today."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can it wait till tomorrow morning?\"","r":"I see why you'd want that. My worry is that her level keeps rising overnight while she's still being sick, and at 72 she can become toxic quickly. Could she be the last visit of the day?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I'm not going to hospital. Last time I was on a trolley for fourteen hours.\"","r":"That sounds awful, and I understand not wanting that again. We'll get a doctor to you at home. If you become confused or can't keep fluids down, hospital may become the safest place."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I take my lithium tonight?\"","r":"Good question. Please don't take it tonight. We'll check your blood level first and tell you when to restart. Keep sipping water, and stop the ibuprofen."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Your lot started her on lithium; monitoring is on you.\"","r":"Fair point that it's shared. Our local agreement puts levels with the practice and dosing with us. Today I'm giving you our advice, and we'll pick up the dose decision from here."}],"bps":{"bio":["Withhold lithium 600 mg from tonight until the level is back and psychiatry has reviewed it.","Stop ibuprofen; paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day for hip pain (lower dose if under 50 kg).","GP bloods today: lithium level (write time of last dose), U&E with creatinine and eGFR, calcium; marked urgent.","Lithium 1.5 mmol/L or above, falling eGFR, worsening tremor, ataxia or confusion: hospital admission.","If restarted: consultant sets dose; level one week after restart, 12 hours after the dose."],"psych":["CPN asks about sleep and mood at each contact while lithium is held, to catch early relapse.","Consultant reviews whether lithium is still right and her target range (often 0.4-0.8 mmol/L in older adults)."],"social":["CPN stays until the visit is confirmed and leaves fluids within reach.","Daughter (next of kin) told with Mrs Doyle's consent; checks on her this evening.","Ask her regular pharmacy to note lithium and advise against over-the-counter NSAIDs."],"safety":["Red flags to her, daughter and GP: confusion, slurred speech, unable to stand, vomiting everything, fits: call 999.","Duty GP receives results until 18:30; out of hours flagged after that.","You phone at 09:00 tomorrow; note and email summary to practice today."]},"facts":[{"h":"Toxicity can occur 'in range'","x":"Toxicity is usually above 1.5 mmol/L, but older people and those who are dehydrated or have kidney impairment can be toxic at lower levels. Treat the symptoms, not just the number."},{"h":"NSAIDs push lithium up","x":"Ibuprofen and other NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers and thiazide diuretics raise lithium levels. Over-the-counter ibuprofen is a common trigger; ask what they buy."},{"h":"Know the toxic signs","x":"Coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, slurred speech, confusion. Severe: seizures, reduced consciousness, arrhythmias. Vomiting and diarrhoea also dehydrate, raising the level further."},{"h":"Timing of the level","x":"Routine levels are taken 12 hours after the last dose. When toxicity is suspected, take it straight away and write the time of the last dose on the form."},{"h":"SBAR in one breath","x":"Situation: who and your worry. Background: drug, dose, last level. Assessment: findings and what you think. Recommendation: one specific request with a time. Then read back who does what."},{"h":"Shared care sets who monitors","x":"In many areas the GP does lithium levels and kidney and thyroid tests under a shared care agreement, and psychiatry advises on dose. Know your local agreement before you ring."}],"sim":{"setup":"Tuesday 13:30, CMHT office. Mrs Margaret Doyle, 72, on lithium and housebound, has vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse tremor and unsteadiness, and refuses A&E. Ring her GP, Dr Moss, for an urgent home visit and blood test.","role":{"who":"Dr Rachel Moss, 45, female GP partner at Mrs Doyle's practice","open":"I've got five minutes between patients. What is it?","manner":"Rushed, tired and defensive at first; pushes the task back to psychiatry and suggests A&E. Softens when her workload is acknowledged and the request is specific and shared; apologises for being short at the end.","free":["She has a full surgery and two visits already booked","She thinks psychiatry should manage lithium","She suggests A&E as the simplest option","She has not seen Mrs Doyle for six months"],"ifasked":["The practice has a paramedic practitioner who does visits","She could go herself after surgery, around 4pm","The duty GP receives critical results until 18:30; after that they go to out of hours","Mrs Doyle's eGFR was 58 last time","Mrs Doyle's daughter is listed as next of kin","The practice prefers plans confirmed by email to the urgent inbox"],"worries":["Why can't your team take the bloods?","Can it wait until tomorrow?","Who's checking the result if it comes back late?"],"rules":["Says no to a vague request; agrees to a clear, specific request with a time","Becomes curt if lectured about duty; softens if her workload is acknowledged","Agrees to visit only once roles for results and follow-up are clear","Asks for the plan in writing before ending the call"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Dr Moss, it's Dr Iqbal, CT2 with the North community mental health team. Is now okay for a quick, urgent call?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I've got five minutes between patients. What is it?"},{"t":"0:19","band":"Situation","w":"D","s":"I'm calling about Margaret Doyle, 72. I'm worried she may have lithium toxicity, and I need your help today.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Go on."},{"t":"0:33","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"She has bipolar disorder and takes lithium 600 milligrams at night. Her level three months ago was 0.7. She's housebound since her hip fracture.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"0:49","band":"Assessment","w":"D","s":"Her CPN is with her now. She's had diarrhoea and vomiting for three days, a coarse tremor, and she's unsteady. She's been taking ibuprofen from the chemist for her hip.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Is she confused?"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"No, she's alert and oriented, and drinking a little. But she's unsteady on her frame, and I'm worried her level is high and her kidneys are struggling.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sounds like she needs A&E."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"I agree that's the safest place, and the CPN offered. She's refused, and the CPN thinks she has capacity for that decision. She will accept a blood test at home.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Then I don't know what you want from me. Can't your team deal with it? Lithium's yours."},{"t":"1:54","band":"Recommendation","w":"D","s":"I hear you, and I know you're flat out. Here's exactly what I'm asking: could someone visit her today, by four, to assess her and take an urgent lithium level and U&E?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"We've no visits left. I've forty patients and two visits already."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"That's a huge day. I'm sorry to add to it.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Can't it wait till the morning? We could send the phlebotomist."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"My worry is that she's still being sick, so her level could keep rising overnight, and at 72 she can become toxic quickly. If she gets confused tonight, it's an emergency.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"And why can't your team take the bloods?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"She needs a medical assessment of her fluids and kidneys too, which we can't do at home. Here's what we'll do: the CPN stays till a visit's confirmed, she holds tonight's lithium and stops the ibuprofen, and I'll ring her tomorrow morning.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"(pause) We do have a paramedic practitioner. Or I could go after surgery, about four."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"That would be brilliant, thank you. Four o'clock, you, at her home. Could the bloods be lithium, U&E with eGFR and calcium, marked urgent, with her last dose time on the form? That was nine last night.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Fine. Her eGFR was 58 last time, by the way."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's useful. Any drop from that would worry me too.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Who's chasing the result? It won't be back before six."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Who does what","w":"D","s":"Can we agree that now? Who gets the result this evening?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"The duty GP gets critical results till half six. After that, it goes to out of hours."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Then could you ask the lab to phone the duty GP and flag her to out of hours? If the level is 1.5 or more, or her kidneys are worse, she needs admission. She may agree once she knows the result.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Agreed. And if it's normal?"},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Then lithium stays stopped until our consultant reviews her tomorrow and advises on the dose. Could you send the result to our team inbox?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. What should she do in the meantime?"},{"t":"5:10","band":"Red flags","w":"D","s":"The CPN will leave fluids within reach and tell her and her daughter: if she becomes confused, can't stand, has slurred speech, keeps vomiting or has a fit, call 999.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"Fine. Remind them about the ibuprofen."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"Will do. Paracetamol for the hip instead.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Okay. Anything else?"},{"t":"5:40","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me read it back. You visit at four and take urgent lithium, U&E and calcium. Duty GP gets results till half six, then out of hours. Level 1.5 or more, or worse kidneys, means admission. We stop lithium and ibuprofen now, the CPN stays till you're confirmed, and I ring her at nine tomorrow.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"I'll put that in our notes and email it to your urgent inbox within the hour, with her medication list.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Please do. Mark it urgent so reception flags it."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"I will. Thanks so much, Dr Moss. I know it's a busy day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"No problem. Sorry I was short. It's been one of those days."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Completely understand. Thanks for fitting her in.","m":[6]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, role and team, and checks the GP can talk now","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States the situation and the worry of lithium toxicity in the first sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives background: diagnosis, lithium dose, last level, housebound, kidney function","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives assessment: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, ibuprofen, alertness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Makes one specific request: a visit today by a set time with urgent lithium level and U&E","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the GP's workload and frustration without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why A&E is not happening and why it cannot wait until tomorrow","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers what the CMHT will do: CPN stays, lithium held, ibuprofen stopped, follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees who receives results, when, and the action thresholds","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees red flags and the route to 999 or admission","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reads back the plan with owners and times","d":"Close"},{"c":"Commits to document and email a summary, and thanks the GP","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the concern first, uses SBAR, makes one specific timed request, handles reluctance with empathy, and agrees owners, results and red flags with a read-back.","borderline":"Secures a visit but leaves results or red flags vague, or takes too long to say what is wanted.","fail":"No clear request, argues about whose job it is, accepts tomorrow without stating the risk, or no documentation."}}},
{"id":"c-s-02","n":1202,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👪","title":"Her mother came instead: collateral history of a daughter's low mood and weight loss","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:15","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"A 21-year-old woman was referred by her GP with low mood and poor eating. She has not come to her first appointment. Her mother, a 52-year-old woman, is in the waiting room instead. Yesterday the daughter phoned the team and agreed that staff can speak to her mother. The mother says, 'I'm so sorry. She just couldn't face it.'"},"task":"Take a collateral history from the mother, with the daughter's consent, covering onset, symptoms, eating, risk, functioning and background, then agree the next steps.","first60":["Read the GP referral: weight, bloods, any medication offered, and exactly what the daughter agreed to share.","Check the notes for any previous contact with mental health services and any risk history.","Plan the structure: onset and course, core symptoms, eating, thinking, risk, background, functioning.","Be ready to explain confidentiality: you can listen to everything; you share only what the daughter allows.","Have the crisis line, NHS 111 mental health option and local carers' service details to hand."],"ask":["'When did you first notice a change in her, and what was going on then?'","'How is her mood most days? Does she still enjoy anything?'","'How is she sleeping? What time does she wake?'","'What is she eating in a day? Has she lost weight, and does she talk about her weight or shape?'","'How is her concentration? What does she say about herself and the future?'","'Has she ever said she wishes she wasn't here, or talked about ending her life?'","'Has anyone in the family had depression, or died by suicide?'","'How is she managing day to day: washing, getting out, work or study?'"],"recognise":["Ten months of low mood, loss of interest and low energy after a break-up, getting worse not better: a depressive episode, not ordinary heartbreak.","Early waking, poor appetite with weight loss, and slowed speech and movement point to a severe, biological depression.","Not eating because food tastes of nothing, with no wish to be thinner and no purging, makes a primary eating disorder unlikely.","Worthlessness, guilt about the break-up and hopelessness are the negative thoughts that drive risk.","Her father's suicide is a major risk factor; a passive wish not to wake plus hopelessness needs prompt action.","A relative's account is often more accurate about change, slowing and self-neglect than the patient's own."],"decide":[{"c":"Thank the mother, explain the likely depression, arrange for the team to phone the daughter today and see her at home within two days, and give the mother safety advice and crisis numbers.","r":"Right: the risk picture (weight loss, hopelessness, father's suicide) needs prompt direct assessment, and the mother leaves with a clear plan.","best":true},{"c":"Rebook the daughter for a routine appointment in six weeks and ask the mother to encourage her to come.","r":"Unsafe: severe depression with weight loss, a death wish and a family history of suicide should not wait weeks, and she has already been unable to attend.","best":false},{"c":"Ask the mother to persuade her daughter to start the sertraline the GP prescribed, and discharge back to the GP.","r":"Wrong: no one has assessed her directly, the severity and risk are high, and starting treatment needs her own consent and follow-up.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality (GMC 2017): the daughter has consented, so you can discuss her care with her mother. Record exactly what was agreed; anything she told you privately needs her permission first.","Listening is always allowed: you can receive information from a relative even without consent.","Carers can ask the council for their own carer's assessment from the local authority under the Care Act 2014.","Severe depression alone does not mean she lacks capacity; any treatment decision needs her own assessment and consent."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"I'm really glad you came. Ella has said we can talk to you, so I can listen to everything and share general information. Anything she tells us privately, I'd check with her first."},{"to":"Mother","s":"From what you describe, this sounds like a fairly severe depression. Because of the weight loss, her wish not to wake up and her dad's history, we won't wait: we'll ring her today and see her at home this week."},{"to":"Team (handover)","s":"Collateral from mother: 10-month depressive episode, 6 kg weight loss, early waking, slowing, hopelessness, passive death wish. Father died by suicide when she was 14. Needs home visit within 48 hours."}],"write":"CMHT. Collateral history from mother (Mrs Sarah Morgan, 52). Ella Morgan, 21F, DNA initial assessment. Ella gave verbal consent by phone (06/11) for team to speak to her mother.\nOnset ~10 months ago after relationship ended; progressive. No previous episodes.\nLow mood most of the day, every day; anhedonia (stopped netball, friends); anergia, in bed until 14:00. Initial insomnia and early waking (05:00). Appetite poor, food 'tastes of nothing'; ~6 kg weight loss in 5 months. No drive for thinness, no purging, laxatives, bingeing or excess exercise. Psychomotor slowing noted by mother. Poor concentration; deferred university. Guilt about break-up, worthlessness, hopelessness.\nNo psychotic or manic features reported. Alcohol minimal now; drug use unknown.\nRisk: said last week she wished she wouldn't wake up. No known plan or self-harm. Paracetamol at home; unstarted sertraline. No risk to others. Self-neglect: showers weekly, not left house in 2 weeks.\nFH: father depression, died by suicide when Ella was 14.\nGP bloods 3 weeks ago incl. TFT normal.\nImpression: probable severe depressive episode without psychotic features (to confirm). Eating disorder unlikely. Risk of suicide raised.\nPlan: phone Ella today; joint home visit within 48 h. Mother advised to secure medicines; crisis numbers given. Carer's assessment and carers' service info given.\nDr L. Chen ST4","make":["Confirm and document the consent first, and say out loud what you can and cannot share.","Thank her for coming: a relative who turns up is an asset, not a substitute.","Ask about the eating with three targeted questions: what she eats, why she isn't eating, and how she sees her body.","Ask about slowing directly: relatives notice it but rarely mention it unprompted.","Ask family history of suicide explicitly; it changes the urgency of the whole plan.","End with a plan for the patient and a plan for the mother: safety at home and support for herself."],"next":"The duty worker reaches Ella by phone that afternoon and she agrees to a home visit. Two days later she is assessed at home with a severe depressive episode, starts sertraline with weekly reviews, and is referred for CBT. Her mother has a carer's assessment the next month.","marks":["Confirmed consent and explained what could and could not be shared.","Established onset, trigger and a worsening course.","Covered mood, interest, energy, sleep, appetite, weight and slowing.","Asked enough about eating to rule out a primary eating disorder.","Asked about death wishes, plans, means, self-neglect and risk to others.","Elicited the father's suicide, responded with empathy, and agreed an urgent plan."],"pitfalls":["Refusing to talk to the mother 'because of confidentiality' despite consent.","Missing the eating disorder differential, or assuming one without asking about weight and shape.","Not asking about family history of suicide.","Forgetting risk to others and self-neglect.","Rebooking a routine appointment and letting her leave without a safety plan."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Carer's assessments (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK an adult's relative has no automatic right to information, but you can always listen. When the patient consents, record it in the notes with the date and its limits. Carers are entitled to their own assessment of their needs from the council.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-03","n":1203,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"'This tablet is driving me mad': restless and refusing haloperidol","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:20","where":"Acute inpatient ward (informal admission)","text":"A 31-year-old woman with schizophrenia, an informal patient, moved from aripiprazole to haloperidol three weeks ago because her voices persisted; the dose went up to 10 mg daily last week. The voices have gone, but she has refused her last two doses. She is pacing the corridor and says, 'This tablet is driving me mad.' The nurse asks you to see her."},"task":"Explore her reason for refusing the medicine, recognise akathisia and rule out its look-alikes, ask about suicidal thoughts, explain it plainly, and agree a plan she accepts.","first60":["Read the drug chart: date of the switch, every dose change, missed doses, any PRN given, and any other medicines.","Check the admission bloods (including thyroid) and the latest ECG and observations.","Ask the nurse what they have seen: pacing, rocking, sleep, distress, and anything said about not wanting to live.","Go in to listen, not to persuade: her concern is the medicine, so start there.","Have the Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale ready to score her today as a baseline."],"ask":["'What is the tablet doing to you? Tell me in your words.'","'When did this start, and what changed with your medicine around then?'","'What does it feel like inside? Where do you feel it most in your body?'","'Could you stand still for a minute while I watch, and tell me how that feels?'","'Any stiffness, shaking, or muscle spasms?'","'Is it worse at night lying down, or all day?'","'How is your mood? Have the voices come back?'","'When it gets this bad, have you had thoughts of not wanting to live?'"],"recognise":["Onset days after a haloperidol increase, with a subjective inner tension and an urge to move: akathisia until proven otherwise.","The visible signs: shifting from foot to foot, rocking, crossing and uncrossing legs, standing up and sitting down, pacing.","Restless legs syndrome is worse in the evening at rest with an urge to move the legs; akathisia is all day and felt as whole-body tension.","Anxiety has worry content; relapse brings back psychotic symptoms; neither fits a clear rise in dose followed by motor restlessness.","Akathisia is linked with suicidal thoughts, impulsive acts and aggression, and is a common reason for stopping antipsychotics.","Refusal here is a rational response to a real side effect, not a lack of insight."],"decide":[{"c":"Take the restlessness seriously, score it, ask about suicidal thoughts, and agree with her a lower haloperidol dose plus propranolol, with a switch if it does not settle; discuss with the consultant today and tell the nurses.","r":"Right: it treats the cause, keeps the antipsychotic benefit, involves her in the choice and addresses the risk.","best":true},{"c":"Explain that she must keep taking haloperidol 10 mg because it has stopped her voices, and give PRN lorazepam for agitation.","r":"Wrong: it dismisses a real side effect, will entrench refusal, and treats a symptom while the cause continues.","best":false},{"c":"Add procyclidine 5 mg three times daily for side effects.","r":"Not first choice: anticholinergics help parkinsonism and dystonia but are generally unhelpful for akathisia, and add their own side effects.","best":false}],"law":["She is informal and has capacity: she can refuse medication. Persuasion must not become pressure, and any change needs her consent.","If refusal led to relapse and a risk of harm, detention would be a separate Mental Health Act assessment, not a lever to make her take a tablet that is harming her.","Propranolol for akathisia is off-label: explain this, record her consent and the reason, and follow local policy.","Tell the nurse in charge about her suicidal thoughts and record the review of her observation level."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I believe you. What you're describing sounds like a known side effect called akathisia. It's real, it's not you relapsing, and we can treat it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"We have three options: a lower dose, a different medicine, or adding propranolol. You choose with me, and we can combine them."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"She has akathisia from haloperidol. She's had thoughts that she'd rather be dead if it continued, no plan or intent. Please review her observation level and let me know if the restlessness worsens."}],"write":"Ward review. 31F, paranoid schizophrenia, informal. Switched aripiprazole to haloperidol 3/52 ago; increased to 10 mg OD 1/52 ago. Refused last 2 doses.\nHx: 5 days of inner tension, 'ants' in legs, unable to sit or stand still, worst in medication queue; poor sleep. No stiffness, tremor or spasms. Not worse at night lying down. No anxious content; mood otherwise fine; no hallucinations since switch. Tea x2/day, no drugs. No other medicines. TFT normal on admission.\nObs: rocking foot to foot when asked to stand still for 1 min; repeatedly crossing legs and standing up. BARS global 3 (moderate).\nRisk: thoughts she would rather be dead if this continued, attributed to restlessness; no plan or intent; no thoughts of harm to others.\nImpression: acute akathisia secondary to haloperidol dose increase.\nPlan (agreed with patient): haloperidol reduce to 5 mg OD; propranolol 10 mg TDS (off-label, consent recorded; no asthma, HR 82, BP 118/74); check HR and BP before doses; hold if HR <60 or SBP <100. BARS daily. Review 3 days; if not improved, discuss switch (e.g. olanzapine) with patient. Discussed with Dr Okoro (consultant). Nurse in charge informed of suicidal thoughts; obs reviewed.\nDr R. Patel FY2","make":["Let her move: asking someone with akathisia to sit still while you talk makes it worse and costs you rapport.","Get the timeline exact: start date, each dose change, and when the restlessness began.","Ask about the feeling inside and watch for the signs outside; you need both.","Ask about suicidal thoughts and who or what she links them to: that is the risk that turns a side effect into an emergency.","Offer real choices and let her pick; agreement beats adherence by pressure.","Score the Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale now and again at each review, so improvement is measured."],"next":"Her consultant agrees the plan. Within three days, on haloperidol 5 mg and propranolol 10 mg three times daily, she sits through ward round and her BARS score falls from 3 to 1. The voices stay away, and she takes every dose.","marks":["Started with her concern and did not try to talk her into the tablet.","Established the timeline from switch to dose increase to restlessness.","Elicited both the inner tension and the visible restlessness.","Ruled out parkinsonism, dystonia, restless legs, anxiety, relapse and substances.","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and linked them to the akathisia.","Offered dose reduction, switch and propranolol, and agreed a plan with her."],"pitfalls":["Calling it anxiety or agitation and giving more sedation or antipsychotic.","Labelling the refusal as 'poor insight' and pushing adherence.","Missing the suicidal thoughts that come with akathisia.","Prescribing an anticholinergic as the main treatment.","Starting propranolol without checking for asthma, slow pulse or low blood pressure."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Haloperidol; Propranolol hydrochloride (checked Nov 2026)","3. Barnes TRE. A rating scale for drug-induced akathisia. Br J Psychiatry 1989;154:672-6","4. Choice and Medication: Akathisia patient leaflet (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK an informal patient can refuse any medication, and side effects are the commonest reason people stop antipsychotics. Ward teams value a doctor who treats the side effect over one who pushes the tablet.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-04","n":1204,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧼","title":"Severe OCD diagnosed: explaining the condition and the treatment choices","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00","where":"Psychiatric outpatient clinic","text":"A 29-year-old male soldier is on medical leave because he washes his hands and uniform for hours each day, fearing he carries germs that will make his partner and baby ill. Last week the consultant diagnosed OCD; his Y-BOCS score was 26 out of 40. He has come back to understand the diagnosis and his options. He says, 'Is this just who I am now?'"},"task":"Explain OCD in plain language and set out the treatment options (CBT with exposure and response prevention, SSRIs, and the two combined), using his severity score, and agree next steps. No history or risk assessment is needed today.","first60":["Read the consultant's letter: his obsessions, compulsions, Y-BOCS score, insight, mood and any risk.","Check his medicines, allergies and any history of mania, seizures or bleeding problems before discussing an SSRI.","Plan a simple drawing of the OCD cycle: thought, anxiety, compulsion, short relief, thought returns stronger.","Have the NHS OCD page and a self-referral route for psychological therapy ready, plus a medicine leaflet.","Find out what he already knows before explaining anything."],"ask":["'What do you already know about OCD, and what have you read?'","'What do you think caused it?'","'What would you most like to get out of today?'","'What do you notice happens to the anxiety after you wash?'","'How do you feel about talking therapy, and about medication?'","'What worries you most about treatment?'","'Can you tell me in your words how the cycle works?'"],"recognise":["His belief that OCD is 'just who I am' is common and feeds hopelessness: separate the person from the condition.","Wanting to 'wash properly once' or take antibiotics 'just in case' is the OCD seeking certainty; agreeing would feed the cycle.","A Y-BOCS of 26 sits in the severe band (24 to 31): NICE recommends combined SSRI and CBT with ERP for severe impairment.","Fear that therapy means digging into childhood: ERP is practical, here-and-now and step by step.","Fear that antidepressants are addictive: they are not, but stopping suddenly can cause discontinuation symptoms.","Hope is evidence-based: most people improve substantially with treatment."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the OCD cycle, recommend CBT with ERP combined with an SSRI such as sertraline, address his worries about therapy and addiction, and let him choose with written information.","r":"Right: it matches NICE advice for severe OCD, answers his real concerns, and leaves the choice with him.","best":true},{"c":"Recommend sertraline alone because therapy waiting lists are long.","r":"Incomplete: ERP is the core psychological treatment and works best combined with medication in severe OCD. Refer now and start medication if he wishes while he waits.","best":false},{"c":"Agree to a course of antibiotics to settle his fear of infection.","r":"Wrong: it is reassurance-seeking dressed as treatment. It would strengthen the OCD, expose him to needless side effects and add to antibiotic resistance.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Decision making and consent (2020): explain the options, including doing nothing, with benefits and risks, and support his choice.","NICE CG31: for adults with severe functional impairment, offer combined treatment with an SSRI and CBT including ERP.","Confidentiality: share information with his unit's medical officer only with his consent, and only what is needed.","Antidepressant leaflets: the MHRA requires patient information on suicidal thinking early in treatment, especially in younger adults; mention it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"OCD isn't who you are. It's a common, treatable condition that hijacks a normal worry and keeps it going through the washing."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Washing brings quick relief, which teaches your brain the washing kept you safe. So the thought comes back stronger. Treatment breaks that loop."},{"to":"Patient","s":"With your score, the best results come from both: a therapy called ERP, and an SSRI medicine. You don't have to decide everything today."}],"write":"Outpatient review. 29M, serving soldier on medical leave. OCD (contamination obsessions, washing compulsions 3-4 h/day). Y-BOCS 26/40 (severe). Good insight.\nToday: explained OCD (intrusive thoughts, anxiety, compulsions, short-term relief maintaining the cycle) using a drawn diagram; Y-BOCS meaning; good prognosis with treatment.\nOptions discussed: CBT with ERP (weekly 60-min sessions, more than 10 therapist hours, homework); SSRI (sertraline 50 mg mane, titrate to up to 200 mg, up to 12 weeks for full effect, continue 12 months after remission; side effects nausea, GI upset, early anxiety, sexual dysfunction; not addictive; taper to stop; report any suicidal thoughts); combined treatment recommended per NICE CG31.\nAddressed: 'just who I am' belief; request for antibiotics (declined, explained); worry about childhood-focused therapy; worry about addiction.\nTeach-back accurate. Patient chooses combined treatment: will start sertraline 50 mg after reading leaflet; referred for CBT with ERP.\nPlan: GP letter to prescribe; review 2 weeks (tolerability), then 4-6 weekly. Consent given to inform unit medical officer of diagnosis and plan.\nDr C. Hughes ST5","make":["Draw the cycle: it explains the illness and the treatment in one picture.","Normalise intrusive thoughts: nearly everyone has them; OCD is what you do with them.","Explain ERP in practical terms: a ladder of feared situations, staying with the anxiety, not washing, and the anxiety falling on its own.","Give the SSRI facts he needs: name, starting dose, how long before it helps, common side effects, and that it is not addictive.","Turn the antibiotics and 'proper wash' ideas into teaching points about the cycle, not a refusal.","Use the severity score to explain why combined treatment is recommended."],"next":"He starts sertraline 50 mg a week later after reading the leaflet, increasing to 100 mg at his 4-week review. His ERP therapy begins in week six. Three months on his Y-BOCS is 15 and he washes for under an hour a day; he is planning a phased return to duty.","marks":["Asked what he knew and believed before explaining.","Explained obsessions, compulsions and the maintaining cycle in plain words.","Explained the severity score and gave honest hope.","Explained ERP practically: what it involves, how often and who delivers it.","Gave the key SSRI facts, including that it is not addictive.","Recommended combined treatment and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Using jargon like 'ego-dystonic' or 'negative reinforcement' without explanation.","Lecturing without checking what he knows or wants.","Implying therapy will dig into his childhood.","Saying SSRIs work in a week, or omitting discontinuation advice.","Agreeing to antibiotics or reassurance to settle his anxiety."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31 OCD and body dysmorphic disorder (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) (checked Nov 2026)","4. Goodman WK et al. The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1989;46:1006-11"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: serving UK military personnel usually receive mental health care through Defence Medical Services, and veterans can use the NHS veterans' service Op COURAGE. Always ask before sharing anything with his chain of command or medical officer.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-05","n":1205,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💓","title":"'Sudden unbearable terrors': history of panic attacks to a diagnosis","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 09:30","where":"Psychiatric outpatient clinic","text":"A 34-year-old male accountant has been referred by his GP with 'sudden, unbearable terrors' that are 'ruining his life'. He was sure it was his heart, but his ECG, 24-hour heart monitor and blood tests were normal. He says, 'I'm not sure I should be seeing a psychiatrist. I don't want to be fobbed off.'"},"task":"Take a history of the attacks to reach a diagnosis of panic disorder: describe the attacks, exclude physical, medication and substance causes and other anxiety disorders, screen for agoraphobia, mood and psychosis, and explain your conclusion briefly.","first60":["Read the GP referral: which tests were done (ECG, 24-hour tape, thyroid, full blood count, glucose) and the results.","Check his medicines, including over-the-counter remedies, inhalers and supplements.","Plan the structure: one attack in detail, then between attacks, then other anxiety disorders, mood, then physical and substances.","Expect him to fear being dismissed: start by acknowledging how real the attacks feel.","Have the NHS Talking Therapies self-referral details ready for the end."],"ask":["'Tell me about the last attack, from the very first thing you noticed.'","'How quickly does it build, how long does it last, and how often do they come?'","'What goes through your mind during an attack?'","'Do they come out of the blue, or only in certain places?'","'Between attacks, do you worry about the next one? Is there anything you now avoid?'","'Do you avoid crowds, queues, public transport, or being far from home alone?'","'Are you a worrier in general? How are you in social situations?'","'How much coffee, energy drinks, alcohol, or other drugs do you use?'"],"recognise":["Discrete surges of fear peaking within minutes, with racing heart, breathlessness, dizziness, tingling and a fear of dying: panic attacks.","Attacks that come out of the blue, plus ongoing worry about the next one or changes in behaviour to avoid them: panic disorder.","Tingling lips and fingers come from overbreathing; feeling unreal during the peak is depersonalisation or derealisation, not psychosis.","Avoiding exercise or caffeine because they raise the heart rate is avoidance of body sensations, common in panic disorder.","Attacks only in social situations suggest social anxiety; only with a specific trigger, a phobia; with reminders of trauma, PTSD.","Normal heart tracing and thyroid tests make the main physical causes unlikely, but caffeine, stimulants and alcohol withdrawal still need asking about."],"decide":[{"c":"Make a working diagnosis of panic disorder without agoraphobia, explain it plainly, advise cutting caffeine, and refer him for CBT for panic through NHS Talking Therapies with a GP letter.","r":"Right: the history is complete, physical causes are excluded, and NICE recommends CBT first for panic disorder.","best":true},{"c":"Ask the GP to arrange a cardiology referral and echocardiogram before any psychological treatment.","r":"Unhelpful: a normal ECG and 24-hour tape with a classic panic history do not justify more tests, which can reinforce his fear that something is wrong with his heart.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe diazepam 5 mg to take when an attack starts.","r":"Wrong: NICE advises against benzodiazepines for panic disorder; they worsen long-term outcome and risk dependence.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG113: offer CBT for panic disorder as first-line treatment; benzodiazepines should not be prescribed for it.","NICE CG113: an SSRI licensed for panic disorder is the drug option if medication is chosen.","GMC Good medical practice: take his concerns seriously and explain test results honestly; avoid repeating tests to reassure.","DVLA: panic disorder alone usually does not need reporting unless it affects safe driving; advise him to check the DVLA guidance if attacks happen while driving."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"These attacks are real and they're terrifying. Your heart tests are normal, which tells us your body's alarm system is going off when there's no danger."},{"to":"Patient","s":"This pattern has a name: panic disorder. It's common, it's not dangerous, and a specific kind of talking therapy works very well for it."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Working diagnosis: panic disorder without agoraphobia. No depressive episode, psychosis or substance cause. Advised caffeine reduction; referred for CBT for panic (NHS Talking Therapies). Please avoid benzodiazepines."}],"write":"Outpatient. 34M accountant. Referred by GP: 'sudden terrors'.\nAttacks for 2 years, ~weekly, sudden onset, peak within 10 min, last 15-30 min. Dry mouth, pounding heart, chest tightness, breathlessness, dizziness, tingling lips and fingers, sweating, urge to escape. Fears dying (heart attack) and 'going mad'. Derealisation at peak. Mostly unexpected (at desk, watching TV, once woke from sleep).\nBetween attacks: worry about next attack; declined promotion; stopped gym (avoids raised heart rate). No avoidance of crowds, transport, queues or going out alone.\nNo GAD (not a worrier generally); no social anxiety; no obsessions or compulsions; no trauma; health worry about heart partly reassured by tests.\nMood good, enjoys life, sleep OK, no suicidal thoughts. No hallucinations or delusions.\nGP: ECG, 24-h tape, FBC, TFT, glucose normal. No medicines. Caffeine: 4 coffees + 1 energy drink/day. Alcohol ~8 units/week at weekends. No drugs.\nFH: mother 'nerves'.\nImpression: panic disorder without agoraphobia. Caffeine a likely aggravating factor.\nPlan: diagnosis explained; caffeine reduction advised; referred to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for panic; self-help leaflet given; GP letter (avoid benzodiazepines). Review 8 weeks.\nDr O. Adebayo ST4","make":["Take one recent attack in slow motion: first sensation, build-up, peak, how it ended, how long.","Ask what he thought was happening at the peak: the catastrophic thought drives the spiral.","Establish that most attacks come out of the blue; triggered-only attacks point elsewhere.","Screen at least three other anxiety disorders with one or two targeted questions each.","Ask about caffeine in numbers; it is the commonest overlooked aggravating factor.","Close with a plain explanation of the body's alarm and a clear next step, so he does not feel dismissed."],"next":"He cuts down to one coffee a day and self-refers to NHS Talking Therapies. After eight sessions of CBT for panic, including deliberately bringing on a racing heart with exercise, his attacks stop. He accepts the manager role three months later.","marks":["Validated his fear of being dismissed and used open questions first.","Described a typical attack in detail, including the catastrophic thoughts.","Established that attacks are unexpected and asked about anticipatory worry and avoidance.","Screened for agoraphobia and at least three other anxiety disorders.","Screened mood, suicidal thoughts and psychosis.","Excluded physical, medicine and substance causes and explained the diagnosis plainly."],"pitfalls":["Saying 'it's just anxiety' without explaining the mechanism.","Mistaking derealisation at the peak for psychosis.","Missing caffeine, energy drinks or stimulants.","Forgetting to screen for agoraphobia and depression.","Ordering more heart tests or prescribing a benzodiazepine."],"refs":["1. NICE CG113 Generalised anxiety and panic disorder in adults (2011, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6B01 Panic disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Panic disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS Talking Therapies: self-referral (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England adults can refer themselves to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for panic; you don't need to wait for a psychiatry referral. UK guidance is firm that benzodiazepines are not a treatment for panic disorder.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-06","n":1206,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Adding lithium to her antidepressant: why, how and the blood tests","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:30, after the ward round","where":"Acute adult inpatient ward","text":"You are the CT1 on an acute ward. Ms Rebecca Doyle, 34, a primary school teaching assistant, is an informal patient in her third episode of depression in three years. She has taken amitriptyline 150 mg at night for 6 weeks with only slight benefit. This morning the consultant and team agreed to add lithium carbonate. Rebecca missed the end of the ward round because she was tearful. She has asked a nurse, 'Why are they giving me a bipolar drug?' Explain the plan to her and answer her questions. A full history has already been taken."},"task":"Explain lithium augmentation to Rebecca: why, the tests, the dose and levels, side effects, toxicity, interactions and pregnancy, then agree her decision.","first60":["Ask what she has already heard about lithium before you explain anything.","Say early that adding lithium does not mean the team think she has bipolar disorder.","Plan your time: rationale and tests first, then side effects, toxicity, then her own worries.","Listen for personal cues (family illness, pregnancy plans, painkillers) and follow them.","Give information in small chunks and check after each one."],"ask":["'What have you heard about lithium so far?'","'What worries you most about taking it?'","'Do you take anything else, including painkillers from the chemist, like ibuprofen?'","'Is a pregnancy possible now, or are you planning one in the next year or two?'","'What contraception, if any, are you using at the moment?'","'You mentioned your dad's kidneys. Do you know what caused his kidney failure?'","'Can you tell me back the signs that would mean your level is too high?'"],"recognise":["Lithium is an established add-on for depression that has not responded enough to an antidepressant; it is not only for bipolar disorder.","Lithium has a narrow gap between a helpful and a toxic level, so blood levels and safety advice are the core of the conversation.","Ibuprofen and other NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazide diuretics raise lithium levels; so do dehydration and vomiting or diarrhoea.","Lithium lowers the risk of suicide in mood disorders, but both lithium and amitriptyline are dangerous in overdose.","A woman of 34 planning a family needs pregnancy, contraception and breastfeeding covered now, not later.","A family history of kidney failure is a reason to check her baseline kidney function and monitor closely, not automatically a reason to refuse."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the rationale, tests, levels and safety advice in chunks, address her kidney and pregnancy worries, and let her decide with the information pack.","r":"Gives her a real choice and the knowledge to take lithium safely.","best":true},{"c":"Start lithium tonight as the ward round decided and give her the leaflet to read later.","r":"She has not agreed and does not yet know the toxicity signs or interactions; this is unsafe and not shared decision-making.","best":false},{"c":"Advise against lithium because her father had kidney failure.","r":"Her own kidney function has not been checked yet; the risk is assessed with tests and monitoring, not assumed.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: as an informal patient she is presumed to have capacity and her consent is needed to start lithium.","GMC Decision making and consent (2020): share the information she would want to know, including material risks to a future pregnancy.","NPSA lithium alert (2009, still current): give the patient booklet, record book and alert card, and keep blood results available to all prescribers."],"say":[{"to":"Rebecca","s":"Lithium is used in bipolar disorder, but it is also a well-tested add-on for depression that keeps coming back. Adding it does not change your diagnosis."},{"to":"Rebecca (toxicity)","s":"If you get sickness, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness, confusion or slurred speech, stop taking the lithium and get a level checked that day."},{"to":"Rebecca (pregnancy)","s":"Please do not stop lithium suddenly if you find you are pregnant. Ring us that day, and we will plan the safest way forward together."}],"write":"Ward note:\nDiscussed lithium augmentation of amitriptyline 150 mg nocte with Rebecca (34), informal.\nCovered: rationale (recurrent depression, partial response), baseline tests, levels 12 h post dose, side effects, toxicity signs, interactions (stop ibuprofen; paracetamol instead), sick-day advice, suicide-protective effect, pregnancy risk, contraception, breastfeeding.\nConcerns: father died of kidney failure (diabetic); plans a baby in about 2 years; using condoms.\nTeach-back: correctly named 4 toxicity signs.\nDecision: agrees in principle; wants baseline results first.\nPlan: U&Es, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC, weight, ECG, pregnancy test today; lithium pack given; review results with consultant Thursday.","make":["Baseline bloods today: U&Es with eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC; weight and BMI; ECG; urine pregnancy test.","If results are normal: Priadel 400 mg at night, level 12 hours after the dose on day 7.","Prescribe by brand and record the brand on the chart and discharge letter.","Give the lithium pack: patient booklet, record book and alert card.","Stop ibuprofen; prescribe paracetamol for period pain.","Set up shared monitoring with the GP before discharge."],"next":"Ten days later Rebecca's level is 0.7 mmol/L and she says she feels slightly brighter. On a weekend visit home she gets a stomach bug with vomiting and diarrhoea. Her partner rings the ward. Advise her to stop lithium for now, drink fluids and attend for a lithium level, U&Es and review the same day, using the emergency department if she is drowsy, confused or unsteady.","marks":["Finds out what she has heard and corrects the 'bipolar drug' worry.","Explains why lithium is added and that amitriptyline continues.","Lists baseline tests and the level-monitoring schedule.","Covers early and long-term side effects, toxicity signs and interactions.","Responds to her father's kidney failure with empathy and a plan.","Covers pregnancy, contraception and breastfeeding.","Checks understanding by teach-back and leaves the decision with her."],"pitfalls":["Delivering a lecture on lithium and never reaching her kidneys or her pregnancy plans.","Forgetting to ask about over-the-counter painkillers.","Saying lithium is 'perfectly safe in pregnancy' or 'must never be used in pregnancy'.","Not explaining that levels are taken 12 hours after the last dose.","Not warning against stopping suddenly."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) (checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: lithium carbonate (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (checked Nov 2026)","4. NPSA Patient Safety Alert: Safer lithium therapy (2009, checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Lithium (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients expect to be told the 'why' as well as the 'what'. Brand matters with lithium: Priadel and Camcolit are not interchangeable without a level check, so always prescribe by brand.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-07","n":1207,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚡","title":"Frightened about ECT: explaining the treatment, memory effects and the alternatives","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 16:00","where":"Acute adult inpatient ward, family room","text":"You are the CT2. Mr Peter Lowe, 66, a widowed retired bus driver, has severe depression. Over five months, sertraline, then venlafaxine with lithium added, have not helped. He was detained under Section 3 ten days ago. For four days he has barely spoken, eaten nothing and taken only sips of fluid; his creatinine is rising. This morning the consultant recommended ECT and judged that Peter lacks capacity to consent to it. His daughter, Ms Joanne Lowe, 39, asks to speak to someone: 'I don't want them giving Dad shock treatment.'"},"task":"Meet his daughter: hear her fears, explain why ECT is recommended, what happens, the side effects including memory, the alternatives and the legal safeguards.","first60":["Find out what she knows and fears about ECT before explaining anything; films often shape the picture.","Name the urgency gently: he is not eating or drinking, and his kidneys are starting to suffer.","Be honest about memory: most effects fade, but some people report lasting gaps.","Explain who decides when he lacks capacity, and that her views genuinely count.","Offer time, a visit to the ECT suite and written information."],"ask":["'What have you heard about ECT?'","'What worries you most about it for your dad?'","'Has your dad ever said what he would want if he became this unwell?'","'Did he ever write anything down, like an advance decision, or give anyone lasting power of attorney?'","'How have you been coping, seeing him like this?'","'What questions would you like me to answer today?'"],"recognise":["Depressive stupor with refusal of food and fluid is a medical emergency: dehydration, kidney injury, clots and pressure damage.","ECT is the fastest and most effective treatment for severe depression with stupor or when medicines have failed.","The fear of ECT is often shaped by films; normalise without dismissing it.","A relative cannot consent or refuse for an adult unless they hold a health and welfare lasting power of attorney, but her views and knowledge of his wishes matter.","For a detained patient who lacks capacity, ECT needs a SOAD certificate (Section 58A) unless an urgent Section 62 situation applies, and cannot conflict with a valid advance decision refusing it.","Relatives often carry guilt ('he'll never forgive me'); take the decision weight off them."],"decide":[{"c":"Explore her fears, explain the reasons, procedure, benefits, risks and alternatives honestly, describe the safeguards, ask about his past wishes, and offer time and a visit.","r":"Respects her role, gives accurate information and gathers what matters for the decision.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her ECT is completely safe and has no lasting effect on memory.","r":"Overstated; some people report lasting memory gaps. False reassurance damages trust.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her the decision is legal and she has no say.","r":"Technically incomplete and needlessly harsh; her views and knowledge of his wishes are part of good practice and the SOAD process.","best":false}],"law":["MHA 1983 Section 58A: ECT for a detained patient who lacks capacity needs a SOAD certificate, and must not conflict with a valid advance decision, a health and welfare attorney, a deputy or the Court of Protection.","MHA 1983 Section 62: ECT may be given urgently only if immediately necessary to save life or prevent serious deterioration.","MCA 2005: a valid advance decision refusing ECT must be respected; a relative cannot consent unless appointed as health and welfare attorney.","MHA Code of Practice: involve carers and relatives in care planning where appropriate; as the eldest child of a widower, Joanne may be his nearest relative under Section 26."],"say":[{"to":"Daughter","s":"It's completely understandable to be frightened. ECT today is very different from what you see in films. He'd be fully asleep under a general anaesthetic, with a muscle relaxant, so his body barely moves."},{"to":"Daughter (memory)","s":"I'll be honest with you. Many people have memory gaps around the weeks of treatment. Most improve over the following weeks, but some say a few memories don't come back."},{"to":"Daughter (guilt)","s":"You are not the one deciding this, and you're not doing this to him. Telling us what he'd want is the most helpful thing you can do."}],"write":"Ward note: met Joanne Lowe (daughter, 39) re proposed ECT.\nHer understanding: 'shock therapy', fears pain, lasting memory loss, harm to heart; feels guilty.\nExplained: indication (depressive stupor, minimal oral intake 4 days, rising creatinine, failure of sertraline and venlafaxine + lithium); procedure; twice weekly, usually 6-12 treatments; short-term effects; memory effects honestly; unilateral placement and cognitive checks; anaesthetic risk; alternatives and risks of not treating; s58A SOAD; s62 if life-threatening.\nPast wishes: never discussed ECT; no advance decision or LPA known. Values his independence and family.\nShe would like to visit the ECT suite and read the leaflet; to speak with consultant tomorrow.\nPlan: inform consultant of her views; SOAD request; physical work-up.","make":["Tell the consultant her views and the absence of any advance decision or LPA.","Request a SOAD under Section 58A; record whether Section 62 criteria are met if intake does not improve.","Physical work-up: U&Es, FBC, LFTs, ECG, fluid balance chart, VTE assessment, anaesthetic review.","Arrange a visit to the ECT suite with the ECT nurse lead.","Give the ECT leaflet and the Royal College of Psychiatrists' information link.","Offer a Carer's assessment through the care coordinator or ward social worker."],"next":"After four treatments, Peter is drinking well and saying a few words, but he asks the same question twice in an afternoon. Joanne rings, alarmed. Acknowledge her worry, review his cognitive checks, discuss right unilateral placement or longer gaps between treatments with the ECT team, and arrange to see her with the consultant.","marks":["Explores her understanding and fears before explaining.","Explains why ECT is recommended now, including the physical risk of not eating or drinking.","Describes the procedure, the number and frequency of treatments, and likely benefit.","Gives an honest account of memory and other side effects, and how they are reduced.","Covers alternatives and the risks of not treating.","Explains capacity, the SOAD safeguard and her role, and asks about his past wishes.","Offers time, a visit, written information and a follow-up meeting."],"pitfalls":["Promising there is no lasting memory loss.","Starting with the law before hearing her fears.","Implying she must consent, or that her views do not matter.","Forgetting to ask about an advance decision or LPA.","Skipping the risk of not treating: dehydration, kidney injury and clots."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE TA59: ECT technology appraisal (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983, Sections 58A and 62, and Code of Practice (2015, checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","5. Royal College of Psychiatrists: ECT information for patients and carers (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, relatives do not give consent for adults, but they expect to be listened to and kept informed. Most ECT clinics are accredited by the Royal College's ECTAS scheme, and families can usually visit the suite beforehand.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-08","n":1208,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧲","title":"He disliked the anaesthetic: is rTMS an option?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT3 in clinic. Mr Graham Fielding, 56, a self-employed electrician, has recurrent depressive disorder. Mirtazapine, escitalopram, amitriptyline and sertraline each helped little. Two years ago a course of ECT as a day patient worked well, but he felt groggy and sick after each anaesthetic and could not drive for two days each time. He now takes trazodone 300 mg at night, and over two months his mood has slipped again. His wife saw that the trust has opened an rTMS clinic. He wants to know whether it could work for him."},"task":"Take a brief history of his mood and past treatments, outline the options, then explain rTMS fully and give a reasoned recommendation.","first60":["Agree the agenda: a few minutes on how he is, then the options, with most time on rTMS.","Confirm there are no thoughts of suicide or self-harm before you move on.","Ask what worked and what didn't about ECT; driving will come up.","Name the options briefly, then give him rTMS in full.","Finish with an honest recommendation and the reasons for it."],"ask":["'How's your mood been over the last couple of months?'","'Have you had any thoughts about ending your life or harming yourself?'","'Which treatments have helped, and which haven't?'","'What was hard about ECT?'","'Do you have any metal in your head, a pacemaker, or have you ever had a fit?'","'How much alcohol do you drink over a typical week?'","'What are you hoping rTMS will do for you?'"],"recognise":["Several failed antidepressants plus a good past response to ECT make this treatment-resistant recurrent depression.","rTMS uses magnetic pulses to stimulate the front of the brain while awake; no anaesthetic and no seizure is intended.","rTMS is less effective than ECT for severe depression but avoids anaesthetic and memory effects; it fits moderate depression in someone well enough to attend daily.","Safety screening matters: metal in the head, implanted devices, a history of seizures, and drugs or alcohol that lower the seizure threshold.","NHS access varies by area; some trusts offer it, others refer out, and waiting lists are common.","Patients who have researched a treatment want it addressed directly; drifting to other options loses them."],"decide":[{"c":"Brief history, short overview of options, a full explanation of rTMS, then a reasoned recommendation and referral.","r":"Answers what he came for while keeping the other options visible.","best":true},{"c":"Spend most of the time on medication changes because rTMS is less proven.","r":"Ignores his question and loses his trust; NICE regards rTMS as an option for depression.","best":false},{"c":"Promise rTMS will work as well as ECT did.","r":"Overstated: response rates are lower than for ECT.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Decision making and consent (2020): discuss reasonable options, including ones the patient raises, with their benefits and risks.","NICE IPG542 (2015): rTMS for depression may be used with normal arrangements for consent, audit and clinical governance.","DVLA: depression alone without significant memory or concentration problems, agitation or suicidal thoughts usually needs no notification; advise him to check the DVLA guidance."],"say":[{"to":"Graham","s":"rTMS uses a magnetic coil held against your head to stimulate the front of the brain, the area involved in mood. You're awake throughout, with no anaesthetic."},{"to":"Graham (efficacy)","s":"Honestly, it's not as powerful as ECT. About four in ten people improve a lot, and about three in ten get fully well. It builds up over the course."},{"to":"Graham (driving)","s":"Most people drive themselves to and from the sessions. Because there's no anaesthetic, you won't have the two-day gap you had with ECT."}],"write":"Clinic letter summary:\nGraham Fielding (56), recurrent depressive disorder, current moderate episode for 2 months.\nNo suicidal thoughts or plans; protective factors: wife, work.\nPast: mirtazapine, escitalopram, amitriptyline, sertraline (limited effect); ECT 2 years ago (good response; post-anaesthetic nausea and grogginess; unable to drive 48 h each time).\nCurrent: trazodone 300 mg nocte.\nOptions discussed: rTMS, augmentation (lithium or aripiprazole), switch to venlafaxine, ECT, CBT.\nSafety screen: no metal in head, no implants, no seizures; alcohol 6 units/week.\nPlan: refer to trust rTMS clinic; continue trazodone; CBT referral; review 4 weeks; crisis numbers given.","make":["Refer to the trust rTMS clinic with the safety screen completed.","Continue trazodone 300 mg at night unchanged during the course.","Discuss lithium or aripiprazole augmentation as a fallback with the consultant.","Refer to NHS Talking Therapies or the CMHT psychologist for CBT.","Review in clinic in 4 weeks; give crisis numbers."],"next":"Three weeks into the course, Graham rings to say his headaches after sessions are bothering him and his mood has not shifted yet. Validate his frustration, advise paracetamol beforehand, ask the rTMS team to review the intensity, and explain that benefit often builds in weeks four to six. Re-check suicidal thoughts.","marks":["Takes a brief history of mood now and confirms no suicidal thoughts.","Elicits past treatments, including ECT's benefit and its downsides.","Outlines the options briefly, then focuses on rTMS.","Explains how rTMS works, its uses, efficacy and time course.","Covers screening, session practicalities and side effects.","Answers medication, driving and top-up questions.","Gives a reasoned recommendation and a clear plan."],"pitfalls":["Spending too long on history and never reaching rTMS in detail.","Repeatedly steering him back to tablets while he asks about rTMS.","Forgetting the safety screen: metal, implants and seizures.","Overstating efficacy, or implying it works like ECT.","Not answering the driving question that matters most to him."],"refs":["1. NICE IPG542 Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation for depression (2015, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: trazodone (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: rTMS information (checked Nov 2026)","5. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, patients often arrive having read about a treatment online. Engage with what they have found rather than redirecting; access to rTMS on the NHS varies by region, so find out your trust's referral route.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-09","n":1209,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👂","title":"Saying 'odd things' after leaving university: eliciting first-rank symptoms","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:00","where":"Early intervention in psychosis (EIP) team, community clinic","text":"You are the CT1 in the EIP team. Mr Callum Reid, 25, left his engineering degree a year ago and has become withdrawn at his mother's home. His GP referred him after his mother said he was 'saying odd things', such as that the neighbours 'know his business'. He has agreed to come in alone. The care coordinator will cover risk and the wider history later today. Your job now is to find out precisely which first-rank symptoms are present."},"task":"Elicit each first-rank symptom in turn with precise questions, separating true symptoms from look-alikes, and summarise what you found.","first60":["Start open: let him tell you what has been happening before any checklist.","Use his own words back to him; they open doors to the precise questions.","Work through the groups: voices, thoughts, control of body and will, and sudden meanings.","For each symptom, check that it is truly from outside him, not just an unwanted thought of his own.","Stay curious and non-judgemental; do not argue with the beliefs."],"ask":["'Do you ever hear your own thoughts spoken out loud, as if someone else were saying them?'","'Do you hear voices talking to each other about you, calling you he?'","'Do the voices describe what you are doing while you do it?'","'Have thoughts come into your head that you feel are not your own, that were put there?'","'Have your thoughts ever been taken out of your head, so your mind suddenly goes empty?'","'Do you feel your thoughts leave your head so that other people can know them, without you saying anything?'","'Does any force or person outside you control your movements, feelings or urges?'","'Did something ordinary you saw suddenly reveal a special meaning to you?'"],"recognise":["First-rank symptoms are not proof of schizophrenia: they occur in mania, delirium and drug-induced psychosis. ICD-11 no longer gives them special weight.","Thought insertion means the thought is felt to be someone else's; obsessional thoughts are unwanted but recognised as one's own.","Thought withdrawal is a sudden removal by an outside force, not ordinary losing of one's train of thought.","Broadcast means thoughts are felt to escape so others know them; 'people can read my face' is not the same.","Commentary and discussion are in the third person (he, him); second-person voices are psychotic but not first-rank.","Delusional perception has two steps: a normal perception, then a sudden, unrelated delusional meaning."],"decide":[{"c":"Begin open, then ask precise questions for each symptom group and check the outside origin of each experience.","r":"Finds the symptoms reliably and separates them from look-alikes.","best":true},{"c":"Read down a checklist of closed questions from the start.","r":"Feels like an interrogation, invites yes-saying and misses his own descriptions.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him the neighbours cannot really know his thoughts.","r":"Arguing with a delusion ends rapport and adds nothing to the assessment.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): he is an adult; ask before sharing details with his mother, though you can always listen to her.","NHS England EIP access standard: people with suspected first-episode psychosis should start a NICE-recommended care package within two weeks of referral.","Mental Health Act 1983 is not relevant while he is engaging voluntarily and risk is assessed as manageable."],"say":[{"to":"Callum","s":"Thank you for telling me. Some of my questions may sound unusual, but I ask everyone them, because these experiences are more common than people think."},{"to":"Callum (insertion)","s":"When those thoughts arrive, do they feel like your own thoughts that you don't want, or like thoughts that belong to someone else and were put into your head?"},{"to":"Callum (summary)","s":"Can I check I've understood? You hear voices discussing you and describing what you do, and your thoughts feel put in, taken out and known by others."}],"write":"EIP assessment, first-rank symptoms (Callum Reid, 25):\nThought echo: present (hears own thoughts repeated aloud from the vent).\nVoices discussing him in third person: present (two male voices).\nRunning commentary: present ('he's going to the kitchen now').\nThought insertion: present (thoughts 'from the network', not his).\nThought withdrawal: present (thoughts 'pulled out mid-sentence').\nThought broadcast: present (neighbours know thoughts without speech).\nMade acts: present (right hand made to tap). Made feelings/impulses: denied.\nSomatic passivity: present (buzzing in stomach 'put there' by machines).\nDelusional perception: present (saw a red van outside, knew at once he was chosen for an experiment).\nTo care coordinator for risk, substance use and full history.","make":["Hand over findings to the care coordinator for risk, substance use and collateral history.","Physical screen for first episode: bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, glucose or HbA1c, lipids), weight, BP and ECG before any antipsychotic.","Urine drug screen, with his consent.","Discuss with the EIP consultant today to agree the working diagnosis and treatment options.","With his consent, update his mother."],"next":"At the end, Callum asks, 'So am I schizophrenic?' Acknowledge his question, explain that the experiences point to a psychosis, that the team needs a fuller picture before naming a diagnosis, and that EIP care usually helps people recover well. Arrange the consultant review.","marks":["Opens with free narrative before precise questions.","Asks about thought echo, third-person discussion and running commentary.","Asks about thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcast, checking outside origin.","Asks about made feelings, impulses and acts, and somatic passivity.","Elicits delusional perception, including its two steps.","Stays non-judgemental and validates his distress.","Summarises findings and explains next steps."],"pitfalls":["Firing a closed checklist from the first minute.","Accepting 'yes' without checking that the experience feels externally caused.","Counting second-person voices as running commentary.","Confusing delusional perception with delusion that follows a hallucination.","Arguing with his beliefs."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6A20 Schizophrenia (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England: Early intervention in psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Schizophrenia information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: the wording of the question decides the answer. Questions like 'Do you hear voices?' are too blunt; describe the experience in plain words and then ask for an example. Patients in the UK expect explanations of why you ask.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-10","n":1210,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🧬","title":"'Will my baby get schizophrenia?': genetic risk counselling at 12 weeks","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 15:00","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in the CMHT. Mrs Hannah Rahman, 27, has had schizophrenia for six years and has been well for two. She is 12 weeks pregnant with her first baby. She rang her care coordinator in tears this morning: she has read that schizophrenia is 'genetic' and is thinking of ending the pregnancy so she does not pass it on. She asked to see a doctor today. Her husband, Imran, is at work and does not know she is here."},"task":"Explain the causes of schizophrenia and the risk to her baby in clear numbers, support her decision about the pregnancy without steering it, and plan her perinatal care.","first60":["Reassure her about confidentiality at the start; she has come alone and is frightened.","Ask what she has read and what she fears most before giving any facts.","Give the risk in both directions: about 13 in 100 children with one affected parent develop it, so 87 in 100 do not.","Make clear the decision about the pregnancy is hers, and offer real support whatever she decides.","Do not forget her own health: ask about medication, symptoms and safety."],"ask":["'What have you read or heard about schizophrenia and families?'","'What worries you most for your baby?'","'How do you feel about the pregnancy itself?'","'Does anyone else in your or Imran's family have a mental illness?'","'Are you taking any medication at the moment?'","'How have your sleep and thoughts been? Any of the old experiences coming back?'","'Any thoughts lately of hurting yourself?'"],"recognise":["Schizophrenia is not caused by a single gene; many genes each add a small vulnerability, which interacts with environment.","Lifetime risk is about 1 in 100 in the general population, about 1 in 8 (13 in 100) with one affected parent, and about 4 to 5 in 10 if both parents are affected.","No genetic, blood or prenatal test can predict whether a child will develop schizophrenia.","Cannabis (especially high-potency), childhood adversity, obstetric complications and migration add risk; some of these can be influenced.","Women with schizophrenia have a raised risk of relapse in pregnancy and especially after birth; stopping medication adds to it.","The decision about the pregnancy is hers; your role is accurate information, non-directive support and confidentiality."],"decide":[{"c":"Hear her fear, explain the causes and numbers clearly, confirm there is no test, support her decision without steering, and refer to the perinatal team.","r":"Gives accurate information, respects her autonomy and protects her mental health.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her the risk is tiny and she has nothing to worry about.","r":"False reassurance: the risk is raised about thirteenfold, though most children are unaffected.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to continue the pregnancy because termination is against her religion.","r":"Directive and outside your role; the decision is hers, and her values are for her to weigh.","best":false}],"law":["Abortion Act 1967 (England, Wales and Scotland): termination is usually possible up to 24 weeks when two doctors agree the criteria are met; she can self-refer to NHS-funded services.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): what she discusses is confidential; her family or husband will not be told without her consent unless there is a serious risk.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): do not express your personal beliefs in ways that could cause her distress or pressure her decision.","MCA 2005: she is presumed to have capacity to make this decision."],"say":[{"to":"Hannah","s":"Schizophrenia isn't passed on by one gene. What's inherited is a slightly higher chance, not the illness itself."},{"to":"Hannah (numbers)","s":"In the general population about 1 in 100 people develop it. With one parent who has it, it's about 13 in 100. That means 87 in 100 children like yours won't."},{"to":"Hannah (decision)","s":"This is your decision, and only yours. Whatever you decide, we'll support you, and nothing you tell me will be shared with your family without your agreement."}],"write":"CMHT clinic note: Hannah Rahman (27), schizophrenia, 12/40 pregnant, first pregnancy.\nConcern: fears passing on schizophrenia; considering termination; conflict with faith; fears family reaction; husband unaware of visit.\nExplained: polygenic vulnerability; lifetime risk ~1% general, ~13% one parent (husband no psychiatric history); no predictive or prenatal test; environmental factors including cannabis.\nDecision: hers; non-directive; information on NHS pregnancy counselling and self-referral given; confidentiality assured.\nMental state: no psychotic symptoms; sleeping well; low anxiety-related mood; no thoughts of self-harm.\nMedication: stopped aripiprazole 6 months ago without telling team.\nPlan: urgent perinatal team referral; perinatal psychiatrist to review medication; inform GP and midwife with consent; review 1 week; crisis numbers.","make":["Refer to the specialist perinatal community mental health team (urgent, as she is off medication and early in pregnancy).","Perinatal psychiatrist to discuss restarting an antipsychotic, weighing relapse risk against risks in pregnancy.","With consent, inform her GP and midwife; record the mental health history in her maternity notes.","Give contact details for NHS-funded pregnancy counselling and self-referral, without pressure either way.","Plan a pre-birth planning meeting at about 32 weeks if the pregnancy continues.","Care coordinator to review her weekly for now."],"next":"A week later Hannah has decided to continue the pregnancy. She tells you she has started sleeping badly and feels people at the mosque are 'looking at her differently'. Treat this as a possible early relapse: assess her mental state the same day, involve the perinatal psychiatrist urgently about restarting medication, and agree an early warning plan with her and, with consent, Imran.","marks":["Assures confidentiality and explores her fears before explaining.","Explains that schizophrenia is polygenic and that vulnerability, not illness, is inherited.","Gives the risk in numbers both ways and states there is no predictive test.","Explains other causes, including ones that can be influenced.","Supports her decision without steering it and offers counselling routes.","Asks about her medication, symptoms and safety.","Plans perinatal referral, medication review and follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Giving false reassurance that the risk is the same as anyone else's.","Steering her towards or away from termination.","Lecturing on genetics while she is visibly distressed.","Not asking about her medication and relapse risk.","Forgetting the perinatal team referral."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, checked Nov 2026)","3. Abortion Act 1967 (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Confidentiality (2017) and Good medical practice (2024, checked Nov 2026)","5. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Mental health in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK practice is non-directive about pregnancy decisions; your personal or cultural views on termination must not shape the advice. Every area has a specialist perinatal mental health team, and women with schizophrenia should be referred early.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-13","n":1213,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🥋","title":"His manager wants the Ritalin stopped: explain adult ADHD within consent, protect the treatment, agree adjustments","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 16:00","where":"Adult ADHD clinic, outpatient department","text":"Tom Reid, a 19-year-old man, has had ADHD since age 7 and takes methylphenidate XL 36 mg each morning. He works as an apprentice coach at a judo academy and has been picked for the national championships. The governing body has told him methylphenidate is banned in competition. His manager and coach, Mr Steve Morgan, 45, has come with Tom's written consent and says, 'He needs to come off the Ritalin today.'"},"task":"Explain adult ADHD and its treatment to Tom's manager within the limits of Tom's consent, answer his questions, advise against stopping, explain the exemption route and suggest reasonable workplace adjustments.","first60":["Read Tom's consent form: what he agreed to share (diagnosis, treatment, competition) and what he did not.","Check the history: diagnosis at 7, current residual symptoms, atomoxetine at 16 (worse symptoms, raised liver enzymes).","Know the facts: methylphenidate is prohibited in competition; a Therapeutic Use Exemption (TUE) is the legal route.","Ask Tom whether he would like to join at the end, and confirm he is in the waiting room.","Have the UK Anti-Doping TUE page and an Access to Work leaflet ready."],"ask":["'Tom has agreed I can talk about his ADHD, his treatment and the competition. What would be most useful for you?'","'What have you been told by the governing body?'","'What do you notice about Tom at work and in training?'","'What worries you most if he stays on the medicine, and if he stops?'","'What would help him at work: what goes wrong, and when?'"],"recognise":["Adult ADHD: inattention (careless mistakes, missed instructions), overactivity (restless, talks a lot) and impulsivity (interrupting, temper) still present since childhood.","Stopping methylphenidate is not dangerous in itself, but symptoms return within a day: focus, impulse control, safety on the mat and on the road all suffer.","Atomoxetine, the main non-stimulant, made Tom worse and affected his liver; the other first-line medicines are also stimulants and also banned in competition.","Methylphenidate restores focus to an ordinary level in ADHD; it is prohibited in competition, but a TUE allows prescribed use.","Herbal 'calming' products have no good evidence and supplements can contain banned substances; the athlete is responsible for anything in his body.","Confidentiality: listen to new information (such as drug use) but disclose nothing beyond Tom's consent."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise Tom to stay on methylphenidate, apply for a TUE now with a specialist report, steer away from herbal products, suggest workplace adjustments, and keep anything outside consent private.","r":"Right: the treatment that works is protected, he has a lawful route to compete, and Tom's confidentiality holds.","best":true},{"c":"Stop methylphenidate now and restart atomoxetine before the championships.","r":"Wrong: atomoxetine made him worse and affected his liver at 16. His symptoms would return with no effective treatment.","best":false},{"c":"Write a letter today certifying Tom is allowed to compete on methylphenidate.","r":"Not yours to give: only the anti-doping body grants an exemption. Your role is the medical evidence for the application.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): share only what the patient has consented to; you may listen to information from others without disclosing anything back.","UK GDPR: health information is special category data; sharing with an employer needs explicit consent.","Equality Act 2010: ADHD can count as a disability if it has a substantial, long-term effect on daily life; employers must then make reasonable adjustments.","UK Anti-Doping and the WADA Prohibited List: stimulants including methylphenidate are prohibited in competition; prescribed use needs a TUE."],"say":[{"to":"Manager","s":"I can see how much you've invested in Tom, and how frustrating this news is. I'd strongly advise against stopping his medicine. There's a proper route to let him compete on it."},{"to":"Manager","s":"Tom's agreed I can talk about his ADHD, treatment and the competition. Anything else about his life I can't discuss, but I'm happy to listen."},{"to":"Tom (afterwards)","s":"Steve raised a worry about drug use at parties. I haven't shared anything about you. Can we talk about it, just us?"}],"write":"Adult ADHD clinic. Meeting with Mr S. Morgan (45, employer and coach) with Tom's written consent (dated today) limited to: diagnosis, treatment, competition and work adjustments.\nTom: 19M, ADHD (combined) since age 7. Methylphenidate XL 36 mg mane. Atomoxetine age 16: worse symptoms and raised ALT; stopped.\nDiscussed: basis of diagnosis; effects of stopping; why alternatives unsuitable; herbal product not advised; methylphenidate prohibited in competition; TUE process with specialist evidence; reasonable adjustments.\nMr Morgan raised third-party information about recreational drug use; listened, nothing disclosed. To discuss with Tom privately.\nPlan: continue methylphenidate XL 36 mg. Consultant report for TUE this week; Tom to apply via UKAD now. Adjustments letter to employer (with Tom's consent). Access to Work information given. Tom joined at end and agreed plan. Review 4/52.\nDr A. Bello ST5","make":["Open by stating the consent boundaries out loud, so the manager knows what you can and cannot discuss.","Give the diagnosis using his own observations: mistakes, missed instructions, restlessness, temper.","Be firm and friendly: stopping is not advised; give reasons, not just 'no'.","Explain the TUE: it is the lawful way to compete on a prescribed medicine; apply early with specialist evidence.","Turn his workplace frustrations into concrete adjustments.","Receive the drug disclosure without confirming or denying anything, then raise it with Tom."],"next":"Tom applies for a TUE with the consultant's report and it is granted three weeks before the championships. His manager puts written task lists and short check-ins in place. Tom tells the clinic privately he has used ecstasy twice and agrees to stop before competing.","marks":["Stated the limits of Tom's consent at the start.","Explained why this is adult ADHD, using the manager's observations.","Advised firmly against stopping and gave reasons.","Explained why other medicines and herbal remedies are not suitable.","Explained the TUE route and the specialist evidence needed.","Kept confidentiality when drug use was raised."],"pitfalls":["Agreeing to stop methylphenidate because a competition is coming.","Discussing anything outside Tom's consent, such as his past or his drug use.","Promising a 'certificate' that allows him to compete.","Calling the medicine a performance enhancer, or dismissing the rules.","Forgetting the work side: adjustments, Access to Work, occupational health."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87 ADHD guideline (updated 2019; checked Nov 2026)","2. UK Anti-Doping: Therapeutic Use Exemptions (checked Nov 2026)","3. WADA Prohibited List, section S6 Stimulants (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017)","5. GOV.UK: Access to Work; Equality Act 2010 guidance on reasonable adjustments (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK an adult patient's employer has no right to clinical information. Always check written consent and its limits first. Access to Work is a government grant that can pay for workplace support, including ADHD coaching.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-15","n":1215,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"'They think she's making it up': explain functional seizures to her father","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 11:20","where":"Neuropsychiatry outpatient clinic, general hospital","text":"Ms Chloe Turner, a 26-year-old woman, has had seizures for a year, now several a week. Video-EEG telemetry recorded three of her usual attacks with normal brain activity, and neurology told her they were 'non-epileptic' and 'psychogenic'. She refused today's appointment, saying the doctors think she is faking. Her father, Mr Graham Turner, 58, has come instead. Chloe has agreed by phone that we can discuss her diagnosis and treatment with him."},"task":"Explain functional (dissociative) seizures to her father without blame, address the 'faking' fear, answer his questions, and outline treatment, outlook and safety advice.","first60":["Read the neurology letter and telemetry report: typical attacks captured, EEG normal throughout, any epilepsy as well.","Confirm Chloe's consent in the notes and its scope: diagnosis and treatment.","Check her medicines: she was started on levetiracetam before telemetry; neurology plans to taper it.","Note the GP history of mild anxiety and depression, and the bereavement 18 months ago.","Print the neurosymptoms.org link and the NHS page on functional neurological disorder to give him."],"ask":["'What has Chloe told you about what the neurologists said?'","'What worries you most?'","'What are the attacks like, and when do they happen?'","'How has Chloe been in herself: mood, worry, sleep?'","'Has she ever talked about harming herself or wishing you weren't here?'","'What has been going on in the family over the last couple of years?'","'What would help Chloe feel able to come and talk to us?'"],"recognise":["Functional or dissociative seizures are real, involuntary attacks caused by a problem in how the brain works, not by abnormal electrical activity.","The diagnosis rests on video-EEG: her typical attacks were recorded with normal brain activity throughout. It is a positive diagnosis, not a 'nothing found'.","They are common: around one in five people referred to specialist seizure clinics have them.","Anti-epileptic drugs do not help; they cause side effects and should be tapered by neurology, not stopped suddenly.","Stress and loss can be part of the picture, though not always; her mother's death and an avoidant coping style may be relevant.","Outlook is better when the diagnosis is understood and accepted early; feeling disbelieved is the biggest barrier."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain functional seizures as real and involuntary, give the evidence, offer specialist psychological therapy, plan the anti-epileptic taper with neurology, give attack first aid and invite Chloe in on her terms.","r":"Right: a clear, blame-free, positive diagnosis with a treatment path and a way back for Chloe.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him the attacks are 'psychological' and she needs to learn to control them.","r":"Harmful: it confirms her fear of being disbelieved. The attacks are not under conscious control.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to stop levetiracetam today because she does not have epilepsy.","r":"Unsafe: stopping suddenly can cause problems. Neurology should plan the taper, and confirm there is no epilepsy as well.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): Chloe has capacity and has consented to her father hearing about her diagnosis and treatment; keep to that scope.","GMC Good medical practice: give information in a way the person can understand, and avoid stigmatising language.","DVLA: people with seizures of any kind must follow DVLA guidance on driving; her neurologist advises on her situation.","Make reasonable efforts to engage a patient who has declined an appointment; do not discharge her for not attending."],"say":[{"to":"Father","s":"Let me be really clear: Chloe isn't making this up. These attacks are real and she isn't doing them on purpose. They're common, and they can get better."},{"to":"Father","s":"Think of the brain as a computer. The hardware is fine, which is why the tests were normal, but the software is crashing. That's what a functional seizure is."},{"to":"Chloe (letter)","s":"We believe your attacks are real and not under your control. We'd like to explain the diagnosis and talk about treatment that helps. You can come with your dad, or we can talk by video."}],"write":"Neuropsychiatry clinic. Seen Mr G. Turner (father, 58). Chloe (26F) declined to attend; consent by phone 10:50 today to share diagnosis and treatment with father (documented).\nBackground: 12-month history of attacks, now several a week, first before a job interview. Video-EEG: 3 typical attacks, EEG normal throughout. Neurology diagnosis: functional/dissociative seizures. On levetiracetam 500 mg BD (neurology plan to taper). Mother died of metastatic breast cancer 18 months ago. Past mild anxiety and depression (GP).\nCollateral: more anxious and withdrawn since mother's death; poor sleep. No known self-harm or suicidal talk.\nExplained: real, involuntary, common; how diagnosis made; mind-body link; AEDs do not help; treatment; outlook; first aid. Father relieved; teach-back correct.\nPlan: letter to Chloe offering appointment with father or video call; neurosymptoms.org; psychology referral (functional seizures therapy); screen mood at assessment; neurology to taper levetiracetam; Cruse details for both. GP copied. Review 4/52.\nDr L. Huang ST4","make":["Start with what he has heard and what he fears; the 'faking' fear must be answered early and plainly.","Say how the diagnosis was made: her own attacks, filmed, with normal brain waves at the same time.","Use an everyday mind-body example: blushing, a racing heart before a speech, or the computer software analogy.","Avoid 'pseudo', 'psychogenic', 'hysterical' and 'medically unexplained'.","Ask about her mood and safety through him, and about the bereavement, gently.","End with a way back for Chloe: her choice of format, written information, no pressure."],"next":"Chloe reads neurosymptoms.org and agrees to a video call. She says, 'At least someone believes me.' She starts 12 sessions of therapy for functional seizures. Neurology tapers levetiracetam. After four months her attacks are down to two a month, and she and her father have started bereavement support.","marks":["Answered the 'faking' fear early and clearly.","Explained how the diagnosis was made.","Gave a clear mind-body explanation without jargon.","Asked about her mood, risk and possible stressors.","Explained treatment, anti-epileptic taper and outlook.","Gave first-aid advice and a way to engage Chloe."],"pitfalls":["Using 'pseudoseizures', 'psychogenic' or 'it's all in her head'.","Implying she can stop the attacks if she tries.","Advising her to stop anti-epileptic medicine suddenly.","Skipping her mood and risk because she is not in the room.","Leaving no route back for Chloe."],"refs":["1. NHS website: Functional neurological disorder (FND) (checked Nov 2026)","2. neurosymptoms.org: Functional and dissociative seizures (patient information, checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Functional neurological disorder information (checked Nov 2026)","4. Goldstein LH et al. CODES trial of CBT for dissociative seizures. Lancet Psychiatry 2020","5. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the preferred terms are 'functional seizures' or 'dissociative seizures'. Words like 'pseudoseizures' or 'hysterical' are seen as offensive and damage trust.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-16","n":1216,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Periods have stopped on risperidone: prolactin 1,100","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20","where":"Outpatient clinic room, community mental health team base","text":"You are the CT2 in the community mental health team. Ms Rachel Dunmore, a 37-year-old woman with schizophrenia, has been well for 14 months on risperidone 6 mg at night; olanzapine was stopped three years ago after she gained 18 kg. Last month she told you her periods had become irregular, so you checked bloods. Her prolactin is 1,100 mIU/L (lab upper limit about 500); her level before risperidone was normal. She does not yet know the result."},"task":"Take a focused history of the effects of high prolactin, exclude pregnancy, other drugs and a pituitary cause, explain the result in plain words, and agree a management plan with her.","first60":["Read the result: the value, the lab's reference range, and her baseline prolactin before risperidone.","Check the drug chart and GP record for other prolactin-raising drugs (metoclopramide, domperidone, antidepressants, oral contraceptives).","Have a urine pregnancy test kit and blood forms ready.","Check her latest mental state and relapse signature in the notes.","Introduce yourself and ask what she understood the blood test was for."],"ask":["'Tell me what's been happening with your periods.'","'Any breast tenderness, swelling, or milky fluid from the nipples?'","'Some people lose interest in sex on this medicine. Has that happened to you?'","'Could you be pregnant at the moment?'","'Do you take anything else, including contraception, sickness tablets, antidepressants or herbal remedies?'","'Any headaches, or any change in your vision, especially at the edges?'","'Are you hoping to have children, and when?'"],"recognise":["Risperidone, amisulpride and paliperidone raise prolactin most; aripiprazole, quetiapine and clozapine hardly do.","Symptoms in women: oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea, low libido, breast tenderness, galactorrhoea, painful sex, acne and hirsutism, infertility.","In men: low libido, erectile problems, breast enlargement, reduced ejaculate, reduced fertility.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding raise prolactin; always exclude pregnancy before blaming the drug.","Hypothyroidism, chronic kidney disease, PCOS and other drugs can all raise prolactin.","Headache, visual field loss or a very high level (thousands) suggests a pituitary tumour: refer to endocrinology for MRI."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm with a repeat level and screening bloods, exclude pregnancy, then offer her a choice between adding aripiprazole, switching, or a lower dose.","r":"She is symptomatic and wants a baby; a shared, gradual change protects both her fertility and her mental health.","best":true},{"c":"Stop risperidone today because it is causing the problem.","r":"She has been well on it for 14 months; abrupt withdrawal risks relapse. Any change should be planned and gradual.","best":false},{"c":"Start cabergoline to bring the prolactin down.","r":"Dopamine agonists can worsen psychosis and are for endocrinology to start, only after antipsychotic options fail.","best":false}],"law":["Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board (2015): she must be told the material risks of each option, including staying on risperidone, so she can choose.","MCA 2005: she has capacity for this decision unless shown otherwise; respect her preference to keep risperidone if informed.","GMC Decision making and consent (2020): share options, benefits and harms in a way she understands, and record the discussion."],"say":[{"to":"Rachel, on the result","s":"Prolactin is a hormone that makes breast milk. When it's high, it switches periods off and lowers sex drive. Yours is about twice the top of normal, and risperidone is the most likely cause."},{"to":"Rachel, on her fear of a tumour","s":"With no headaches or sight changes, and a level like this on risperidone, a tumour is very unlikely. If it stays high after we change things, we'd arrange a scan."},{"to":"Rachel, on fertility","s":"As the prolactin comes down, you can become fertile again quite quickly. Use condoms until you've decided you're ready, and we'll get you perinatal advice first."}],"write":"Outpatient letter / notes entry:\nProlactin 1,100 mIU/L (ref < 500); baseline normal pre-risperidone.\nSymptoms: oligomenorrhoea since summer, amenorrhoea 6 weeks, breast tenderness, low libido. No galactorrhoea, headache or visual change.\nNo other medicines. Pregnancy unlikely; urine hCG negative today.\nImpression: antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinaemia, symptomatic.\nOptions discussed (switch, add aripiprazole, reduce, monitor) with risks. Patient chose to add aripiprazole 5 mg mane; plan agreed with Dr Moss (consultant).\nBloods: repeat prolactin (rested; lab to screen for macroprolactin), TFTs, U&E.\nAdvised: barrier contraception until ready to conceive; folic acid 400 micrograms when trying. Perinatal mental health preconception referral sent.\nReview 2 weeks; prolactin at 3 months. GP letter today.","make":["Ask what she knows, then take the symptom history: periods, breasts, libido, skin.","Exclude pregnancy, other drugs, thyroid and kidney disease.","Ask about headache and vision.","Explain prolactin, her level, and the risperidone link.","Explain why it is worth treating: bones, fertility, possible breast cancer link.","Offer four options and let her choose.","Plan pregnancy advice, bloods, follow-up and safety-netting; check understanding."],"next":"Her pregnancy test is negative. She starts aripiprazole 5 mg each morning. At two weeks she is well with no restlessness. At three months her prolactin is 420 mIU/L and her periods have returned. The perinatal team sees her to plan a single medicine before she tries to conceive.","marks":["Asked about periods, breasts, galactorrhoea and libido.","Excluded pregnancy and arranged a test.","Checked other prolactin-raising drugs and thyroid or kidney disease.","Asked about headache and visual change.","Explained the result and the risperidone link without jargon.","Offered options, including continuing, and respected her choice.","Covered fertility returning, contraception and preconception advice."],"pitfalls":["Blaming the drug before excluding pregnancy.","Stopping risperidone abruptly in a well patient.","Starting a dopamine agonist yourself.","Forgetting that falling prolactin restores fertility.","Rushing past the embarrassment about sex and periods."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 on psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, updated; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: risperidone and aripiprazole monographs (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG192. Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Antipsychotics: information for patients (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website. Folic acid in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients expect to be asked plainly about sex, periods and pregnancy, and to choose between options. Say why you are asking, use everyday words, and offer the choice of continuing as one real option."},
{"id":"c-s-17","n":1217,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💉","title":"Refusing tablets: explaining depot injections to his father","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:00","where":"Family room, acute adult inpatient ward","text":"You are the CT1 on the ward. Daniel Price, a 25-year-old man who has paranoid schizophrenia, was admitted four days ago under Section 3 with his third relapse this year. He believes the police are after him and that his father is helping them. He has refused all medication since admission. At home his father found unopened boxes of risperidone 6 mg; when Daniel takes it, he is well with no side effects. His father, Mr Martin Price, 58, has asked if there is an alternative to tablets. Daniel has said staff can discuss his treatment with his father."},"task":"Explain long-acting injectable antipsychotics to his father: what they are, the choices, the schedule, test doses, side effects and monitoring, and how consent, Section 3 and a CTO apply. Answer his questions.","first60":["Confirm in the notes that Daniel agreed his father can be told about his treatment.","Check Daniel's drug history: risperidone response, side effects, recent bloods and ECG.","Check his legal status (Section 3 start date) and when medication was first given in this detention.","Find a quiet room and have a depot information leaflet ready.","Introduce yourself and ask what Mr Price already knows and wants to know."],"ask":["'What have you heard about injections so far?'","'What happens with his tablets at home?'","'How is he when he takes them? Any side effects?'","'Why do you think he stops?'","'What worries you most about an injection?'","'How are you coping with all this?'"],"recognise":["Repeated relapse after stopping tablets, with good response when taken, is the main reason to offer a depot.","Lack of insight is the commonest reason for covert non-adherence; it is not defiance.","A depot works exactly like the oral drug but releases from muscle over weeks.","If he responded to risperidone, paliperidone (its active metabolite) is the natural choice.","Once given, a depot cannot be removed; side effects can last weeks, so prior oral tolerance matters.","The father may feel guilty about 'forcing' his son; validate before giving information."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain a depot in plain words, link the choice to his risperidone response, and be honest about Section 3 and a CTO.","r":"The father needs accurate information to support Daniel; honesty about the law prevents later mistrust.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him an injection can be given at home if Daniel refuses.","r":"False: no one can give an injection at home against his will. A CTO allows recall to hospital, not forced treatment at home.","best":false},{"c":"Decline to discuss the plan until Daniel agrees to see his father.","r":"Daniel has agreed his father may be told about treatment; withholding helps no one.","best":false}],"law":["MHA 1983 Section 58: medication for a detained patient beyond three months from the first dose in the detention needs consent with capacity (Form T2) or a SOAD certificate (Form T3).","MHA Code of Practice (2015): seek consent at every stage, even within the first three months; record capacity and the discussion.","MHA Sections 17A–17E: a CTO can include a condition to accept medication; if he refuses and it is needed, the RC can recall him to hospital for up to 72 hours.","Part 4A MHA: a CTO patient with capacity who refuses cannot be treated in the community.","GMC Confidentiality (2017) and the Care Act 2014: share with consent, listen to carers, and offer a carer's assessment."],"say":[{"to":"Mr Price, on what a depot is","s":"It's the same kind of medicine as the tablet, given into a muscle. It releases slowly over weeks, so Daniel doesn't have to remember a tablet every day."},{"to":"Mr Price, on the law","s":"On Section 3, for the first three months his consultant can authorise treatment if he refuses. We'll always try for his agreement first."},{"to":"Mr Price, on refusal at home","s":"Nobody can inject him at home against his will. A Community Treatment Order can make it a condition, and he can be recalled to hospital if he refuses and becomes unwell."}],"write":"Notes entry:\nMet Mr Martin Price (father) with Daniel's consent.\nHistory: third relapse this year after stopping risperidone 6 mg; good response, no side effects when adherent.\nExplained: depot principle; paliperidone palmitate as first choice (150 mg day 1, 100 mg day 8 deltoid, then monthly; 3-monthly option); alternatives; test doses; side effects; monitoring.\nExplained Section 3, Section 58 three-month rule and SOAD; CTO and recall.\nUnderstanding checked; depot leaflet given. Carer's assessment offered. For consultant discussion with Daniel at ward round Thursday.","make":["Confirm consent to share; ask what he knows and what worries him.","Establish the adherence pattern and past response.","Explain what a depot is.","Explain the choice and schedule, tied to his risperidone response.","Explain test doses, side effects and monitoring.","Explain consent, Section 3 and a CTO honestly.","Check understanding, give a leaflet, offer a carer's assessment and a family meeting."],"next":"At ward round Daniel refuses an injection, then agrees two days later after being offered a choice of arm. He has paliperidone 150 mg on day 1 and 100 mg on day 8. He is discharged on a CTO with a condition to accept monthly injections from his care coordinator at home.","marks":["Confirmed consent to share and asked what the father knew.","Established adherence, past response and reason for stopping.","Explained a depot without jargon.","Linked the choice to his risperidone response and gave the schedule.","Explained test doses, side effects and monitoring.","Explained Section 3, the three-month rule and a CTO accurately.","Checked understanding and offered a leaflet, carer's assessment and follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Saying a CTO means injections can be forced at home.","Calling it a stronger drug, or a punishment.","Forgetting that a depot cannot be removed once given.","Lecturing without checking what the father understood.","Ignoring his guilt about going behind his son's back."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 on psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, updated; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: paliperidone, risperidone, aripiprazole, flupentixol monographs (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters 24 and 29 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Depot medication: information for patients and carers (checked Nov 2026)","5. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK a CTO does not allow treatment to be forced at home; it allows recall to hospital. Families often expect more power than the law gives, so be precise."},
{"id":"c-s-18","n":1218,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Stopped going out: home visit history to diagnose agoraphobia","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00","where":"Patient's home, a terraced house in town","text":"You are the CT2 in the community mental health team. The GP has referred Mr Kofi Mensah, a 57-year-old man who works from home as an IT consultant, after his daughter Abena said that over two months he has become withdrawn, stopped going shopping and hardly leaves the house. He has no psychiatric history and takes no medication. You are visiting him at home."},"task":"Take a history to reach a diagnosis of agoraphobia, separate it from panic disorder, depression, psychosis and other anxiety disorders, and assess risk, including self-neglect.","first60":["Read the GP letter: physical history, medication, any blood results.","Tell the team base your visit times and carry a charged phone (lone working).","Accept the tea: in his home, rapport comes before questions.","Expect minimising; plan to normalise early.","Open with his daughter's worry, then ask what has changed."],"ask":["'Has anything changed in the last couple of months?'","'What happened in your body at the time? What went through your mind?'","'Which places bring that feeling back?'","'What's the worst thing you fear might happen there?'","'Have you had an attack out of the blue, at home?'","'Any sense those people are watching or following you on purpose?'","'How are you managing for food, washing and laundry?'"],"recognise":["Agoraphobia: fear in several situations where escape is hard or help unavailable (transport, shops, crowds, queues, being out alone), with avoidance.","The feared outcome is usually panic, collapse or embarrassment, not attack by others.","Panic disorder needs recurrent unexpected attacks; attacks only in feared places point to agoraphobia.","A realistic fear of one group is not paranoia; test whether he thinks they target him.","Anxiety disorders cluster; screen for PTSD, social anxiety, OCD and depression.","ICD-11 expects symptoms for several months; at two months, call it provisional."],"decide":[{"c":"Normalise his embarrassment, map the panic and avoidance, then screen the differentials and self-neglect.","r":"He minimises out of shame; once he talks, the diagnosis depends on what is ruled out as much as what is found.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose depression because he is withdrawn and has stopped shopping.","r":"His mood, enjoyment, sleep and appetite are normal at home; withdrawal is avoidance, not low mood.","best":false},{"c":"Treat his fear of the youths as paranoid and screen for psychosis only.","r":"The event was real; his fear is of panic and collapse, not of being targeted. Still screen, but do not assume psychosis.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005: he is presumed to have capacity; self-neglect from fear does not by itself mean he lacks it.","Care Act 2014: self-neglect can be a safeguarding concern; here it is mild, with family support, so no referral is needed now. Review if it worsens.","Lone working: follow trust policy for home visits (log, check-in call)."],"say":[{"to":"Mr Mensah, on his embarrassment","s":"Sometimes the things people feel embarrassed about are exactly what help us understand. Whatever it is, I won't judge."},{"to":"Mr Mensah, testing for paranoia","s":"Some people after something like that start to feel those lads are watching or following them on purpose. Has anything like that happened?"},{"to":"Mr Mensah, on the diagnosis","s":"That day was a panic attack. Since then your mind has learned that going out is dangerous. It's called agoraphobia, and it's very treatable."}],"write":"Home visit, 11:00. Mr Kofi Mensah, 57.\nHPC: intimidated by youths outside supermarket ~2 months ago; panic attack (palpitations, dyspnoea, fear of dying/going mad), relieved at home. Since: fear and avoidance of supermarket, buses, church, crowds, queues; fears being trapped and collapsing without help. No uncued attacks at home.\nNegatives: mood, sleep, appetite, enjoyment normal; no PTSD features; no fear of scrutiny; no obsessions or compulsions; no persecutory beliefs.\nSubstances: whisky 2 units twice weekly; 2 coffees/day; no drugs. No thyrotoxic symptoms.\nRisk: mild self-neglect (laundry); daughter shops online; adequate food. No suicidal or homicidal thoughts.\nImpression: agoraphobia with panic attacks (provisional; 2 months).\nPlan: GP bloods incl. TFTs; discuss NHS Talking Therapies (CBT with graded exposure).","make":["Settle in; open with the daughter's worry.","Normalise to get past the minimising.","Elicit the panic attack and relief at home.","Map feared places and the feared outcome.","Ask about unexpected attacks at home.","Screen depression, psychosis, PTSD, social anxiety, OCD and substances.","Assess self-neglect and risk; summarise the diagnosis."],"next":"His GP's bloods, including TFTs, are normal. He self-refers to NHS Talking Therapies and begins CBT with graded exposure, starting with walks to the front gate. Three months later he travels by bus to church with Abena.","marks":["Normalised embarrassment and uncovered the event.","Elicited the panic attack and its catastrophic thoughts.","Mapped several feared situations and the fear of being trapped.","Asked about unexpected attacks at home.","Screened depression, psychosis, PTSD, social anxiety, OCD and substances.","Assessed self-neglect and suicidal thoughts.","Gave the diagnosis in plain words."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I'm fine' and leaving.","Calling withdrawal depression without checking mood.","Calling a realistic fear paranoia.","Missing self-neglect: food, laundry, the state of the house.","Skipping alcohol, caffeine and thyroid."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-11: 6B02 Agoraphobia; 6B01 Panic disorder (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG113 on generalised anxiety and panic disorder in adults (2011, updated; checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website. Agoraphobia (checked Nov 2026)","4. Care Act 2014 statutory guidance: self-neglect (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: older men from many cultures may feel deep shame about fear. Accept hospitality, let him lead at first, and normalise before probing. Directness too early can close the door."},
{"id":"c-s-19","n":1219,"prog":"Special Challenges","icon":"🧠","title":"The routine CT shows a meningioma: telling his sister","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30","where":"Interview room, community mental health team base","text":"You are the CT2 in the community mental health team. Samuel Owusu, a 43-year-old man who drives a delivery van, was referred by his GP with two months of increasing suspicion about his neighbours, headaches and weakness. A routine CT head before starting an antipsychotic shows a frontal meningioma. The radiologist advises urgent discussion, has booked an MRI with contrast for Friday, and recommends urgent neurosurgical referral, which your consultant sent today. Samuel lives with his mother and sister. He phoned this morning asking that his sister, Miss Esi Owusu, 39, come in on his behalf and be told everything."},"task":"Break the news of the meningioma to his sister, explain how it may link to his symptoms, answer her questions honestly, explain the urgent next steps, and agree how Samuel will be told.","first60":["Re-read the CT report and the radiologist's recommendations; know the MRI date and that the referral has gone.","Confirm Samuel's consent to share, and document it.","Book a private room with no interruptions; bleep to a colleague; tissues ready.","Agree with your consultant what you will and won't say about prognosis.","Prepare a written summary with your number and charity details."],"ask":["'Would you like anyone with you, or on the phone?'","'What's your understanding of why we did the scan?'","'Shall I tell you more about what it means, or is that enough for now?'","'What worries you most?'","'How would you like Samuel to hear this?'","'What will you take away from today?'"],"recognise":["A routine screening scan has changed the diagnosis: this is now possibly an organic psychosis.","She expects good news, so the shock will be large; a warning shot matters.","'Meningioma' is jargon; 'a growth from the lining around the brain' is not.","Most meningiomas are benign and slow-growing, but you do not yet know about this one: say so.","Her practical questions (driving, work, decisions) are real and need answers today.","Samuel should hear it from a clinician soon; offer to do it together."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell her, as Samuel asked, with a warning shot and plain words, then follow her questions and offer to tell Samuel together tomorrow.","r":"He has consented; delay would harm trust and the urgent plan. Joint disclosure supports both.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her that meningiomas are always benign and he will be fine.","r":"You do not know the grade or the treatment yet; false reassurance destroys trust later.","best":false},{"c":"Give a full account of grades, surgery and radiotherapy before she asks.","r":"She cannot take it in; overloading her ignores her emotional state and is not your specialty.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): share with his consent; record it.","MCA 2005: Samuel is presumed to have capacity; capacity is decision-specific. His sister cannot decide for him unless he makes a lasting power of attorney.","DVLA: drivers must tell DVLA of a brain tumour and of a psychotic illness; doctors must advise the patient not to drive and record it.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): be honest, and do not give false assurance."],"say":[{"to":"Esi, the news","s":"I'm afraid the scan has shown something we weren't expecting. There's a growth in the front of his head, called a meningioma: a lump from the lining around the brain."},{"to":"Esi, on prognosis","s":"Most meningiomas aren't cancer and grow slowly. I can't tell you exactly about Samuel's until the MRI and the specialists. I won't guess."},{"to":"Esi, on telling Samuel","s":"Shall I come and see him at home tomorrow with you, so he hears it from a doctor and you don't carry it alone?"}],"write":"Notes entry:\nMet Miss Esi Owusu (sister) at Samuel's request (consent by phone 09:10, documented).\nCT head: frontal meningioma. MRI with contrast booked Friday. Urgent neurosurgical referral sent by Dr Lane today.\nNews given with warning; plain-language explanation; prognosis not stated beyond 'most are benign, details after MRI/specialists'.\nAdvised: no driving, inform DVLA; fit note; support worker for work/benefits; capacity presumed.\nRed flags given: worsening headache, vomiting, drowsiness, confusion, seizure, new weakness → 999/A&E.\nPlan: joint home visit to tell Samuel tomorrow; antipsychotic on hold pending neurosurgical advice; GP informed today.","make":["Prepare facts, room and consent.","Ask what she knows; warn; give the news in plain words.","Stop. Allow silence and feelings.","Answer her questions honestly, within what is known.","Explain MRI, urgent referral and possible treatments.","Cover driving, work, decisions and red flags.","Agree how Samuel will be told; check understanding; give written information."],"next":"You and Esi tell Samuel at home the next day. The MRI on Friday confirms a meningioma. The neurosurgical team sees him the following week and plans surgery. Your team continues to see him, and his suspiciousness eases after the operation.","marks":["Confirmed consent and prepared a private room.","Asked what she knew first.","Warned, then named the finding in plain words.","Allowed silence and validated her shock.","Was honest about prognosis without false promises.","Covered next steps, driving, work, decisions and red flags.","Agreed how Samuel will be told and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Saying 'it's benign, he'll be fine' before the MRI.","Using 'meningioma' or 'lesion' without explaining.","Talking through her tears.","Forgetting to advise no driving.","Implying she now makes his decisions."],"refs":["1. NICE NG99. Brain tumours (primary) and brain metastases in over 16s (2018, updated; checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA. Assessing fitness to drive: a guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC. Confidentiality (2017) and Good medical practice (2024) (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","5. The Brain Tumour Charity: meningioma information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, the patient owns the news. A relative is told only with consent, and never instead of the patient for long. Offer to tell him together, soon."},
{"id":"c-s-20","n":1220,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🩺","title":"'Slow, blank and shuffling' on haloperidol: EPSE history and examination","src":"","checked":"7 Nov 2026","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:00","where":"Clinic room, adult inpatient ward","text":"You are the CT1 on the ward. Mr Harjit Sandhu, a 57-year-old man with schizoaffective disorder, had his haloperidol increased from 5 mg once daily to 5 mg twice daily two weeks ago. His mother, visiting yesterday, told the nurses he has become 'slow', looks 'blank' and 'shuffles'. He fell in the corridor last week. The nurse in charge asks you to see him. You do not need to assess his mental state or risk."},"task":"Take a brief history of extrapyramidal side effects, examine him for parkinsonism, dystonia, akathisia and tardive dyskinesia, explain your findings and discuss options.","first60":["Check the drug chart: haloperidol dose, date of increase, PRN use, other drugs.","Check his observations: temperature, pulse, BP (lying and standing if possible).","Ask a nurse to chaperone; clear space for him to walk.","Clean your hands; confirm his identity.","Acknowledge his mother's concern and explain what you will do."],"ask":["'When did the stiffness start? Any dose change?'","'Is it hard to get out of a chair or start walking? Any falls?'","'Any shaking in your hands?'","'Any sudden spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue?'","'Do you feel restless inside, like you can't keep still?'","'Any movements of your mouth or tongue you can't control?'","'Any fever, sweating or feeling muddled?'"],"recognise":["Four EPSE groups: acute dystonia (hours to days), akathisia (days to weeks), parkinsonism (weeks), tardive dyskinesia (months to years).","Parkinsonism: rigidity (cogwheel), resting tremor, bradykinesia, hypomimia, shuffling gait, reduced arm swing; often symmetrical.","Onset after a dose increase points to the drug.","Rigidity with fever, autonomic instability and confusion is NMS: a medical emergency.","Falls are a real harm; check injury and postural BP.","Anticholinergics help parkinsonism and dystonia but worsen tardive dyskinesia and cognition."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a brief four-group history, examine in order, then discuss reducing the dose or switching with the consultant, with short-term procyclidine if needed.","r":"Treats the cause, uses anticholinergics only as a bridge, and protects him from further falls.","best":true},{"c":"Prescribe regular procyclidine and leave the haloperidol unchanged.","r":"Long-term anticholinergics cause confusion, constipation and retention, and mask the cause; review the dose first.","best":false},{"c":"Stop the haloperidol today.","r":"Abrupt withdrawal risks relapse; agree a change with the consultant.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Decision making and consent (2020): explain the examination and gain consent; offer a chaperone (GMC Intimate examinations and chaperones, 2024).","MCA 2005: presume capacity to consent to examination.","If detained: Section 58 rules govern any change of medication after three months."],"say":[{"to":"Mr Sandhu, before examining","s":"I'd like to look at your face, hands, arms and walking. Nothing painful. Alice will be with us. Is that okay?"},{"to":"Mr Sandhu, on findings","s":"Your muscles are stiff, your hands shake at rest and your walking has slowed. It's a side effect of the higher haloperidol dose, and it usually gets better when we change it."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He has drug-induced parkinsonism after the haloperidol increase, and fell last week. No NMS features. Please do lying and standing BP and refer to physio; I'll discuss the dose with the consultant today."}],"write":"Notes entry:\nReview re mother's concern (slow, blank, shuffling). Haloperidol increased 5 mg OD to 5 mg BD 2 weeks ago.\nHx: stiffness, difficulty rising, shuffling gait, fall last week (bruised hip), resting tremor, hypomimia, flat voice. No dystonia, akathisia or orofacial movements. No fever, sweating, confusion.\nO/E (chaperone: SN Alice): right resting tremor; hypomimia; cogwheel rigidity right wrist; bradykinesia on finger tapping; slow rise, shuffling gait, reduced arm swing, turning in several steps. No akathisia. Tongue normal at rest and protruded. Obs normal, apyrexial.\nImpression: antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism.\nPlan: discuss dose reduction or switch with consultant today; procyclidine 2.5 mg TDS short-term if needed; lying/standing BP; physio falls assessment; Simpson-Angus and AIMS baseline; review tomorrow; mother updated with consent.","make":["Ask about all four EPSE groups and falls.","Link onset to the dose change; screen for NMS.","Consent, chaperone, hand hygiene, pain.","Observe at rest; examine face, mouth and tongue.","Examine outstretched hands, tone and finger tapping.","Watch standing, arm drop and gait.","Explain, offer options, plan falls safety, check understanding."],"next":"The consultant reduces haloperidol back to 5 mg once daily and adds procyclidine 2.5 mg three times daily for two weeks. The physio provides a walking plan. By ten days his stiffness and gait improve; procyclidine is stopped, and his mood stays settled.","marks":["Asked about all four EPSE groups and falls.","Linked onset to the dose increase and screened for NMS.","Gained consent, offered a chaperone and cleaned hands.","Examined face, mouth, hands, tone, finger tapping, standing and gait.","Explained findings in plain words.","Offered dose reduction, switch or short-term procyclidine.","Planned falls safety and a review."],"pitfalls":["Checking tone only and missing mouth, standing and gait.","Not asking about restlessness, spasms or tongue movements.","Missing NMS features (fever, confusion, autonomic signs).","Starting long-term procyclidine without reviewing the dose.","Ignoring the fall."],"refs":["1. BNF: haloperidol and procyclidine monographs (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 on psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, updated; checked Nov 2026)","3. Simpson GM, Angus JW. A rating scale for extrapyramidal side effects. Acta Psychiatr Scand 1970","4. Guy W. Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS). ECDEU Assessment Manual, 1976","5. GMC. Intimate examinations and chaperones (2024) (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: talk the patient through each step in plain words ('let your arm go floppy'), and say what you see. UK patients expect to be partners in the examination, not passive subjects."},
{"id":"c-s-21","n":1221,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🩺","title":"Health check in alcohol dependence: the abdomen and signs of hepatic encephalopathy","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00","where":"Willow Ward, adult acute inpatient unit","text":"Gary Pendleton, 54, was admitted eight days ago with suicidal thoughts after a heavy binge. For three years he has drunk about half a litre of whisky and four cans of strong lager a day (roughly 40 units), starting on waking. He is on day 8 of a chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen, now 10 mg twice daily, and finished 5 days of Pabrinex. His mood has lifted. He was told years ago that his liver was 'fatty' and has asked the nurses for a liver check. His admission bloods were haemolysed and need repeating."},"task":"Carry out an abdominal examination with the peripheral signs of chronic liver disease and the bedside signs of hepatic encephalopathy, telling him what you find. No rectal or genital examination.","first60":["Gel your hands in front of him, introduce yourself and name his worry: the liver.","Ask about pain and symptoms in one line; this is not the moment for a drinking history.","Say that a rectal and genital check belong to a full examination but are not needed today.","Offer a chaperone, lie him flat on one pillow, top off, legs covered.","First, from the end of the bed: colour, alertness, tremor, breath."],"ask":["'Any pain or tenderness anywhere before I start?'","'Have you noticed yellow eyes, itching, a swollen tummy or ankles, or black stools?'","'What's today's date, and where are we right now?'","'Could you copy this five-pointed star for me?'","'Is it all right if I press on your tummy? Tell me if anything hurts.'","'How would you like me to tell you what I find: as I go, or at the end?'"],"recognise":["Peripheral stigmata of chronic liver disease: palmar erythema, leukonychia, clubbing, Dupuytren's contracture, spider naevi (more than five is significant), gynaecomastia, loss of body hair, jaundice, bruising.","Hepatic encephalopathy at the bedside: asterixis (a coarse, irregular flap of the outstretched wrists), drowsiness, disorientation, slowed or slurred speech, fetor hepaticus, poor star or clock copying, brisk reflexes.","A fine, fast tremor of the outstretched fingers is withdrawal or anxiety; a slow, jerky flap is asterixis. Hold the wrists back for at least 15 seconds to tell them apart.","Nystagmus, ataxia and confusion in a drinker point to Wernicke's encephalopathy, which needs Pabrinex today whatever the liver shows.","Splenomegaly, ascites and dilated veins around the navel point to portal hypertension, meaning cirrhosis is likely.","A normal examination does not rule out cirrhosis; most liver scarring is silent until late."],"decide":[{"c":"Work from the end of the bed down to the legs, test for the flap, orientation and star copy, tell him findings as you go, then order LFTs, INR, FBC and a FibroScan.","r":"Covers both the stigmata and encephalopathy, answers his worry honestly and leads to tests that can stage the liver.","best":true},{"c":"Go straight to palpating the abdomen, then reassure him that his liver feels normal.","r":"Misses the hands, the flap and the confusion screen, and gives false reassurance; a normal feel does not exclude cirrhosis.","best":false},{"c":"Take a full alcohol history first, then examine if time allows.","r":"He asked for a check, not a history; the history is already in the notes and the examination is what he needs today.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: explain what you will do and why, and check agreement before touching; he can stop you at any point (GMC Decision making and consent, 2020).","Offer a chaperone for any intimate or exposing examination and record the offer and the name of the chaperone (GMC Intimate examinations and chaperones, 2024).","He is informal: the Mental Health Act gives no authority for physical examination; it rests on his consent, or on the Mental Capacity Act if he lacked capacity."],"say":[{"to":"Patient (starting)","s":"I'll look at your hands, face, chest, tummy and legs and test your coordination. A full check would include the back passage and genitals, which we don't need to do today."},{"to":"Patient (the flap)","s":"Arms straight out, wrists bent right back as if stopping traffic, and hold it there while I count to fifteen."},{"to":"Patient (findings)","s":"No signs your liver is failing or affecting your brain. But there are signs of strain, and a hands-on check can't tell how scarred a liver is, so we'll do bloods and a scan."}],"write":"Physical examination (abdomen and liver signs), chaperone: HCA Steve Brennan.\nConsent verbal. Rectal and genital examination not indicated today.\nGeneral: alert, oriented to time and place, no jaundice, pallor or fetor. Speech normal.\nHands: palmar erythema bilaterally; no clubbing, leukonychia or Dupuytren's. Pulse 76 regular.\nEncephalopathy: no asterixis (15 s); star copied correctly; finger-nose normal; no nystagmus.\nChest: 4 spider naevi upper chest; no gynaecomastia.\nAbdomen: soft, non-tender; liver edge 2 fingerbreadths below costal margin, smooth; spleen and kidneys not palpable; no shifting dullness; bowel sounds normal.\nLegs: no oedema; reflexes normal; gait and Romberg normal.\nImpression: stigmata of chronic liver disease with hepatomegaly; no encephalopathy or portal hypertension signs.\nPlan: LFTs, GGT, albumin, INR, FBC, U&E, glucose today; liver ultrasound; FibroScan request; review chlordiazepoxide dose with consultant; alcohol liaison referral.","make":["Bloods today: LFTs, GGT, albumin, bilirubin, INR, FBC (platelets, MCV), U&E, glucose, magnesium.","Request a liver ultrasound and transient elastography (FibroScan) through the local hepatology pathway.","Ask the consultant whether a shorter-acting benzodiazepine is needed if liver tests are poor.","Refer to the alcohol liaison or community alcohol team for relapse prevention before discharge."],"next":"Two days later the night nurse says Gary is drowsy, keeps asking what day it is and has a coarse flap. Check NEWS2 and glucose, look for a precipitant (infection, constipation, bleeding, sedation from chlordiazepoxide), withhold sedatives and arrange urgent transfer to the medical team.","marks":["Gels hands, introduces, gains consent and states that rectal and genital checks are omitted.","Offers a chaperone and positions and exposes him with dignity.","Inspects from the end of the bed, including alertness and fetor.","Checks hands, nails, palms and pulse.","Tests for asterixis for at least 15 seconds and checks orientation and star copying.","Checks eyes, nystagmus, mouth and chest for spider naevi and gynaecomastia.","Palpates, percusses (including shifting dullness) and auscultates the abdomen.","Checks legs for oedema, reflexes and gait.","Tells him findings as he goes and closes with tests and a safety net."],"pitfalls":["Starting at the abdomen and skipping the hands and the flap.","Holding the wrists back for two seconds and calling it 'no flap'.","Mistaking a fine withdrawal tremor for asterixis.","Examining silently until he is left to ask how he's doing.","Telling him his liver is fine because nothing was felt.","Taking a long drinking history the task did not ask for."],"refs":["1. NICE NG50 Cirrhosis in over 16s: assessment and management (2016, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: chlordiazepoxide; thiamine (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Intimate examinations and chaperones (2024, checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: alcohol-related liver disease (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK psychiatric wards the junior doctor is expected to do the physical examination; the medical team will not come unless you ask. Record the chaperone's name, and say aloud why you are not doing a rectal check.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-35","n":1235,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🧒","title":"Underachieving at school: ADHD collateral history from his mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:00, CAMHS clinic","where":"Child and adolescent mental health outpatient clinic","text":"Mrs Kerry Walsh, 41, comes alone to talk about her son Callum, aged 12, who is at school. He is in Year 8 and has been moved down to the lowest sets, with detentions several times a week for shouting out and walking out of lessons. The GP referral says: 'Mum wonders about ADHD.' You see her first; you'll meet Callum next week."},"task":"Take a collateral history from Callum's mother covering inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity across settings, onset and development, other explanations, risk and impact, and her concerns.","first60":["Check the referral and any school letters; confirm she has parental responsibility and that Callum knows about the appointment.","Start with her main worry, then tell her you'll cover his whole life, not just school.","Ask for everyday examples rather than labels: 'How long can he do homework?' not 'Can he concentrate?'","Keep three columns in mind: inattention, hyperactivity, impulsivity; then onset, settings, impact.","Leave time for the differentials, risk and her ideas about ADHD and medicine."],"ask":["'How long can he stick at something a bit boring, like homework?'","'Does he lose things, or start jobs and leave them half done?'","'Can he stay sitting through a meal or a lesson?'","'Does he blurt out, interrupt or push in? Does he act first and say sorry later?'","'When did you first notice this? Was it the same at primary school?'","'Is it the same at home, at school and at clubs?'","'Was he born early? Any delays in walking or talking? Anyone in the family the same?'","'Any trouble reading or spelling? With friends, routines or noise? Defiance on purpose?'","'How is it affecting him in himself? Any injuries, fights, or being picked on?'"],"recognise":["ADHD needs a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity, beginning in childhood, beyond what is expected for age and ability, present in more than one setting and causing real impairment.","Risk factors: prematurity and low birth weight, a parent with ADHD, brain injury, other neurodevelopmental conditions.","Long gaming sessions do not disprove inattention: highly rewarding activities hold attention even in ADHD.","Differentials and co-occurring problems: specific learning difficulty, autism, oppositional or conduct problems, anxiety or low mood, poor sleep, hearing problems, absence seizures, trauma, substance use.","Untreated ADHD brings accidents, impulsive aggression, bullying, harsh parenting, exclusion and falling self-esteem.","Symptoms often worsen at the move to secondary school, when demands for sitting still and organising rise."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full collateral history, then request school reports and teacher and parent rating scales, see Callum himself, and advise the school's SENCo on support now.","r":"A diagnosis needs evidence from more than one setting and the child's own account; school help shouldn't wait for it.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her it sounds like ADHD and start methylphenidate.","r":"Unsafe and against NICE: no diagnosis, no school information, no baseline checks, no assessment of Callum.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her it is a behaviour problem and refer her to a parenting class only.","r":"Dismisses a likely neurodevelopmental condition with clear impairment across settings.","best":false}],"law":["Parental responsibility: check who holds it. Information from school needs parental consent, and Callum should be told and involved at a level he understands.","Safeguarding: ask about how discipline is handled; physical punishment causing harm or ongoing emotional harm needs a safeguarding discussion under local children's partnership procedures.","School support does not need a diagnosis: the SEND Code of Practice (2015) requires schools to identify and support needs; an EHC needs assessment is possible if needs are severe."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"From what you've told me, ADHD is a real possibility: difficulties since nursery, at home, school and football, and they're affecting him. I can't diagnose from one conversation, so I'll get the school's view and see Callum."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Medicine is never automatic. If it is ADHD, we start with information and support, and talk about medicine together. Used carefully, it shouldn't change his personality."},{"to":"School SENCo (with consent)","s":"We're assessing Callum for possible ADHD. Please complete the teacher questionnaire and a short report. Meanwhile, seating near the front, instructions in short steps and a homework planner checked at the end of the day would help."}],"write":"10:50 CAMHS: collateral history from mother re Callum Walsh, 12, Year 8.\nInattention: distractible, doesn't finish work, forgets homework daily, loses belongings weekly; homework tolerance about 10 min (gaming for hours).\nHyperactivity: fidgets, leaves seat at meals and in class, 'like a motor'.\nImpulsivity: shouts out, pushes in, apologises later; 2 A&E attendances (fracture, laceration).\nOnset: from nursery; worse since Year 7. Settings: home, school, football, grandmother's.\nDevelopment: born 35/40 by emergency CS; milestones normal. FHx: father possibly ADHD.\nDifferentials: no reading or maths difficulty; good friendships, no restricted interests or sensory issues; not defiant; late gaming; hearing normal; no absences; no substances reported.\nImpact/risk: low self-esteem, school dread, no self-harm; pushed a peer once; targeted by peers; parents shout, no physical punishment.\nICE: fears exclusion; worried medicine will sedate him.\nImp: presentation consistent with possible ADHD (combined); needs multi-setting information and assessment of Callum.\nPlan: consent for school report and teacher/parent rating scales; letter to SENCo; see Callum next week; sleep hygiene and screen-time advice; safety net given.\nDr H. Iqbal CT3","make":["Open with her main worry and her ideas: 'What do you think is going on?'","Inattention: finishing tasks, homework time, losing things, forgetting, following instructions.","Hyperactivity: staying seated, fidgeting, noise, energy compared with peers.","Impulsivity: blurting, interrupting, waiting turns, risky acts, injuries, apologising afterwards.","Onset, duration and settings: since when, primary school reports, home, school, clubs, relatives.","Development and family: pregnancy, prematurity, milestones, family history of ADHD.","Differentials, impact and risk: learning, autism, defiance, mood, sleep, hearing, absences, substances; self-esteem, injuries, peers, discipline at home."],"next":"School returns teacher rating scales that match hers, and the SENCo moves Callum to the front with a homework planner. You meet Callum the following week; he says lessons feel 'like a radio that won't tune in' and he hates being called lazy.","marks":["Explored all three symptom domains with everyday examples.","Established onset in early childhood and pervasiveness across settings.","Asked about pregnancy, prematurity, milestones and family history.","Screened for learning difficulty, autism, defiance, mood, sleep, hearing and substances.","Assessed impact and risk: injuries, peers, self-esteem, discipline at home.","Explored her concerns and explained the next steps without promising a diagnosis."],"pitfalls":["Asking 'Can he concentrate?' instead of asking for examples.","Taking gaming for hours as proof he can concentrate.","Forgetting to ask about home discipline and injuries.","Diagnosing or promising medicine on a single parent's account."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87 (2018, updated 2019), on diagnosing and managing ADHD; checked Nov 2026","2. WHO. ICD-11, 6A05 Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. Department for Education and Department of Health. SEND Code of Practice: 0 to 25 years (2015; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK CAMHS rarely diagnose ADHD from one appointment. Expect to gather school reports and rating scales and to see the child yourself; the school's SENCo is a key ally.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-36","n":1236,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Explaining ADHD and medication to a parent","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00","where":"CAMHS outpatient clinic, neurodevelopmental pathway","text":"Jake Bradley, a 9-year-old boy in Year 5, was diagnosed with ADHD two weeks ago after parent and teacher rating scales, a school report and clinic observation. He has run into the road twice this year. His father, Mr Tom Bradley, 42, an electrician, has come back alone with questions. He opens with: 'Half the family reckon it's just a label for a naughty kid.'"},"task":"Explain ADHD to Jake's father: what it is, what causes it and how it runs in families, parent training and school support, and methylphenidate with the checks before and during treatment.","first60":["Have the assessment summary, Jake's height, weight, pulse and BP from the last visit, and an ADHD leaflet ready.","Sit at an angle, not across a desk; he came alone and may feel judged.","Ask first: 'What do you already know, and what's the one thing you most want answered today?'","Write his questions down and tell him you will come back to each one.","Plan to explain in small chunks and check after each one."],"ask":["'What have you read or been told about ADHD so far?'","'What worries you most: the label, the medicine, or something else?'","'What has the school already put in place for him?'","'Was anyone in the family like Jake as a child?'","'Has Jake ever fainted while running, had chest pain or palpitations? Has anyone in the family died suddenly under 40?'","'How are his sleep and appetite now?'","'What does Jake himself think is going on?'"],"recognise":["Scepticism ('just a label') often hides guilt or fear of being blamed; name it gently before giving facts.","A father who recognises himself in his son ('I was the same at school') may have undiagnosed ADHD; note it kindly and offer adult assessment via his GP later.","Road-running and impulsive danger make this a safety issue, not only a learning one.","Information overload is the commonest failure: three or four key messages per chunk, then check.","Medication is one part of the plan; parent training and school changes come with it, not instead."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain ADHD, causes and heritability, then offer an ADHD parent-training group, a school plan through the SENCO and methylphenidate after baseline cardiac checks, with a written summary.","r":"Matches NICE NG87: the whole plan, not just a prescription, delivered in chunks he can take home to Jake's mother.","best":true},{"c":"Start methylphenidate today and give him the leaflet to read.","r":"Skips his questions, the cardiac screen and the home and school parts of the plan; he leaves with a prescription and no understanding.","best":false},{"c":"Advise cutting out sugar and food additives first and review in six months.","r":"No good evidence; NICE advises against routine elimination diets, and Jake stays at risk near roads meanwhile.","best":false}],"law":["Methylphenidate is a Schedule 2 controlled drug: specialist starts and titrates it; the GP may take over prescribing under a shared care agreement once the dose is stable.","Jake's parents hold parental responsibility and consent to treatment; involve Jake in the decision at his level and record his views.","Ask consent before sharing the diagnosis and report with the school; the parent decides, with Jake's views considered.","Under the SEND Code of Practice (2015), the school must provide SEN support; an EHC needs assessment can be requested from the local authority if needs exceed that."],"say":[{"to":"Father","s":"ADHD isn't bad behaviour and it isn't bad parenting. It's the way Jake's brain manages attention, activity and stopping to think. It runs strongly in families."},{"to":"Father","s":"The medicine doesn't change who he is. It helps the brain's brakes work for a few hours. If he seems flat or not himself, the dose is too high and we change it."},{"to":"Father","s":"Before he starts, we check his heart history, pulse, blood pressure, height and weight. Then we keep checking every few months."}],"write":"14:40 Clinic review with father (Mr T Bradley). Jake 9M, ADHD combined presentation dx 2 wks ago.\nExplained: ADHD, causes, heritability (father reports similar school difficulties), prognosis.\nAgreed: ADHD parent-training group referral; letter to SENCO with consent re SEN support plan; methylphenidate discussed.\nCardiac hx: no syncope, chest pain or palpitations; no FH sudden death <40. Baseline: Ht 134 cm (50th), Wt 29 kg (50th), HR 84, BP 102/64 (normal for age). No ECG indicated.\nPlan: start methylphenidate MR 18 mg mane after mother agrees; weekly phone titration; ht, wt, HR, BP at each dose change; review 6 wks. Leaflet given. Safety net: stop and call if chest pain, fainting, marked low mood.\nDr S Khan CT2","make":["Baseline before any stimulant: cardiac history (exercise fainting, chest pain, palpitations), family history of sudden death in the young, height and weight on a growth chart, pulse and BP on an age centile chart.","ECG only if there is a cardiac history, a family history of sudden death, or an abnormal finding.","Titrate weekly with a rating scale from parent and teacher; aim for the lowest dose that works.","Agree who gives the morning dose, where it is stored (locked), and who collects controlled-drug prescriptions.","Send the school letter only with consent; ask the SENCO for a written SEN support plan."],"next":"Jake's mother agrees by phone the next day. Jake starts methylphenidate MR 18 mg each morning; at 36 mg his teacher's rating scale halves, his appetite dips at lunch, and his parents start the parent group. His father asks his GP about an adult ADHD assessment for himself.","marks":["Asked what he knew and feared before explaining.","Explained ADHD in plain words with its three core features.","Said clearly it is not caused by parenting and that it runs in families.","Covered parent training and school support, not just medication.","Explained the cardiac screen and growth, pulse and BP monitoring.","Checked understanding and gave a safety net."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing for three minutes without checking he is keeping up.","Promising medication will 'fix' him or that he will grow out of it.","Forgetting the family cardiac history before a stimulant.","Ignoring the father's hint that he had the same problems."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87 ADHD: diagnosis and management (2018, updated 2019; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF for Children: methylphenidate hydrochloride (checked Nov 2026)","3. Department for Education. SEND Code of Practice: 0 to 25 years (2015; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. ADHD and hyperkinetic disorder: information for parents and carers (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, stimulants for children are started by a specialist and moved to the GP under shared care. Families expect a plan that includes school, so learn the role of the SENCO and what SEN support and an EHC plan mean.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-37","n":1237,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Late to talk and different from his brother: an autism developmental history","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:00","where":"CAMHS neurodevelopmental clinic","text":"Noah Bennett, a 5-year-old boy in Reception, was referred by his GP after his health visitor and teacher raised concerns about his speech. His mother, Mrs Laura Bennett, 33, has come alone; Noah is at school. She says, 'His brother Alfie was chatting in sentences at two. Noah just isn't the same, and I don't know why.'"},"task":"Take a chronological developmental history from Noah's mother, focused on social communication and interaction, restricted and repetitive behaviour and sensory differences, and the differentials and co-occurring conditions, then explain the next steps.","first60":["Read the referral and any health visitor or school notes; check whether a hearing test has been done.","Open with her words: 'Tell me what you've noticed about Noah.' Let her talk for a minute.","Tell her the plan: 'I'll go through his story from pregnancy to now, then how he talks, plays and copes with change.'","Keep a timeline on paper: ages down the side, milestones and concerns across.","Ask for specific examples, not yes or no: 'Show me what he does when he wants a drink.'"],"ask":["'How was the pregnancy? Was he born on time? Any problems after birth?'","'When did he sit, walk, babble, say his first words, and join two words together?'","'Has he ever lost skills or words he once had?'","'When you call his name from across the room, what happens?'","'Does he point to show you something interesting, like a plane, just to share it?'","'Does he play pretend, say giving a teddy its dinner or driving a toy car to the shops?'","'What happens if his routine changes, or you take a different route?'","'Is he bothered by noise, labels in clothes, or certain food textures?'","'Does he ever hurt himself when upset?'"],"recognise":["Social communication: poor response to name, little pointing to share, limited eye contact with gesture, few shared smiles, no pretend play, playing alongside but not with peers.","Restricted and repetitive: lining up, spinning wheels, intense narrow interests, insistence on sameness, echolalia (repeating phrases from TV), hand-flapping or rocking.","Sensory: distress at hand dryers or sock seams, very narrow diet, seeking spinning things.","Red flag: any loss of language or social skills; ask directly. In girls think of Rett syndrome; in any child, consider Landau-Kleffner syndrome (loss of language with epileptiform EEG).","Differentials: hearing loss, developmental language disorder, intellectual disability, attachment difficulties after neglect or care changes, selective mutism, ADHD.","Normal motor milestones with delayed and odd language points away from global developmental delay."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full chronological history, ask for examples in each autism area, screen differentials, regression and self-injury, then explain a multidisciplinary autism assessment with hearing confirmed and speech and language input.","r":"Gives the team what it needs and gives the mother a clear route, without naming a diagnosis before the assessment.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her it sounds like autism and refer for a diagnosis.","r":"Premature: differentials are not excluded, and naming it before assessment can mislead or distress.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her that boys talk late and review in a year.","r":"Falsely reassuring: the social and repetitive features need assessment now, and delay costs school support.","best":false}],"law":["His parents hold parental responsibility and consent to assessment and to contacting school; record consent before asking the school for a report.","NICE CG128: the autism assessment should start within 3 months of referral to the autism team.","If you hear about neglect, unexplained injuries or a change of carer, follow local safeguarding procedures; attachment difficulty is a differential and a safeguarding question.","A child does not need a diagnosis to get SEN support at school; the SEND Code of Practice is needs-led."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"You know Noah best, and you've noticed real differences. I'd like to take his story from the beginning, so we don't miss anything."},{"to":"Mother","s":"From what you've told me, there are differences in how Noah communicates, plays and copes with change. The next step is a full assessment by our team, so we can understand them properly."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Nothing you've described is caused by going back to work. These differences start in how a child's brain develops."}],"write":"10:45 Collateral hx from mother (Mrs L Bennett). Noah 5M, Reception. Brother Alfie 7.\nPregnancy/birth: uneventful, 39/40 SVD, no SCBU. Motor: sat 7 m, walked 13 m.\nLanguage: few babbles; first words ~2y6m; now short phrases to request; echolalia (TV phrases). No regression.\nSocial: poor response to name, leads mother by hand, no pointing to share, little eye contact, no pretend play, plays alone at school.\nRRB/sensory: lines up cars, spins wheels, intense trains interest, same route to school, meltdowns with change; hand dryers, sock seams; narrow diet.\nRisk: head-banging in meltdowns, no injuries needing treatment.\nOther: audiology last spring normal; no seizures; sleep 2 h to settle; not overactive; FH paternal cousin autistic. No SC involvement.\nImp: social communication and RRB differences from early childhood, impairing; autism to be assessed; DLD, hearing, ID considered.\nPlan: MDT autism assessment (ADOS-2, school report with consent), SALT, sleep advice, consultant review.\nDr P Adeyemi CT3","make":["Use a timeline: pregnancy and birth, 0 to 12 months, 1 to 2 years, 2 to 3 years, nursery, school.","Language milestones: babble by about 10 months, first words around 12 months, two-word phrases by about 2 years.","Ask for one real example in each area: social communication, repetitive behaviour, sensory.","Always ask: 'Has he ever lost skills or words?' and 'Has his hearing been tested, and when?'","Screen co-occurring: learning, attention and activity, seizures, sleep, eating, self-injury, constipation.","Close with a summary back to her and the assessment pathway: ADOS-2 observation, school report, speech and language therapy, hearing."],"next":"With consent, the team gathers a report from Noah's teacher and sees him for an ADOS-2 observation and speech and language assessment. Hearing is already normal. Three months later the team meets his parents to share the outcome.","marks":["Took pregnancy, birth and milestones in time order.","Asked for examples of response to name, pointing, eye contact, pretend play and peer play.","Asked about routines, narrow interests, repetitive movements and sensory sensitivities.","Asked directly about loss of skills and hearing.","Screened co-occurring conditions and self-injury.","Summarised back and explained the next steps without naming a diagnosis too soon."],"pitfalls":["Jumping straight to autism questions and missing the milestones.","Closed yes/no questions instead of asking for real examples.","Forgetting to ask about regression.","Missing self-injury in meltdowns, or a mother's guilt."],"refs":["1. NICE CG128 Under-19s autism recognition guideline (2011, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG170 Autism in under 19s, support and management: 2013, last updated 2021 (checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6A02 Autism spectrum disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. Department for Education. SEND Code of Practice: 0 to 25 years (2015; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK autism assessments are multidisciplinary and use a parent developmental history, school information and a structured observation such as ADOS-2. Your history is the backbone of that assessment, so record ages and examples precisely.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-38","n":1238,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"The assessment is complete: explaining autism to her mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:30","where":"CAMHS neurodevelopmental clinic","text":"Isla Doyle, a 14-year-old girl in Year 9, has completed a multidisciplinary autism assessment (developmental history, ADOS-2 observation and school report). She meets criteria for autism, without intellectual disability or language impairment. Isla has agreed that her mother, Mrs Claire Doyle, 45, can hear the outcome first and that everything can be shared. Mrs Doyle says, 'My friend's boy was diagnosed at four. How did everyone miss this for ten years?'"},"task":"Explain the autism diagnosis to Isla's mother: what it means for Isla, why it was recognised late, its causes, co-occurring conditions to watch for, support at school and home, and next steps including how Isla hears it.","first60":["Confirm Isla's agreement to share is recorded; have the report and a leaflet ready.","Ask what she knows about autism and what she most wants to know.","Give the headline early and plainly: 'The assessment shows Isla is autistic.' Then pause.","Watch for relief, grief or guilt; name it before more facts.","Plan four chunks: what it is, why now, what else to watch for, what helps."],"ask":["'What do you already know about autism?'","'How are you feeling, hearing this?'","'How has Isla been lately: mood, worry, sleep, eating?'","'Has she ever talked about hurting herself or wishing you weren't here?'","'What's hardest for her at school right now?'","'How would Isla like to hear about this, and who should be there?'"],"recognise":["Autism is a lifelong difference in social communication and in flexibility of thinking and behaviour, often with sensory differences and real strengths (focus, detail, honesty).","Girls are often recognised later: they copy peers and hide difficulties (masking or camouflaging) until the social demands of secondary school outgrow their coping.","Exhaustion after school, Monday refusal or meltdowns at home can be signs of masking strain.","Co-occurring conditions are common: anxiety, low mood, ADHD, sleep problems, eating difficulties including ARFID and anorexia, and self-harm. Screen for each.","'Asperger's' is no longer used; ICD-11 and DSM-5 include it within autism spectrum disorder."],"decide":[{"c":"Give the diagnosis plainly, explain what it means and why it came late, screen for co-occurring problems, explain that support is the treatment, agree school adjustments and plan how Isla hears it.","r":"Answers her questions, keeps Isla at the centre, and turns a label into a plan.","best":true},{"c":"Read the report aloud and offer a follow-up in six months.","r":"Jargon, no support plan, and co-occurring anxiety and school avoidance are missed.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest a medicine to help her social skills.","r":"Wrong: no medicine treats autism itself; medicines are only for co-occurring conditions.","best":false}],"law":["Isla is 14: involve her in decisions and assess Gillick competence for each decision; she has agreed to this sharing, and her views shape who else is told.","Equality Act 2010: autism can count as a disability, so schools must make reasonable adjustments.","SEND Code of Practice (2015): SEN support from the school; an EHC needs assessment can be requested from the local authority if needs are greater.","Carers can ask the local authority for a carer's needs assessment (Children and Families Act 2014 for parent carers)."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"The assessment shows Isla is autistic. Her brain works differently in how she connects with people and copes with change. It's part of who she is, not an illness she caught."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Lots of girls copy what others do and hide their difficulties. That can work until secondary school, when friendships get more complicated. Then it becomes exhausting."},{"to":"Mother","s":"There's no tablet for autism itself. What helps is understanding, the right adjustments at school, and treating anything else that comes along, like anxiety."}],"write":"16:10 Feedback of autism assessment to mother (Mrs C Doyle); Isla 14F consented to sharing.\nExplained: autism (ICD-11 6A02), without ID or language impairment; late recognition and masking; Asperger's term; genetic causes, not parenting or vaccines.\nCo-occurring screen (mother): anxiety at school, refuses Mondays ~2/month, sleep onset 1-2 h, narrow diet but weight stable, no self-harm or suicidal talk known.\nPlan: feedback session with Isla next week (her choice); report to GP; with consent, letter to SENCO re adjustments; post-diagnostic parent group; adapted CBT for anxiety via CAMHS; sleep plan; NAS signposted; carer's assessment info.\nSafety net: contact if low mood, self-harm, weight loss or school refusal increases.\nDr R Mensah ST4","make":["Confirm and record consent from the young person before sharing with a parent.","Start with the headline, then pause and let the parent react.","Screen for co-occurring: anxiety, mood, self-harm, sleep, eating, attention, school attendance.","Agree how the young person hears the diagnosis and from whom.","Send the written report to parents and GP; school copy only with consent.","Offer post-diagnostic support: parent group, written information, named contact."],"next":"Isla meets the team the following week with her mother and asks to read her report. She chooses to tell two friends. The SENCO agrees a time-out card, a quiet lunch space and advance warning of timetable changes; adapted CBT for her anxiety starts a month later.","marks":["Gave the diagnosis plainly and early.","Explained late recognition in girls and masking.","Answered the Asperger's and cause questions without jargon.","Screened for anxiety, mood, self-harm, sleep and eating.","Explained that no medicine treats autism itself, and what helps instead.","Planned how Isla hears it and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Calling it a disease or promising a cure.","Talking only about deficits and forgetting her strengths.","Missing anxiety, school avoidance or eating difficulties.","Forgetting that Isla, not her mother, owns the diagnosis."],"refs":["1. NICE CG170 Autism in under 19s, support and management: 2013, last updated 2021 (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG128 Under-19s autism recognition guideline (2011, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6A02 Autism spectrum disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. Equality Act 2010; SEND Code of Practice 0 to 25 years (2015; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: many UK families and autistic people prefer identity-first language ('autistic') and talk of difference rather than disorder. Ask which words the young person prefers and use them.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-39","n":1239,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🚑","title":"Ibuprofen overdose at 15: agreeing the plan with the paediatric charge nurse","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 01:30, on call","where":"Paediatric emergency department, district general hospital","text":"Chloe Hughes, a 15-year-old girl, took sixteen 200 mg ibuprofen tablets at 22:00 after learning she had been left out of her friends' group chat. The ED team have cleared her medically. She has felt low for six weeks and told the ED doctor she was 'better off dead'. Her mother was on a night shift and is on her way. The paediatric charge nurse, Mr Sam Patel, 38, has six children waiting for cubicles and asks you: 'She's medically fit. Can she go home when mum arrives?'"},"task":"Explain to the paediatric charge nurse the likely outcome of your assessment and the management options: where Chloe stays tonight, how she is kept safe, consent and confidentiality, safeguarding, how tomorrow's decision is made and the follow-up offered.","first60":["Acknowledge the pressure first: 'I know you're full and you need a bed back.'","Check the medical clearance yourself: dose in mg/kg, timing, and paracetamol and salicylate levels at 4 hours.","Say the bottom line early: she shouldn't go home tonight; a paediatric bed is the right place.","Agree what keeps her safe overnight before you discuss tomorrow.","Write the plan in the notes and tell the nurse who to call if anything changes."],"ask":["To the nurse: 'What did ED check: weight, dose per kilo, paracetamol and salicylate levels at 4 hours?'","'Has she tried to leave, or harmed herself here?'","'Is there a paediatric bed or observation bay? Can her mum stay with her?'","'Is she known to children's social care? Has anyone checked CP-IS?'","'Who is the on-call CAMHS consultant tonight?'","To Chloe later: 'If you went home now, what would you do?'"],"recognise":["Medically fit is not the same as safe to discharge: suicidal intent, six weeks of low mood, hopelessness, a first presentation and no collateral yet.","Out of hours there is no school information, no social care check and no senior CAMHS review; a daytime decision is safer.","An adult psychiatric ward is not appropriate for a 15-year-old; MHA s131A requires an environment suitable for her age.","CAMHS inpatient admission is for severe, ongoing risk or illness not manageable at home, not a holding bed.","Ibuprofen under 100 mg/kg rarely causes serious toxicity; teenagers may under-report, so check the paracetamol level."],"decide":[{"c":"Admit overnight to a paediatric bed with agreed observations and a safe room, mother staying; full psychosocial assessment with mother in the morning by CAMHS liaison, with safeguarding checks and community follow-up planned.","r":"Keeps her safe, gathers the missing information, and fits NICE NG225: admission when there are safety concerns or safeguarding planning is incomplete.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge home when her mother arrives with a CAMHS referral.","r":"Unsafe: ongoing intent, no collateral, home not assessed, and a first presentation at night.","best":false},{"c":"Ask for transfer to the adult psychiatric ward tonight to free the cubicle.","r":"Inappropriate for a 15-year-old, breaches the age-appropriate duty, and no mental health bed is indicated yet.","best":false}],"law":["She is 15: assess Gillick competence for each decision. The MCA 2005 does not apply under 16.","If a Gillick-competent 15-year-old refuses to stay, do not rely on parental consent to override her for mental health care (MHA Code of Practice, ch. 19); call the on-call CAMHS consultant and consider an MHA assessment if criteria are met.","MHA s131A: a patient under 18 admitted for mental disorder must be in an environment suitable for their age.","Confidentiality can be breached when there is risk of serious harm; her mother is told about the overdose and the safety plan. Tell Chloe what will be shared and why.","Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023): if you suspect harm or neglect, refer to children's social care; record your reasoning either way."],"say":[{"to":"Charge nurse","s":"I know you need the cubicle and I'll help you get it. She's medically fit, but she still feels she'd be better off dead, and we've no information from home or school yet."},{"to":"Charge nurse","s":"The safest plan is a paediatric bed tonight with her mum, and our liaison team sees them both in the morning. Most young people go home from that with a safety plan and quick follow-up."},{"to":"Charge nurse","s":"If she tries to leave, please don't let her walk out. Call me straight away; I'll come and involve the on-call consultant."}],"write":"01:50 Discussed with Charge Nurse S Patel, paeds ED.\nChloe Hughes 15F. Ibuprofen 3.2 g (~58 mg/kg) at 22:00 after peer exclusion online. Paracetamol and salicylate negative at 4 h; ED medically cleared.\nRisk: stated 'better off dead'; low mood 6 weeks; first presentation; alone at home at time; mother en route. Collateral and school information not yet available.\nPlan: overnight admission to paeds observation bed; mother to stay; ligature-reduced room, belongings checked with consent, no medicines in room; obs within eyesight (intermittent 15 min when mother present and settled).\nIf attempts to leave: staff to call on-call psychiatry; Gillick competence to be assessed; on-call CAMHS consultant informed.\nSafeguarding: CP-IS checked by ED nurse; hospital safeguarding team to be informed in morning.\nAM: CAMHS liaison psychosocial assessment with Chloe and mother, collateral from school/GP with consent; decide discharge with safety plan and community CAMHS follow-up within 7 days, or referral for CAMHS inpatient care.\nDr F Ali CT2, bleep 3361","make":["Check dose in mg/kg: ibuprofen under 100 mg/kg is rarely serious; above 400 mg/kg risks severe toxicity.","Confirm 4-hour paracetamol and salicylate levels are back and negative.","Agree the observation level with the nurse in charge and write it down.","Room: remove cords, medicines and sharps; check belongings with her agreement.","Check CP-IS and the safeguarding record; tell the hospital safeguarding team in hours.","Hand over to the CAMHS liaison team for a morning assessment with her mother."],"next":"Chloe sleeps on the paediatric ward with her mother beside her. In the morning the CAMHS liaison nurse and registrar see them both, speak to her head of year with consent, and agree a safety plan. She goes home that afternoon with a community CAMHS appointment in four days and a plan with school about the online bullying.","marks":["Acknowledged the bed pressure before disagreeing.","Checked the medical clearance and paracetamol level.","Explained why medically fit is not safe to go home tonight.","Explained why a paediatric bed, not a psychiatric ward.","Agreed observations, a safe room and a plan if she tries to leave.","Covered safeguarding, confidentiality and tomorrow's follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Arguing guidelines without hearing the nurse's pressure.","Agreeing to discharge because she is 'medically fit'.","Suggesting an adult psychiatric bed.","Forgetting Gillick competence or what her mother will be told."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 19: children and young people (checked Nov 2026)","3. HM Government. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. TOXBASE: ibuprofen overdose (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK paediatric EDs a young person who self-harms is usually admitted to a paediatric bed, not a psychiatric ward, while CAMHS assesses. Under 16, use Gillick competence, not the Mental Capacity Act.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-40","n":1240,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🚑","title":"A headache and strange new words: history of early-onset psychosis","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 17:20","where":"Emergency department, district general hospital","text":"Jayden Clarke, a 16-year-old boy, came to A&E with a headache since yesterday. Obs, a neurological check and fundoscopy are normal. The ED doctor asks for a psychiatric review because he says the headache comes from 'the device they put in'. His mother is in the waiting room. When you arrive he glances behind him and says, 'Not here. They can hear what I think.'"},"task":"Take a history from Jayden to establish whether he has psychosis: delusions and their conviction, hallucinations, thought interference and other first-rank symptoms, thought disorder including neologisms, and the organic, substance, mood and developmental questions needed for the differential, with a risk screen.","first60":["Read the ED notes: obs, neuro findings, fundoscopy, any bloods or drug screen.","Find a quiet room near the door; offer him the seat he prefers.","Start with what he is worried about, in his words, and his fear of being overheard.","Do not argue with the belief or agree with it; be curious.","Observe as you go: glancing round, muttering, pauses, odd words; you are doing the mental state at the same time."],"ask":["'Tell me about the headache. When did it start? What do you think is causing it?'","'How sure are you about the device, out of 100? Could there be any other explanation?'","'Do you ever hear voices when no one is around? What do they say? Do they speak to you, or talk about you?'","'Can other people know what you're thinking? Can anyone put thoughts in or take them out?'","'Does anyone control your body or your actions?'","'Any head injury, fits, fever, vomiting, blurred vision or weakness?'","'What have you used: cannabis, skunk, spice, cocaine, ketamine, alcohol? When did you last use?'","'Has anyone in the family had problems like this?'","'Have you thought of doing anything about the people tracking you? Do you carry anything to protect yourself?'"],"recognise":["Psychosis: fixed false belief (implanted device, government monitoring), auditory hallucinations in the third person, thought broadcast, neologisms, guardedness, responding to unseen stimuli.","Prodrome: months of social withdrawal, stopped college, discarded phone and laptop.","Organic red flags in headache: worse lying down or on waking, vomiting, new focal signs, seizures, fever, confusion, papilloedema. Any of these: neuroimaging and medical review first.","Differentials: schizophrenia, drug-induced psychosis (cannabis, synthetic cannabinoids, stimulants), psychotic depression or mania, organic causes (encephalitis, epilepsy, tumour, thyroid), autism with unusual beliefs.","Risk: acting on delusions against 'trackers', command hallucinations, self-harm, self-neglect and vulnerability."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full psychosis history with organic and substance screen, assess risk, explain the next steps, speak to his mother with his agreement, and refer to early intervention in psychosis after medical tests.","r":"Establishes psychosis, excludes treatable causes and moves him into the right service quickly.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose schizophrenia and start an antipsychotic in A&E.","r":"Too soon: organic and drug causes are not excluded, and NICE advises specialist initiation for young people.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him there is no device and discharge with GP follow-up.","r":"Argues with a delusion, loses trust and misses risk; first-episode psychosis needs specialist assessment.","best":false}],"law":["He is 16: the MCA 2005 applies; presume capacity and assess it for each decision.","If he refuses assessment or treatment and the risk is significant, an MHA assessment can be requested; for 16 to 17s with capacity, parents cannot consent to admission against his wishes (MHA s131).","MHA s131A: under-18s admitted for mental disorder need an age-appropriate environment.","Share information with his mother with his agreement; without it, share what is needed if there is a serious risk."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see this is really frightening for you. I'm not here to argue, I want to understand what's been happening."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Sometimes, when people are very stressed or the brain is under strain, it can create experiences that feel completely real. I'd like to check whether that might be part of this."},{"to":"ED doctor","s":"This looks like a first episode of psychosis in a 16-year-old. Please send bloods and a urine drug screen; I'll refer to early intervention and speak to his mother."}],"write":"17:55 Psychiatric review in ED. Jayden Clarke 16M, with mother.\nHPC: headache 1 day, dull, frontal; no vomiting, visual change, focal symptoms, fever, head injury or seizures. ED: obs and neuro normal, fundi normal.\nPsychosis: belief government implanted a device in his brain this week (conviction 100%), monitored via devices for 6 months; threw phone/laptop in canal. Third-person voices for 1 week commenting and saying 'don't trust the doctor'. Thought broadcast. Neologism ('zintered'). Responding to unseen stimuli at interview. No passivity.\nMood: frightened, poor sleep; no manic or depressive syndrome.\nSubstances: weekend cannabis until ~4 months ago; denies spice, stimulants, alcohol.\nBackground: normal development; stopped college 3 weeks ago; father had psychiatric admission ('paranoid').\nRisk: no SI or self-harm; no intent to harm others, no weapons; vulnerable, no insight.\nImp: first-episode psychosis; organic and drug causes to exclude.\nPlan: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium, CRP, urine drug screen; EIP referral today; discuss with on-call CAMHS consultant; speak to mother with consent.\nDr L Okoro CT2","make":["Test conviction gently: 'How sure are you? What would it take to change your mind?'","Clarify any odd word: 'I'm not sure I know that word. Can you tell me what it means?' Record it verbatim.","Ask about all first-rank symptoms: thought echo, insertion, withdrawal, broadcast, voices arguing or commenting, passivity, delusional perception.","Headache red flags and neuro screen before you call it psychiatric.","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium, CRP; urine drug screen; consider HIV and syphilis tests, MRI or EEG if indicated.","Refer to early intervention in psychosis the same day."],"next":"Jayden's bloods and drug screen are normal. With his agreement, you speak to his mother, who confirms three months of withdrawal and describes his father's illness. The early intervention team assess him at home within days and start CBT for psychosis, family intervention and, after discussion, a low-dose antipsychotic.","marks":["Explored the belief and tested conviction without arguing or colluding.","Asked about voices and every first-rank symptom.","Clarified a neologism and noted responding to voices.","Asked headache red flags, head injury, fits and fever.","Took substance, mood, development and family history.","Assessed risk to self and others, and explained the next steps."],"pitfalls":["Arguing with the delusion or pretending to believe it.","Skipping the organic screen because ED 'cleared' him.","Asking only about cannabis and missing synthetic cannabinoids and stimulants.","Forgetting risk to others when someone feels persecuted."],"refs":["1. NICE CG155 Psychosis and schizophrenia in children and young people: recognition and management (2013, updated 2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS England. Early intervention in psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG150 Headaches in over 12s: diagnosis and management (2012, updated 2021; checked Nov 2026)","4. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 19 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK early intervention in psychosis teams see people aged 14 to 65, and the national standard expects treatment to start within two weeks of referral. Refer the same day from A&E.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-41","n":1241,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"🚑","title":"Explaining early-onset psychosis and possible detention to his father","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 18:40","where":"Paediatric area of A&E, relatives' room","text":"You are the CAMHS on-call core trainee (CT2). Josh Whitfield, 16, was brought in by paramedics after pacing in the street, shouting and stepping into traffic to 'get away from them'. Over three months he has withdrawn to his room and muttered to himself. Today he believes classmates are filming him through his phone and hears voices commenting on him. Bloods, urine drug screen, ECG and CT head are normal; the paediatric team have checked him over and found no physical cause. He lacks capacity to decide about admission and refuses to stay. Your consultant has asked the AMHP to assess him for Section 2. His father, Mr Paul Whitfield, 48, a delivery driver, has just arrived; his mother is on a night shift and asked you to speak to him. Josh has agreed that you can talk to his dad."},"task":"Explain to Josh's father what psychosis is, the treatment plan, early intervention and why Josh may be detained under the Mental Health Act, and answer his fears.","first60":["Introduce yourself and confirm Josh agreed you could talk to his dad.","Ask what he has noticed at home and what he already understands before you explain anything.","Ask what worries him most; write down his questions so none get lost.","Tell him Josh is safe now and the physical tests are normal.","Plan the order: psychosis, causes, treatment, early intervention, the law, his rights."],"ask":["'What have you noticed in Josh over the last few months?'","'What has anyone told you so far today?'","'What worries you most right now?'","'Has Josh used cannabis or anything else, as far as you know?'","'Is there anyone in the family who has had mental health problems?'","'Does that make sense? Can you tell me in your words what psychosis is?'","'Who will be at home to support you and Josh's mum tonight?'"],"recognise":["Months of withdrawal and muttering before a crisis is a common prodrome; families often read it as normal teenage behaviour.","First-episode psychosis needs organic causes excluded first; say clearly this has been done.","At 16 or 17, a parent cannot consent to an admission that deprives the young person of liberty; the Mental Health Act is the lawful route.","Parents fear 'mad', 'dangerous', 'locked up for ever' and 'zombified'; each needs a direct, honest answer.","Early treatment and early intervention support improve the outlook; delay worsens it."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain psychosis, the normal tests, treatment and early intervention, then why Section 2 is being considered, with rights and appeal.","r":"Answers his fears in order, keeps him on side and protects Josh lawfully.","best":true},{"c":"Let his father take him home tonight because the parents can look after him.","r":"Unsafe: Josh stepped into traffic, lacks capacity and refuses help; home cannot keep him safe tonight.","best":false},{"c":"Admit him informally on his father's consent.","r":"Unlawful at 16 if it deprives him of liberty; parental consent cannot authorise this.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Health Act 1983, section 2: admission for assessment (and treatment) for up to 28 days; an AMHP applies on two medical recommendations, one from a section 12 approved doctor.","Section 131(4): a 16 or 17-year-old with capacity cannot be admitted informally against their wishes on a parent's consent. If they lack capacity and admission deprives them of liberty, parents cannot consent either (Re D, Supreme Court 2019).","Section 131A: under-18s must be in an environment suited to their age, usually an adolescent inpatient unit.","Nearest relative (here, a parent): the AMHP must consult them; they can ask for discharge with 72 hours' written notice to the hospital managers (section 23), which the responsible clinician can bar if Josh would be dangerous (section 25).","Josh can appeal to the Mental Health Tribunal within the first 14 days of Section 2 and must be told his rights (section 132) and offered an IMHA."],"say":[{"to":"Father (diagnosis)","s":"Psychosis means that, for now, Josh's mind is struggling to tell what's real from what isn't. He's hearing voices that others can't hear and believes things that aren't true, like being filmed."},{"to":"Father ('Is he mad?')","s":"No. He is unwell, the way someone with pneumonia is unwell. It's an illness, and it's treatable."},{"to":"Father (the law)","s":"Josh doesn't feel ill, so he won't stay. Because he's at risk tonight, the law allows us to keep him in hospital to assess and treat him. It's for up to 28 days, not for ever, and he can appeal."}],"write":"CAMHS on-call note, A&E:\nSeen: Mr Paul Whitfield (father, nearest relative), with Josh's consent.\nExplained: working diagnosis first-episode psychosis; organic screen normal (bloods, UDS, ECG, CT head).\nExplained: plan for adolescent inpatient admission; AMHP assessment for Section 2 under way; rights, 28 days, Tribunal appeal within 14 days, IMHA, his rights as nearest relative.\nTreatment discussed: low-dose antipsychotic after ward assessment, family intervention, CBT for psychosis, early intervention follow-up.\nFather's concerns: dangerousness, 'zombified', cause; answered. Teach-back correct.\nLeaflets: Section 2 and psychosis (Rethink, Mind). Contact: ward and CAMHS crisis line given.\nPlan: 1:1 nurse in A&E until bed; consultant to call mother tomorrow 09:00.","make":["AMHP and section 12 doctor to complete the Section 2 assessment tonight; consultant gives one recommendation.","Bed manager to find an adolescent inpatient bed (section 131A); 1:1 nursing in A&E while waiting.","Refer to the early intervention in psychosis service now so they meet Josh on the ward.","Give the father Section 2 and nearest relative leaflets, and a Rethink or Mind leaflet on psychosis.","Book a family meeting with the ward consultant within a week, with both parents."],"next":"Two days later Josh's mother rings the ward, angry that nobody called her and asking to discharge him as nearest relative. Listen first, explain the 72-hour notice and that the responsible clinician can bar discharge if Josh would be a danger, and offer a meeting with the consultant the same week.","marks":["Confirms consent and asks what the father knows and has noticed first.","Explains psychosis in plain words with Josh's own symptoms.","States the physical tests are normal and what they ruled out.","Answers 'mad', 'dangerous' and 'locked up for ever' directly and honestly.","Explains treatment: low-dose medicine, family work, CBT, early intervention.","Explains Section 2, 28 days, appeal, IMHA and the nearest relative's rights.","Checks understanding by teach-back and gives leaflets, contacts and next steps."],"pitfalls":["Launching into a lecture before finding out what he knows.","Using jargon: 'delusions', 'first-rank symptoms', 'section 12'.","Saying 'schizophrenia' on day one.","Implying the parents can sign him in instead of the Act.","Forgetting the right to appeal and the nearest relative's role."],"refs":["1. NICE CG155 Psychosis and schizophrenia in children and young people (2013, updated 2016, checked Nov 2026)","2. Code of Practice to the Mental Health Act 1983 (2015), chapter 19, on children and young people (checked Nov 2026)","3. Re D (A Child) [2019] UKSC 42 (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF for Children: aripiprazole (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS England: Early intervention in psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England a parent's consent cannot replace the Mental Health Act for a 16 or 17-year-old who refuses or lacks capacity and would be deprived of liberty. Ask the AMHP early; they also consult the parents.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-42","n":1242,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"⚡","title":"Rhyming, restless and overfamiliar: mental state in a 15-year-old","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 21:15","where":"A&E, paediatric side room","text":"You are the CAMHS on-call core trainee (CT1). Maya Rossi, 15, has been brought in by her parents. For about a week she has been 'buzzing': sleeping three hours a night, hardly eating, skipping school and staying up writing what she calls a new theory of particle physics. Tonight she shouted at her sister and threw a phone. The paediatric registrar has checked her physically and sent bloods; the urine drug screen is negative. Maya is smiling, restless and talking fast. Your consultant is at home and wants a full mental state and risk picture by phone before deciding on admission."},"task":"See Maya on her own, carry out a full mental state assessment for mania, including risk to herself, to other people and from other people, then present it concisely to your consultant.","first60":["Ask the parents to wait outside; explain confidentiality and its limits to Maya.","Start observing at once: dress, make-up, restlessness, eye contact, overfamiliarity.","Let her talk for a minute: note rate, volume, rhymes, puns, how hard she is to interrupt.","Keep a calm, professional tone; do not match her energy or answer personal questions.","Plan to cover mood, thoughts, perception, cognition, insight and risk before you present."],"ask":["'How would you describe your mood these days, on a scale of 0 to 10?'","'How much sleep have you needed this week? Do you feel tired?'","'Tell me about the theory. What does it mean about you?'","'Do your thoughts feel like they're racing? Does anyone put thoughts in your head?'","'Have you been hearing voices or seeing things others couldn't?'","'Have you been spending money, or talking to people online you don't know?'","'Have there been times you felt very low, or thought of ending your life?'","'Do you think anything is wrong at the moment?'"],"recognise":["Elated mood, pressured speech with rhyming (clang associations), distractibility, little need for sleep and grandiosity over a week: a manic episode.","Overfamiliarity and irritability when challenged are part of the picture; respond calmly and with clear limits.","Young people with mania are vulnerable to sexual and online exploitation; ask directly and sensitively.","A grandiose idea may be a delusion; test conviction gently without arguing.","Always ask about low mood and suicidal thoughts: mixed states carry high risk.","Family history of unexplained mood swings or admission supports a bipolar picture."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete every MSE domain and a three-way risk check, then present in a structured way with a working diagnosis and differentials.","r":"Gives the consultant what they need to decide on admission tonight.","best":true},{"c":"Focus on the physics theory to prove it is delusional.","r":"Provokes irritability, wastes time and misses risk.","best":false},{"c":"Interview her with both parents present throughout.","r":"She is unlikely to disclose online contact, sex or drugs in front of them.","best":false}],"law":["Gillick competence: a 15-year-old may agree to confidential discussion; explain that you must share if she or someone else is at serious risk.","Children Act 1989 and Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023): contact from adults online asking to meet is a safeguarding concern; discuss with the consultant and make a referral if indicated.","Code of Practice to the Mental Health Act 1983 (2015), chapter 19: if admission is needed and she refuses, the Act is considered; for under-16s, decide with parents and the consultant whether parental consent is appropriate.","GMC 0–18 years guidance: involve young people in decisions and respect their confidentiality as far as safety allows."],"say":[{"to":"Maya (overfamiliar)","s":"I'm going to keep things about you, not me. That's how I work with everyone. Tell me more about your week."},{"to":"Maya (irritable)","s":"I can see I've annoyed you. That's not my aim. Let's leave the theory there and talk about sleep."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Maya Rossi, 15, a week of elated mood, three hours' sleep, pressured speech with rhyming and a grandiose idea about physics. No hallucinations. No insight. Risks: online strangers, overspending, poor intake."}],"write":"CAMHS on-call MSE, A&E:\nAppearance/behaviour: 15-year-old girl, heavy make-up, new clothes, restless, overfamiliar, distractible, briefly irritable when challenged.\nSpeech: pressured, loud, hard to interrupt, rhyming.\nMood: 'amazing', 10/10; affect elated, labile, irritable when contradicted.\nThought: flight of ideas; grandiose belief of a revolutionary physics theory, firmly held, possibly delusional; no passivity.\nPerception: no hallucinations.\nCognition: oriented, distractible.\nInsight: none.\nRisk: self (poor intake, 3 hours' sleep, spent £400 online, no suicidal thoughts); others (threw phone at sister); from others (19-year-old online wants to meet).\nImpression: manic episode, first. Differentials: substance-induced (UDS negative), organic (TFT pending), schizoaffective.\nPlan: discussed with Dr Adeyemi; admission to adolescent unit; safeguarding discussion re online contact.","make":["Phone the on-call consultant with a structured presentation and agree the admission decision.","Chase TFT, FBC, U&E, LFT and glucose; record pulse, BP and temperature.","Discuss the online contact with the hospital safeguarding team and document it.","Ask the parents for collateral: onset, previous low moods, grandmother's illness.","Arrange 1:1 nursing while she waits; remove access to parents' bank cards and phone if agreed with parents."],"next":"On the adolescent unit two days later, Maya is tearful one hour and euphoric the next and says she 'might as well die'. Reassess for a mixed state, review suicide risk and observation level, and inform the consultant the same day.","marks":["Sees her alone, explains confidentiality and its limits.","Comments on appearance, behaviour and speech as observed.","Explores mood, sleep, energy, appetite and grandiosity.","Asks directly about hallucinations and other delusions.","Asks about previous low mood and suicidal thoughts.","Covers risk to herself, to other people and from other people, including online contact.","Handles overfamiliarity and irritability calmly.","Presents a concise MSE, diagnosis, differentials and risk."],"pitfalls":["Arguing about the theory and escalating irritability.","Answering personal questions or laughing along with flirtation.","Forgetting perception or insight.","Missing the depressive and suicidal screen.","Not asking about exploitation and online contact."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management, section on children and young people (2014, updated 2023, checked Nov 2026)","2. ICD-11 6A60 Bipolar type I disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC: 0–18 years guidance for all doctors (checked Nov 2026)","4. Working Together to Safeguard Children 2023 (checked Nov 2026)","5. MHRA: valproate safety measures, 2024 update (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK child mental health, seeing a teenager alone for part of the assessment is routine and expected. Tell the parents this is standard practice, not secrecy.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-43","n":1243,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Starting methylphenidate: explaining the plan, the ECG and the checks to a woman with learning disability","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30","where":"Community learning disability team clinic","text":"You are the CT3 in the community learning disability team. Ms Bethany Lane, 24, has a moderate learning disability and lives in supported living. Last month she struggled to concentrate and sit still in her all-day Saturday catering course. Assessment has confirmed ADHD, and she has been told the diagnosis. The plan is to start methylphenidate at a low dose, increase it slowly and, if it helps, move to a once-daily long-acting form to cover her whole class day. She needs her pulse, blood pressure and weight checked first, and an ECG because her uncle has a heart rhythm problem and has a device fitted. Her support worker, Dawn, has brought her and is in the waiting room at Bethany's request."},"task":"Explain to Bethany, in clear, simple words, why methylphenidate is recommended, how it will be started, why she needs an ECG and physical checks, and the follow-up, checking her understanding throughout.","first60":["Use short sentences, one idea at a time, and her own words for her problem.","Ask what she remembers about ADHD and what she thinks might help.","Have an easy-read leaflet and a picture of an ECG ready.","Check understanding by asking her to tell you back, not 'Do you understand?'","Watch for worry, especially about the ECG, and slow down."],"ask":["'What do you remember about what we found last time?'","'What's hard for you at your cooking class?'","'Have you ever had a funny turn, a racing heart or chest pain, or fainted?'","'Does anyone in your family have heart problems?'","'Can you tell me back why we check your heart?'","'Would you like Dawn to come with you to the heart test?'","'What questions do you have?'"],"recognise":["People with learning disability are presumed to have capacity; the duty is to make information accessible so she can decide herself.","Stimulants can raise pulse and blood pressure and reduce appetite: that is why the checks matter.","A family history of heart rhythm problems or sudden death means an ECG before starting.","Fear of the ECG is common; a calm, concrete explanation and a support person usually solve it.","Start low and go slow is good practice in learning disability; side effects may be reported as behaviour change."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the medicine, the start-low plan, the ECG and checks in simple words, use a picture, check by teach-back and offer an easy-read leaflet and a follow-up.","r":"Supports her to make her own informed decision, as the MCA requires.","best":true},{"c":"Explain everything to Dawn instead, as Bethany might not follow.","r":"Bypasses Bethany, breaches the presumption of capacity and is disrespectful.","best":false},{"c":"Skip the ECG because she is frightened of it.","r":"Unsafe: with a family history of arrhythmia, the ECG is needed before a stimulant.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005, principles 1 and 2: presume capacity, and take all practicable steps (simple words, pictures, time) to help her decide.","Equality Act 2010: reasonable adjustments, such as longer appointments, easy-read leaflets and a support person.","NHS Accessible Information Standard: record and meet her communication needs.","Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001: methylphenidate is a Schedule 2 controlled drug; prescriptions are usually limited to 30 days' supply."],"say":[{"to":"Bethany (the medicine)","s":"The tablet is called methylphenidate. It helps your brain focus and helps you sit still for longer. Lots of people with ADHD take it."},{"to":"Bethany (the ECG)","s":"An ECG is a heart picture. A nurse puts small stickers on your chest, arms and legs. They listen to your heart. Nothing goes into you, and it doesn't hurt."},{"to":"Bethany (teach-back)","s":"I've told you a lot. Can you tell me, in your words, why we check your heart?"}],"write":"CLDT clinic note:\nSeen: Bethany Lane, 24, alone at her request; support worker in waiting room.\nExplained in simple language with pictures: ADHD; methylphenidate; start low, increase weekly; move to once-daily long-acting if helpful.\nBaseline: pulse 76, BP 118/72, weight 64 kg, height 1.60 m. No syncope, palpitations or chest pain. FH: uncle with arrhythmia and device.\nECG explained; initially anxious, then agreed with support worker present and female nurse.\nTeach-back correct for: reason for medicine, ECG, weight and BP checks.\nCapacity: understands, retains, weighs and communicates; consents to ECG and to methylphenidate if ECG normal.\nEasy-read leaflets given. Plan: ECG this week; start methylphenidate 5 mg twice daily if normal; phone review 1 week; clinic 4 weeks.","make":["Book the ECG this week with a female nurse; Dawn to attend.","If the ECG is abnormal, seek a cardiology opinion before any stimulant.","Start methylphenidate 5 mg at breakfast and lunchtime if the ECG is normal; increase weekly.","Record pulse, BP and weight at each dose change and every 6 months.","Send an easy-read letter to Bethany and a letter to her GP; plan shared care once the dose is stable."],"next":"Three weeks later Dawn rings: Bethany is skipping lunch and lying awake. Check weight, pulse and BP, ask about timing (give the last dose no later than early afternoon), advise breakfast before the tablet and a snack later, and review the dose.","marks":["Asks what she knows and what she finds hard first.","Uses short, simple sentences and checks by teach-back.","Explains what the medicine does and the start-low plan.","Explains the long-acting tablet for her all-day class.","Explains why pulse, BP and weight are checked.","Explains the ECG concretely and responds kindly to her fear.","Answers how long and what if it doesn't work; gives easy-read leaflet and follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Talking to the support worker instead of the patient.","Using words like 'titrate', 'cardiovascular' or 'arrhythmia'.","Asking 'Do you understand?' instead of teach-back.","Brushing past her fear of the ECG.","Sounding patronising: a childish tone or over-praise."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87 (2018, updated 2019): ADHD, diagnosis and management (checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: methylphenidate hydrochloride (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice, chapter 3 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England Accessible Information Standard (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: ADHD treatment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: 'teach-back' (asking the person to say it back in their own words) is the expected way to check understanding in the UK, especially with learning disability. It checks your explanation, not their intelligence.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-44","n":1244,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💔","title":"Explaining morbid jealousy to his partner, and her safety","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 17:30","where":"A&E, liaison psychiatry interview room","text":"You are the liaison psychiatry CT3. Mr Callum Reid, 32, came to A&E with cuts after falling off his bike; staff were worried by his talk. He is convinced his partner, Ms Hannah Brooks, 29, is having an affair with a colleague he saw beside her in a work-party photo five months ago. He checks her phone, logs her times home, followed the colleague home and bought a flick knife 'for protection'. Six years ago he stalked and assaulted a former partner over the same belief, was convicted and spent two years in a medium secure unit, where risperidone helped. He stopped it a year ago. He has no insight, refuses community follow-up and informal admission. You have asked the AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment. Callum has agreed you can talk to Hannah, who has just arrived. They live together; no children."},"task":"Explain to Hannah the likely diagnosis of morbid jealousy and the management plan, including admission, and talk honestly about her safety, including whether to separate.","first60":["Confirm Callum's consent to share, and see Hannah alone.","Ask what she knows and has noticed before you name anything.","Ask sensitively, without judgement, whether anything has happened with the colleague.","Prepare to be kind but firm: this is high risk, and admission is needed.","Have the domestic abuse helpline and the IDVA referral ready."],"ask":["'What have you noticed about Callum over the last few months?'","'Has he checked your phone or tracked when you come home?'","'Has he ever threatened or hurt you, or anyone else?'","'I have to ask, and I'm not judging: has anything happened with this colleague?'","'Did you know he was carrying a knife?'","'Are there children who live with you or visit?'","'Is there somewhere safe you can stay tonight?'"],"recognise":["Morbid jealousy is a fixed, false belief that a partner is unfaithful, built on trivial 'evidence'; it is a form of delusional disorder.","Checking her phone, logging her times, following the rival and buying a knife are escalating warning signs.","The past assault over the same belief is the strongest predictor; the risk is to Hannah and to the colleague.","Partners often minimise ('he's just protective'); the doctor must stay kind and persistent.","Even a real affair would not exclude the diagnosis; it is about how the belief is formed and held.","The belief may fade if the relationship ends but often returns with a new partner."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the diagnosis, the risk to her and the colleague, recommend admission (under the Act if he refuses), and give her a safety plan and support.","r":"Honest, protects both people at risk and keeps Hannah informed and supported.","best":true},{"c":"Agree to restart risperidone and send him home with her, as she wishes.","r":"Unsafe: same belief, a knife and stalking preceded the last assault; medicine takes weeks to work.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her she must leave him immediately.","r":"It is her decision; ordering her alienates her. Give honest advice and options instead.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Health Act 1983: if he refuses admission and the AMHP and two doctors agree, Section 2 or 3 (with his known diagnosis and response to treatment, Section 3 may fit).","GMC Confidentiality (2017): disclose to the police without consent where needed to protect others from serious harm; here the colleague is at risk.","Serious Crime Act 2015, section 76: coercive or controlling behaviour in an intimate relationship is an offence; phone checking and tracking can form part of it.","Domestic Violence Disclosure Scheme (Clare's Law): Hannah can ask the police about a partner's history of abuse.","Domestic Abuse Act 2021: abuse includes controlling and coercive behaviour; refer to an IDVA and MARAC if the DASH checklist shows high risk."],"say":[{"to":"Hannah (diagnosis)","s":"Callum has an illness called morbid jealousy. His brain has fixed on a false belief that you're unfaithful. No evidence will change his mind, because the illness is driving it."},{"to":"Hannah (risk)","s":"Six years ago the same belief led him to hurt a partner. Now he's bought a knife and followed your colleague home. I'd be letting you down if I didn't say clearly: I'm worried for you and for him."},{"to":"Hannah (separation)","s":"Whether you stay together is your decision, and we'll support you either way. For now, while he's this unwell, please don't be alone with him."}],"write":"Liaison note:\nSeen: Ms Hannah Brooks (partner), with Callum's consent.\nExplained: likely morbid jealousy (delusional disorder, jealous type), relapse of past illness; past assault over same belief.\nHannah's account: phone checking and logging times, minimised as 'protective'; denies affair; aware of knife; no threats to her; no children.\nRisk: high to Hannah and to colleague (followed home, knife). Police informed of risk to colleague (GMC public interest disclosure).\nPlan explained: MHA assessment under way; antipsychotic; risk assessment; forensic advice.\nSafety: Hannah to stay with sister tonight; DASH completed (high); IDVA referral; Refuge helpline given; Clare's Law explained.\nTeach-back correct. Follow-up call from ward within 48 hours.","make":["AMHP-led Mental Health Act assessment tonight; inform the consultant.","Ring the police: knife and risk to the named colleague; record the disclosure and its reasons.","Complete the DASH risk checklist with Hannah; refer to the IDVA and MARAC if high risk.","Contact his former forensic team for records and advice.","Ward to phone Hannah within 48 hours and invite her to a carer meeting."],"next":"A week later, on the ward, Callum asks Hannah to bring his phone so he can 'check something'. Hannah rings you for advice. Thank her, advise her not to bring it, alert the nurse in charge, review his leave and risk plan, and keep her updated.","marks":["Confirms consent and asks what she knows and has noticed.","Explains morbid jealousy as a false, fixed belief in plain words.","Asks sensitively about any affair and about threats or violence.","States the risk to her and to the colleague clearly, using the history.","Explains why admission is needed now, under the Act if he refuses.","Explains treatment, prognosis and recurrence honestly.","Addresses separation honestly and leaves a safety plan and support."],"pitfalls":["Giving in and agreeing to send him home on tablets.","Avoiding the past assault for fear of upsetting her.","Calling her naive or ordering her to leave him.","Forgetting the risk to the colleague and the duty to share.","No safety plan, helpline or IDVA referral."],"refs":["1. Kingham and Gordon (2004), 'Aspects of morbid jealousy', Adv Psychiatr Treat 10: 207–15","2. GMC: Confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (2017, checked Nov 2026)","3. Domestic Abuse Act 2021 and statutory guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","5. BNF: risperidone (checked Nov 2026)","6. SafeLives: DASH risk checklist and MARAC guidance (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, phone checking and tracking a partner can be coercive control, a crime. Name it gently, and offer the partner the national domestic abuse helpline and an IDVA, even if she sees him as 'just protective'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-45","n":1245,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💌","title":"Presenting erotomania to your consultant: diagnosis and plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 00:45","where":"A&E, liaison psychiatry office (consultant on the phone)","text":"You are the liaison psychiatry CT2 on nights. Police brought Ms Rachel Doyle, 38, a dental nurse, after the wife of her employer, Mr Simon Hale, 52, found her in their back garden at 23:00. She had a kitchen knife in her bag 'in case his wife attacks me'. Over seven months she has become certain Mr Hale secretly loves her, since he praised her at the Christmas party. She has sent over 200 texts and cards, gone to his house four times, and believes his wife intercepts his replies. He has asked her to stop; she is suspended from work and received a police warning last month. Her mood is normal, there is no thought disorder or hallucination, cognition is grossly intact, and she has no insight. She has hypothyroidism on levothyroxine, drinks a bottle of wine at weekends and has no psychiatric or forensic history. She lives alone. You are about to ring your consultant, Dr Ruth Mensah."},"task":"Present the assessment concisely to your consultant, give erotomania as the most likely diagnosis with your reasons and differentials, and propose an immediate and longer-term, risk-based management plan.","first60":["Write a one-line opener: name, age, why here, the headline risk.","Order: presentation, history, MSE, risk, diagnosis, differentials, plan.","Aim for under 2 minutes for the summary; let the consultant ask questions.","List the extra information you need: police, family, GP, workplace.","Have the immediate safety question ready: can she stay safely in A&E, and does she agree to admission?"],"ask":["'Can I present a new patient with a significant risk to others?'","Before you ring: has she been medically cleared, and is she intoxicated?","Before you ring: has the knife been taken by police, and is she on a 1:1 with security?","Before you ring: does she agree to stay or to admission?","'Would you like me to request police records and speak to her sister?'","'Shall I contact the on-call forensic consultant for advice?'"],"recognise":["A fixed belief that a higher-status person secretly loves her, despite clear rejection, with stalking: erotomania (de Clérambault's), a form of delusional disorder.","Seven months, circumscribed belief, normal mood, no hallucinations or thought disorder: fits delusional disorder better than schizophrenia or mania.","Danger rises when love turns to resentment or the 'rival' (here his wife) is seen as the obstacle; she carried a knife.","Erotomania is persistent; untreated, it is unlikely to go away on its own.","Both Mr Hale and his wife are at risk; police and information sharing are part of the plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Present concisely, name erotomania with reasons, give differentials and propose admission (under the Act if she refuses), police liaison and a long-term biopsychosocial plan.","r":"Gives the consultant a safe, structured basis to decide tonight.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge with CMHT follow-up because she is calm and euthymic.","r":"Ignores a knife, a named rival and escalating stalking; unsafe.","best":false},{"c":"Read the whole clerking down the phone.","r":"Too long; the risk gets lost. Consultants want a structured summary and a plan.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Health Act 1983: if she refuses admission and meets the criteria, Section 2 (first presentation, assessment needed). While she waits in A&E, Section 5(2) does not apply (she is not an inpatient).","Mental Capacity Act 2005: if she lacks capacity and tries to leave A&E before the MHA assessment, staff may act in her best interests to prevent serious harm, short term.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): disclose to police without consent to protect Mr Hale and his wife from serious harm; record the reasons.","Stalking Protection Act 2019: police can apply for a Stalking Protection Order; Protection from Harassment Act 1997 underpins her earlier warning.","The Fixated Threat Assessment Centre handles threats to public figures; it does not apply to a private colleague."],"say":[{"to":"Consultant (opener)","s":"Rachel Doyle, 38, dental nurse, brought by police after being found in her employer's garden with a knife. I think this is erotomania with significant risk to him and his wife."},{"to":"Consultant (diagnosis)","s":"Seven months of a fixed belief that a higher-status man loves her, despite rejection, with stalking and no mood or psychotic features beyond it. That's delusional disorder, erotomanic type."},{"to":"Consultant (plan)","s":"Tonight: medically cleared, 1:1 with security, offer informal admission and request an MHA assessment if she refuses. Share the risk with police. Longer term: antipsychotic, stalking risk assessment, no contact, work and finances."}],"write":"Liaison note, presented to Dr Mensah 00:50:\nImpression: delusional disorder, erotomanic type, 7 months. Differentials: schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, substance-induced psychosis, organic (thyroid; TFT sent).\nRisk: high to Mr Hale and his wife (knife, 4 home visits, rival blamed); low current risk to self (ask again at review).\nPlan: medically cleared; 1:1 with security; knife held by police; informal admission offered and declined; MHA assessment requested (AMHP called 01:00).\nCollateral: police records, GP summary, sister (consent declined; risk overrides for police only).\nInformation shared with police to protect Mr Hale and wife (GMC public interest), reasons recorded.\nForensic on-call advice to be sought in the morning.\nConsultant agrees.","make":["Request an MHA assessment via the AMHP if she declines admission.","Ask police for the harassment record and the knife details; share the current risk with them.","Send TFT, FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose and a urine drug screen; record vital signs.","Seek forensic advice; consider a stalking risk assessment on the ward.","Ensure the police tell Mr Hale and his wife of the plan and any discharge."],"next":"Three weeks into admission, Rachel's belief is unchanged but she says she'll 'move on' and asks for leave. Discuss with the consultant, check for signs of covert pursuit (letters, social media), involve police before any leave, and do not rely on her word alone.","marks":["Opens with a one-line summary and the headline risk.","Presents history and MSE in a clear order, within 2 minutes.","Names erotomania and gives the features that support it.","Gives differentials, including organic and substance causes.","Lists extra sources of information.","Gives an immediate plan: medical clearance, setting, legal route.","Gives a long-term biological, psychological and social plan with risk management and police liaison."],"pitfalls":["Reading the clerking verbatim and running out of time.","Calling it schizophrenia without justifying it, or naming only 'delusional disorder' without the type.","Forgetting the risk to the wife as the perceived rival.","Suggesting the Fixated Threat Assessment Centre for a private colleague.","No plan if she tries to leave A&E."],"refs":["1. ICD-11 6A24 Delusional disorder (checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC: Confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (2017, checked Nov 2026)","4. Stalking Protection Act 2019 and Home Office statutory guidance (checked Nov 2026)","5. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Assessing and managing stalking risk (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK consultants expect a short structured presentation (one-line summary, key findings, impression, risk, plan) and then questions. Give your own impression and plan; don't wait to be asked.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-46","n":1246,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🔔","title":"Fire in the activity room: psychopathology and risk after ward fire-setting","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:10, ward doctor","where":"Male acute admissions ward, adult mental health unit","text":"The fire alarm has just been silenced. Ryan Kelly, a 28-year-old man on Section 2, admitted four days ago after his parents found him walking the hard shoulder of the motorway, lit a pile of paper and paint pots in the activity room next to the fire exit. A nurse put it out with an extinguisher. Nobody is hurt. He is sitting in the quiet room, smelling of smoke, calm and polite. The nurse in charge asks you to see him now."},"task":"Explore the psychotic experiences behind the fire-setting, assess the risk of a further fire and other risks, and agree immediate ward safety actions with the nurse in charge.","first60":["Check with the nurse in charge: is everyone accounted for, anyone hurt or smoke-affected, has the fire service been called (trust policy), and has he been searched for a lighter?","Look at him before you sit down: burns to the hands, singed hair or eyebrows, coughing, hoarse voice or soot round the nose and mouth mean airway review first.","Sit at an angle near the door, a nurse with you; introduce yourself and say why you have come.","Open with him, not the fire: 'What has been happening for you since you came in?'","Keep the fire questions until you understand his beliefs; asking 'why did you do it?' in the first minute gets you a closed door."],"ask":["'What was happening in the weeks before your parents brought you to hospital?'","'When did things first start to feel different? What happened at that moment?'","'What do you understand your purpose to be? How would you carry it out?'","'Are you getting messages or signs meant just for you, from the radio, TV or people around you?'","'Do you hear anyone speaking when no one is there? Is it addressed to you, or do they discuss you? Do they tell you to do things, and do you do them?'","'Do thoughts get put into your head, or taken out of it? What were they today?'","'Why did you light the fire, and why that room? How did you get the lighter?'","'Might you light another one? What, if anything, would stop you?'"],"recognise":["Delusional perception: a normal perception (finding an object) gives a sudden, unrelated, fixed meaning. A Schneiderian first-rank symptom.","Grandiose delusion of a mission: always screen for mania (elation, sleep, energy, pressure of speech) before calling it schizophrenia.","Referential delusions: neutral sources (radio adverts, number plates) carry personal messages.","Third-person voices commenting or discussing him are first-rank; command voices he obeys raise risk directly.","Thought insertion and withdrawal (passivity of thought) linked to the act: an inserted thought that removes fear lowers the barrier to acting.","Fire-setting motive here is instrumental (to escape), planned, with fuel and an exit chosen, and with stated intent to repeat: the risk is current and high.","Protective: no persecutory ideas, no wish to harm others or himself, family involved, already detained."],"decide":[{"c":"Finish the psychopathology and fire-risk assessment now, start enhanced observations within eyesight, search him and his room for ignition sources, inform the RC today, complete an incident report and consider a PICU referral.","r":"Right: the fire was planned, driven by commands and inserted thoughts, and he says he will repeat it. Safety measures must start before the end of your shift.","best":true},{"c":"Record 'fire set while unwell, no harm done' and leave the risk review to the ward round on Monday.","r":"Unsafe: he has stated intent to repeat, has access to fire-escape routes and is untreated. The risk is today, not next week.","best":false},{"c":"Ask the police to arrest him for arson and take him off the ward.","r":"Wrong first step: he is acutely psychotic and detained for treatment. Police involvement is a team decision with the RC under the trust's policy, alongside clinical care, not instead of it.","best":false}],"law":["He is on Section 2: in the first three months, Section 63 allows treatment for mental disorder without consent, so refusal of antipsychotics can be lawfully managed after a proper attempt at consent.","Searching a detained patient or his room follows the trust search policy and MHA Code of Practice chapter 8: explain why, seek consent, use the least intrusive search, record it.","Arson endangering life is a serious offence; whether to report to police is decided with the RC and nurse in charge under trust policy, considering victims (staff and patients) and his mental state.","If he moves to a PICU, Section 2 continues; the receiving unit needs the section papers and a risk summary."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm glad you're not hurt. I'd like to understand what's been going on for you these past weeks, so I can make sense of today. Is that OK?"},{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. I'm worried another fire could hurt you or someone else, so staff will stay close to you for now."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He set it deliberately to trigger the alarm and escape, on the orders of voices, and says he'd do it again. Let's start eyesight observations now, search him and his room for lighters, and I'll ring the RC."}],"write":"15:40 Seen after fire in activity room. 28M, Section 2, day 4.\nPhysical: no burns, no cough, sats 98% air, RR 16.\nMSE: calm, polite, good eye contact. Mood 'focused', not elated. Delusional perception (finding late grandfather's compass, 'knew' he was chosen). Grandiose delusion: mission to switch off phone masts. Referential delusions (radio). Delusion of implant in tooth. 2nd- and 3rd-person AH with commands, obeys. Thought insertion and withdrawal via implant. No broadcast, no passivity of action, no formal thought disorder. No insight.\nFire: planned, lighter kept from smoking shelter, chose room with paper and fire exit, aim to escape; no intent to harm. Will repeat; nothing would stop him except discharge.\nOther risks: high AWOL, refuses medication, no SH/SI, no intent to harm others.\nRisk: HIGH, imminent, of further fire-setting and absconding.\nPlan: eyesight obs; search person and room for ignition sources (consent sought, policy followed); smoking shelter escorted only; RC informed 15:45; PICU referral discussed; incident report done; family to be told by RC; Section 63 treatment plan with RC today.\nDr S. Okafor CT2","make":["Psychopathology in order of onset: how it started, the key moment, the mission, other beliefs, the implant, voices, thoughts, mood, insight.","Fire: antecedents (beliefs, commands, opportunity), behaviour (planning, ignition source, fuel, location, exit), consequences (what he did next, feelings now, would he repeat).","Other risks: absconding, harm to others to escape, self-harm, refusing medication, vulnerability.","Immediate actions: observation level, search, smoking access, RC, incident report, PICU decision, police decision, family."],"next":"The RC reviews him at 17:00, agrees eyesight observations and starts an antipsychotic under Section 63 after he declines. A PICU bed is offered next morning. The incident review asks how a lighter reached the ward from the smoking shelter.","marks":["Checked for injury and smoke inhalation first.","Elicited the delusional perception and how the belief grew.","Characterised the voices: person, content, commands and obedience.","Linked inserted thoughts and commands to the fire.","Established planning, motive, ignition source and intent to repeat.","Screened mood for elation, and insight.","Agreed concrete ward safety actions and escalated to the RC."],"pitfalls":["Opening with 'Why did you start a fire?' and getting stuck in an argument.","Ticking first-rank symptoms like a list without linking them to the act.","Missing the question 'Would you do it again?'","Forgetting absconding and refusal of medication as linked risks."],"refs":["1. NICE NG10: short-term management of violence and aggression (2015; checked Nov 2026)","2. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters 8 and 26 (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Assessment and management of risk to others, CR201 (2016; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK units, lighters are usually banned on wards and patients smoke (if at all) in a supervised shelter. After any fire, check how the lighter got in: it shapes the safety plan and the incident report.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-47","n":1247,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🛏","title":"He has just hit another patient: MSE and violence risk assessment","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 10:20, on call","where":"Mixed general adult ward, adult mental health unit","text":"Dean Holloway, a 45-year-old man admitted overnight on Section 2, punched and kicked a 30-year-old patient who said good morning to him in the corridor. Staff restrained him for three minutes; he has declined oral lorazepam. The other patient has a cut eyebrow and is being checked. The nurse in charge asks you to assess Mr Holloway now in the low-stimulus room."},"task":"Assess his mental state and the risk of further violence (historical, clinical and situational factors), and agree immediate measures to keep everyone on the ward safe.","first60":["Ask the nurse in charge: who is hurt and how badly, is the victim being examined, how long was the restraint and in what position, any PRN given or refused.","Check post-restraint safety: breathing, colour, injuries; ask nursing to start obs (pulse, BP, RR, sats, temperature) and NEWS2.","See him with a nurse, sit nearer the door, keep the room quiet and don't block his way out.","Open with who you are and what you are not: 'I'm one of the doctors. I'm not police, and I'm not here to harm you.'","Ask about the incident in his words before you ask about anything else."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what happened in the corridor this morning?'","'What did you think he was going to do? How did you know?'","'What did you want to happen when you hit him?'","'Do you feel safe on the ward now? Is anyone else here a danger to you?'","'Have you heard anyone talking when nobody was there?'","'Have the police ever been involved because of fights? Ever been to prison?'","'What medicine were you on, and when did you stop it? Any cannabis, alcohol or other drugs?'","'If someone else came up to you today, what would you do?'"],"recognise":["Persecutory delusion with a named threat (an 'assassin') and a 'kill or be killed' stance is the strongest clinical driver of violence here.","Historical factors: previous violence and imprisonment, early-onset illness with many admissions, PICU stays, poor adherence, substance misuse, unemployment, isolation.","Clinical factors: active psychosis, no insight, intent to repeat, no remorse, refusing treatment, possible cannabis withdrawal.","Situational factors: a busy mixed ward, other patients he sees as disguised assassins, no social support, ongoing detention he resents.","Impulsive versus planned: he acted on the spot, but now has a plan to defend himself again, which makes the risk current.","Always assess risk to himself too: frightened, cornered patients can harm themselves or abscond."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the MSE and risk assessment, separate him from the victim, start enhanced observations, offer oral medication with a rapid tranquillisation plan ready, inform the RC and consider PICU today.","r":"Right: active persecutory beliefs, no remorse and intent to repeat mean the risk is immediate. Measures must start now, in the least restrictive way that is still safe.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him he must apologise and send him back to the day area once calm.","r":"Unsafe: his belief that patients are assassins is unchanged, so he may attack again. Calm behaviour does not mean lower risk.","best":false},{"c":"Give IM haloperidol straight away to prevent a repeat.","r":"Wrong: he is now calm and talking. Rapid tranquillisation is for imminent risk after de-escalation and oral options fail, and IM haloperidol needs a recent ECG.","best":false}],"law":["Section 2: Section 63 allows treatment for mental disorder without consent in the first three months, so medication can be given if he refuses, using the least restrictive route.","The victim has the right to report the assault to the police; staff support this. The decision to involve police is made with the victim, nurse in charge and RC under trust policy.","Restraint must be recorded, and he needs a post-incident debrief and physical checks (NICE NG10).","A transfer to PICU continues his section; the receiving team needs the section papers and a risk summary."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Mr Holloway, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors. I'm not police. I'd like to hear what happened this morning from you."},{"to":"Patient","s":"It sounds like you've been really frightened since you got here. That must be exhausting."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He believes patients are assassins in disguise and says he'd do it again. I'd like him nursed away from the main corridor on enhanced obs, and I'm ringing the RC about PICU."}],"write":"10:50 Reviewed after assault on co-patient 10:05. 45M, Section 2, admitted 02:00. Restrained 3 min (seated), no injuries, obs normal, NEWS2 0.\nMSE: guarded then forthcoming, good eye contact, tense. Speech normal. Mood 'scared'; no depressive or manic features. Persecutory delusions: MI5 plot, ward bugged, 'assassins' disguised as patients and staff. Heard whispering outside room overnight (denies it is internal). No insight. Cognition grossly intact.\nIncident: impulsive attack, intended to kill, no weapon, believed self-defence; no remorse; would repeat.\nHistorical: two prison sentences for violence, schizophrenia since 20s, multiple admissions incl. PICU, stopped medication months ago, daily cannabis, lives alone, unemployed.\nRisk to others: HIGH, imminent. Risk to self: low, no SI. AWOL: moderate.\nPlan: enhanced obs (within eyesight), nursed away from main corridor; oral olanzapine offered; RT plan written per NICE NG10; ECG when settled; RC informed 10:55; PICU referral; incident report; victim supported and offered police contact.\nDr K. Mensah CT1","make":["Mental state: appearance, behaviour (tension, scanning the room), speech, mood, thoughts (delusions: content, conviction, who is involved, plans), perceptions, cognition, insight.","Incident: before (trigger, beliefs), during (intent, weapon, target), after (feelings, remorse, intent to repeat).","Historical: violence, offending, prison, illness course, PICU, adherence, substances, relationships, employment.","Clinical now: symptoms, insight, intent, substances; situational: the ward, triggers, supports.","Risk to self and absconding."],"next":"The RC agrees enhanced observations and PICU referral. Mr Holloway accepts oral olanzapine at lunchtime after a second conversation. The other patient decides to report the assault; the police take a statement on Monday.","marks":["Checked victim and post-restraint safety before the interview.","Explained his role and was not confrontational about the beliefs.","Explored the delusional system: content, conviction, who is involved, plans.","Asked about hallucinations, mood and insight.","Took a structured history of past violence, illness, adherence and substances.","Established intent, remorse and intent to repeat.","Assessed risk to self and agreed immediate measures."],"pitfalls":["Arguing that the other patient 'was not an assassin'.","Assessing only the incident and forgetting historical factors.","Equating calm now with low risk.","Forgetting risk to himself and absconding."],"refs":["1. NICE NG10: short-term management of violence and aggression (2015; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Assessment and management of risk to others, CR201 (2016; checked Nov 2026)","3. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 26 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Douglas KS et al. HCR-20 Version 3 (2013; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK risk assessment is structured professional judgement, not a score. Use historical, clinical and situational headings, write a formulation of why and when he might be violent, and link each risk to an action.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-48","n":1248,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🛏","title":"Before discharge from low secure care: risk and early warning signs with his mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00, ward doctor","where":"Family room, low secure unit","text":"Callum Price, 24, has schizophrenia. Five months ago, psychotic after stopping olanzapine and smoking high-strength cannabis daily, he believed his mother was poisoning his food and punched her repeatedly, fracturing her cheekbone. On clozapine 400 mg a day he is now well, cannabis-free and remorseful. He moves to 24-hour supported accommodation in two weeks. He has consented in writing for you to talk openly with his mother, Karen Price, 50, who asked for this meeting."},"task":"With his consent, go through his relapse signature, early warning signs, risk factors and the crisis plan with his mother, check her understanding of his illness and discharge plan, and agree a safety plan that protects her.","first60":["Read the consent form, the latest risk assessment and his written relapse signature before she arrives.","Book a private room, allow enough time, and have the crisis numbers and the accommodation's phone number printed for her.","Introduce yourself, confirm Callum has agreed you can speak openly, and say what the meeting is for.","Acknowledge her position early: she is his mother and the person he hurt.","Ask what she already knows before explaining anything."],"ask":["'What have you been told about Callum's illness? What do you make of it?'","'What part do you think the cannabis played?'","'Looking back, what was the first thing you noticed changing before that night?'","'How have you been since, in your health and your sleep? Who supports you?'","'How are you hoping to see him after he leaves?'","'Is there anything at home that could be used as a weapon? Have his fears ever involved your home or neighbours?'","'Some parents carry a lot of anger after an assault like this. Has any part of you wanted to hurt him back?'","'What would you do if you felt frightened of him?'"],"recognise":["A relapse signature is his own sequence of changes, early to late. Families often see the earliest signs first.","His risk factors: stopping medication, cannabis, a change in smoking habit (alters clozapine levels), stress, losing contact with the team, and beliefs about his mother.","The previous violence was driven by a specific delusion about her. If that belief returns, the risk to her rises sharply.","She may have post-traumatic symptoms: startle, poor sleep, avoidance. She is both the person he hurt and his main carer.","Reassurance without a plan is useless; a plan she cannot use is also useless. Check it works for her."],"decide":[{"c":"Check her understanding, explain the discharge plan, build his relapse signature with her, agree visits at the accommodation at first, and give a written three-step crisis plan with named numbers.","r":"Right: it uses what she knows, protects her, and gives her clear actions at each level of concern.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her he is well now and the risk has gone.","r":"Dishonest: risk is lower but not zero. False reassurance leaves her unprepared if he relapses.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her not to see him at all.","r":"Not your decision, and it removes a key support. Help her see him safely instead.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: Callum has agreed in writing to full sharing. Without consent you could still listen to her and give general information (GMC confidentiality guidance), and share without consent if needed to protect her from serious harm.","If discharged on a Community Treatment Order, conditions (e.g. living at the accommodation, attending clozapine monitoring) support the plan, and the RC can recall him to hospital if needed.","Any carer can ask for a carer's assessment from the local authority under the Care Act 2014.","He is entitled to Section 117 aftercare, funded jointly by the ICB and local authority."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"Thank you for coming. Callum has asked us to be open with you. I know this is hard: you're his mum, and you were the one who got hurt."},{"to":"Mother","s":"I can't promise it will never happen again. What I can do is make sure you know what to look for and exactly what to do."},{"to":"Mother","s":"If you ever feel in danger, leave, get somewhere safe and call 999. Then tell us."}],"write":"14:50 Met Mrs Karen Price (mother) with Callum's written consent (filed).\nUnderstanding: knows diagnosis is schizophrenia; thought it would 'go away'; explained long-term illness, ongoing clozapine. Understood cannabis as trigger and relapse risk.\nHer health: startle, poor sleep since assault; supported by sister; agreed to GP referral for talking therapy and carer's assessment.\nRelapse signature agreed: (1) sleeping late, missing texts; (2) watching windows, irritable, talk of neighbours; (3) refusing food she makes, poisoning ideas.\nRisk factors: stopping clozapine, cannabis, smoking change, stress.\nVisits: at accommodation, staff aware, short, daytime, for first 3 months. No visits to her home until reviewed at CPA. No weapons at home; no fears involving her home now; no thoughts of harming him.\nCrisis plan: early signs, care coordinator (number given); worse or out of hours, crisis line/111 option 2; danger, leave and call 999.\nShe repeated the plan back accurately. Written copy given.\nDr L. Bose ST4","make":["Her understanding of schizophrenia and cannabis; fill gaps simply.","The discharge plan: accommodation, clozapine and blood monitoring, care coordinator, psychiatrist, drug and alcohol service, CTO if used.","Relapse signature: early, middle, late, in Callum's own pattern.","Risk factors and what changes them.","Visits, home risks, her own feelings and health.","Three-step crisis plan, written, with numbers; teach-back; where might it break down."],"next":"Mrs Price takes the written plan and the carer's assessment form. At the first CPA review after discharge she reports two good visits at the accommodation and agrees to a supervised Sunday lunch at her home after three months.","marks":["Confirmed consent and acknowledged her as mother and victim.","Checked her understanding of schizophrenia and cannabis.","Explained the discharge plan clearly.","Built the relapse signature with her and named his risk factors.","Asked about her health, support, home risks and her own feelings.","Agreed a written, stepped crisis plan with emergency options.","Checked she understood and could use the plan."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing on early warning signs without asking what she noticed.","Promising he will never be violent again.","Forgetting that she was injured and may be traumatised.","Giving a crisis plan with no numbers or names."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG150 Supporting adult carers (2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Assessment and management of risk to others, CR201 (2016; checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC. Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, sharing with families depends on the patient's consent, which you should seek and record. Even without it, you can always listen to relatives and give general information.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-49","n":1249,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Teaching the ward nurse: how you diagnosed DTs and what happens now","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 11:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical admissions unit, general hospital","text":"You have just assessed Mr Anil Mehta, 64, admitted last night after a neighbour found him on his kitchen floor, probably there for two days. He speaks mainly Gujarati. He is tremulous, sweating, picking at the air and shouting, and cannot hold attention. HR 112, BP 156/94, T 37.7, sats 98%. CT head is normal; CK and GGT are raised and he is dehydrated. The neighbour says he drinks a bottle of whisky a day. You have alerted the medical registrar. Staff Nurse Chloe Barnes, newly qualified, asks you to explain."},"task":"Explain to the ward nurse how you reached a diagnosis of delirium tremens and the management plan: benzodiazepines, thiamine, fluids, monitoring, nursing care and longer-term follow-up.","first60":["Confirm the medical registrar is aware and coming, and check the latest obs and capillary glucose before you start teaching.","Find somewhere you can talk without leaving him alone; a healthcare assistant stays with him.","Ask what she already knows: 'Have you looked after anyone in alcohol withdrawal before?'","Lead with the bottom line: 'This is a medical emergency, but very treatable if we act now.'","Use plain, specific language and check understanding as you go."],"ask":["'What have you noticed in him this morning, compared with when he arrived?'","'Has he had any glucose or dextrose yet?'","'Any shaking episodes that looked like a fit?'","'What's his urine output been, and is the IV line working?'","'Is there a professional Gujarati interpreter we can book, in person or by phone?'","'Is a side room free close to the nurses' desk?'","'Which part would be most useful for me to go through?'"],"recognise":["Delirium tremens is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal: delirium (fluctuating confusion and inattention) plus withdrawal signs.","Timing fits: heavy daily drinking, then forced abstinence for about two to three days on the floor and in hospital. DTs usually starts 2 to 4 days after the last drink.","Features he has: inattention and disorientation, visual hallucinations (picking at the air), fear and agitation, coarse tremor, sweating, tachycardia, hypertension, low-grade fever.","Rule out or treat alongside: head injury (CT normal), hypoglycaemia, sepsis, hepatic encephalopathy, Wernicke's encephalopathy (often co-exists), electrolyte disturbance, rhabdomyolysis from the long lie.","Risks if missed: seizures, arrhythmias, aspiration, falls, Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, death."],"decide":[{"c":"Oral lorazepam now, titrated per protocol; Pabrinex IV before or with any glucose; IV fluids with electrolyte replacement; side room with one-to-one nursing, interpreter and frequent obs; escalate to HDU if he can't be safely sedated.","r":"Right: matches NICE CG100 for delirium tremens and treats the long lie, dehydration and the Wernicke's risk at the same time.","best":true},{"c":"Haloperidol 5 mg IM alone to settle the hallucinations.","r":"Wrong: antipsychotics alone do not treat withdrawal and lower the seizure threshold. Benzodiazepines are first line; an antipsychotic is only an add-on if needed.","best":false},{"c":"Wait for the full blood results before starting anything.","r":"Unsafe: DTs can kill. Start lorazepam, thiamine and fluids now and adjust as results arrive.","best":false}],"law":["He lacks capacity to consent to treatment while delirious; give treatment in his best interests (MCA 2005), involving the neighbour or family where possible, and record the capacity assessment.","Proportionate restraint to give urgent treatment or prevent harm is allowed under MCA sections 5 and 6; if he is continuously supervised and not free to leave for more than a short emergency, consider a DoLS urgent authorisation.","The Mental Health Act is not used to treat delirium caused by a physical condition.","Use a professional interpreter, not the neighbour, for assessment and consent discussions."],"say":[{"to":"Nurse","s":"Good question. Delirium tremens is the most severe kind of alcohol withdrawal. His brain has got used to a lot of alcohol, and when it suddenly stops, it goes into overdrive."},{"to":"Nurse","s":"The key things for you: lorazepam as prescribed, obs at least hourly, a quiet lit side room, and call us straight away if he fits, gets drowsy or his NEWS2 rises."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"64-year-old man with delirium tremens after a two-day long lie: HR 112, T 37.7, CK raised. I've suggested oral lorazepam, Pabrinex IV and fluids; could you review him and his fluid plan now?"}],"write":"11:55 Liaison psychiatry. Discussed diagnosis and plan with SN Barnes; medical SpR informed and reviewing.\nDx: Delirium tremens. Basis: daily heavy drinking (collateral: ~1 bottle whisky/day), abstinence ~48-60 h, acute fluctuating confusion and inattention, visual hallucinations, tremor, sweating, HR 112, BP 156/94, T 37.7. CT head normal. Raised CK and GGT, dehydrated.\nPlan: oral lorazepam per trust alcohol withdrawal protocol, target calm and rousable; Pabrinex IV 2 pairs tds for 3-5 days, before any glucose; IV fluids, check U&E, Mg, PO4, K, glucose, LFT, clotting, CK daily; obs and NEWS2 at least hourly, sedation score, fluid balance, glucose 4-hourly; side room, one-to-one, lights on, clock, interpreter (Gujarati); falls bundle. Avoid antipsychotic unless lorazepam insufficient (NICE CG100). Escalate to HDU if unable to sedate safely, seizure or NEWS2 >= 5.\nCapacity: lacks capacity for treatment; best interests under MCA.\nFollow-up: alcohol care team referral; liaison review tomorrow.\nDr R. Chaudhry ST5","make":["How the diagnosis was made: history (collateral), timing, signs, and what was ruled out.","Immediate plan: benzodiazepine, thiamine, fluids and electrolytes, treat any medical cause.","Monitoring: obs and NEWS2, sedation level, fluid balance, glucose, bloods.","Nursing care: side room, light, clock, one-to-one, same staff, interpreter, falls and seizure precautions.","Escalation triggers and longer-term follow-up."],"next":"By evening Mr Mehta is calmer and sleeping in short spells. On day 3 the hallucinations stop. With an interpreter he agrees to see the alcohol care team, who plan a community follow-up and talk to him about relapse prevention.","marks":["Explained what DTs is and why it happens.","Justified the diagnosis from the history, timing and signs.","Named the differentials and what excludes them.","Gave a clear plan: lorazepam, Pabrinex, fluids, electrolytes.","Gave specific monitoring and escalation triggers.","Covered nursing care, interpreter and longer-term follow-up.","Checked her understanding without being patronising."],"pitfalls":["Using jargon and not checking she has understood.","Relying on CIWA-Ar in a patient who cannot answer the questions.","Giving glucose before thiamine in a confused drinker.","Treating the hallucinations with an antipsychotic alone."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (2010, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG115: harmful drinking and alcohol dependence (2011; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Pabrinex intravenous high potency, Wernicke's encephalopathy dosing (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE CG103 Delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management (2010, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK hospitals the Pabrinex IV preparation comes as 'pairs' of ampoules (I and II) mixed together. Prescribe it as pairs and frequency, and check whether your trust uses a printed alcohol withdrawal protocol.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-50","n":1250,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Woke from a GBL coma: substance history and risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 13:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical unit, general hospital","text":"Tom Ashworth, a 29-year-old male solicitor, was found unresponsive in a stairwell at 01:00 with a small bottle of clear liquid beside him. GCS was 6 in resus and he needed airway support; he woke suddenly about four hours later. His urine screen shows methamphetamine. He is now alert, apologising to everyone for 'wasting a bed', a little shaky and sweaty, pulse 104. The medical team ask liaison psychiatry to see him."},"task":"Take a substance use history focused on GBL (pattern, dependence, withdrawal), assess risk including chemsex, sexual health and suicidality, and agree a plan.","first60":["Check with the medical team: current obs, any sedatives given, and whether he is in early withdrawal (tremor, sweating, tachycardia, agitation).","Find a private space or draw the curtains and lower your voice; this history includes sex and drugs.","Introduce yourself and explain why liaison psychiatry sees everyone after a serious overdose: routine, not a judgement.","Explain confidentiality and its limits early; he may fear for his job.","Normalise: 'I see a lot of people after G. Nothing you tell me will shock me.'"],"ask":["'What do you remember about last night?'","'What did you take, how much, and how often?'","'When did you first use G? How has your use changed?'","'Do you need more now for the same effect? Do you dose through the night or first thing in the morning?'","'What happens if you go without it for a few hours? Shaking, sweating, not sleeping, panic?'","'Is G part of sex for you? Do you go to chemsex parties or hook-ups?'","'Did you mean to take that much? Have you thought about killing yourself?'","'Did you use condoms last night? When were you last tested for HIV and hepatitis? Has anything ever happened at a party that you didn't agree to?'"],"recognise":["GHB and GBL have a very narrow gap between the dose that gets you high and the dose that causes coma; alcohol and other depressants close that gap.","Dependence markers: tolerance, dosing round the clock or every few hours, dosing to sleep or on waking, withdrawal when stopping.","GBL withdrawal can start 1 to 6 hours after the last dose in heavy users and can progress quickly to delirium, seizures and death. Shaky, sweaty and tachycardic now means early withdrawal.","Chemsex risks: overdose, injecting (slamming), condomless sex with multiple partners, HIV and hepatitis, non-consensual sex while unconscious, low mood after sessions.","Accidental overdose is common, but always ask directly about intent and suicidal thoughts; the 'comedown' after a chemsex weekend can bring low mood."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full GBL-focused history, treat early withdrawal as urgent with the medical team (benzodiazepine per protocol, addiction advice), arrange same-day HIV PEP review, offer BBV testing, and refer to a chemsex support service.","r":"Right: covers the life-threatening withdrawal risk, the time-limited PEP window and the drug and sexual health risks, not only the overdose.","best":true},{"c":"Accept that it was accidental, give him a leaflet and discharge once medically fit.","r":"Unsafe: he shows signs of withdrawal that can turn into delirium within hours, and the 72-hour PEP window would be missed.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him he must inform his employer and the regulator.","r":"Wrong: confidentiality applies. Discuss his own fitness to work supportively, but disclosure is his decision unless there is a serious risk to others.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: what he tells you stays within the clinical team unless there is a serious risk to him or others; ask before writing to his GP and share only what is needed.","He has capacity to decide whether to stay for withdrawal treatment. If he wants to leave while withdrawing, explain the risks clearly and record his capacity decision.","If he discloses sexual assault, he decides whether to involve police; offer the local Sexual Assault Referral Centre, ideally within 7 days for forensic samples.","GHB and GBL are controlled drugs in the UK; his use is a health issue for you, not something you report."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You haven't wasted anyone's time. G can stop people breathing, and we see this often. I'd like to understand your use, so we can keep you safe."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You're shaking and your pulse is fast. That can be early withdrawal from G, which can get serious quickly, so I'd like the medical team to start treatment now."},{"to":"Medical team","s":"He's using GBL every few hours at weekends and nightly to sleep, last dose around midnight, now tremulous with HR 104. Please treat as GBL withdrawal per protocol; I'll get addiction advice. He also needs same-day PEP."}],"write":"13:30 Liaison psychiatry. 29M, solicitor. GBL overdose with alcohol, GCS 6, woke after ~4 h.\nSubstance hx: GBL 5 years, chemsex; now every 2-3 h at weekend parties and ~1.5 ml at night most nights for 4 months to sleep. Tolerance. Shaky, sweaty, anxious if misses night dose. Last dose ~00:00. Methamphetamine monthly at parties, injected; says clean needles but shared a spoon once. Alcohol: 4-5 beers last night, otherwise light. Collapses on G x2 before.\nO/E: tremor, sweating, HR 104, anxious, orientated. Early GBL withdrawal.\nRisk: overdose accidental; no suicidal intent or plan; low mood after weekends. Condomless sex with several men last night; memory gap; unsure about consent for the last part of the night. Never BBV tested. Not on PrEP.\nPlan: medical team to treat GBL withdrawal (benzodiazepine per protocol), addiction advice today; sexual health review today for HIV PEP (within 72 h), BBV screen, hep B vaccination, PrEP; SARC information given; chemsex support service referral; needle exchange info; GP letter with consent; liaison review tomorrow.\nDr P. Adeyemi CT3","make":["Last night: what, how much, with what else, what he remembers.","GBL history: onset, route, dose, frequency, escalation, dosing pattern, tolerance, withdrawal, previous collapses, attempts to stop.","Other substances: methamphetamine, mephedrone, alcohol, injecting practice.","Risk: intent and suicidality, mood, sexual health, consent, BBV, PrEP, injecting.","Impact: work, relationships, finances, driving; motivation to change."],"next":"The medical team start diazepam per protocol and his tremor settles. The sexual health team give PEP that afternoon and book BBV results. He accepts a referral to the chemsex support service and asks for help to have sex without G.","marks":["Explained role and confidentiality, and normalised.","Took a detailed GBL history: dose, frequency, round-the-clock use, tolerance.","Asked about withdrawal and spotted early withdrawal now.","Asked about other drugs, alcohol and injecting.","Asked directly about intent and suicidal thoughts.","Covered sexual health, consent, BBV and PEP.","Agreed a practical plan with harm reduction."],"pitfalls":["Assuming the overdose was accidental without asking.","Missing that 'just a dose at night to sleep' means dependence.","Missing the 72-hour HIV PEP window.","Embarrassment leading to vague questions about sex."],"refs":["1. NEPTUNE (Novel Psychoactive Treatment UK Network). Guidance on the clinical management of acute and chronic harms of club drugs and novel psychoactive substances (2015; checked Nov 2026)","2. BHIVA/BASHH UK guideline for the use of HIV post-exposure prophylaxis (2021; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, sexual health clinics are free, confidential and open-access; patients do not need a GP referral. Many run dedicated chemsex services.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-51","n":1251,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Six weeks low and drinking daily: depression or alcohol?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:30","where":"General adult outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in clinic. Mr Gareth Hollis, 46, a council office administrator, has been referred by his GP with six weeks of low mood and loss of interest. He has hypertension and was admitted with acute pancreatitis four months ago. He smokes and 'drinks regularly'. He has been off work for two weeks and his wife moved to her sister's three weeks ago. He expected antidepressants; the GP said it was 'more complicated' and referred him to you."},"task":"Take a history of his low mood and his drinking, decide whether this is alcohol dependence, a depressive episode or both, and assess his risk.","first60":["Let him tell you about the low mood first; he came expecting to be treated for depression.","Get the depressive symptoms and their start date before you move to alcohol.","Turn drinking into numbers: what, how much, when in the day, in units.","Ask what happens when he stops: shakes, sweats, confusion, seeing things, fits.","Ask about suicide directly; heavy drinking plus hopelessness is a dangerous mix."],"ask":["'When did you last feel really like yourself?'","'Talk me through yesterday, from waking up, and every drink you had.'","'What happens if you go a morning without a drink?'","'Have you ever stopped completely? What happened, and how was your mood then?'","'Which came first: the drinking getting heavy, or the low mood?'","'Do you ever think about ending your life? Have you thought how?'","'Has anything risky happened while you were drunk: cooking, driving, falls?'"],"recognise":["Low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest are common in heavy drinkers and often lift within a few weeks of stopping.","Drinking that started and escalated before the mood fell points to alcohol dependence with alcohol-related depressive symptoms.","Confusion and frightening visions two days into a past attempt to stop suggest complicated withdrawal; he must not stop suddenly on his own.","Pancreatitis, a cooking fire and a marriage breakdown are harms he keeps drinking despite: a core dependence feature.","A specific plan (drinking heavily, then the river) with hopelessness and recent losses is high risk, raised further by intoxication."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose likely alcohol dependence with depressive symptoms, act on suicide risk today, and plan to reassess mood 3 to 4 weeks after supervised withdrawal.","r":"Treats the driver first, keeps him safe and avoids labelling or medicating a depression that may resolve.","best":true},{"c":"Start sertraline today for a depressive episode and tell him to cut down.","r":"Misses dependence, complicated withdrawal and acute suicide risk; antidepressants work poorly while he drinks this heavily.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him to stop drinking from tonight and see his GP in a month.","r":"Unsupervised stopping risks delirium tremens and fits, and leaves a man with a suicide plan without follow-up.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assume capacity; intoxication can impair it, so reassess if he presents drunk.","Mental Health Act 1983, section 1(3): dependence on alcohol alone is not a mental disorder for detention, but a coexisting mental disorder can be.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): you may share information without consent to protect him from a serious risk of death; seek consent to speak with his wife first.","DVLA: alcohol dependence must be declared by a licence holder; he does not drive, so record that."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You came expecting help for depression, and the low mood is real. I also want to understand the drinking, because the two are tangled together."},{"to":"Patient (diagnosis)","s":"From what you've told me, the drinking has a hold on you, and heavy drinking on its own can cause exactly this kind of low mood. Once you're safely off alcohol for a few weeks, we look at your mood again."},{"to":"Patient (risk)","s":"Thank you for telling me about the river. That tells me how much pain you're in, and it means we act today."}],"write":"Outpatient note:\nGareth Hollis, 46. GP referral: 6 weeks low mood.\nMood: low, anhedonia, poor sleep and appetite, fatigue, guilt; 6 weeks.\nAlcohol: started 3 years ago; now 4-6 pints 4% lager daily (about 80 units/week), daytime at home and evenings in pub. Craving every 2 hours, loss of control, tolerance, persisting after pancreatitis. Morning shakes and diarrhoea; 2-day stop last year with confusion and visual hallucinations (probable delirium tremens). AUDIT 31.\nTimeline: heavy drinking for 3 months preceded mood change; no sober period since.\nNo psychosis or anxiety disorder outside withdrawal. FH: father and grandmother alcohol dependence.\nRisk: thoughts of drinking then jumping in the river, intent fluctuating; protective: hope of reconciling with wife. Small kitchen fire while drunk. Does not drive.\nImpression: alcohol dependence (ICD-11 6C40.2) with depressive symptoms, probably alcohol-related; independent depressive episode not excluded. High suicide risk.\nPlan: discussed with consultant; same-day crisis team assessment; referral for inpatient assisted withdrawal; reassess mood 3-4 weeks after abstinence.","make":["Discuss with your consultant before he leaves clinic.","Same-day referral to the crisis resolution and home treatment team for suicide risk.","Urgent referral for medically assisted withdrawal; inpatient because of past delirium tremens and suicide risk.","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFTs with GGT, U&Es, glucose, clotting; record AUDIT score.","With his consent, contact his wife; give written crisis numbers.","Letter to the GP today: diagnosis, risk and plan; no antidepressant started."],"next":"Three weeks after inpatient withdrawal, he is sober and attending the alcohol service. Repeat the depression assessment: if low mood, anhedonia and poor sleep persist after 3 to 4 weeks of abstinence, treat a depressive episode in its own right.","marks":["Hears his account of low mood first and validates it.","Covers core depressive symptoms and their duration.","Quantifies drinking in units and describes a typical day.","Elicits dependence features, including withdrawal and complicated withdrawal.","Establishes which came first, and mood during any sober period.","Screens for psychosis, anxiety and family history.","Asks directly about suicide plan, intent and protective factors.","Covers fire, accident and physical health risks.","Gives a clear, plain-language likely diagnosis."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I drink regularly' without counting units.","Diagnosing depression without asking which came first.","Missing the past confusion and visions during withdrawal.","Skipping a direct question about suicide plans.","Advising him to stop drinking abruptly at home."],"refs":["1. NICE CG115 Alcohol-use disorders: harmful drinking and dependence (2011, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: physical complications (2010, checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6C40.2 Alcohol dependence (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), checked Nov 2026)","5. DVLA Assessing fitness to drive: alcohol misuse and dependence (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services think in units. One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a pint of 4% lager is about 2.3 units. Always convert, write the weekly total, and name the drink.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-52","n":1252,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Unsteady and confabulating: the cerebellar examination","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:00","where":"Community alcohol service clinic","text":"You are the CT1 in the alcohol service clinic. Mr Desmond Clarke, 60, has a long history of alcohol dependence. His keyworker is worried: he has been falling, walks with his feet wide apart, and gives confident but untrue accounts of his week. The team suspect Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. He is happy to see you; a clinic nurse, Sam, is available as a chaperone. Examine his cerebellar function and act on what you find."},"task":"Carry out a full cerebellar examination, adapting to his memory problems, then interpret the findings and decide what must happen today.","first60":["Confirm who he is, wash your hands, explain simply and get consent; offer a chaperone and ask about pain.","He forgets instructions: demonstrate each test, give one step at a time, and repeat without impatience.","Stand close during every standing test; he falls.","Look before you touch: walking aid, posture, swaying while seated.","Keep the question in mind: is there new nystagmus or confusion that means Wernicke's today?"],"ask":["'Can I check your full name and date of birth?'","'Is it alright if I check your balance and coordination? Sam will stay with us.'","'Do you have pain anywhere, in your back, hips or legs?'","'Watch me first, then you have a go.'","'Keep your head still; just your eyes follow my finger.'","'Please say after me: British constitution.'"],"recognise":["Alcohol-related cerebellar degeneration mainly affects the midline (vermis): wide-based gait and leg ataxia, with arms, speech and eyes often less affected.","Unsteadiness with feet together and eyes open is cerebellar; marked worsening only when eyes close (positive Romberg) points to lost position sense, often alcohol-related neuropathy.","Confusion, eye signs (nystagmus or eye movement weakness) and ataxia are Wernicke's encephalopathy; all three appear together only in a minority.","Confident false answers to fill memory gaps (confabulation) suggest Korsakoff syndrome, which may follow untreated Wernicke's.","Reduced ankle jerks and numb feet suggest peripheral neuropathy, which adds to falls."],"decide":[{"c":"Treat new nystagmus, ataxia and confusion as possible Wernicke's: arrange IV Pabrinex today via the acute medical team.","r":"Wernicke's is an emergency; delay risks permanent memory loss. Treatment is safe and cheap.","best":true},{"c":"Write to the GP recommending oral thiamine and a neurology referral.","r":"Oral thiamine is poorly absorbed in heavy drinkers and too slow for suspected Wernicke's.","best":false},{"c":"Wait for an MRI brain before starting treatment.","r":"Wernicke's is a clinical diagnosis; imaging can be normal and must not delay thiamine.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assess capacity for consent to examination and to hospital treatment; his memory loss does not by itself mean he lacks capacity.","If he lacks capacity to decide on IV thiamine, make a best-interests decision under the MCA 2005, involving his keyworker and any family.","GMC Intimate examinations and chaperones (2024): offer a chaperone for examinations that need close contact and record the offer."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like to check your balance and how well your arms and legs work together. It won't hurt. I'll show you each step first."},{"to":"Patient (Romberg)","s":"I'm standing right beside you, and I'll catch you if you wobble. Feet together, arms by your sides. Now close your eyes."},{"to":"Clinic nurse","s":"He has new nystagmus, a wide-based ataxic gait and confabulation. I'm treating this as possible Wernicke's. He needs IV Pabrinex today; I'm calling the medical registrar now."}],"write":"Cerebellar examination, alcohol service clinic. Chaperone: clinic nurse Sam (offered and accepted). Consent given.\nInspection: walking stick; sways when seated unsupported (truncal ataxia).\nGait: wide-based, short steps; unable to heel-toe walk.\nRomberg: unsteady with feet together, eyes open; worse with eyes closed, needed support.\nSpeech: mildly slurred, not clearly scanning.\nEyes: horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus both directions (new; not recorded before).\nUpper limbs: tone normal; mild dysdiadochokinesis R and L; mild past-pointing on finger-nose, no marked intention tremor.\nLower limbs: knee jerks present, not pendular; ankle jerks absent; heel-shin markedly impaired bilaterally.\nCognition: confabulates, disorientated to date.\nImpression: midline cerebellar syndrome with possible acute Wernicke's encephalopathy on a background of probable Korsakoff syndrome and peripheral neuropathy.\nPlan: same-day referral to acute medical team for IV Pabrinex; consultant informed; falls risk to keyworker.","make":["Phone the acute medical registrar; send him with the nurse or keyworker for IV Pabrinex today.","Tell your consultant and record the referral time.","Bloods: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, glucose, magnesium, phosphate, B12, folate.","Request MRI brain via the medical team; it must not delay treatment.","Falls risk: physiotherapy and occupational therapy assessment; check his home set-up with the keyworker.","Plan a structured cognitive assessment once he is sober and treated."],"next":"He returns from hospital two weeks later after IV thiamine. Nystagmus has gone, gait is a little better, but memory and confabulation persist. Arrange cognitive testing, a capacity assessment for where he lives, and a care needs assessment with the local authority.","marks":["Confirms identity, washes hands, explains, gains consent, offers chaperone, asks about pain.","Inspects for aids, posture and truncal ataxia.","Assesses gait, heel-to-toe walking and Romberg's test safely.","Tests speech and eye movements for nystagmus.","Tests tone, rapid alternating movements and finger-nose on both sides.","Tests reflexes and heel-shin on both sides.","Demonstrates and repeats instructions for his memory loss.","Thanks him, washes hands, summarises and interprets findings.","Recognises possible Wernicke's and arranges same-day IV thiamine."],"pitfalls":["Getting him to stand with eyes closed without being ready to catch him.","Long, multi-step instructions he cannot hold in mind.","Testing one side only.","Calling a positive Romberg a cerebellar sign.","Recommending oral thiamine and a routine letter for possible Wernicke's."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (2010, checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: thiamine and vitamins B and C (Pabrinex) (checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Physicians: The management of Wernicke's encephalopathy (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Intimate examinations and chaperones (2024, checked Nov 2026)","5. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK alcohol services, suspected Wernicke's means IV Pabrinex today, usually via the acute medical team. Psychiatry clinics rarely give IV treatment themselves; your job is to recognise it, phone, and make sure he gets there.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-53","n":1253,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Using cannabis to cope with voices: short-term plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 16:00","where":"Urgent slot, community mental health team clinic","text":"You are the CT2 covering urgent clinic. Mr Ryan Okafor, 25, a graphic designer, has paranoid schizophrenia: two previous episodes, both treated at home with olanzapine 10 mg at night. His GP faxed an urgent referral: he stopped olanzapine six weeks ago, is hearing a voice again and is smoking cannabis to cope. He has come alone, straight from work."},"task":"Take a brief history of his relapse and cannabis use, then agree a short-term management plan with him.","first60":["Ask what came back first and when, and tie it to the week he stopped olanzapine.","Always ask why he stopped; the reason shapes the whole plan.","Count the cannabis: how much, how often, since when, and in past episodes.","Check risk to himself and others, especially if his beliefs involve named people.","He has insight and wants help: build the plan with him, at home if safe."],"ask":["'When did you first feel things weren't right?'","'What made you decide to stop the olanzapine?'","'How much are you smoking, and what does it do for you?'","'Was cannabis around when you were unwell before?'","'Have you thought of hurting anyone, or felt you need to defend yourself?'","'Any thoughts of self-harm or of being better off dead?'","'Who is around you in the evenings?'"],"recognise":["Symptoms returning two weeks after stopping, matching earlier episodes, is a relapse of schizophrenia, not a new illness.","Sexual side effects are a common, hidden reason for stopping; ignore them and he will stop again.","Cannabis at every episode, now nightly, worsens psychosis and weakens the antipsychotic's effect.","Ideas about colleagues need a check on risk to others, even when he is frightened rather than angry.","With insight, no risk and willingness to take medicine, home treatment is reasonable; admission is offered, not imposed."],"decide":[{"c":"Restart olanzapine now with a planned switch once settled (or aripiprazole now if he prefers), advise stopping cannabis, refer to drug service, add crisis team support and review within a week.","r":"Uses what has worked, respects his reason for stopping, and keeps him safe at home.","best":true},{"c":"Restart olanzapine and tell him he must take it long term.","r":"Ignores the sexual side effect that made him stop; he will likely stop again.","best":false},{"c":"Insist on admission because he is using cannabis.","r":"Disproportionate: he has insight, no risk to others and will engage with home treatment.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assume he has capacity to choose his medication; he is engaging informally.","Mental Health Act 1983: not indicated today; consider assessment only if risk rises or he stops engaging while unwell.","DVLA: during an acute psychotic episode a driver must stop driving and tell the DVLA; he does not drive, so record that.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): ask his consent before involving his mother."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Side effects are a very common reason people come off tablets. What was it for you?"},{"to":"Patient (cannabis)","s":"I can see why you smoke when the voice is frightening. The trouble is, cannabis tends to feed the voice and the fear, and it blunts what the medicine can do."},{"to":"Patient (plan)","s":"Olanzapine has got you well twice. Let's restart it now, and once you're settled we switch to one that's kinder on sex. Does that feel fair?"}],"write":"Urgent clinic note:\nRyan Okafor, 25. Paranoid schizophrenia, 2 prior episodes (cannabis at both), treated at home with olanzapine 10 mg nocte.\nStopped olanzapine 6 weeks ago due to erectile dysfunction. Well 2 weeks, then persecutory ideas about colleagues sabotaging his work and influencing his manager; ideas of reference (parked cars); 2 weeks of a single voice above his head. Frightened, avoids windows. Some insight.\nCannabis: 3-4 joints most evenings for 3 weeks, to cope. No alcohol or other drugs. Does not drive.\nRisk: no thoughts of harm to self or others; mother lives nearby; still at work.\nImpression: relapse of paranoid schizophrenia, precipitated by stopping olanzapine and cannabis use.\nPlan agreed: restart olanzapine 10 mg nocte, plan switch to aripiprazole once settled; prolactin and baseline physical checks; advice to stop cannabis, drug service referral; CRHT home visits; care coordinator; 1 week off work; review within 1 week. Discussed with consultant.","make":["Prescribe olanzapine 10 mg at night; write the planned switch into the care plan.","Bloods today: prolactin, HbA1c, lipids, FBC, U&Es, LFTs; weight, BMI, BP, pulse.","Refer to the crisis resolution and home treatment team for home visits this week.","Refer to the local drug and alcohol service, ready for when he wants it.","Fit note for one week; with consent, phone his mother.","GP letter today; discuss with consultant; review in clinic within a week."],"next":"At one week the voice is quieter and he has cut to two joints a week. At four weeks he is well and asks about switching. Cross-taper to aripiprazole over 2 to 4 weeks, recheck prolactin, and refer for CBT for psychosis.","marks":["Times the relapse against stopping olanzapine.","Elicits why he stopped: erectile problems.","Quantifies cannabis and its link with past episodes.","Assesses risk to self and others, and support.","Offers an antipsychotic that addresses his reason for stopping, with side effects.","Advises stopping cannabis with reasons; offers the drug service.","Arranges crisis team and community support; admission mentioned, not pushed.","Agrees the plan with him and checks understanding."],"pitfalls":["Restarting olanzapine without asking why he stopped.","Missing cannabis at earlier episodes.","Forcing admission on an engaged man with insight.","Lecturing about cannabis instead of explaining why it matters to him.","Leaving without a review date or crisis numbers."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG120 Coexisting severe mental illness (psychosis) and substance misuse (2011, checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: olanzapine; aripiprazole (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: aripiprazole and impulse-control disorders (checked Nov 2026)","5. DVLA Assessing fitness to drive: psychotic disorders (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients expect to choose between medicines. Offer two reasonable options with their main side effects, give your recommendation, and let him decide. Shared decision-making is NICE's expectation, not a courtesy.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-54","n":1254,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🏥","title":"One mistake in the match: identifying his thinking errors","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:00","where":"General adult outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in clinic. Mr Josh Pennington, 23, is the top-scoring batsman for a semi-professional cricket club. Three weeks ago, in a deciding match, he was caught out for 22; nobody else on the team reached 10 and they lost. Since then he has refused to play and talks of retiring. His GP referred him with low mood. He is married with two young children. Your consultant has asked you to identify his thinking errors and explain them to him."},"task":"Identify the cognitive distortions in what he says, test how firmly he holds them, and explain each one to him in a way he understands.","first60":["Go slowly; he is flat and takes time to answer. Leave silences.","Let him tell the story of the match in his own words; distortions appear in the wording.","Collect evidence he gives you: his average, the scores, the coach's view.","Test the belief gently: a distortion bends to evidence, a delusion does not.","Explain in small chunks and check after each; use his words, not jargon."],"ask":["'Tell me about the match, in your words.'","'What went through your mind as you walked off?'","'How did the rest of the team score that day?'","'What does your coach think about your season?'","'What makes you feel you're failing as a dad?'","'How sure are you about that, out of 100?'","'Do you ever think about ending your life?'"],"recognise":["Personalisation: blaming himself for a team loss when he top-scored.","Minimisation and maximisation: brushing off a season average of 74 while magnifying one shot.","Selective abstraction: one dismissal outweighs a season of evidence.","Catastrophising and black-and-white thinking: 'I'll never be good again, I should retire.'","Arbitrary inference: concluding he fails his family with no evidence from them.","If he can admit, when shown the evidence, that he might be wrong, these are distortions of depression, not delusions."],"decide":[{"c":"Hear his account, gently test each belief against his own evidence, name the thinking error in plain words, check understanding, and offer CBT.","r":"He discovers the errors himself, which is more persuasive than being told.","best":true},{"c":"List the distortion names to him and tell him he is being irrational.","r":"Jargon and judgement shame him and close the conversation.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure him that he is a great player and move on to antidepressants.","r":"Reassurance does not test or shift the belief, and skips the task.","best":false}],"law":["No legal framework is engaged; he is a voluntary outpatient with capacity.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): give information in a way the patient can understand and check that he has."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I remember you said you feel like a failure as a dad. That sounds a heavy thing to carry, especially when you clearly love your kids."},{"to":"Patient (testing)","s":"You scored 22 and nobody else reached 10. Is it possible the loss wasn't down to you?"},{"to":"Patient (explaining)","s":"When people feel low, the mind tends to shrink the good and blow up the bad. Doctors call that minimisation and maximisation. Does that sound like what's happening?"}],"write":"Outpatient note:\nJosh Pennington, 23, semi-professional cricketer. Low mood for 3 weeks after dismissal in deciding match.\nCognitions elicited: 'I lost us the match' (personalisation); 'my average means nothing' (minimisation); 'that shot shows what I really am' (maximisation, selective abstraction); 'I'll never be good again, I should retire' (catastrophising, black-and-white thinking); 'I'm failing as a husband and father' (arbitrary inference).\nBeliefs tested against evidence: conviction fell from 95 to about 60; he accepts he may be wrong. Not delusional. No hallucinations.\nNo suicidal thoughts; protective: wife and two children.\nExplained distortions in plain language; understanding checked.\nPlan: thought record before next appointment; refer for CBT; PHQ-9 today (14); review 4 weeks.","make":["Give him a simple thought record: situation, thought, feeling, evidence for and against, balanced thought.","Refer to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT.","Record PHQ-9 at baseline.","Review in 4 weeks; earlier if mood worsens or thoughts of suicide appear."],"next":"At review he has filled in four thought records and played one net session. He still feels he let the team down. Introduce behavioural experiments: play one match and record what actually happens against his prediction.","marks":["Lets him tell the story at his pace; uses his words.","Identifies and names at least five distortions.","Explains each in plain language with his examples.","Tests conviction against evidence; distinguishes from delusion.","Checks understanding in small chunks; invites questions.","Asks about suicidal thoughts and psychotic symptoms.","Offers hope, normalises, and agrees a next step."],"pitfalls":["Reeling off technical labels without explaining them.","Arguing with him instead of letting him weigh the evidence.","Forgetting to check for suicidal thoughts in a man who feels a burden.","Talking for long stretches without checking understanding.","Not distinguishing a distortion from a delusion."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), checked Nov 2026)","2. Beck AT. Cognitive therapy of depression. Guilford Press (1979)","3. NHS: Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) (nhs.uk, checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Good medical practice (2024, checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients often prefer to reach conclusions themselves. 'Is it possible that...?' and 'What would you say to a teammate who...?' land better than telling a patient he is wrong.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-55","n":1255,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🏠","title":"Housebound by agoraphobia: explaining systematic desensitisation and graded exposure","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 11:00","where":"Home visit, patient's house","text":"You are the CT3 who has just taken over an outpatient caseload. Mrs Helen Marsh, 42, has agoraphobia with panic and has not been seen for almost a year after missing appointments. Online self-help two years ago did not help despite good attendance. She has taken sertraline 50 mg for 18 months with little change. She lost her job as a shop assistant two months ago through lateness, and only leaves home with her husband. Your consultant has asked you to visit."},"task":"Review what she has tried, assess risk, explain systematic desensitisation and graded exposure, build a first hierarchy with her, and agree a treatment plan.","first60":["She is calmer at home; use that. Thank her for letting you in and acknowledge the missed year without blame.","Ask what went wrong with self-help: too fast, too confusing, or no support.","Ask directly about suicidal thoughts; hopelessness and job loss raise risk.","Explain the anxiety curve before the treatment; it is the reason the treatment works.","Plan the first steps at home, with her husband's role and a plan for overwhelming panic."],"ask":["'What happens when you try to go out?'","'What did you make of the online course? Where did it get stuck?'","'How has the sertraline been? Do you take it every day?'","'On the worst mornings, do you ever think about ending your life?'","'How much do you drink? Anything else to calm your nerves?'","'What's the easiest step outside you could imagine? And the hardest?'","'How could your husband help, without doing it for you?'"],"recognise":["Avoidance keeps agoraphobia going: each escape brings relief, which teaches the brain the danger was real.","Self-help failed because the steps were too big and unsupported, not because she lacked motivation.","If she stays in a feared situation, anxiety peaks and then falls by itself, usually within an hour; repeating this lowers the peak (habituation).","Systematic desensitisation adds relaxation, paired with each step, because relaxation and anxiety cannot easily coexist.","Morning thoughts of ending her life with no plan still need a safety plan and follow-up."],"decide":[{"c":"Offer therapist-led graded exposure with relaxation, starting at home, with her husband's support, daily practice, a sertraline dose review and a plan for overwhelming panic.","r":"Matches NICE care for panic with agoraphobia and removes the barrier that stopped her attending.","best":true},{"c":"Ask her to attend clinic weekly for CBT.","r":"She cannot yet leave home; she will miss appointments again.","best":false},{"c":"Add diazepam to help her get out of the house.","r":"NICE advises against benzodiazepines in panic disorder; they block learning and risk dependence.","best":false}],"law":["No legal framework is engaged; she is a voluntary patient with capacity.","Equality Act 2010: services must make reasonable adjustments, such as home-based sessions for a disabling anxiety disorder.","GMC Good medical practice (2024): share information in a way she understands; agree decisions with her."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for letting me come to your home. It sounds like the last year has been really hard, and I'm not here to blame you for missed appointments."},{"to":"Patient (explaining)","s":"Anxiety is like a wave. If you stay put, it rises, peaks and then falls by itself, usually within the hour. Each time you ride it out, the next wave is smaller."},{"to":"Patient (plan)","s":"We'll start with the smallest step, here at home, and only move up when it feels manageable. You're in charge of the pace."}],"write":"Home visit note:\nHelen Marsh, 42. Agoraphobia with panic, housebound except with husband.\nPrevious treatment: online self-help 2 years ago (good attendance, overwhelmed by tasks); sertraline 50 mg for 18 months, taken daily, slight benefit.\nLost job 2 months ago. Shopping online.\nRisk: fleeting thoughts of ending her life on bad mornings; no plan, no past attempts; husband protective. Alcohol 2 glasses wine/week; no drugs. No family history.\nExplained anxiety curve, graded exposure and systematic desensitisation; she understood (teach-back).\nHierarchy (0-100): imagining going out 20; opening front door 30; standing on doorstep 2 min 40; walk to gate 55; walk to post box with husband 65; corner shop with husband 80; supermarket alone 100.\nPlan: refer to psychological therapies for home-based CBT with graded exposure and relaxation, weekly 60 min; daily practice; husband as coach. Sertraline to 100 mg. Safety plan given. Review 4 weeks.","make":["Refer to the psychological therapies team for therapist-led CBT, starting at home.","Increase sertraline from 50 mg to 100 mg each morning; GP letter.","Give a written hierarchy and daily practice record.","Written safety plan with crisis line and duty number for urgent review.","Offer her husband a session on supporting exposure without reassurance or escape.","Review in 4 weeks by phone or home visit."],"next":"Six weeks on, she can stand at the gate for 20 minutes and walk to the post box with her husband. Plan the shift from home to clinic: her husband drives her, waits in the car, and she walks in alone.","marks":["Reviews previous self-help and medication and why they fell short.","Assesses suicidal thoughts, past attempts, alcohol and family history.","Explains the anxiety curve, habituation and avoidance.","Explains graded exposure and systematic desensitisation accurately and differently.","Builds a rated hierarchy with her.","Agrees where, how often, who and homework, with husband's role.","Plans for overwhelming anxiety and reviews medication.","Checks understanding and agrees a review."],"pitfalls":["Telling her to come to clinic for therapy she cannot reach.","Explaining 'flooding' or big jumps that frighten her off.","Treating systematic desensitisation and graded exposure as identical without explaining relaxation pairing.","Skipping the direct question about suicide.","Offering benzodiazepines."],"refs":["1. NICE CG113 Generalised anxiety and panic disorder in adults (2011, checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS: Agoraphobia (nhs.uk, checked Nov 2026)","4. Wolpe J. Psychotherapy by reciprocal inhibition. Stanford University Press (1958)","5. Equality Act 2010 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: home visits for housebound patients are routine in UK community teams. Agree lone-working arrangements before you go: tell the team where you are and when you'll call in.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-56","n":1256,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🛋","title":"She wants to quit therapy: exploring transference","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 23:10","where":"Emergency department, liaison psychiatry","text":"You are the liaison CT2. Ms Erin Walsh, 29, a trainee solicitor, is three months into weekly psychodynamic psychotherapy for low mood (nine months still to go). Her therapist told her to come to A&E if she ever felt unable to cope, so she has. She is physically unhurt. She tells the triage nurse she feels worse since starting therapy and wants you to tell her therapist she is quitting. The therapy notes say she is engaging well, the outlook is good, and a negative transference has started that needs careful work. Her next session is tomorrow at 10:00."},"task":"Explore her experience of therapy and the feelings behind her wish to stop, explain transference, and agree how she takes it back to her therapist.","first60":["Name her embarrassment early: talking about therapy to a stranger at 11 at night is hard.","Say you are not here to take sides or to repeat her depression history.","Ask what made tonight the night she came, and what she hoped you would do.","Hold back from agreeing to pass on her resignation in the first minute.","Plan to rule out real problems with the therapist before you think about transference."],"ask":["'What has made you want to stop now, three months in?'","'Is anything practical getting in the way: times, travel, cost, work?'","'Has your therapist done anything that worried you, or said they are leaving?'","'What is it like for you sitting in the room with her, especially in the silences?'","'What goes through your mind about what she thinks of you?'","'Does that feeling remind you of anyone else, now or when you were younger?'","'If you told her what you have told me, what do you imagine she would do?'"],"recognise":["Transference is the patient re-living feelings from an earlier important relationship with the therapist; in psychodynamic therapy it is expected and is the material the work uses.","A wish to quit that echoes an old fear of being left often means the therapy has reached something important, not that it has failed.","Feeling worse for a while in the middle of dynamic therapy is common and is not on its own a reason to stop.","Not everything is transference: a therapist who is late, cancels, crosses boundaries or is leaving is a real problem and must be checked first.","Feelings about the therapy belong back in the therapy; your job is to help her take them there, not to replace the therapist.","The same feeling may appear with you tonight; noticing it gently is useful evidence."],"decide":[{"c":"Explore her reasons, check the therapist's conduct, link the feeling to her past and present, explain transference and agree she raises it tomorrow, with a note to the therapist by consent.","r":"Respects her choice, keeps a useful therapy alive and puts the work back where it belongs.","best":true},{"c":"Agree to email the therapist tonight that she is withdrawing.","r":"Acts out the very pattern the therapy is working on and ends a therapy that is going well.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her she is misreading the therapist and must go back.","r":"Dismissive and paternalistic; she is likely to feel unheard and drop out anyway.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): share what she tells you with her therapist only with her consent, unless there is a serious risk.","Mental Capacity Act 2005: she is presumed to have capacity to decide whether to continue therapy; the choice is hers.","NHS complaints: if she feels the therapist has acted wrongly, PALS can help her raise it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"It sounds as if the silences feel like proof she has stopped caring. I wonder if that feeling is an old one, one you have known long before therapy."},{"to":"Patient (transference)","s":"There is a name for this: transference. Feelings from an important earlier relationship get carried into a new one. In your kind of therapy, that is expected, and it is often where the real work starts."},{"to":"Patient (plan)","s":"It is your decision whether to carry on. Before you decide, could you tell her tomorrow exactly what you told me tonight?"}],"write":"Liaison note 23:45:\nSelf-referred on therapist's advice; no injury.\nReason: wishes to stop psychodynamic therapy (month 3 of 12); feels therapist 'bored' and uncaring during silences.\nNo practical barrier; no concern about therapist conduct; therapist not leaving.\nLinks made with patient: father left at 7, partner working away, manager 'prefers others'; urge to leave first.\nTransference explained; she found it helpful.\nBrief safety check: no thoughts of self-harm; partner returns tomorrow.\nPlan: attends 10:00 session and raises wish to stop; with consent, email to therapist tonight; GP letter.\nDischarged home. Safety net: therapy service, GP, 111 option 2.","make":["With her consent, email the therapist tonight: she attended, why, and that she plans to raise it at 10:00.","Copy a brief letter to her GP.","If she still wishes to stop after discussing it, the therapist and supervisor can review the fit or offer a different therapist.","Give PALS details if she later feels the therapist has acted wrongly."],"next":"A week later the therapist emails: Erin raised it in the session, cried about her father for the first time, and has decided to continue. She asks whether liaison can avoid giving advice about therapy in future. Thank her, and agree that liaison will always return such feelings to the therapy.","marks":["Acknowledges her embarrassment and keeps a curious, non-judging tone.","Explores her reasons and rules out practical barriers.","Checks the therapist's experience, conduct and plans to leave.","Finds the feeling in the room and links it to past and present relationships.","Explains transference in plain words as normal and useful.","Respects her choice and agrees she takes it back to the therapist.","Seeks consent to contact the therapist and gives a safety net."],"pitfalls":["Agreeing to pass on her resignation without exploring it.","Telling her the feelings are 'just transference' without checking the therapist's conduct.","Using jargon or definite interpretations instead of tentative suggestions.","Taking a full depression and risk history when the task is her therapy.","Becoming her new therapist instead of returning the work to the therapy."],"refs":["1. NHS website: Psychotherapy (checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Psychotherapies information resource (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017, checked Nov 2026)","5. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK NHS psychodynamic therapy the therapist often says little on purpose. Patients may read silence as coldness. Explain it, and always send feelings about the therapy back to the therapist rather than fixing them yourself.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-57","n":1257,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"👪","title":"Still depressed after CBT: explaining family therapy to her father","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 16:30","where":"Community CAMHS clinic","text":"You are the CT3 in CAMHS. Maya Kowalski, 13, has moderate depression. After 14 sessions of individual CBT and 12 weeks of fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, she is a little better but still low. She tells her CBT therapist the work keeps being undone at home: when she asked her father, Mr Tomasz Kowalski, 46, to help with a thought diary, he told her she had nothing to be sad about, her mother shouted at him, and Maya cried upstairs while they argued. Her parents used to take turns bringing her; for a month only her father has come. Maya thinks they are splitting up. Your consultant has asked you to discuss family therapy with Mr Kowalski while Maya is with her CBT therapist. Maya has agreed to this."},"task":"Explain family therapy to Maya's father, answer his questions, and explore whether it suits this family, including the conflict between her parents.","first60":["Thank him for coming and ask what he already knows and thinks about Maya's treatment.","Make clear early that no one is being blamed; families are part of every child's recovery.","Ask how things are at home and how the parents are getting on, gently and without accusation.","Find out his questions before you start explaining.","Remember Maya's own view counts: she wants her parents involved."],"ask":["'How do you see Maya at the moment, and how have you found the CBT?'","'What happens at home when Maya is low? How do you and her mum each respond?'","'How are things between you and Maya's mum at the moment?'","'When you argue, does it ever become physical, or does anyone feel frightened?'","'How is Maya's mum herself? Is she getting support?'","'What would you want to know before agreeing to family therapy?'","'Who could realistically come, and when?'"],"recognise":["Family therapy treats the family as a system: everyone's responses affect the young person, and change in one part helps the whole.","Parental conflict and a separation hidden from the child are common reasons individual work stalls; children usually sense tension even when told nothing.","Implying the parents caused the depression will lose the father and the family.","Family therapy is a NICE-recommended option for moderate or severe depression in young people, alongside individual CBT, IPT-A and psychodynamic therapy.","Ongoing domestic abuse makes joint sessions unsafe and is a safeguarding matter; screen for it.","The family therapist will set the exact model, frequency and who attends; give general information and promise a first planning meeting."],"decide":[{"c":"Explore the home situation without blame, explain family therapy and its flexible format, answer his doubts, screen for safety and agree a referral if he and Maya agree.","r":"Keeps him engaged, uses the evidence and puts the family's own choice at the centre.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him the parents' arguments are why Maya is still depressed.","r":"Blaming makes him angry and defensive; he is unlikely to attend.","best":false},{"c":"Increase the fluoxetine and repeat the CBT instead.","r":"Ignores the family problem the CBT has exposed and Maya's own view.","best":false}],"law":["Gillick competence: Maya, 13, can consent to her own treatment if she understands it; her views must be sought about who attends.","Children Act 1989: if conflict at home involves domestic abuse, a child who witnesses it may be at risk of harm; follow local safeguarding procedures.","GMC 0–18 years guidance: share information with parents in Maya's best interests; she has agreed you can talk to her father."],"say":[{"to":"Father","s":"No one here thinks you or Maya's mum caused this. Depression is rarely down to one thing. But families are a big part of how young people recover, so involving you is a strength, not a judgement."},{"to":"Father (why it may work)","s":"CBT has given Maya skills, and she has improved a little. What she says is that it gets undone at home. Family therapy works on what happens between you all, which CBT on her own cannot reach."},{"to":"Father (format)","s":"It's flexible. Often sessions are every two to three weeks for a few months. Not everyone has to come every time, and parents who are separating can still take part, together or separately."}],"write":"CAMHS note:\nSeen: Mr Kowalski (father) alone; Maya consented.\nContext: moderate depression, partial response to 14 CBT sessions and fluoxetine 20 mg. Parental separation under way, not yet told to Maya. Mother being treated for depression by GP.\nHome: arguments verbal only; no violence; no one frightened (father's account).\nExplained: systemic family therapy, flexible attendance, possible observing team with consent.\nFather: sceptical but willing; asks for evening sessions; agrees to discuss with mother.\nPlan: referral to CAMHS family therapy team; ask Maya's view at next CBT session; continue fluoxetine; parents to consider telling Maya together.\nReview: 4 weeks.","make":["Refer to the CAMHS family therapy team, noting the separation and the need for flexible or evening sessions.","Ask Maya, at her next CBT session, who she wants to attend.","Offer her mother a separate meeting; encourage her to keep up her own GP treatment.","Continue fluoxetine 20 mg each morning; review mood and suicidal thoughts at each contact.","If any domestic abuse emerges, discuss with the trust safeguarding children lead the same day."],"next":"At the first family session the mother says she wants to tell Maya about the separation but fears Maya will blame her. The family therapist plans a session with both parents to agree what to say together, then a session with Maya.","marks":["Engages the father and establishes his view without blame.","Explores parental conflict and the separation sensitively.","Screens for domestic abuse and the mother's own health.","Explains what family therapy is, including systemic ideas.","Explains the practical format and its flexibility.","Answers why it may help when CBT has not.","Checks understanding and agrees a next step, with Maya's view sought."],"pitfalls":["Implying the parents caused Maya's depression.","Lecturing on therapy models instead of answering his questions.","Avoiding the separation because it feels awkward.","Promising exact numbers of sessions that the therapist will decide.","Forgetting to ask about domestic abuse and Maya's own wishes."],"refs":["1. NICE NG134 Depression in children and young people: identification and management (2019, checked Nov 2026)","2. Association for Family Therapy and Systemic Practice: What is family therapy? (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF for Children: fluoxetine (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC 0–18 years: guidance for all doctors (checked Nov 2026)","5. Children Act 1989 and Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023, checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK family therapy often uses a team watching by video or behind a screen, with the family's consent, who then share ideas in the room. Explain this in advance so families do not feel spied on.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-58","n":1258,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🧩","title":"Is CBT for psychosis right for him?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00","where":"Early intervention in psychosis (EIP) community clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in the EIP team. Mr Callum Reid, 19, a second-year engineering student, had a first episode of psychosis seven months ago: he believed his housemates had hidden cameras in the smoke alarm and heard voices whispering about him through the wall. After a short informal admission and home treatment he is well on risperidone 3 mg at night and back at university. He still smokes cannabis at times. The team psychologist has a CBT for psychosis slot and has asked you to see him first."},"task":"Explore his vulnerability to psychosis, past and recent, share a preliminary formulation, and assess his suitability and motivation for CBT for psychosis.","first60":["Explain why you are meeting: to understand how the illness came about and whether a talking therapy fits.","Get a brief timeline of the episode and a quick check of his mental state now.","Then work through the 5Ps in order: what made him vulnerable, what tipped it, what keeps the risk up, what protects him.","Ask about cannabis, medication and the diagnosis without judging.","Keep time to share the formulation and explain CBT for psychosis yourself."],"ask":["'Can you take me back to when things started to feel wrong at university?'","'Were there any problems in your mum's pregnancy, or when you were born?'","'Is there any family history of mental health problems?'","'Was there a time someone badly let you down, or you were bullied?'","'What changed in the months before you became unwell? Were you using cannabis?'","'How do you feel about the tablets, and about being told it was psychosis?'","'What matters most to you, and what keeps you well?'","'When you're stressed, do you notice how your thoughts, feelings and actions affect each other?'"],"recognise":["NICE recommends offering CBT for psychosis to everyone with psychosis, including a first episode, one-to-one over at least 16 planned sessions.","Predisposing influences include family history of psychosis, obstetric complications, childhood adversity and experiences of betrayal or bullying.","Cannabis, especially high-strength, raises both the risk of a first episode and the risk of relapse.","Doubt about the diagnosis, side effects and ongoing cannabis use are the main things that keep relapse risk up.","Good signs for CBT: curiosity about his experiences, some insight, ability to link thoughts and feelings, and wanting to stay well.","Sexual side effects are common with risperidone and are a hidden reason for stopping; ask directly."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused 5P history, share a tentative formulation, explain CBT for psychosis in his terms and agree referral to the psychologist.","r":"Gives him a shared understanding and a reason to engage.","best":true},{"c":"Just hand him a leaflet and book the psychologist.","r":"Misses the formulation and his ambivalence, so he is less likely to attend.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him CBT is only for people who accept they have an illness.","r":"Wrong: CBT for psychosis works with doubt and does not require full insight.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: he is presumed to have capacity to choose therapy; the decision to take up CBT is his.","He is informal; no Mental Health Act powers apply."],"say":[{"to":"Patient (formulation)","s":"Can I tell you how I'm making sense of it? Some things may have made you more sensitive to stress and to mistrust. The move, the pressure and the cannabis then pushed that over the edge. Does that fit?"},{"to":"Patient (CBT)","s":"CBT looks at how thoughts, feelings, our body and what we do all feed each other. With the psychologist you'd map your experiences, test worrying thoughts, and build a plan to spot early warning signs."},{"to":"Patient (suitability)","s":"You're curious about why it happened, you can see links, and you want to stay well. Those are exactly the things that help CBT work."}],"write":"EIP note:\nCallum (19), FEP 7 months ago; well on risperidone 3 mg nocte. No current psychotic symptoms; mood fine; no thoughts of harm.\nPredisposing: born 33 weeks (2 weeks neonatal unit); maternal uncle schizophrenia; parental divorce age 11; private messages shared at 15, lasting mistrust.\nPrecipitating: move to shared house, coursework pressure, daily high-strength cannabis.\nPerpetuating: weekend cannabis; doubts diagnosis; erectile difficulty on risperidone; considers stopping.\nProtective: wants to finish degree; close to mother and sister; takes medication daily; curious.\nFormulation shared and accepted.\nSuitability for CBTp: good (psychologically minded, partial insight, motivated).\nPlan: refer to EIP psychologist for CBTp; prolactin and side-effect review with consultant; EIP substance use worker offered.","make":["Refer to the EIP psychologist for CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions).","Check prolactin and discuss sexual side effects with the consultant; consider a switch if confirmed.","Offer the EIP substance use worker for cannabis.","Offer family intervention to Callum and his mother.","Send him a short written summary of the formulation."],"next":"At session 4 the psychologist reports Callum has stopped cannabis but has missed two risperidone doses after reading about weight gain online. The team plan a medication review and weave his beliefs about medication into the CBT.","marks":["Gets a clear timeline of the episode and his current mental state.","Covers predisposing influences: family history, birth, development, childhood adversity.","Covers precipitating and perpetuating influences, including cannabis, medication and diagnosis.","Identifies protective influences and strengths.","Shares a tentative formulation and checks it fits.","Explains CBT for psychosis in his terms.","Judges suitability and motivation and agrees a referral."],"pitfalls":["Turning it into a full psychosis history with no formulation.","Forgetting obstetric and developmental history.","Judging him for cannabis, so he stops being honest.","Saying CBT needs full insight.","Leaving all the explanation to the psychologist."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 (2014): guideline on psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE QS80 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: risperidone (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Cannabis and mental health (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS England: Early intervention in psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK EIP services, CBT for psychosis and family intervention are routine offers, not extras. A short shared formulation is a standard part of preparing someone for therapy.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-59","n":1259,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍽","title":"Home from university and very thin: explaining anorexia to her mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00","where":"Adult community eating disorders service, outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT2 in the eating disorders service. Last week you assessed Chloe Bennett, 18, who came home from her first year at university after losing a lot of weight. She counts calories, runs every day to stop weight gain, has had no periods for four months, and denies vomiting, laxatives or diet pills. BMI 15. Pulse 54, BP 92/64, ECG normal, white cell count 3.4 (low); other bloods normal. You diagnosed anorexia nervosa. Chloe knows she has a problem but is frightened of gaining weight. She has consented for you to talk to her mother, Mrs Karen Bennett, 49, who has asked to see you."},"task":"Explain anorexia nervosa to Chloe's mother, including the physical risks, treatment and outlook, answer her questions, and explain how she can help.","first60":["Confirm Chloe's consent and that her mother knows you can share what Chloe agreed.","Ask what she already knows and what worries her most before you explain.","Expect fear: she may know anorexia can kill. Acknowledge it before giving facts.","Plan to cover five things: what it is, why, risks, treatment, outlook, then how she can help.","Pause after each part to check she has followed."],"ask":["'What do you already know about anorexia?'","'What worries you most?'","'What have you noticed at home since Chloe came back?'","'Is there anything you've been wondering about yourself?'","'What questions would you like answered today?'","'How are you coping, and who supports you?'"],"recognise":["Anorexia is very low weight caused by restricting food, with behaviours that stop weight gain and an intense fear of weight gain or a distorted view of body size.","Its cause is a mix of genes, personality (such as perfectionism), life stresses and social pressure; no single person or event causes it.","It has the highest death rate of any mental illness, from heart problems, starvation and suicide; physical risk comes first.","Chloe's slow pulse, low blood pressure and low white count fit her low weight and need regular checks.","Treatment is mainly outpatient: a structured meal plan with a dietitian and an eating-disorder therapy. Medication does not treat anorexia itself.","Starting treatment early improves the outlook; family support helps recovery."],"decide":[{"c":"Hear her fears, explain what anorexia is, why it happens, the risks, the treatment and the outlook in plain words, check understanding, and give her a role and support.","r":"Informs and steadies her, and turns her into an ally in treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her it's a phase and Chloe will grow out of it.","r":"False reassurance; anorexia is serious and can be fatal.","best":false},{"c":"List the diagnostic criteria and mortality figures in one go.","r":"Overwhelming and frightening; she will remember little.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): share only what Chloe has agreed to; you can always listen to her mother.","Mental Capacity Act 2005: Chloe is an adult and presumed to have capacity to make treatment decisions.","Mental Health Act 1983: feeding can count as treatment for anorexia under the Act, but only if she is detained and the criteria are met; it is a last resort."],"say":[{"to":"Mother (what it is)","s":"Anorexia is an illness where someone eats far too little, often exercises a lot, and is very frightened of putting on weight. Chloe's weight is now well below the healthy range."},{"to":"Mother (guilt)","s":"You haven't caused this. Anorexia comes from a mix of genes, personality and life pressures. Your own past doesn't mean you passed it on, and your support now really matters."},{"to":"Mother (outlook)","s":"Many people recover fully, especially when treatment starts early like now. About half recover completely, and many more improve a lot. It does take time."}],"write":"Clinic note:\nMet Mrs Bennett (mother) with Chloe's consent.\nExplained: diagnosis of anorexia nervosa; causes a mix of causes; physical risks (heart, bones, periods, infection, salts); results so far (BMI 15, pulse 54, BP 92/64, WCC 3.4, ECG normal).\nTreatment: outpatient, dietitian meal plan, CBT-ED; weekly physical monitoring; admission if risk rises.\nOutlook: about half recover fully; early treatment helps.\nMother disclosed own teenage eating difficulty; reassured not causal.\nFamily support discussed; Beat information, carers' group and Carer's assessment offered.\nPlan: weekly weight, obs and bloods with GP; dietitian next week; therapy start.","make":["Give written information (NHS website, Beat) and the Beat helpline number.","Offer her mother a place in the service's carers' skills group and a Carer's assessment from the local authority.","Agree weekly weight, pulse, BP and bloods (shared with the GP) and a dietitian appointment next week.","Explain the red flags that need urgent help: fainting, chest pain, breathlessness, confusion, weakness on standing or climbing stairs."],"next":"Three weeks later the GP rings: Chloe's weight has fallen to BMI 14.2, pulse 42, and she struggles to stand up from squatting. Arrange same-day medical review, involve her mother with consent, and consider admission under MEED guidance.","marks":["Confirms consent and asks what the mother already knows and fears.","Explains anorexia in plain words and why Chloe meets the diagnosis.","Explains causes as a mix of causes and addresses guilt.","Explains the physical risks and the monitoring, including Chloe's results.","Explains treatment: dietitian, therapy, no medication for anorexia itself, admission if needed.","Gives a balanced outlook.","Explains how the family can help and offers carer support and written information."],"pitfalls":["False reassurance that Chloe will grow out of it.","Jargon such as 'BMI criteria' or 'bradycardia' without explanation.","Missing the mother's guilt cue.","Saying medication will treat anorexia.","Forgetting written information and carer support."],"refs":["1. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020, checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists CR233: Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED) (2022, checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Anorexia nervosa (checked Nov 2026)","4. Beat eating disorders charity: support for carers (checked Nov 2026)","5. GMC Confidentiality (2017, checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK eating disorder care is mostly outpatient, and the physical risk guide is MEED (RCPsych 2022). For 16 to 25-year-olds early in the illness, many areas offer FREED, a fast-track early intervention pathway.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-60","n":1260,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🌱","title":"What predicts recovery? Anorexia prognosis and personality","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 10:30","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"You are the CT3 in the CMHT. Ms Leah Morgan, 25, has diagnoses of recurrent depression, emotionally unstable personality disorder (EUPD) and anorexia nervosa. Her notes mention two overdoses with admissions and a course of intensive eating disorder treatment some years ago. She is not close to her parents but has other support, and she attends clinic regularly. The team is reviewing whether she can be discharged to her GP, and your consultant wants a clear view of her outlook. Leah is known to be quiet in appointments."},"task":"Take a focused history of the favourable and poor prognostic signs in her anorexia nervosa, and briefly explore her personality traits.","first60":["Explain the purpose: to understand her journey and what has helped, not to re-diagnose her.","She is quiet: use open questions, reflections and patience, and tell her she can pause.","Work in time order: how it began, what she did and didn't do, the delay to treatment, treatment and recovery.","Ask about mood, self-harm and overdoses, then and now.","End on her personality, her family and her strengths, then summarise back."],"ask":["'Can you take me back to the time eating first became hard?'","'What were you doing to lose weight? Did you ever binge, or make yourself sick, or use laxatives?'","'How long did it go on before anyone knew, or you got help?'","'What treatment did you have, and how have food and weight been since?'","'Your notes mention two overdoses. What was happening each time?'","'Have you ever hurt yourself in other ways? When did that stop?'","'How would you describe yourself as a person?'","'Who is there for you now?'"],"recognise":["Favourable signs in anorexia: onset in adolescence, restricting without bingeing or purging, completing treatment, full and lasting weight restoration, good relationships, work and no substance misuse.","Poor signs: long illness before treatment, very low lowest weight, bingeing or purging, comorbid depression, personality disorder, self-harm, substance misuse and difficult family relationships.","Relapse risk is highest in the first year or two after weight restoration; several years well is reassuring.","Personality traits that matter here: perfectionism, introversion, emotional intensity and fear of abandonment.","A patient who has recovered still needs current mood and suicide risk checked, given past overdoses.","Ask about what she did not do, not only what she did: absent behaviours are prognostic too."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused, time-ordered history weighing poor against favourable signs, explore personality briefly, screen current risk and summarise.","r":"Gives the consultant a balanced view of prognosis and safe discharge.","best":true},{"c":"Take a full eating disorder assessment with weights, bloods and physical risk.","r":"She has recovered; this misses the task and the personality picture.","best":false},{"c":"Focus only on the EUPD and overdoses.","r":"Misses the favourable signs and paints a falsely bleak picture.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Confidentiality (2017): discuss with her what will go in the discharge letter to her GP.","Mental Capacity Act 2005: she is presumed to have capacity to take part in decisions about her discharge."],"say":[{"to":"Patient (opening)","s":"I'd like to understand your journey with food and what has helped you. Some of this may be difficult to talk about, so take your time, and we can pause whenever you like."},{"to":"Patient (absent behaviours)","s":"Some people also binge, make themselves sick or use laxatives. Did any of that ever happen for you?"},{"to":"Patient (summary)","s":"So you had a long, hard road: three years before help, two overdoses and self-harm. But you finished treatment, have stayed well for four years, and have work, friends and a therapist you trust. Is that fair?"}],"write":"Clinic note (prognosis review):\nAN onset 15 after weight-related bullying; restriction, slow eating, daily running, mirror checking; no binge, purge, laxatives or pills. ~3 years untreated; lowest BMI 14.\nDay-programme at 18, completed; weight stable (BMI 20) for 4 years.\nDepression: two episodes (17, 21), each with paracetamol overdose and admission.\nSelf-harm (cutting) 14–19 when emotions were intense or she feared rejection; none for 6 years.\nPersonality: introverted, perfectionist, intense emotions, fears abandonment.\nFamily: emotionally distant parents; no abuse.\nNow: mood good; no self-harm or suicidal thoughts; alcohol minimal; no drugs. Works full time; two close friends; monthly psychologist.\nView: poor signs (long untreated illness, depression, EUPD, overdoses, self-harm, distant family) outweighed by favourable signs (adolescent onset, restricting only, completed treatment, 4 years weight-stable, support, work).\nPlan: discuss discharge with consultant; relapse plan to GP.","make":["Present a balanced summary to the consultant: poor signs mostly historical, favourable signs current.","Write a relapse prevention plan with Leah for the GP: early warning signs and how to re-refer.","Continue her psychologist sessions until discharge is agreed.","Agree with her what goes in the GP letter."],"next":"Six months after discharge, Leah's GP writes: she has split up with her partner, is skipping lunch and running daily again, BMI 18.6. Use the relapse plan, offer a rapid re-assessment and check mood and self-harm.","marks":["Engages a quiet patient with open questions, reflections and patience.","Gets a time-ordered anorexia history including behaviours absent as well as present.","Identifies poor prognostic signs: long untreated illness, depression, overdoses, self-harm, EUPD.","Identifies favourable signs: restricting only, completed treatment, sustained recovery, no substances.","Explores personality traits and fear of abandonment.","Explores family relationships and bullying, and screens for abuse.","Checks current mood, self-harm and suicidal thoughts, and summarises."],"pitfalls":["Treating her as currently unwell and doing a physical risk assessment.","Asking only what she did, not what she never did.","Firing closed questions at a quiet patient.","Skipping current risk because she 'is better'.","Forgetting her strengths and support."],"refs":["1. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020, checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG78 Borderline personality disorder: recognition and management (2009, checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022, checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Eating disorders information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: 'prognosis' questions are a skill in their own right. Sort what you hear into poor and favourable signs as you go, and always ask about behaviours that were absent.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-61","n":1261,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Trying for a baby on lithium: the risks, the options and a perinatal plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30","where":"Outpatient clinic, community mental health team (perinatal advice slot)","text":"A 29-year-old woman with bipolar disorder has been well for five years on lithium (Priadel 800 mg at night, last level 0.7 mmol/L). Before lithium she had three manic relapses and two admissions, despite taking antipsychotics. She and her partner want to start trying for a baby. She is still using contraception and says, 'I read lithium damages the baby's heart. Do I have to choose between staying well and having a child?'"},"task":"Explain the risks of lithium and of relapse in pregnancy and after the birth, set out the options with their trade-offs, explain monitoring, fertility and breastfeeding, and agree a written perinatal plan she owns.","first60":["Read the notes: episodes, admissions, what happened on antipsychotics, and any relapse when lithium was missed.","Check the last lithium level, U&Es, eGFR, calcium and thyroid tests, and when they were done.","Confirm she is not pregnant now and is still using contraception.","Have the bumps lithium leaflet and the local perinatal team referral route ready.","Plan to give her three options, each with its risk, and let her choose."],"ask":["'What have you read, and what worries you most?'","'How have you been lately: mood, sleep, energy, any early warning signs?'","'What were the relapses like, and what happened on the earlier medicines?'","'Have you ever missed or stopped lithium? What happened?'","'Could you be pregnant now? What contraception are you using?'","'Any other medicines, including ibuprofen or water tablets? Any folic acid?'","'Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby?'","'Are you hoping to breastfeed?'"],"recognise":["Three relapses on antipsychotics and five well years on lithium: her relapse risk off lithium is high and lithium is the drug that works for her.","Bipolar disorder carries a high risk of severe illness after the birth, including postpartum psychosis, and the risk is highest when medicine is stopped.","First-trimester lithium is linked with a small rise in heart defects, more at higher levels; Ebstein's anomaly is rare and most exposed babies have normal hearts.","Pregnancy lowers lithium levels as fluid volume and kidney clearance rise; levels jump back after delivery, so toxicity is a real risk around the birth.","Lithium is not known to reduce fertility. Breastfeeding on lithium is usually not advised because the baby can reach high levels.","Stopping abruptly is the riskiest choice of all: relapse is more likely after a sudden stop than a slow taper."],"decide":[{"c":"Give her the three options with their risks, recommend continuing lithium with close monitoring given her history, start folic acid 400 micrograms, refer to the perinatal team for preconception advice and write the plan.","r":"Right: an informed, shared decision grounded in her high relapse risk, with a safe monitoring plan and specialist input before conception.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her she must stop lithium before trying to conceive.","r":"Unsafe and paternalistic: her illness relapsed three times without lithium. Stopping is one option, not an instruction, and if chosen it must be a slow taper with a relapse plan.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her that lithium is now known to be safe in pregnancy and leave the plan to the GP.","r":"False reassurance: there is a small, real rise in heart defects, and lithium in pregnancy needs specialist monitoring and a birth plan.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Decision making and consent (2020): set out each option, including stopping and doing nothing, with risks that matter to her; the decision is hers.","NICE CG192: for women with bipolar disorder planning a pregnancy, discuss the risks of relapse and of each medicine, and offer preconception input from specialist perinatal services where the illness is severe.","NICE CG192: if lithium continues in pregnancy, check levels every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks and within 24 hours of birth, with adequate fluids and delivery in hospital.","Confidentiality: share the plan with the GP, and with her consent with the midwife, obstetrician and health visitor."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You don't have to choose between staying well and having a baby. There are three routes, each with its own risks, and I'll help you weigh them honestly."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If your period is late or a test is positive, please don't stop the lithium suddenly. Ring us that day; we'll check your level and see you within the week."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Plan: she has chosen to continue lithium (Priadel 800 mg nocte, target level at the lowest effective point). Folic acid 400 micrograms daily started. Please avoid NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors and arrange levels as below once pregnant."}],"write":"Perinatal advice clinic. 29F, bipolar affective disorder. Lithium (Priadel) 800 mg nocte 5 years; level 0.7 mmol/L (Oct); eGFR > 90, TSH and calcium normal.\nHistory: 3 manic relapses aged 19 to 24 on antipsychotics despite adherence; 2 admissions. Well on lithium except a short depressive episode last year. Mother had 'a breakdown' after a birth (to clarify).\nPlanning pregnancy with partner; on progestogen-only pill; not pregnant. Not on folic acid.\nMSE: euthymic, no psychotic symptoms, no suicidal thoughts.\nDiscussed: postpartum relapse and psychosis risk; small rise in cardiac defects with first-trimester lithium; level changes in pregnancy and after birth; toxicity signs; fertility unaffected; breastfeeding not advised on lithium; options (continue, switch to antipsychotic, slow taper) with risks. Teach-back correct.\nPlan: she chooses to continue lithium. Folic acid 400 micrograms daily now. If pregnant: do not stop, contact team same day. Perinatal team preconception referral. Leaflet given. Review with partner in 6 weeks.\nDr K. Mensah CT3","make":["Say the trade-off out loud: a small rise in heart risk for the baby against a high risk of relapse for her.","Give three options, each with its main risk: continue lithium, switch to an antipsychotic, or taper slowly over at least 4 weeks.","Explain the level plan: every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, within 24 hours of birth, with dose changes as needed.","Name the toxicity signs and the triggers: vomiting, diarrhoea, dehydration, ibuprofen, ACE inhibitors.","Tell her breastfeeding is usually not advised on lithium, so she can plan feeding early.","Refer to the perinatal team now and agree a written care plan by 32 weeks, shared with maternity."],"next":"She and her partner choose to stay on lithium. The perinatal team sees her before conception. She conceives five months later, has 4-weekly levels and a detailed heart scan at 20 weeks, delivers in hospital with a written plan, bottle-feeds, and stays well.","marks":["Asked what she knew and feared before giving facts.","Established relapse history and what happened off lithium.","Explained the heart defect risk honestly, without false reassurance.","Explained the high risk of relapse and psychosis after the birth.","Set out all three options with their risks and let her decide.","Covered level monitoring, fertility, breastfeeding and a perinatal referral."],"pitfalls":["Telling her to stop lithium, or stopping it abruptly on a positive test.","Calling lithium 'safe now' or quoting only the scary Ebstein's figure.","Forgetting that levels fall in pregnancy and rise sharply after the birth.","Leaving out breastfeeding, so she learns on the postnatal ward."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. UKTIS. bumps: Use of lithium in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: Lithium carbonate, interactions and monitoring (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the perinatal mental health team offers preconception advice to women with severe illness. The written perinatal care plan goes to the midwife, obstetrician, GP and health visitor, and copying letters to the patient is routine.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-62","n":1262,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Valproate and wanting a baby: the risks, the safety rules and a planned switch","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00","where":"Perinatal mental health outpatient clinic","text":"A 33-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder has been well for three years on sodium valproate 1 g at night and sertraline 100 mg each morning, with a contraceptive implant. She has had three admissions under section, two for mania with psychotic symptoms. Lithium caused incontinence, aripiprazole did not work and olanzapine worked but she gained weight. She married last year and says, 'I know valproate is a problem. Can I still have a baby, and will social services take it?'"},"task":"Explain the risks of valproate in pregnancy and the MHRA safety rules, address her worries about relapse, sertraline and social services, and plan a safe, supervised switch before she stops contraception.","first60":["Read the history so you do not retake it: the episodes, what each medicine did, and three years well and drug-free.","Check the Annual Risk Acknowledgement Form is in date and the implant is in place.","Confirm she is not pregnant now.","Have the MHRA valproate patient guide and the bumps leaflets on olanzapine and sertraline ready.","Know the alternatives and why each does or does not suit her before you start."],"ask":["'What are your thoughts about a pregnancy, with your illness and your medicines in mind?'","'What do you already know about valproate in pregnancy?'","'What worries you most?'","'Is the implant still in, and could you be pregnant now?'","'How did you get on with olanzapine apart from the weight?'","'Who supports you at home, and does your husband know the plan?'"],"recognise":["Valproate is the most harmful common mood stabiliser in pregnancy: about 11 in 100 babies have a major birth defect and 30 to 40 in 100 children have developmental problems.","The MHRA rules require a pregnancy prevention programme, highly effective contraception and annual specialist review with a signed risk form; she must switch before contraception stops.","Bipolar I with past psychosis gives a high risk of severe illness, including postpartum psychosis, in the weeks after birth.","Olanzapine kept her well before and is a commonly used mood-stabilising antipsychotic in pregnancy; weight is the cost to manage.","Sertraline is a well-studied antidepressant in pregnancy and breastfeeding and can continue.","Having bipolar disorder is not a bar to parenthood; social care involvement, if any, is usually supportive."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the risks, keep the implant for now, start olanzapine and taper valproate slowly under the perinatal team, continue sertraline, and only stop contraception once she has been stable on olanzapine alone.","r":"Right: removes the main risk before conception, avoids an abrupt change, and keeps her safe while the new drug proves itself.","best":true},{"c":"Stop valproate today and remove the implant so she can start trying.","r":"Dangerous: abrupt withdrawal risks a manic relapse, and she would have no proven mood stabiliser if she conceived quickly.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her that with three admissions, pregnancy is unwise.","r":"Wrong and discriminatory: her diagnosis does not bar parenthood. The job is to make a pregnancy as safe as possible.","best":false}],"law":["MHRA valproate Pregnancy Prevention Programme: highly effective contraception, pregnancy tests as needed, annual specialist review and a signed Annual Risk Acknowledgement Form.","MHRA (January 2024): valproate should not be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists independently document that no other treatment is effective or tolerated; women of childbearing potential need a second specialist to agree continuation at review.","GMC Decision making and consent (2020): give her the material risks of each option and record her decision.","NICE CG192: plan medicine changes before conception with women with severe mental illness, involving specialist perinatal services."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Wanting a family is completely reasonable, and having bipolar disorder doesn't change that. My job is to help you get there as safely as possible."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Please keep the implant in until we've switched and you've been settled on the new medicine for a few months. If you ever find you're pregnant on valproate, don't stop it suddenly: ring us that day."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Plan: cross-taper from sodium valproate to olanzapine under the perinatal team; implant to stay until stable on olanzapine alone. Sertraline 100 mg continues. Please add weight, HbA1c and lipid monitoring to her recall."}],"write":"Perinatal clinic. 33F, bipolar I disorder. Sodium valproate MR 1 g nocte, sertraline 100 mg mane. Well 3 years; no substance use 3 years. Implant in situ (fitted 2024). ARAF signed 2026.\nPast: 3 admissions under MHA (2 manic with psychosis, 1 depressive). Lithium: incontinence. Aripiprazole: ineffective. Olanzapine: effective, weight gain. Risperidone: effective in first admission.\nWants pregnancy with husband. Not pregnant.\nDiscussed: valproate risks (about 11/100 major malformations; 30 to 40/100 neurodevelopmental problems); PPP rules; postpartum relapse and psychosis risk; sertraline in pregnancy; newborn checks; social services role. Teach-back correct.\nPlan: start olanzapine 5 mg nocte, then 10 mg after 1 week; once settled, reduce valproate 250 mg every 2 weeks to stop. Implant stays until stable on olanzapine alone 3 to 6 months. Folic acid 5 mg daily while on valproate. Weight/BMI/waist, HbA1c, lipids baseline and at 12 weeks. Dietitian referral. Review 2 weeks.\nDr A. Nwosu ST5","make":["Give valproate's risks as absolute numbers: about 11 in 100 major birth defects; 30 to 40 in 100 developmental problems.","Explain the rules she lives with: contraception, annual review and the signed form, and why they exist.","Lay out the switch in steps: olanzapine up first, valproate down slowly, contraception last.","Say clearly: never stop valproate suddenly, even if a test is positive; ring the team that day.","Answer social services directly and honestly: illness alone is not a reason to remove a baby.","Plan weight support before olanzapine starts, not after she has gained."],"next":"She starts olanzapine and comes off valproate over two months with no relapse. With dietitian input her weight rises by 2 kg. After four stable months the implant is removed; she conceives, and the perinatal team writes her birth plan by 32 weeks.","marks":["Elicited her views and worries about pregnancy and her medicines.","Explained valproate's birth defect and developmental risks with numbers.","Explained the pregnancy prevention rules and why contraception stays until the switch is done.","Gave the postpartum relapse and psychosis risk honestly.","Proposed a safe alternative with its downsides and a gradual switch plan.","Reassured about parenthood and social services without false promises."],"pitfalls":["Retaking her whole history when it is already in the notes.","Stopping valproate or the implant on the day.","Suggesting lithium or aripiprazole again despite past failure, or lamotrigine alone for bipolar I.","Ducking the social services question."],"refs":["1. MHRA: Valproate use by women and girls; Pregnancy Prevention Programme materials (checked Nov 2026)","2. MHRA Drug Safety Update: Valproate, new safety and educational materials (January 2024; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (checked Nov 2026)","4. UKTIS. bumps: Use of sodium valproate, olanzapine and sertraline in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK every woman of childbearing potential on valproate must be on the Pregnancy Prevention Programme with a signed annual form. Pharmacists check, and auditors look for the form in the notes.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-63","n":1263,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Five months postnatal and not improving: what's keeping her stuck, and what next","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 12:15","where":"Perinatal mental health team office, by phone with the health visitor","text":"A 32-year-old woman is five months after the birth of her daughter. Postnatal depression was diagnosed at four weeks; her GP's sertraline has been increased to 200 mg, now taken for eight weeks, and she has finished a course of CBT with the team. She is still low. Her health visitor rings: 'She's doing everything right and nothing's working. What's stopping her getting better, and what else can we do?'"},"task":"With the health visitor, summarise the course so far, explore the factors keeping the depression going, check risk, and recommend specific biological, psychological and social next steps, with a plan B if they fail.","first60":["Pull up the notes: diagnosis date, sertraline doses and dates, CBT sessions attended, last risk assessment.","Confirm the patient agreed to information sharing with the health visitor (she is part of the care team).","Have the team's resources in mind: perinatal psychology, occupational therapy, nursery nurse, peer support.","Check when bloods were last done (thyroid, full blood count, ferritin).","Plan to ask about risk to her and the baby before you hang up."],"ask":["'Can you give me a quick picture of how she is now compared with the start?'","'Is she taking the sertraline every day? Any alcohol or drugs?'","'How are things at home: her husband, family, money, housing?'","'How is she with the baby, and how is the baby doing?'","'What does she say about herself as a mum and a partner?'","'What does a typical day look like for her now?'","'How does she feel about stopping breastfeeding?'","'Has she mentioned thoughts of harming herself or the baby, or anything unusual in her thinking?'"],"recognise":["Eight weeks at the maximum dose of sertraline with little change is an adequate trial that has failed; after one antidepressant, switching is usually favoured over adding a second drug.","Protective factors are strong: supportive husband and family, a stable home, good bonding, adherence and no substance use. Look elsewhere for what keeps it going.","Harsh beliefs about being a bad mother and partner, and a shrinking, housebound routine, are classic perpetuating factors.","Stopping breastfeeding when overwhelmed can leave guilt or regret that feeds those beliefs.","Treatment resistance should prompt a check for physical causes (thyroid, anaemia) and a rethink of the diagnosis, including bipolar disorder.","If she does not respond, options include augmentation, ECT, rTMS and a mother and baby unit, which can admit until the baby is 12 months old."],"decide":[{"c":"Review her this week, switch to a different antidepressant with a cross-taper, check thyroid and blood count, ask psychology about IPT or parent-infant work, add OT and peer support, and agree risk triggers with the health visitor.","r":"Right: a failed adequate trial changed, physical causes excluded, and her negative beliefs and isolation targeted, with a shared safety net.","best":true},{"c":"Advise the health visitor to give it another month on sertraline.","r":"Wrong: eight weeks at maximum dose without benefit is enough. Waiting prolongs illness during a key time for her and the baby.","best":false},{"c":"Add quetiapine to sertraline today without seeing her.","r":"Premature: augmentation is a later step after one failed antidepressant, and no medicine change should be made without reviewing her.","best":false}],"law":["NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022): if there is no adequate response, review adherence and diagnosis, then consider switching, increasing or combining treatments, using shared decision-making.","NICE CG192: offer high-intensity psychological interventions and timely access for women in the perinatal period, and consider the mother-baby relationship.","Information sharing: the health visitor is part of her direct care team; confirm the patient agreed, and share only what is needed.","Safeguarding duty: any concern about risk to the baby goes to the named safeguarding lead and, if needed, children's social care."],"say":[{"to":"Health visitor","s":"Thank you for flagging this; you're right to be worried. Eight weeks at the top dose without real change means sertraline isn't working for her, and we need to change course."},{"to":"Health visitor","s":"What stands out to me is how harshly she judges herself as a mum and partner, and how little she's doing. Those two things can keep depression going even when everything else is in her favour."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Postnatal depression not responding to sertraline 200 mg for 8 weeks and CBT. Plan: switch antidepressant after review this week; please check TFTs, FBC, ferritin, B12 and folate. Perinatal team will coordinate psychology, OT and peer support."}],"write":"Telephone liaison with health visitor (patient consented to sharing). 32F, 5/12 postnatal, daughter Isla. Postnatal depression diagnosed at 4/52. Sertraline titrated to 200 mg, 8 weeks at that dose; CBT completed. Minimal response; EPDS 19 last week.\nNo concerns re: adherence, alcohol, drugs, support (husband, mother nearby), housing or finances. Bonding good; Isla thriving.\nPerpetuating factors: negative beliefs about self as mother and partner; housebound with few activities; possible guilt after stopping breastfeeding at 2/12.\nRisk: HV asked last week: no thoughts of self-harm or harming Isla; no unusual beliefs reported.\nPlan: urgent perinatal review this week; switch antidepressant via cross-taper (options mirtazapine, venlafaxine, another SSRI); bloods (TFTs, FBC, ferritin, B12, folate); psychology to consider IPT or parent-infant work; OT and nursery nurse; peer support group; carers' assessment for husband; Early Help if wanted. Plan B discussed. HV to call same day if risk changes.\nDr L. Cohen ST4","make":["Summarise the course back to the health visitor first, so you are both working from the same facts.","Rule out the usual culprits aloud: adherence, substances, relationships, money, housing, bonding.","Name the perpetuating factors you found and why each keeps depression going.","Give named options in each domain, not 'consider medication and therapy'.","Give a plan B: augmentation, rethink the diagnosis, ECT or rTMS, mother and baby unit.","Agree what the health visitor should escalate and how fast."],"next":"The patient is seen two days later and starts mirtazapine while sertraline is tapered. TSH is normal. She joins a perinatal peer group and starts IPT. By three months her EPDS has fallen to 9 and she is going out daily with Isla.","marks":["Summarised the history and treatments so far with the health visitor.","Checked adherence, substances, support, relationships and the baby's care.","Identified negative self-beliefs, low activity and feeding regret as perpetuating factors.","Recommended switching antidepressant after an adequate trial, with physical checks.","Recommended named psychological and social interventions inside and outside the team.","Gave a plan B and agreed risk escalation with the health visitor."],"pitfalls":["Advising more time on a failed dose.","Generic advice ('therapy and support') with nothing named.","Forgetting thyroid and anaemia checks, or never questioning the diagnosis.","Ending the call without asking about risk to mother and baby."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) guideline (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Mirtazapine; Venlafaxine; Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Postnatal depression (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: health visitors are specialist public health nurses who see every family with a child under five. They know the home well; treat them as a key colleague and agree exactly what they should report back.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-64","n":1264,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"15 weeks pregnant on quetiapine: the gestational diabetes question","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 09:40","where":"Perinatal mental health clinic, district general hospital","text":"A 35-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder is 15 weeks pregnant. She has been well for four years on quetiapine XL 400 mg at night; her last episode, six years ago, was mania with psychosis. She stayed well on quetiapine through her first pregnancy, but developed gestational diabetes and had a caesarean at 38 weeks, which upset her. She has recently separated and her parents live two hours away. She asks, 'Is the quetiapine going to give me diabetes again? Should I change it?'"},"task":"Review quetiapine in this pregnancy, explain its risks including gestational diabetes and breastfeeding, compare the alternatives, explain her postpartum relapse risk, give a recommendation, and agree a plan with obstetrics for early screening and support.","first60":["Read the notes: episodes, response to quetiapine, the first pregnancy and the diabetes, and any other drugs tried.","Check her booking bloods, BMI, and whether an early glucose test has been booked.","Know the obstetric team, the antenatal diabetes clinic and the midwife's names to share the plan.","Have the bumps leaflet on quetiapine and NHS information on gestational diabetes ready.","Be ready to give a clear recommendation; she has asked for one."],"ask":["'How did your first pregnancy go, for your mood and physically?'","'What worries you most about this pregnancy?'","'How are you now: mood, sleep, any early signs?'","'Has anyone checked your blood sugar yet this time?'","'How are you eating with the sickness? Are you managing the quetiapine?'","'Who is around to support you, now and after the birth?'","'Are you hoping to breastfeed?'"],"recognise":["Quetiapine is not linked with a clear rise in major birth defects; babies may be unsettled or sleepy for a few days after birth.","Gestational diabetes in a past pregnancy is a strong risk for recurrence (roughly 1 in 3 or more), whatever the medicine.","Quetiapine and olanzapine are linked with weight gain and may add to diabetes risk, though the evidence is mixed.","Bipolar I with past psychosis carries a high risk of severe relapse, including postpartum psychosis, especially without effective preventive medicine.","Switching a medicine that has worked, mid-pregnancy, risks relapse and exposes the baby to two drugs.","Separation, distance from family and a toddler at home raise her risk and need practical planning."],"decide":[{"c":"Recommend continuing quetiapine XL 400 mg, arrange an early glucose tolerance test now and again at 24 to 28 weeks, refer to the antenatal diabetes clinic and dietitian, and write a perinatal plan with obstetrics.","r":"Right: keeps the medicine that has protected her, tackles the diabetes risk directly, and shares a clear plan with obstetrics.","best":true},{"c":"Switch to aripiprazole now because it has less effect on weight.","r":"Reasonable to discuss, but a mid-pregnancy switch from a proven drug risks relapse, aripiprazole is untested for her, and it may reduce milk supply. Only if she chooses it, after full discussion.","best":false},{"c":"Switch to lamotrigine, which is well studied in pregnancy.","r":"Wrong: lamotrigine protects mainly against depression and is not enough to prevent mania or postpartum psychosis in bipolar I.","best":false}],"law":["NICE NG3 Diabetes in pregnancy: after previous gestational diabetes, offer early self-monitoring or a 75 g OGTT as soon as possible after booking, and an OGTT at 24 to 28 weeks if the first is normal.","NICE CG192: weigh the risks of stopping or switching against continuing, and write a perinatal care plan in the third trimester, shared with maternity, GP and health visitor.","GMC Decision making and consent (2020): give a recommendation when asked, with options and their risks, and respect her decision.","Information sharing: copy the plan to obstetrics, midwife, GP and health visitor with her agreement."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You asked what I'd recommend, so I'll be straight: I think staying on quetiapine is safest for you and the baby, with extra steps to cut the diabetes risk and catch it early."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Gestational diabetes after a previous one is common whatever tablet you take, so the testing and diet plan matter either way."},{"to":"Obstetric team (letter)","s":"Bipolar I, well on quetiapine XL 400 mg nocte, continuing. Previous GDM. Please arrange an early 75 g OGTT now and at 24 to 28 weeks if normal, refer to the antenatal diabetes clinic and dietitian, and alert paediatrics for neonatal observation. Perinatal care plan to follow by 32 weeks."}],"write":"Perinatal clinic. 35F, G2P1, 15/40. Bipolar I; last episode 6 years ago (mania with psychosis). Quetiapine XL 400 mg nocte; well 4 years and through first pregnancy.\nFirst pregnancy: GDM (diet then metformin), caesarean 38/40, found distressing. Daughter Lily, 3.\nNow: nausea, eating little and often; taking quetiapine nightly. Euthymic; no psychotic symptoms; no suicidal thoughts. Recently separated; parents 2 hours away. BMI 31 at booking. No glucose test yet.\nDiscussed: quetiapine and malformations, neonatal adaptation, GDM risk (mixed evidence; previous GDM main risk), breastfeeding, alternatives (aripiprazole, olanzapine; not lamotrigine or valproate), postpartum relapse and psychosis risk. Recommendation given; she chooses to continue quetiapine.\nPlan: early OGTT this fortnight and at 24 to 28/40 if normal; antenatal diabetes clinic and dietitian; Letter to obstetrics, midwife, GP. Birth choices midwife referral. Perinatal care plan by 32/40. Review 4 weeks.\nDr F. Siddiqui CT2","make":["Ask about the first pregnancy before explaining; her story holds the key worries.","Separate what is known (no clear rise in birth defects) from what is uncertain (diabetes link).","Say that previous gestational diabetes is the biggest risk factor, whatever the medicine.","Compare alternatives honestly, including why lamotrigine and valproate are not options.","Give a clear recommendation when asked, then check what she wants to do.","Write to obstetrics the same day: early glucose test, diabetes clinic, dietitian, neonatal observation."],"next":"She stays on quetiapine. Her early glucose test is normal; at 26 weeks it shows gestational diabetes, picked up early and managed with diet and metformin. She has a planned vaginal birth after discussion, stays well, and breastfeeds with mixed feeds.","marks":["Explored her first pregnancy and her current worries.","Explained quetiapine's risks in pregnancy and breastfeeding accurately.","Explained the diabetes evidence is mixed and her previous diabetes is the main risk.","Compared alternatives with their risks and excluded unsuitable ones.","Explained her postpartum relapse risk and the value of staying on prevention.","Gave a recommendation and agreed screening and support with obstetrics."],"pitfalls":["Switching a medicine that works because of an uncertain risk.","Offering lamotrigine or valproate.","Forgetting the early glucose test that previous diabetes requires.","Refusing to give a view when she asks for one."],"refs":["1. NICE NG3 Diabetes in pregnancy: management from preconception to the postnatal period (2015, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (checked Nov 2026)","3. UKTIS. bumps: Use of quetiapine in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Gestational diabetes (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, women with previous gestational diabetes should be offered early testing after booking. Psychiatrists do not order the OGTT themselves; write to the obstetric team and midwife and ask them to arrange it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-65","n":1265,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Washing the bottles six times: a postnatal OCD history","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 15:20","where":"Outpatient clinic, community mental health team","text":"A 35-year-old woman with a six-week-old son has been referred by her GP. She has had OCD since she was 29, treated with CBT and medicines, and has been stable on sertraline. Since the birth her worries have got much worse. She sits down, embarrassed, and says, 'I know it sounds mad. I wash his bottles over and over and I can't stop.'"},"task":"Take a history of how her obsessions and compulsions have changed since the birth, link them to the birth and her past OCD, assess their impact on her functioning and on the baby, screen for other illness, and assess risk.","first60":["Read the GP letter and past notes: age at onset, previous treatments and doses, and what worked.","Check her current sertraline dose and whether it changed in pregnancy.","Plan to normalise early: intrusive thoughts after birth are common, and shame stops women telling.","Remember the perinatal lens: the baby's care, feeding, sleep, harm thoughts and the health visitor.","Keep a mental checklist: obsessions, form, compulsions, avoidance, impact, risk, comorbidity."],"ask":["'Tell me what happens when you make up a bottle.'","'What goes through your mind just before you wash it again?'","'Whose thoughts are these? Do they feel like your own?'","'What happens if you try not to wash it again?'","'Are there other things you have to do, check or count?'","'Some new mums get sudden, upsetting thoughts or pictures of the baby coming to harm. Has anything like that happened to you?'","'Has any of this ever delayed a feed or stopped you doing something for him?'","'How is your mood, sleep and eating? Any thoughts about ending your life?'"],"recognise":["Postnatal onset or worsening is common in OCD; the new baby gives fears a new, high-stakes focus.","Recognised as her own, unwanted and senseless, resisted with rising anxiety and relieved by rituals: these are obsessions and compulsions, not delusions.","Intrusive harm thoughts in OCD are horrifying to the mother, lead to avoidance and are not linked with acting on them; they differ from psychotic or depressive ideas of harm.","Spread to checking and counting, hours lost and avoidance of help are signs of a significant relapse.","Risk to the baby is usually indirect: delayed feeds, over-washing, or avoiding care because of the thoughts.","Screen carefully for postnatal depression and psychosis, which can coexist and change the urgency."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history, explain this sounds like a postnatal relapse of OCD, offer CBT with exposure and response prevention through the perinatal team, review sertraline with her, and tell the health visitor with her consent.","r":"Right: an accurate picture, a clear and non-judgemental explanation, evidence-based treatment, and support around the baby.","best":true},{"c":"Refer to children's social care because she has thoughts of the baby being harmed.","r":"Wrong: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts in OCD do not mean she is a danger. A referral would deepen shame and stop disclosure. Refer only if there is a real safeguarding concern.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to stop sterilising so much and reassure her the bottles are clean.","r":"Unhelpful: reassurance feeds the cycle. She needs structured treatment, not advice to stop.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG31 OCD: offer CBT including exposure and response prevention; for more than mild impairment, CBT with ERP or an SSRI, and the combination when severe.","NICE CG192: in pregnancy and the postnatal period, offer psychological treatment within weeks of assessment.","Safeguarding: intrusive harm thoughts alone are not a safeguarding concern; act if there is neglect, intent, psychosis or severe depression.","Confidentiality: share with the health visitor and GP with her consent, explaining why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for telling me. You're not mad. Lots of new mums get thoughts like these, and they're a sign of how much you care, not of what you'd do."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing sounds like your OCD coming back after the birth. It's very treatable, and we'd want to start quickly."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Postnatal relapse of OCD: contamination obsessions with washing, plus new checking and counting. Ego-dystonic harm images, no intent, no psychosis, not depressed. Baby well cared for. Plan: perinatal CBT with ERP; sertraline review; health visitor informed with consent."}],"write":"Clinic assessment. 35F, son Leo 6/52. OCD since age 29: CBT (helpful), clomipramine for some years (sedation), sertraline 100 mg for 3 years, well until birth.\nSince birth: obsessions that germs will give Leo an infection; recognised as own and excessive; resists, anxiety rises. Washes each bottle 6 times and re-sterilises. Last 3 weeks: checking locks, switches and cooker 6 times. Rituals about 3 hours/day. Avoids letting partner prepare feeds; avoids visitors and baby group.\nIntrusive image of dropping Leo on the stairs: horrified, no intent; carries him downstairs only when partner home.\nImpact: one feed delayed about 20 minutes; Leo gaining weight per HV; hands cracked; sleep 4 to 5 hours; eating less.\nMood anxious, not depressed; enjoys Leo. No suicidal thoughts. No psychotic symptoms. No alcohol or drugs. Organised, perfectionist traits.\nImpression: postnatal relapse of OCD, moderate to severe.\nPlan: perinatal CBT with ERP; sertraline review; Y-BOCS; HV and GP informed with consent; review 2 weeks.\nDr T. Nowak CT1","make":["Normalise before you ask about harm thoughts; shame is the main barrier to disclosure.","Establish the form: her own thoughts, unwanted, senseless to her, resisted, with anxiety relieved by rituals.","Ask about every ritual, not just the bottles: checking, counting, washing herself or the baby.","Measure the cost in hours a day, and ask directly about delayed feeds and over-washing the baby.","Screen mood, suicide, psychosis and substances before you summarise.","Ask why she is not breastfeeding; correct the idea that sertraline rules it out."],"next":"She starts CBT with exposure and response prevention through the perinatal team two weeks later, and her sertraline is increased after review. By three months her washing is down to one rinse and she has taken Leo to a baby group.","marks":["Established onset, course and the link to the birth and to her past OCD.","Explored the content of obsessions, including harm thoughts, without judgement.","Established the form: own thoughts, unwanted, resisted, anxiety relieved by rituals.","Elicited all compulsions, avoidance and the time they take.","Assessed risk to the baby, self-neglect and suicide.","Screened for depression, psychosis, anxiety and substances."],"pitfalls":["Stopping at the bottles and missing the checking and counting.","Not asking about harm thoughts, or reacting with alarm when she discloses them.","Calling her intrusive thoughts a safeguarding concern without evidence of risk.","Using jargon such as 'ego-dystonic obsessions' with her."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31 OCD and BDD guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) (checked Nov 2026)","4. UKTIS. bumps / NHS: Antidepressants and breastfeeding (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK perinatal teams see intrusive harm thoughts in new mothers often. A calm, normalising response is expected; fear of social services is the main reason women hide these thoughts, so say early that having thoughts is not the same as risk.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-66","n":1266,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🔁","title":"'People will think I'm a paedophile': history of obsessional thoughts and checking","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00, new patient slot","where":"General adult outpatient clinic, community mental health centre","text":"Mr Daniel Hughes, 32, a warehouse supervisor, was referred by his GP. He told the GP he fears people see him as a paedophile, and that he reads every text message many times before sending it. The GP letter ends: 'OCD, psychosis, or something more worrying?' He sits nearest the door and will not meet your eye."},"task":"Take a history that shows his thoughts are obsessions with checking compulsions, track how his insight has slipped over time, separate them from paedophilic interest, psychosis and depression, check risk, and tell him the diagnosis.","first60":["Read the GP letter for onset, triggers, any past episode and current medication.","Open with a line that removes shame: 'People bring all sorts of unwanted thoughts to this clinic. Nothing you tell me will shock me.'","Let him name the thought in his own words before you ask anything closed.","Watch his manner: shame, horror and avoidance fit an unwanted thought, not a wish.","Plan the arc: the thought now, how it started, how he saw it then and now, checking and impact, the 'is it a wish' question, psychosis, mood and risk, past episode, then the diagnosis."],"ask":["'What exactly goes through your mind when it happens?'","'Do these thoughts push in, or do you choose to think them? Whose thoughts are they?'","'What do you do when one arrives: push it away, test yourself, go over it?'","'When it first started, what did you make of the thoughts? And how true do they feel today?'","'Is there anything you must check or repeat, over and over? What has all this stopped you doing?'","'I ask this plainly and without judgement: have you ever felt sexually drawn to children, had urges to act, or looked at images of children?'","'Do you feel watched, talked about on TV or online, or that your thoughts are interfered with?'","'Has anything like this happened before?'"],"recognise":["Obsession: his own thought, intrusive, unwanted, resisted, and once seen by him as excessive. Rumination and mental testing ('am I one?') are part of it.","Insight can fall as OCD worsens. ICD-11 lets you specify OCD 'with poor to absent insight'; poor insight alone does not make it psychosis.","Paedophilic interest looks different: arousal and fantasies are welcome rather than horrifying, he would seek out material or contact, and there is little distress at the content.","Checking the body for arousal near children, avoiding children and seeking reassurance are compulsions that keep sexual-theme OCD going.","Psychosis: a fixed belief with no room for doubt, often with reference, persecution, hallucinations or passivity. Depression: pervasive low mood, anhedonia and guilt, with ruminations secondary to it.","Severity markers: thoughts most of the day, over an hour a day on compulsions, sick leave and isolation."],"decide":[{"c":"Name severe OCD with poor insight, explain why from his own history, and offer CBT with exposure and response prevention plus an SSRI, with consultant review.","r":"Right: his history shows classic obsessions that lost insight with severity; with marked impairment NICE advises combined treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him the thoughts sound delusional and start an antipsychotic.","r":"Wrong: there is no fixed belief, no other psychotic feature, and the early ego-dystonic course points to OCD. An antipsychotic alone would not treat it.","best":false},{"c":"Make a safeguarding referral because he has sexual thoughts about children.","r":"Wrong: unwanted thoughts with no urge, arousal or behaviour pose no risk to children. Reporting would breach confidentiality, confirm his fear and stop him seeking help.","best":false}],"law":["GMC confidentiality: intrusive thoughts alone are not a disclosure of risk. Share without consent only if he reveals a real risk of serious harm to a child, such as contact, intent or indecent images.","He has capacity and is informal; no MHA or MCA issue arises.","Any doctor can issue a fit note (Med3); give one if he needs continued time off work while treatment starts."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I think this is obsessive-compulsive disorder, and a severe episode. The thoughts push in, they horrify you, and you fight them. That is how OCD behaves. It is not a hidden wish, and it is not psychosis."},{"to":"Patient (why it feels true)","s":"When OCD gets this strong, the doubt grows until the thought feels real. Feeling true is not the same as being true. Treatment brings that doubt back down."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Severe OCD with poor insight, sexual and harm themes, with checking. No psychotic features, no depressive episode, no risk to self or others. Plan: CBT with ERP via our psychology team and sertraline. Please do not offer repeated reassurance about the thoughts."}],"write":"14:00 New patient. Daniel Hughes, 32, referred by GP.\nPC: 8 months of intrusive thoughts that others see him as a paedophile, and fear it might be true. Onset after relationship ended.\nHPC: Own thoughts, unwanted, resisted, then ruminated on. Initially once a day and seen as absurd; now most of the day, believes them '50:50'. Checks texts 5-6 times, sockets, cooker, doors and windows (~1 h nightly). Checks body for arousal near children; avoids nephews. Off work 6 weeks, isolating.\nNo sexual interest in children, no urges, no images, no contact; horrified by the idea.\nNo persecutory or referential ideas, no hallucinations, no passivity. Mood low secondary to symptoms; enjoyment, sleep and appetite preserved. No suicidal thoughts.\nPast: similar episode at 19 (contamination/harm checking), self-resolved. Mother anxious. Alcohol ~6 units/week, no drugs.\nImp: Severe OCD with poor insight (ICD-11), sexual and harm themes, checking compulsions.\nPlan: Y-BOCS today; CBT with ERP (psychology); sertraline 50 mg mane, review 2 weeks; fit note; GP letter; crisis numbers given.\nDr K. Mensah ST4","make":["Score Y-BOCS at the first visit and repeat at each review; 24 or more suggests severe OCD.","Record his insight in his words ('50:50') so you can track it with treatment.","Refer for CBT with ERP to the psychology team, marked 'severe, sexual-theme OCD'.","Write the GP letter, copied to him, in plain words that name OCD and avoid 'paedophile' as a label.","Give a fit note if needed and NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans (116 123) numbers."],"next":"Two weeks later he has started sertraline and tolerated it. He tells you his first ERP task is to send a text without rereading it. He rates his belief in the thoughts at 30% now.","marks":["Let him name the thought before using closed questions.","Asked how the thoughts came and how he saw them at first, then how true they feel now.","Found the full range of checking, body checking and avoidance, and the sick leave.","Asked plainly about sexual interest, urges, images and contact, without judgement.","Screened for psychosis, depression and suicide risk, and found the past episode.","Committed to severe OCD and explained it from his own history."],"pitfalls":["Calling the thoughts delusional because insight has slipped, without asking how they began.","Avoiding the direct question about sexual interest, or asking it in a shocked tone.","Making a safeguarding referral for thoughts alone.","Giving repeated reassurance ('of course you're not'), which works like another compulsion."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD, treatment guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11 6B20 Obsessive-compulsive disorder, insight specifiers (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline, indications and dose (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, sexual-theme OCD is common and well recognised. Doctors are expected to ask about sexual interest calmly and directly, and patients are entitled to confidentiality unless there is real risk to others.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-67","n":1267,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"📅","title":"'Is it bipolar?': premenstrual dysphoric disorder","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:30, new patient slot","where":"General adult outpatient clinic","text":"Miss Chloe Bennett, 22, a trainee accountant, was referred by her GP with 'mood swings, ?bipolar disorder'. The letter mentions low, irritable spells and some thoughts of not wanting to be alive. She has looked up bipolar disorder online and is frightened by what she read."},"task":"Take a history that maps the low spells to her menstrual cycle, exclude bipolar disorder, psychosis, emotionally unstable traits, physical causes and substances, assess risk, and diagnose premenstrual dysphoric disorder.","first60":["Name her fear early: 'Your GP mentioned bipolar disorder. That's a big word to hear. Let's find out together what's really going on.'","Ask what the spells are like before you ask about highs.","Then ask the question that unlocks it: 'How does a spell start, and how does it end?'","Have a calendar or her phone's period tracker handy: dates beat memory.","Keep two minutes at the end to name the diagnosis, explain it and agree a diary."],"ask":["'Talk me through one of these spells. How do you feel, and what changes?'","'How are you in between? How would friends describe you then?'","'How does a spell start and end? Have you noticed it links with anything?'","'Where in your cycle do they come? Does the mood lift once your period starts?'","'Were you ever on the pill or any other hormonal contraception? Did the spells change?'","'Have you ever felt the opposite of low: full of energy, needing little sleep, racing thoughts?'","'Outside that week, does your mood swing within a day? How do you feel about yourself, relationships, any self-harm?'","'When you're low, have you had thoughts of not being here? Any plans, or anything you've done?'"],"recognise":["PMDD: at least five symptoms, including one core mood symptom (lability, irritability, low mood or anxiety), in the final week before bleeding, improving within a few days of the period starting and minimal in the week after, in most cycles.","Core symptoms: sudden tearfulness or rejection sensitivity, irritability or conflict, low mood or hopelessness, tension. Others: less interest, poor concentration, fatigue, overeating or cravings, too much or too little sleep, feeling overwhelmed, bloating, breast tenderness, aches.","Bipolar disorder needs an episode of elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and reduced need for sleep, lasting days, unrelated to the cycle.","Premenstrual exacerbation: depression or anxiety present all month and worse premenstrually. That is not PMDD.","EUPD: mood shifts within hours triggered by relationships, all month, with chronic emptiness, unstable self-image, impulsivity and self-harm.","Physical mimics: thyroid disease, anaemia, PCOS (irregular cycles, acne, hirsutism); substances, including alcohol."],"decide":[{"c":"Make a provisional diagnosis of PMDD, ask her to rate symptoms daily for two cycles, write to the GP about treatment options (CBT, an SSRI, a drospirenone-containing pill), and safety-plan the suicidal thoughts.","r":"Right: the cycle link is clear, the mimics are excluded, prospective ratings confirm it, and the risk is addressed now.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose bipolar II disorder and start lamotrigine.","r":"Wrong: she has never had an elevated phase; mood swings tied to the cycle are not bipolar disorder.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her it is 'just PMS' and discharge to the GP.","r":"Unsafe and dismissive: PMDD is disabling, she has suicidal thoughts, and she needs a diagnosis, a plan and a safety net.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and attends informally; no MHA or MCA issues arise.","Write the clinic letter to her and copy it to the GP, in plain English.","If an SSRI is started at 22, review within 1 week for agitation or suicidal thoughts (NICE NG222 advises closer early review under 26)."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"This doesn't look like bipolar disorder to me. There's never been a high, and your low spells come in the week before your period and lift once it starts. That pattern fits premenstrual dysphoric disorder, PMDD."},{"to":"Patient (explaining)","s":"Most women notice some change before a period. In PMDD the brain is extra sensitive to the normal hormone shifts, so the mood change is severe enough to disrupt life. It's real, it's recognised and it's treatable."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Probable PMDD; no evidence of bipolar disorder, psychosis or EUPD. Passive suicidal thoughts premenstrually, safety plan agreed. Two-cycle symptom diary started. Please check TFTs and FBC and consider an SSRI (continuous or luteal) or a drospirenone-containing COC; CBT referral made."}],"write":"10:30 New patient. Chloe Bennett, 22, ref GP ?bipolar.\nHPC: 6 yrs of spells, worse 12 months: low, tearful, irritable, poor motivation, tired, sleeps and eats more, self-critical. Start ~7 days before menses, lift within 1-2 days of bleeding; most cycles. Well between spells. Better while on COC age 18-19. Bloating, breast tenderness, aches.\nNever elevated or needing less sleep. No hallucinations or unusual beliefs. No mood instability outside luteal phase; stable self-image and relationships; no self-harm.\nPhysical: regular 28-day cycles, no known illness. Alcohol ~6 units/week, no drugs.\nRisk: passive SI premenstrually for 6 months, no plan or acts; protective: family, boyfriend, career. Distressed by thoughts.\nImp: Probable PMDD. Bipolar disorder not supported.\nPlan: daily symptom diary (DRSP) 2 cycles; GP: TFT, FBC; CBT referral; GP to consider SSRI or drospirenone COC; safety plan given; review 8 weeks.\nDr S. Iqbal CT3","make":["Give her a daily symptom rating sheet (the Daily Record of Severity of Problems) or set up a tracking app, for two full cycles.","Ask the GP for TFT and FBC, and for an ultrasound only if cycles are irregular.","Write a short safety plan for the premenstrual week: warning signs, what helps, two contacts, Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2.","Refer for CBT, which helps premenstrual symptoms and coping.","Book review in 8 weeks with the completed diaries."],"next":"Eight weeks later her diaries show clear symptoms on days 21 to 28 and almost none from day 3 to day 14. Her GP starts sertraline 50 mg in the second half of each cycle, and she says the last premenstrual week was 'bearable for the first time in years'.","marks":["Asked what the spells were like and how she is in between before screening for mania.","Asked how a spell starts and ends, and found the premenstrual link.","Excluded elevated mood, psychosis and unstable personality traits.","Screened thyroid, PCOS, alcohol and drugs.","Explored the suicidal thoughts fully and agreed a safety plan.","Named PMDD unprompted, said clearly it is not bipolar disorder, and set up prospective ratings."],"pitfalls":["Accepting the GP's 'bipolar' label and spending the time on mania questions.","Never asking how spells begin and end, so the cycle link stays hidden.","Calling it 'just PMS' and dismissing the suicidal thoughts.","Diagnosing without planning prospective daily ratings."],"refs":["1. RCOG Green-top Guideline 48: Management of premenstrual syndrome (2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CKS: Premenstrual syndrome (checked Nov 2026)","3. American Psychiatric Association. DSM-5-TR criteria for premenstrual dysphoric disorder (2022)","4. NICE NG222: adult depression guideline, antidepressant review (2022; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK most PMDD treatment is started by the GP or gynaecology, not psychiatry. Your job is a clear diagnosis, risk management and a specific letter telling the GP what to consider.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-68","n":1268,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🌡","title":"Stable for six years, now struggling: is it the menopause?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:00, follow-up slot brought forward","where":"General adult outpatient clinic","text":"Mrs Karen Doyle, 49, a school business manager, has recurrent depressive disorder with one episode of psychotic depression in 2016. She has been well for six years on lithium (Priadel) 800 mg at night and citalopram 20 mg each morning, with normal monitoring. Her GP asks you to see her: 'a few months of irritability, tearfulness and poor concentration. Relapse?'"},"task":"Take a history of the causes of her new symptoms: relapse of depression, perimenopause, a lithium or thyroid problem, and life stresses; assess risk; and tell her the likely cause and what happens next.","first60":["Check her last lithium level, U&E, eGFR, TFT and calcium, and when they were done.","Name her fear: 'You've been well a long time, so I imagine you're worried it's coming back.'","Ask what is new before you ask what is old: when it started, what it's like, what sets it off.","Plan to ask 'What wakes you at night?' The answer often opens the menopause history.","Keep the comparison with past episodes short once she denies low mood."],"ask":["'What made you go to your GP, and when did you first notice a change?'","'When do you get irritable or tearful? Does anything set it off?'","'How is your mood underneath? Do you still enjoy things? How do you see the future?'","'Is this like your depression before, or different?'","'What's getting in the way of your sleep? What happens when you wake?'","'Any hot flushes in the day? Any vaginal dryness or discomfort during sex?'","'What have your periods been doing this last year? When was the last one?'","'Any shaking hands, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, thirst, or new tablets such as ibuprofen? Feeling cold, weight gain, dry skin?'"],"recognise":["Perimenopause: irritability, tearfulness, anxiety, poor concentration and fatigue, with hot flushes, night sweats, vaginal dryness and periods becoming irregular then stopping.","NICE NG23: in women over 45, diagnose perimenopause from symptoms and irregular periods; menopause after 12 months without a period. No FSH test needed.","Not a depressive relapse: mood not low, enjoyment kept, no negative thoughts, and she says it feels different from before.","Lithium toxicity: coarse tremor, diarrhoea, vomiting, ataxia, slurred speech, confusion. Sweating, poor fluid intake and NSAIDs all raise levels.","Lithium-induced hypothyroidism can mimic low mood and fatigue; hypercalcaemia from hyperparathyroidism causes irritability, tiredness and poor concentration.","Night sweats without panic, or a racing heart, point to vasomotor symptoms rather than anxiety attacks."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell her the likely cause is perimenopause, not relapse; check lithium level, U&E, TFT and calcium now; keep her medication; ask the GP to discuss HRT and vaginal oestrogen; agree relapse warning signs.","r":"Right: the pattern fits perimenopause, the blood tests exclude the lithium and thyroid mimics, and stability is protected.","best":true},{"c":"Increase citalopram to 40 mg for a depressive relapse.","r":"Wrong: she has no depressive episode; this treats the wrong problem and adds side effects without addressing the cause.","best":false},{"c":"Request FSH and wait for the result before saying anything.","r":"Unnecessary: at 49 with vasomotor symptoms and changing periods, NICE advises diagnosis without FSH; delay leaves her worried.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and attends informally; no MHA or MCA issue arises.","Driving: she must not drive if she feels unsafe through poor concentration or tiredness. Recurrent depression that is stable does not need DVLA notification for a car licence.","Write to her and copy the GP; HRT is usually prescribed in primary care."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I don't think your depression is coming back. Your mood is fine underneath, you still enjoy things, and this feels different to you. The night sweats, flushes and your periods stopping point to the perimenopause."},{"to":"Patient (lithium)","s":"Sweating a lot can push lithium levels up, so I'd like to check your level, kidneys, thyroid and calcium today. Please don't stop your tablets."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Likely perimenopause, no depressive relapse. Lithium level, U&E, eGFR, TFT and calcium requested. Please discuss HRT and vaginal oestrogen; no important interaction with her lithium or citalopram. Avoid NSAIDs. Contraception still needed."}],"write":"15:00 Review. Karen Doyle, 49. Recurrent depressive disorder (psychotic episode 2016). Lithium 800 mg nocte, citalopram 20 mg mane; well 6 years.\nHPC: 4 months irritable, tearful, anxious, poor concentration, tired. Trigger unclear; daughter left for university in Sept; some strain with husband.\nMood not low, enjoyment kept, no negative cognitions; she says unlike past episodes.\nSleep broken by night sweats, no panic. Daytime flushes. Vaginal dryness, dyspareunia. Periods lighter, irregular, none for 3 months.\nNo tremor, D&V, ataxia or thirst. Occasional ibuprofen for headaches. No cold intolerance or weight gain.\nRisk: no thoughts of self-harm or harm to others; no psychotic symptoms. Concentration affecting work; stopped motorway driving.\nImp: Likely perimenopausal symptoms. No evidence of depressive relapse.\nPlan: lithium level (12 h post dose), U&E, eGFR, TFT, Ca today. Stop ibuprofen, use paracetamol. Continue lithium and citalopram. GP: HRT and vaginal oestrogen discussion, contraception advice. Relapse signs agreed. Review 6 weeks.\nDr T. Adeyemi ST5","make":["Lithium level 12 hours after the night dose, plus U&E, eGFR, TFT and calcium, today.","Ask about over-the-counter NSAIDs and advise paracetamol instead.","Write down her relapse signature from ten years ago: early waking, guilt, withdrawal, odd beliefs.","Letter to the GP, copied to her, naming likely perimenopause and the treatment options to discuss.","Review in 6 weeks with blood results."],"next":"Her lithium level is 0.9 mmol/L, a little higher than her usual 0.7, and TFT and calcium are normal. She has stopped ibuprofen and started HRT with her GP. At six weeks the sweats have eased, she sleeps through and says she feels like herself again.","marks":["Asked about time course, triggers and life stresses.","Screened mood, enjoyment and negative thoughts, compared once with past episodes, then moved on.","Found night sweats, flushes, vaginal symptoms and the change in periods.","Checked for lithium toxicity, NSAIDs and thyroid symptoms.","Assessed risk, including work and driving.","Named likely perimenopause with reasons and set out the bloods and next steps."],"pitfalls":["Fixating on the lithium and the old psychotic episode, and using the whole consultation to look for relapse.","Never asking what wakes her, so the menopause history stays hidden.","Forgetting that sweating and NSAIDs can push lithium up.","Ending with 'I'll ask my consultant' instead of giving a likely cause."],"refs":["1. NICE NG23 Menopause: identification and management (2015, updated 2024; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: lithium monitoring recommendations (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: lithium carbonate, monitoring and interactions (checked Nov 2026)","4. FSRH Clinical Guideline: Contraception for women aged over 40 years (2017, amended 2023; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK women often see the GP for HRT, and many will not mention flushes or painful sex to a psychiatrist unless asked. Ask plainly and kindly; it is routine here.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-69","n":1269,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🛌","title":"Unresponsive with normal tests: explaining dissociative stupor to her mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 11:00, liaison referral","where":"Relatives' room, acute medical unit, district general hospital","text":"Sophie Ward, 18, a college student, collapsed in the street yesterday afternoon. Since then she has lain still with her eyes closed, not speaking, eating or drinking. CT and MRI head, EEG, bloods and a urine drug screen are normal, and the medical team has started IV fluids. They ask liaison psychiatry to see her mother, Mrs Helen Ward, 45, who has been at the bedside all night."},"task":"Take a brief collateral history from her mother about the collapse, the tests, Sophie's background and any recent stress, then explain the likely diagnosis of dissociative stupor, answer her questions honestly, and set out the plan.","first60":["Read the medical notes: obs, NEWS2, fluid balance, the tests and what the neurologist or medical registrar thinks.","See Sophie first if you can: eyes, resistance to eye opening, tone, posture, response to voice; note any catatonic signs.","Introduce yourself to her mother and acknowledge the night she has had.","Explain that Sophie cannot give consent, so you will share what her mother needs to support her care.","Plan about three minutes of history, then the explanation and her questions."],"ask":["'Who was with Sophie when she collapsed, and what did they see?'","'Any shaking, jerking, wetting herself, tongue biting or injury?'","'Since then, has she moved, spoken, opened her eyes, eaten or drunk anything?'","'What tests has she had, and what did the doctors tell you?'","'Any illness, medicines, alcohol or drugs, as far as you know?'","'Has anything been worrying Sophie lately? How did she react?'","'How close has she been to her uncle over the years?'","'Has she ever had problems with her mood or nerves, harmed herself, or had anything like this before? Anyone in the family?'"],"recognise":["Dissociative stupor: profound reduction or absence of movement, speech and response to light, noise and touch, with normal muscle tone and breathing, nothing physical to explain it, and a recent stress that clearly fits.","Clues: eyes resist opening, gaze turns away from a bright light, a dropped arm avoids the face, and tests are normal.","Catatonia overlaps: look for posturing, waxy flexibility, mutism, negativism, staring; rate it with the Bush-Francis scale and discuss a lorazepam challenge with your consultant.","Depressive stupor: preceding weeks of severe depression with weight loss and early waking.","Organic causes still to keep in mind if anything changes: encephalitis (including autoimmune, in young women), non-convulsive status, metabolic or drug causes.","A disclosure of abuse is possible later, but must never be assumed or probed for while she is unwell."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain dissociative stupor plainly, link it to the recent shock, answer the abuse question honestly, keep her on the medical unit for fluids and nutrition with daily liaison review, and rule out catatonia with your consultant.","r":"Right: it is honest, it keeps her physically safe, and it guides the family's support.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her mother it is 'psychological' and that Sophie will snap out of it if left alone.","r":"Wrong: it implies Sophie is choosing this, misleads the family, and ignores her physical risk without fluids or food.","best":false},{"c":"Transfer Sophie to the psychiatric ward today because the tests are normal.","r":"Unsafe: she is not eating or drinking and needs IV fluids, possibly NG feeding, and nursing care that a mental health ward cannot provide.","best":false}],"law":["Sophie, 18, cannot communicate a decision, so she lacks capacity for now (MCA 2005). Fluids, NG feeding and nursing care are given in her best interests, consulting her mother.","Share with her mother what she needs to know to support Sophie's care; keep detail to what is necessary (GMC confidentiality).","If Sophie later discloses abuse, follow adult safeguarding procedures, and consider any risk to other children, including her cousins.","The MHA 1983 is not needed while she is on the medical ward and treated under the MCA; review this if she later needs psychiatric admission and objects."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"All the tests show Sophie's brain and body are healthy. I think she has a dissociative stupor: after a big shock, the mind can shut down movement and response for a while. It's real, it isn't put on, and most people recover."},{"to":"Mother (the abuse question)","s":"I can't tell you that, and I won't pretend to. It's possible, and it's just as possible that the shock of the news alone did this. Please don't question her. Once she's recovered, we'll give her the chance to talk, in her own time."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Likely dissociative stupor after a major family stress a week ago; tests normal. Please continue IV fluids and NEWS2, consider NG feeding if no intake by Monday, pressure care and VTE assessment. We'll see her daily and screen for catatonia with our consultant."}],"write":"11:00 Liaison review. Sophie Ward, 18F, AMU bay 4. Seen with mother Helen (45).\nCollapse 16:00 yesterday walking home with friends, mid-conversation, onto grass. No jerking, incontinence, tongue bite or injury reported. Since: eyes closed, mute, immobile, no oral intake.\nInvestigations: CT, MRI head, EEG, FBC, U&E, LFT, CRP, glucose, TFT, UDS normal (per notes).\nBackground: well, no past psychiatric history, no self-harm, no substances. No FH.\nStressor: maternal uncle arrested 8 days ago re indecent images of children; police search; he has left his home (daughters 12 and 15). Sophie withdrawn, missing college since. Close to uncle in childhood. No known disclosure.\nO/E: eyes resist opening, normal tone, no posturing or waxy flexibility. BFCRS 3 (mutism, immobility, staring).\nImp: Dissociative stupor, likely. Differentials: catatonia, depressive stupor; organic causes excluded so far.\nPlan: remain on AMU, IV fluids, NEWS2, pressure care; NG feed under MCA best interests if no intake by Monday. Liaison daily. Discuss lorazepam challenge with consultant. Mother updated; asked not to question Sophie about uncle. Safeguarding if any disclosure.\nDr R. Patel CT2 Liaison bleep 6210","make":["Check her obs, NEWS2, fluid balance chart and urine output.","Look for catatonic signs and score the Bush-Francis Catatonia Rating Scale.","Agree with the medical team: IV fluids, pressure area care, VTE assessment, and a date to decide on NG feeding.","Record her capacity and the best-interests basis for treatment.","Give her mother the ward and liaison contact details and the next update time."],"next":"Two days later, after calm daily visits from her mother and a lorazepam challenge that made no difference, Sophie opens her eyes and sips water. Over the week she starts talking. She is referred for psychological therapy, and the liaison team arranges a first session before discharge.","marks":["Clarified the collapse, any seizure features and how she has been since.","Confirmed which tests were done and that all were normal.","Found the uncle's arrest, how Sophie reacted, and how close they were.","Explained dissociative stupor in plain words, including what normal tests mean.","Answered the abuse question directly, without assuming either way.","Gave the plan: medical care, daily review, likely recovery, and how her mother can help."],"pitfalls":["Dodging 'Did he do something to her?'","Saying 'it's psychological' in a way that sounds like she is pretending.","Sending her to a psychiatric ward while she needs IV fluids or feeding.","Forgetting catatonia, which can look the same and responds to lorazepam."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-10 F44.2 Dissociative stupor; ICD-11 dissociative disorders chapter (checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (2007; checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Liaison psychiatry and functional neurological disorder resources (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK liaison psychiatry you give clear, honest explanations to families, including saying 'I don't know' where that is true. Avoid words like 'hysterical' or 'attention-seeking'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-01","n":1301,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🫀","title":"Low after a heart attack: presenting the case to a medical student","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:15, liaison psychiatry","where":"Cardiology ward, general hospital","text":"You have just assessed Graham Whitfield, a 63-year-old estate agent, three weeks after an NSTEMI. The cardiologists say his heart is recovering well, but he is flat, eating little and has stopped turning up to cardiac rehab. Priya, a final-year medical student, is shadowing the liaison team this week. Your consultant asks you to present Mr Whitfield to her, talk through the diagnosis and plan, and answer her questions."},"task":"Present the case clearly to a medical student, give the likely diagnosis with sensible differentials, explain why it matters after a heart attack, and set out a specific short- and long-term biopsychosocial plan, answering her questions.","first60":["Have your notes in front of you, but plan to tell the story, not read the clerking.","Fix the structure before you start: who he is, the symptoms, background and risk, diagnosis, why it matters, plan, then questions.","Check the drug chart once more: aspirin plus clopidogrel and bisoprolol shape every drug choice you will mention.","Look up today's bloods and ECG (sodium, TFTs if sent, QTc) so you can answer 'what else would you check?'","Find a quiet corner off the ward: no patient names spoken in the corridor."],"ask":["'Before I start, what have you seen of liaison psychiatry so far?'","'Would you like the whole story first, or shall I stop after each part?'","'From what I've said, what do you think is going on?'","'Who else would you want to speak to before you were sure?'","'Which symptoms here could be caused by the heart attack itself, and which point to depression?'","'Which antidepressant would you pick for him, and why that one?'","'What would you say the plan should be in the longer term, after he goes home?'","'What would you take away from this case?'"],"recognise":["Two weeks or more of low mood and loss of enjoyment, with early waking, poor appetite, weight loss, low energy and guilt: a depressive episode, not just 'understandable sadness'.","Excessive self-blame ('it's all my fault') and guilt about his wife travelling are cognitive symptoms; tiredness and poor appetite alone could be physical.","Early waking at 4 a.m. may partly be ward noise: ask what happens on quiet nights, and ask the nurses.","No suicidal thoughts today, but he has several risk factors: older man, new serious illness, heavy drinking, fear of losing his job.","Three weeks without alcohol after 3-5 pints a night: withdrawal is over, but heavy drinking can itself cause low mood and will return at discharge.","Depression after a heart attack is common and is linked with poorer rehab uptake and worse cardiac outcomes, so it is worth treating actively."],"decide":[{"c":"Present a moderate depressive episode after NSTEMI, name the differentials, get collateral from his wife and the nurses, and outline sertraline with gastroprotection, CBT-based therapy, rehab and alcohol and smoking support.","r":"Right: clear diagnosis, safe drug choice for his heart and antiplatelets, and a plan that covers mind, body and life now and after discharge.","best":true},{"c":"Tell Priya it is a normal reaction to a heart attack that will settle once he goes home.","r":"Wrong: he has a full set of depressive symptoms for three weeks and is missing rehab. Waiting risks his recovery and his mood.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest amitriptyline at night to help both sleep and mood.","r":"Unsafe after a recent myocardial infarction: tricyclics can cause arrhythmias and are dangerous in overdose. An SSRI such as sertraline is the usual choice.","best":false}],"law":["He has agreed to the assessment; share teaching details only with the team, and keep identifying details out of shared spaces.","Collateral from his wife needs his agreement: ask him first, and record it.","He has capacity for treatment decisions; any antidepressant is his choice after a balanced explanation.","GMC: doctors have a duty to help teach students, and patients may decline student involvement in their care."],"say":[{"to":"Medical student","s":"Mr Whitfield is 63, three weeks after an NSTEMI. His heart is doing well, but since the infarct he's been low, has lost all enjoyment, wakes at 4 and blames himself. I think this is a moderate depressive episode."},{"to":"Medical student","s":"I'd choose sertraline because it has the best safety evidence after a heart attack. But he's on aspirin and clopidogrel, so I'd add lansoprazole to protect his stomach and check his sodium."},{"to":"Cardiology team","s":"We've seen Mr Whitfield. He has a moderate depressive episode. We suggest sertraline 50 mg in the morning with lansoprazole, low-intensity CBT on the ward, and we'll review him again on Friday."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 63M, D21 post NSTEMI, cardiology ward. Referred: low mood, not engaging with cardiac rehab.\nSince MI: persistent low mood, anhedonia (misses guitar), EMW 4 a.m., poor appetite (eats when prompted), subjective weight loss, low energy, guilt about wife's travel, self-blame for MI, fears losing job as estate agent.\nPMH: HTN, T2DM. Drugs: amlodipine, metformin, aspirin, clopidogrel, atorvastatin, bisoprolol.\nAlcohol: 3-5 pints nightly before admission, none since; smokes 30/day.\nRisk: denies suicidal thoughts or plans; no harm to others. Risk factors: male, older, new illness, alcohol, job worry.\nImpression: moderate depressive episode. Ddx: adjustment disorder, hypoactive delirium (unlikely), hypothyroidism, alcohol-related low mood.\nPlan: collateral from wife (with consent) and nurses; TFTs, B12, folate, LFTs, GGT, U&E, ECG/QTc; sertraline 50 mg mane with lansoprazole 15 mg; behavioural activation on ward; refer Talking Therapies (long-term conditions); alcohol liaison nurse and stop smoking service; wife to bring guitar; review Fri.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Tell a story, not a list: age, the event, what changed, what worries you, in under a minute.","Name the diagnosis early, then justify it with the core symptoms you heard.","Give two or three differentials with the one fact that argues against each.","Be specific about drugs: name, dose, why that one, and the interaction you would guard against.","Cover short and long term, and biological, psychological and social, without being prompted.","Check her understanding with a question, not 'any questions?'"],"next":"Mr Whitfield starts sertraline and lansoprazole. His wife brings in his guitar, and the rehab nurses set him one small walking target a day. By discharge two weeks later he is eating better and has booked his first outpatient rehab class. Priya presents her own liaison case on Friday using the same structure.","marks":["Presented a clear, concise summary with the key positives and negatives.","Named a moderate depressive episode and gave reasoned differentials.","Identified sources of collateral: wife, ward nurses, GP and medical notes.","Chose sertraline and explained cardiac safety and the bleeding interaction with antiplatelets.","Gave a short- and long-term biopsychosocial plan, including therapy type, alcohol and smoking.","Taught interactively and checked the student's understanding."],"pitfalls":["Reading the clerking word for word instead of summarising.","Calling it 'normal sadness' and offering no treatment.","Suggesting citalopram at high dose or a tricyclic without mentioning cardiac risk.","Forgetting the bleeding risk of an SSRI with aspirin and clopidogrel.","Leaving out alcohol, smoking and his fears about work."],"refs":["1. NICE CG91: depression with a chronic physical health problem (2009; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline, interactions with antiplatelets (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clopidogrel and proton pump inhibitors (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK teaching hospitals you will often present cases to students. Summarise in under a minute, state the diagnosis, then the plan. Students are taught to ask 'which drug and why?', so have a reason ready.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-02","n":1302,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Starting clozapine: explaining the plan to Daniel's mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 16:30, ward doctor","where":"Acute male admissions ward, family room","text":"Daniel Mensah, 25, with paranoid schizophrenia, is eight weeks into his second admission and still unwell: he believes ministers read his messages and tamper with his bank account, hears other patients commenting on him, and is often verbally aggressive. Olanzapine and now haloperidol 10 mg twice daily have not helped. This morning's ward round decided to start clozapine. His mother, Abena, has asked to see you. Daniel has agreed you can share his treatment details."},"task":"Explain to Daniel's mother why clozapine is being started, how it is started and monitored, its common and serious side effects, and what she can do to help, and answer her questions.","first60":["Check Daniel's written or recorded consent to share information with his mother, and its limits.","Read the drug chart: haloperidol 10 mg twice daily, clonazepam 1 mg four times daily, promethazine 25 mg twice daily; note what will change.","Check his legal status and whether he smokes, and whether baseline bloods, ECG and monitoring-service registration are done.","Have a clozapine leaflet for carers and the ward phone number ready to give her.","Plan to listen first: her frustration ('nobody tells me anything') needs naming before any facts."],"ask":["'What do you understand about Daniel's illness and his treatment so far?'","'How does he seem to you when you visit?'","'What worries you most about this new medicine?'","'Have you heard anything about clozapine already, from the internet or others?'","'Does Daniel smoke? Has that changed recently?'","'What would help you most in supporting him?'","'Can you tell me back the warning signs that would mean calling us straight away?'","'What other questions do you have?'"],"recognise":["Treatment-resistant schizophrenia: no adequate response to at least two different antipsychotics at proper dose and duration. Daniel has had three.","Clozapine is the only antipsychotic with proven extra benefit in treatment resistance; NICE says offer it after two have failed.","Her fears ('last resort', 'kill your blood', 'zombie') are common; acknowledge them, then give accurate, balanced facts.","Serious risks (low neutrophils, myocarditis, severe constipation, seizures) are rare but need monitoring; common effects (drooling, sleepiness, weight gain) are manageable.","Carers notice fever, sore throat and constipation early: give her a clear role.","No long-acting injection exists for clozapine, so adherence to tablets is key."],"decide":[{"c":"Validate her frustration, explain treatment resistance and why clozapine now, then cover titration, blood and heart monitoring, side effects, constipation and infection warning signs, smoking, and her role, with a leaflet and follow-up meeting.","r":"Right: she gets honest, balanced information, a part to play in his safety, and a reason to support the plan.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her that clozapine is very safe and the blood tests are just routine.","r":"Misleading: serious side effects are rare but can be fatal. Carers need to know the warning signs, especially infection and constipation.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her that, as the ward round has decided, there is nothing to discuss.","r":"Wrong: Daniel has agreed to share information, she is a key carer, and her support improves adherence and safety.","best":false}],"law":["Daniel has consented to sharing; stay within what he agreed. Without consent you could still listen to her and give general information.","If he is detained and past three months of medication, clozapine needs his capacitous consent (Form T2) or a SOAD certificate (Form T3).","Clozapine needs registration with a clozapine monitoring service and a valid blood result before each supply.","Under the Care Act 2014 she can ask the local authority to assess her own needs as a carer."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"I'm sorry it's felt as if no one tells you anything. You've watched Daniel struggle for eight weeks, and you deserve a clear picture. Can I start with what you already know?"},{"to":"Mother","s":"Clozapine isn't old-fashioned or a punishment. It's the one medicine that works best when others haven't, and that's why we're offering it now."},{"to":"Mother","s":"If he has a fever, sore throat or flu-like symptoms, chest pain or breathlessness, or hasn't opened his bowels for three days, ring the ward straight away."}],"write":"Met Mrs Abena Mensah (mother), Daniel's consent to share confirmed (recorded in notes 07/11).\nHer understanding: knows three medicines tried; worried about 'last resort' and blood dangers; frustrated at lack of updates.\nExplained: treatment-resistant schizophrenia; rationale for clozapine; cross-titration from haloperidol (clozapine 12.5 mg, rising over 2-3 weeks); monitoring service registration; FBC weekly 18 wks, fortnightly to 1 yr, then monthly; ECG, troponin/CRP, obs during titration; common SEs (drooling, constipation, sedation, weight, fast pulse); serious SEs (neutropenia, myocarditis, bowel obstruction, seizures); warning signs; smoking effect on levels; no depot form; aim to reduce other medicines.\nTeach-back: correct for warning signs. Leaflet given. Carer's assessment referral made.\nPlan: joint meeting with Daniel, named nurse and pharmacist next week.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Name her frustration and fear before giving any facts.","Explain 'treatment-resistant' in one sentence, then why clozapine is the logical next step.","Separate common, manageable side effects from rare, serious ones; give the serious ones with their warning signs.","Give the blood test schedule exactly: weekly, then fortnightly, then monthly.","Give her a role: warning signs, bowels, smoking, and being part of his care.","Pause every minute or so to check she's following, and leave room for her questions."],"next":"Mrs Mensah takes the leaflet home and comes to a joint meeting with Daniel and the pharmacist the next week. Clozapine reaches 300 mg by week three with a laxative from day one. By week ten Daniel is calmer and stops shouting at visitors; he still mentions ministers but no longer acts on it. She texts the ward when he has a sore throat in week six; his neutrophils are normal.","marks":["Confirmed consent to share and acknowledged her frustration.","Established her understanding and worries early.","Explained treatment resistance and why clozapine now.","Described starting, cross-titration and the full monitoring schedule.","Covered common and serious side effects, including constipation and infection.","Gave her a carer's role and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Talking only about blood tests and forgetting constipation, heart checks and infection signs.","Saying it is 'completely safe' or, the other way, frightening her with a list of fatal effects.","Promising it will work within weeks.","Forgetting that smoking changes clozapine levels.","Dodging 'why not before?' instead of explaining the two-antipsychotic rule."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 (psychosis and schizophrenia in adults, 2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: clozapine, monitoring and dosing (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and gastrointestinal hypomotility (2020; checked Nov 2026)","4. Rethink Mental Illness: clozapine information for carers (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, clozapine cannot be supplied without registration with a monitoring service and a valid blood result. Carers expect to be involved when the patient agrees, and NICE expects you to offer them information and support.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-03","n":1303,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🎖️","title":"Afghanistan memories that won't settle: a diagnostic history for PTSD","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team, assessment room","text":"Major Owen Fairbairn, a 45-year-old British Army officer, has been referred by his GP. The letter says he is struggling with events from an operational tour of Afghanistan some years ago, sleeping badly and drinking more. He has agreed to come, but in the waiting room he told the receptionist he was 'not a head case'. He sits upright, scanning the door."},"task":"Take a history to reach a diagnosis: outline the trauma without forcing detail, cover re-experiencing, avoidance, numbing, hyperarousal, comorbidity including alcohol, and risk including firearms, then explain your conclusion.","first60":["Read the GP letter for the year of the tour, any physical injury, current drinking and medication.","Choose a seat with a clear view of the door for him, and keep the room quiet: no sudden noises.","Plan to give him control from the start: he decides how much detail to share about the event.","Have the PTSD clusters in your head: re-experiencing, avoidance, sense of threat, plus numbing, guilt and function.","Know the routes for serving personnel: his military GP and the Department of Community Mental Health, as well as the NHS."],"ask":["'Share only what feels OK. In rough terms, what happened?'","'Do memories of it come back when you don't want them to? Does it feel as if it's happening again, now?'","'What do your nightmares tend to be about?'","'Are there places, sounds or things you avoid because they bring it back?'","'Do you feel cut off from people, even those close to you?'","'How do you react to sudden noises? Do you find yourself on guard, checking exits?'","'How much are you drinking now, and what does it do for you?'","'Has it ever got so bad you've thought of ending it? Do you have access to any weapons, at home or at work?'"],"recognise":["Exposure to a threatening, catastrophic event: an explosion in which two of his soldiers were killed.","Re-experiencing in the present: nightmares and flashbacks that feel as if it is happening now, with racing heart and sweating, not just remembering.","Avoidance of reminders: fireworks, news, vehicles, talk of the tour; patchy memory of the hours after the blast.","Ongoing sense of threat: jumpiness, hypervigilance, poor sleep, irritability and poor concentration.","Emotional numbing, detachment from his wife, and guilt ('I should have chosen another route') are common and worsen outcome.","Heavy alcohol use to sleep and numb feelings is the commonest comorbidity; screen for depression and for head injury from the blast."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history, diagnose PTSD with harmful alcohol use, check firearms and suicide risk, explain that trauma-focused CBT works, and refer with his consent to the right service with a GP letter.","r":"Right: a clear diagnosis, safety checked, and a treatment route he can accept, offered with hope.","best":true},{"c":"Ask him to describe the explosion in full detail so you can judge how severe it was.","r":"Harmful: forcing detail in a first meeting can overwhelm him and stop him returning. A brief outline is enough for the diagnosis.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe diazepam at night to help him sleep and stop drinking.","r":"Wrong: NICE advises against benzodiazepines in PTSD, and combining them with heavy drinking is dangerous. Trauma-focused therapy comes first.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: serving personnel often fear their chain of command will be told. Explain that information is shared with his employer only with his consent, unless there is a serious risk to him or others.","Firearms: if he holds a civilian firearm or shotgun certificate and there is a serious risk, GMC guidance allows disclosure to the police; discuss it with him first where safe.","Driving: if he drinks heavily or has severe symptoms affecting driving, advise him of his duty to tell the DVLA.","Serving personnel are normally treated through Defence Medical Services; with consent, liaise with his military GP and Department of Community Mental Health."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You don't have to go into detail about what happened. Just a rough idea is enough for me, and you can stop whenever you want."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you've described is called post-traumatic stress disorder. It's a normal brain's reaction to an abnormal event, and it's very treatable."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: PTSD after combat trauma, with harmful alcohol use. No current suicidal intent; no access to firearms. Referred for trauma-focused CBT; alcohol plan agreed. Please avoid benzodiazepines."}],"write":"CMHT assessment. 45M, serving Army officer (Major). GP referral.\nTrauma: roadside explosion on operational tour in Afghanistan; two of his soldiers killed; he was in the following vehicle; patchy memory of the next few hours. Did not wish to give more detail; not pressed.\nRe-experiencing: nightmares of the convoy most nights; daytime flashbacks with palpitations, sweating; intrusive images of the scene.\nAvoidance: fireworks, news of conflict, being driven, talking about the tour.\nNumbing: feels cut off from wife and children; lost interest in running.\nArousal: severe startle, hypervigilance (sits facing doors), initial insomnia till ~2 a.m., irritability, poor concentration. No violence.\nGuilt: blames himself for the route.\nComorbidity: whisky ~half a bottle most nights (~100+ units/wk) to sleep; morning shakes denied; no drugs. Low mood 'numb' rather than sad; enjoys little. No head injury or LOC at blast.\nRisk: fleeting thoughts that family would be better off, no plans or intent; protective: wife, children, career. No weapons at home; no firearm certificate; on leave, no access to service weapons. No harm to others.\nImpression: PTSD (ICD-11), harmful alcohol use; screen positive for depressive symptoms, not a full episode.\nPlan: explained diagnosis; referral for trauma-focused CBT (via DCMH, with consent); AUDIT, LFTs, GGT; alcohol reduction plan; crisis numbers given; GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Give control early: 'only as much as you're comfortable with' and mean it.","Ask about flashbacks as reliving in the present, not just remembering: that is the core feature.","Cover every cluster with one clear question each, then ask for an example.","Ask about alcohol plainly and without judgement: it is how many cope, and it changes the plan.","Ask directly about weapons, at home and at work: serving personnel have unusual access.","End with hope: name the condition and say it responds to specific therapy."],"next":"Owen agrees to referral to his Department of Community Mental Health for trauma-focused CBT and to cut his drinking with his GP's help. After ten sessions his nightmares fall to once a week and he drives to the supermarket again. His wife says he laughs at the children's jokes for the first time in years.","marks":["Approached the trauma sensitively and gave him control over detail.","Elicited re-experiencing, including flashbacks in the present and nightmares.","Elicited avoidance, numbing and memory gaps.","Elicited hyperarousal: startle, hypervigilance, sleep, irritability, concentration.","Screened for depression, alcohol and drugs, guilt and head injury.","Assessed suicide risk, access to firearms, harm to others, and function."],"pitfalls":["Pushing for a detailed account of the explosion.","Asking only 'any flashbacks?' without checking it feels like the present.","Forgetting to ask about alcohol, or asking in a judging way.","Not asking about weapons in a serving soldier.","Ending without explaining that treatment works."],"refs":["1. NICE NG116 (post-traumatic stress disorder, 2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: post-traumatic stress disorder, 6B40 (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Op COURAGE veterans mental health service (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC: confidentiality, disclosures about firearms (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK armed forces personnel are usually treated by Defence Medical Services while serving, and by the NHS (including Op COURAGE) after they leave. Many fear a mental health diagnosis will end their career: be clear about confidentiality from the start.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-04","n":1304,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚉","title":"Found at the platform edge at night: suicide risk assessment in A&E","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 06:20, on call","where":"Emergency department, liaison psychiatry room","text":"Lukas Petrov, a 30-year-old man, arrived with British Transport Police just before midnight after a rail worker became worried about how he was standing near the edge of a crowded platform. He agreed to come. The ED team say he is medically fit, his breath alcohol is now zero, and he has slept a few hours. The nurse says he keeps asking when he can go."},"task":"Assess suicide risk: what happened last night, intent and planning, current intent, the lead-up and mood, background risk factors and protective factors. Formulate the risk and agree a safe plan with him.","first60":["Read the ED notes and the BTP handover: what he did, what he said, anything found on him.","Confirm his legal status: he came voluntarily, so he is informal. Agree with the nurse what happens if he tries to leave before you finish.","Check he is medically fit and sober now: breath alcohol, any injuries, observations.","Find a private room, with a nurse aware and a clear exit for both of you.","Hold the order in your head: last night, now, lead-up, background, protective, then plan."],"ask":["'Can you take me back to yesterday evening? What was happening?'","'When did you decide to head for the railway? What did you do before you left?'","'What did you think would happen? Did you expect to die?'","'How do you feel now about still being here?'","'Are you still having thoughts about ending your life? Any plan?'","'How much had you drunk? How much do you drink most days?'","'Have you ever hurt yourself or tried to end your life before?'","'Who or what keeps you going, even a little?'"],"recognise":["A highly lethal chosen method (an express train), a note written beforehand and a deliberate wait: this was a serious attempt, interrupted by chance.","Ambivalence now ('not sure I'm glad') with ongoing hopelessness means current risk remains high.","Recent losses (job and partner), living alone, no local friends, male, heavy alcohol use: several dynamic risk factors at once.","Low mood for weeks, poor sleep and appetite and hopelessness suggest a depressive episode; alcohol may be driving or deepening it.","Impulsivity and alcohol together lower the barrier to acting; a past history of cutting is relevant.","Protective factors are thin but real: a supportive sister in Leeds, his help-seeking now, and his freezing when spoken to."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the full assessment, formulate risk as high and rising with alcohol and isolation, and agree informal admission tonight, contacting his sister with consent; if he refuses and risk remains, arrange a Mental Health Act assessment.","r":"Right: a lethal, planned attempt with ongoing hopelessness and no safe person nearby needs a high level of support, preferably with his agreement.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge him home with crisis numbers, because he says he won't do it again.","r":"Unsafe: he is unsure he is glad to be alive, lives alone, drinks heavily and had a lethal plan. A promise is not a plan.","best":false},{"c":"Detain him under Section 5(2) to stop him leaving A&E.","r":"Wrong: Section 5(2) applies only to admitted inpatients, not ED patients. Work with him first; if needed, arrange a full MHA assessment.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal. If he tries to leave mid-assessment, assess his capacity for that decision; act in his best interests using the MCA if he lacks it, and call security and seniors.","Section 5(2) cannot be used in the emergency department, because he is not an admitted inpatient.","If he refuses admission and risk remains high, request a Mental Health Act assessment by an AMHP and two doctors.","Confidentiality: you may contact his sister with consent; without consent, share only if needed to prevent serious harm, and record why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for talking with me after such a hard night. I'm not here to judge you. I want to understand how things got so bad."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm worried about your safety, Lukas. You still don't feel glad to be alive, you're on your own at home, and the drink makes it worse. I think a few days on the ward would keep you safe."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Lukas has agreed informal admission. Until a bed is ready, please keep him in eyesight, and call me straight away if he tries to leave."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, ED. 30M. Brought by BTP 23:50 (voluntary) after rail worker concerned at platform edge. Medically fit; BrAC 0 at 05:45.\nEvent: decided to end life after ~2 weeks of planning thoughts; wrote note on phone to sister and ex-partner; drank ~half bottle vodka; waited for non-stopping express at platform end; froze when rail worker spoke to him; did not try to resist. Expected to die.\nNow: 'not sure' glad to be alive; ongoing hopelessness; no specific new plan; would not promise safety if home alone.\nLead-up: redundant 5 wks ago; partner left 2 wks ago. Low mood ~6 wks, poor sleep, appetite and concentration, hopeless; no psychotic symptoms; no manic symptoms.\nBackground: cut arms once aged 24 after a breakup; no previous attempts; no FH of suicide. Alcohol daily since job loss, ~8 cans/night; no withdrawal so far. Occasional cannabis. Impulsive by own account.\nSocial: lives alone in rented flat; no local friends; sister Elena in Leeds, supportive. No children. No thoughts of harm to others.\nProtective: sister, his response to the rail worker, willingness to accept help.\nFormulation: high and current risk of suicide: lethal planned attempt, ongoing hopelessness, recent losses, isolation, alcohol, impulsivity; likely depressive episode, alcohol contributing; ddx acute stress reaction.\nPlan: informal admission agreed; eyesight obs in ED until bed; sister called with consent; CIWA-Ar and thiamine; reassess mood when sober for days; GP informed.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 on call","make":["Ask about the attempt step by step, like a film: before, during, after.","Ask specifically about the note, planning and expected outcome: these show intent.","Ask 'how do you feel about still being here?' and listen for ambivalence.","Ask about alcohol twice: last night, and every day.","Name protective factors with him; they become the safety plan.","Say the risk out loud in a formulation, not just a word: why high, why now, what would change it."],"next":"Lukas agrees to informal admission. His sister drives down the next morning and stays with him for the first visit. On the ward he starts thiamine and alcohol withdrawal monitoring; a week later, sober, he still has a depressive episode and starts sertraline. He is discharged to the home treatment team after twelve days, staying with his sister in Leeds.","marks":["Explored the attempt: planning, note, alcohol, method, expected outcome and intervention.","Assessed current intent, feelings about surviving and access to means.","Explored the lead-up: losses, mood, hopelessness, and a psychosis screen.","Covered past self-harm, family history, substances and impulsivity.","Identified protective factors and support.","Formulated the risk and agreed a safe plan, with legal options if he refuses."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I won't do it again' as the end of the assessment.","Forgetting to ask about the note, final acts or what he expected to happen.","Missing the alcohol, or not asking about daily drinking and withdrawal.","Using a risk score in place of a formulation.","Trying to hold him under Section 5(2) in A&E."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225 (self-harm, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters on holding powers (checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists: self-harm and suicide in adults, CR229 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Samaritans: 116 123, free, 24 hours (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK guidance (NICE NG225) says not to use risk scales or tools to predict suicide or decide discharge. Write a narrative formulation: what drives the risk, what protects, and what would change it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-05","n":1305,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🔁","title":"Three antidepressants and still low: is this treatment-resistant depression?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"Clarissa Oduya, a 45-year-old woman, has been treated by her GP for a depressive episode for eight months. Fluoxetine, then citalopram, each for three months, and now mirtazapine for two months, have not helped, and she stopped a course of talking therapy part way through. The GP asks: 'Is this treatment-resistant? What next?' She sits down slowly and says, 'I'm starting to think nothing works.'"},"task":"Take a focused history to judge whether this is treatment-resistant depression: current symptoms and severity, each treatment's dose, duration, adherence and response, other contributing factors and risk; then explain the conclusion and agree next steps with her.","first60":["Read the GP letter and prescribing record: exact doses, start and stop dates, and any gaps.","Check recent bloods: TFTs, FBC, B12, folate, U&E, LFTs, calcium, HbA1c.","Note any past episodes and what helped then: a past response is a strong guide.","Have a simple map in your head: symptoms, treatments, other factors, risk, then options.","Plan to give hope early: she has already told the receptionist nothing works."],"ask":["'Can you tell me how your mood has been, day to day, over the last eight months?'","'What's your sleep like? Your appetite and weight? Your energy?'","'Have you ever had times of feeling unusually high, or needing much less sleep?'","'For each tablet: what dose, how long, did you take it every day, and did it help at all?'","'How did the talking therapy go? How many sessions did you manage?'","'How much alcohol do you drink? Any pain, health problems or big stresses at home or work?'","'Do you ever feel that life's not worth living? Have you thought about ending it?'","'Do you have any tablets saved up at home?'"],"recognise":["Treatment-resistant depression: no adequate response to two or more antidepressants at an adequate dose for an adequate time (at least 4-6 weeks), taken properly.","Check adherence and dose before calling it resistant: 'pseudo-resistance' from missed doses or low doses is common.","Rule out a missed diagnosis: bipolar depression, psychotic depression, alcohol misuse, hypothyroidism, anaemia, chronic pain or a personality factor.","Severity here: daily low mood worse in the mornings, early waking, weight loss of a stone, days in bed and hopelessness: more severe depression.","Passive death wishes ('sleep and not wake') with hopelessness are a real risk marker even without a plan; faith and her husband are protective.","Past response to fluoxetine with CBT tells you combined treatment can work for her."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm treatment-resistant depression, give hope, offer clear options (optimise, augment, switch or combine) with high-intensity therapy at a manageable pace, agree one with her, and set a safety plan and early review.","r":"Right: it matches NICE's approach for depression that has not responded, involves her in the choice, and keeps her safe.","best":true},{"c":"Switch straight to a fourth SSRI because the others 'just weren't right for her'.","r":"Weak: a third SSRI-type switch after two failures is unlikely to work. Augmentation, combination or a different class is more likely to help.","best":false},{"c":"Refer for ECT today because three tablets have failed.","r":"Premature: ECT is for severe, life-threatening depression or when other options have failed. She has several effective options left; discuss ECT as a later step.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity for treatment choices: give balanced information about benefits, side effects and monitoring, and record the decision.","If lithium is chosen: a lithium record book, written information, and shared care with the GP for level monitoring.","Aripiprazole and quetiapine are used off-label for augmentation in the UK; explain this and record consent.","If she becomes actively suicidal and refuses help, an MHA assessment may be needed; not indicated today."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm sorry this has gone on so long. I want you to know there are still several good options, and many people get better after a change like this."},{"to":"Patient","s":"When two antidepressants taken properly haven't helped, we call it treatment-resistant depression. It doesn't mean nothing will work; it means we change the approach."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Treatment-resistant depression: three adequate antidepressant trials, good adherence. Plan: aripiprazole augmentation added to mirtazapine 30 mg; CMHT psychology for CBT. Baseline weight, HbA1c, lipids done. Please continue mirtazapine; review in 2 weeks."}],"write":"CMHT outpatient. 45F. GP referral: depression, 8 months, no response to 3 antidepressants.\nCurrent: low mood daily, worse mornings, tearful; anhedonia (husband, reading); low energy, days in bed; EMW 5 a.m.; appetite poor, ~1 stone weight loss; poor concentration; guilt about being unproductive (not delusional); hopeless.\nNo psychotic symptoms. No history of elevated mood or reduced need for sleep.\nPast episodes: late teens (3 months, resolved), mid-30s (responded to fluoxetine + CBT).\nTreatments this episode: fluoxetine 20 mg 3 months, citalopram 20 mg 3 months, mirtazapine 30 mg nocte 2 months (current). Adherence good, minimal side effects, no response. CBT via Talking Therapies: 4 sessions, stopped due to exhaustion.\nOther: alcohol 1-2 units/week; no drugs; GP bloods normal (TFTs, FBC, B12, folate, calcium); no chronic pain; off work 3 months (school administrator); husband supportive.\nRisk: passive wish to 'sleep and not wake', no active thoughts, plans or stockpile; protective: faith, husband. No self-harm history. Self-care maintained with husband's help.\nImpression: recurrent depressive disorder, current episode severe without psychotic symptoms; treatment-resistant.\nPlan (shared decision): continue mirtazapine 30 mg; add aripiprazole 2.5 mg each morning, increase to 5 mg after 1 week (max 10 mg); baseline weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids; CMHT psychology for CBT/BA; safety plan; phone review 2 weeks, clinic 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Open with empathy and hope before you ask anything.","Pin down each treatment: dose, length, adherence, side effects and response. That is the evidence for resistance.","Screen for bipolarity and psychosis: both change the plan completely.","Ask about previous episodes and what worked then.","Ask about passive death wishes as carefully as active plans.","Lay out options simply, then let her choose: a shared decision improves adherence."],"next":"Clarissa chooses aripiprazole augmentation and agrees to try CBT again through the CMHT psychologist, with shorter sessions at first. At four weeks she is getting up most mornings; at eight weeks she reads again and her PHQ-9 has halved. She has mild restlessness for a week, which settles.","marks":["Assessed current symptoms, severity and course, including morning worsening and weight loss.","Screened for bipolar and psychotic features and took a past episode history.","Established each treatment's dose, duration, adherence, side effects and response, and the therapy history.","Explored contributing factors: alcohol, physical health, stresses and support.","Assessed risk, including passive death wishes, plans, means and self-neglect.","Explained treatment-resistant depression with hope and agreed specific next steps."],"pitfalls":["Calling it treatment-resistant without checking dose, duration and adherence.","Missing past hypomania: bipolar depression is a common cause of 'non-response'.","Ignoring passive death wishes because there is no plan.","Listing every option without helping her choose.","Forgetting that the therapy was never completed."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: aripiprazole, lithium, mirtazapine, venlafaxine (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE TA59: ECT guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: depression treatment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, GPs start most antidepressants; CMHT psychiatrists see people when two have not worked. Your letter back to the GP should give a clear drug plan, monitoring and who does what.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-06","n":1306,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💉","title":"Refusing insulin after a hyperosmolar collapse: does he have capacity to decide?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical ward, teaching hospital","text":"Geoffrey Amankwah, a 56-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia and type 2 diabetes, was found collapsed in the street three days ago in a hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state. He is now medically stable but has refused every dose of insulin the endocrinology consultant prescribed. The ward has told him he cannot go out to smoke until 'the psychiatrist' has seen him. The referral asks: does he have capacity to refuse insulin?"},"task":"Assess his capacity to refuse insulin: check understanding, retention, use and weighing, and communication; explore what is driving the refusal; reach a decision-specific conclusion and agree the least restrictive next steps.","first60":["Pin the exact question: capacity to refuse insulin for his diabetes, now. Not capacity in general, not capacity to stay in hospital.","Ask the endocrinology team what they told him, the real risk of refusing, and whether any alternative (oral agents, a different regimen) is clinically possible.","Read the notes: glucose and osmolality trend, any confusion on the ward, his antipsychotic and whether he has been taking it, CMHT care coordinator.","Ask the nurse how he has been: settled or agitated, what he says when insulin is offered, anyone visiting.","Plan to be honest about who you are and why you have come; people with schizophrenia often expect to be written off."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what happened when you collapsed, and what the doctors think caused it?'","'What have they told you about insulin, and why they recommend it now?'","'What do you think might happen if you don't have it?'","'What makes you say no? Talk me through your reasons.'","'How sure are you about that? Could there be another explanation?'","'Is there any form of treatment you would accept?'","'How's your mood been? Any thoughts of not wanting to live?'","'Before I go, can you tell me back what the team said would happen without insulin?'"],"recognise":["Capacity is about one decision at one time. He may lack it for insulin today and have it for where he smokes, or for insulin next week.","An unwise refusal is not proof of incapacity. You need to show which part of the functional test fails, and why.","He understands and retains the facts, but a fixed belief that the vials carry thought-control chips overrides every risk: that is a failure to use and weigh.","The belief is new, held with full conviction, part of a pattern (the TV before admission), and he has been taking olanzapine irregularly: it is driven by his psychosis.","Oriented, no fluctuating attention, sugars now controlled: delirium is unlikely, but say you checked.","He would accept tablets from a sealed pharmacy box: a less restrictive option to put to the diabetes team before any best-interests insulin plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Conclude that, for the decision to refuse insulin today, he cannot use and weigh the information because of a psychotic belief; document it; ask the diabetes team about oral treatment he would accept; consult his sister; reassess daily.","r":"Right: the conclusion is specific, evidenced and lawful, and the plan starts with the least restrictive option he may actually accept.","best":true},{"c":"Record that he lacks capacity because he has schizophrenia and is refusing life-saving treatment.","r":"Wrong: a diagnosis and an unwise choice prove nothing on their own. The record must show which part of the test fails and how the illness causes it.","best":false},{"c":"Detain him under Section 3 so the insulin can be given as treatment under the Mental Health Act.","r":"Wrong: the Mental Health Act authorises treatment for mental disorder. Insulin for his diabetes is a Mental Capacity Act best-interests decision. The Act may still be considered for his psychosis in its own right.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005, section 1: presume capacity; take all practicable steps to help him decide; an unwise decision is not enough; any act must be in his best interests and the least restrictive option.","Sections 2 and 3: he lacks capacity only if he cannot understand, retain, use or weigh, or communicate the decision, as a result of an impairment of mind or brain. Since the 2021 Supreme Court JB judgment, ask the functional question first, then the causal link.","Section 4: best interests must weigh his past and present wishes, beliefs and values, and the views of family. If nobody appropriate can be consulted about serious medical treatment, instruct an IMCA.","Restraint is lawful under the MCA only if necessary to prevent harm and proportionate. Repeated forced injections, or care amounting to a deprivation of liberty, need senior legal advice and may need a DoLS authorisation or the Court of Protection."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm a psychiatrist, and I'll be straight with you: the team have asked whether you can make this decision. I don't start from the idea that you can't. I want to hear your reasons."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You've understood and remembered everything. Where I'm stuck is the weighing: the worry about chips outweighs everything else, and I think that worry comes from your illness. That's about insulin, today, nothing more."},{"to":"Endocrinology consultant","s":"He lacks capacity for this decision at present because a psychotic belief about the vials stops him weighing the risks. He'd accept tablets from a sealed box. Is oral treatment safe enough for now, before we discuss a best-interests insulin plan?"}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 56M, paranoid schizophrenia, T2DM. Day 3 after HHS. Referral: capacity to refuse insulin.\nDecision: to refuse SC insulin for diabetes, at this time.\nUnderstand: yes. Explains collapse as very high sugar and dehydration; knows insulin is advised because tablets alone not enough. Gaps filled in plain words.\nRetain: yes. Repeated risks of refusal (recurrence, coma, death, eye/kidney damage) correctly 10 min later.\nUse and weigh: NO. Says 'nothing would change my mind' because nurses put tracking chips in insulin vials that read and control his thoughts. 100% conviction since admission; similar belief about TV at home. Would refuse even a sealed pen. Would accept tablets from a sealed pharmacy box.\nCommunicate: yes, clear and consistent refusal.\nImpairment and causal link: psychotic relapse (fixed delusion), olanzapine taken irregularly. Oriented in time and place, attention intact, 4AT 0: no delirium. Mood 'fed up', not depressed; no suicidal ideation; wants to live.\nConclusion: lacks capacity to refuse insulin at present, because a delusional belief prevents him using and weighing the information. Time- and decision-specific.\nPlan: discussed with endocrinology re oral agents he would accept. Sister Abena to be consulted (consent given). No restraint for insulin; senior and legal advice before any restrictive plan. Care coordinator informed; consultant review re antipsychotic and whether MHA assessment needed for psychosis. Reassess capacity daily. Escorted smoking agreed with nurse in charge.\nDr Chinonso CT2, liaison psychiatry","make":["Be honest in the first minute about why a psychiatrist is there; defensiveness drops when the patient is treated as an equal.","Give the information before testing understanding. A capacity test of someone who was never told the facts is unfair.","Test retention by asking again near the end, not straight after explaining.","Separate 'I disagree' from 'I cannot weigh'. Write the exact words that show the belief overriding the risks.","Check the cause: delirium, mood and psychosis, each by name.","End with the least restrictive next step and a date to reassess, not just a verdict."],"next":"The endocrinology consultant agrees to a trial of gliclazide with close capillary glucose monitoring, which Geoffrey takes from sealed boxes he opens himself. His sister Abena visits that evening. The ward consultant restarts olanzapine supervision with the care coordinator; ten days later the belief about the vials has faded and he agrees to discuss insulin again with the diabetes nurse.","marks":["Explained his role and purpose honestly and stated the decision was his.","Gave the key information in plain words before testing understanding.","Explored understanding, retention, use and weighing, and communication, one by one.","Elicited the belief about the vials and tested its conviction and origin.","Screened for delirium, low mood and suicidal thinking.","Gave a decision- and time-specific conclusion with reasons.","Offered a less restrictive alternative and a plan to reassess."],"pitfalls":["Finding him incapable because he has schizophrenia, or because refusing is unwise.","Testing his understanding without first giving him the information.","Trying to persuade him instead of assessing him.","Forgetting to check retention later in the conversation.","Suggesting the Mental Health Act to give insulin for his diabetes."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (2007), chapters 2 to 5 (checked Nov 2026)","2. A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52: order of the capacity test (checked Nov 2026)","3. Joint British Diabetes Societies for Inpatient Care: management of hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state in adults (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG28 Type 2 diabetes in adults: management (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, capacity belongs to whoever proposes the treatment, here the endocrinology consultant; liaison psychiatry advises. Your note must stand alone: name the decision, record his exact words for each part of the test, and state the causal link.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-07","n":1307,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👪","title":"First episode of psychosis: answering his mother's questions","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 16:30, after the ward round","where":"Adult acute psychiatric ward, family room","text":"Owen Pritchard, a 21-year-old man, was admitted this week after his parents found him at night with a kitchen knife and a cut to his upper arm. On the ward round he said he was digging out an MI5 microchip, as the archangel Gabriel had warned him. The consultant has started olanzapine for a first episode of psychosis. His mother, Bethan, has arrived upset; she overheard a nurse say 'schizophrenia'. Owen has agreed you can talk with her."},"task":"Hear her concerns and collect her account, then explain psychosis and how it differs from depression and schizophrenia, its causes, checks, olanzapine, safety at home, early intervention and the outlook, checking her understanding as you go.","first60":["Check the notes: Owen's written consent to share information, today's ward round entry, his legal status and any leave plan.","Know what was found: the microchip belief, Gabriel's voice for months, food fears, no clear depressive syndrome, drug screen and bloods pending.","Ask the nurse what Bethan has already been told and by whom; she may have heard a diagnosis in passing.","Book a quiet room, have a psychosis leaflet, the carers' support details and the ward number ready.","Plan to let her speak first and fully; an angry relative cannot hear information until she has been heard."],"ask":["'What have you noticed at home since he left university?'","'What worries you most right now?'","'What do you understand by schizophrenia, and where did you hear it?'","'Has Owen used cannabis or other drugs, as far as you know?'","'Has anyone in the family had serious mental health problems?'","'Has he said anything to you about voices, or about being watched or controlled?'","'Are there knives or tablets at home that we should think about before any leave?'","'What will you tell your husband tonight?'"],"recognise":["Six months of withdrawal, falling self-care and dropping out looked like depression: this is the prodrome, and it often gets mislabelled.","Eating only food he made himself and muttering suggest the beliefs and voices were present long before the knife.","The self-injury was driven by a delusion (removing a microchip), not by a wish to die; that changes the safety plan but not its urgency.","Her anger comes from fear, from being left out, and from hearing 'schizophrenia' in passing. Name that before you explain.","Psychosis is the description; schizophrenia is one possible cause. After one week, 'first episode of psychosis' is the honest term.","Heavy cannabis use at university and an uncle's 'breakdown' are risk factors to note without blame."],"decide":[{"c":"Let her speak, validate her fear and frustration, take her account, then explain first-episode psychosis in plain words, answer her four questions, agree safety steps for leave, and book a family meeting with written information.","r":"Right: she gives you useful history, understands the illness, becomes an ally in his care and knows what to do in a crisis.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her Owen has schizophrenia, as the nurse said, and that he will need medication for life.","r":"Wrong: it is too early to diagnose schizophrenia, and 'for life' removes hope. Many recover well from a first episode.","best":false},{"c":"Agree that it is probably depression to calm her down, and say the consultant will explain later.","r":"Wrong: it is untrue, delays her understanding of the risks, and loses her trust when she hears otherwise.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Owen has consented to sharing; check the notes record it and what he is happy for her to know. Without consent, you may still listen to her and give general information.","Under the Care Act 2014, the local authority must offer a carer's assessment to anyone who appears to need support as a carer.","If Owen, as an informal patient, wanted to leave while still at risk, the team would assess whether detention under the Mental Health Act is needed.","NICE CG178 and the early intervention standard: people with first-episode psychosis should start NICE-recommended care from an Early Intervention in Psychosis team within two weeks of referral."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"I'm sorry you heard that word in passing; that should never happen. Owen has said I can talk openly, so let me hear everything that's worried you first."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Psychosis means the brain starts to misread what's real: hearing a voice nobody else hears, or firmly believing something that isn't true. It's an illness. It's not his fault, and it's not yours."},{"to":"Ward manager","s":"Owen's mother overheard 'schizophrenia' at the nursing desk before anyone had spoken to her. Could we remind staff, and make sure she has a named nurse who rings her after ward rounds?"}],"write":"Family meeting: Bethan Pritchard (mother). Owen consents to sharing (recorded 4 Nov).\nHer account: left university in May after falling behind; withdrew to room, stopped seeing friends, poor self-care; since summer eats only food he prepares from sealed packets; heard muttering as if in conversation. Night of admission: shouting, knife, cut upper arm. Flatmate reported daily cannabis in first year. Maternal uncle 'breakdown' age 22, admitted.\nHer concerns: overheard 'schizophrenia'; believed he was depressed; fears violence and lifelong illness; guilt.\nExplained: first-episode psychosis (microchip belief, Gabriel's voice); why not primary depression; psychosis vs schizophrenia; stress-vulnerability, genes, cannabis; not her fault. Ward checks (bloods, UDS, ECG; MRI if indicated). Olanzapine: benefits, sedation, appetite and weight, monitoring, weeks to work, continue 1-2 years after recovery. Prognosis: many recover well; some relapse; fewer persistent.\nSafety: knives and sharps to be removed at home before any leave; risk reviewed before leave; crisis contacts given.\nUnderstanding checked: accurate summary back.\nPlan: psychosis leaflet, carers' leaflet; carer's assessment referral; EIP referral; family meeting with consultant Monday. Concern about overheard diagnosis passed to ward manager.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Let her finish. An angry, frightened parent needs to be heard before she can listen.","Use what Owen actually said and did; a personal explanation lands better than a textbook one.","Answer the depression question head on: say why it looked like depression, and why the team thinks otherwise.","Separate psychosis (the symptoms) from schizophrenia (one possible cause) in two sentences.","Give hope with honesty: most improve, some relapse, cannabis matters.","Check understanding after each chunk, not only at the end."],"next":"Bethan and her husband clear the knives from the kitchen before Owen's first afternoon leave the following week. The family meeting with the consultant goes ahead on Monday, and the Early Intervention team meet Owen on the ward before discharge. Bethan joins a carers' group run by the team.","marks":["Let her voice her anger and fear before giving information.","Took a brief collateral history: prodrome, food fears, muttering, cannabis, family history.","Explained psychosis in plain words using Owen's own experiences.","Explained why depression is not the main diagnosis, and psychosis versus schizophrenia.","Explained causes without blame, plus olanzapine and its monitoring.","Agreed safety steps at home and gave crisis contacts.","Gave realistic hope, checked understanding, and offered written information and another meeting."],"pitfalls":["Talking over her before she has finished her complaint.","Confirming 'schizophrenia' after one week, or saying he will be ill for life.","Dismissing her view that he is depressed instead of explaining why it looked that way.","Leaving out knives and sharps before home leave.","Not offering written information, a carers' contact or a follow-up meeting."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE QS80 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England: Early Intervention in Psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Psychosis; Schizophrenia (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK families expect to be included and will complain if left out; 'triangle of care' standards ask wards to involve carers from day one. Use 'first episode of psychosis' early on: it is accurate, less frightening and keeps the diagnosis open.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-08","n":1308,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"❤️","title":"\"Do people like me die younger?\" Physical health on clozapine","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:15, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"Desmond 'Des' Achterberg, a 47-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia, has taken clozapine for 15 years after two earlier antipsychotics failed. His symptoms are well controlled and he is content in supported accommodation. He asked for this appointment after reading that people with severe mental illness die 10 to 25 years earlier. He has brought the cutting. He is shocked that no doctor ever mentioned it, and wants to know what he can do."},"task":"Explore what he read and how it has affected him, explain why the mortality gap exists, assess his own cardiovascular and metabolic risks, and agree practical steps, including safe smoking cessation on clozapine and the monitoring you and his GP will do.","first60":["Check the clozapine record: dose, last plasma level, FBC monitoring status, and his last weight, BP, HbA1c and lipids.","Look for physical problems in the GP summary: blood pressure readings, prescriptions he has declined, family history.","Note his smoking status: any plan to quit changes his clozapine level, so you must cover it today.","Have a QRISK3 calculator, local stop-smoking service details and a constipation leaflet ready.","Plan a partnership tone: 'we' and 'you might consider', not a lecture."],"ask":["'What did the article say, and how has it left you feeling?'","'How many cigarettes do you smoke a day? Have you thought about stopping, and how?'","'How has your weight changed over the years? What's a typical day's eating?'","'How active are you in a normal week?'","'Has anyone mentioned your blood pressure, sugar or cholesterol?'","'Has anyone in your family had a heart attack or stroke? At what age?'","'How are your bowels? How often do you open them?'","'Since reading this, what has your mood been like? Any thoughts of giving up?'"],"recognise":["The gap is real: people with severe mental illness die on average 15 to 20 years earlier, mostly from heart disease, stroke, lung disease, diabetes and cancer, much of it preventable.","Causes stack: smoking, weight gain and inactivity, antipsychotic metabolic effects, poverty, and physical problems being missed or under-treated.","His own risks: male, 47, 20 a day, weight gain, inactive, borderline HbA1c, raised BP he won't treat, father died of a stroke.","His plan to stop smoking 'cold turkey' is the hidden danger: smoke speeds clozapine breakdown, so stopping can push levels to toxic within days.","Constipation on clozapine is not trivial: bowel obstruction can kill. Ask every time.","He is shaken, not hopeless; still check mood and make sure he doesn't decide to stop clozapine."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm the gap honestly, map his personal risks, support him to stop smoking with NRT or varenicline and a clozapine level check before and after, agree one or two lifestyle goals, and write to his GP for BP, HbA1c, lipids and a QRISK3 score.","r":"Right: it answers his question truthfully, makes his quit attempt safe, and turns worry into a shared, specific plan.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure him the article exaggerates and his health is fine because he sees his GP.","r":"Wrong: the gap is real and he has several untreated risks. False reassurance loses his trust and his motivation.","best":false},{"c":"Encourage him to stop smoking immediately and leave his clozapine dose as it is.","r":"Unsafe: without a level check and dose review, his clozapine level may rise sharply, causing sedation, confusion or seizures.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: he has capacity; share results and the plan with his GP with his agreement, and invite him to involve his accommodation staff.","Shared care: the CMHT keeps responsibility for clozapine and its monitoring; physical health checks are shared with primary care, and the letter should say who does what.","MHRA Drug Safety Update (2020): clozapine can cause fatal gastrointestinal hypomotility; screen bowel habit before starting and at every review, and treat constipation promptly.","Clozapine is prescribed under a registered monitoring service; FBC every 4 weeks continues after the first year."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're right to ask, Des, and I'm sorry nobody raised it before. The gap is real. The good news is that most of it comes from things we can change together."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Stopping smoking is the single best thing you can do. But on clozapine we need to plan it: the smoke itself speeds up how fast your body clears clozapine, so when you stop, your level can climb quickly."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Des plans to stop smoking with our support. Please do not start varenicline or NRT without telling us, as we will check his clozapine level before and one week after quitting. He agrees to discuss his BP, HbA1c of 44 and a QRISK3 score with you."}],"write":"Outpatient review. 47M, paranoid schizophrenia, clozapine 400 mg/day for 15 yrs, well controlled.\nReason: read article on 10-25 yr mortality gap in SMI; shocked, upset nobody discussed it.\nRisk factors: smokes 20/day since 18; plans 'cold turkey' this weekend. Weight up 'about 3 stone' since clozapine; constant hunger; tired; walks only to shop. Diet mostly takeaways and white bread. Alcohol 2 cans at weekends. GP told him BP 'high', declined treatment as feels well. HbA1c 44 mmol/mol (6 months ago). Lipids not known. Father died of stroke at 68. No personal CVD. Constipated, opens bowels every 3-4 days, hard stools, no laxatives.\nMood: shaken, slept poorly 2 nights; no hopelessness, no SI; wants a long life; will continue clozapine.\nExplained: mortality gap real, causes, much preventable. Smoking and clozapine interaction explained and understood (teach-back).\nPlan: stop date in 2 weeks with combination NRT via stop-smoking service; clozapine level before and 1 week after quitting, dose reduced if needed; warn of sedation, drooling, confusion, jerks. Senna 15 mg at night + docusate 100 mg twice daily; report no bowel opening for 3 days. Walking group 3x/wk with key worker; healthy cooking support from accommodation staff. GP letter: BP confirm and treat, HbA1c, fasting lipids, QRISK3, Diabetes Prevention Programme. Review 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Start from his worry and his words; then confirm the facts honestly.","Cover the full cardiac risk list: age, sex, smoking, weight, activity, BP, glucose, cholesterol, family and personal history.","Never discuss stopping smoking on clozapine without the level check: it is the one thing that cannot be missed.","Ask about bowels; patients rarely volunteer it.","Agree one or two small goals he chose himself, with named helpers.","Say who will do each check: GP, clinic, accommodation staff."],"next":"Des stops smoking two weeks later on a patch plus lozenges. His clozapine level rises from 0.45 to 0.71 mg/L a week later and he feels drowsy; the dose is reduced by 100 mg and he settles. His GP starts amlodipine and atorvastatin after a QRISK3 of 14%. At six months he has lost half a stone and joins the walking group twice a week.","marks":["Explored what he read and his feelings, and apologised that it had not been discussed.","Confirmed the mortality gap honestly and explained the main causes.","Elicited his cardiovascular and metabolic risk factors in full, including family history.","Asked about constipation and explained why it matters on clozapine.","Explained the clozapine level rise when stopping smoking and planned level checks.","Agreed practical lifestyle goals and named who will help.","Explained monitoring and GP liaison, checked mood and understanding, and arranged review."],"pitfalls":["Falsely reassuring him the statistic does not apply to him.","Lecturing instead of agreeing goals he chooses.","Encouraging him to stop smoking without mentioning clozapine levels.","Leaving out family history, BP or constipation.","Suggesting he come off clozapine to improve his physical health."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults, physical health sections (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and gastrointestinal hypomotility (2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG209 Tobacco: preventing uptake, promoting quitting and treating dependence (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: physical health checks for people with severe mental illness (checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE NG238 Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, people on the SMI register should get a yearly physical health check from their GP, but many are missed. Psychiatrists are expected to own the physical health of patients on clozapine, not just refer it on.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-09","n":1309,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏍️","title":"After the crash: collateral history from his mother about a changed son","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 11:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Trauma and orthopaedic ward, district general hospital","text":"Liam Doherty, a 27-year-old man, came off his motorbike four days ago and has broken ribs and a fractured arm, now fixed. The surgical team have asked for a psychiatric review: he swears at nurses, pulls at his cannula and makes crude remarks to young staff, and he is worse at night. His mother, Maureen, has asked to speak to a psychiatrist. Liam has agreed. She says, 'He's not himself at all.'"},"task":"Take a collateral history from his mother covering the injury, the change in behaviour, his previous personality, alcohol and drugs, other causes and risks; explain the likely causes and next steps; and address her worries.","first60":["Read the trauma notes: mechanism, GCS at scene and on arrival, how long he was unconscious, CT head report, ICU stay, operations, current drugs (opioids, anticholinergics).","Check the observation chart: temperature, pulse, BP, sats, sweating, and any CIWA-Ar or delirium scores; look at the timing of his last drink.","Look at bloods: FBC, CRP, U&E, sodium, calcium, LFT, GGT, MCV, glucose.","Ask the nurse in charge what happens at night and what he has said or done; has he tried to leave?","Confirm Liam's consent to speak to his mother is recorded, and find a private room."],"ask":["'What do you know about the crash? Was he knocked out, and for how long?'","'What's the last thing he remembers before it, and when did his memory start working again?'","'What was Liam like before the accident: work, friends, temper, mood?'","'What have you noticed that's different? Is he worse at certain times of day?'","'How much does he usually drink, and when did he last have a drink before the crash?'","'Has he been shaky, sweaty, or seeing things that aren't there?'","'Was anything troubling him before the crash? Could it have been anything other than an accident?'","'What worries you most about him?'"],"recognise":["Knocked out for minutes, no memory of the day, and still muddled about recent events four days on: a significant traumatic brain injury until shown otherwise.","Disinhibition, crude remarks, irritability and laughing at odd moments in a gentle man: classic frontal-lobe change, but it can also be delirium.","Worse at night, poor attention, pulling at lines: delirium. Look for causes: pain, opioids, chest infection after rib fractures, low sodium, poor sleep.","Drinking most evenings, more since a break-up, and day 3 to 4 after his last drink: alcohol withdrawal and delirium tremens must be excluded today.","A normal CT on arrival does not exclude diffuse axonal injury or a later bleed; new or persistent change needs repeat imaging.","Ask whether the crash could have been deliberate: low mood after a break-up and heavy drinking matter."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the collateral, then advise the surgical team: delirium screen, CIWA-Ar scoring with chlordiazepoxide and IV Pabrinex if withdrawing, opioid review, discuss repeat brain imaging, and a non-drug delirium plan; explain all this to Maureen and arrange review.","r":"Right: it treats the dangerous, reversible causes first and keeps the brain injury in view, while supporting his mother.","best":true},{"c":"Tell Maureen this is brain damage and his personality has changed for good.","r":"Wrong: four days in, delirium and withdrawal are likely contributors and much brain-injury change improves over months. It is too early to predict.","best":false},{"c":"Advise haloperidol for his behaviour and sign him off as 'behavioural'.","r":"Unsafe: it misses withdrawal, which needs benzodiazepines, and an untreated cause; haloperidol also lowers the seizure threshold.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Liam has agreed you can speak to his mother; record this. You can always listen to relatives even without consent.","If he tries to leave and may lack capacity to decide, the ward assesses capacity for that decision; under the MCA staff may act in his best interests with proportionate restraint. Prolonged restriction may need a DoLS authorisation.","Driving: after a significant head injury he must not drive or ride until cleared by his doctors, and he must tell the DVLA if the injury is notifiable.","If police took a blood sample after the crash, that is a separate legal matter; do not speculate about it with his mother."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"Thank you for waiting, Maureen. Liam is happy for us to talk. You know him better than anyone, and what you tell me will really help us work out what's going on."},{"to":"Mother","s":"There are a few possible reasons, and more than one may be at work: confusion from being so unwell, coming off alcohol suddenly, and the knock to his head. The first two are very treatable."},{"to":"Surgical registrar","s":"Collateral suggests heavy daily drinking, last drink the night before the crash, now day 4 with night-time worsening and tremor. Please start CIWA-Ar, chlordiazepoxide and IV Pabrinex, do a delirium screen, review his morphine, and discuss repeat imaging."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry: collateral from mother, Maureen Doherty (Liam consents).\n27M, day 4 after motorbike RTC (car pulled out, helmet on). Knocked out 'several minutes' per paramedics; no memory from morning of crash to ~2 days later; still forgets recent conversations. CT head on arrival: no bleed. ICU 1 night (rib fractures, chest drain). ORIF L radius day 2. On morphine PCA.\nChange: since waking, irritable, swearing, crude sexual remarks to young nurses, laughs at odd moments, cannot follow a conversation, asks for ex-girlfriend (split 4 months ago), frets about finished job. Worse at night; last night pulled cannula and tried to leave; picking at sheets; sweaty, hands shaking.\nPremorbid: gentle, easy-going, sociable; partner in building firm. No psychiatric history. Low after break-up, 'drowning sorrows'.\nAlcohol: most evenings, 6-8 pints, some nights vodka, increased since break-up; last drink night before crash. Cannabis as teenager only. Crash at 07:15; police took blood, result unknown to mother. No known suicidal ideas; mother does not think it was deliberate.\nImpression: hyperactive delirium, likely multifactorial: alcohol withdrawal (?early DTs), opioids, possible chest infection; on background of moderate-severe TBI (prolonged PTA). Frontal disinhibition possible; reassess when delirium clears.\nRisks: aggression to staff, leaving the ward, falls. No known SH. Must not drive.\nAdvised: CIWA-Ar, chlordiazepoxide per protocol, IV Pabrinex 3-5 days; delirium screen incl. CXR, cultures, Na, Ca; review morphine; discuss repeat CT/MRI with surgical team; non-drug delirium care; capacity assessment if he tries to leave. Mother: explanation given, Headway info, review Monday.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Pin the injury: mechanism, how long he was out, how long the memory gap, scans, ICU, operations.","Describe the change with examples, onset and timing (night-time worsening points to delirium).","Always get a detailed alcohol history from relatives of trauma patients; it is often missed on admission.","Ask what he was like before; personality change only means something against a baseline.","Ask, gently, whether the crash could have been intentional.","Be honest about uncertainty: treat what is treatable now, judge the brain injury later."],"next":"His CIWA-Ar score is 18 that afternoon and he starts chlordiazepoxide and IV Pabrinex; a chest X-ray shows a left lower lobe pneumonia. By day 8 his nights are settled and the crude remarks have stopped, but he still struggles with attention and planning. An MRI shows small frontal contusions, and he is referred to the community brain injury team.","marks":["Established mechanism, loss of consciousness, length of memory gap, scans and ICU stay.","Described the behaviour change with examples, onset and night-time pattern.","Took his previous personality, work and mental health history.","Took a detailed alcohol and drug history including last drink and withdrawal signs.","Considered delirium causes, withdrawal, brain injury and whether the crash was deliberate.","Assessed risks: aggression, leaving, self-harm, driving.","Explained causes and next steps honestly and supported his mother."],"pitfalls":["Calling it 'just bad behaviour' or 'just the head injury' without a delirium screen.","Missing the alcohol history, then missing withdrawal on day 3 to 4.","Asking his mother for the GCS or technical details she cannot know.","Promising a full recovery, or predicting permanent damage, this early.","Forgetting the driving advice and the question of whether the crash was deliberate."],"refs":["1. NICE CG103 Delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management in hospital (updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG232 Head injury: assessment and early management (2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)","5. Headway, the brain injury association: information for families (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK surgical wards, trauma patients often arrive unconscious and their drinking goes unrecorded. Liaison psychiatry is frequently the first to take a proper alcohol history; relatives are your best source.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-10","n":1310,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🤝","title":"Handover from his community nurse: collateral history and an initial ward plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:45, ward doctor","where":"Adult acute psychiatric ward, office","text":"Darren Okafor, a 35-year-old man with schizophrenia, arrived on the ward an hour ago under Section 3. His community psychiatric nurse, Paul Henshaw, has worked with him for two weeks and has watched him deteriorate. Paul has come over to hand over before going back to the team base. Darren is in his room, guarded and refusing to talk."},"task":"Take a structured collateral history from his CPN: recent change, background, medication, substances, physical health, forensic history, risks and social situation; then agree an initial ward plan and how ward and community will stay in touch.","first60":["Glance at the Section 3 papers and the AMHP's report: what prompted the assessment, and is there a nearest relative?","Read the electronic record summary: diagnosis, previous admissions, last clinic letter, current prescription and any risk history.","Ask the nurse in charge how Darren is now: settled, agitated, eating, any searches done.","Find a room and a pen; plan to take the history in a fixed order so nothing is missed.","Remember Paul is a colleague: be efficient and direct, but thank him and listen to his worries."],"ask":["'How long have you known Darren, and what's changed over that time?'","'What are his symptoms now: beliefs, voices, sleep, eating, self-care?'","'What happened at previous relapses? What tends to set them off?'","'What's he prescribed, when did he stop, and why? What has worked best before?'","'What's he using: cannabis, alcohol, anything else, how much and how much does it cost him?'","'Has he harmed or threatened anyone, now or before? Any weapons? Any police contact?'","'Any thoughts of suicide or self-harm? Is anyone vulnerable around him, especially children?'","'Where is he living, who supports him, and how are his money and benefits?'"],"recognise":["A classic relapse: stopped olanzapine three weeks ago, restarted cannabis, then persecutory beliefs about neighbours filming him; his previous relapse followed the same pattern.","Sleeping in his car to escape surveillance is both a sign of the delusion's grip and a vulnerability (cold, self-neglect, driving while unwell).","Threatening a neighbour who is part of his delusional system is a risk marker; previous violence when unwell (a caution in 2022) raises it further.","A daughter of 7 with contact visits means you must consider safeguarding and pause unsupervised contact until he is well.","Reasons for stopping matter: weight gain and feeling flat. Fix the reason, or the next relapse is already planned.","Paul is new and feels he should have acted sooner; a short word of support helps the working relationship."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full structured collateral, restart olanzapine at his previously effective dose, set observations and no leave until RC review, order bloods, drug screen and ECG, consider safeguarding for his daughter, and agree weekly CMHT contact and a CPA review.","r":"Right: it captures the history while it is fresh, starts treatment that has worked, manages the identified risks and keeps the community team involved.","best":true},{"c":"Thank Paul, tell him you'll read the notes, and wait for Darren to engage tomorrow.","r":"Wrong: the CPN holds the most recent information on risk, adherence and substances. Losing it now leaves the ward planning blind.","best":false},{"c":"Start a long-acting injection today because he stopped his tablets.","r":"Premature: a depot may be right later, but discuss it with Darren once he is settled, with his preference and previous response in mind.","best":false}],"law":["Section 3: treatment for mental disorder can be given without consent during the first three months under Section 63; after that a T2 or T3 is needed. Explain his rights under Section 132 and give access to an IMHA.","Section 17 leave can only be granted by the responsible clinician; no leave until the RC has reviewed him.","Sharing information with the police, the ex-partner or children's social care is justified when it is needed to protect others; follow trust policy and record the reasoning.","Driving: he should not drive while acutely unwell, and he must tell the DVLA about his condition; if he will not and continues driving, GMC guidance allows disclosure to the DVLA."],"say":[{"to":"CPN","s":"Thanks for coming over, Paul. Your view is the freshest we've got. Can I take it in a set order so I don't miss anything: what's changed, background, meds, substances, risk, then social?"},{"to":"CPN","s":"You spotted it, you escalated it, and he's safe now. That's exactly what was needed. Let's make sure the ward and the team stay joined up from today."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Darren stopped olanzapine three weeks ago, is using cannabis daily, believes neighbours are filming him, and threatened one last week. Can we start 15-minute observations, search his belongings, and no leave until the RC sees him tomorrow?"}],"write":"Collateral from Paul Henshaw, CPN (CMHT), known Darren 2 weeks.\n35M, schizophrenia (dx 2018). Admitted today, Section 3.\nRecent: stopped olanzapine 10 mg ~3 weeks ago (weight gain ~2 stone, 'feeling flat'). Restarted daily cannabis (skunk, ~£20/day). Over 2 weeks: believes neighbours film him through his smoke alarm; voices commenting on his actions; disengaging, missed 2 visits; sleeping in his car for a week; poor eating, weight loss; poor self-care. Last week banged on neighbour's door threatening to 'smash his face in'; police attended, no arrest. Said once 'might as well end it if they keep watching'.\nBackground: 2 previous admissions (S2 2018; S3 2022 after stopping meds and cannabis, punched a stranger he thought was following him: caution). Risperidone stopped for sexual side effects; good response to olanzapine 10 mg. Previously declined depot.\nSubstances: cannabis as above; ~4 cans strong lager/day; no known other drugs. 20 cigs/day.\nPhysical: no known problems. Forensic: caution for assault 2022.\nSocial: council flat, alone; ESA/UC; daughter (7) lives with ex-partner Kelly, contact alternate Saturdays (last 2 weeks ago); mother in town, supportive. Car: has been driving.\nRisks: to others (threat to neighbour, past violence when psychotic): high while unwell. To self: passive SI once, self-neglect, vulnerability. Children: daughter, contact to pause.\nPlan: 15-min obs; search; no s17 leave until RC review tomorrow; olanzapine 10 mg at night (S63); lorazepam 1 mg PO PRN, max 4 mg/24 h; NRT; bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin), UDS, ECG; CIWA-Ar given alcohol; rights read; IMHA. Safeguarding discussion with ward social worker re daughter today; ex-partner informed via policy. Advise no driving; DVLA. Paul to attend ward review weekly; CPA review before discharge.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Use a fixed order with a colleague; it is faster and more complete than a free chat.","Turn every symptom into a timeline: when it started, how it changed, what he did because of it.","Ask why he stopped his medication, not just whether he did.","Quantify substances: type, amount, frequency, cost.","Cover risk in every direction: others, self, neglect, exploitation, children, driving.","End by agreeing who does what and when the ward and community will next speak."],"next":"Darren takes olanzapine that night after a long talk with the nurse in charge. Children's social care note the referral; his ex-partner pauses contact until he is well. Two weeks later the beliefs about the neighbours are less intense and Darren agrees to discuss a long-acting injection and weight support. Paul attends the CPA review and helps plan a move away from the neighbour.","marks":["Took a timeline of the recent change, with specific symptoms and behaviours.","Covered past psychiatric history, relapse pattern and triggers.","Established medication, adherence, reasons for stopping and previous response.","Quantified cannabis, alcohol and other substances.","Covered physical health, forensic history and social situation.","Assessed risk to others, to self, self-neglect, children and driving.","Agreed an initial ward plan and ongoing contact with the community team."],"pitfalls":["Letting the handover drift without a structure and missing whole areas.","Asking whether he stopped his medication but not why.","Forgetting children in contact with the patient.","Leaving out driving when he has been sleeping in, and using, his car.","Ending without agreeing how and when the CMHT stays involved."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG10: violence and aggression, short-term management (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015) (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG53 Transition between inpatient mental health settings and community or care home settings (checked Nov 2026)","5. GMC: Confidentiality, patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, community nurses and care coordinators often know the patient far better than the ward does. Treat a CPN handover as a key history, write it up in full, and invite them to ward reviews; NICE expects joint discharge planning from admission.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-11","n":1311,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏨","title":"Hotel room hanging: is he safe to go home?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:20, liaison psychiatry","where":"Emergency department, side room","text":"Roger Whitfield, a 52-year-old man, was found collapsed on his hotel room floor last night after trying to hang himself; the fitting he tied the rope to gave way. He has been medically cleared. He lives 80 miles away, has no psychiatric history, and wants to go home. A nurse says he seems strangely cheerful and wonders if he is putting it on to get discharged."},"task":"Take a full history of the suicide attempt, understand his bright mood, screen for depression, psychosis and alcohol misuse, weigh risk against protective factors, and decide whether he can safely leave.","first60":["Read the ED and paramedic notes: how he was found, the ligature, any neck marks, alcohol level, and who has his belongings.","Check the medical clearance: neck injury, voice change, swallowing or breathing problems, and any head injury from the fall.","Ask the nurse what she has seen: attempts to leave, phone calls, what he has said about going home.","Make the room safe before you sit down: no cords, belts or bag straps; a nurse nearby; know where security is.","Go in expecting him to play it down: a calm, cooperative man after a hidden, planned hanging is high risk until shown otherwise."],"ask":["'How did you come to be at a hotel so far from home?'","'When did you first start planning this, and what did you prepare?'","'Did you do anything to make sure nobody would find you or interrupt you?'","'Did you leave a note, or sort out money or a will?'","'How do you feel now about having survived?'","'You seem calm this morning. What has changed since yesterday?'","'What has been happening in your life over the last few months?'","'If you went home today, what would you do?'"],"recognise":["Features of high suicidal intent: planned over days, method researched, travelled far away, chosen to avoid discovery, final acts (letters, will) and found only by chance.","Hanging is a highly lethal, violent method; survival here was luck, not a sign of low intent.","Calm or cheerfulness after a decision to die is a known warning sign: relief that a plan exists, not recovery. Ask what has changed; if nothing has, the calm is not reassuring.","Regret at surviving, ongoing hopelessness ('no way out') and an unchanged problem (debt) mean intent persists.","Depressive syndrome: low mood, loss of interest, early waking, weight loss, guilt; screen for psychotic features such as nihilistic or guilty delusions.","Alcohol escalation in recent weeks lowers inhibition and worsens mood.","Static risk: male, middle-aged, family history of suicide. Dynamic risk: debt, secrecy, shame, alcohol, access to means at home."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise informal admission today, with close observation in the ED until a bed is found; involve his local crisis team and bed manager; if he refuses and tries to leave, arrange an urgent Mental Health Act assessment.","r":"Right: a planned, hidden, violent attempt with ongoing hopelessness, regret at surviving and an unchanged crisis is high risk. Admission manages the next days and starts treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge him home to his wife with a crisis line number and a GP letter, as he is calm and cooperative.","r":"Unsafe: the calm is the relief of a decision made, his wife does not know about the debt or the letters, and he has said nothing has changed.","best":false},{"c":"Use Section 5(2) to stop him leaving the department.","r":"Wrong: the holding powers apply only to hospital inpatients, not to people in the ED. Use an urgent MHA assessment and, if needed meanwhile, the MCA or common law with senior advice.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal and has the right to leave. If he tries to go before an MHA assessment, get senior help: assess his capacity for that decision; the MCA (best interests) or common law can justify brief, proportionate restraint.","Section 5(2) and 5(4) cannot be used in the ED. Police can use section 136 in an ED if he leaves and is found in a public place.","If admission is refused and the risk remains high, request an AMHP and two doctors for assessment under Section 2.","Confidentiality: seek his consent to tell his wife. If he refuses and the risk to his life is serious, sharing what is needed to keep him safe is justified (GMC guidance); record why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Roger, I can see you'd like to get home, and I'm grateful you waited. What happened last night was very serious, and I'd like to understand it properly before we decide anything."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You've said you feel at peace and still can't see a way out. When someone feels that, the peace often comes from having made a decision. That worries me, and I think you need to stay with us for now."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"High risk: planned hanging far from home, letters left, found by chance, regrets surviving, still hopeless. He's agreed to informal admission. One-to-one, within arm's length, until a bed is found. If he tries to leave, call me and security."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, ED. 52M. Brought in after attempted hanging in hotel room last night; medically cleared. No psychiatric history.\nAttempt: planned over ~1 week; researched method online; bought rope 2 days before; booked hotel 80 miles from home so family would not find him; told wife he was at a supplier event. Letters to wife and sons left in desk drawer at home; changed will. ~half bottle whisky before. Do-not-disturb sign; rope tied to wardrobe rail, which gave way; staff heard noise. Regrets surviving.\nNow: calm, 'at peace', 'no way out'; no new specific plan disclosed; would not commit to safety.\nMental state: low mood ~3 months, anhedonia (stopped golf), early waking, 6 kg weight loss, guilt about failing family; not delusional; no psychotic symptoms. Alcohol escalated to ~half bottle whisky/day for 3 weeks.\nStressors: building firm went into liquidation a month ago; ~£90,000 debt incl. loan against house, hidden from wife.\nRisk factors: male, 52, highly planned violent attempt, avoidance of discovery, final acts, hopelessness, alcohol, father died by suicide, rope and tools in garage.\nProtective: wife and two sons, accepts help, agreed wife can be told he is in hospital.\nImpression: severe depressive episode without psychotic symptoms; alcohol misuse. Suicide risk HIGH and ongoing.\nPlan: informal admission agreed; 1:1 obs within arm's length in ED; bed manager and his local crisis team contacted; MHA assessment if he tries to leave; CIWA-Ar 4-hourly; bloods; GP informed; wife informed with consent.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Open with his wish to leave: name it, thank him for waiting, and give a reason to talk.","Go through the attempt in time order: the plan, the precautions, the final acts, the act itself, how he was found.","Ask directly about the bright mood: 'What has changed since last night?'","Screen for depression with biological symptoms and check for psychotic features, not just 'how is your mood?'.","Find the crisis behind it (here, debt and shame) and ask if anything about it has changed.","Be honest about your decision and the legal route, and still offer him a choice."],"next":"Roger accepts informal admission. A bed is found in his home area the same evening and he is transferred with a nurse escort. On the ward he tells his wife about the debt with the social worker present, starts sertraline, and is referred for debt advice under the Breathing Space scheme.","marks":["Acknowledged his wish to leave and agreed the purpose of the conversation.","Reconstructed the attempt: planning, research, distance, precautions, letters, will, method, alcohol, discovery.","Explored the bright mood: regret at surviving, relief of a decision, ongoing hopelessness.","Screened for depression, psychotic features and alcohol misuse.","Identified the debt and business loss and the secrecy around them.","Weighed risk and protective factors, including access to means at home and family history.","Recommended admission honestly and explained the route if he refused."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I'm fine now' and discharging a calm man after a hidden, planned hanging.","Skipping the final acts: letters, will, money and the effort not to be found.","Using Section 5(2) in the ED.","Not asking why he chose a hotel 80 miles away.","Forgetting alcohol withdrawal and the means waiting at home."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters 14 and 18 (checked Nov 2026)","4. National Confidential Inquiry into Suicide and Safety in Mental Health, annual report (checked Nov 2026)","5. Debt Respite Scheme (Breathing Space): Mental Health Crisis Breathing Space guidance, Insolvency Service (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the ED is not a ward, so the nurse's and doctor's holding powers do not apply there. Know your trust's policy for patients at high risk who try to leave the ED before an MHA assessment, and who to call.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-12","n":1312,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🦋","title":"Gender clinic first assessment: readiness for hormones and surgery","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00, gender identity clinic","where":"Adult gender identity clinic, outpatient room","text":"Nadia Bright, a 33-year-old trans woman, assigned male at birth, attends her first appointment after a GP referral. The letter says she has long felt she was in the wrong body, identifies fully as a woman, and has read about hormones and surgery. No notable physical or mental health history. She arrives with her partner, who waits outside."},"task":"Assess her gender history, social transition, goals and expectations, screen mental and physical health and risk without pathologising her identity, and explain what happens next.","first60":["Read the referral: the name and pronouns she uses, any name already changed on the GP record, and medical history that affects hormones (clots, smoking, migraine with aura, liver disease).","Check that the clinic letter and screen show her chosen name; correct the record before she sees it, not after.","Plan to ask her name and pronouns in your first sentence and use them every time.","Remind yourself why you will screen for psychosis, OCD and body dysmorphia: to make sure nothing else needs help, not to question who she is. Say so when you ask.","Have the next-step information ready: bloods, fertility, voice therapy, peer support, the usual pathway and waiting times."],"ask":["'What name and pronouns would you like me to use?'","'Going back as far as you remember, what has your sense of your gender been?'","'How was puberty for you?'","'What does your life look like now: name, clothes, work, who knows?'","'What are you hoping hormones and surgery will change? What do you expect them not to change?'","'Have you thought about whether you might want children in future?'","'Have you ever bought hormones online, or thought about harming parts of your body?'","'Has anyone ever hurt you, or treated you badly, because of being trans?'"],"recognise":["Gender incongruence that is marked and persistent, from early childhood, through a distressing puberty, and stable in adult life, supports readiness.","ICD-11 places gender incongruence in a chapter on sexual health, not among mental disorders; you are assessing needs and readiness, not curing an illness.","Two years of living fully as Nadia, with a supportive partner and work, shows a well-established social transition.","Things to rule out or treat alongside: depression and anxiety (common, often from stigma), psychosis, OCD, and body dysmorphic disorder focused on one feature rather than gender as a whole.","Risk signals: thoughts of self-surgery, online hormones without monitoring, suicidal thoughts, unsafe sex or exploitation, and assault or harassment.","Realistic expectations: oestrogen softens skin, grows breasts and redistributes fat, but does not raise voice pitch and only partly thins facial hair."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the assessment today, record a working view of gender incongruence, arrange baseline bloods, fertility referral and voice therapy, and plan the second assessment towards hormones; give clear safety advice about online hormones.","r":"Right: her history is long-standing, consistent and well supported, with no mental illness. A clear, honest path respects her and keeps her safe while she waits.","best":true},{"c":"Defer any plan until she has had a year of psychological therapy to explore her identity.","r":"Wrong: there is no mental disorder to treat and therapy is optional support, not a hurdle. Unneeded delay raises the risk of unmonitored online hormones.","best":false},{"c":"Promise her hormones from today and a surgery date within months.","r":"Unsafe and untrue: hormones need baseline tests and a recommendation after full assessment, and surgery needs a second opinion. Over-promising damages trust.","best":false}],"law":["The Equality Act 2010 protects gender reassignment as a characteristic; she does not need any medical treatment to be protected at work or in services.","A Gender Recognition Certificate (Gender Recognition Act 2004) needs evidence of living in her gender for two years and a medical report; it is optional and separate from treatment.","Consent: she has capacity to consent to hormones; explain effects, which may be irreversible (fertility), and record the discussion.","Confidentiality: information about gender history is sensitive; share only with her consent and on a need-to-know basis."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Some of my questions are about mood and other experiences. I ask everyone this, and it's not because I think being trans is an illness. I want to make sure you have all the support you need."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Hormones can do a lot: softer skin, breast growth, a change in body shape. They won't raise your voice, so voice therapy helps with that, and facial hair usually needs laser or electrolysis too."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Nadia (she/her). Long-standing gender incongruence; two years living as Nadia; no mental illness found. Please update her name and gender on the record. Baseline bloods requested; fertility referral for sperm storage; second assessment booked."}],"write":"Gender identity clinic, first assessment. Nadia Bright (she/her), 33. Assigned male at birth.\nGender history: felt she was a girl from about age 5; wore her sister's clothes; puberty (voice, facial hair) deeply distressing. Identity consistent since. Lived fully as Nadia for 2 years; changed name by deed poll; out at work (dental nurse); partner Marcus (36) supportive.\nGoals: oestrogen, then vaginoplasty; would like breast growth; expects voice to rise (corrected: needs voice therapy). Would like children with Marcus some day; had not considered sperm storage.\nHealth: mood good, sleeps and eats well, anxious on buses since assault. No psychotic symptoms, obsessions or body dysmorphic features beyond gender. No self-harm or suicidal thoughts. Smokes 5/day. Alcohol ~6 units/week. No medical history.\nRisk: punched on a night bus last year (fractured cheekbone), reported to police; no nightmares or flashbacks. Friend has offered online oestrogen; has not taken any. No thoughts of self-surgery. Monogamous; last sexual health check 2 years ago.\nImpression: gender incongruence (ICD-11), long-standing and consistent; good support; no mental disorder; capacity to consent.\nPlan: baseline bloods; smoking cessation; NHS fertility referral; SLT voice therapy; peer support; second assessment towards hormone recommendation; advised against online hormones; sexual health clinic offered. GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Ask name and pronouns in your first sentence, and never slip.","Let her tell the story from childhood; ask for examples, not ticks.","Explain why you screen for mood, psychosis and OCD before you ask.","Correct unrealistic expectations kindly, with what will help instead.","Ask about online hormones and self-surgery directly; frustration with waiting is a real risk.","End with concrete next steps and dates, not 'we'll see'."],"next":"Nadia has her baseline bloods the next week, stops smoking with a stop-smoking adviser, and stores sperm at the NHS fertility unit. At her second assessment, hormones are recommended and her GP starts transdermal oestradiol under shared care. She joins a local trans women's group.","marks":["Asked name and pronouns at the start and used them throughout.","Explored gender feelings from childhood, through puberty, to now.","Explored social transition, support and who knows.","Explored goals and expectations, including voice, hair and fertility.","Screened for depression, anxiety, psychosis, OCD and body dysmorphia without pathologising.","Asked about suicidal thoughts, self-surgery, online hormones, assault and sexual health.","Explained the next steps clearly and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Using her old name or the wrong pronoun, even once.","Asking about voices without explaining why, so it sounds like you think she is unwell.","Treating psychological therapy as a gate she must pass through.","Forgetting fertility before hormones.","Not asking about online hormones, self-surgery or assault."],"refs":["1. NHS England: Service specification, Gender Dysphoria Services for adults (non-surgical) (checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: Gender incongruence of adolescence or adulthood, conditions related to sexual health (checked Nov 2026)","3. Equality Act 2010 and Gender Recognition Act 2004 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Gender dysphoria, treatment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK practice is affirming and non-judgemental. Use the person's chosen name and pronouns, avoid words like 'sex change' or 'transsexual', and never imply that being trans is a mental illness. If you slip, apologise briefly and carry on.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-19","n":1319,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Care home worker worried about a quiet resident","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00","where":"Community learning disability team clinic","text":"Josh Okonkwo-Banks, a 32-year-old man with a moderate learning disability, has changed over six weeks: withdrawn, quieter, skipping meals, crying at night, banging his head, and last week he hit a staff member. His key worker, Declan Fairweather, 41, has come in to talk. He looks tired and says, 'We just don't know what's wrong with him.'"},"task":"Take a collateral history from Josh's key worker, covering his baseline, the change, biological signs, physical, psychological and social causes and risks, then address the key worker's concerns about safety and what staff should do.","first60":["Read the referral and Josh's health action plan: cause of learning disability, communication level, epilepsy, current medicines and last annual health check.","Check the GP record for recent appointments, new prescriptions or bloods.","Confirm Josh has agreed to Declan sharing information, or that it is in his best interests if he lacks capacity for that decision.","Expect a tired, shaken carer; acknowledge the assault on his colleague early.","Hold the frame: baseline, change, body, mind, environment, then risk to Josh, to others and from others."],"ask":["'What is Josh like when he's well? How does he communicate?'","'What exactly has changed, and when did you first notice?'","'How is he sleeping and eating? Has he lost weight?'","'Any signs of pain: holding his tummy, jaw or ear, or not opening his bowels?'","'Has anything changed around him: staff, visitors, the day centre?'","'Has he hurt himself, or said anything about not wanting to live?'","'Any bruises, fear of a particular person, or changes after visits?'","'Has he heard voices, been unusually high, or done anything odd?'","'What happened when he hit out? What was going on just before?'"],"recognise":["Withdrawal, tearfulness, broken sleep, poor appetite, weight loss and loss of interest over six weeks point to depression, often shown through behaviour rather than words.","Diagnostic overshadowing: blaming change on the learning disability and missing a physical cause.","Pain, constipation, dental problems, ear infections and reflux commonly present as distress, aggression or head-banging.","Loss of a valued person, such as a favourite worker or fewer family visits, is a common trigger.","Abuse must always be asked about directly, even with no obvious signs.","Head-banging is a common form of self-harm and a sign of unmet need, not just 'behaviour'."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the collateral and risk assessment, ask the GP to review the constipation today, plan blood tests and a physical review, treat probable depression, and agree a positive behaviour support approach and safety steps with the home.","r":"Right: it catches the urgent physical problem, treats the likely depression, and gives staff a plan they can use tonight.","best":true},{"c":"Start risperidone 0.5 mg twice daily to stop the aggression.","r":"Wrong: antipsychotics should not be used for behaviour without a clear cause and other options tried first (STOMP). They would also mask pain and depression.","best":false},{"c":"Put it down to his learning disability and autism and advise staff to give him more space.","r":"Wrong: this is diagnostic overshadowing. A clear six-week change in a cheerful man needs physical and mental health causes found.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assess Josh's capacity for each decision (tests, treatment); if he lacks it, decide in his best interests after consulting his mother and staff.","Safeguarding adults (Care Act 2014): ask about abuse; raise a concern with the local authority if any suspicion arises.","STOMP: psychotropics should not be used to manage behaviour without a clear reason and regular review.","Any restraint by staff must be proportionate, recorded and part of a behaviour support plan."],"say":[{"to":"Key worker","s":"You know Josh better than anyone in this room, so your view really matters. Tell me what you've noticed, and then I'll ask some more detailed questions."},{"to":"Key worker","s":"From what you've told me, this sounds most like depression. But constipation and pain can show up exactly like this in people who can't easily tell us, so we sort that first."},{"to":"GP","s":"Josh Okonkwo-Banks, 32, moderate LD. Six weeks of withdrawal, tearfulness, poor sleep, weight loss and head-banging. No bowel movement for five days despite laxatives started two days ago. Please review today for impaction. Probable depression; we will follow up."}],"write":"CLDT. Collateral from Declan Fairweather, key worker 2 yrs, with Josh's agreement.\nBaseline: Josh (32M), moderate LD, genetic cause unknown, autistic traits, no epilepsy. Speaks in short phrases; cheerful, sociable; attended day centre twice weekly; enjoys music and helping in the kitchen.\nChange over 6 weeks: stays in room, speaks less, cries at night, no enjoyment, stopped day centre, skips meals, ~4 kg weight loss. Hit a staff member when prompted out of room (two staff to calm). Head-banging against wall several times, no injury.\nPhysical: GP 2 days ago found severe constipation; laxatives started; no bowel movement for 5 days. No other illness known. No new medication (no regular meds). No dental or ear check recently.\nPsych: no voices, odd behaviour or high mood observed. No suicidal statements or attempts.\nSocial: favourite day centre worker left 2 months ago; mother's visits reduced from weekly to monthly due to work. No staff changes at home.\nAbuse: no bruises, no fear of a particular person, no disclosures.\nRisk: self (head-banging, weight loss, self-neglect); others (one assault on staff); from others (none identified).\nImpression: probable depressive episode; severe constipation needing urgent review; possible pain contributing.\nPlan: GP review today for constipation; bloods, weight, dental and ear check; mood and sleep chart; functional assessment and PBS plan; consider sertraline once physical causes treated; liaise with mother; review in 2 weeks.","make":["Let the key worker tell the story first, then fill gaps with focused questions.","Always establish baseline: you can only judge change against what is normal for Josh.","Ask about the body before the mind: bowels, teeth, ears, pain, weight.","Ask about abuse directly, even when there are no signs.","Cover three directions of risk: to self, to others, from others.","Give staff something they can do tonight, not just a referral."],"next":"The GP finds faecal loading and gives an enema; Josh is more comfortable within a day but stays low. Bloods are normal. He starts sertraline 25 mg, increased to 50 mg after two weeks, and the team psychologist begins adapted behavioural activation. His mother agrees to fortnightly video calls. Eight weeks later he is back at the day centre and the head-banging has stopped.","marks":["Acknowledged Declan's worry and the assault on staff, and set out the plan for the conversation.","Established Josh's baseline personality, communication and routine.","Took a clear six-week history of change with biological features.","Screened physical causes: constipation, pain, teeth, ears, medication.","Explored psychological and social triggers, including losses, and asked directly about abuse.","Assessed risk to Josh, to others and from others, and gave staff clear next steps."],"pitfalls":["Attributing everything to the learning disability (diagnostic overshadowing).","Missing the constipation, which can become dangerous.","Not asking about abuse directly.","Reaching for an antipsychotic for the aggression.","Ignoring Declan's own distress and his colleagues' safety."],"refs":["1. NICE NG54 Mental health problems in people with learning disabilities (2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG11 Challenging behaviour and learning disabilities (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England STOMP: stopping over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Constipation (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK learning disability services, carers are expected partners. The term 'diagnostic overshadowing' is widely used, and LeDeR reviews have shown that missed constipation and other physical problems contribute to early deaths.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-20","n":1320,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🧩","title":"Down's syndrome: forgetful, low and getting lost","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 10:30","where":"Community learning disability team clinic","text":"Owen Brightwell, a 39-year-old man with Down's syndrome, has become low and forgetful over the past month. His mother and carer, Bernadette Lyle, 68, asked for an urgent review because the change has been so quick. Owen is having a drink with a nurse. Bernadette sits on the edge of her chair: 'I'm sorry, I'm a bit on edge.'"},"task":"Take a collateral history from Owen's mother to reach a likely diagnosis, covering his baseline, the course of the change, memory, function and mood, physical, sensory and medication causes, seizures and psychosis, risk, and her coping.","first60":["Read the file for any baseline cognitive assessment (often done around age 30), thyroid history, hearing and vision checks, epilepsy and current medicines.","Check the GP record for recent bloods, especially thyroid function, and any new prescriptions.","Confirm Owen is happy for you to talk to his mother; plan to see him yourself afterwards.","Expect an anxious parent who has read about dementia in Down's syndrome; validate before informing.","Hold the frame: baseline, memory and function, mood, physical and sensory causes, seizures and psychosis, risk, her coping."],"ask":["'What was Owen like before this started, at his best?'","'When did you first notice a change, and has it come on suddenly or gradually?'","'What has he forgotten? Has he got lost, or struggled with things he used to manage?'","'Does he still recognise family and friends?'","'How is his mood, sleep and appetite? Does he still enjoy football?'","'Any change in hearing or eyesight? Does he snore or stop breathing at night?'","'Any fits, jerks, staring spells or wetting himself?'","'Any new medicines, illness, or changes at home or the day centre?'","'How are you coping?'"],"recognise":["People with Down's syndrome have a very high lifetime risk of Alzheimer's disease, often starting in their 40s or 50s.","A change over a month is fast for dementia; it raises depression, hypothyroidism, sensory loss, sleep apnoea, infection or medication effects.","Low mood, loss of enjoyment, poor sleep and reduced appetite can produce a 'pseudodementia' that improves with treatment.","New-onset seizures or myoclonic jerks in an adult with Down's syndrome can herald Alzheimer's disease.","Hypothyroidism, hearing loss from wax or cataracts are common and treatable causes of apparent decline.","Compare against his own baseline, not against the general population."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the collateral, see Owen, arrange bloods including thyroid, hearing and vision checks and a sleep assessment, treat depression if confirmed, start serial cognitive assessment against his baseline, and support his mother.","r":"Right: it looks for treatable causes before labelling dementia, and puts safety and carer support in place now.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose Alzheimer's disease today and start donepezil.","r":"Wrong: one month's change is too quick and too mixed with low mood to call. Treatable causes must be excluded and decline shown over time.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her it is just depression and refer to the GP.","r":"Wrong: early dementia is a real possibility in a 39-year-old with Down's syndrome, and he needs specialist follow-up with serial assessment.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assess Owen's capacity for each decision about tests and treatment; involve his mother in best-interests decisions if he lacks capacity.","Locking the front door to stop him leaving may be a restriction; it should be proportionate, recorded and reviewed, and may need consideration under the MCA.","Carers' rights: under the Care Act 2014, Bernadette can ask adult social care for a carer's assessment in her own right.","Safeguarding: ask about abuse or exploitation, even when a medical explanation seems likely."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"It's completely understandable to feel on edge. You know Owen better than anyone, so what you've noticed is exactly what I need."},{"to":"Mother","s":"You're right that people with Down's syndrome are more likely to develop dementia. But a change this quick, with his mood so low, makes me want to look for treatable causes first, like thyroid problems, hearing, sleep or depression."},{"to":"GP","s":"Owen Brightwell, 39, Down's syndrome. One month of low mood, poor sleep, reduced appetite, disorientation to day, misplacing items, difficulty with buttons and one episode of getting lost. No seizures or psychosis. Please check TFTs, FBC, U&Es, LFTs, calcium, glucose, B12, folate and urine; we will assess cognition and mood."}],"write":"CLDT. Collateral from Bernadette Lyle (68F), mother and carer, with Owen's agreement.\nBaseline: Owen (39M), Down's syndrome, mild-moderate LD; chatty, cheerful; dresses himself; helps with simple chores; football at day centre twice weekly. Baseline cognitive assessment done age 30 (on file).\nOver ~1 month: muddles days of the week, keeps losing his comfort blanket, struggling with buttons, got lost outside once looking for the blanket (brought back by neighbour). Recognises family. Low, withdrawn, no enjoyment of football, up walking at night, eating less. Snores loudly.\nNo voices, no odd behaviour, no seizures, jerks or incontinence. No new medicines (none regular). No recent illness, falls or head injury known. No change at home or day centre. Hearing and vision: not checked for 2 years.\nNo self-harm or suicidal talk. No signs of abuse.\nRisk: wandering and getting lost (door now locked), road safety, kitchen, self-neglect. Mother exhausted, arthritis, no regular help.\nFH: no dementia or depression known.\nImpression: depressive episode vs early Alzheimer's disease; exclude hypothyroidism, sensory loss, sleep apnoea, infection.\nPlan: see Owen today; bloods incl TFTs, urine dip; hearing and eye tests; sleep study referral; DSQIID and comparison with baseline; consider MRI; carer's assessment; Herbert Protocol; review 4 weeks.","make":["Settle the parent first: name her worry and say her knowledge matters.","Anchor everything to his baseline; ask whether there is a baseline assessment on file.","Pin down speed of onset: a month is fast and widens the differential.","Ask about thyroid, hearing, eyes and sleep apnoea every time in Down's syndrome.","Ask about seizures and jerks: new fits in adults with Down's syndrome can signal dementia.","Cover risk and carer strain before you close."],"next":"Owen's TFTs show a high TSH and low T4; the GP starts levothyroxine. Hearing tests show impacted wax, which is cleared. He starts sertraline for depression and behavioural activation with the team. Three months later his mood and orientation are much better, and repeat assessment is close to his baseline. The team plans yearly reviews given his dementia risk.","marks":["Validated her anxiety and set out the plan for the conversation.","Established Owen's baseline and any previous cognitive assessment.","Described onset, speed and course of memory and functional change, including recognition of faces.","Explored mood, enjoyment, sleep and appetite.","Screened thyroid, hearing, vision, sleep apnoea, infection, medication, seizures, psychosis and life events.","Assessed wandering, safety and abuse risks, asked about her coping, and explained the differential and plan."],"pitfalls":["Diagnosing dementia on the day because he has Down's syndrome.","Forgetting thyroid, hearing and vision.","Not asking about seizures or myoclonic jerks.","Not comparing with his baseline.","Ignoring an exhausted older carer."],"refs":["1. NICE NG54 Mental health problems in people with learning disabilities (2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists and BPS: Dementia and people with intellectual disabilities (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Down's syndrome (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK learning disability teams often do a baseline cognitive assessment for people with Down's syndrome around age 30, so later change can be measured against it. Ask for it before you assess.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-21","n":1321,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🚔","title":"Police custody: naked in the garden, history and sexual risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20, liaison and diversion","where":"Police custody suite, interview room","text":"Gareth Mulvaney, 47, out of work and separated from his wife last year, was arrested this morning on suspicion of indecent exposure. His neighbour, who works from home and can see his garden from her kitchen, told police he has sunbathed naked most days for a fortnight. He is angry: 'It's my own garden.' The custody sergeant wants a psychiatric view before interview."},"task":"Take a history from Mr Mulvaney, including a sensitive psychosexual history, test whether he believes the neighbour returns his interest, screen for mental illness, and assess the risk to the neighbour, the public, children and himself.","first60":["Ask the custody sergeant and the healthcare practitioner: the allegation, how long he has been in, whether he has seen a solicitor, any alcohol or drugs on arrest, and any risk flags on the custody record.","Check whether he needs an appropriate adult; you will give a view on that and on fitness for interview at the end.","Read any NHS record you can reach: past mental health contact, learning disability, medication.","Sit level with him, no desk between you if it is safe; make clear you are a doctor, not police.","Plan your order: his account, then arousal, then past pattern, then mental state, then risk. Intimate questions go after trust, not before."],"ask":["'Can you take me through what's been happening in the garden these last two weeks?'","'Did you know your neighbour could see you? What did you hope or expect would happen?'","'I need to ask some personal questions. Did you become aroused, have an erection, or touch yourself out there?'","'Do you think about her sexually? Have you imagined going round there?'","'Do you believe she is interested in you? How sure are you, out of ten?'","'Has anything like this happened before, at any age? Any trouble with the police?'","'Have you ever had sexual thoughts about children, or anyone who couldn't agree?'","'How's your mood been since the separation? Any thoughts about ending your life, especially now?'"],"recognise":["Being seen naked was the point: arousal from exposing to an unsuspecting person, with erections and masturbation, is the core of exhibitionism, not a lifestyle choice.","Escalation: a specific known woman, knowledge that she is home alone, and fantasies of going round are a shift from exposure towards possible contact.","Teenage exposure to strangers that stopped during the marriage and returned after the separation: a long-standing pattern unmasked by loss.","Erotomania is excluded if he can accept she may not be interested; a fixed belief that she secretly loves him would point to a psychotic disorder.","Screen mania, psychosis, autism, learning disability, drink and drugs; at 47, a new disinhibited pattern would also raise frontotemporal dementia.","Arrest, shame and a recent separation together raise the risk of suicide after release."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history and risk assessment, advise custody that he is fit for interview with no appropriate adult needed, report the escalating risk to the neighbour, and recommend forensic psychology assessment and a GP letter.","r":"Right: no mental illness explains the behaviour, so the criminal process continues, but the custody team and police need the risk picture to protect the neighbour.","best":true},{"c":"Tell the sergeant it is a lifestyle choice and no risk, as it was on his own property.","r":"Wrong: the motive was sexual arousal from being seen by a specific woman, with contact fantasies. Missing that understates the risk to her.","best":false},{"c":"Recommend Mental Health Act assessment and divert him to hospital.","r":"Wrong: there is no mental disorder needing hospital care. Paraphilic behaviour without illness is managed through the justice system and psychological treatment.","best":false}],"law":["Exposure under the Sexual Offences Act 2003 (section 66) requires intent that someone sees the genitals and is caused alarm or distress; the police and CPS decide the charge, not you.","PACE Code C: an appropriate adult is needed if a detained person may be mentally vulnerable; fitness for interview is a judgement you advise on, recorded clearly.","Confidentiality is limited here: tell him at the start that information pointing to serious risk to others will be shared with the police and custody team.","If convicted, notification requirements and MAPPA may follow; this is for the court and police, but your risk opinion informs it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to judge you or decide what the police do. I'm a doctor, and I want to understand what's been happening so you get the right help."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Some of my next questions are personal, and I ask everyone in this situation the same things. Answer as honestly as you can."},{"to":"Custody sergeant","s":"No mental illness explains this. He's fit for interview and doesn't need an appropriate adult. But the behaviour is sexually motivated, aimed at the neighbour, with fantasies of going round, so please factor that into her safety and any bail conditions."}],"write":"L&D custody assessment 14:20. 47M, arrested ?indecent exposure.\nAccount: naked in garden most days x2/52, aware neighbour sees from kitchen, works from home alone. Initially framed as freedom; later: aroused by idea of her seeing him, erections, recent masturbation in garden. Fantasies of going round; has not approached. Thought she 'might like it'; accepts she may not and may be frightened (no erotomanic conviction).\nPast: exposed to women in street in teens/early 20s, never caught; stopped during marriage. Heterosexual, high libido, legal online pornography, no interest in children, no other paraphilias. Separated last year.\nMH: no previous contact. Mood 'fed up', no biological features, no psychosis, no mania, no cognitive concern, alcohol rare, no drugs. No SI now; would contact GP if low.\nImpression: exhibitionistic behaviour (likely exhibitionistic disorder); no mental illness.\nRisk: escalating sexual risk to neighbour (specific target, knows she is alone, contact fantasies); low current evidence of risk to children; moderate risk of low mood after release.\nAdvice: fit for interview; AA not required. Police informed of risk to neighbour. GP letter; forensic psychology referral; Stop It Now details given.\nDr Chinonso, L&D","make":["Explain who you are and the limits of confidentiality before any intimate question.","Ask about arousal, erection and masturbation in plain words, once, calmly; vague questions get vague answers.","Test the belief that she is interested: 'Could she just be upset?' separates hope from erotomania.","Map the past pattern across his life, not just the last fortnight.","Name children explicitly in the risk questions.","End with what custody needs: illness or not, fitness for interview, appropriate adult, risk, follow-up."],"next":"He is interviewed with his solicitor that evening and released on bail with a condition not to contact the neighbour or enter his garden unclothed. The GP refers him to forensic psychology, and he calls the Stop It Now helpline the following week.","marks":["Explained role and limits of confidentiality before probing.","Took a clear account of the fortnight in the garden.","Asked directly about arousal, erections, masturbation and fantasies about the neighbour.","Established past exposure, other paraphilias and forensic history.","Tested conviction to exclude erotomania and screened for other illness.","Assessed risk to the neighbour, public, children and self.","Gave custody a clear view on illness, fitness, appropriate adult and risk."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'naturist on my own property' and never asking about arousal.","Asking intimate questions before explaining your role, so he shuts down.","Forgetting the earlier history: the teenage exposures change the picture.","Leaving out children from the risk questions.","Not giving custody the answers they need: fitness, appropriate adult, risk."],"refs":["1. Sexual Offences Act 2003, section 66 (legislation.gov.uk; checked Nov 2026)","2. Home Office. PACE Code C (2023 revision; checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England. Liaison and Diversion Service Specification (2019; checked Nov 2026)","4. WHO ICD-11: paraphilic disorders, exhibitionistic disorder 6D30 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: liaison and diversion teams work in every police custody suite in England. Your job is a health opinion, not a verdict. Write it so the sergeant can act on it in one read: illness, fitness, appropriate adult, risk.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-22","n":1322,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💔","title":"Wants sleeping tablets, certain his partner is cheating: morbid jealousy and risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30, CMHT clinic","where":"Community mental health team, outpatient room","text":"Wesley Thorne, 35, a plasterer, saw his GP a fortnight ago asking for something to help him sleep. What he said worried the GP enough to refer him urgently. He lives with Shona, his partner of three years; there are no children. He sits forward, tapping his foot, and says he only came for tablets."},"task":"Take a focused history to reach the likely diagnosis, test the strength of his belief, screen for other causes, and assess the risk to his partner, to others and to himself.","first60":["Read the GP letter for the exact words that worried the GP, and check the record for any forensic history or previous contact.","Check the room: a clear route to the door, an alarm, and a colleague who knows you are seeing him.","Start with what he wants (sleep) and earn the right to ask about the stress behind it.","Plan to ask about the partner's safety in plain words before he leaves; you will not get another chance today.","Know who to call if the risk is high: your consultant and the duty AMHP."],"ask":["'What's been keeping you awake?'","'When did you first think Shona was seeing other men? What made you think so?'","'How many men do you think there have been?'","'Is there any other way to explain what you've seen? On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?'","'What have you done to find out? Checked her phone, followed her, asked her?'","'You said you'd get the truth one way or another. What do you mean by that?'","'Have you ever been violent to a partner? Any trouble with the police?'","'How much are you drinking now, compared with before all this started?'"],"recognise":["A fixed, unshakeable belief of infidelity built on trivial 'evidence' (a scent, a greeting) with hundreds of lovers: a delusion, not an overvalued idea.","Checking, following, interrogating and demanding a confession are the classic behaviours; escalation to planned force is the danger point.","Searching for injectable 'truth serums' shows planning and a method: treat as high, imminent risk to the partner.","Past violence to an ex-partner is the strongest predictor of future violence to a partner.","Drinking rose after the belief began, so alcohol fuels it but did not cause it; still screen for alcohol-related psychosis.","Screen schizophrenia (other delusions, voices), mood disorder and organic causes before calling it delusional disorder."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell him honestly you are worried about Shona's safety, discuss with your consultant now, arrange urgent review today (Mental Health Act assessment if he declines admission), and make sure Shona is warned and offered domestic abuse support.","r":"Right: a delusion, planned force, a researched method and past partner violence make the risk serious and imminent. Her safety outweighs his confidentiality.","best":true},{"c":"Prescribe zopiclone 7.5 mg for sleep and review in four weeks.","r":"Unsafe: it treats the complaint, not the illness, and sends him home with intent and a plan towards his partner.","best":false},{"c":"Keep it confidential, start risperidone and ask him to return next week.","r":"Unsafe: medication takes weeks to work and he has not agreed to it. Confidentiality does not stand when someone faces a serious risk of harm.","best":false}],"law":["GMC confidentiality guidance: disclose without consent when it is needed to protect someone from serious harm or death; tell him if safe to do so, and record the reasons.","If he declines admission and the risk is high, request a Mental Health Act assessment (AMHP and two doctors); Section 2 allows assessment for up to 28 days.","Domestic abuse: complete a DASH risk checklist with the partner where possible; high-risk cases go to MARAC and an IDVA.","Police: share information when there is a credible threat of serious violence; the decision should involve your consultant and be documented."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see you're exhausted and desperate for sleep. I'd like to understand the stress behind it properly, because tablets alone won't fix that."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I have to be honest with you. What you've told me makes me worried for Shona's safety, and for yours. I can't keep that to myself."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"35-year-old man with a six-month fixed belief his partner has had hundreds of affairs. Checking and following her, plans to force a confession, has researched injectable truth serums, past ABH on an ex-partner. I think he needs a same-day review and she needs warning."}],"write":"CMHT urgent assessment 10:30. 35M, plasterer, lives with partner Shona (3 yrs), no children.\nReferred by GP: poor sleep, stress.\nHx: 6/12 fixed belief Shona unfaithful. Started with smell of aftershave on her coat; now 'flirting' with every man, e.g. greeting to postman today. Estimates hundreds of lovers. No alternative explanation accepted; 100% certain. Checks her phone, follows her, repeated confrontations; she denies. Plans to get a confession 'one way or another': would use force; has searched online for injectable truth serums.\nNo intent towards suspected lovers. No other delusions, no hallucinations, mood 'stressed', not depressed or elated. No SI/SH. Alcohol 5–6 cans/day, increased since belief began. No drugs. Off sick from work to watch her. No weapons.\nForensic: 2-year prison sentence for ABH on ex-partner; restraining order.\nImpression: delusional disorder, jealous type (morbid jealousy). No insight.\nRisk: HIGH, imminent, to partner (delusion, planned force, method researched, past partner violence, alcohol). Low to others. Low to self.\nPlan: discussed with consultant 11:05; same-day consultant review; declined admission, MHA assessment requested; Shona to be informed today by team; DASH and MARAC referral; police informed per consultant. No hypnotics.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Start with sleep, then the stress, then the belief: let him tell it before you test it.","Test the belief gently: alternative explanations and how sure he is, out of a hundred.","Follow the behaviour ladder: suspicion, checking, following, confronting, threats, plan, method.","Pick up the cue 'one way or another' and ask exactly what he means.","Ask about past violence to partners and police contact every time.","Never leave with the partner unaware of a credible plan to harm her."],"next":"The consultant sees him at 13:00. He declines admission and is assessed under the Mental Health Act that afternoon and admitted on Section 2. Shona is told by the team, meets an IDVA, and the case is heard at MARAC the following week.","marks":["Linked the sleep complaint to the stress and explored the belief.","Tested conviction and alternative explanations.","Elicited checking, following, confrontation and the plan to use force.","Asked directly about method, weapons and past partner violence.","Screened for other psychosis, mood disorder and alcohol as a cause.","Explained the concern honestly and acted the same day to protect the partner."],"pitfalls":["Prescribing a hypnotic and missing the delusion.","Arguing with the belief instead of testing it.","Letting 'one way or another' pass without asking what he means.","Not asking about past violence to partners.","Promising confidentiality, then needing to break it."],"refs":["1. GMC. Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. SafeLives. Dash risk checklist and MARAC guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. WHO ICD-11: delusional disorder 6A24 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, warning a partner at risk is not optional when the threat is credible. Talk to your consultant, record the reasons, and use domestic abuse pathways (DASH, MARAC, IDVA) as well as the police.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-23","n":1323,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🪲","title":"Day three after back surgery: frightened, shaking and seeing insects","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:45, liaison bleep","where":"Orthopaedic ward, bay by the nurses' desk","text":"Margaret Penrose, 64, a retired bank manager, is three days after lumbar decompression surgery. Over the last few hours she has become suddenly confused, agitated and shaky. She is restless in her chair, stamping on the floor, and has shouted out twice. The staff nurse says, 'She was chatting normally yesterday. Can you see her now?'"},"task":"Carry out a mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis: calm and engage her, describe what you observe, elicit the perceptual disturbances and cognitive change, assess risk, take the alcohol history, and name the likely diagnosis and immediate steps.","first60":["Ask the nurse for the latest observations and NEWS2 score: temperature, pulse, BP, sats, glucose, and when they last changed.","Check the drug chart: opioids, anticholinergics, sedatives, missed regular medicines, and whether any alcohol withdrawal protocol was ever started.","Ask about pain, bowels, urine output, catheter and wound: common causes of delirium after surgery.","Approach slowly, at her eye level, in good light, with a nurse beside you; lower the noise around the bed.","Plan to start with reassurance, not questions: a frightened person cannot answer until she feels safe."],"ask":["'Margaret, you're safe. I'm a doctor. Can you look at me for a moment?'","'What are you seeing? Where are they? Are they there now?'","'Can you feel anything on your skin? Do you hear anything others can't?'","'How are you feeling in yourself? Frightened? Any sense that something terrible is about to happen?'","'Any urge to hurt yourself, or to get away from here?'","'What's the date today? What is this place, and who am I?'","'When did you last have a drink? How much would you usually drink in a day?'","'Have you ever had the shakes, fits or seen things when you stopped before?'"],"recognise":["Acute onset over hours, fluctuating, with poor attention and disorientation: delirium until proven otherwise, not a new psychosis.","Vivid visual hallucinations of small moving creatures plus a crawling feeling on the skin (tactile hallucinations) point to alcohol withdrawal delirium.","Coarse tremor, sweating, restlessness, fear and a fast pulse: autonomic overactivity of severe withdrawal.","Heavy daily drinking for years with the last drink the day before surgery puts her right in the window (usually 48–96 hours) for delirium tremens.","Look for other causes alongside: infection, pain, urinary retention, constipation, opioids, low sodium, low glucose, low magnesium.","Confusion, unsteadiness or eye movement problems in a heavy drinker: treat as Wernicke's encephalopathy until shown otherwise."],"decide":[{"c":"Name delirium tremens as the likely diagnosis, tell the orthopaedic team it is a medical emergency, recommend lorazepam per protocol, IV Pabrinex before any glucose, bloods and fluids, close observation in a quiet side room, and look for other causes.","r":"Right: untreated DTs can kill through seizures, arrhythmia and dehydration. Benzodiazepines and thiamine treat the cause; the physical search catches anything added on top.","best":true},{"c":"Start haloperidol for the hallucinations and agitation.","r":"Wrong as first step: an antipsychotic alone does not treat withdrawal and lowers the seizure threshold. NICE advises benzodiazepines first; antipsychotics only if symptoms persist.","best":false},{"c":"Recommend transfer to the psychiatric ward for her psychosis.","r":"Wrong: this is a medical emergency with a physical cause. She needs medical care, monitoring and possibly HDU, not a mental health bed.","best":false}],"law":["She lacks capacity to consent to treatment right now; treat her under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, acting in her best interests, recording the assessment and the reasons.","Restraint must be the least restrictive option and proportionate (MCA section 6); if she needs continuous supervision and is not free to leave over days, consider a DoLS authorisation.","The Mental Health Act is not the route for treating a physical cause of delirium on a general ward.","Talk to her husband: he can tell you her drinking history and her usual self, which feeds the best-interests decision."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Margaret, you're safe. I'm a doctor, and I'm staying here with you. I can't see the insects, but I believe you're seeing them, and I know how frightening that is."},{"to":"Staff nurse","s":"She has delirium tremens until proved otherwise: heavy daily drinking, last drink four days ago, visual and tactile hallucinations, tremor, disorientation. Please get a full set of obs now and bleep the orthopaedic registrar; I'll write the plan."},{"to":"Husband","s":"This is a serious form of alcohol withdrawal. It's treatable, and the medical team are starting treatment now. It's no one's fault that it wasn't spotted before the operation, but it's important we know now."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry 16:45. 64F, day 3 post lumbar decompression.\nAppearance/behaviour: sweating, coarse tremor of hands, restless, stamping at floor, frightened, intermittent shouting. Distractible; settles briefly with reassurance.\nSpeech: rapid, loud when frightened, coherent between episodes.\nMood: 'terrified'; sense of imminent danger. No SI/SH. Wants to get away from insects.\nThought: preoccupied with insects; no passivity, no persecutory delusions about people.\nPerception: visual hallucinations (insects from walls, on floor); tactile (crawling up legs). No AH.\nCognition: disoriented to date, hospital and my role; months backwards failed at Nov; 4AT 10. Events since surgery 'a blur'.\nInsight: none; believes insects real.\nAlcohol: several bottles red wine/day since retirement 4 yrs; last drink day before surgery. No previous psychiatric history. No drugs.\nObs (nurse): HR 118, BP 168/96, T 37.6, sats 97% RA, BM 5.8.\nImpression: delirium tremens. Differentials: delirium from infection/pain/retention/opioids; Wernicke's; less likely primary psychosis or intoxication.\nRecommendations: medical emergency, ortho reg informed 17:05. Lorazepam per CG100 and local protocol; IV Pabrinex 2 pairs TDS before glucose; bloods incl. Mg, PO4, LFT, CK, clotting; IV fluids; NEWS2 hourly; side room near nurses; falls precautions; CIWA-Ar; consider HDU if seizures or unstable. MCA best interests. Husband updated. Liaison review tomorrow.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Settle her first: name, reassurance, calm voice, one question at a time.","Say what you see: 'I notice you're shaking and keep looking at the floor.' Observation counts as part of the assessment.","Acknowledge the hallucinations without agreeing they are real.","Ask about mood and risk even in a confused person: fear can drive her to climb out of bed.","Test orientation and attention briefly; the 4AT takes two minutes.","Ask about alcohol in every acutely confused patient: when was the last drink?"],"next":"The registrar starts lorazepam and IV Pabrinex within the hour. Overnight she has no seizures. By day two the insects have gone, she is oriented, and she agrees to see the alcohol liaison nurse before discharge.","marks":["Calmed and engaged her using reassurance and redirection.","Described appearance, behaviour and speech as observed.","Explored mood, fear and risk to self.","Elicited visual and tactile hallucinations without colluding.","Tested orientation and attention.","Took the alcohol history: amount, duration and last drink.","Named delirium tremens and the urgent medical plan."],"pitfalls":["Firing questions at a terrified woman before settling her.","Telling her the insects are not there, or pretending to see them.","Calling it a psychosis and missing the alcohol history.","Forgetting thiamine, or giving glucose first.","Leaving the plan as 'psychiatry will follow up' instead of treating a medical emergency."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (2010, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG103 Delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management (2010, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. 4AT rapid clinical test for delirium, www.the4at.com (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: Pabrinex and lorazepam monographs (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK hospitals, every acutely confused patient should have a 4AT and an alcohol history. Ask 'When did you last have a drink?' rather than 'Do you drink?': it makes it easier to answer honestly.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-24","n":1324,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🎵","title":"Nursery rhymes through the keyhole: voices a week after stopping drink","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00, CMHT new patient clinic","where":"Community mental health team, outpatient room","text":"Natalia Petrenko, 49, a cook at a primary school, has been referred by her GP because of a recent decline in her mental health, mainly odd beliefs about her neighbour. The letter mentions years of heavy drinking and abnormal liver tests. She is friendly and relaxed, and puzzled about why she has been sent to a psychiatrist."},"task":"Take a focused psychiatric history to establish the likely diagnosis: characterise the experiences, set out the drinking and when it stopped, exclude delirium tremens, a primary psychotic or mood disorder and a medical cause, and check the risk to herself and her neighbour.","first60":["Read the GP letter: the LFT results, what the GP advised about drinking, and the exact dates.","Check the record for any previous mental health contact, prescriptions, or family history of psychosis.","Plan your order: her experiences first, in her words, then the drinking, then the timeline linking the two.","Have the timeline in mind as you listen: when did she stop, and when did the voices start?","Keep an eye on her for tremor, sweating or drifting attention: any sign of confusion changes the urgency."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what's been happening with your neighbour?'","'Where does the singing seem to come from? Is it as clear as my voice?'","'When you open the door and no one is there, how do you make sense of that?'","'Do you hear other voices, or see things others can't? Any sense that people are against you?'","'When did you last have a drink? And how much would you have on a usual day before that?'","'When you stopped, did you get the shakes, sweats, fits or feel muddled?'","'When exactly did the singing start, compared with when you stopped?'","'Have you thought about having it out with him? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'"],"recognise":["Clear, true auditory hallucinations of a recognisable voice, in clear consciousness, starting within days of stopping heavy drinking: alcohol-induced psychotic disorder (alcoholic hallucinosis).","No confusion, disorientation, tremor or sweating now: this is not delirium tremens, which would need urgent medical treatment.","No other first-rank symptoms, no mood disorder, no past psychosis, no family history, onset at 49 directly after stopping: a primary psychosis is unlikely.","A secondary explanation forms around the voice ('he's tormenting me'), which is why the GP heard 'odd beliefs about the neighbour'.","Heavy drinking since bereavement: grief is the driver to address for lasting abstinence.","Usually settles within days to weeks of continued abstinence; persistence beyond that, or return without drink, needs reassessment for another psychosis."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain alcohol-related hallucinations, check she is not confused or in withdrawal, start oral thiamine, arrange bloods, refer to the community alcohol service and bereavement support, and review in two weeks with a low threshold for a short antipsychotic.","r":"Right: the timeline and clear consciousness make hallucinosis most likely; it usually settles with abstinence, and the bigger task is keeping her sober and healthy.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose schizophrenia and start a long-term antipsychotic.","r":"Wrong: one week after stopping heavy drinking, with no other psychotic features or history, the cause is alcohol. A long-term label and treatment would be unjustified.","best":false},{"c":"Send her to the emergency department for treatment of delirium tremens.","r":"Not indicated now: she is oriented, not shaking or sweating, and her withdrawal symptoms settled days ago. Safety-net that new confusion or shakes need urgent care.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is an informal outpatient; the plan rests on her agreement.","Confidentiality: ask before speaking to her employer; any return-to-work advice goes through occupational health with her consent.","If she planned to confront or harm the neighbour, the risk to him would justify further action; she does not.","DVLA: alcohol dependence is a notifiable condition for driving; ask whether she drives and advise accordingly."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for telling me about this; it sounds really upsetting at night. I'd like to understand it fully, and also ask about other parts of your life, including drink."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You're not going mad. After a lot of drinking over years, stopping suddenly can make the brain produce voices that sound completely real. It usually fades if you stay off alcohol."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Likely alcohol-induced psychotic disorder (hallucinosis): neighbour's voice singing at night, onset days after stopping two bottles of wine daily; clear consciousness, no withdrawal signs now. Please start thiamine 100 mg three times daily; bloods requested; alcohol service referral made."}],"write":"CMHT new patient 14:00. 49F, school cook, widowed 5 yrs, lives alone.\nPC: hears neighbour's voice singing nursery rhymes through her front-door keyhole at night for ~4 days; opens door, no one there. Clear, 'like you talking', recognises his voice. Puzzled why he would torment her; good relationship before. Not confronted him, no intention to. No other voices, no VH, no tactile; no thought interference, passivity or delusional perception; no persecutory beliefs beyond the explanation of the singing.\nAlcohol: 2 bottles red wine/day for ~4 yrs, escalated after husband's death. Never 'drunk', no work impact. Stopped abruptly 7 days ago on GP advice (abnormal LFTs). Shaky and nauseous for 2–3 days, settled. No fits, no confusion. Singing began ~day 3.\nMSE: relaxed, friendly, oriented x3, attention intact, no tremor or sweating, mood 'fine', no SI/SH.\nPMH: nil apart from LFTs. No meds. No drugs. No psychiatric or family history of psychosis.\nImpression: alcohol-induced psychotic disorder (alcoholic hallucinosis). DTs excluded clinically. Primary psychosis unlikely.\nPlan: explained; oral thiamine via GP; FBC, LFT, GGT, U&E, clotting, Mg; community alcohol service referral; Cruse; review 2/52, sooner if voices worsen; consider short antipsychotic if distressing or persistent. Safety-net: shakes, confusion or fits = 999/ED.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Let her describe the voice fully before you mention drink: you need the psychopathology clean.","Pin down whether it is a true hallucination: outside her head, clear, in clear consciousness.","Ask 'When did you last drink?' and 'How much on a usual day?' rather than 'Do you drink?'","Build the timeline out loud: drinking, stopping, withdrawal, then the voice.","Rule out delirium tremens with orientation, attention and physical symptoms.","Ask about confronting the neighbour and harming herself before you close."],"next":"The GP starts thiamine and her bloods show a raised GGT and MCV. The singing stops nine days after her last drink. She sees the alcohol service weekly, starts Cruse bereavement support, and returns to work with occupational health support.","marks":["Characterised the voice: modality, source, clarity, content, timing, insight.","Screened for other psychotic symptoms and mood disorder.","Took a full alcohol history with quantity and duration.","Established the timeline from stopping to the voices.","Excluded delirium tremens with physical symptoms and orientation.","Assessed risk to the neighbour and herself.","Named the likely diagnosis kindly and gave a plan."],"pitfalls":["Seeing only a row with the neighbour and skipping the drink history.","Calling it schizophrenia without the timeline.","Not checking for confusion, tremor or fits.","Forgetting thiamine.","Missing the bereavement behind the drinking."],"refs":["1. NICE CG115: harmful drinking and alcohol dependence (2011; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: physical complications (2010, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: alcohol-induced psychotic disorder 6C40.6 (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: thiamine and acamprosate monographs (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients often don't see heavy wine drinking as 'a problem' if they never feel drunk. Ask for amounts in bottles and convert: a 750 ml bottle of 13% wine is about 10 units, so two bottles is about 20 units a day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-25","n":1325,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧼","title":"Cracked hands and HIV fears: planning ERP and CBT for OCD","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:15, general adult outpatients","where":"Psychiatry outpatient clinic","text":"Tobias Mensah, 24, a chef at a busy bakery, has been referred by his GP. His hands are red and cracked from cleaning products. He wipes every surface twice with detergent after each batch, stays late scrubbing colleagues' areas, and washes his hands repeatedly, fearing customers will catch HIV. He has taken fluoxetine for 12 weeks, recently raised to 40 mg, and wants to stay on it. He keeps glancing at the door handle."},"task":"Agree a treatment plan with him, focusing on exposure and response prevention and CBT: check symptoms, impact and risk, explain OCD and how ERP works, plan the first steps, review medication and skin care, and agree practical support at work.","first60":["Read the GP letter: duration, fluoxetine dates and doses, side effects, and any mention of mood or suicidal thoughts.","Look at his hands when you greet him; do not offer a handshake if he seems reluctant.","Keep the room uncluttered and let him choose his seat; acknowledge if the clinic itself feels hard for him.","Plan to check current symptoms and risk briefly before explaining treatment: the plan must fit where he is now.","Have a simple drawing of the OCD cycle ready to sketch with him."],"ask":["'What goes through your mind when you see the counter after a batch?'","'What do you do to make that feeling go away? How long does the relief last?'","'How much of your day does the cleaning and washing take?'","'Part of you thinks it's over the top. How sure are you that HIV could spread this way, out of ten?'","'How's your mood been? Any times you've felt life isn't worth it, or wanted to hurt yourself?'","'If we built a ladder of situations from easiest to hardest, what would be near the bottom?'","'How has the fluoxetine been? Any side effects? Are you taking it every day?'","'Would you like someone at work, or at home, to know about the plan?'"],"recognise":["Intrusive, unwanted thoughts of contamination with HIV, recognised as excessive, plus wiping and washing rituals that bring brief relief: obsessive-compulsive disorder.","Partial insight: he half-believes it; this predicts a good response to ERP.","Cracked skin has become a new trigger: the damage from washing fuels the fear of infection, so skin care is part of treatment.","Two hours a day of rituals, staying late and painful hands mean moderate to severe functional impairment.","OCD with low mood is common: screen for depression and suicidal thoughts every time.","Reassurance-seeking and avoidance are hidden compulsions; they need to come into the plan too."],"decide":[{"c":"Continue fluoxetine 40 mg, refer for CBT with ERP now (more than 10 therapist hours, with sessions at work or by video if the clinic is hard), treat the dermatitis, and agree a work cleaning standard with occupational health.","r":"Right: after 12 weeks of an SSRI with partial benefit, NICE supports adding CBT with ERP. Combining treatments gives the best chance of recovery.","best":true},{"c":"Switch to clomipramine now because the response is partial.","r":"Too early: he has had only weeks at 40 mg and no therapy yet. Clomipramine comes after an adequate trial of SSRIs plus CBT, and needs an ECG.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him to stop cleaning completely from tomorrow.","r":"Unhelpful: sudden total stopping is overwhelming and unsafe in a food kitchen. ERP works step by step, up a ladder he agrees, towards a normal hygiene standard.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity and the plan rests on informed consent; explain that therapy is demanding and can briefly raise anxiety.","Confidentiality: share with his employer or occupational health only with his consent; he decides what they are told.","Under the Equality Act 2010, OCD that has a substantial, long-term effect on daily activities can count as a disability, so reasonable adjustments may apply.","Food hygiene law still applies: the agreed cleaning standard should match his workplace food safety policy, not go below it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thanks for coming, Toby. Your GP told me a bit about the cleaning and your hands. That must be really hard to talk about, and you're not alone with this."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Each time you clean, the fear drops for a bit, so your brain learns cleaning is the only way to feel safe. ERP helps you learn the fear falls on its own."},{"to":"Therapist (referral)","s":"24-year-old chef with contamination OCD (HIV fears), washing and surface-wiping rituals about two hours daily, partial insight, irritant dermatitis. On fluoxetine 40 mg. Requests CBT with ERP; prefers some sessions at work or by video."}],"write":"Psychiatry OPD 11:15. 24M, chef.\nOCD: intrusive thoughts surfaces carry HIV; wipes each surface twice after every batch, stays late cleaning colleagues' areas, repeated handwashing; urge 9/10; ~2 h/day. Partial insight ('I know it's silly but I can't risk it'). Irritant dermatitis both hands; cracked skin now increases infection fears.\nMood: lower over 2 months, sleeping and eating OK. Denies suicidal ideas and self-harm. No psychosis. Alcohol minimal.\nMeds: fluoxetine 20 mg x 8/52 then 40 mg x 4/52, taking daily, mild nausea early only, slight benefit.\nExplained: OCD cycle, ERP (ladder, staying with anxiety without ritual), CBT (responsibility, HIV facts, experiments). Teach-back good.\nPlan: continue fluoxetine 40 mg, review 6/52, could rise to 60 mg. Refer psychology for CBT with ERP (>10 h); requested some sessions at work/by video. GP: emollient, soap substitute, short steroid if needed; dermatology if not settling. OH referral with consent: agreed cleaning standard, glove use while healing. OCD-UK info given. Review 6/52; crisis line 111 option 2 if mood worsens.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Check how things are now before explaining: the plan must fit his current symptoms and risk.","Draw the cycle with him: trigger, thought, anxiety, ritual, relief, and round again.","Explain ERP as a ladder he controls, starting near the bottom.","Name the hidden compulsions: reassurance-seeking and avoidance count too.","Answer his practical questions: where, how often, who, and whether it can be at home or by video.","Check understanding regularly; preoccupied people lose the thread."],"next":"He starts weekly CBT with ERP six weeks later, with three sessions in the bakery kitchen. By session ten he wipes once, leaves on time, and his hands have healed. He stays on fluoxetine 40 mg.","marks":["Checked current obsessions, compulsions, insight and impact.","Screened mood and suicide risk.","Explained OCD and the anxiety-ritual cycle simply.","Explained ERP with a personal fear ladder.","Explained the CBT elements and homework.","Covered medication, skin care and work support.","Summarised, checked understanding and agreed next steps."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing about therapy without checking his current state.","Promising ERP will not make him anxious; it will, briefly.","Telling him to stop all cleaning at once.","Missing low mood and suicide risk in OCD.","Ignoring his request for sessions outside the clinic."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD treatment guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: fluoxetine monograph (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD), treatment (checked Nov 2026)","4. OCD-UK: information and support (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, OCD therapy is often delivered through NHS Talking Therapies for mild to moderate cases, and secondary care psychology for more severe cases. Home, workplace and video sessions are common and fine to offer.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-26","n":1326,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍽️","title":"Bingeing and vomiting: explaining bulimia nervosa and what treatment involves","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Community eating disorder service","text":"Ms Hannah Mwangi, a 24-year-old account manager at a media agency, has been sent by her GP with two years of evening binges followed by making herself sick, now every weekday. Her BMI is 22 and her observations are normal. She has never seen a psychiatrist. She sits down, looks at the floor and says she is embarrassed to be here."},"task":"Check the pattern briefly, explain the diagnosis of bulimia nervosa and the cycle that keeps it going, outline the physical risks, and agree the treatment options with her. No risk assessment is needed today.","first60":["Read the GP letter: duration, frequency of vomiting, BMI, observations and any blood results already taken.","Check whether potassium has been measured; if not, plan U&Es today before she leaves.","Set a calm, matter-of-fact tone: shame keeps this illness hidden, so your first job is to make it safe to talk.","Hold the order in your head: brief check, name it, draw the cycle, body effects, treatment, close.","Have the Beat helpline details and a self-help leaflet ready to hand over."],"ask":["'Can you walk me through a typical weekday, from waking up to bedtime, in terms of food?'","'During a binge, what do you eat, and how much in control do you feel?'","'Apart from being sick, do you use laxatives, water tablets, extra exercise or fasting to make up for eating?'","'What happens at weekends? Is the pattern different?'","'How much does your weight or shape affect how you feel about yourself as a person?'","'Have you noticed heartburn, swelling in your cheeks, palpitations, dizziness or cramps?'","'What do you already know about bulimia, and what would you like help with most?'"],"recognise":["Binges: a large amount in a short time with loss of control, followed by guilt and shame.","Compensation: self-induced vomiting daily, plus daytime fasting and extra exercise.","Self-worth judged largely by weight and shape, with mirror checking and avoiding meals with friends.","BMI 22 excludes anorexia nervosa; purging excludes binge eating disorder.","Signs of vomiting: swollen cheeks (parotid glands), heartburn, calluses on the knuckles of the hand used to vomit.","Weekends with regular meals and no vomiting show that restriction is driving the binges."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain bulimia nervosa and the restrict-binge-purge cycle, check potassium and give dental advice, and offer bulimia-focused guided self-help with a plan of regular eating, moving to CBT-ED if it is not working after 4 weeks.","r":"Right: this follows NICE, tackles the cycle at its root, and checks the main physical danger today.","best":true},{"c":"Start fluoxetine 60 mg and discharge to the GP.","r":"Wrong: NICE says medication should not be the only treatment for bulimia. Psychological treatment comes first; fluoxetine can be added if therapy alone is not enough.","best":false},{"c":"Advise a strict calorie-controlled diet so she stops gaining weight and no longer needs to vomit.","r":"Harmful: dieting and daytime restriction are what drive the binges. Treatment aims for regular eating, not weight loss.","best":false}],"law":["She is an adult with capacity: treatment is voluntary, and her consent is needed to share information with family or her employer.","Confidentiality: she can choose whether to tell work; occupational health referral only with her agreement.","Driving is not normally affected by bulimia, but fainting or arrhythmia from low potassium would be: advise her not to drive if she has blackouts."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for telling me. Lots of people live with this, and it is very treatable. Nothing you say will shock me."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Skipping food in the day makes your body desperate by the evening; the binge is the body catching up, not a lack of willpower."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: bulimia nervosa. BMI 22. Daily vomiting on weekdays. Please check U&Es today and again in 4 weeks; ECG if potassium is low. Starting bulimia-focused guided self-help with our team."}],"write":"Eating disorder clinic. 24F, account manager. GP referral. No previous psychiatric contact.\nPattern 2 yrs: skips breakfast and lunch, gym at lunchtime, evening binges (dinner then cereal, crisps, chocolate) with loss of control; self-induced vomiting with fingers every weekday. Weekends: eats regularly, no vomiting. No laxatives, diuretics or insulin. Avoids meals out; mirror checking; self-worth tied to shape.\nPhysical: heartburn, swollen cheeks, calluses on right knuckles. No palpitations or fainting. BMI 22, obs normal.\nImpression: bulimia nervosa (ICD-11 6B81). Anorexia excluded (BMI normal); binge eating disorder excluded (purging present).\nExplained: diagnosis, restrict-binge-purge cycle, physical risks, treatment options. Teach-back good.\nPlan: U&Es today; ECG if K+ low or palpitations. Dental advice. Regular eating plan and food diary. Bulimia-focused guided self-help (4-9 sessions over 16 weeks); review at 4 weeks, CBT-ED if not improving. No medication now. Beat details given. GP letter. Review 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Open by lowering shame: a calm, unshockable tone does more than any reassurance speech.","Use her own week to draw the cycle; when she sees it, the treatment makes sense to her.","Name both differentials aloud: normal BMI rules out anorexia, purging rules out binge eating disorder.","Explain physical risk without frightening her: potassium, heart, teeth, throat, glands.","Be clear that treatment aims for regular eating, not weight loss, and say this before she asks.","Give a concrete first step she can try tomorrow: breakfast and lunch, no gap over 3 to 4 hours."],"next":"Her potassium is 3.4 mmol/L; the GP repeats it a fortnight later and it is normal. She completes guided self-help with a therapist, and by week 10 she is eating three meals and two snacks on most days and vomiting once or twice a week. She is booked for a review to decide whether she needs CBT-ED.","marks":["Opened with warmth and a non-judgemental tone that reduced her shame.","Checked bingeing, loss of control, all forms of purging and the weekend pattern.","Named bulimia nervosa and explained why it is not anorexia or binge eating disorder.","Explained the restrict-binge-purge cycle using her own week.","Explained the physical risks and arranged potassium and dental advice.","Outlined guided self-help, CBT-ED and regular eating, without promising weight loss."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing about health risks before making it safe to talk.","Forgetting to ask about laxatives, diuretics, exercise and insulin misuse.","Offering fluoxetine as the main treatment.","Promising that treatment will make her lose weight.","Not checking potassium in someone vomiting daily."],"refs":["1. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Fluoxetine, indications and dose (checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11 6B81 Bulimia nervosa (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Bulimia (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, bulimia is managed by community eating disorder services, mostly as outpatients. Avoid any comment on her weight or appearance; even 'you look healthy' can be heard as 'you look big'. Beat is the national eating disorder charity patients are signposted to.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-27","n":1327,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚓","title":"Found in the road at 3am: mental state assessment in the place of safety","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 04:20, on call","where":"Section 136 suite, adult mental health unit","text":"Mr Declan Ferris, a 30-year-old man, was found by police walking down the middle of a main road at 3am. He told officers he was getting away from people shouting at him, and he smelled strongly of cannabis. He is on section 136 and has never been known to mental health services. He is sitting in the suite with a support worker nearby, glancing at the door."},"task":"Carry out a mental state assessment: elicit and characterise his psychotic symptoms, explore his cannabis use, check cognition, insight and risk, and summarise what happens next.","first60":["Read the police form: time of arrival at the place of safety (the 24-hour clock starts then), what he said and did, any injuries or weapons.","Check physical safety first: injuries from the road, observations, signs of intoxication or withdrawal.","Ask the support worker how he has been: settled, pacing, talking to himself, looking round as if hearing something.","Sit at an angle with a clear exit for both of you; speak slowly and keep your hands visible. He is frightened, not hostile.","Hold the order: tonight, appearance and behaviour, speech and mood, thoughts, perceptions, cannabis, cognition and insight, risk."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what happened tonight, from when you left your flat?'","'When did you first notice something was wrong? What was the very first thing?'","'Who do you think is behind this, and what do they want? How sure are you?'","'Do things on TV, radio or online seem to be about you or meant for you?'","'When you hear them shouting, where does it come from? Can you hear it now, here?'","'Are the voices speaking to you directly, or discussing you between themselves? Do they give you orders?'","'How much cannabis do you smoke, what kind, and when did you last have some?'","'Have you thought of hurting yourself, or of arming yourself against them?'"],"recognise":["Delusional perception: a normal event (a car flashing its lights) suddenly gave a fixed, unrelated meaning (they know he is a 'grass').","Persecutory delusion held with full conviction, growing over three weeks, with delusions of reference via the TV.","Second-person abusive voices, plus voices heard here in the suite, far from their supposed source: an extracampine quality, and clearly psychotic.","Escalating daily high-potency cannabis, used to cope with fear: likely contributor, not proof of a purely drug-induced state.","Orientated with good attention: this is not delirium or acute intoxication.","Risk is mainly accidental harm and vulnerability while fleeing, plus a possible defensive weapon at home."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the mental state assessment, document risk, arrange a Mental Health Act assessment with the AMHP within the section 136 time limit, keep him in the place of safety meanwhile, and request urine drug screen and bloods.","r":"Right: he has a first episode of psychosis without insight and ran into traffic to escape the voices. An MHA assessment is the lawful route to treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Wait until the cannabis wears off, then let him go home in the morning.","r":"Unsafe: symptoms have built up over three weeks and he is still hallucinating while orientated. This is not simple intoxication.","best":false},{"c":"Give intramuscular haloperidol now because he looks frightened.","r":"Wrong: he is calm and cooperative. Rapid tranquillisation is a last resort after de-escalation and oral medication are offered, and he is antipsychotic-naive.","best":false}],"law":["Section 136 allows him to be kept in a place of safety for up to 24 hours from arrival, extendable by 12 hours by the doctor for clinical reasons, for assessment by a doctor and an AMHP.","He cannot be treated against his will under section 136; any treatment needs his consent, or the MCA in an emergency if he lacks capacity.","If detained after assessment, section 2 (up to 28 days) fits a first presentation with no previous assessment.","DVLA: with acute psychosis he should stop driving and tell the DVLA; tell him and record it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're in a safe place, Declan. The police brought you here under a law that lets a doctor and a social worker check how you are. You haven't done anything wrong."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I believe you're hearing them, and it sounds terrifying. I want to understand it properly so we can help you feel safe."},{"to":"AMHP","s":"30-year-old man on 136, arrived 03:40. First-episode psychosis: persecutory delusions, delusional perception, second-person voices, no insight, daily skunk. Ran into traffic to escape the voices. I'm recommending MHA assessment for admission."}],"write":"S136 suite. Arrived 03:40. Seen 04:20. 30M, no previous MH contact.\nAppearance and behaviour: unshaven, T-shirt despite cold, no injuries. Hypervigilant, glances at door, pauses as if listening. Cooperative.\nSpeech: normal rate, coherent; occasional loss of thread.\nMood: 'scared'. Anxious, not low, not elated.\nThought: persecutory delusion (gang believe he is a 'grass', plan to hurt him), full conviction, 3 weeks; delusional perception (car flashing lights); referential delusions (TV news warnings). No passivity or thought interference.\nPerception: 2nd-person abusive and threatening voices, several men, heard in flat and here in suite; no commands. No other modalities.\nSubstances: skunk daily, ~5 joints/day, increasing since job loss 2 months ago; last joint 22:00. Alcohol 4 cans/wk. No other drugs.\nCognition: orientated x3, attention intact. Not intoxicated now.\nInsight: absent; agrees he needs help to feel safe.\nRisk: accidental harm (ran into road), vulnerability, hammer by front door for protection, no intent to harm others, no SI/SH.\nImpression: first-episode psychosis, likely schizophrenia-spectrum, cannabis a contributing factor; drug-induced psychosis less likely given duration.\nPlan: remain in place of safety; AMHP contacted 04:45 for MHA assessment; UDS, FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, TFT; obs per policy; advised not to drive.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Explain section 136 in one plain sentence early: fear drops when people know why they are there.","Start with his story of tonight, then follow it back to the first odd moment: that is where the delusional perception hides.","For every voice: who, how many, where from, talking to or about him, commands, and can he hear it now.","If he pauses or glances away, ask gently: 'Are you hearing something now?'","Ask about defensive weapons: frightened people arm themselves.","Close with a clear, honest next step, not a vague 'we'll see'."],"next":"The AMHP and a section 12 doctor assess him at 08:30, and he is admitted under section 2. The urine drug screen shows cannabis only. The early intervention team picks him up on the ward, and he agrees to aripiprazole after two days. His sister removes the hammer from the flat.","marks":["Explained section 136 plainly and checked for injuries.","Described appearance and behaviour, including responding to unseen stimuli.","Elicited persecutory and referential delusions, conviction and the delusional perception.","Characterised the voices fully and screened other senses.","Quantified cannabis use and timing, and screened other substances.","Checked cognition and insight, then assessed self, others and accidental harm risk."],"pitfalls":["Arguing with the beliefs or telling him the voices are 'not real'.","Calling it drug-induced psychosis without weighing the three-week course.","Missing the defensive weapon question.","Forgetting to ask whether he can hear the voices now.","Leaving out the AMHP and the section 136 time limit."],"refs":["1. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 16: police powers and places of safety (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG58 Coexisting severe mental illness and substance misuse (2016; checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: Psychosis and driving (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England a police cell is not used as a place of safety for adults; the health-based suite is the norm. The AMHP coordinates the MHA assessment, not the doctor. Note the arrival time: the 24-hour clock starts then, not when you see him.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-28","n":1328,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Back in the hostel and withdrawing again: is this an early relapse?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:00, CMHT urgent review","where":"Quiet room in a supported hostel","text":"Mr Marcus Adeyemi, a 43-year-old man with long-standing paranoid schizophrenia, moved into a supported hostel six weeks ago after a three-month admission for relapse. For three weeks he has kept to his room, skipped group meals and avoided his key workers. Staff are worried he is becoming unwell again and have asked the CMHT for an urgent review."},"task":"Take a history of what has happened since discharge, including medication, and assess his mental state for a relapse, its early warning signs, insight and risk.","first60":["Read the discharge summary: diagnosis, medication and dose at discharge, what the last relapse looked like, and the relapse signature if one was written.","Ask the key worker exactly what they have seen: eating, sleep, self-care, medication prompts, smoking in the room, any odd remarks.","Check the hostel's medication record: has he been collecting or taking his tablets?","Sit somewhere he can leave easily; keep the tone of a check-in, not an inspection.","Order: since discharge, medication and substances, warning signs, psychotic symptoms, mood and negative symptoms, risk, plan."],"ask":["'How have things been since you left the ward? What's a normal day like here?'","'How are you getting on with the other residents and the staff?'","'How have you been getting on with your olanzapine? Have you missed any, or stopped?'","'What were the first signs last time you became unwell? Have any of those come back?'","'Has anyone been bothering you or doing things behind your back?'","'Do you hear voices when no one is around? What do they say?'","'How is your mood, and do you still enjoy anything?'","'Any urge to hurt yourself, or to hurt anyone here?'"],"recognise":["Stopped olanzapine a month ago because of weight gain: the commonest route to relapse.","Same pattern as his last relapse: poor sleep and withdrawal first, then suspicion.","Persecutory belief that staff read his post, held firmly; a male voice commenting on what he does (running commentary, a first-rank symptom).","Withdrawal can be protective (avoiding a feared threat), negative symptoms, or depression: ask which.","Self-neglect: takeaway cartons, poor intake, curtains drawn, smoking in his room (fire risk).","Partial insight ('stressed') is a foothold: work with it, do not argue."],"decide":[{"c":"Agree to restart an antipsychotic he will accept, increase care coordinator contact, involve the home treatment team if he worsens, and agree a written early warning signs plan with him and the hostel.","r":"Right: he is in an early relapse after stopping medication, is engaging, and has partial insight. Community treatment is the least restrictive safe option.","best":true},{"c":"Arrange a Mental Health Act assessment today for admission.","r":"Premature: he is engaging, has no intent to harm, and accepts some help. Revisit if he refuses all treatment, deteriorates or risk rises.","best":false},{"c":"Restart olanzapine 20 mg as before and tell him the weight gain is the price of staying well.","r":"Likely to fail: the side effect that made him stop has not been addressed. Shared decision-making gives the best chance of adherence.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal and has capacity to refuse; treatment is by agreement. If he lacks capacity or risk escalates, consider a Mental Health Act assessment.","Share information with hostel staff on a need-to-know basis; ask his consent and record it.","He should have a named care coordinator and a written care and crisis plan, shared with him and, with consent, the hostel."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've done well to get out of hospital and settle here. I'm not here to send you back. I want to help you feel more comfortable."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The weight gain bothered you, and that's fair. Let's find a medicine that keeps you well without that."},{"to":"Hostel key worker","s":"Marcus is showing early signs of relapse after stopping olanzapine. He's agreed to try aripiprazole. Please prompt and record each dose, and call the CMHT duty line if he stops eating or mentions the voice giving orders."}],"write":"CMHT urgent review at hostel. 43M, paranoid schizophrenia, discharged 6/52 ago after 3-month admission.\nSince discharge: settled first 2 weeks; then noise and mess from residents stressful; withdrew to room 3/52; skipping group meals; takeaways.\nMedication: stopped olanzapine 15 mg ~4/52 ago due to weight gain (+12 kg). No cannabis; 2-3 cans beer/wk.\nEarly warning signs: poor sleep and withdrawal (same as last relapse).\nMSE: unkempt, curtains drawn, cartons in room. Guarded but polite. Mood 'stressed', not depressed; no hopelessness. Persecutory belief staff read his post, firm conviction. 3rd-person male voice commenting on his actions for 2/52; no commands. No passivity. Some loss of motivation.\nInsight: partial: accepts 'stressed', agrees medication may help sleep.\nRisk: self-neglect (poor intake, lost weight?), smoking in room (fire), no SI, no intent to harm staff, may leave hostel.\nImpression: early relapse of paranoid schizophrenia after non-adherence.\nPlan: aripiprazole 10 mg mane, staff-prompted; bloods and ECG; care coordinator 2x/wk; HTT if worsens; EWS plan with hostel; fire safety check; review 1/52.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Begin with what has gone well since discharge: it lowers his guard.","Ask about medication without blame: 'Lots of people stop when the weight goes on. What happened for you?'","Compare with last time: his own relapse signature is the most useful tool you have.","For each belief or voice, ask conviction and what he does about it.","Separate withdrawal driven by fear from low mood and from loss of drive.","Close with a plan he has helped make, including the side effect that made him stop."],"next":"He starts aripiprazole 10 mg, prompted by staff. By three weeks the voice has faded and he eats one group meal a day. His care coordinator writes an early warning signs card with him, and a long-acting injection is discussed at his next review.","marks":["Explored the time since discharge, routine and stresses.","Established non-adherence and its reason without blaming him.","Compared current changes with his previous relapse signature.","Elicited persecutory beliefs and the commenting voice, with conviction.","Separated low mood and negative symptoms from fearful withdrawal.","Assessed suicide, harm to others, self-neglect and fire risk, then agreed a plan."],"pitfalls":["Opening with 'Have you stopped your tablets?' and getting a closed door.","Dismissing the weight gain as minor.","Treating all withdrawal as negative symptoms.","Forgetting fire safety and food intake in someone staying in his room.","Arranging admission when community treatment is safe and acceptable."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Aripiprazole, olanzapine (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England Community Mental Health Framework (2019; checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK community teams aim to treat relapse at home where safe. The home treatment (crisis resolution) team can visit daily. Weight gain is the commonest reason people stop olanzapine; switching is routine, not giving in.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-30","n":1330,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"📝","title":"Writing a safety plan together for long-standing suicidal thoughts","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:30, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"Mr Rhys Hollingworth, a 43-year-old man, has had suicidal thoughts on and off for about ten years, usually without a firm plan or strong intent. Many medicines and talking treatments have not helped for long. His GP has referred him back because the thoughts are persisting. His history and mental state have already been reviewed today. He sighs: 'It's the same old thing.'"},"task":"Build a written safety plan with him for the moments the thoughts arrive, and discuss ways he can manage them himself. History, mental state and past treatment have already been covered.","first60":["Read today's assessment: current intent, any plan, means mentioned, alcohol, and protective factors.","Have a blank safety plan template and a pen ready; he should leave with a copy in his own words.","Look up the local crisis line number and crisis café hours so you can write them in, not just mention them.","Plan a collaborative tone: he is the expert on his own crises; you are the scribe and guide.","Order: brief risk check, warning signs, own coping, people and places, professionals, making it safer, close."],"ask":["'Are the thoughts with you today? Any plan or intent to act on them?'","'What usually sets them off? What do you notice in yourself first?'","'When they come, what have you tried? What helps, even a little?'","'Who could you be with, or where could you go, to take your mind off things?'","'Who could you tell if it gets bad? Would you be able to contact them?'","'Do you drink when the thoughts come?'","'What do you have at home that you might use to harm yourself? Could we make that safer?'","'What keeps you going? What are your reasons to stay alive?'"],"recognise":["Chronic, fluctuating suicidal thoughts with no current plan: the job is a usable plan, not admission.","Clear triggers: rows with his ex-wife about access to his son, and drinking alone in the evenings.","Strong reason for living: his son. Write it at the top of the plan.","Means at home: a drawer of old tablets. Removal is the most concrete safety step today.","Alcohol is both a trigger and a disinhibitor: it lowers the barrier between thought and act.","He fears being a burden, so he under-uses friends; name that and problem-solve it."],"decide":[{"c":"Write a collaborative safety plan with warning signs, his own coping steps, distraction places and people, named contacts, crisis numbers and means reduction; agree to return the old tablets to a pharmacy today and to cut back drinking alone.","r":"Right: a written, personalised plan with means restriction is effective, respects his autonomy, and is something he can use at 10pm.","best":true},{"c":"Give him the crisis line number and a leaflet and close the case.","r":"Too thin: a leaflet is not a plan. Without his own warning signs, coping steps and means reduction, it will not help in the moment.","best":false},{"c":"Admit him informally because he has had suicidal thoughts for ten years.","r":"Unhelpful: he has no current plan or intent and has never acted. Admission does not treat chronic thoughts and can erode his coping.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity and is an informal outpatient: the plan is his choice to use; you cannot force him to remove the tablets, only agree it.","NICE NG225: do not use risk scales or tools to predict suicide or to decide treatment; base care on a needs-based assessment and safety plan.","Confidentiality: ask whether he wants his friends or family told; break it only if there is serious, imminent risk."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've lived with these thoughts for a long time and you're still here. That tells me you already know some things that help. Let's write them down."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The plan is like a fire drill: we write it now, while you're steady, so you don't have to think at 10 at night."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Collaborative safety plan agreed today. No current plan or intent. Agreed to return stockpiled tablets to the pharmacy. Please prescribe in weekly quantities and avoid tricyclics. CMHT care coordinator review in two weeks."}],"write":"CMHT clinic. 43M, 10-yr history intermittent SI, no previous self-harm. Referred back by GP.\nRisk today: passive thoughts most evenings; no current plan or intent. Thoughts of overdose at worst.\nTriggers: rows with ex-wife over access to son; drinking alone (4-6 cans) in the evenings.\nWarning signs: pacing, not answering phone, 'what's the point' thoughts, opening a drink early.\nCoping: walk the dog, shower, music, fishing videos, Stay Alive app; delay rule (wait 30 minutes).\nPeople and places: friend Gaz (text), friend Tom; sister in Swindon; football with son Saturdays; crisis café.\nProfessional: care coordinator, GP, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, SHOUT 85258; 999 or A&E if unable to keep safe.\nMeans: drawer of old tablets (sertraline, co-codamol): agrees to return to pharmacy today. Alcohol: agreed no drinking alone after rows.\nReasons for living: son (age 11).\nPlan copy given and photographed on his phone. Review 2 weeks with care coordinator.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Ask him what helps before suggesting anything: his own ideas are the ones he will use.","Write in his words, not yours: 'Ring Gaz' beats 'utilise social supports'.","Make every step concrete: a name, a number, a place, a time.","Means: ask exactly what is at home and agree a specific action today.","Ask about alcohol directly; it is both trigger and accelerator.","End with a copy in his hand and a photo on his phone."],"next":"He returns the old tablets to the pharmacy that afternoon and texts Gaz the plan. Three weeks later he tells his care coordinator he used the delay rule and a walk with the dog after a row about access, and rang Samaritans instead of opening a drink. He agrees to a DBT skills group.","marks":["Checked current intent, plan and protective factors before planning.","Identified triggers and personal warning signs.","Elicited his own coping strategies and added practical ones.","Named people and places for distraction and support.","Listed professional and crisis contacts with numbers.","Agreed means reduction, including the old tablets and alcohol, and gave him a copy."],"pitfalls":["Re-taking the whole history the task said was done.","Lecturing a list of helplines instead of building the plan with him.","Not asking what is at home, or accepting 'I'll sort the tablets' without a when and how.","Leaving out alcohol.","Ending without a written copy he takes away."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. Stanley B, Brown GK. Safety planning intervention. Cogn Behav Pract 2012","3. NHS website: Help for suicidal thoughts (checked Nov 2026)","4. Samaritans and SHOUT: service information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services use collaborative safety plans, not 'no-suicide contracts', which have no evidence and can harm trust. NHS 111 option 2 reaches the local mental health crisis line in England. Write numbers in full on the plan.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-32","n":1332,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🧪","title":"Back from leave, twitching and refusing a drug test","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 17:45, ward doctor","where":"Male acute ward, adult mental health unit","text":"Mr Tyrone Bailey, a 31-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia and a history of using several drugs, is detained under Section 3 and has just returned from four hours' unescorted section 17 leave in town. Nurses say he is making sudden jerky movements, glancing at empty corners and muttering. They suspect drugs, but he refuses a urine sample. The nurse in charge asks you to speak with him."},"task":"Speak with Tyrone, understand why he is refusing, and try to persuade him to agree to a urine drug screen; explore what happened on leave and any risks arising. A full history and mental state assessment are not needed.","first60":["Ask the nurse in charge for his observations since return (pulse, temperature, blood pressure, NEWS2), what was seen, and whether he has been searched.","Check his leave conditions on the section 17 form: was agreeing to drug screening on return written down and explained to him?","Look at his drug chart: antipsychotic, recent dose changes, PRN given, and any past acute dystonia.","See him with a nurse present, in a calm room, door accessible; sit, don't stand over him.","Plan your aim: understand his fear first, then make the case for the test, then agree what happens either way."],"ask":["'The nurses are worried since you came back. How are you feeling right now?'","'What's making you not want to give a sample?'","'What do you think will happen if it comes back positive?'","'Talk me through your leave: where did you go, and who did you see?'","'Did you take anything while you were out? What, and how much?'","'Did anyone ask you for money, or ask you to bring anything back?'","'Are the voices saying anything about the test, or about the staff?'","'Is anything worrying you about going home when you're discharged?'"],"recognise":["Jerky movements after leave: think intoxication (synthetic cannabinoids, stimulants), acute dystonia from his antipsychotic, serotonin toxicity and seizures, not just 'bad behaviour'.","Refusal usually comes from fear: losing leave, being discharged, police, or being judged. Name the fear and you can often address it.","Psychosis can drive refusal: voices or paranoid beliefs about the staff or the sample itself.","Someone sold drugs to a detained patient on leave: think exploitation, debt and safeguarding.","Standard urine screens often miss synthetic cannabinoids ('spice'); a negative result does not rule it out.","A urine test needs consent. You persuade, you don't force; refusal is recorded and informs leave and risk decisions."],"decide":[{"c":"Use a calm, non-judgemental approach to explore his fears, explain why the test helps his safety and treatment, be honest that leave will be reviewed, confirm results are not passed to the police, and arrange physical checks either way.","r":"Right: it respects his autonomy, gives him real reasons to agree, keeps him safe medically and is honest about consequences.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him that if he refuses, all his leave is cancelled and the test will be done anyway.","r":"Wrong: a urine test cannot be taken by force, and threats destroy trust. Leave decisions belong to the responsible clinician after review, not as punishment.","best":false},{"c":"Accept the refusal, document it and leave him on general observations.","r":"Unsafe: his movements and possible intoxication need physical checks and closer observation, and there is a possible safeguarding issue to explore.","best":false}],"law":["MHA 1983 section 17: only the responsible clinician grants or changes leave; leave can be suspended pending review or revoked in writing where necessary for his health or safety or others' protection.","A urine drug screen needs his consent; Part 4 treatment powers do not authorise taking a sample by force. Record the refusal and the reasons.","Confidentiality: results stay within the care team; they are not passed to the police for a positive test alone.","Searches follow the trust's policy and the MHA Code of Practice (chapter 8): proportionate, with consent sought first and reasons recorded."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're not in trouble with me. I'm worried about the jerky movements, and the test helps us work out what's causing them so we treat you properly."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'll be honest: your leave will be looked at by your consultant either way. But a test and being open about it counts in your favour."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He's agreed to a urine screen if he can do it in private. Please do NEWS2 hourly for four hours, an ECG and bloods including CK. He owes money to the man who sold him spice: I'm raising a safeguarding concern."}],"write":"Ward review, 17:45. Tyrone Bailey, 31M, paranoid schizophrenia, polysubstance use history, s3. Returned from 4 h unescorted s17 leave.\nNursing concern: jerky movements, responding to unseen stimuli, refusing UDS.\nSeen with staff nurse. Initially hostile. Fears: losing all leave, police, being discharged to flat with no electricity.\nOn leave: met a man he knows in the park, smoked 'spice' (one joint, ~30 min before return). Paid £20, now told he owes £40 and must bring his tablets next time. Voices louder since; voices told him staff would 'use his urine'.\nObs: HR 104, BP 138/84, T 37.2, RR 16, sats 98%, NEWS2 1. Intermittent myoclonic-type jerks, no sustained posturing, no tongue or eye deviation.\nExplained purpose of UDS (safety, treatment, interactions), that results stay with care team, not police, and that leave will be reviewed by RC.\nOutcome: agreed to give urine sample privately. Sent; requested synthetic cannabinoid testing.\nPlan: NEWS2 hourly x4 then 4-hourly; ECG, U&E, CK, glucose. Increase to intermittent obs (15 min) tonight. s17 leave suspended pending RC review Monday; documented and explained. Safeguarding concern (financial exploitation) to ward manager. Care coordinator re electricity at flat. Dual diagnosis worker referral. Escalate if seizure, chest pain, T >38, reduced consciousness.\nDr N. Chinonso, CT2","make":["Lead with concern for him, not suspicion: 'I'm worried about you' opens doors that 'did you use?' shuts.","Ask why he is refusing before you argue; the fear you uncover is what you need to answer.","Give reasons that matter to him: his safety, getting the right treatment, a fair leave review.","Be honest about leave: no false promises, and no threats.","Look for the hidden risks: debt, exploitation, voices driving the refusal, home problems.","Agree what happens next whatever he decides: physical checks, observation, review."],"next":"Tyrone gives a sample in private. The ward point-of-care test is negative, but the lab later confirms a synthetic cannabinoid. His movements settle over six hours with normal observations and CK. His consultant restarts escorted leave a week later, the safeguarding concern leads to a police intelligence report agreed with him, and the dual diagnosis worker starts weekly sessions.","marks":["Approached him calmly, without judgement, and acknowledged his annoyance.","Explored his understanding of the test and the fears behind his refusal.","Explained clearly why the test matters and that it needs his agreement.","Was honest about how leave will be reviewed under section 17 and that results are not given to police.","Explored what happened on leave, including exploitation, debt and concerns about home.","Considered psychosis driving refusal, arranged physical checks and observation, and agreed a plan."],"pitfalls":["Opening with an accusation or a lecture about drugs.","Threatening to cancel all leave or to take the sample by force.","Promising that leave won't change, then being caught out.","Missing the debt and exploitation behind the drug use.","Forgetting physical observations, ECG and CK for a twitchy, tachycardic patient."],"refs":["1. Mental Health Act 1983, section 17, and Code of Practice (2015), chapters 8 and 27 (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG120 Coexisting severe mental illness and substance misuse (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG58 Coexisting severe mental illness and substance misuse: community health and social care services (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Physicians: National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK wards a positive drug test is a clinical finding, not a crime report. Leave is a treatment decision for the responsible clinician, not a reward. Staff who stay honest and non-punitive get far more disclosure.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-33","n":1333,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"A man in his phone: new behaviour change in learning disability and autism","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30, community clinic","where":"Community intellectual disability team clinic","text":"Mr Callum Reid, a 26-year-old man with mild intellectual disability and autism, lives in a supported living flat with daily staff visits. Over about five weeks staff have noticed him pacing at night, shouting at the wall, refusing to use his kitchen and missing his garden centre placement. He has come with his support worker, Gemma, who waits outside unless he asks for her."},"task":"Take a history from Callum to understand what is happening now and what may be causing it: psychotic and mood symptoms, physical causes, life changes, substances and risk. A management plan is not needed in the conversation.","first60":["Read the referral and his health action plan or hospital passport: level of disability, communication needs, epilepsy, medicines, past mental health history.","Ask Gemma briefly, with Callum's agreement, how he likes to be spoken to and what has changed, then let him speak for himself.","Use a quiet room with little noise or clutter; tell him how long it will take and that he can have breaks.","Plan short, single, concrete questions; avoid idioms and 'how do you feel about...' openers that are too vague for him.","Keep the structure in mind: what changed, psychosis, mood and anxiety, body, life events and substances, risk."],"ask":["'What has been different for you in the last few weeks?'","'Tell me about the man. When did you first meet him?'","'How sure are you that the man is real? A bit sure, or completely sure?'","'When you hear him, is it a real voice in your ears, or a thought in your head?'","'Does anything hurt? Your teeth, your tummy, your head?'","'Has anyone new come to your flat?'","'Do you smoke anything, or drink alcohol?'","'Have you hurt anyone, or yourself, or thought about it?'"],"recognise":["Psychosis in learning disability can show first as behaviour change: pacing, shouting at walls, avoiding rooms, poor eating and sleep.","A fixed, firmly held belief with voices heard through the walls is psychotic, not autistic literalness; check conviction and whether he acts on it.","Rule out pain and physical illness first: dental pain, constipation, infection, seizures and new medicines commonly drive behaviour change.","Change of routine (placement ending), a new neighbour, and cannabis from someone who visits his flat are possible triggers.","Someone who 'gives' cannabis and then takes money from a vulnerable adult is a safeguarding concern: think exploitation and cuckooing.","Compare everything with his autistic baseline: what is new, not what is just him."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a careful history in plain, concrete language, one question at a time, cover psychosis, mood, body, life events, substances and risk, then gain consent for collateral from his support worker.","r":"Right: it lets Callum give his own account, finds treatable causes and risks, and fills gaps from someone who knows his baseline.","best":true},{"c":"Take the history mainly from the support worker because Callum has a learning disability.","r":"Wrong: he has mild disability and can describe his experiences. Talk to him first; use staff for collateral, not as a replacement.","best":false},{"c":"Label the shouting as challenging behaviour linked to autism and refer for a behaviour plan.","r":"Too narrow: new voices and a fixed belief point to psychosis, and pain or substances must be excluded first.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: presume capacity; adapt your communication before concluding he lacks it.","Equality Act 2010: make reasonable adjustments, such as longer appointments, plain language, easy-read information and a trusted person present.","Care Act 2014 section 42: if he may be exploited by someone visiting his flat, raise a safeguarding concern with the local authority.","Confidentiality: ask Callum before you talk to his support worker, and tell him what you will share."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm going to ask some questions. If a question is confusing, tell me and I'll ask it another way. We can stop for a break any time."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I believe the voice is really upsetting you. I want to understand it properly so we can help it get quieter."},{"to":"Support worker","s":"Callum has said I can talk with you. Could you tell me what his sleep, eating and behaviour were like before all this started, and who visits his flat?"}],"write":"CLDT clinic. Callum Reid, 26M. Mild ID, autism. Supported living, daily staff. Seen alone first (support worker Gemma outside), then jointly with consent.\nReferral: 5 weeks pacing at night, shouting at wall, not using kitchen, eating crisps only, missed garden centre placement.\nHistory (Callum): man on the bus put a 'chip' in his phone about 6 weeks ago; hears the man's voice through the bedroom wall at night, outside his head, saying 'we're watching you' and his name; certain it is real. Stopped using kitchen because the man listens through the cooker. Sleeping 3-4 hours. Frightened, not sad; still enjoys train videos. No thought interference described.\nTriggers: garden centre placement ended for winter 7 weeks ago; new neighbour; 'Kai' from the bus stop visits, gives him 'smoke' (cannabis) most days and takes money from his card.\nPhysical: no pain, bowels normal, no seizures, no new medicines. Hearing and vision fine.\nRisk: punched the wall once (bruised knuckles). No thoughts of harming others or self. Vulnerable: financial exploitation by Kai.\nCollateral (Gemma): baseline settled and routine-bound; ate well; changes began after placement ended and Kai appeared.\nImpression: probable first-episode psychosis, possibly cannabis-related, in context of routine loss; exploitation concern.\nNext: bloods, urine drug screen, consultant review this week, safeguarding concern today, staff diary.\nDr N. Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask one short, concrete question at a time, and wait; silence is thinking time.","Use his words: if he calls it 'the man', you call it 'the man'.","Check conviction and action: 'How sure are you?' and 'What have you done because of it?'","Ask about the body every time: pain, bowels, teeth, fits, new tablets.","Ask who comes to his flat and who has his money; exploitation hides in plain sight.","Close with a short summary, check he agrees, and ask permission for collateral."],"next":"Bloods are normal and the urine drug screen is positive for cannabis. The consultant confirms a first episode of psychosis and starts risperidone 0.5 mg at night with easy-read information. A safeguarding enquiry stops Kai's visits, the garden centre offers an indoor winter placement, and after six weeks Callum says the man is 'very quiet now'.","marks":["Adapted language: short, single, concrete questions, offered breaks and checked understanding.","Clarified what has changed, when it started and how it compares with his usual self.","Explored the voice and the belief, including conviction and actions taken.","Asked about mood, anxiety, sleep and eating.","Looked for physical, life-event and substance causes, including exploitation.","Assessed risk to self and others and vulnerability, summarised and sought collateral with consent."],"pitfalls":["Talking to the support worker instead of Callum.","Long or multiple questions, idioms and jargon.","Writing it off as 'challenging behaviour' or autism without exploring psychosis.","Forgetting pain, constipation, seizures and medicines.","Missing the person who supplies cannabis and takes his money."],"refs":["1. NICE NG54 Mental health problems in people with learning disabilities: prevention, assessment and management (2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG11 Challenging behaviour and learning disabilities (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS England: STOMP (stopping over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both) (checked Nov 2026)","4. Care Act 2014, section 42 safeguarding enquiries (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services expect you to speak with the person with learning disability first and adapt how you talk, not to take the history from carers over their head. 'Diagnostic overshadowing', blaming new symptoms on the disability, is a well-known cause of missed illness.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-35","n":1335,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧼","title":"OCD taking over: obsessions, rituals and their cost","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00, outpatient clinic","where":"General adult psychiatry outpatient clinic","text":"Ms Priya Sharma, a 35-year-old woman, is referred by her GP. She has had obsessional symptoms for many years, but they have become much worse over recent months and she is struggling to keep up with work and home life. She is a pharmacy technician and lives with her partner and their three-year-old daughter. The GP letter mentions cracked hands and 'germ worries'."},"task":"Take a history of Priya's obsessional thoughts and compulsive behaviours, how they began and worsened, how they affect her daily functioning, comorbidity and risk, then summarise and explain the likely diagnosis.","first60":["Read the GP letter: duration, past treatment, current medicines, any mention of mood or harm thoughts.","Set a non-judgemental tone: people with OCD often hide the content of thoughts they find shameful.","Plan to normalise early: 'many people have unwanted thoughts; I won't be shocked by anything you tell me'.","Keep the structure in mind: obsessions, compulsions, course, impact and severity, comorbidity, risk, summary.","Have the Yale-Brown (Y-BOCS) domains ready: time, interference, distress, resistance, control."],"ask":["'Do any upsetting thoughts or pictures keep coming into your mind, even when you try to push them away?'","'Where do those thoughts come from: your own mind, or somewhere outside?'","'What do you do to make the worry go away? How long does that take each day?'","'What happens if you try not to do it?'","'When did this start, and what changed recently?'","'What does it stop you doing, at work and at home?'","'Some people with OCD get thoughts of harming someone they love. Has anything like that happened to you?'","'What's your mood been like? Have you ever wished you weren't here?'"],"recognise":["Obsessions: intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges, recognised as her own, resisted and distressing.","Compulsions: washing, cleaning, checking, reassurance-seeking and mental rituals that ease anxiety briefly and keep the cycle going.","Harm thoughts in OCD are unwanted and horrify the person; they lead to avoidance, not action. This is not a child protection risk on its own.","Thoughts recognised as her own and as excessive separate OCD from psychosis; content that is a realistic worry about life separates GAD.","Depression often follows OCD; ask about mood and suicidal thoughts every time.","Severity matters for treatment: time spent, interference, distress, resistance and control (Y-BOCS)."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a full history of obsessions, compulsions, course, impact, comorbidity and risk, reassure her about the harm thoughts, explain OCD and its treatments briefly, and arrange follow-up.","r":"Right: it captures the evidence for the diagnosis and its severity, removes shame and opens the door to treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Make a safeguarding referral for her daughter because she has thoughts about harming her.","r":"Wrong: ego-dystonic harm thoughts in OCD, with avoidance and no intent, are not a risk to the child. A referral would add shame and harm. Consider safeguarding only if the child's needs are not being met.","best":false},{"c":"Focus on the washing only and skip the thoughts because they are distressing to discuss.","r":"Incomplete: the obsessions drive the rituals, and hidden harm thoughts are often the most disabling. Ask gently and normalise.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: what she tells you stays with the clinical team and her GP unless there is a serious risk to her or others.","Safeguarding: children's needs come first; refer only if her rituals stop her daughter's needs being met, not because of unwanted thoughts.","Equality Act 2010: OCD can count as a disability; she may ask her employer for reasonable adjustments."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Lots of people with OCD get thoughts that horrify them. The fact that they upset you so much tells me they're the opposite of what you want."},{"to":"Patient","s":"This sounds like obsessive-compulsive disorder. It's common, it's not your fault, and it responds well to a specific talking therapy and, if needed, medication."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: OCD, moderate to severe (contamination and harm themes, with washing, cleaning, checking and reassurance). No psychosis. Mild low mood, no suicidal intent. Harm thoughts ego-dystonic; no safeguarding concern. Plan: CBT with ERP and sertraline."}],"write":"Outpatient clinic. Priya Sharma, 35F, pharmacy technician (hospital pharmacy), lives with partner Dev and daughter Anaya (3).\nObsessions: intrusive images of Anaya becoming seriously ill from germs she brings from the hospital; recognised as own thoughts and excessive; strongly resisted; highly distressing. Second theme (4 months): images of giving Anaya the wrong medicine; horrified by them; no intent.\nCompulsions: showers 1 h after every shift; bleaches door handles and light switches nightly; washes hands 30-40 times a day; asks Dev for reassurance many times daily; checks medicine cupboard lock repeatedly; avoids giving Anaya any medicine (Dev does it). About 4-5 h/day. Anxiety rises sharply if she resists.\nCourse: checking from age 19, manageable; guided self-help at 24 helped. Worse over 4 months since moving to ward-based work and Anaya starting nursery.\nImpact: late for work twice (informal warning); in bed after 1 am; hands cracked and bleeding; stopped soft play and visits to friends with children; arguments with Dev.\nComorbidity: low mood 'some days', enjoys time with Anaya, sleep reduced by rituals; no tics; alcohol 2 glasses wine at weekends; eating normal. No psychotic features.\nRisk: no suicidal thoughts or plans; harm thoughts ego-dystonic with avoidance; Anaya's needs met (Dev confirms).\nY-BOCS 27 (severe).\nImpression: OCD, contamination and harm-related obsessions, severe.\nPlan: explained diagnosis; CBT with ERP referral; sertraline discussed; review 4 weeks; OCD-UK leaflet.\nDr N. Chinonso, CT2","make":["Normalise before you ask: people disclose shameful thoughts when they hear you won't be shocked.","Pin down the phenomenology: own thought, unwanted, resisted, distressing, recognised as excessive.","Quantify everything: how many times, how many minutes, how many hours a day.","Ask what she avoids as well as what she does; avoidance is often the bigger loss.","Ask directly and kindly about harm thoughts, then explain what they mean.","Close with a summary, the name of the condition and hope."],"next":"Priya starts sertraline 50 mg, increased to 150 mg over eight weeks, and begins CBT with exposure and response prevention. Dev joins a session on stepping back from reassurance. By week 14 her rituals are down to under an hour a day, her hands have healed and she gives Anaya her Calpol herself for the first time in months.","marks":["Normalised and set a non-judgemental tone before asking about thoughts.","Elicited the obsessions with their phenomenology: own, unwanted, resisted, distressing.","Elicited compulsions, reassurance-seeking and avoidance, quantified in time.","Established onset, course and recent triggers.","Explored impact on work, relationship, parenting, sleep and skin, and comorbidity.","Asked about harm thoughts and suicidal thoughts sensitively, and closed with a clear explanation."],"pitfalls":["Not asking about harm thoughts because they feel awkward.","Reacting to harm thoughts with alarm, or making a safeguarding referral for them alone.","Taking a list of rituals without measuring time, distress and resistance.","Forgetting to screen for depression, tics and psychosis.","Ending without naming the condition or giving hope."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD, treatment guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6B20 Obsessive-compulsive disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) (checked Nov 2026)","4. OCD-UK: information and support (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK clinicians are trained to treat intrusive harm thoughts in OCD as a symptom, not a danger. Showing calm, informed reassurance is a key skill. Use 'OCD' only after the history supports it; avoid saying 'you're just a perfectionist'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-36","n":1336,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧼","title":"Raw hands and a referral for CBT: explaining exposure and response prevention for OCD","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20, outpatient clinic","where":"General adult psychiatry outpatients","text":"Mr Tomasz Nowak, a 30-year-old warehouse supervisor, was diagnosed with obsessive-compulsive disorder in this clinic last month after his GP referred him for excessive hand washing. Your consultant has made a psychology referral for CBT. He has come back because he has no idea what the therapy involves and wants an overview before his first appointment. His hands are red and cracked."},"task":"Find out briefly how the OCD affects him now, explain CBT with exposure and response prevention in plain words, describe how the sessions run, answer his worries honestly, mention medication as an option, and close with written information and next steps.","first60":["Read the clinic letter: diagnosis, severity (Y-BOCS if recorded), mood, any risk, and what the psychology referral asked for.","Glance at his hands as he sits: cracked or bleeding skin tells you how severe the washing is and gives you a practical point for later.","Plan to ask what he already knows and what he most wants answered before you explain anything.","Have a pen and paper ready to draw the OCD cycle and a short fear ladder: a picture lands better than words.","Keep the order in your head: his OCD now, the cycle, ERP, how sessions run, his worries, medication, close."],"ask":["'What have you heard about CBT, and what do you most want to know today?'","'How many times a day do you wash, and how long does it take you altogether?'","'What goes through your mind just before you wash?'","'What happens to the anxiety straight after you wash, and then later?'","'How is it affecting work, home and your mood? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'What worries you most about facing things without washing?'","'How would you explain to someone at home how the therapy will help?'"],"recognise":["Washing gives quick relief, and that relief is what keeps OCD going: each wash teaches the brain the danger was real.","Anxiety rises when he touches a trigger, peaks, then falls on its own if he does not wash. He has never stayed long enough to find out.","Fear of the anxiety itself is the commonest reason people drop out of ERP. Expect it and plan for it.","Reassurance seeking from a partner works like washing: it brings relief and keeps the cycle going.","Cracked, raw skin and bleach use need practical skin advice now; they also show how severe the compulsion is.","Low mood often comes with OCD. Ask, because depression changes the plan and the urgency."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the OCD cycle and ERP in plain words with a drawing, describe the ladder and the session structure, answer his fears honestly, mention sertraline as an option, and give OCD-UK information with teach-back.","r":"Right: he leaves understanding why facing fears works, knowing he controls the pace, and more likely to attend.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him the psychologist will explain everything at the first session.","r":"Wrong: he came for an overview now. People who start ERP without understanding it often drop out at the first exposure task.","best":false},{"c":"Start sertraline today instead, because he seems too frightened for therapy.","r":"Not the best answer: CBT with ERP is the first-line treatment, and fear is expected and workable. Medication can be offered alongside, not as a substitute for explaining therapy.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: therapy is voluntary. He chooses each step of the ladder with the therapist; nothing is forced.","NICE CG31 (OCD and BDD): offer CBT including ERP; for moderate impairment offer more intensive CBT with ERP or an SSRI; for severe impairment, combine both.","Confidentiality: he decides what his employer is told. Occupational health can arrange time off for therapy without details of the diagnosis.","If his partner joins a session, check his consent first."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Each wash brings the anxiety down quickly, so your brain learns that washing kept you safe. That relief is what keeps OCD going."},{"to":"Patient","s":"In therapy you touch something a bit uncomfortable and then hold off washing. The anxiety rises, peaks and then falls by itself. Your brain learns it can cope."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Seen for explanation of CBT with ERP before psychology starts. Understands the rationale; main fear is the anxiety during exposure. Not depressed, no risk. Please consider sertraline 50 mg if he wishes; emollient advice given."}],"write":"OP clinic. Tomasz Nowak, 30M. OCD (contamination, washing). Here for explanation of CBT before psychology.\nNow: washes up to 60x/day, ~3 h total; bleach on hands; dreads touching pallets at work. Insight good. Hands cracked, no infection seen. Mood OK, no SI/self-harm.\nExplained: OCD cycle (thought, anxiety, wash, relief), ERP with fear ladder, anxiety curve, response prevention, homework, 12-20 weekly sessions, outpatient, therapist from psychology. Efficacy and relapse prevention discussed.\nConcerns: anxiety would be too much (graded, his pace), fear of illness, work time off, privacy.\nMedication: sertraline offered as option alongside; he will think about it.\nPlan: OCD-UK and NHS leaflets given; emollient, stop bleach; partner may join a session; review 6 weeks; GP letter. Teach-back good.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Draw the cycle on paper: thought, anxiety, wash, relief, back to thought. One picture beats five minutes of talk.","Use his own trigger as the example: the pallets at work, not 'a dirty object'.","When he says he couldn't bear the anxiety, stop explaining and validate first, then show him the ladder starts low.","Be honest about the discomfort: 'It's hard work, and it's the treatment that works best.'","Give numbers: how often, how long, how many sessions, how well it works.","End with teach-back, not 'any questions?'"],"next":"Tom starts with the psychologist six weeks later. His first ladder step is touching a door handle at home and waiting 30 minutes before washing. By session ten he is moving pallets at work without washing straight after, and he has stopped using bleach. He decides against medication for now.","marks":["Asked what he knew and wanted, and briefly checked his OCD, mood and risk.","Explained the OCD cycle using his own washing.","Explained exposure and response prevention clearly, including the anxiety falling on its own.","Described the fear ladder, session structure, homework and who delivers it.","Answered his fear of the anxiety with validation and a graded plan.","Covered effectiveness, relapse prevention, medication option, support and leaflets, with teach-back."],"pitfalls":["A lecture full of jargon: 'habituation', 'neutralising', 'inhibitory learning' without explanation.","Rushing past his fear of the anxiety, or implying he will be made to do things.","Promising a cure or that the anxiety will not be uncomfortable.","Forgetting to ask about mood and risk before explaining treatment.","Leaving out the role of family reassurance in keeping OCD going."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD, treatment guideline (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Obsessive compulsive disorder, treatment (checked Nov 2026)","4. OCD-UK: Understanding CBT and ERP (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, CBT with ERP is usually delivered by NHS Talking Therapies or a psychology team, not by the psychiatrist. Your job is to explain it so well that he turns up and stays. Patients can also self-refer to NHS Talking Therapies.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-37","n":1337,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚆","title":"Work stress, no help from paroxetine: the train driver who cannot stop seeing it","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:30, outpatient clinic","where":"General adult psychiatry outpatients","text":"Mr Adebayo Coker, a 37-year-old train driver, has been referred by his GP with 'stress causing problems at work'. The GP started paroxetine 20 mg four weeks ago and he says it has made no difference at all. The letter gives little else. He sits forward, jaw tight, and flinches when a trolley clatters past the door."},"task":"Take a history to reach a diagnosis: find what lies behind the 'stress', explore the event and the core symptom clusters, screen for depression, alcohol, drugs and other conditions, review the paroxetine, assess risk and fitness for safety-critical work, and give a plain summary.","first60":["Read the GP letter for dates: when the stress began, sick leave, the paroxetine start date and dose.","Note what you saw in the waiting area: startle, scanning the room, sitting near the door. These are findings.","Plan your key question early: 'Did anything happen around the time this started?' Work stress is often the label, not the cause.","Expect the account of a trauma to be hard to give; allow pauses and do not ask for graphic detail.","Remember he drives trains: alcohol, sleep and concentration are safety questions as well as symptoms."],"ask":["'When did all this begin? Did anything happen around then?'","'Only tell me what you're comfortable with. What happened that day?'","'Does it come back to you when you don't want it to, as pictures, or in dreams?'","'What do you do to avoid reminders: places, routes, people, conversations?'","'Do you jump at noises? How is your sleep, concentration and temper?'","'How much are you drinking now? Any other drugs? Any drinking before a shift?'","'How are you taking the paroxetine, and has anyone offered you talking therapy?'","'Do you ever feel life's not worth it, or think of ending it?'"],"recognise":["Work stress with startle, poor sleep and irritability is a cue to ask about a specific event: a fatality on the line is a known risk for train drivers.","PTSD needs re-experiencing in the present (flashbacks, nightmares), avoidance, and a sense of current threat (hypervigilance, startle), lasting several weeks and impairing function.","Emotional numbing and loss of interest overlap with depression; ask separately about pervasive low mood, guilt and hopelessness.","Drinking to sleep is common self-medication; it worsens nightmares and mood and is a safety issue in safety-critical work.","Four weeks of paroxetine 20 mg is a short trial, and drugs are not the first choice for PTSD: trauma-focused therapy is.","Guilt ('I should have stopped in time') and an inquest or police statement often keep symptoms going."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose PTSD with harmful alcohol use, explain it plainly, refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, advise against driving trains until occupational health review, and agree an alcohol and safety plan.","r":"Right: it names the real problem, starts the treatment that works, and keeps him and the public safe.","best":true},{"c":"Increase the paroxetine to 40 mg and review in four weeks.","r":"Not enough: it treats 'stress' without the diagnosis, leaves out therapy, the first-line treatment, and misses the alcohol and work safety issues.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe a benzodiazepine for sleep and anxiety while he waits for therapy.","r":"Wrong: NICE advises against benzodiazepines in PTSD; with nightly drinking and a safety-critical job they add real danger.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: what he tells you stays confidential, but if he continues to drive trains while unsafe and will not stop, you may need to inform his employer's occupational health; follow GMC guidance and discuss with your consultant first.","NICE NG116 (PTSD, 2018): offer trauma-focused CBT or, for non-combat trauma more than 3 months ago, EMDR; drug treatment if he prefers or cannot engage.","Fit note: the GP issues it; you can advise on fitness for work in your letter.","Ask about an inquest or police statement: legal processes can re-expose him and should be planned around."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing has a name: post-traumatic stress disorder. It's a normal brain's reaction to something terrible, not weakness, and it's very treatable."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The tablets were a reasonable start, but the main treatment for this is a talking therapy that helps your brain file the memory away."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"PTSD after a fatality on the line 5 months ago, with harmful alcohol use (about 55 units/week). No depressive episode; passive thoughts, no plan. Referred for trauma-focused CBT. Advise not fit for train driving pending occupational health. Please review paroxetine with us."}],"write":"OP clinic. Adebayo (Bayo) Coker, 37M, train driver. GP: 'stress', paroxetine 20 mg x 4 wks, no response.\nEvent: 5 months ago a man stepped in front of his train; Bayo braked, saw his face; man died. No injury. Gave statement to BTP; inquest pending.\nRe-experiencing: daily flashbacks of the face, nightmares 5 nights/wk, distress at horns and brakes.\nAvoidance: swaps shifts to avoid that line, won't talk about it, feels numb, dropped football.\nArousal: startle, scans platforms, sleeps 3-4 h, poor concentration, snaps at children. Froze on a platform last week; colleague took over.\nComorbidity: low at times, still enjoys kids; guilt 'should have stopped'. No panic attacks, no psychosis, no head injury. Alcohol ~4 cans/night (~55 units/wk) to sleep; denies drinking before shifts but unsure he is clear by morning. No drugs.\nParoxetine: most days, nausea first 2 wks, no benefit. No therapy offered.\nRisk: passive 'what's the point' thoughts, no plan or intent; protective: wife, 2 children. No risk to others.\nImpression: PTSD; harmful alcohol use; subthreshold depressive symptoms.\nPlan: explained PTSD. Trauma-focused CBT referral. Not to drive trains until OH review. Alcohol: cut down gradually, AUDIT, LFT/GGT via GP. Crisis numbers. GP letter. Review 4 wks.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Ask the one question that unlocks it: 'Did anything happen around the time this started?'","Slow down for the event: short questions, pauses, 'only what you're comfortable with'.","Use his words for the clusters: 'see it again', 'keep away from', 'on edge'. Avoid 'hyperarousal'.","Quantify the drink in cans and strength, and link it to his shifts.","Separate guilt about the event from pervasive depressive guilt.","Raise fitness to drive trains kindly but clearly; it protects him too."],"next":"Bayo is signed off by his GP and seen by occupational health, who arrange support through the rail operator's trauma scheme. He cuts down to two cans at weekends. Trauma-focused CBT starts eight weeks later; by session ten the nightmares have eased and he is planning a phased return on a different line.","marks":["Found the traumatic event behind the 'work stress' and explored it sensitively.","Elicited re-experiencing: flashbacks, nightmares and distress at reminders.","Elicited avoidance, numbing and hyperarousal, with impact on work and home.","Screened for depression, panic, psychosis and head injury, and quantified alcohol and drugs.","Reviewed paroxetine use and assessed suicide risk and fitness for safety-critical work.","Summarised and named PTSD plainly with a clear next step."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'work stress' and running a depression checklist without asking what happened.","Pushing for graphic detail of the death, or rushing him through it.","Not quantifying alcohol, or not linking it to driving trains.","Simply increasing the paroxetine dose and missing that therapy is first line.","Forgetting guilt, the inquest, and the effect on his children."],"refs":["1. NICE NG116 Post-traumatic stress disorder (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6B40 post-traumatic stress disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Paroxetine, Sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Post-traumatic stress disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK train drivers, pilots and others in safety-critical jobs are covered by strict employer fitness and alcohol rules. Advising him not to drive trains until occupational health has seen him is routine and protective, not a punishment. GPs write fit notes; your letter supports them.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-39","n":1339,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"💉","title":"Heroin, street diazepam and a baby on the way: is this dependence?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 13:45, drug and alcohol clinic","where":"Community drug and alcohol service, outpatient clinic","text":"Mr Liam O'Connor, 32, has been sent by his GP because he has used several drugs over some years. He is out of work and lives in a rented flat with his girlfriend, Chloe, who is five months pregnant. He arrives ten minutes early, hood up, yawning, with a runny nose, and keeps rubbing his forearms."},"task":"Take a history of his drug use: each drug, amount, route and cost, the story from first use, the features of a dependence syndrome, complications, past treatment, background and risk, including to the unborn baby. Summarise and outline what help is available.","first60":["Read the GP letter: drugs named, any prescriptions (especially benzodiazepines or opioids), blood-borne virus tests, overdoses and police contact.","Notice the signs as he sits: yawning, runny nose, gooseflesh, restlessness and dilated pupils suggest he is in early opioid withdrawal now.","Plan to state confidentiality and its limits early, including the unborn baby, so nothing later feels like a trap.","Have the dependence features ready as questions in plain words, not a checklist: craving, control, tolerance, withdrawal, priority, and carrying on despite harm.","Have a naloxone kit to hand before he leaves."],"ask":["'What are you using at the moment? Let's go through each one.'","'How much, how often, how much does it cost, and when did you last use?'","'Do you smoke it or inject? Where do you inject? Do you ever share anything?'","'When did you first try it, and how did it build up?'","'What happens if you go without for a day? Describe it in your words.'","'Have you ever overdosed? Have you got naloxone?'","'What treatment have you had before, and what helped?'","'How are you and Chloe getting on? What's the plan for the baby?'"],"recognise":["Dependence is a pattern, not an amount: strong craving, loss of control, tolerance, withdrawal relieved by use, drugs taking priority, and carrying on despite clear harm.","Heroin with street benzodiazepines or alcohol is the classic overdose mix; street 'diazepam' is often a stronger, unknown benzodiazepine.","Overdose risk is highest after reduced tolerance: release from prison, detox, hospital stays, or a break.","Sharing spoons, filters or water carries hepatitis C risk even if needles are never shared.","A pregnant partner is a powerful motivation and a safeguarding responsibility; both can be held together.","Withdrawal signs in clinic (yawning, rhinorrhoea, gooseflesh) confirm physical dependence and help later with buprenorphine timing."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history, confirm opioid dependence with probable benzodiazepine dependence, give naloxone today, offer BBV testing, plan urine screen and assessment for opioid substitution, and agree to involve the midwife and children's social care with him.","r":"Right: it establishes the diagnosis, reduces the immediate death risk, opens the door to treatment, and protects the baby openly.","best":true},{"c":"Prescribe methadone 40 mg today because he is in withdrawal and motivated.","r":"Unsafe: no confirmed urine screen, unknown tolerance and street benzodiazepine use. Start low after assessment and titrate with supervised dosing.","best":false},{"c":"Keep the pregnancy out of it to protect his trust in the service.","r":"Wrong: risk to an unborn child is a safeguarding matter. Explain why and involve him; most parents engage when this is done openly.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: what he says stays within the service and his GP, except where someone is at serious risk, including the unborn baby; tell him this at the start.","Safeguarding: concerns about an unborn child go to children's social care for a pre-birth assessment; inform the midwife with his knowledge and, where possible, his agreement.","Naloxone can be supplied by drug services without a prescription to anyone likely to witness an overdose.","DVLA: persistent misuse of or dependence on opioids must be reported by the driver; ask if he drives."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to judge. I'd like to understand what you use and how it affects you, so the help actually fits."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've told me, your body and your life now depend on heroin. That's called dependence. It's a health condition, and there's solid treatment for it."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Opioid dependence (heroin ~1 g/day, smoked and injected) with probable benzodiazepine dependence (street diazepam). One overdose last year. Partner 22 weeks pregnant: midwife and children's social care informed with his knowledge. Naloxone given. Assessment for opioid substitution next week."}],"write":"Drug and alcohol clinic. Liam O'Connor, 32M. Unemployed. Lives with girlfriend Chloe (22/40 pregnant), rented flat.\nNow: heroin ~1 g/day (~£40), smoked; injects arms 2-3x/wk when short of money; never shared needles, shared spoon/filter once; uses needle exchange sometimes. Street diazepam ('blues') 4-6/day. Crack most weekends. Cannabis occasionally. Alcohol 2-3 cans/day. Last heroin 7am today.\nLongitudinal: cannabis 14, heroin smoked 19 with friends, injecting from 24. Methadone 27-28 (8 months abstinent from heroin), stopped when he lost interest in collection; relapsed after 3 months prison (shoplifting) at 29.\nDependence: craving on waking; cannot cut down; tolerance (needs ~4x first amount); withdrawal within 12 h: sweats, aches, cramps, diarrhoea, gooseflesh, yawning, uses to relieve; drug takes priority over food and bills; continues despite abscess, debt, overdose. Meets ICD-11 opioid dependence.\nComplications: overdose last year (heroin + blues), naloxone by paramedics; groin abscess last year; BBV tests 3 yrs ago negative, scared to retest; low mood at times, no psychosis; debt to dealer; shoplifting conviction.\nRisk: overdose risk high (benzos, alcohol, injecting, uses alone sometimes). Passive thoughts after rows, no plan. Unborn baby: drugs in flat; Chloe does not use.\nO/E: yawning, rhinorrhoea, gooseflesh, restless.\nPlan: naloxone kit and training given. UDS today. BBV tests and hep B vaccine offered. Midwife and children's social care referral discussed and agreed. Assessment for OST next week; keyworker. No driving licence.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Name confidentiality and its limits in the first minute; it makes later honesty easier.","Go drug by drug: amount, frequency, cost, route, last use. Then 'anything else?'","Ask about spoons, filters and water as well as needles.","Get withdrawal in his own words; it is stronger evidence than a yes to a list.","Ask when tolerance last dropped: prison, detox, hospital. That is when people die.","Raise the baby as shared concern, not threat: 'We both want the baby safe.'"],"next":"Liam's urine screen confirms opioids and benzodiazepines. He starts supervised methadone 20 mg, titrated to 70 mg over four weeks, with a slow diazepam reduction. His hepatitis C antibody is positive with detectable RNA, and he starts treatment. The pre-birth assessment finds he and Chloe engaging well; the baby is born at 39 weeks.","marks":["Set out a non-judgemental stance and confidentiality limits.","Took a full current-use history: drugs, amounts, cost, route, sharing, last use.","Took a longitudinal history from first use, with abstinence and relapse.","Elicited all dependence features, with withdrawal in his own words.","Covered complications, past treatment, background and forensic history.","Assessed overdose, suicide and safeguarding risk, summarised dependence and gave naloxone."],"pitfalls":["Asking only about heroin and missing benzodiazepines, alcohol and crack.","Reading the dependence criteria as a checklist without his own examples.","Not asking about sharing equipment other than needles.","Avoiding the unborn baby, or raising it as a threat.","Letting him leave without naloxone."],"refs":["1. NICE TA114 Methadone and buprenorphine for opioid dependence (2007; checked Nov 2026)","2. DHSC 'Orange Book': UK clinical guidelines for drug misuse (2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6C43 disorders due to use of opioids (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG64 Drug misuse prevention: targeted interventions (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK drug services work on harm reduction. Giving naloxone, clean equipment and substitution treatment to someone still using is standard care, not condoning drug use. Language matters: say 'person who uses drugs', not 'addict' or 'junkie'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-41","n":1341,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Memory worries: anxiety rather than dementia","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:20","where":"Memory and mental health outpatient clinic","text":"Mrs Linda Okoro, a 58-year-old former secondary school teacher, has come back to clinic. Her GP referred her for memory difficulties and anxiety. CT head and a full screen of bloods for reversible causes are normal. Her ACE-III was 82/100 a year ago and is 88/100 today (memory 22/26, attention 12/18, language 26/26, fluency 12/14, visuospatial 16/16). Her late mother developed Alzheimer's disease in her eighties. As you call her in she asks, 'Is it dementia?'"},"task":"Take a history to work out the diagnosis, then share it with her and explain what her scan, bloods and ACE-III scores mean. A detailed treatment plan is not needed.","first60":["Read the referral, last year's letter and both ACE-III forms: note which items she dropped, not just the total.","Check the bloods include B12, folate, thyroid, calcium, glucose, renal and liver function, and that the CT report mentions no vascular change or atrophy.","Check her medication list for anything that dulls thinking: codeine, sedating antihistamines, anticholinergics, sleeping tablets.","Hold the question in mind: is this a progressive brain disease, or a mind too busy with worry to pay attention?","She is frightened. Plan to name the fear in your first sentence and promise the results today."],"ask":["'Tell me about the memory problems. Can you give me a recent example?'","'Is it more that things don't go in, or that things you knew have gone?'","'Has it got steadily worse, or does it come and go with how stressed you are?'","'What do you find yourself worrying about? Is it hard to switch the worry off?'","'How are you sleeping? Do you feel tense or on edge in your body?'","'Has your mood been low, or have you stopped enjoying things?'","'What tablets do you take, including anything for pain? How much do you drink?'","'Do you still manage money, cooking, driving and finding your way on your own?'"],"recognise":["Forgetting why she walked into a room, losing the thread of a page, checking the oven: these are failures of attention, not loss of stored memories.","Worse when stressed, better on calm days: fluctuation tied to anxiety, not steady decline.","Excessive, hard-to-control worry about several things (money, health, family) for many months with tension, restlessness, poor sleep and poor focus: generalised anxiety disorder.","She brought herself, is checking her memory with apps and searching online: worried preoccupation, typical of anxiety; people with dementia are more often brought by others.","ACE-III up from 82 to 88, with points lost mainly on attention: improvement and an attention-heavy pattern argue against a neurodegenerative process.","Everyday function fully intact: no getting lost, no trouble with money, appliances or faces."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell her clearly this is not dementia but generalised anxiety disorder affecting her concentration, explain each result, and write to the GP with a stepped-care plan (Talking Therapies, SSRI if she wishes) and no routine repeat testing.","r":"Right: the history and every test point the same way. A clear, confident explanation is itself the first treatment for her fear.","best":true},{"c":"Say the results are 'probably fine' and book a repeat ACE-III and MRI in six months to be sure.","r":"Unhelpful: hedging and more tests feed health anxiety and keep her checking. Re-test only if there is a new decline in function.","best":false},{"c":"Start donepezil as a precaution because of her mother's history.","r":"Wrong: there is no dementia. Cholinesterase inhibitors are not indicated for anxiety or for family history, and they cause nausea and slow heart rate.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: ask before discussing her results with her husband or son; offer them a joint conversation if she wants it.","DVLA: anxiety does not need to be reported; she is safe to keep driving.","Shared decision-making (GMC Decision making and consent, 2020): explain options and let her choose between therapy and medication."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how frightened you've been, especially after caring for your mum. I'll give you the results today and tell you plainly what I think."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Your score has gone up, not down, and the points you lost were mostly on concentration. Dementia doesn't usually improve like that; anxiety does."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: generalised anxiety disorder with secondary attention and memory complaints. No evidence of dementia. Please refer to NHS Talking Therapies; consider sertraline if she wishes. Please review codeine use."}],"write":"Memory clinic review. 58F, retired teacher. Referred by GP for memory concerns and anxiety.\nHx: 18-24 months forgetting why she enters rooms, losing thread of books, misplacing keys, checking oven. Worse when stressed; better on holiday. Constant worry about money (early retirement), her health, son's job; tense shoulders, restless, initial insomnia, poor concentration; checks memory with brain-training apps and searches online. Mother had late-onset AD; Linda was main carer until her death last year. Retired early 2 years ago (back pain, caring).\nMood: some low days, enjoyment preserved; no panic attacks; no psychosis; no suicidal thoughts. Post-menopausal, no HRT. Meds: codeine 15 mg PRN most days, paracetamol. Alcohol ~10 units/week (wine to unwind).\nFunction: drives, manages finances, cooks, no getting lost, recognises people.\nTests: CT head and bloods normal. ACE-III 82 -> 88; losses mainly attention (12/18) and memory 22/26 (registration-dependent).\nImpression: generalised anxiety disorder with cognitive symptoms. No evidence of dementia.\nPlan: diagnosis and results explained; teach-back accurate, visibly relieved. Letter to GP: Talking Therapies, consider SSRI, review codeine, reduce alcohol. No routine repeat ACE; re-refer if new functional decline.\nDr Chinonso, psychiatry","make":["Name her fear in the first sentence and promise an answer today; then she can concentrate on your questions.","Ask for real examples of forgetting and sort them: not going in (attention) versus going missing (memory loss).","Ask about function directly: money, appliances, getting lost, recognising people. Intact function is your strongest reassurance.","Cover the dull but vital things: codeine, alcohol, sleep, menopause, mood and psychosis screen.","Explain each result in one plain sentence: what it tested, what it showed, what it means for her.","Say the word 'not dementia' clearly. Vague reassurance keeps the checking going."],"next":"Linda's GP refers her to NHS Talking Therapies. After guided self-help and eight sessions of CBT for worry, her GAD-7 falls from 16 to 7. She deletes the brain-training apps, cuts back the codeine and starts volunteering at a school library. Her memory complaints fade as she worries less.","marks":["Acknowledged her fear early and agreed to explain the results.","Characterised the memory complaints with examples, onset and fluctuation.","Elicited generalised worry, tension, restlessness, sleep and concentration problems.","Screened mood, psychosis, alcohol, medicines and physical causes.","Checked daily function and the family and social background.","Explained the diagnosis and each result clearly, with confident reassurance."],"pitfalls":["Reassuring before taking a history: she will not believe you.","Quoting the ACE-III total without explaining the rise and the attention pattern.","Missing codeine and evening wine, both of which blunt concentration.","Offering a repeat scan or test 'just to be sure', which feeds health anxiety.","Saying 'it's just stress' rather than naming a treatable disorder."],"refs":["1. NICE CG113 Generalised anxiety disorder and panic disorder (adults): management (2011, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","3. Hsieh S et al. Validation of the ACE-III in frontotemporal dementia and Alzheimer's disease. Dement Geriatr Cogn Disord 2013","4. NHS website: Generalised anxiety disorder in adults (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK memory services see many 'worried well' patients, often after caring for a parent with dementia. A firm, kind 'this is not dementia' with reasons is good practice; open-ended follow-up for reassurance is not.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-42","n":1342,"prog":"Perinatal Psychiatry","icon":"🍼","title":"New mum, low mood and a frightening home: risk to her and to Alfie","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00","where":"Perinatal mental health clinic","text":"Ms Leah Brennan, 23, comes in with her six-week-old son Alfie in a car seat. Her midwife referred her for low mood and anxiety since the birth. She had one episode of depression at 19 and does not want medication for now. Her partner Dean drinks heavily, smokes a lot of cannabis, shouts at her, and once shook Alfie briefly when he would not stop crying. The three of them share a cramped one-bedroom flat."},"task":"Take a history focused on the risks to Leah and to Alfie, respond to her worries, and agree with her a plan to manage those risks.","first60":["Read the midwife's referral: when the shaking happened, what she said, and whether anyone has checked Alfie since.","Check the record for any existing children's social care involvement, previous perinatal care and the name of her health visitor.","Look at Alfie when they come in: alert, feeding, colour, any marks or bruises. Any concern means paediatrics today.","Plan your order: her mood and her own safety, then Alfie's safety, then Dean and home, then her fears, then the plan.","Expect fear of social services. Be honest from the start that keeping Alfie safe may mean sharing information."],"ask":["'How have things been for you since Alfie was born?'","'Has it ever felt like life isn't worth going on with, or that you'd harm yourself?'","'Some mums get frightening thoughts about the baby. Have you had any thoughts of harming Alfie?'","'Tell me exactly what happened when Dean shook him. When was it, and has anyone checked Alfie since?'","'Has Dean ever pushed, hit or hurt you? Does he control money, your phone or who you see?'","'Has he ever pressured you into sex or anything sexual?'","'Who else is around to support you: family, friends?'","'What worries you most about what happens next?'"],"recognise":["Two months of low mood, tearfulness, poor sleep beyond feeds and constant worry, with a past episode: probable postnatal depression with anxiety.","No psychosis, no mania or mixed features, no self-harm, no thoughts of harming Alfie or Dean: her own risk is low today.","A six-week-old shaken by an intoxicated adult and never checked: possible hidden head injury, and a high risk of recurrence while Dean remains in the home.","Shouting, a push in pregnancy, control of money and her phone, cutting her off from her mum: domestic abuse, which also harms Alfie.","Protective factors: she breastfeeds well, notices Alfie's needs, moves him away when Dean smokes, and her mum lives nearby.","Fear that Alfie will be removed is the biggest threat to engagement; honest, supportive framing keeps her working with you."],"decide":[{"c":"Arrange a same-day paediatric check for Alfie, refer to children's social care today with her knowledge, agree she and Alfie stay with her mum tonight, complete a DASH checklist and refer to an IDVA, and arrange perinatal follow-up within a week.","r":"Right: a shaken infant needs medical assessment today, and the risk at home cannot wait. Doing it openly, with her, protects both of them and keeps her engaged.","best":true},{"c":"Respect her wish to keep things private, start CBT, and review the home situation at the next appointment.","r":"Unsafe: a baby has been shaken. The duty to protect Alfie overrides confidentiality, and delay leaves him in the same flat as Dean tonight.","best":false},{"c":"Phone the police from the room and tell Leah social services will decide whether she keeps Alfie.","r":"Wrong approach: it frightens her into silence and ignores her as a protective parent. Social care lead, with police joining their strategy discussion if needed.","best":false}],"law":["Children Act 1989 and Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023): a concern that a child has suffered significant harm needs a same-day referral to children's social care; a strategy discussion with police and health may follow.","GMC Confidentiality (2017): you may share information without consent to protect a child from serious harm; tell her what you are sharing unless that would increase the risk.","Domestic Abuse Act 2021: a child who sees or hears domestic abuse is a victim in their own right.","She has capacity and is an informal patient: the plan for her own care is hers to agree."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've done the right thing telling me, Leah. My job is to help keep you and Alfie safe, together if at all possible."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Because Alfie was shaken, I have to let children's social care know today. I'd much rather do that with you, so they hear how much you protect him."},{"to":"Children's social care (referral)","s":"Six-week-old boy shaken by father about a week ago while intoxicated; not medically reviewed until today. Mother has postnatal depression, is protective, and is engaging. Domestic abuse by father. Mother and baby staying with maternal grandmother tonight."}],"write":"Perinatal clinic. Leah Brennan, 23F. Son Alfie, 6/52, present, alert, feeding, no visible marks.\nMood: 2 months low mood, tearful, poor sleep beyond feeds, constant worry, low confidence as a mother. Bonding good, breastfeeding. Depressive episode age 19 treated with sertraline (helped), stopped after 8 months. No mania or mixed features, no psychosis.\nRisk to self: no suicidal thoughts or self-harm; no alcohol or drugs.\nRisk to Alfie from Leah: none; no thoughts of harm; protective.\nRisk to Alfie from Dean: ~1 week ago, intoxicated at night, shook Alfie for a few seconds when crying; not seen by any professional. Dean drinks daily, cannabis heavily, shouts, intolerant of crying.\nRisk to Leah: verbal abuse, pushed against door in pregnancy, controls money, checks her phone, discourages contact with her mother. No sexual abuse. No thoughts of harming Dean.\nImpression: moderate postnatal depression with anxiety. Significant safeguarding concern for Alfie; domestic abuse.\nPlan (agreed with Leah): Alfie to paediatric ED today; children's social care referral today (Leah aware); Leah and Alfie to stay with her mother tonight; DASH completed, IDVA referral; health visitor, midwife and GP informed. Perinatal nurse visit within a week; Talking Therapies (perinatal priority). Sertraline information leaflet; decision at review. Crisis numbers given. Safe contact via mother's phone agreed.\nDr Chinonso, perinatal psychiatry","make":["Greet Alfie and notice how Leah is with him; it shows respect and gives you real evidence of her care.","Ask about risk in every direction: to herself, to Alfie from her, to Alfie from Dean, to her from Dean, and her toward Dean.","Pin down the shaking: when, how long, Dean's state, Alfie's condition after, and whether anyone has checked him.","Screen the full range of abuse: physical, sexual, financial, controlling and isolating behaviour.","Tell her plainly what you must share and why, and that the aim is support, not removal.","Ask what might stop the plan working, and agree a safe way to contact her."],"next":"Alfie is checked by the paediatric team that evening and found well. Social care hold a strategy discussion; Dean agrees to leave the flat while assessments go ahead. Leah stays with her mum, meets the IDVA, and after reading about sertraline decides to start it. By three months her mood is lifting and she is using a parent-infant group.","marks":["Opened warmly, acknowledged Alfie, and explained the purpose of the meeting.","Assessed her mood, anxiety, bonding, past depression and treatment.","Assessed risk to herself and to Alfie from her, including intrusive thoughts.","Detailed the shaking incident and Alfie's need for medical review.","Screened domestic abuse fully, including coercive control and isolation.","Explained the safeguarding referral honestly and agreed a collaborative safety plan."],"pitfalls":["Focusing only on her mood and forgetting the baby in the room.","Promising confidentiality before you know what she will disclose.","Leaving the shaken baby without a same-day medical check.","Threatening social services, or hiding the referral from her.","Not asking what might stop her following the plan."],"refs":["1. HM Government. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG192 Antenatal and postnatal mental health guideline (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC. Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG76 Child abuse and neglect (2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, children's social care aim to keep children with a safe parent. Most referrals lead to support, not removal. Saying this honestly is often what keeps a frightened mother talking to you.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-44","n":1344,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚡","title":"ECT: will it damage my memory?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:00","where":"Older adults inpatient ward, interview room","text":"Mr Desmond Clarke, 65, a retired bus driver, was admitted with a severe depressive episode. Six weeks of fluoxetine 40 mg and then six weeks of venlafaxine 150 mg, taken reliably, have not helped. He has high blood pressure on ramipril and mild kidney impairment. At ward round his consultant suggested ECT. He knows little about it and is afraid it will harm his memory. He has stopped eating properly."},"task":"Give Mr Clarke clear information about ECT to help him decide whether he wants it, and explore his beliefs, worries and expectations. Capacity and consent are not needed today.","first60":["Read the ward round entry: why ECT, what alternatives were discussed, and his current food and fluid intake, weight and risk.","Check his physical health: latest BP, U&Es and eGFR, ECG, and whether an anaesthetic review has been booked.","Pick up the ECT leaflet and the clinic's video link to give him at the end.","Plan to hear his picture of ECT first; correcting fears you haven't heard wastes time.","Today is information, not consent. Say so; it lowers the pressure."],"ask":["'What have you heard about ECT, and where from?'","'What worries you most about it?'","'What would you hope it might do for you?'","'What does your memory mean to you day to day?'","'How have the last few months been, with the tablets not working?'","'Who would you like to talk this over with?'","'What else would help you decide?'"],"recognise":["His picture of ECT comes from an old film: screaming, being held down, no anaesthetic. That image drives the fear more than the facts.","Memory matters to him: crosswords every morning for forty years. Address memory honestly and in detail, not with a blanket 'it's fine'.","Two adequate antidepressant trials have failed, he is eating poorly and feels a burden: severe, treatment-resistant depression where ECT is most effective and quickest.","Older age and severe illness predict a good response to ECT.","Hypertension on ramipril and mild kidney impairment need workup and anaesthetic planning, but are not barriers."],"decide":[{"c":"Explore his beliefs, explain ECT clearly (why, what happens, benefits, memory and other side effects, physical health, alternatives), give written and video information, and arrange a follow-up talk with the consultant about consent.","r":"Right: an informed, unhurried choice is what consent needs later, and it respects his right to say no.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him memory problems don't happen with modern ECT, to reassure him.","r":"Misleading: memory gaps around the course are common, and a few people report lasting gaps. Honest information is required for valid consent.","best":false},{"c":"Book the first treatment for Monday and explain it on the day.","r":"Wrong: he needs time, information, physical workup and an anaesthetic review, and his consent must be voluntary and informed.","best":false}],"law":["He is an informal patient: ECT can only be given with his valid consent, which he can withdraw at any time, even between treatments.","Consent is taken later by a doctor who can explain the procedure, using the trust's ECT form; capacity is assessed at that point.","If detained under the Mental Health Act, Section 58A still forbids ECT to a capacitous patient who refuses, except in an emergency under Section 62.","NICE NG222 (2022): ECT is an option in severe depression if a fast response matters or other treatments have failed."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Modern ECT is done under a short general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant. You're asleep, you don't feel it, and your body barely moves."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I won't pretend memory isn't affected. Many people have gaps for the weeks around treatment, which usually improve. We check your memory throughout and can change how it's given."},{"to":"Consultant (note)","s":"Information session done. Main concern memory; leaflet and video given. Wants family meeting before deciding. Please discuss consent at Tuesday ward round. Anaesthetic review and ECG requested."}],"write":"Ward note. Mr Desmond Clarke, 65M, informal. Severe depressive episode; no response to fluoxetine 40 mg x 6/52 then venlafaxine 150 mg x 6/52 (adherent). HTN on ramipril; mild CKD.\nInformation about ECT given at consultant's request. Not assessing capacity or taking consent today.\nICE: picture of ECT from an old film (screaming, restraint, no anaesthetic). Fears memory loss; does a crossword daily. Hopes to 'feel like myself again'. Feels a burden; eating poorly.\nExplained: rationale (severe, treatment-resistant depression; works well in older adults; usually quicker than medication); procedure (workup, fasting, GA and muscle relaxant, brief seizure, twice weekly, usually 6-12); benefits; side effects (headache, aches, short confusion; memory gaps around course, usually improve, rare lasting gaps; right unilateral and ultrabrief pulse reduce effects; MoCA monitoring); BP and kidneys: anaesthetist review, ramipril timing to be advised; alternatives (lithium augmentation, adding mirtazapine) slower. Right to refuse or stop at any time.\nUnderstood and repeated key points. Wants to talk with wife and daughter. Leaflet and video given.\nPlan: family meeting Monday; consultant to discuss consent Tuesday; bloods, ECG, anaesthetic review requested; food and fluid chart continues.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask what he has heard before you explain anything; then answer his picture, not a generic script.","Tie the reason for ECT to his story: two medicines failed, he is not eating, he wants to be himself again.","Describe the day step by step: fasting, the anaesthetic, waking up, a cup of tea.","Be honest and specific about memory: what is common, what improves, what is rare, and what we do to reduce it.","Mention his blood pressure and kidneys before he asks; it shows you have thought about him.","Leave the choice with him: alternatives, the right to stop, time to decide and someone to talk to."],"next":"Des watches the video with his wife and daughter and agrees to ECT. After eight right unilateral treatments he is eating again and doing half a crossword a day. He has patchy memory for two weeks around the course, which mostly returns. He continues venlafaxine, adds lithium for relapse prevention, and goes home six weeks later.","marks":["Explored his beliefs, fears and hopes before explaining.","Explained why ECT is being suggested now and how likely it is to help.","Described the procedure, course length and aftercare.","Gave honest information about memory and other side effects, and how they are reduced.","Addressed his blood pressure, kidneys and workup.","Stressed his choice, alternatives, sources of information and time to decide."],"pitfalls":["Launching into a lecture before hearing his fears.","Claiming ECT has no effect on memory.","Using jargon such as 'refractory', 'bilateral' or 'seizure threshold' without explaining.","Ignoring his blood pressure and kidney concerns.","Pressuring him to agree today."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) guideline (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists. Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT): patient information (checked Nov 2026)","3. ECT Accreditation Service (ECTAS) standards, RCPsych (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 25 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK ECT clinics are accredited by ECTAS, and patients are encouraged to visit the suite, meet the ECT nurse and watch a video before deciding. Honest discussion of memory effects is expected; 'nothing to worry about' is not acceptable.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-45","n":1345,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🎬","title":"Off lithium and feeling unstoppable: explaining mania and agreeing a plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:30","where":"Community mental health clinic, urgent appointment","text":"Mr Jamal Whitfield, 28, a marketing assistant, had a manic episode with grandiose delusions two years ago and was detained in hospital; lithium brought full recovery. He stopped it about two months ago. His family took him to the GP, who referred him urgently. He feels limitless, sleeps about two hours a night, trains at the gym twice daily, works late, is planning a film at home, has bought expensive camera gear and has been approaching strangers to star in it. No alcohol or drugs."},"task":"Talk to Mr Whitfield about the likely diagnosis and agree a management plan with him.","first60":["Read the discharge summary from two years ago: what the episode looked like, the section used, early warning signs, and the lithium level that worked.","Check for a relapse plan or advance statement, and whether the GP letter mentions money, driving or aggression.","Ask the clinic nurse to stay nearby; note how he is in the waiting room.","Plan to let him talk briefly, then take the lead: interrupt kindly but firmly to keep to the agenda.","Know your options before you start: medication choices, home treatment, admission, and when the Mental Health Act applies."],"ask":["'How much are you sleeping, and do you feel tired?'","'Tell me about the film. Who have you been approaching, and where?'","'How much have you spent recently, and how have you paid for it?'","'When did you stop lithium, and what made you decide to?'","'Are you driving at the moment? How fast?'","'Has anyone been angry with you, or have you lost your temper?'","'Do you have any special abilities or a mission others don't have?'","'How does this compare with how you felt before your last admission?'"],"recognise":["Elation, sleeping two hours without tiredness, high energy, big plans, spending and disinhibition for weeks: a manic episode.","A second episode of mania means bipolar disorder, type I. He may never have been told this clearly.","Stopping lithium two months ago is the likely trigger; abrupt stopping carries a particularly high risk of manic relapse.","Risks: debt, impulsive resignation from work, vulnerability while approaching strangers, unsafe driving, conflict with family; check for psychosis and sexual risk.","He accepts 'something might be wrong' and will take medication with support: home treatment is reasonable now, admission if that fails."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain that this is a manic relapse and that two episodes mean bipolar disorder, start an antipsychotic today, involve home treatment and family, pause big decisions and driving, and discuss lithium or another long-term option once settled.","r":"Right: it treats the episode now in the least restrictive setting he will accept, with a clear route to admission if needed.","best":true},{"c":"Arrange a Mental Health Act assessment straight away because he was detained last time.","r":"Premature: he is willing to take medication and has family support. Detention is for when less restrictive care is refused or unsafe.","best":false},{"c":"Restart lithium at his old dose today and review in a month.","r":"Unsafe and slow: lithium needs baseline renal and thyroid tests and level monitoring, takes 1-2 weeks to act, and he says he won't take it. A month is too long between reviews.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity for treatment decisions today and agrees to medication: treat informally in the least restrictive setting (MHA Code of Practice guiding principles).","If he stops treatment and risk rises, request a Mental Health Act assessment via the AMHP service.","DVLA: people with mania must stop driving and notify the DVLA; tell him and record that you did.","Confidentiality: ask his consent to share the plan with his family; he can choose what they hear."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Can I pause you there, Jamal? I want to make sure we get to what will help, so I'm going to steer us a bit."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Two episodes like this, with the highs coming back after stopping lithium, means this is bipolar disorder. It's common, treatable, and people with it run businesses and make films."},{"to":"Home treatment team (referral)","s":"28M, manic relapse 2 months after stopping lithium. Sleeping 2 h, overspending (about £4,500), approaching strangers. No psychosis today, no substances. Starting olanzapine 10 mg nocte. Staying with parents. Please visit daily from tomorrow."}],"write":"Urgent CMHT clinic. Jamal Whitfield, 28M. Bipolar affective disorder (manic episode with psychotic features 2 years ago, detained, full recovery on lithium). Stopped lithium ~2 months ago (tremor, thirst, blood tests; thought he was cured).\nNow ~4 weeks: elated, sleeps 2 h/night without fatigue, gym twice daily, pressured speech, flight of ideas, plans award-winning film, bought camera and sound deck (~£4,500 on credit card), approaching strangers in town centre late at night; one man became angry. Plans to quit job. No hallucinations; grandiose but not fixed today. No alcohol or drugs. No suicidal thoughts. Driving.\nInsight partial: accepts 'might be getting high again'; agrees to medication.\nImpression: manic episode, bipolar I disorder, relapse after stopping lithium.\nPlan agreed: olanzapine 10 mg nocte (weight and sedation discussed); lorazepam 1 mg nocte PRN up to 5 nights; baseline bloods, weight, BP today. CRHTT daily visits from tomorrow; stay with parents (consent to share). Fit note 2 weeks; no job decisions; credit card to parents for now; return equipment if possible. Advised not to drive and to notify DVLA. Lithium vs other long-term options to discuss when settled. Admission if not taking medication or risk rises. Crisis numbers given.\nDr Chinonso, ST4","make":["Let him speak for a minute, then take the lead. Interrupt with warmth and a reason.","Collect the evidence for mania from him: sleep, energy, plans, spending, approaching strangers.","Name it plainly: 'This looks like mania coming back.' Then explain why two episodes mean bipolar disorder.","Offer real choices: which medicine, where he is treated, how family are involved.","Respect his reasons for stopping lithium; they are common and fixable.","Set limits on the dangerous things: driving, spending, quitting his job, late-night approaches."],"next":"Jamal stays with his parents and the home treatment team visit daily. On olanzapine he sleeps six hours within a week and agrees the film can wait. Three weeks later he discusses long-term options and decides to restart lithium with a plan to keep the level steady and check bloods every three months.","marks":["Kept control of the conversation kindly and set boundaries.","Elicited core manic symptoms and the story of stopping lithium.","Assessed risks: money, work, driving, vulnerability, aggression and psychosis.","Explained the diagnosis of mania and bipolar disorder clearly.","Offered medication choices with benefits and side effects, including lithium.","Agreed setting, home treatment, family involvement, safety steps and admission as a fallback."],"pitfalls":["Letting him talk for five minutes without steering.","Arguing about the film or telling him his ideas are mad.","Insisting on admission when he accepts treatment and has support.","Forgetting driving and money.","Starting an antidepressant, or lithium without bloods."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: olanzapine; lithium carbonate (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA. Assessing fitness to drive: psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015) (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services prefer home treatment through crisis teams when a person with mania accepts medication and has support. Detention is a last resort, not a default because of a past section.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-46","n":1346,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🩸","title":"Refusing a scope after vomiting blood: explaining capacity to his support worker","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 17:20, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical ward, general hospital","text":"Liam Fairweather, 35, male, has a mild learning disability, lives in supported housing and takes risperidone. He vomited blood twice today after six months of upper abdominal pain. The medical team think he is bleeding from his stomach. He is refusing bloods, a drip and an endoscopy because he believes the police injected him in his sleep and that hospital staff work for them. You have just assessed his capacity. His support worker, Tessa Oduya, is waiting by the nurses' desk."},"task":"Explain the result of the capacity assessment to his support worker, answer her questions about consent, delay, legality and restraint, and agree an urgent best-interests plan, including mental health follow-up.","first60":["Check with the medical registrar: latest observations, haemoglobin if known, Glasgow-Blatchford score, and how soon endoscopy is planned.","Confirm your capacity record is written: the decision, the impairment, each of the four abilities, and the evidence for each.","Ask the nurse in charge whether Liam has tried to leave and whether the learning disability liaison nurse has been called.","Find a side room for Tessa; she came in the ambulance and may be shaken.","Hold the order in your head: her view of Liam, the finding, what it means, best interests, people and safeguards, mental health, close."],"ask":["'What is Liam usually like when he's well, and what has changed lately?'","'When did he start talking about the police coming in at night?'","'Has he been taking his risperidone? How do you know?'","'What helps him feel safe when he's frightened?'","'Who else matters to him? Can we reach his mum today?'","'Has he ever said what he'd want if he were seriously ill?'","'What would you like to know before we go back to him?'"],"recognise":["He can understand, remember and state his choice, yet cannot weigh the information: the belief that tests are a police plot blocks any balancing of the risk of death.","That failure has to stem from an impairment of mind or brain: here a learning disability plus what look like new psychotic beliefs.","A new fear of police and night staff, a few weeks old, with tablets found in the bin, suggests relapse after missed risperidone.","Capacity is specific to this decision and this time: he may manage other choices today and may regain this one when settled.","Upper GI bleeding with haematemesis is a medical emergency; delay to wait for calm can cost his life.","A paid support worker is a key source of his wishes and values but cannot consent and does not count as the person consulted for IMCA purposes."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain he lacks capacity for this decision now, proceed in his best interests with the least restrictive approach (Tessa and the LD liaison nurse persuading first, sedation planned), consult his mother today, and reassess capacity once he is stable.","r":"Right: lawful under the MCA, urgent, respects his wishes and values, and keeps force as a last resort.","best":true},{"c":"Respect his refusal because he understands and remembers the information.","r":"Wrong: understanding is only one of four abilities. He cannot weigh the risk because of a belief caused by his mental state, so the refusal is not a capacitous one.","best":false},{"c":"Detain him under section 5(2) of the Mental Health Act so the medical team can treat the bleed.","r":"Wrong: the MHA authorises treatment for mental disorder, not a bleeding stomach. Physical treatment for someone lacking capacity is given under the MCA.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005 section 1: assume capacity, help him decide first, an unwise decision is not proof of incapacity, act in best interests, choose the least restrictive option.","MCA sections 2 and 3: inability to understand, retain, use or weigh, or communicate, because of an impairment of mind or brain.","MCA section 4 best interests: his past and present wishes, beliefs and values; consult carers and family; consider whether he may regain capacity and whether it can wait.","MCA section 6: restraint only if necessary to prevent harm and proportionate; MCA section 37: an IMCA for serious treatment if no one appropriate to consult, but emergency treatment is not delayed.","If he is kept on the ward under continuous supervision and not free to leave for some days, consider a DoLS urgent authorisation."],"say":[{"to":"Support worker","s":"Liam understands what the doctors told him and can repeat it back. What he can't do is weigh it up, because he's sure the tests are a police plot. So for this decision, today, he lacks capacity."},{"to":"Support worker","s":"No one can sign for him, not you and not his mum. But you know him best, so your view shapes how we do this."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Capacity assessed: lacks capacity to refuse bloods, IV access and endoscopy, due to beliefs that seem psychotic. Best-interests plan agreed with his support worker; mother being consulted. Please proceed; LD liaison nurse and support worker to be present; sedation for endoscopy."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. Liam Fairweather, 35M, mild LD, supported housing, on risperidone. Haematemesis x2 today; suspected upper GI bleed.\nDecision: consent to bloods, IV access/fluids and OGD.\nImpairment: mild LD; probable psychotic relapse (beliefs police injected him; staff work for police; tests will harm him) after ~3 weeks of missed risperidone (support worker found tablets in bin).\nUnderstands: yes. Retains: yes. Communicates: yes. Uses/weighs: NO, cannot balance risk of death because of fixed belief tests are a police plot.\nConclusion: lacks capacity for this decision at this time. Reassess when stable.\nBest interests: discussed with support worker Tessa Oduya (knows his values: trusts familiar staff, calmed by simple step-by-step explanations). Mother to be phoned today. IMCA not required if mother consulted; emergency treatment not delayed.\nPlan: persuasion with Tessa and LD liaison nurse, easy-read pictures; bloods and IV now; OGD when stable with sedation; restraint only if necessary and proportionate, recorded. MHA not applicable to physical treatment.\nPsychiatry: review mental state and risperidone when stable; inform community LD psychiatrist and social worker.\nDr C. Chinonso, liaison psychiatry","make":["Give the verdict early and plainly, then the reasons: carers lose trust in a doctor who circles round the answer.","Name all four abilities and show which one fails, with his exact belief as the evidence.","Say out loud that an unwise choice is not the same as lacking capacity; it shows you know the law protects him too.","Answer the hard questions directly: proxy consent, can it wait, is it legal, will he be held down.","Make Tessa part of the plan: her presence and his routines are the least restrictive tools you have.","Do not forget the psychiatry: a new psychosis after missed risperidone needs its own plan."],"next":"With Tessa beside him and the liaison nurse using picture cards, Liam accepts bloods and a drip. His mother agrees by phone. Endoscopy that evening under sedation finds a duodenal ulcer, treated endoscopically. Two days later, back on supervised risperidone, he says the police idea 'feels less strong', and he regains capacity for his ongoing care.","marks":["Gave the capacity finding early and in plain words.","Explained the four abilities and why weighing up fails here.","Made clear capacity is decision- and time-specific and will be reassessed.","Explained best interests and the least restrictive approach, with Tessa involved.","Answered proxy consent, delay, legality and restraint accurately.","Planned mental health follow-up and consulted his mother."],"pitfalls":["Saying he has capacity because he understands and remembers what he was told.","Telling Tessa or his mother they can consent on his behalf.","Suggesting the Mental Health Act to treat the bleed.","Agreeing to wait until tomorrow while he is still bleeding.","Ignoring the new paranoid beliefs and missed risperidone."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG141 Acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding in over 16s: management (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG108 Decision-making and mental capacity (2018; checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Mental Capacity Act and best interests (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, next of kin has no legal power to consent for an adult. Doctors decide in best interests after consulting family and carers. Paid support workers are vital for knowing the person's wishes but cannot sign for them.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-47","n":1347,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"A mother asks for her son's sterilisation: rights, fears and a cry for help","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00, community learning disability clinic","where":"Community learning disability team, outpatient room","text":"Patricia Okoye, a single mother in her fifties, has asked to see you about her son Callum, 26, male, whose mild intellectual disability followed oxygen starvation during his birth. He lives in a supported flat with his partner Jade, who also has a mild intellectual disability and is six months pregnant. Callum has exposed himself again while stressed that the baby will be removed. Patricia sits down and says, 'I want him sterilised.'"},"task":"Explore her concerns, explain clearly why sterilisation is not something she or you can arrange, reassure her with facts about the baby and parenting support, and agree a plan for Callum's behaviour and for her own wellbeing.","first60":["Read Callum's file: cause of his disability, past police contact, current support hours, social worker, any capacity assessments.","Check whether children's social care and the midwife already know about the pregnancy and the recent incident.","Plan to listen before you explain: under a blunt demand there is usually exhaustion and fear.","Have the facts ready: who can consent to sterilisation, the cause of his disability, pre-birth assessment, carers' assessment.","Keep the order in mind: her concerns, Callum now, her wellbeing, sterilisation, the baby, the exposing, then a plan."],"ask":["'What worries you most about the baby coming?'","'What happened with the police this time? Who was involved, and how old were they?'","'Has anything like that happened at other times, or has he ever touched anyone?'","'What were you told about why Callum has a learning disability?'","'Does anything run in Jade's family? What did her scans show?'","'How are you sleeping, and how is your mood? Have you had thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'Who helps you? Has anyone ever offered you a carer's assessment?'"],"recognise":["The demand for sterilisation is a cry for help: 26 years of caring alone, money worries, night shifts and fear of being handed a baby.","Disability caused by lack of oxygen at birth is an injury, not an inherited condition; it does not pass to his children.","Two episodes of indecent exposure, both when stressed, with no contact offending, suggest a disinhibited stress response that needs specialist assessment, not a permanent operation.","Many parents with a mild intellectual disability manage well with the right support; the child's welfare is judged by the help in place, not the label.","Carer strain can hide depression; screen her mood and safety briefly but properly."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain kindly that sterilisation is Callum's own decision, give the facts about inheritance and parenting support, reassure her she will not be made to raise the baby, refer Callum for assessment of the exposing and stress, and refer her for a carer's assessment.","r":"Right: lawful, respects Callum's rights, and answers every fear underneath the request.","best":true},{"c":"Agree to refer Callum to a urologist for a vasectomy discussion because his mother is worried.","r":"Wrong: a relative cannot consent for an adult, and the operation would not be for Callum's benefit. Sterilisation without valid consent breaches his rights.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her the baby will probably be taken into care, so she need not worry about raising it.","r":"Wrong and harmful: removal is not the default. Children's social care assesses and supports parents first; this false reassurance also misleads her.","best":false}],"law":["Human Rights Act 1998, Articles 8 and 12: respect for private and family life and the right to found a family apply equally to people with intellectual disability.","MCA 2005: only Callum can consent; if he lacked capacity, non-therapeutic sterilisation would in practice need a Court of Protection decision, in his own best interests, considering less restrictive options first.","Children Act 1989: children's social care assesses need before birth and supports parents; the child's welfare is paramount. Equality Act 2010: support must be adapted for disabled parents.","Care Act 2014: Patricia is able to ask for a carer's assessment. PACE Code C: Callum needs an appropriate adult if interviewed by police."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"I can't arrange that operation, and I want to be honest about why. It's Callum's own decision. Nobody can make it for him, not me and not you."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Callum's disability came from lack of oxygen when he was born. That's an injury at birth, not something in his genes, so it isn't passed on to his baby."},{"to":"Mother","s":"You won't be expected to raise this baby. Social care's job is to help Callum and Jade parent. How involved you are as a grandmother is your choice."}],"write":"CLDT clinic. Seen Mrs Patricia Okoye (mother of Callum Okoye, 26M, mild ID secondary to perinatal hypoxia).\nConcerns: requests Callum's sterilisation; fears baby will be disabled and she will have to raise it; financial and emotional strain; Callum's repeat indecent exposure.\nIncident: exposed himself to an adult woman near shops last week when distressed about baby being removed; police interviewed, on bail. Previous similar episode some years ago when stressed about money. No contact offending reported.\nPatricia: poor sleep, tearful, isolated, works nights; denies thoughts of self-harm or harm to others.\nDiscussed: sterilisation is Callum's own decision; family cannot consent; court only if he lacked capacity, and only in his interests. Hypoxic cause is not inherited; Jade's scans reportedly normal. Pre-birth assessment and adapted parenting support explained; Patricia not expected to care for baby.\nPlan: CLDT psychology and forensic risk assessment of exposing; accessible sex and relationships education; contraception discussion offered to couple via GP. Liaise with police/solicitor; appropriate adult. Liaise with Callum's social worker and children's social care. Carer's assessment referral for Patricia; GP letter re her mood and sleep; benefits advice. Review with Callum and Patricia in 2 weeks.\nDr C. Chinonso","make":["Listen first and name her exhaustion; the request softens once she feels heard.","Say no to the operation early and clearly, with the reason: it is his decision, and his rights are the same as anyone's.","Use the cause of his disability to answer the inheritance fear with a fact, not a hope.","Separate support from removal: social care's first job is helping the couple parent.","Take the exposing seriously but proportionately: a few precise questions, then a referral.","Leave her with something for herself: a carer's assessment, her GP, and a date."],"next":"Patricia's carer's assessment leads to a monthly support group and a small carer's break budget. Callum works with the team psychologist on stress and safer ways to cope; there are no further incidents. The pre-birth assessment sets up a specialist parenting programme for Callum and Jade, who take their baby home with daily support.","marks":["Elicited all her concerns before explaining.","Explained clearly that only Callum can consent to sterilisation.","Answered the inheritance fear using the cause of his disability.","Explained parenting support and that she will not be made to raise the baby.","Asked precise questions about the exposing and planned specialist help.","Screened her wellbeing and offered a carer's assessment."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing her about human rights before hearing why she is asking.","Hinting that sterilisation might be possible if she pushes harder.","Promising the baby will be healthy, or that it will be removed.","Skipping the exposing because it feels awkward to ask about.","Forgetting Patricia herself: mood, sleep, money, isolation."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG54 Mental health problems in people with learning disabilities (2016; checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Carers' assessments and support (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in some countries families decide medical matters for adults with intellectual disability. In the UK, only the adult can consent; a court decides if he cannot, and only in his own interests. Families are consulted, not deciders.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-48","n":1348,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🔐","title":"Voices after violence on the prison wing: handover, risk and the bed question","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:30, prison healthcare","where":"Inpatient healthcare unit, category B prison","text":"Shane Mulvey, 32, male, three months into a sentence for assault, was moved to the healthcare unit two days ago. Since watching one prisoner attack another a few weeks ago, he hears voices warning him he will be harmed and must defend himself, feels someone may be controlling him, and has urges to hurt others. Staff suspect GHB. Nurse Kiran Bhatt meets you at the office door: 'Can we talk about Mulvey? I need that bed.'"},"task":"Take a brief, structured handover from the nurse, form an initial view of diagnosis and risk, agree an initial management plan, and decide safely whether he can return to the wing.","first60":["Read the healthcare record: GP entry on arrival, observations, any drug test result, medicines given, any ACCT already open.","Check his index offence, previous violence, previous psychiatric contact and substance history from the prison record.","Ask for the handover in SBAR order so you get facts, not impressions.","Expect pressure for the bed; decide now that the plan rests on risk, not on capacity.","Hold the order: handover, risk to others, risk to self, what it might be, plan, the wing question, close."],"ask":["'How has he slept and eaten since he arrived?'","'What is he saying about the voices now? Any sense of being controlled or his thoughts being read?'","'Any incidents, threats, or aggression? Has he named anyone?'","'What were his observations, and was he sweaty or shaky early on?'","'What drug test was done, when, and was GHB included? Has anyone done bloods?'","'Any self-harm, talk of dying, debt or bullying on the wing?'","'Who knows him best on his old wing, and is family in touch?'"],"recognise":["Voices from outside his head, ideas of being controlled and of thoughts being read point to a psychotic episode.","Feeling threatened together with feeling controlled, a violent offence, urges to harm and substance use is a high-risk mix for violence.","GHB withdrawal starts within hours and settles within days to two weeks; it does not explain voices that began weeks before the move.","A standard urine screen rarely includes GHB and it clears within about 12 hours, so a negative result proves little.","Two calm days on a staffed unit are not an assessment; the wing, with real threats, is a different setting.","A drug debt and 'I'd rather die than be attacked' signal vulnerability and self-harm risk."],"decide":[{"c":"Keep him on the healthcare unit, see him yourself today for a full mental state and risk assessment, arrange bloods, ECG and repeat drug screen, gather collateral, and consider transfer to hospital if psychosis is confirmed.","r":"Right: safe, proportionate, and based on risk rather than bed pressure.","best":true},{"c":"Return him to the wing today because he has been calm and it is probably GHB.","r":"Wrong: nobody has assessed him fully, and his symptoms carry real risk to others and to himself on the wing.","best":false},{"c":"Start a high-dose antipsychotic by injection today without seeing him, to settle the voices quickly.","r":"Wrong: treatment follows assessment. Treatment without consent is not authorised in prison; that needs transfer to hospital.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Health Act 1983 section 47 (with section 49 restrictions): transfer of a sentenced prisoner to hospital on two medical recommendations and a Ministry of Justice warrant.","The MHA does not allow treatment without consent in prison; emergency action relies on the common law or the MCA only.","NHS England guidance: aim to transfer within 28 days of first referral; escalate delays.","ACCT (Assessment, Care in Custody and Teamwork) for any prisoner at risk of self-harm; share risk information on a need-to-know basis."],"say":[{"to":"Nurse","s":"I can see the bed pressure, and I know you've had a lot of drug cases. But nobody has fully assessed him yet, and what he's describing carries real risk, so he stays here until I've seen him."},{"to":"Nurse","s":"A negative urine doesn't rule out GHB; it's often not tested and clears within hours. But withdrawal doesn't explain voices that started weeks ago."},{"to":"Healthcare manager","s":"Mr Mulvey has probable psychosis with threat and control symptoms and a violent index offence. He remains on healthcare pending my full assessment today. Possible section 47 referral to follow."}],"write":"Prison healthcare. Shane Mulvey, 32M, sentenced (assault), day 2 on healthcare unit.\nHandover (Nurse K. Bhatt): night 1 sweaty, tremor, poor sleep, tachycardic; settled by day 2. Ongoing voice warning he will be attacked; sits back to wall; asked twice if officers can read his thoughts. Eats little; polite, guarded. No incidents. Urine screen negative (GHB not reliably detected). Examined by prison GP on arrival; no bloods.\nRisk to others: violent index offence; voices urging self-defence; passivity; urges to harm others, no named target. HIGH if returned to wing.\nRisk to self: 'rather die than be attacked'; possible drug debt; no self-harm.\nImpression: probable psychotic episode; substance intoxication/withdrawal possible contributor; trauma after witnessed assault.\nPlan: remain on healthcare unit. Bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, TFT, CK), ECG, repeat urine screen. Observations per unit policy. My full MSE and risk assessment today. Promethazine 25 mg PRN for distress. Antipsychotic after assessment if indicated. Collateral from wing staff and family; previous records. ACCT to be considered. Need-to-know risk sharing with wing and security. Consider s47 transfer if psychosis confirmed. Escalate if aggressive, confused or unwell.\nDr C. Chinonso, forensic psychiatry","make":["Ask for the handover in a structure; it stops a busy colleague giving you only conclusions.","Name the dangerous combination aloud: threat, control, urges, past violence, drugs.","Be honest about uncertainty: 'we don't know yet' is the reason he stays, not a weakness.","Respect the nurse's view and pressure, then decide on risk; never be talked out of a safe plan.","Commit to seeing him yourself, with a time.","Know the route to hospital: in prison, compulsory treatment means transfer."],"next":"Your assessment that afternoon confirms persecutory delusions, third-person voices and passivity. Bloods are normal. He accepts oral aripiprazole on the unit and a section 47 referral is made. He transfers to a medium secure unit three weeks later, with no violent incidents in the meantime.","marks":["Took a structured handover covering symptoms, behaviour, observations and drug testing.","Identified likely psychosis, with substances as a possible factor.","Explained the risk to others clearly, with reasons.","Asked about risk to himself and his vulnerability.","Planned investigations, collateral and a personal review today.","Kept him on healthcare despite pressure, and explained the route to hospital."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'it's just GHB' because the nurse is confident.","Treating a negative urine screen as proof he is drug-free.","Agreeing to a wing return to free the bed.","Starting medication before anyone has assessed him.","Forgetting risk to himself: debt, bullying, 'I'd rather die'."],"refs":["1. Mental Health Act 1983, sections 47, 48 and 49, and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS England: Guidance for the transfer and remission of adults from prison to hospital (2021; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG66 Mental health of adults in contact with the criminal justice system (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK prisons, healthcare units are not hospitals under the Mental Health Act, so nobody can be treated there against their will. If a prisoner needs compulsory treatment, the route is transfer to a secure hospital.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-49","n":1349,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"📈","title":"Bipolar relapse two months off lithium: naming mania and agreeing a plan he accepts","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 16:15, urgent clinic","where":"Community mental health team, urgent assessment clinic","text":"Rhys Llewellyn, 28, male, a graphic designer, was detained two years ago with mania and grandiose beliefs and recovered fully on lithium. He stopped it about two months ago. His parents took him to the GP, who referred urgently. He sleeps two hours a night, trains twice a day, works late, is making a music video, has spent heavily on equipment and is approaching strangers to perform in it. He taps his foot in the waiting room: 'I haven't got long.'"},"task":"Clarify the current picture and risks briefly, explain that this is a manic relapse of bipolar disorder linked to stopping lithium, and agree a treatment plan and setting he will accept.","first60":["Read the discharge summary from two years ago: what he was like, what worked, lithium level achieved, any side effects.","Check the GP letter and any message from his parents: spending, driving, work, alcohol or drugs.","Plan to hold the frame: he will be fast and charming and may try to recruit you; redirect kindly and keep moving.","Know your options: antipsychotic now, lithium for long-term protection, home treatment team, admission as a fallback.","Keep the order: his view, the picture now, lithium, risk, name it, treatment, his worries, close."],"ask":["'How have you been feeling these last few weeks, in your words?'","'How many hours are you sleeping, and do you feel tired?'","'What have you been spending, and on what?'","'What made you stop lithium, and did you stop it all at once?'","'Are you driving? Any alcohol, drugs, or new relationships you wouldn't usually have?'","'Do you feel you have special abilities or a special mission? Any thoughts of self-harm or anyone?'","'What worries you most about treatment?'"],"recognise":["Elation, two hours' sleep without tiredness, overactivity, big spending, grandiose plans and approaching strangers make a manic episode.","Irritability when challenged is part of mania, not just personality.","A second manic episode removes any doubt about bipolar disorder; ICD-11 already allows bipolar type I after just one manic episode.","Stopping lithium abruptly raises the risk of manic relapse; tremor and feeling flat are common reasons people stop.","Risk in mania is mostly practical: debt, reckless driving, sexual risk, exploitation and losing a job.","He is partly accepting; a plan he agrees to at home with intensive support is better than a fight over admission."],"decide":[{"c":"Name mania and bipolar disorder, start an antipsychotic now, offer to restart lithium for long-term protection, arrange home treatment team visits while he stays with his parents, stop driving and hand cards to a relative, with admission as a fallback.","r":"Right: effective, least restrictive, and built on what he will accept.","best":true},{"c":"Insist on informal admission today, and request a Mental Health Act assessment if he declines.","r":"Too much, too soon: he is accepting treatment, has family support and no acute dangerousness. Pressing admission now risks losing his engagement.","best":false},{"c":"Restart lithium alone at his old dose and review in a month.","r":"Not enough: lithium takes time to act in acute mania and needs level checks; an antipsychotic is needed now, with close follow-up.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity and is engaging: treatment is voluntary. A Mental Health Act assessment is for if he refuses and risk to himself or others becomes high.","NICE CG185: for mania not on an antimanic drug, offer haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone; consider lithium for long-term treatment.","DVLA: while manic or hypomanic, driving stops and the DVLA has to be informed.","Confidentiality: ask his permission before sharing the plan with his parents; most people agree when they see why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Very little sleep without feeling tired, huge energy, big spending and lots of plans at once: those are the signs of mania, the same pattern as two years ago."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Your talent is real. It's the lack of sleep and the spending that worry me. Getting this settled protects your work and your savings."},{"to":"Home treatment team","s":"28-year-old man with bipolar disorder, manic relapse two months after stopping lithium. Accepting quetiapine; staying with parents; not driving; father holding his cards. Please visit daily from tomorrow; admission if deteriorating or stops treatment."}],"write":"Urgent CMHT clinic. Rhys Llewellyn, 28M, graphic designer. Bipolar disorder: manic episode with grandiosity 2 years ago (MHA admission), full recovery on lithium. Stopped lithium abruptly ~2/12 ago (tremor, felt flat).\nNow: elated, irritable when challenged, ~2 h sleep without fatigue, gym twice daily, working late, music video plans; ~£4,000 on credit for equipment; approaching strangers to perform; believes a famous band will 'definitely' want it. No alcohol or drugs. No psychotic beliefs beyond grandiose overvalued ideas. No thoughts of harm to self or others. Driving.\nImpression: manic relapse of bipolar disorder after abrupt lithium discontinuation. Capacity to consent to treatment present.\nPlan: quetiapine IR titrated to 400 mg/day over 4 days; baseline bloods, weight, BP, ECG. Discuss lithium restart at 1-week review. HTT daily visits; staying with parents; father to hold cards; fit note; advised not to drive and to notify DVLA. Crisis numbers given. Review 1 week.\nDr C. Chinonso","make":["Use his own words as the evidence when you name mania; he can argue with a label, not with his own two hours' sleep.","Hold boundaries warmly: decline the film role with a smile and steer straight back.","Ask why he stopped lithium before defending it; the answer shapes the plan.","Give a menu with honest side effects: antipsychotic now, lithium later, and why each.","Make the setting concrete: which team visits, whose house he stays at, which relative keeps the cards, and the fallback if it fails.","Close on teach-back: ask what he'll tell his parents tonight."],"next":"He stays with his parents and the home treatment team visits daily. On quetiapine he sleeps six hours by the end of the week. At two weeks he agrees to restart lithium; his father returns the cards a month later, and he goes back to work on a phased return.","marks":["Kept control of the conversation and held boundaries kindly.","Gathered the current picture and the lithium story.","Asked about practical risks, including driving and money.","Named mania and bipolar disorder with reasons from his account.","Offered an antipsychotic now and lithium for the long term, with side effects.","Agreed home treatment with admission as a fallback."],"pitfalls":["Letting him talk about the video for five minutes, or agreeing to appear in it.","Lecturing about lithium before asking why he stopped.","Pushing for a ward bed after he has already accepted tablets and family support.","Forgetting driving, credit cards and work.","Offering an antidepressant or leaving one in place during mania."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Quetiapine; Lithium carbonate (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Bipolar disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services treat most manic relapses at home with crisis or home treatment teams, keeping admission for when home care fails or risk is high. Practical safety (cards, driving, work) is part of the plan, not an afterthought.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-50","n":1350,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Doubting antidepressants: the serotonin question and a depression plan for a doctor","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 10:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Psychiatry outpatient clinic, neighbouring NHS trust","text":"Dr Farhan Qureshi, 40, male, a consultant microbiologist at a nearby teaching hospital, has a moderately severe depressive episode that began as his marriage ended. He has been signed off for three weeks and wants to return soon. He takes mirtazapine and has developed ejaculatory problems. He told the nurses he has read that the serotonin theory of depression has been disproved and doubts any further medicine will help."},"task":"Take a focused history of his depression, risk and medicines, answer his doubts about the serotonin theory honestly, and agree a management plan covering medication options, therapy and return to work.","first60":["Read the referral and GP record: onset, previous antidepressants with doses and durations, side effects, alcohol, any suicidal thoughts on treatment.","Check where he works: he should be treated outside his own hospital, and his records protected.","Expect a well-read, sceptical colleague; agree what is true before you explain anything.","Have the menu ready: switch, augment, psychological therapy, rTMS, and how each works.","Keep the order: how he is now, risk, medicines so far, the serotonin question, options, work, close."],"ask":["'How did this start, and what's been happening with the divorce?'","'How are your sleep, appetite, energy and concentration now? What do you still enjoy?'","'How much are you drinking?'","'Has it ever felt like life isn't worth carrying on with? Any plans? Any access to medicines at work?'","'Which antidepressants have you taken, at what dose, for how long, and did you take them every day?'","'What side effects have you had? Anything that made you feel worse?'","'What would you want from treatment, and what worries you about it?'"],"recognise":["Low mood, early waking, poor appetite and concentration, and anhedonia after a major loss: a moderate to severe depressive episode.","Three adequate trials without response, with one causing worse suicidal thoughts and one causing sexual dysfunction: this is difficult-to-treat depression.","Passive thoughts that his children would cope without him need a careful risk assessment; male doctors in midlife going through divorce carry raised risk.","His serotonin point is partly right; mechanism and effectiveness are separate questions, and saying so builds trust.","Alcohol most nights may be worsening sleep and mood and blunting treatment.","A doctor-patient needs care outside his own workplace, an occupational health plan and confidential support."],"decide":[{"c":"Agree the simple serotonin story is outdated, separate it from trial evidence, switch mirtazapine to vortioxetine with high-intensity CBT, set out augmentation and rTMS as next steps, and refer to occupational health and Practitioner Health.","r":"Right: honest, evidence-based, addresses the side effect, and supports a safe return to work.","best":true},{"c":"Defend the chemical imbalance model firmly and increase mirtazapine.","r":"Wrong: it is inaccurate, loses his trust, and ignores the side effect that is driving his doubts.","best":false},{"c":"Agree that medication is pointless and offer counselling only.","r":"Not enough: for moderate to severe depression after several failed trials, NICE favours combined medication and structured therapy; counselling alone is unlikely to help enough.","best":false}],"law":["NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), 2022): for more severe depression, offer combined antidepressant and individual CBT; after poor response, consider switching, augmenting or combining.","NICE TA367: vortioxetine for a major depressive episode when two antidepressants have not worked in the current episode.","GMC Good medical practice: doctors must seek and follow advice if a health condition could affect their judgement or performance; depression that is treated and managed need not be reported.","Confidentiality: his care and records sit outside his own hospital where possible; occupational health shares fitness advice, not clinical detail, with his employer."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're right: the neat story that depression is simply low serotonin hasn't held up. But how a medicine works and whether it works are separate questions, and the trials on whether they work still stand."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Three medicines not working is frustrating, but it isn't the end of the road. There are options that work differently, and therapy works best alongside them."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Moderately severe depressive episode; no response to sertraline, paroxetine (worsened suicidal thoughts) or mirtazapine (ejaculatory dysfunction). Plan: cross-taper to vortioxetine 10 mg, high-intensity CBT, OH and Practitioner Health referral. Please continue fit note; small prescriptions."}],"write":"Outpatients. Dr Farhan Qureshi, 40M, consultant microbiologist (other trust). Moderately severe depressive episode since separation; divorce finalised this year. Off sick 3/52.\nSymptoms: low mood, anhedonia, early waking (~5 h), poor appetite, poor concentration. Alcohol: 2 glasses wine most nights.\nRisk: passive thoughts children would cope without him; no plan or intent; no access to or use of work medicines; protective: children (alternate weekends).\nMedication: sertraline 100 mg 8/52 no response; paroxetine 30 mg 6/52, stopped due to worsening suicidal thoughts; mirtazapine 45 mg ~7/52, sleep improved, mood not; ejaculatory dysfunction, weight gain. Adherent.\nDiscussed: serotonin hypothesis vs trial evidence for efficacy. Options: switch (vortioxetine, agomelatine), augmentation (lithium, aripiprazole), rTMS, CBT.\nPlan (shared decision): cross-taper mirtazapine to vortioxetine 10 mg mane, titrate to 20 mg at 2–4 weeks; baseline bloods; high-intensity CBT referral; behavioural activation; reduce alcohol. OH referral for phased return without on-call; Practitioner Health referral. Crisis plan given. Review 2 weeks.\nDr C. Chinonso","make":["Agree with what is true about serotonin before you say anything else; a sceptical colleague needs honesty first.","Separate mechanism from effectiveness, then move on to options; do not debate the paper for five minutes.","Take the full medication history with doses and durations; you cannot call depression treatment-resistant without it.","Ask about sexual side effects plainly and kindly; it is often the real reason people give up on tablets.","For each option, say how it works and whether it suits him.","Do not skip risk because he is a doctor; ask about plans and access to medicines."],"next":"He switches to vortioxetine and starts CBT through Practitioner Health. By week six his sleep and concentration are better and the sexual side effect has gone. Occupational health agrees a phased return without on-call at week eight.","marks":["Took a brief history of onset and current symptoms.","Assessed suicide risk, including access to medicines.","Took a full medication history with doses, durations and side effects.","Answered the serotonin question honestly and separated mechanism from effectiveness.","Discussed switching, augmentation, rTMS and therapy with how each works.","Agreed a shared plan including return to work."],"pitfalls":["Defending the chemical imbalance model or dismissing his reading.","Skipping the history or the risk assessment because he is a doctor.","Listing every possible treatment without recommending one.","Ignoring the sexual side effect.","Suggesting he be treated by colleagues in his own hospital."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 guideline on adult depression (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE TA367 Vortioxetine for treating major depressive episodes (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE IPG542 Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation for depression (2015; checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC Good medical practice (2024; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK doctors with mental illness can use Practitioner Health, a free confidential NHS service in England. Treating a doctor-patient as a colleague does not mean skipping the history or risk assessment; doctors have a higher suicide risk than the general population.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-52","n":1352,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧂","title":"Puffy hands and trouble swallowing after a week of big meals: refeeding syndrome","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:30, CAMHS duty doctor","where":"CAMHS community team base, phone call back to a nurse","text":"Rhiannon Bell, a newly qualified CAMHS community nurse, has been visiting Amira Hughes, a 16-year-old girl with anorexia nervosa, for two weeks. Last week Amira's frightened mother pushed her to eat large meals and she gained 3 kg in seven days. Now her hands and feet are swollen and swallowing is hard. GP bloods a week ago showed only a mildly low phosphate. Rhiannon has left a message: 'Please call me back, I'm worried about her.'"},"task":"Without taking a history, address the nurse's concerns, explain refeeding syndrome and the cause of the swelling and swallowing difficulty, arrange same-day medical assessment, and discuss treatment and prevention.","first60":["Pull up Amira's record: latest weight, height and BMI centile, the GP bloods (phosphate, potassium, magnesium) and any ECG.","Check who is her consultant and which paediatric team covers her, and have the paediatric registrar's bleep ready.","Remember the task: no full history. Confirm the key facts and ask one safety question about how she is right now.","Plan the order: name it, cause, mechanism, her two symptoms, danger, today's plan, prevention, who does what.","Rhiannon is newly qualified and frightened: be calm, plain and supportive, and never imply she missed something."],"ask":["'Tell me what you've noticed, and how she seemed today.'","'Is she breathless, faint, confused, or complaining of chest pain or palpitations right now?'","'How much did she gain, and over how long?'","'Has she had any ECG or bloods since the GP's a week ago?'","'What do you understand might be happening?'","'How are you feeling about this? Is your supervisor aware?'"],"recognise":["Weight gain of 3 kg in a week is far above the 0.5 to 1 kg a week aimed for; most of it is fluid, not tissue.","New swelling after an increase in intake in a starved person is refeeding syndrome until proved otherwise, and can herald heart failure.","Low phosphate is the hallmark: it drives muscle weakness, including swallowing and breathing muscles.","Risk is highest in the first days to two weeks of increased intake; a 'normal' blood test a week ago does not reassure today.","Stopping food is also dangerous: underfeeding in hospital is a recognised harm. The answer is controlled, monitored feeding."],"decide":[{"c":"Arrange same-day paediatric assessment now: ECG, urgent electrolytes, replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium, start thiamine, and a dietitian-led feeding plan with daily bloods.","r":"Right: refeeding syndrome can kill through arrhythmia and heart or breathing failure, and it is treatable when caught early.","best":true},{"c":"Tell Rhiannon to ask the mother to stop the big meals and recheck bloods with the GP next week.","r":"Unsafe: symptoms show it is already happening. Delay risks arrhythmia, and stopping food without a plan risks underfeeding.","best":false},{"c":"Advise a diuretic from the GP for the swelling.","r":"Wrong: diuretics worsen potassium and magnesium loss and treat the sign, not the cause.","best":false}],"law":["At 16 Amira is presumed to have capacity under the MCA 2005. Seek her consent for admission and tests; share information with her mother in her interests and tell her you are doing so.","If she refuses urgent treatment, the paediatric and CAMHS consultants assess capacity; do not rely on her mother's consent to override a 16-year-old's refusal. Treatment for anorexia, including feeding, can be given under the Mental Health Act if criteria are met.","Record the advice, the time and who was told. Inform the CAMHS consultant today."],"say":[{"to":"Nurse","s":"You were right to call. I think this is refeeding syndrome: her body is struggling with food coming in too fast after a long time without enough. It's serious, but very treatable if she's seen today."},{"to":"Nurse","s":"Please don't stop her eating. Feeding carries on, just slowly and with the hospital checking her bloods every day."},{"to":"Paediatric registrar","s":"Sixteen-year-old with anorexia, 3 kg gain in a week after a rapid intake increase, new peripheral oedema and difficulty swallowing, phosphate low a week ago. I'm worried about refeeding syndrome. Can she be seen today for ECG, electrolytes and admission?"}],"write":"CAMHS duty. Phone call to Rhiannon Bell, CAMHS community nurse, re Amira Hughes, 16F, anorexia nervosa. No history taken, as agreed.\nReported: 3 kg gain in 7 days after mother increased meals; new swelling hands and feet; difficulty swallowing; GP bloods 1/52 ago: phosphate mildly low, otherwise normal. Not breathless, no chest pain or faints reported today.\nImpression: probable refeeding syndrome.\nAdvice: explained cause, mechanism, oedema and swallowing, complications. Feeding to continue at a controlled rate, not stopped. Advised no food pushed while swallowing is weak.\nAction: spoke to paediatric registrar 11:45, Amira to attend paediatric assessment unit today with mother: ECG, phosphate, K, Mg, Ca, U&E, glucose, LFT, FBC; replacement and thiamine; dietitian plan per MEED; likely admission. CAMHS consultant informed. Rhiannon to phone mother now and document.\nSafety net: 999 if chest pain, palpitations, faint, breathlessness, confusion or fit.\nDr Chinonso, CT3","make":["Respect 'do not take a history': confirm the facts you were given and ask one safety question, then explain.","Answer each of her questions in turn: what is it, why, why the swelling, why the swallowing, what now, how to prevent it.","Say 'today' and say who will do it: a plan with no time and no name is not a plan.","Explain the mechanism in one picture: insulin surges and drags the salts into the cells.","Feeding does not stop. Say this clearly, or a frightened family will stop all food.","Support the new nurse: she did the right thing by ringing; name her next steps and her supervisor."],"next":"Amira is admitted to the paediatric ward that afternoon. Her phosphate is 0.6 mmol/L and potassium 3.1. She has oral phosphate, potassium, thiamine and a vitamin B compound, and the dietitian sets a steady plan. The swelling settles in five days and her swallowing returns. Family therapy starts on discharge, and her mother says she finally understands why slower is safer.","marks":["Confirmed the key facts and one safety question without taking a history.","Named refeeding syndrome and explained the cause and mechanism plainly.","Explained the swelling and swallowing difficulty and the risks.","Arranged same-day paediatric assessment with ECG, bloods, replacement and thiamine.","Explained controlled feeding, prevention and support for the mother.","Agreed who does what, safety-netted and supported the nurse."],"pitfalls":["Taking a full eating disorder history instead of answering her questions.","Explaining well but never arranging assessment today.","Saying 'stop feeding her' or implying food is the danger.","Blaming the mother or the nurse.","Suggesting a diuretic for the swelling."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Psychiatrists CR233: Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED): guidance on recognition and management (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG32 Nutrition support for adults (refeeding risk principles; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF for Children: phosphate supplements; thiamine (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, MEED (2022) replaced MARSIPAN and Junior MARSIPAN. It warns against both refeeding too fast and underfeeding too cautiously. Teams now start higher than they used to, with close monitoring, so 'start low and stay low' is outdated.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-53","n":1353,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚨","title":"Staying in his room: schizophrenia relapse, or PTSD after a police restraint?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:30, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team clinic","text":"Kieran Adebayo, a 26-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia, was discharged from an inpatient ward six months ago after a relapse. His parents say that for several months he has been short-tempered, stays in his bedroom and will not leave the house. He takes olanzapine 10 mg at night. The team assumed another relapse; his care coordinator is not sure. He has come to clinic but sits with his hood up and says, 'They worry about everything.'"},"task":"Take a history from a guarded young man to reach a diagnosis: rule out psychotic relapse, find the trauma and its reminder, elicit the post-traumatic symptoms, assess mood and risk, and explain your conclusion.","first60":["Read the discharge summary: what happened before the last admission, how he came in (police, Section 136, restraint), cannabis use, and his relapse signature.","Check the care coordinator's notes and the pharmacy record or depot-free adherence evidence: is olanzapine collected and taken?","Ask the care coordinator what changed and when: did it start suddenly after an event, or creep up like his previous relapses?","Sit at an angle, not between him and the door; plan to go slowly with open questions, then specific ones.","Keep the structure: change, the event, reliving, avoidance, on edge, psychosis screen, mood, risk, then explain."],"ask":["'Your family say you're staying in a lot. What's that like from your side?'","'Can you tell me what happened before you went into hospital last time?'","'At that moment, what did you think was going to happen to you?'","'When did these memories start coming back, and was there something that set them off?'","'Do you get pictures in your head, or dreams, that make you feel you're back there?'","'Are there things you avoid because they remind you of it?'","'Have you heard voices, or felt people were watching or plotting against you, like before?'","'Do you ever feel like harming yourself, or anyone else, including the police?'"],"recognise":["Re-experiencing in the present: flashbacks with the same body sensations (weight on his back, grass on his face), and nightmares of the restraint.","Avoidance: no TV dramas, films or games; stays in to avoid police cars and sirens.","Sense of current threat: on edge, startles at loud noises, scans the street, sleeps badly.","Onset after a reminder months after the event is common; delayed onset does not exclude PTSD.","Psychosis screen negative, olanzapine taken nightly, cannabis stopped: the new symptoms are a new disorder, not a relapse.","Low mood, poor motivation and appetite are common with PTSD; note he blames the police, not himself."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose PTSD with schizophrenia in remission, continue olanzapine unchanged, and refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, with grounding skills and family support while he waits.","r":"Right: it treats what is actually wrong, keeps him well from psychosis, and uses the first-line treatment that works in people with psychosis too.","best":true},{"c":"Increase olanzapine to 15 mg for a probable relapse.","r":"Wrong: there are no psychotic symptoms. Extra antipsychotic adds weight gain and sedation and leaves the PTSD untreated.","best":false},{"c":"Avoid trauma therapy because talking about the restraint might trigger a psychotic relapse.","r":"Outdated: trauma-focused therapy is safe and effective in people with stable psychosis. Withholding it leaves him housebound.","best":false}],"law":["If he was detained by police under Section 136 or restrained during admission, he may wish to complain; explain that he can, and that it will not affect his care. Signpost to PALS or the police complaints route.","Confidentiality: ask before sharing details of the trauma with his parents; agree what they can be told to help them support him.","Risk to others: anger at the police without intent or plan does not justify sharing outside the team; record it and review."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for telling me that. Being held down and believing you'd die is terrifying. It makes sense your mind keeps going back there."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've said, your schizophrenia looks settled. What you're describing is post-traumatic stress disorder, a reaction to that arrest. It's common, it's not your fault, and therapy works well for it."},{"to":"Care coordinator","s":"Not a psychotic relapse. PTSD after restraint during his last admission, triggered by a TV arrest scene. Olanzapine unchanged. Please refer for trauma-focused CBT and start grounding work with him now."}],"write":"CMHT clinic. Kieran Adebayo, 26M. Paranoid schizophrenia, discharged 6/12 ago after 2/12 admission (relapse after stopping olanzapine and cannabis).\nPresenting: 3/12 irritability, staying in room, not leaving house.\nTrauma: restrained face-down by police on grass before admission; could not breathe, believed he would die.\nOnset: TV drama arrest scene ~3/12 ago brought it back.\nSymptoms: daily intrusive images with weight on back and smell of grass; nightmares 3-4/week; avoids TV, films, games, going out (sirens, police cars); startle, hypervigilance, irritable; sleep poor.\nMood: low, little motivation, appetite down; no guilt, no worthlessness; blames police.\nPsychosis: no voices, no persecutory beliefs; olanzapine nightly (mother sees); no cannabis since discharge; alcohol minimal.\nRisk: no suicidal thoughts or self-harm; angry at police, no intent or plan to harm; no aggression to family.\nMSE: guarded then engaged, hood up, startled at a door bang, mood low, no psychotic features, insight good.\nImpression: PTSD; schizophrenia in remission.\nPlan: explained, teach-back good. Continue olanzapine 10 mg nocte. Refer psychology for trauma-focused CBT. Care coordinator: grounding skills, nightmare plan, graded outings. Family session with consent. Crisis numbers. Review 4/52.\nDr Chinonso, ST5","make":["Go slowly: a guarded man gives more to gentle, specific questions than to a barrage of open ones.","Screen for psychosis once, properly, and accept the answer; don't spend the whole interview hunting for voices.","Ask about the admission itself: how he came in is often where the trauma is.","Find the reminder and the timing: symptoms that began after a trigger point to PTSD rather than relapse.","Ask for the senses: what he saw, felt and smelled in the flashback makes the diagnosis clear.","Ask about harm to others, including the people he is angry with."],"next":"Kieran's care coordinator teaches him grounding with a smell and a phrase, and he starts trauma-focused CBT with the psychology team six weeks later. After ten sessions his nightmares fall to one a week and he walks to the shop with his brother. His olanzapine stays the same and his psychosis remains settled.","marks":["Engaged a guarded man and clarified the recent change and its onset.","Elicited the traumatic arrest and the reminder that started the symptoms.","Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal.","Excluded psychotic relapse, checking adherence and cannabis.","Assessed mood and risk to self, family and police.","Explained PTSD with his own experiences and the treatment that helps."],"pitfalls":["Assuming relapse and spending the time hunting for voices.","Never asking how he came into hospital last time.","Calling it 'just anxiety' without asking about flashbacks and nightmares.","Increasing olanzapine without evidence of psychosis.","Leaving out risk to others when he is angry with the police."],"refs":["1. NICE NG116 Post-traumatic stress disorder (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: 6B40 Post-traumatic stress disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Post-traumatic stress disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, restraint and police detention are recognised as possible trauma, and services now ask about them routinely. Trauma-focused therapy is offered to people with psychosis; it is not seen as too risky.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-54","n":1354,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🥃","title":"Empty bottles and spare clozapine: a formulation for his sister","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:00, community clinic","where":"Community mental health team base, interview room","text":"Leah Farrow, a 38-year-old woman, has driven two hours to talk about her younger brother, Martin Ellery, 35, who has paranoid schizophrenia and has taken clozapine for years. Both their parents, his main carers, died within the last six months. He now lives alone in the family home. Leah has found empty whisky and beer bottles and far more clozapine than there should be; he says people are watching him again. Martin has agreed you can speak to her. She says, 'It's been a horrible year.'"},"task":"Speak with Martin's sister: clarify what has changed and what she has seen, explain a formulation of his difficulties, and explain the effects of alcohol on his physical and mental health, including with clozapine.","first60":["Confirm Martin's consent to share information with Leah is recorded, and check what he asked to keep private.","Read the clozapine record: dose, last plasma level, last FBC and neutrophils, smoking status, and when the pharmacy last dispensed.","Check for seizures, constipation, heart or liver problems in his history, and his relapse signature before clozapine.","Plan: condolences, what changed, what she sees, the gaps, her worries, then formulation, alcohol, clozapine and the plan.","Remember she is grieving too, with two small children and a two-hour drive."],"ask":["'What were your parents doing for Martin day to day?'","'Do you have a sense of how often he drinks, and how much?'","'When you arrive in the morning, does he seem shaky or sweaty before he's had a drink?'","'How often do you think he's missed his clozapine, and when did he last take it?'","'Does he smoke, or use anything else, like cannabis?'","'Any seizures ever? Heart or bowel trouble?'","'Has he ever talked about wishing he was dead, or not wanting to go on?'","'What worries you most, and how are you coping?'"],"recognise":["Predisposing: long-standing treatment-resistant schizophrenia and years of relying on his parents for tablets, meals and outings.","Precipitating: the deaths of both parents within months, with sudden loss of care, routine and company.","Perpetuating: drinking to numb grief and fear, missed clozapine, living alone, and early paranoia that keeps him indoors.","Protective: years of stability on clozapine, a sister who cares, a team he knows, and his agreement to let you talk.","Spare tablets mean missed doses: if more than 48 hours, clozapine must be restarted at a low dose; the stockpile is also an overdose risk.","Morning shakes or drinking on waking suggest dependence; stopping suddenly risks withdrawal fits, made worse by clozapine."],"decide":[{"c":"Arrange an urgent team review this week: assess mental state, risk and drinking, check when clozapine was last taken, restart and retitrate if needed, remove the stockpile, and offer bereavement and alcohol support with a carers' assessment for Leah.","r":"Right: it addresses the three linked problems together, relapse, alcohol and grief, and makes the stockpile safe.","best":true},{"c":"Advise Leah to tell him to restart his usual clozapine dose tonight.","r":"Dangerous: after more than 48 hours off, his usual dose can cause collapse, fits or heart problems. It must be restarted at 12.5 mg and built up.","best":false},{"c":"Tell Leah he must stop drinking at once.","r":"Risky: if he is dependent, sudden stopping can cause withdrawal fits and delirium, especially on clozapine. Withdrawal needs to be planned.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Martin has consented; share what is needed and respect any limits he set. Listening to Leah's information is always allowed.","Carers' assessment: Leah has a right to one from the local authority under the Care Act 2014, even though she lives away.","If he relapses and refuses help, an MHA assessment may be needed; Leah, as nearest relative, can ask for one from the local AMHP service."],"say":[{"to":"Sister","s":"I'm so sorry about your parents. You've lost them too, and you're still driving two hours with two little ones to check on him."},{"to":"Sister","s":"Here's how I see it. Martin has an illness that leaves him vulnerable. He lost the two people who held his days together. He's drinking to cope, missing his clozapine, and the old fears are creeping back."},{"to":"Care coordinator","s":"Sister reports heavy drinking, possible morning shakes, about two weeks of missed clozapine and early paranoia since both parents died. Please visit within 48 hours: mental state, risk, AUDIT, last clozapine dose, remove stockpile, bloods and level."}],"write":"CMHT. Met Leah Farrow (sister), 38F, re Martin Ellery, 35M, TRS on clozapine 400 mg/day. Martin's consent to share recorded.\nChange: both parents died within 6/12; they managed his tablets, meals, washing and daily outings. Now alone in family home.\nLeah reports: low mood, drinking more (whisky and beer bottles; possibly a bottle of whisky every 2-3 days plus beer), hands shaky in mornings; says people watching him outside (as before clozapine); ~2 weeks of clozapine left over; says he forgets. Smokes ~20/day. No other drugs. No fits. Mentioned constipation. Said 'what's the point' once; no plans known. Leah is only family support; neighbour checks occasionally.\nLeah's concerns: relapse to pre-clozapine state, guilt at distance, coping with own grief.\nExplained: formulation (predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating, protective), alcohol effects on mind and body, alcohol with clozapine, 48-hour rule, stockpile risk. Teach-back good.\nPlan: care coordinator visit within 48 h: MSE, risk, AUDIT, last dose, retitrate if >48 h, FBC/ANC, LFT, GGT, U&E, clozapine level, ECG; spare tablets to pharmacy; weekly blister pack; alcohol service; bereavement support; carers' assessment referral for Leah. Crisis numbers given.\nDr Chinonso, ST5","make":["Start with the loss: ask what the parents did for him, because that is the heart of the formulation.","Ask for numbers even from a relative: how often, how much, and any morning shakes.","Screen the clozapine-specific risks: smoking, fits, constipation and when the last dose was.","Give the formulation as a story with a beginning, a trigger and a vicious circle, then name the strengths.","Explain the 48-hour rule and the stockpile in plain words; it is the most dangerous fact in the room.","Remember Leah is grieving too, and offer her support in her own right."],"next":"The care coordinator visits the next day. Martin last took clozapine three days ago; it is restarted at 12.5 mg and built up over two weeks with daily checks. The spare tablets go back to the pharmacy and a weekly blister pack starts. He accepts help from the alcohol service and a bereavement counsellor. Leah has a carers' assessment and joins a Rethink carers' group online.","marks":["Clarified the bereavement and the care the parents provided.","Gathered drinking, mood, paranoia and missed clozapine, plus smoking, fits, health and support.","Explored Leah's own worries.","Gave a clear formulation with strengths.","Explained alcohol's physical and psychological effects, and with clozapine.","Addressed the stockpile and the 48-hour rule and agreed next steps."],"pitfalls":["Reciting alcohol's harms with no formulation linking grief, drink and missed tablets.","Missing the 48-hour restart rule for clozapine.","Ignoring the stockpile as an overdose risk.","Forgetting smoking, constipation and seizure risk with clozapine.","Not acknowledging Leah's own grief."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG115 Alcohol-use disorders (2011; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG58 Coexisting severe mental illness and substance misuse (2016; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF and SPC: clozapine (checked Nov 2026)","5. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and gastrointestinal hypomotility (2020; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, a formulation is shared in plain words, often using the 4 Ps (predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating, protective). Relatives value hearing it as a story that makes sense of what they've seen.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-55","n":1355,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🌧️","title":"'My husband is going to kill me': psychotic depression in the emergency department","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 12:15, liaison psychiatry","where":"Emergency department, quiet assessment room","text":"Kayleigh Doherty, a 27-year-old hairdresser, was brought in by two colleagues after telling them her husband means to kill her. She has been off sick most of the last month; before that she was distracted, restless, withdrawn and often found crying. She has never been aggressive and often looked scared. In the taxi she said she deserved to die because she was useless. The summary care record shows citalopram 20 mg for four weeks and possible depression before she moved here. She whispers, 'I didn't know where else to go.'"},"task":"Interview her: brief history of the presenting complaint, screen the past history, and assess her mental state to reach a diagnosis; then share your impression and what happens next.","first60":["Read the triage notes and the summary care record: citalopram start date, any previous psychiatric letters, allergies and observations.","Ask her colleagues what they saw and heard, then thank them and see her alone.","Check her physical state: has she eaten or drunk, observations, any self-harm, and whether bloods are needed.","Find a quiet room with a nurse nearby; sit at her level; plan to go slowly, as she may be slowed.","Hold the structure: the belief, the mood, psychotic features, past, risk, mental state, then explain."],"ask":["'You said you're frightened of your husband. Can you tell me what's been happening?'","'What makes you sure he wants to harm you? Has he ever hurt you or threatened you?'","'Why do you think he would want to?'","'When did you first start feeling low, and what came first?'","'Has food tasted or smelled different lately?'","'Have you heard voices when no one is around?'","'Have you been unwell like this before? Were you ever in hospital?'","'Have things felt so bad you've thought of killing yourself? Any thoughts of hurting someone else?'"],"recognise":["The belief grew out of the depression and fits it: she is useless, so she deserves punishment. That is a mood-congruent delusion, not a primary psychosis.","Early waking, weight loss, slowed speech and movement, guilt and worthlessness make this a severe depressive episode.","A bitter taste in food she links to poisoning is a gustatory hallucination; ask, as she will not volunteer it.","Not eating for days is self-neglect and a medical risk; it raises urgency even without active suicidal plans.","A previous admission with two medicines, stopped after moving, and a poor response to citalopram point to recurrent and severe illness.","Real abuse must be excluded privately: a frightened woman who says her husband will kill her might be right."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose severe depression with psychotic symptoms, explain it plainly, recommend admission today (informal if she agrees), arrange bloods and ECG, and plan an antidepressant with an antipsychotic.","r":"Right: she is severely ill, not eating, believes she deserves to die, and has failed outpatient treatment; she needs care she cannot get at home.","best":true},{"c":"Increase citalopram to 40 mg and discharge her home with crisis team follow-up.","r":"Unsafe: psychotic depression rarely responds to an antidepressant alone, she is not eating, and home is where her delusion is centred.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose a persecutory delusional disorder and start an antipsychotic only.","r":"Wrong: the belief is driven by guilt and worthlessness within a severe depression. Treating the psychosis alone misses the illness.","best":false}],"law":["She is in the emergency department, not admitted to a ward, so the doctor's holding power (Section 5(2)) does not apply. If she tries to leave and lacks capacity for that decision, act in her best interests using the MCA and request an urgent MHA assessment.","If she has capacity and refuses admission, ask for an MHA assessment if the risk is high; record capacity and reasons.","Domestic abuse: ask when alone. If there is abuse, follow the safeguarding procedure and offer a referral to an IDVA; do not share with her husband.","Confidentiality: ask what she wants her husband and colleagues told; share without consent only to prevent serious harm."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're safe here, and I'm glad you came. Take your time; we can go slowly."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I think you have a severe depression, and it has become so heavy that it's changing how you see things, including your husband. That's an illness, it's not your fault, and it's very treatable."},{"to":"Ward bed manager","s":"27-year-old woman, severe depressive episode with mood-congruent delusions and a gustatory hallucination, not eaten for two days, believes she deserves to die. Agreeing to informal admission. Needs a bed today, with enhanced observations."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, ED. Kayleigh Doherty, 27F, hairdresser. Brought by colleagues.\nHPC: low mood ~2/12, began with waking at 4am; anhedonia, poor concentration, low energy, crying, off work 1/12; weight loss ~5 kg; not eaten 2 days, drinking water; unwashed for a week.\nPsychotic features: believes husband Liam is planning to kill her as punishment because she is useless; food has tasted bitter for ~1 week, believes he is poisoning it; certain. Guilt, worthlessness, 'I deserve to die'. No voices, visions or passivity. No nihilistic or poverty beliefs.\nNo actual threats or violence from husband (asked alone); he is described as kind and worried.\nPast: admission for depression ~3 years ago in previous area, treated with two medicines; stopped 6/12 ago after moving; no services since. Citalopram 20 mg 4/52, no benefit. Never manic. No alcohol or drugs. No children.\nRisk: no plan or intent to end life but would not resist dying; high self-neglect risk; no thoughts of harming husband.\nMSE: unkempt, hunched, psychomotor retardation, quiet slow speech; mood low, affect flat and fearful; mood-congruent persecutory and guilt delusions; gustatory hallucination; oriented, poor concentration; insight partial.\nImpression: recurrent depressive disorder, current episode severe with psychotic symptoms.\nPlan: explained; agrees informal admission. Bloods incl. FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium, pregnancy test; ECG. Enhanced observations. Consultant informed. Consent to tell husband she is in hospital only.\nDr Chinonso, CT3","make":["Make her feel safe before asking anything: she has come because she is terrified.","Ask how the belief started and what came first: depression before the belief marks it as psychotic depression.","Ask about taste and smell of food; the gustatory hallucination and the self-neglect both hide there.","Ask about real abuse privately and take her seriously until you are sure.","Ask about the past episode and medicines: she won't volunteer the admission.","Tell her the diagnosis and that hospital is likely, gently and honestly, and stay with her reaction."],"next":"Kayleigh agrees to come in informally. Citalopram is switched to sertraline and olanzapine 5 mg at night is added; she starts eating with nurse support on day three. Three weeks later the bitter taste has gone and she says Liam 'was never going to hurt me'. She is discharged with the home treatment team and starts CBT through the CMHT.","marks":["Made her feel safe and explored the belief, including asking privately about real abuse.","Elicited the depressive syndrome, guilt and worthlessness.","Elicited the gustatory hallucination and screened other psychotic features.","Screened the past episode, medicines, mania and substances.","Assessed suicide, self-neglect and risk to her husband.","Explained severe depression with psychotic symptoms and the need for admission."],"pitfalls":["Calling it a primary persecutory psychosis because she fears her husband.","Never asking about food, taste or eating.","Assuming her husband is safe without asking about abuse privately.","Missing the previous admission and the medicines she stopped.","Agreeing to let her go home because she has no suicidal plan."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6A70/6A71 Depressive episode, severe, with psychotic symptoms (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters on holding powers and emergency departments (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE PH50 Domestic violence and abuse: multi-agency working (2014; checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE TA59 ECT guidance (2003; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, ask about domestic abuse routinely and alone, even when the fear sounds delusional. And remember Section 5(2) only applies to inpatients; in the emergency department, use the MCA or request an MHA assessment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-56","n":1356,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🫀","title":"Depression after a heart attack: preparing a medical student to present Mr Desai","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 13:30","where":"Community mental health team, outpatient clinic office","text":"Mr Vikram Desai, a 57-year-old man, has been re-referred by his GP. You treated his depression for years; he stopped sertraline after discharge, then had a heart attack. Now he is low, sleeping and eating poorly, skipping cardiac rehab and his heart tablets, and still smoking. Freya Lindqvist, a third-year medical student, will see him next week. She says, 'I don't really get why psychiatry cares about cardiac rehab.'"},"task":"Teach a third-year medical student about depression after a heart attack: find what she needs, link mood and heart outcomes, cover assessment, safe medicines, therapy, rehab, smoking and the wider team, and check she can present him.","first60":["Skim the GP letter and your old clinic letters: past episodes, what worked (sertraline), when he stopped and why.","Note the cardiac facts the student will need: date of the heart attack, his current cardiac medicines, and whether he is on antiplatelets.","Plan to start by asking what she already knows and what her consultant expects, rather than lecturing.","Keep three parts in your head: why it matters, what to assess, and what the plan looks like.","Have one reading suggestion ready for her: NICE CG91 on depression alongside physical illness."],"ask":["'What do you know about Mr Desai so far, and what does your consultant want from you?'","'What worries you most about seeing him?'","'Why do you think a psychiatrist might care whether he goes to cardiac rehab?'","'Which medicines might be risky for someone just after a heart attack?'","'How would you ask him about suicidal thoughts?'","'Can you give me the plan back in two or three sentences, the way you'd present it?'"],"recognise":["About one in five people have depression after a heart attack; many more have milder symptoms that still matter.","The link runs both ways: depression is an independent risk factor for heart disease, and a heart attack can trigger depression.","Untreated depression after a heart attack goes with worse quality of life, poorer adherence to medicines and rehab, and higher rates of further cardiac events and death.","Stopping an antidepressant because you feel well is a common cause of relapse; explore his beliefs, not just the fact.","Missing heart tablets can be passive self-harm; ask directly.","Low energy, poor sleep and appetite can come from heart failure or medicines too; check the physical picture."],"decide":[{"c":"Find her goals first, then teach in three parts (why it matters, what to assess, the plan), using plain terms, and finish with her presenting the plan back to you.","r":"Right: it meets her actual need, keeps the teaching tied to Mr Desai, and proves she can use it with her consultant.","best":true},{"c":"Give her a detailed talk on the major trials and send her a reading list.","r":"Unhelpful: she needs practical points for next week, not trial names, and you never check what she can use.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her the cardiac rehab side is for the cardiologists and she should focus on his mood.","r":"Wrong: treating his depression is how he gets back to rehab and his tablets; the two plans are one plan.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: the student may read the notes and present him for teaching, but her own notes must be anonymised and stay off personal devices and social media.","Mr Desai should be told a student will see him and can say no; ask for his consent at the start of the appointment.","GMC: you remain responsible for supervising the student; she should not give him treatment decisions on her own."],"say":[{"to":"Student","s":"Think of it as one problem, not two. If we treat his mood, he's far more likely to take his heart tablets and get back to rehab."},{"to":"Student","s":"Sertraline is the antidepressant with the best safety evidence after a heart attack. It worked for him before, which helps too."},{"to":"Student","s":"Ask about suicide plainly. With him, also ask whether skipping his heart tablets is deliberate."}],"write":"Teaching session with medical student (Freya Lindqvist, 3rd year), re: Mr V Desai, 57M, clinic next week.\nCovered: post-MI depression (about 1 in 5), two-way link, effect on adherence, rehab and cardiac outcomes; assessment points (depressive symptoms, suicide and passive self-harm via missed medicines, alcohol, smoking, beliefs about his heart); plan (restart sertraline, bleeding and sodium checks, CBT via NHS Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway, cardiac rehab liaison, smoking cessation, dietitian and OT, work).\nStudent summarised accurately. Reminded re anonymised notes. She will see him with me present for the risk part. Debrief after clinic.\nDr Chinonso CT3","make":["Ask before you teach: her goals, what she knows, what her consultant wants.","Keep every point tied to Mr Desai: 'for him, that means…'.","Give numbers she can quote: about one in five, sertraline 50 mg, 8 to 16 CBT sessions.","Join up mood and heart: adherence, rehab, smoking and survival.","Name the drugs to avoid and why, in one breath each.","End with teach-back and a plan for supervision on the day."],"next":"Freya sees Mr Desai with you nearby. He admits he stopped his heart tablets because 'what's the point'. He has no plan to end his life. He restarts sertraline and accepts a home-based rehab start and a referral to the stop smoking service. Freya presents him clearly to her consultant, with a short formulation.","marks":["Found her goals, knowledge and her consultant's expectations before teaching.","Explained how common post-MI depression is and the two-way link with heart disease.","Linked untreated depression to poor adherence, missed rehab and worse cardiac outcomes.","Named sertraline as the safest choice, with interactions and drugs to avoid.","Covered CBT alongside rehab, smoking cessation, alcohol and the wider team.","Checked her understanding with a summary and arranged supervision."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing on trials without saying what they mean for Mr Desai.","Forgetting his cardiac medicines when choosing an antidepressant.","Leaving out suicide risk, including deliberate non-adherence.","Treating rehab and smoking as someone else's job.","Never checking what the student can actually present."],"refs":["1. NICE CG91: depression alongside chronic physical illness in adults (2009; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG185 Acute coronary syndromes, including cardiac rehabilitation (2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline, interactions with antiplatelets (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG209 Tobacco: preventing uptake, promoting quitting and treating dependence (2021; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK you will often teach medical students, and you are expected to supervise them safely. Ask what they need first. Students must anonymise notes, and patients can decline to see them.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-57","n":1357,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🧩","title":"Autism or psychosis? Assessing Jordan's relapse in A&E","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 17:20","where":"Emergency department, mental health assessment room","text":"Mr Jordan Ashworth, a 28-year-old autistic man with schizophrenia, was brought in by police after shouting at a stranger he thought was following him. His mother says he has been suspicious and preoccupied for weeks. He has taken risperidone for ten years but may have stopped. He paces by the door and says, 'How long will this take? I need to go.'"},"task":"Carry out an autism-adapted mental state assessment: find what happened and what changed, elicit and characterise psychotic symptoms, tell them apart from his autistic traits, screen mood and substances, and assess insight and risk.","first60":["Check his legal status with the police and the nurse: brought under section 136, or voluntarily?","Read the notes and any hospital passport: his usual presentation, special interests, communication needs and previous relapse signs.","Ask the family or police for the incident in brief: what he did, any weapon, anyone hurt.","Find a quiet room with low light and little noise; turn off the radio; one person speaks.","Plan short, literal questions, one at a time, with time to answer. No idioms."],"ask":["'What happened before the police brought you here?'","'What has changed for you in the last few months?'","'Are you still taking your risperidone? When did you stop?'","'Do you hear people talking when nobody is with you? What do they say?'","'Do you get messages from the TV, radio or internet?'","'Can other people hear or know your thoughts without you saying them?'","'Is this new, or have you always thought about this?'","'Do you want to hurt anyone? Do you want to hurt yourself?'"],"recognise":["Autistic baseline: lifelong special interests (for Jordan, trains), literal thinking, routines, flat expression and limited eye contact. These are not new and not symptoms of relapse.","Relapse features: new beliefs held with total conviction, voices, messages from the TV, thought broadcasting and decline in functioning.","The key question is 'what is new compared with his usual self?' Ask him and his family.","Loss of routine (the day centre closing) is a real trigger for an autistic person, and may lead to stopping medication.","Brief, literal answers are communication style, not evasiveness or negative symptoms.","Distress from noise, waiting and crowded rooms can look like agitation; reduce stimulation first."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the adapted assessment, explain clearly that he is unwell again and needs to stay, involve family, restart risperidone, and arrange an MHA assessment if he refuses admission and risk is high.","r":"Right: it rests on clear evidence of relapse, respects his autism and uses the least restrictive legal route.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge him home because he is calm now and his spy beliefs are part of his autism.","r":"Wrong: the beliefs are new, firmly held and paired with voices and thought broadcasting, after he stopped risperidone, and there was a street incident.","best":false},{"c":"Give IM haloperidol now so the assessment can go faster.","r":"Wrong: he is not violent, and autistic people may be more sensitive to side effects. Reduce stimulation and offer oral medicine first.","best":false}],"law":["If brought under section 136 (A&E counts as a place of safety), he can be held for up to 24 hours for assessment by a doctor and an AMHP.","If informal and he lacks capacity to decide about staying, short-term MCA steps may apply while an MHA assessment is arranged; get senior help.","Equality Act 2010 and the Oliver McGowan training standard: reasonable adjustments are a legal duty.","If admission is considered, a Care (Education) and Treatment Review is expected for autistic people."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Jordan, I will ask short questions. You can take your time. Tell me if a question is not clear."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I think your illness has come back. The voices and the TV messages are part of it. I want you to stay in hospital so we can help."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Is the interest in spies new, or has he always talked about it? That helps me tell his autism from the illness."}],"write":"ED. Mr J Ashworth, 28M. Autism, schizophrenia. Brought by police after shouting at a stranger he believed was following him. No weapon, nobody hurt.\nChanges: day centre closed ~3 months ago; stopped risperidone around then (\"I was told to stop by the agents\"); beliefs ~2 months; sleep poor.\nMSE: tense, pacing, little eye contact (baseline). Speech brief, literal (baseline). Mood 'fine'. Delusions: special agent awaiting a mission (fixed, 100%); TV sends coded messages (reference); strangers following him (persecutory). Hallucinations: second-person voices of agents giving updates; no commands to harm. Thought broadcasting. No insertion or withdrawal; no passivity. No cannabis or alcohol. Insight absent.\nAutism vs psychosis: train interest unchanged and lifelong; spy beliefs, voices and TV messages new.\nRisk: to others moderate (confrontation with strangers he believes are following him); to self low; absconding high; non-adherence high.\nImpression: relapse of schizophrenia after stopping risperidone, triggered by loss of routine.\nPlan: admission advised; restart risperidone; bloods, ECG; mother informed; if refuses, MHA assessment (AMHP contacted). Reasonable adjustments documented.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Adapt first: quiet room, short literal questions, one at a time, and silence while he thinks.","Always ask 'Is this new?' and check with family; that is how you separate autism from psychosis.","Find the trigger: change in routine, and why he stopped risperidone.","Go through psychosis in order: delusions and conviction, voices and their form, thought interference, passivity.","Check mood and substances briefly; don't skip them.","Finish with insight, risk including leaving, and a clear, literal explanation of what happens next."],"next":"Jordan refuses admission. An AMHP and two doctors assess him that evening, and he is admitted under section 2 to a quiet side room. Risperidone is restarted. A Care and Treatment Review is held within two weeks, and the day centre team agrees a new weekly timetable before discharge.","marks":["Adapted communication: short, literal questions asked singly, with time to reply.","Clarified the incident, the timeline and changes in routine, and when risperidone stopped.","Elicited and characterised delusions, voices and thought broadcasting, and screened insertion, withdrawal and passivity.","Separated lifelong autistic traits from new psychotic symptoms.","Screened mood and substances.","Assessed insight and risk to others, self, absconding and non-adherence."],"pitfalls":["Long, double questions or idioms like 'feeling under the weather'.","Calling his flat affect or literal answers negative symptoms.","Missing that losing the day centre is a cause, not background.","Accepting 'I'm fine' without asking what he would do if strangers followed him again.","Letting him leave without checking legal status."],"refs":["1. NICE CG142 Autism spectrum disorder in adults: diagnosis and management (2012, updated 2021; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","3. MHA 1983 Code of Practice: police powers and places of safety, chapter 16 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: Care (Education) and Treatment Reviews policy and guidance (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: the UK expects reasonable adjustments for autistic people as a legal duty. Ask about a hospital passport, offer a quiet room, and speak literally. Family often know best what is 'new'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-58","n":1358,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💍","title":"Dreading his own wedding: diagnosing social anxiety and its causes","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00","where":"Community mental health outpatient clinic","text":"Mr Aaron Whitfield, a 27-year-old nursery assistant, has been referred by his GP. He marries in six weeks and dreads the reception. He has always slipped away from weddings and parties, and his manager has temporarily excused him from leading group activities. He sits on the edge of his chair, flushed, and says, 'I know it's stupid. It's my own wedding.'"},"task":"Take a history to reach a diagnosis of social anxiety disorder: the feared situations and fear of judgement, body symptoms, avoidance and safety behaviours, impact, differentials including panic, agoraphobia, depression and alcohol, and the causes, then explain it briefly.","first60":["Read the GP letter: any previous anxiety or depression, current medicines, alcohol history.","Set a calm, private room; he is likely to find being looked at hard, so sit at an angle and keep it relaxed.","Plan to congratulate him first: the wedding is good news as well as a source of fear.","Keep two halves in mind: the diagnosis and its differentials, then the causes and what keeps it going.","Be ready to give a short, hopeful explanation and a wedding plan at the end."],"ask":["'What goes through your mind when you picture the reception?'","'What do you fear people will think or see?'","'Which other situations bring this on: eating in front of others, phone calls, meetings?'","'What happens in your body at the time?'","'What do you do to get through, or to avoid it?'","'Do you ever get panic attacks out of the blue, when nobody is around?'","'When did this start? What was school like?'","'Does anyone in your family worry in the same way?'"],"recognise":["The core is fear of being judged, embarrassed or humiliated in social or performance situations, out of proportion and lasting months or more.","Body signs that others can see (blushing, sweating, trembling) are especially feared, which feeds the fear.","Safety behaviours (rehearsing, avoiding eye contact, holding a drink, leaving early) keep the fear going because the feared outcome is never tested.","Panic attacks only in social settings fit social anxiety; unprovoked attacks suggest panic disorder.","Pervasive avoidance in all areas from adolescence, with feeling inadequate, raises avoidant personality disorder.","Pre-event drinking is common and risky: ask how much and how often."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose social anxiety disorder, explain it plainly, offer individual CBT for social anxiety and agree a specific wedding plan with him and, if he wishes, his fiancée.","r":"Right: NICE first-line treatment, a clear explanation and a practical goal he cares about.","best":true},{"c":"Prescribe propranolol for the wedding day and discharge him to the GP.","r":"Not recommended routinely: NICE advises against beta-blockers for social anxiety, and it leaves the lifelong problem untreated.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe diazepam to take before the reception.","r":"Wrong: benzodiazepines are not recommended for social anxiety, and mixing with wedding alcohol is dangerous.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: ask before involving his fiancée or contacting his employer.","Equality Act 2010: if his anxiety has a substantial long-term effect on daily life, his employer may need to make reasonable adjustments; occupational health can advise.","Driving: no DVLA issue unless alcohol misuse or sedating medicines affect driving."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Congratulations on the wedding. It's very common for something happy to bring an old fear to a head."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you've described is social anxiety disorder. It's common, it's not weakness, and it responds really well to a specific kind of CBT."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The leaving early and the pints before help in the moment, but they stop you learning that you'd have coped."}],"write":"OPC. Mr A Whitfield, 27M, nursery assistant. GP referral: dread of own wedding reception in 6 weeks.\nFear of negative evaluation in social and performance settings since primary school (teased for blushing; reading aloud first remembered episode). Avoids parties, weddings, eating in front of others, group phone calls; leaves early with excuses. Work: manager temporarily removed group-leading duties.\nSymptoms: blushing, dry mouth, sweating, racing heart, tremor; situational panic only. No unprovoked attacks, no agoraphobia.\nSafety behaviours: rehearsing, avoiding eye contact, two pints before events (no daily use; AUDIT-C 4).\nMood: worried, not depressed; sleep fine; no suicidal thoughts. No drugs.\nFamily: mother anxious; father strict. Shy, cautious child.\nImpression: social anxiety disorder, longstanding. Differentials considered: panic disorder, agoraphobia, depression, avoidant PD (not met: close friends, fiancée), alcohol misuse.\nPlan: explained; individual CBT for social anxiety via NHS Talking Therapies; wedding goals; alcohol advice; fiancée involvement with consent; review 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso ST4","make":["Congratulate him before anything else; then use an open question about the day.","Ask what he fears others will think: that is the diagnostic core.","Map the range: performance, eating, phone, groups, work.","Ask for safety behaviours and alcohol by name; he will not volunteer them.","Rule out unprovoked panic, agoraphobia, depression and avoidant personality.","Spend real time on causes: temperament, family, childhood events, and what keeps it going."],"next":"Aaron starts CBT for social anxiety two weeks later. With his fiancée, he plans a short speech, a quiet corner and planned breaks. He rehearses with video feedback in therapy and agrees not to drink before the ceremony. He gives the speech, shaking but proud, and returns to leading circle time a month later.","marks":["Opened warmly, congratulated him and explored the wedding fear.","Elicited fear of negative judgement across several social and performance situations.","Elicited body symptoms: blushing, sweating, dry mouth, trembling, racing heart.","Explored avoidance, safety behaviours, alcohol and the impact on work and life.","Screened for panic disorder, agoraphobia, depression, suicide and avoidant personality.","Explored causes: temperament, family history, childhood events, and maintaining factors."],"pitfalls":["Calling it shyness or wedding nerves and moving on.","Forgetting the second half of the task: the causes.","Missing the pre-event drinking.","Offering propranolol or diazepam as the main plan.","Asking closed, rapid questions that increase his embarrassment."],"refs":["1. NICE CG159 Social anxiety disorder: recognition, assessment and treatment (2013; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6B04 Social anxiety disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: Social anxiety (social phobia) (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression manual (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, people with social anxiety can self-refer to NHS Talking Therapies. Many patients feel embarrassed about such a 'small' problem; taking it seriously is itself therapeutic.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-59","n":1359,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"Refusing neck wound surgery: assessing Shane's capacity","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:10","where":"General hospital surgical ward, side room with two prison officers","text":"Mr Shane Mulholland, a 24-year-old man with emotionally unstable personality disorder, is serving two years for assault occasioning actual bodily harm. He cut his neck deeply with a piece of metal; no vessels, nerves or muscle are damaged. The plastic surgeons want to close it under general anaesthetic. He is refusing, and wants to be back in prison today. The surgical registrar says, 'Can you section him? Or say he lacks capacity?'"},"task":"Assess his capacity to refuse wound closure under general anaesthetic: a brief history of the cut and his background, a screen for depression and psychosis, the four functional tests, risk of further self-harm, and tell him and the team the outcome and what happens next.","first60":["Ask the surgeons exactly what they propose, the risks of not operating, and any alternatives (local anaesthetic closure, dressings).","Read the prison healthcare notes: diagnosis, past self-harm, current ACCT document, medicines.","Ask the officers for a quiet space; agree they stay nearby for safety but out of earshot if possible.","Clarify that the MHA cannot be used to treat a physical wound that is not part of treating mental disorder.","Write the decision down in one line before you start: 'Closure of neck wound under general anaesthetic now.'"],"ask":["'Could you tell me what the surgeons have told you about your neck?'","'What could happen if it isn't closed in theatre?'","'What would happen if you stayed in for the operation?'","'What matters most to you in making this choice?'","'What happened just before you cut yourself?'","'How's your mood been? Anything unusual going on, like voices?'","'Do you have any thoughts of hurting yourself again, or of ending your life?'"],"recognise":["Capacity is presumed and decision-specific: this decision, now.","An unwise decision is not proof of incapacity; he may weigh a scar as less important than therapy.","EUPD does not remove capacity, but acute distress or intoxication at the time can.","The self-harm followed a delay to his DBT wing transfer: frustration, not suicidal intent.","Risk of further self-harm is ongoing and raised, but not immediately high if he denies intent and has a reason to go back.","Pressure to 'find' incapacity to get treatment done is a red flag; stay objective."],"decide":[{"c":"Find he has capacity, explain the result, give advice once without pressure, ask the surgeons to offer alternatives, and hand over to prison healthcare with wound care and self-harm plans.","r":"Right: lawful, respectful, and it reduces risk by keeping him engaged.","best":true},{"c":"Detain him under section 5(2) so the surgeons can operate.","r":"Wrong: the MHA treats mental disorder, not a neck wound, and he is not an inpatient on a psychiatric basis for this purpose.","best":false},{"c":"Say he lacks capacity because he has a personality disorder and self-harmed.","r":"Wrong: a diagnosis is not a capacity test. He understands, retains, weighs and communicates.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005: presume capacity; take all practicable steps to help him decide; an unwise decision is not incapacity.","Functional test: can he understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate? If not, is that because of an impairment of mind or brain (the order set by the Supreme Court in 2021)?","MHA 1983 cannot authorise surgery for a self-inflicted wound unless it is part of treating the mental disorder; not the case here.","Prison: an ACCT plan must be opened or reviewed after self-harm; share relevant information with prison healthcare."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to make you have the operation. I'm here to understand your decision and make sure it's yours to make."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You understand the risks and you've thought it through. I believe you have capacity, so it's your decision, even though the surgeons and I would advise the operation."},{"to":"Surgical registrar","s":"He has capacity to refuse. Could you offer closure under local anaesthetic or a dressing plan instead, and write a wound care letter for prison healthcare?"}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. Mr S Mulholland, 24M. EUPD. Serving 2 years (ABH), ~1 year in. Neck laceration, deep, no vessel, nerve or muscle injury.\nDecision: closure under GA now (plastics), which needs a stay of a few nights.\nContext: cut with metal off-cut from workshop after told again no date for DBT wing transfer; aim was relief of frustration, not death. Past deep cutting, some stitched.\nMood: not low; no psychotic features. Not intoxicated. Calm.\nCapacity (MCA 2005): Understands: depth, benefits of GA closure, risks of refusal (infection, poor or failed healing, large scar, bleeding). Retains: repeated accurately after 10 min. Weighs: therapy transfer matters more than scar; dislikes ward noise; would accept dressings, antibiotics, return if complications. Communicates: clear and consistent.\nOutcome: has capacity to refuse; advised once to have closure; not pressured.\nRisk: further self-harm ongoing, raised by transfer delays; no current suicidal intent; no risk to others identified today.\nPlan: plastics to offer LA closure or dressings; wound care letter; prison healthcare and in-reach informed; ACCT review; request transfer date; return advice given.\nDr Chinonso CT3","make":["Name the decision precisely, and say you're not there to force anything.","Ask him to explain the surgeons' advice in his own words, then fill gaps and check again later.","Test weighing by asking why: what matters more to him and why.","Screen mood and psychosis briefly, and check he is not intoxicated or in acute crisis.","Do a focused self-harm risk assessment: trigger, intent, future, means.","Tell him the outcome, advise once, offer alternatives and leave the door open."],"next":"The plastic surgeons close the wound under local anaesthetic as a day case. Shane returns to prison that evening with a wound care letter. The ACCT is reviewed, the in-reach team gets him a transfer date three weeks away, and the metalwork tutor counts off-cuts at the end of each session.","marks":["Explained the purpose without pressure and named the specific decision.","Took a brief history: the cut, trigger, intent, diagnosis, past self-harm and sentence.","Tested understanding and retention of the surgeons' information.","Tested weighing by exploring his reasons and priorities.","Screened for depression, psychosis and intoxication.","Assessed ongoing self-harm and suicide risk.","Explained the outcome, advised once, offered alternatives and planned the return to prison."],"pitfalls":["Equating EUPD or self-harm with lacking capacity.","Using the Mental Health Act to treat the wound.","Spending the time trying to persuade him.","Not testing weighing: just asking if he understands.","Forgetting the risk assessment and the prison handover."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005, sections 1 to 3, and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52: order of the capacity test (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","4. HM Prison and Probation Service: ACCT (Assessment, Care in Custody and Teamwork) guidance (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK law strongly protects the right to refuse treatment if a person has capacity, even if the choice seems unwise. Surgeons may ask you to 'find' incapacity; your job is an honest, decision-specific assessment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-62","n":1362,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🗣️","title":"Voices back after a row at work: violence risk, treatment and a safety plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:30, urgent slot","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"Tomasz Nowak, a 34-year-old factory supervisor, has had schizophrenia since an admission six years ago and has been well since on quetiapine MR 500 mg at night. His GP has sent an urgent referral: since an argument with a colleague three weeks ago he has begun hearing voices again. He is in the waiting room with his partner."},"task":"Assess the relapse and the risk he poses to himself and others, especially the colleague, then agree a management plan and a written safety plan with him.","first60":["Read the old discharge summary: what the first episode looked like, any violence, any detention, and which early warning signs came first.","Check his prescription record: when quetiapine was last collected, and any recent changes.","See him alone first; agree to bring his partner in later, with his permission.","Sit with a clear exit and tell a colleague you are seeing him; the referral mentions a named person at work.","Hold the order: his experiences first, then risk to the colleague in detail, then plan together."],"ask":["'Tell me about the voices: how many, whose, and what do they say?'","'Do they tell you to do things? How hard is it to resist?'","'What do you believe Gary is doing? How sure are you?'","'Have you thought about harming him? What exactly have you imagined?'","'Have you made any plan, carried anything, or gone looking for him?'","'Have you ever hurt anyone before, when unwell or well?'","'Have you missed any quetiapine? Any alcohol or drugs?'","'Have you had thoughts of hurting yourself?'"],"recognise":["Sleep loss and irritability before voices: a typical relapse signature, often after stress and missed doses.","Command voices plus a persecutory belief about one named person is the combination that drives violence risk.","Distress about the thoughts, asking for help and no plan or weapon are reassuring, but do not cancel the risk.","Missed doses because of morning drowsiness on early shifts: a practical, fixable cause.","Daily contact with the person in his beliefs is ongoing exposure; distance is part of the treatment."],"decide":[{"c":"Restart quetiapine reliably with consultant review of the dose, short-term help with sleep, crisis team home visits, sick leave, partner involved with consent, and a written safety plan; admission if things escalate.","r":"Right: it treats the relapse quickly, removes contact with Gary, and keeps him engaged in a plan he chose.","best":true},{"c":"Restart his usual dose and see him in clinic in four weeks.","r":"Unsafe: command voices about a named person need daily review and time away from work now.","best":false},{"c":"Phone the police and his employer straight away without telling him.","r":"Disproportionate at present: no intent, no plan, and he is staying off work. Share without consent only if risk to Gary becomes serious and identifiable, and tell him if you can.","best":false}],"law":["GMC confidentiality: you may disclose without consent to protect an identifiable person from serious harm. Discuss with your consultant, record the reasoning, and tell him unless that increases risk.","He has capacity and agrees to treatment; if he later refuses help while risk rises, request a Mental Health Act assessment.","Fit note: you can issue one for sick leave; he does not have to disclose the diagnosis to his employer.","DVLA: a relapse of psychosis should be reported, and he should not drive until stable and advised."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Coming in and telling me this is exactly the right thing to do. It shows me you want to keep everyone safe, including you."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I need to be honest: if I ever thought Gary was in real danger, I might have to share that to protect him. I'd always try to talk to you first."},{"to":"Crisis team (referral)","s":"34M, schizophrenia, relapse over 3 weeks after work conflict and missed quetiapine. Command voices to 'deal with' a named colleague, plus belief he is being poisoned. No plan, weapon or past serious violence; frightened by the thoughts. Off work. Please visit daily."}],"write":"Urgent clinic. 34M, schizophrenia (1 admission 6 yrs ago), well on quetiapine MR 500 mg nocte. Factory supervisor. Lives with partner Ola.\nHPC: argument with colleague Gary 3 wks ago. Poor sleep (3-4 h), irritable. Voices began ~2 wks ago: 2 male voices, 2nd person, daily, louder; say Gary is poisoning his tea and he should 'deal with him'. Believes this ~70%; brings own flask. No other hallucinations. Missed ~6 doses (morning drowsiness before early shifts). 2-3 beers at weekends; no drugs.\nRisk to others: thoughts of hitting Gary, imagined it at the canteen; no plan, no weapon, has not sought him out; frightened by the thoughts. Pushed brother during first episode; no other violence, no forensic history. Works same shift as Gary.\nRisk to self: no suicidal thoughts; self-care intact.\nProtective: partner, insight, help-seeking, good engagement before.\nImpression: relapse of schizophrenia with command hallucinations and persecutory belief about a named colleague. Risk to Gary moderate, reduced by distance and treatment.\nPlan: restart quetiapine MR 500 mg nightly, consultant to review increase; short-term lorazepam for sleep/agitation per consultant; crisis team daily visits from today; fit note 2 wks; partner informed with consent; written safety plan; admission if voices escalate or intent emerges. Confidentiality limits explained. Discussed with consultant: no disclosure to employer at present; review daily. Bloods and ECG.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask what the voices say word for word; 'deal with him' only comes out if you ask for the exact words.","Separate thought, intent, plan and preparation, and ask about each one.","Ask about past violence, including while unwell the first time.","Find the reason for missed doses; the fix depends on it.","Write the safety plan with him, in his words, and give him a copy.","Be open about the limits of confidentiality before he discloses more, not after."],"next":"The crisis team visits daily and his partner holds the tablets. The consultant increases quetiapine MR to 600 mg. Within ten days the voices are quieter and he no longer believes his tea was tampered with. He returns to work on a phased plan, on a different shift from Gary, six weeks later.","marks":["Elicited the time course of relapse after the argument and missed doses.","Explored the voices: number, person, content, commands and his response.","Screened for delusions about the colleague, mood, sleep and substances.","Assessed thoughts, intent, plans, means and past violence towards others.","Assessed risk to self and protective factors.","Agreed treatment, crisis team input, sick leave and a written safety plan, with confidentiality limits explained."],"pitfalls":["Asking 'do you hear voices?' and stopping, without the exact content and commands.","Not asking about a plan, a weapon or past violence.","Sending him back to work while the voices are about a colleague.","Promising total confidentiality.","Raising the dose without asking why doses were missed."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 (2014): adult psychosis and schizophrenia guideline; checked Nov 2026","2. NICE NG10 Violence and aggression: short-term management (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: quetiapine modified-release (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK crisis resolution and home treatment teams can visit daily or more, so many relapses are managed at home. A fit note is issued by any doctor; employers see only that he is unfit for work unless he chooses to say more.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-63","n":1363,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🕯️","title":"Seeing her late husband at home: grief or mental illness?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:00, new patient","where":"General adult psychiatry outpatient clinic","text":"Bernadette Quinn, a 53-year-old school administrator, lost her husband Frank to a sudden heart attack three months ago. She told her GP that she sometimes sees Frank around the house, and the GP has referred her, unsure whether this is the start of a mental illness. She arrives on time, neatly dressed, holding a tissue."},"task":"Take a history of the bereavement and her experiences, screen for depression, psychosis and risk, and give her a brief formulation of what is happening.","first60":["Read the GP letter: date and cause of death, her past history, current medication, and what exactly she reported seeing.","Have tissues to hand and allow longer than usual; she may cry early.","Start with condolences and Frank as a person, not with the symptom.","Plan the order: the death, the experiences, her grief, then depression, psychosis and risk, then explain.","Know what you will say at the end: normal grief, why sudden death hits harder, and when to come back."],"ask":["'Can you tell me about Frank, and what happened when he died?'","'When you see him, what exactly happens? Where, when, how long for?'","'Do you know at the time that he isn't really there?'","'What other things have you noticed: hearing him, smelling him, sensing he's near?'","'How have you felt since: numb, angry, guilty, longing for him?'","'Is your mood low all the time, or does it lift with family and friends?'","'Do you ever feel you'd like to be with him? Have you thought of ending your life?'","'Has anyone said your thoughts or beliefs seem unusual?'"],"recognise":["Seeing or hearing a dead spouse briefly, with full insight, often comforting, is common in grief and not psychotic.","Seeing him on waking that fades as she rubs her eyes is a hypnopompic experience.","Waves of crying and longing, set off by reminders, with mood that lifts with others, point to grief rather than depression.","Persistent worthlessness, inability to feel any pleasure, slowing, or guilt beyond the death suggest a depressive episode.","Prolonged grief disorder needs at least 6 months of intense, impairing longing; three months is too early.","A wish to be with him is common; still ask about plans and preparations."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain that this is a normal grief reaction made more intense by the sudden death, offer bereavement support through Cruse, give safety advice, and arrange follow-up in 4-6 weeks.","r":"Right: it answers her fear directly, avoids medicalising grief, and leaves a door open if things change.","best":true},{"c":"Start an antipsychotic at low dose because she is having visual hallucinations.","r":"Wrong: brief grief perceptions with full insight are not psychosis; medication adds harm and fear.","best":false},{"c":"Start an antidepressant because she cries daily and sleeps poorly.","r":"Wrong: these are grief, not a depressive episode. Antidepressants are not a treatment for normal grief.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: ask before speaking to her daughter or son; offer to include them if she wishes.","Fitness to work is her choice; offer a fit note only if she asks and feels unable to cope.","No legal powers are relevant: she is an informal outpatient with full capacity."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Seeing or hearing someone you've lost is very common, especially in the first months. It doesn't mean you're going mad."},{"to":"Patient","s":"When a death is sudden, there's no chance to prepare or say goodbye, so grief often hits harder and takes a little longer."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Normal grief reaction after sudden bereavement, intensified by its suddenness. Brief visual and auditory perceptions of her husband with full insight; no psychosis or depressive episode. Passive wish to join him, no plans. Signposted to Cruse; no medication. Review in 6 weeks; please re-refer if low mood becomes persistent."}],"write":"Outpatient clinic. 53F, school administrator. Husband Frank (56) died suddenly 3 months ago: collapsed at home with chest pain, died in A&E same evening.\nCourse: numb and disbelieving for first 2 weeks; then anger (at herself, 'should have made him see the GP'), guilt easing. Cries daily, longing, sleep broken. Appetite fair. Back at work, sees friends and children. Mood lifts with grandchildren.\nPerceptions: sees Frank in his armchair from corner of eye, gone when she looks; sees him at end of bed on waking, fades as she wakes properly; hears him in kitchen occasionally; smells his aftershave. Full insight; comforting rather than frightening.\nNo pervasive low mood, anhedonia, worthlessness or hopelessness. No other hallucinations, no delusions, no first-rank symptoms. Alcohol 1-2 glasses wine at weekends. No regular meds. No past psychiatric history.\nRisk: sometimes wishes to be with Frank; no suicidal plans or preparations; protective: children, grandchildren, work.\nImpression: normal grief reaction, intensified by sudden death. Perceptions are grief-related and hypnopompic, not psychotic. No depressive episode.\nPlan: formulation explained, teach-back good. Cruse details given. No medication. Sleep advice. Safety advice. Review 6 weeks; GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Let her tell Frank's story first; it builds trust and gives you the timeline.","Pin down the perceptions: duration, insight, setting, emotional tone. That is what separates grief from psychosis.","Ask about the good moments too; mood that lifts is a strong sign against depression.","Ask the risk question kindly but plainly: 'wanting to be with him' needs follow-up.","Answer 'am I going mad?' early and directly, then explain why.","Give normal grief a shape: waves, not stages to pass in order."],"next":"She contacts Cruse the following week and starts fortnightly sessions with a volunteer. At review six weeks later she still sees Frank's chair as 'his', but the glimpses are rarer, she is sleeping better, and she says knowing it was normal took away the fear.","marks":["Opened with condolences and let her describe Frank and his death.","Characterised the perceptions: modality, timing, duration, insight and emotional tone.","Explored grief reactions: numbness, anger, guilt, longing, sleep and appetite.","Screened for a depressive episode and for psychotic symptoms.","Asked about supports, past history, alcohol and suicidal thoughts.","Explained normal grief and the effect of a sudden death, checked understanding and planned follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Labelling the perceptions as hallucinations needing treatment.","Rushing to symptoms before acknowledging the loss.","Skipping the risk question because she seems to be coping.","Offering an antidepressant or sleeping tablet for normal grief.","Giving a vague reassurance without explaining why it is normal."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-11 6B42 Prolonged grief disorder (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (2022): adult depression guideline; checked Nov 2026","3. NHS website: Grief after bereavement or loss (checked Nov 2026)","4. Cruse Bereavement Support: cruse.org.uk (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, grief is usually supported outside mental health services, by GPs, Cruse and local hospices. Patients often fear that admitting to 'seeing' someone will get them labelled; say clearly and early that it is common.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-64","n":1364,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🎨","title":"Withdrawn after rehab: explaining psychological and social help for negative symptoms","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:00, outreach review","where":"Supported residential home, community rehabilitation team","text":"Shanice Morrow, a 31-year-old woman with schizophrenia and recurrent depressive disorder, moved here a month ago after a year on a rehabilitation unit. Her delusions have faded, but staff say she rarely joins activities and spends most of the day in her room. She takes clozapine 400 mg at night and sertraline 200 mg daily. She has agreed to see you in the lounge."},"task":"Understand how she is spending her days, rule out other causes of her withdrawal, explain negative symptoms, and discuss specific psychological and social approaches linked to her own goals.","first60":["Read the rehab discharge summary: how many episodes, admissions under section, what she enjoyed on the unit, and her last clozapine level.","Ask staff for specifics: hours in bed, meals, contact with other residents, any odd behaviour or talk.","Check physical monitoring: clozapine FBC schedule, weight, bowels, thyroid and haemoglobin.","Expect short, slow answers; allow pauses and do not fill every silence.","Plan to finish with two or three concrete, named options she has chosen, not a list."],"ask":["'What does a normal day look like for you here?'","'Is there anything you enjoy, even a little? Anything you look forward to?'","'What stops you joining in with things downstairs?'","'Is your mood low, or more flat and empty?'","'Are you sleepy in the day? Any dribbling, constipation or weight change?'","'Have the old worries or voices come back at all?'","'What did you used to love doing? What would you like to be doing in six months?'"],"recognise":["Flat, unmotivated, little speech, withdrawn, little pleasure in looking forward: the core negative symptoms.","Enjoying food in the moment but not looking forward to anything is anticipatory anhedonia, typical of negative symptoms.","'I'll only fail' and 'I'll look stupid' are defeatist beliefs that keep withdrawal going; CBT can target them.","Secondary causes to exclude: depression, clozapine sedation, residual psychosis, hypothyroidism, anaemia and an under-stimulating setting.","Losing the structure of a rehab unit often reveals how much prompting she needs; that is an environment problem as well as a symptom."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain negative symptoms, agree arts therapy, OT-led weekly routine and a recovery college art course, refer for CBT for psychosis, involve her sister, and review clozapine sedation with bloods.","r":"Right: specific, linked to her goals, and it treats the possible secondary causes at the same time.","best":true},{"c":"Increase sertraline because she seems depressed.","r":"Wrong: she is already on the maximum dose, she denies low mood, and the picture fits negative symptoms.","best":false},{"c":"Tell staff to encourage her more and review in three months.","r":"Too vague: it offers no named treatment and leaves her without a plan she has chosen.","best":false}],"law":["She is informal in a supported home; any plan depends on her consent and choice.","Ask her permission before sharing details with her sister or involving family in therapy.","If she is on a community treatment order or Section 117 aftercare, record the agreed services in her aftercare plan."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Feeling flat and finding it hard to get going is part of the illness itself. It isn't laziness, and it can get better."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You mentioned you used to paint. Would trying an art group once a week be a good first step?"},{"to":"Care coordinator (email)","s":"Reviewed today. Prominent negative symptoms, no positive symptoms, no depressive episode. Afternoon sedation. Agreed: OT routine, arts therapy referral, recovery college art course, CBTp referral, sister involved with consent. Clozapine level and bloods requested."}],"write":"Review, supported residential home. 31F. Schizophrenia (6 episodes since 19, several admissions under section) and recurrent depressive disorder. 12 months on rehab unit; here 1 month. Clozapine 400 mg nocte, sertraline 200 mg mane.\nNow: in room most of day, rarely joins activities or talks to residents. Feels 'flat', not sad. Little drive. Likes meals while eating them, yet anticipates nothing (anticipatory anhedonia). Short answers, blunted affect. Thoughts: 'I'll only fail', worries about looking stupid. Sleepy each afternoon. Residents loud.\nNo delusions or hallucinations. No pervasive low mood, hopelessness or suicidal thoughts. Constipation mild.\nSupport: sister Kerry monthly; mother caring for grandmother, visits less.\nGoals: paint again, see sister more.\nImpression: prominent primary negative symptoms; possible contribution from clozapine sedation and low structure.\nPlan: negative symptoms explained. Agreed: OT weekly routine; arts therapy referral (group); recovery college art course; CBTp referral; sister involved with consent; walking group. Clozapine level, FBC, TFTs, ferritin. Consultant to review dose timing. Review 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask about a real day, hour by hour; 'fine' tells you nothing.","Separate flat from sad: it decides whether this is depression or negative symptoms.","Check the tablets: daytime sleepiness on clozapine is common and fixable.","Name the options precisely: who delivers them, how often, how long.","Link each option to something she said, especially painting and her sister.","Leave with one first step she has chosen, with a day and a person attached."],"next":"The OT helps her plan a weekly timetable, and she starts a Tuesday art class at the recovery college with a support worker. Her clozapine level is high-normal; the consultant reduces the dose slightly and her afternoons improve. Three months later she has joined the arts therapy group and sees her sister every fortnight.","marks":["Explored her daily routine, motivation, enjoyment and contact with others.","Screened for depression, sedation and side effects, and residual psychotic symptoms.","Explained negative symptoms as part of the illness, without blame and with hope.","Offered specific psychological options and explained how each would help her.","Offered specific social and occupational options.","Agreed goals and a first step, with follow-up and staff involvement."],"pitfalls":["Calling her lazy or unmotivated as if it were a choice.","Listing therapies without saying how they would help her.","Missing clozapine sedation as a cause.","Increasing sertraline without evidence of a depressive episode.","Doing all the talking and never asking what she wants."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 (2014): adult psychosis and schizophrenia guideline; checked Nov 2026","2. NICE QS80 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults: quality standard (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: clozapine (checked Nov 2026)","4. Rethink Mental Illness: negative symptoms (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK rehabilitation services lean heavily on occupational therapists, recovery colleges and supported employment (IPS). These are real treatments here, not extras; refer to them by name.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-65","n":1365,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🌗","title":"Low after mania: explaining bipolar depression and the treatment options","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 14:30, ward review","where":"Adult acute admissions ward, interview room","text":"Jordan Asante, a 26-year-old man, came to A&E with his girlfriend six weeks ago after two weeks of elated, reckless behaviour and was admitted under the Mental Health Act. He declined olanzapine and quetiapine because of weight worries and took risperidone. The mania settled in three weeks, then he quickly became depressed. Sertraline, now 150 mg daily, has done little. He and his family are worried."},"task":"Review his current mood and risk, explain that this is bipolar depression and why the current tablets are not working, and discuss medication, psychological and lifestyle options so he can make an informed choice.","first60":["Read the admission notes: what the mania looked like, legal status now, leave so far, and any family history of mood disorder.","Check the drug chart: risperidone dose, sertraline start date and increases, weight and metabolic bloods since admission.","Ask the nurses how he has been: sleep, eating, contact with others, any self-harm talk.","Have the facts ready: lamotrigine titration, lithium monitoring, why antidepressants alone are risky in bipolar disorder.","Plan to give a clear diagnosis early; he wants an answer."],"ask":["'How's your mood been since the high spell settled?'","'What do you think about the things that happened when you were high?'","'How are your sleep, appetite, energy and concentration?'","'Do you ever feel that life's not worth living? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'What do you understand about why you were in hospital?'","'What matters most to you when choosing a medicine?'","'What are you drinking these days? Any cannabis or other drugs?'"],"recognise":["One clear manic episode, followed by a depressive episode, means bipolar I disorder; the low is part of the same illness.","Long sleep, heaviness, guilt about the high and hopelessness are typical of bipolar depression.","Antidepressants alone help bipolar depression less than unipolar, and can trigger switching or mood instability.","Risperidone is an antimanic treatment; it does not reliably lift bipolar depression.","Weight is his top concern; lamotrigine is weight-neutral, which makes it a good fit for him.","Embarrassment after mania is a real suicide risk factor; ask directly."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain bipolar disorder, taper sertraline, offer lamotrigine (weight-neutral) for the depression and discuss lithium for long-term prevention, with CBT for bipolar depression, psychoeducation and a relapse plan; continue risperidone for now.","r":"Right: evidence-based, respects his weight concerns, and treats both the current episode and the future.","best":true},{"c":"Increase sertraline to 200 mg and wait four more weeks.","r":"Wrong: antidepressants without proven benefit in bipolar depression, at higher dose, raise the risk of switching and delay effective treatment.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him the diagnosis can't be confirmed yet and avoid labelling until a second episode.","r":"Wrong: one manic episode is enough for bipolar I. Holding back leaves him confused and without a plan.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: he has capacity to choose between treatment options; give balanced information on benefits and risks.","If still detained under Section 2 or 3, discuss his views and record them; if informal, he may decline any option.","DVLA: after a manic episode he must tell the DVLA and not drive until they say he can.","Confidentiality: ask before sharing details with his girlfriend and family; involve them if he agrees."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"A spell of mania followed by a low like this tells us it's bipolar disorder. The low isn't a separate problem; it's the other side of the same illness."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Antidepressants on their own don't work as well in bipolar depression, and can sometimes make mood less stable. That's likely why sertraline hasn't helped."},{"to":"Consultant (handover)","s":"26M, bipolar I, current moderate to severe depressive episode, passive death wishes, no plan. Sertraline 150 mg ineffective. Prefers weight-neutral options. Suggest taper sertraline, start lamotrigine 25 mg; he'd like to discuss lithium for prevention."}],"write":"Ward review. 26M. Admitted under MHA 6 wks ago with 2-wk manic episode (elation, no sleep, £3,000 spent, grand business plans). Risperidone 4 mg nocte (declined olanzapine/quetiapine: weight). Mania settled by wk 3, then rapid onset depression. Sertraline 50 → 150 mg over 3 wks: little benefit.\nNow: low, empty, hopeless; sleeps 12 h; low energy; poor concentration; avoids others; guilt and embarrassment about manic behaviour. Passive thoughts life not worth it; no plan or intent; protective: girlfriend Amara, family, wish to work again. No psychotic symptoms. No alcohol or drugs since admission.\nImpression: bipolar I disorder, current depressive episode, moderate to severe, without psychotic symptoms.\nDiscussed: diagnosis; why sertraline and risperidone not lifting mood; options: lamotrigine, quetiapine, lithium, ECT if severe; CBT for bipolar depression; psychoeducation group; mood diary; DVLA.\nDecision: wishes to taper sertraline and start lamotrigine; will read about lithium; continue risperidone for now. Leaflets given. Girlfriend to join next review with consent.\nRisk: general observations; crisis plan agreed for leave.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Give a clear diagnosis; vagueness feeds his fear and hopelessness.","Explain why things haven't worked before offering something new.","Lead with his priority: weight. Say which options are weight-neutral.","Give two or three real options with practical details, not a lecture on all of them.","Ask directly about suicidal thoughts; shame after mania is common and dangerous.","Let him choose, or think it over, and agree when you'll revisit."],"next":"Sertraline is tapered over a week and lamotrigine started, with the rash warning explained. By week six on 100 mg his sleep and mood improve. He joins a bipolar psychoeducation group, starts lithium on his consultant's advice for long-term prevention, and returns to work part-time three months later.","marks":["Reviewed current depressive symptoms and the transition from mania.","Assessed suicide risk and protective factors.","Gave a clear diagnosis of bipolar disorder and explained it plainly.","Explained why sertraline and risperidone are not lifting his mood.","Discussed at least two medication options with practical details, including weight.","Discussed psychological and lifestyle options, agreed a choice and next steps."],"pitfalls":["Refusing to give a diagnosis.","Increasing the antidepressant.","Offering quetiapine or olanzapine without acknowledging his weight concerns.","Forgetting the rash warning for lamotrigine or monitoring for lithium.","Skipping the suicide question because he is on a ward."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 (2014, updated 2023): bipolar disorder guideline; checked Nov 2026","2. BNF: lamotrigine, lithium carbonate (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA: lithium patient pack and monitoring (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: psychiatric disorders and driving (checked Nov 2026)","5. Bipolar UK: bipolaruk.org (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK lithium is started with a purple record book and patient pack, and levels are shared with the GP under a shared-care arrangement. Patients expect to be offered choices; explaining weight effects openly builds trust.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-66","n":1366,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"Three kilos in a week: refeeding syndrome in anorexia nervosa","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 11:20, community eating disorder team","where":"Team office, community eating disorder service","text":"Ms Ruth Adeyemi, a locum community nurse three weeks into the team, asks for you. Two days ago she visited Chloe Whitfield, 24, who has anorexia nervosa and started with the team a month ago at a BMI of 13.5. Chloe felt unwell; BP was 90/60, pulse 60. Her weight has jumped 3 kg in a week. A colleague recorded BP 100/70 this morning. Ruth says, 'Three kilos in a week. It doesn't feel right.'"},"task":"Gather the symptoms, eating pattern, weight trend and observations from the nurse, name the likely cause, check risk, and agree a same-day plan for assessment, treatment and prevention.","first60":["Pull up Chloe's record: starting BMI, weight chart, the dietitian's meal plan and its start date, last bloods and ECG.","Sit with Ruth, not across a desk; she is new and anxious that she has missed something.","Hold the structure: symptoms, eating and weight, observations and bloods, cause, risk, then plan.","Treat 3 kg in a week after starvation as fluid until proved otherwise; that is a same-day problem.","Have the acute medical team's number and the MEED guidance to hand before you finish."],"ask":["'What did Chloe tell you, in her own words, and how is she today?'","'Any swelling, breathlessness, palpitations, chest pain, dizziness or confusion?'","'What changed in her eating, and when did the new meal plan start?'","'Is she vomiting, using laxatives or drinking extra water before weigh-ins?'","'What were her pulse, BP and temperature, and was BP checked sitting and standing?'","'Has anyone checked her bloods or done an ECG since the plan began?'","'Who knew about this visit, and when?'"],"recognise":["Weight gain of 3 kg in a week after slow gain is mostly fluid: true tissue gain is about 0.5 to 1 kg a week.","New weakness, tiredness, nausea, blurred vision, ankle swelling and palpitations within days of more food point to refeeding syndrome.","Mechanism: food raises insulin, which drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells and uses up thiamine; blood levels fall and salt and water are held.","Danger: low phosphate, potassium or magnesium can cause arrhythmia, heart failure, fits, confusion and death.","A better BP today does not rule it out; only bloods and an ECG can.","Differentials: hidden purging with low potassium, water loading before weigh-ins, low blood sugar, infection, early heart failure."],"decide":[{"c":"Arrange a same-day medical assessment through A&E or the acute medical unit: observations, ECG, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, LFT, FBC; start thiamine; keep feeding at a safe set amount; tell the consultant, GP and dietitian.","r":"Right: the results are needed today and treatment depends on them. Feeding continues but is steadied, not stopped.","best":true},{"c":"She is a bit better and BP is up, so recheck at the next routine visit next week.","r":"Unsafe: refeeding syndrome kills through low salts and arrhythmia, and symptoms can improve while bloods stay low.","best":false},{"c":"Tell Chloe to stop the meal plan until the bloods are back.","r":"Wrong: stopping food worsens her starvation and underfeeding also carries risk. Steady the intake with the dietitian instead.","best":false}],"law":["Chloe has capacity unless shown otherwise; if she declines same-day assessment, explain the risks, assess capacity for that decision and involve the consultant.","If capacity is lacking and the danger is immediate, the MCA allows urgent treatment in her best interests; the MHA can be considered, as feeding and correcting complications can be treatment for anorexia nervosa.","Duty of candour does not apply to a near miss, but log the two-day delay as an incident for team learning, without blame."],"say":[{"to":"Nurse","s":"You were right to flag this. Three kilos in a week after starvation is mostly fluid, and with her symptoms I think this is refeeding syndrome."},{"to":"Nurse","s":"She needs bloods and an ECG today, and the results today, so I'll ring her and the medical team now."},{"to":"Acute medical team","s":"24-year-old woman, anorexia nervosa, BMI 13.5 a month ago, on a new meal plan for ten days, 3 kg gain in a week, weak, nauseous, ankle oedema, palpitations. Query refeeding syndrome; please assess today with ECG, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose."}],"write":"CEDS duty note. Discussed with locum nurse R. Adeyemi re Chloe Whitfield, 24F, anorexia nervosa (relapse), BMI 13.5 at referral 4/52 ago.\nNew dietitian meal plan 10 days ago; Chloe eating all of it plus extra snacks. Gain ~0.5 kg/wk, then 3 kg in last week.\nHV 2 days ago: weak, tired, nauseous, blurred vision; BP 90/60, P 60. Today (colleague): still weak, puffy ankles, breathless on stairs, palpitations last night; BP 100/70. No purging, laxatives or water loading reported. No bloods/ECG since plan began. Reported to team today (nurse on training yesterday).\nNo self-harm or suicidal thoughts reported.\nImpression: probable refeeding syndrome with fluid retention; MEED BMI amber. Differentials: hidden purging, early heart failure, hypoglycaemia.\nPlan: same-day assessment, A&E/AMU (rung 11:40): obs incl. lying/standing BP, temp, ECG (QTc, rhythm), U&E, PO4, Mg, Ca, glucose, LFT, FBC. Thiamine 100 mg tds + vit B co strong + multivitamin. Salts replaced per protocol. Feeding continues at a set amount; dietitian to review; target 0.5-1 kg/wk. Bloods daily until stable.\nConsultant, GP and dietitian informed. Same-day escalation rule reinforced. Dr Chinonso, CT3","make":["Ask the three hidden questions: why the weight jumped, whether bloods were done, and who knew when.","Name it plainly: 'I think this is refeeding syndrome', then explain the mechanism in two sentences.","Say where and when: seen today, where results come back today.","Name every test and every treatment; vague 'bloods' loses the point.","Keep feeding going; teach that steady beats fast.","Teach the escalation rule kindly; blame makes the next nurse hide the next problem."],"next":"Chloe's phosphate is 0.48 mmol/L and potassium 3.1. The medical team admit her for three days of oral phosphate and potassium, thiamine and daily bloods. The dietitian sets a steady plan, and back home she gains 0.7 kg a week. Ruth adds 'abnormal obs: ring the duty doctor the same day' to her visit checklist.","marks":["Elicited the symptoms in Chloe's words and how she is today.","Established the weight trend, the 3 kg jump and why it happened.","Asked about purging, laxatives and water loading.","Asked for observations, bloods, ECG and who knew when.","Named refeeding syndrome and explained its cause and dangers.","Arranged same-day assessment, named the tests and treatments, and planned prevention."],"pitfalls":["Reassured by today's better BP and booking routine review.","Telling Chloe to stop eating until results return.","Saying 'do some bloods' without naming phosphate, magnesium and potassium.","Blaming Ruth or Chloe for the delay or the extra eating.","Forgetting thiamine before or alongside feeding."],"refs":["1. RCPsych CR233 Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED) (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG32 Nutrition support for adults (2006, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: thiamine; vitamin B compound strong; phosphate (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK eating disorder care is mostly community-based, so refeeding problems often appear at home, not on a ward. MEED (2022) replaced MARSIPAN; know its red and amber flags. Escalation is shared: any team member can and should ring the duty doctor the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-67","n":1367,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🎯","title":"Newly diagnosed adult ADHD: agreeing medication, monitoring and practical support","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Adult ADHD and neurodevelopmental clinic","text":"Kwame Asante, a 25-year-old man in his final year of a law degree, was diagnosed with ADHD last month. Months ago he went to his GP after failing several assessments, though his tutors said he understood the material. A friend diagnosed as a child spotted the similarities. Today he has come to talk about medication. He says, 'I've read a bit online.'"},"task":"Without retaking the history, understand his goals and what he has tried, complete the pre-medication checks, agree a first-line medicine with titration, monitoring and safety rules, and add practical support.","first60":["Read the diagnostic report: core symptoms, impairment, any coexisting anxiety, depression, substance use or tics.","Check the record for baseline height, weight, BP and pulse; if missing, plan to do them today.","Have the NICE NG87 order in your head: lisdexamfetamine or methylphenidate first, atomoxetine if neither suits.","Agree the agenda early and say you won't go over his history again.","Expect questions about 'study drugs' and personality change; welcome them."],"ask":["'What are you hoping treatment will change for you?'","'What have you already tried at university or at home, and did it help?'","'What have you read, and is anything worrying you about tablets?'","'Any heart problems, fainting, chest pain or palpitations on exercise?'","'Did any relative die suddenly or young, or have heart disease?'","'Any tics or fits? Any other medicines?'","'Tell me about alcohol and drugs, including other people's ADHD tablets.'"],"recognise":["Impairment despite understanding the work, after study adjustments, is the reason to offer medication in adults.","No cardiac history, no family sudden death and normal BP and pulse mean no ECG is needed before starting.","Light weekend drinking and no drug use: a stimulant remains first choice.","Worry about 'becoming a different person' usually means fear of feeling flat or speeded; the right dose should feel like a focused version of himself.","Curiosity about 'study drugs' flags a diversion risk to discuss openly, not judge."],"decide":[{"c":"Record baseline checks, start lisdexamfetamine 30 mg each morning and titrate weekly with BP, pulse and weight at each step, alongside psychoeducation, CBT for ADHD and university adjustments; GP shared care once stable.","r":"Right: NICE first-line medicine, built-in monitoring, and practical help that medication alone does not give.","best":true},{"c":"Start atomoxetine first because stimulants are controlled drugs.","r":"Not first choice: atomoxetine is for when stimulants are unsuitable, not tolerated or ineffective. He has no substance misuse to justify it.","best":false},{"c":"Order an ECG and cardiology review before any medicine.","r":"Unneeded here: an ECG is for a cardiac history, abnormal examination, raised BP or family sudden death, none of which he has.","best":false}],"law":["Lisdexamfetamine and methylphenidate are Schedule 2 controlled drugs: prescriptions valid 28 days, normally no more than 30 days' supply, no early or lost-script replacements.","Giving or selling his tablets to anyone is a supply offence.","Amphetamine is covered by the drug-driving law; taking it as prescribed is lawful if driving is not impaired. He must tell the DVLA if ADHD or its treatment affects his driving.","Equality Act 2010: ADHD can count as a disability, so the university must make reasonable adjustments."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've done the hard bit already, getting a diagnosis. Today is about what helps, and you get a say in all of it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"At the right dose most people say they feel like themselves, just able to stay on task. If you feel flat or wired, the dose is wrong, and we change it."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Adult ADHD. Started lisdexamfetamine 30 mg mane; titrating by 20 mg weekly to effect (max 70 mg). Baseline BP 118/74, pulse 72, weight 74 kg. We will request shared care once the dose is stable."}],"write":"Adult ADHD clinic. Kwame Asante, 25M, final-year law student. ADHD (combined) diagnosed last month.\nGoals: finish reading, meet deadlines, pass finals. Already tried: told tutors, library study in short blocks, phone off; helped a little.\nPre-treatment: no cardiac symptoms, no family sudden death, no tics/seizures. Meds: cetirizine prn. Alcohol 6-8 units at weekends, no drugs. BP 118/74, P 72 regular, wt 74 kg, ht 1.80 m. No ECG needed.\nPlan agreed: lisdexamfetamine 30 mg mane; increase by 20 mg weekly to effect (max 70 mg). BP, pulse, weight at each change. Side effects discussed (appetite, sleep, headache, anxiety, raised BP/pulse). Controlled-drug rules, no sharing, DVLA and drug-driving advice given. If ineffective or not tolerated after 6 weeks at optimal dose: methylphenidate MR; atomoxetine if stimulants unsuitable.\nPsychoeducation with ADHD nurse; CBT for adult ADHD referral; DSA and disability service registration.\nTeach-back good. Leaflet given. Review 2 weeks (phone) and 4 weeks (clinic). Dr Chinonso, ST4","make":["Say early that you won't retake the history; then keep to it.","Ask what he has already tried: adjustments come first, and saying so earns credit.","Do the cardiac questions out loud: own heart, family sudden death, BP and pulse.","Give real numbers: start dose, step, maximum, and when checks happen.","Name the controlled-drug rules plainly, including 'never share', without lecturing.","Pair medicine with practical study support; tablets help focus, not planning skills."],"next":"Kwame settles on 50 mg each morning. Appetite dips at lunch, so he eats a big breakfast and a late snack. BP is 124/78. His disability adviser arranges extra time and a quiet room, and the GP takes over prescribing under shared care at three months. He passes his next two modules.","marks":["Avoided retaking the history and set a shared agenda.","Explored his goals, worries and what he had already tried.","Completed the cardiac and other pre-medication checks.","Explained first-line medicine with dose, titration and how it works.","Covered side effects, monitoring, controlled-drug rules and alternatives.","Added non-drug support and closed with teach-back and follow-up."],"pitfalls":["Retaking the developmental history when the diagnosis is made.","Starting a stimulant without asking about family sudden death.","Saying 'we'll start low and go slow' without real numbers.","Brushing off his 'study drugs' comment instead of discussing diversion.","Offering medicine alone with no study or university support."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87 ADHD: diagnosis and management (2018, updated 2019; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: lisdexamfetamine mesilate; methylphenidate hydrochloride; atomoxetine (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, ADHD and drug driving guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: ADHD in adults, treatment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, adult ADHD stimulants are started by specialists, then the GP often takes over under a shared care agreement. Universities fund support through Disabled Students' Allowance; writing a short letter for the disability service is part of good care.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-71","n":1371,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🧩","title":"Frequent attender after an overdose: what's underneath?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 09:20","where":"Emergency department side room, liaison psychiatry","text":"Keira Ashdown, a 22-year-old woman who works shifts in a city-centre bar, swallowed twelve 500 mg paracetamol tablets last night after a row with her boyfriend, then rang an ambulance herself. Her level was below the treatment line and the medical team have cleared her. A&E have flagged her as a frequent attender: several visits with cut forearms and one earlier small overdose. She asks, 'Are you here to tell me off as well?'"},"task":"Take a history to a working psychiatric diagnosis: last night's overdose, self-harm, mood, relationships, self-image, impulsivity and childhood adversity; rule out other conditions; assess her current risk; and explain your impression kindly.","first60":["Read the A&E record: dates and methods of past visits, any liaison letters, any previous diagnosis or care plan.","Confirm medical clearance: timed paracetamol level, LFTs, INR, and that no infusion is still running.","Ask the nurse how she has been: tearful, angry, settled, any attempt to leave, who has visited.","Use the side room, sit down, and plan your first sentence: why this talk matters to her, not to the department.","Hold the order in mind: last night, self-harm, feelings and relationships, self and impulses, what it isn't, background, risk."],"ask":["'Can you walk me through last night, from the end of your shift?'","'When you cut, what does it do for you?'","'When your mood changes, how fast does it happen, and how long before it settles?'","'What are your relationships usually like, start to finish?'","'How would you describe yourself? Does that shift with who you're with?'","'What have you done on the spur of the moment that you regretted later?'","'Have you had two weeks or more of feeling low every single day?'","'Growing up, was home a safe place to be? Only share what you're comfortable with.'"],"recognise":["Feelings that swing within minutes, set off by a missed text or a row, then settle by the end of the day: emotional instability, not a mood episode.","Partners who start perfect and turn into idiots within weeks, and panic at any hint of being left.","Self-image that changes with the company, plus feeling empty and worthless between storms.","Impulsive acts in two or more areas (spending, quitting jobs, binge drinking, cocaine) and cutting used to release pressure.","Feeling detached from herself or that people are talking about her, only under stress and gone by the next day, is part of the pattern; voices and fixed false beliefs are not.","A childhood with violence or neglect is common here; ask gently once and accept a no."],"decide":[{"c":"Finish the history and risk assessment, explain a working diagnosis of EUPD with hope, agree a written crisis plan with tablets removed, refer to the CMHT for structured therapy and write to the GP.","r":"Right: honest, safe and collaborative, and it points her to the treatment that works without an admission she does not need.","best":true},{"c":"Admit her informally overnight because she is a frequent attender.","r":"Usually unhelpful: she has no current intent, has supports and a plan. Unplanned admission can reinforce crisis-led care; brief admission is for a defined crisis only.","best":false},{"c":"Discharge with a note that she is 'well known' and needs no further follow-up.","r":"Wrong: NICE expects a full psychosocial assessment after every self-harm episode. Repeated attendance raises long-term suicide risk; it does not lower it.","best":false}],"law":["She is informal and appears to have capacity: she may leave. If she tries to go mid-assessment, assess capacity for that decision and call a senior; do not hold her without a legal basis.","NICE NG225 (2022): a psychosocial assessment after every self-harm episode; do not use risk scales to predict suicide or decide discharge.","Confidentiality: share the plan with her GP; ask her consent before telling her boyfriend, flatmate or sister.","Safeguarding and domestic abuse: ask privately about violence; with consent, offer referral to an IDVA or the local domestic abuse service."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to tell you off. You've been here a lot, and to me that means the help so far hasn't fitted. I'd like to change that."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you describe has a name, and it isn't 'broken'. Your feelings fire up fast and hard and take a while to settle. There's a therapy that really helps with that."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Working diagnosis: emotionally unstable personality disorder, borderline type. No depressive episode, hypomania or psychosis. Referred to CMHT for structured psychological therapy. Please prescribe in weekly supplies only."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 22F, bar worker. Paracetamol OD last night (12 x 500 mg) after phone row with boyfriend, ~5 vodkas after shift. Wanted to die at the time; rang ambulance herself ~30 min later (thought of sister). Level below treatment line; medically cleared. No current intent.\nSelf-harm: cuts forearms with razor ~weekly for relief; one previous OD (6 ibuprofen, last year). Several A&E attendances.\nPattern since mid-teens: mood shifts within minutes, triggered by perceived rejection, settles same day; frequent anger (smashed phone); feels empty, worthless; self-image changes with company; idealise-devalue relationships, intense fear of being left; impulsive spending, walked out of 3 jobs; binge vodka several nights/wk; cocaine some weekends, never injected. Brief stress-related depersonalisation and ideas others talk about her, insight kept, resolves within a day.\nNo 2-week low mood; highs last <1 h; no hallucinations or delusions; no PTSD symptoms; no eating disorder.\nBackground: physical violence from father, mother misused alcohol, left home at 16 to aunt (declined to say more). One college counselling session. No forensic history.\nRisk: boyfriend grabbed her wrist once; no children. Further paracetamol at home. Protective: sister Lily, flatmate Mia, regret, help-seeking.\nImpression: EUPD, borderline type (ICD-11 personality disorder with borderline pattern). Risk of repeat self-harm high, especially with rows and alcohol; immediate risk low.\nPlan: diagnosis explained, teach-back good. Crisis plan agreed; Mia to hold tablets. Alcohol advice. CMHT referral for structured therapy. GP letter. Domestic abuse support offered. Crisis numbers given. Discharge home.\nDr Chinonso, liaison CT2","make":["Give her a reason to talk before the first question, and name the feeling of being judged.","Ask how long moods last: minutes to hours after people let her down is not a fortnight of depression or days of hypomania.","Get one real example for every feature: 'What did you buy?', 'What happened next?'","Use her words: 'feeling empty', 'scared people will leave', not 'chronic emptiness' or 'abandonment'.","Ask about childhood once, gently, and respect a no.","Name the diagnosis with hope and a next step, never as a closed door."],"next":"Mia takes the spare paracetamol home. The CMHT personality disorder pathway sees Keira four weeks later and offers DBT. Three months on she has cut twice but not overdosed, and she rang the crisis line instead of A&E after the next row with Reece.","marks":["Explained why the conversation mattered and acknowledged her feeling of being judged.","Clarified last night's overdose: trigger, amount, alcohol, intent and how help came.","Explored self-harm: method, frequency, what it relieves, past overdoses.","Covered fast mood shifts, anger, emptiness, relationships, fear of being left, self-image and impulsivity, with examples.","Excluded depressive episode, hypomania, psychosis, PTSD and eating disorder, and placed stress-linked experiences correctly.","Asked sensitively about childhood adversity; assessed current intent, tablets at home, supports and relationship violence.","Explained the working diagnosis plainly and hopefully, checked understanding and agreed a crisis plan."],"pitfalls":["Treating 'frequent attender' as a reason to do less, or letting it show in tone.","Calling it bipolar because she says her mood 'goes up and down'.","Reading criteria aloud instead of asking for examples.","Pushing for details of childhood abuse after she has said no.","Forgetting the second box of tablets at home or violence from her boyfriend."],"refs":["1. NICE CG78 Borderline personality disorder: recognition and management (2009; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: personality disorder with borderline pattern specifier (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Borderline personality disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK A&E notes, 'frequent attender' is an administrative flag, not a diagnosis. Avoid 'manipulative' or 'attention-seeking'. Many UK services still write EUPD; ICD-11 says 'personality disorder with borderline pattern'. Being taken seriously is part of the treatment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-01","n":1401,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍽️","title":"Not eating, withdrawn: is it low mood or psychosis?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 21:30, liaison psychiatry on call","where":"A&E side room, general hospital","text":"Callum Adeyemi, a 21-year-old male history undergraduate, has been brought in by two flatmates. He has barely eaten for weeks and has shut himself in his room; they assume he is depressed. His observations are normal apart from a pulse of 104. The A&E registrar asks you to see him, work out what is going on and tell her the plan. He is waiting in a side room and has agreed to talk."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history from Callum covering eating, his unusual experiences, mood, cannabis, family history and risk, then give the A&E team a working diagnosis and a named next step.","first60":["Check the A&E card: pulse 104, temperature, capillary glucose, any weight. Has anyone offered him a drink yet?","Ask a flatmate one quick question in the corridor: when did they last see him eat and drink, and has anything odd been said?","Tell the nurse which side room you are in; sit nearer the door, without blocking his way out.","Plan the ten minutes: eating (1), experiences (3), mood (1), background (1), risk (1.5), summary (1.5).","Use his words. Avoid 'delusion', 'hallucination', 'paranoid' and 'mad'."],"ask":["'Your friends were worried about your eating. What's been happening, from your side?'","'You said something isn't right. What have you noticed?'","'How do you know people have come into your room? How sure are you, out of ten?'","'Who do you think is behind it, and why would they pick you?'","'You said the food gives you horrible thoughts. Do they feel like your own thoughts, or put there?'","'When people go through something this frightening, some hear voices when no one is around. Has that happened to you?'","'How's your mood been lately? Is there anything you still get pleasure from? Do you feel you deserve what's happening?'","'Do you ever feel like getting back at these people? Have you thought of ending your life?'"],"recognise":["Perplexity, slow and flat replies, and a detailed belief about night-time intruders who erase his memory: the picture of a primary psychosis.","The 'proof' is ordinary (shadows in a different place) but held with total certainty: score his conviction and note how far the belief has spread, for example to his flatmates.","Food 'putting thoughts in' may be thought insertion. Clarify whether the thoughts feel like his own; that decides whether it is a first-rank symptom.","Mood 'okay', sleep and energy fine, no guilt or sense of deserving it: psychotic depression is much less likely. Depressive delusions usually match the mood: guilt, ruin, illness.","Weekend cannabis, an uncle with a long psychiatric admission and onset at 21: first-episode psychosis, which needs the Early Intervention in Psychosis (EIP) team.","Not eating for weeks plus a pulse of 104 is a physical risk: check fluids, U&E and glucose before anyone talks about discharge."],"decide":[{"c":"Name first-episode psychosis as the working diagnosis, ask A&E for bloods, a urine drug screen and an ECG, discuss home treatment versus informal admission with your senior, refer to EIP and update his flatmates with his consent.","r":"Right: a reasoned diagnosis, the physical risk treated as real, and a named next step with the right team.","best":true},{"c":"Accept the flatmates' view that he is depressed, suggest sertraline and send him to his GP.","r":"Wrong: his mood is not low, and he has a systematised belief about intruders and thoughts put in through food. An antidepressant alone misses the illness and the risk.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him gently that no one could really be getting into his room, to help him see sense.","r":"Wrong: arguing with the belief costs his trust and changes nothing. Show you take his fear seriously without agreeing it is true.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal and has agreed to talk. If he decides to leave while unwell and at risk, Section 5(2) cannot be used in A&E: assess capacity, call your senior and consider a Mental Health Act assessment through the AMHP.","You may listen to his flatmates without his consent. Ask him before you share anything back with them, and record his answer.","He describes being assaulted. Record his account in his own words, and ask the A&E doctor to check for any injury; do not dismiss it without thought.","There is no duty to report his cannabis use to anyone."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"That sounds really frightening, Callum, and exhausting. I'm not here to argue with you. I want to understand it and help you feel safe again."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've told me, your mind has been under huge strain. I think this is an illness that can affect how people see what's going on around them, and it's treatable. A specialist team for young people sees exactly this."},{"to":"A&E registrar","s":"Callum has a first-episode psychosis, not a depression. He's eating little because of his beliefs, so please do U&E, glucose, FBC, a urine drug screen and an ECG. I'm discussing admission versus home treatment with my consultant now."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 21M, history undergraduate. Brought by 2 flatmates: eating very little for weeks, isolating in his room.\nHx: feels 'something's not right' for some months. Believes strangers enter his room at night and assault him, wiping his memory with a 'glove'; evidence = morning shadows in a different place. Conviction 10/10. Unsure who; wonders if flatmates know. Eats little because food 'puts horrible thoughts' in his head (?thought insertion: says the thoughts are not his). Denies voices.\nMood 'okay'; some loss of interest; sleep and energy fine; no guilt; no hopelessness.\nCannabis most weekends (~1 joint/day Fri-Sun); alcohol minimal. No past psychiatric history. Uncle had a long psychiatric admission.\nRisk: self-neglect (poor intake, pulse 104). Denies suicidal thoughts. Denies wish to harm persecutors or flatmates; called police once. Vulnerable: alone in room, suspicious of flatmates.\nMSE: perplexed, slow, flat; no thought disorder; oriented. Insight: wants help 'for his body'.\nImpression: first-episode psychosis (ddx: schizophrenia-spectrum, cannabis-induced; psychotic depression unlikely).\nPlan: U&E, FBC, LFT, TFT, glucose, UDS, ECG; fluids offered; discussed with consultant re informal admission vs HTT; EIP referral; flatmates updated with consent.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Let him tell the story first; the detail of the belief is the diagnosis.","Score conviction out of ten and ask what would change his mind.","Normalise before asking about voices, so he does not feel labelled.","Do a three-question mood screen to rule psychotic depression in or out.","Ask about anger at the persecutors as a natural follow-on, not a checklist item.","Finish with a diagnosis and an action, not 'needs further assessment'."],"next":"Callum's U&E show mild dehydration; he drinks with encouragement. After discussion with the consultant he agrees to an informal admission. The EIP team meets him on the ward within the week, he starts aripiprazole after baseline checks, and his flatmates are given the ward number with his permission.","marks":["Explored the night-time belief: evidence, who, why and certainty.","Asked about thought insertion and voices sensitively, without labelling words.","Screened mood to separate primary psychosis from psychotic depression.","Covered cannabis, past history and family history briefly.","Assessed self-neglect, suicide, anger at the persecutors and vulnerability.","Gave a working diagnosis and a named next step with EIP."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'he's depressed' and doing only a depression history.","Asking 'Are you hearing voices?' bluntly and losing him.","Challenging the belief or agreeing it is true.","Forgetting the physical risk of weeks of poor eating.","Leaving out harm to others because he seems gentle."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS England: Early Intervention in Psychosis access and waiting time standard (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015) (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: psychosis (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, anyone aged 14 to 65 with a first episode of psychosis should be seen by an Early Intervention in Psychosis team, with treatment starting within two weeks of referral. Say 'EIP referral' in your plan.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-02","n":1402,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🩺","title":"Worried sick about her health: explaining health anxiety","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:30, CMHT outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team clinic room","text":"Bernadette Kowalski, a 52-year-old woman, has been referred by her GP, Dr Ellison, for persistent fears of serious illness. Over several years she has had X-rays, ultrasound and CT scans, all normal. Lately she has been sure her skin is turning yellow; nobody else can see it and her liver tests were normal. She still feels something is being missed. You have a ten-minute slot before the team meeting."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a brief history of her health fears, rule out depression and a delusional belief, explain health anxiety in terms of her own symptoms, and agree a concrete plan.","first60":["Read Dr Ellison's letter and the results list: which scans, when, and the latest LFTs and bilirubin.","Count the contacts in the last year if the record shows them: GP appointments, A&E visits, private tests.","Have a pen and paper ready: you will draw her worry-check-relief cycle in the explanation.","Have the local NHS Talking Therapies self-referral details and a health anxiety leaflet to hand.","Split the time: history 4 minutes, explanation 2, plan 2, close 1."],"ask":["'What worries you most about your health right now?'","'When did the health worries start? Was anything happening in your life then?'","'How many times a day do you check your skin or eyes?'","'How long do you spend reading about symptoms online?'","'When a test comes back normal, how long does the relief last?'","'Part of you may wonder if this is worry rather than illness. How likely does that feel, out of ten?'","'What's your mood been like? Are you still enjoying things?'","'Has the worry ever got so bad you felt life wasn't worth living?'"],"recognise":["Years of fearing one serious disease after another, normal tests and short-lived relief: health anxiety (hypochondriacal disorder).","Mirror checking, nightly searching, frequent GP and A&E visits and repeated tests keep the fear going: they are the targets for treatment.","Onset months after her mother died of pancreatic cancer, and the jaundice fear echoes that illness: link the two kindly.","She can say 'it might be worry': the belief is not fixed, so this is not a delusional disorder.","Bodily distress disorder centres on distressing symptoms themselves; health anxiety centres on the feared disease. Depression and OCD can coexist: screen briefly.","Repeated scans carry real harm: radiation, incidental findings and more anxiety."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain health anxiety using her own checking and searching, refer for CBT for health anxiety through NHS Talking Therapies, and write to Dr Ellison asking for one named GP, planned appointments and no new tests without new objective signs.","r":"Right: a personal explanation, a proven treatment, and a plan that stops the cycle of tests and reassurance.","best":true},{"c":"Order one more set of tests to settle her mind, then discharge her to the GP.","r":"Wrong: each normal test brings a few hours of relief and then the fear returns. More testing feeds the problem.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her the tests are normal and there is nothing wrong with her.","r":"Wrong: her distress is real and she will feel dismissed. Name the problem and offer treatment.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and chooses her treatment; CBT and any medicine are offered, not imposed.","Ask her consent before involving her husband, and record it.","The clinic letter goes to her GP; offer her a copy, as NHS good practice expects.","Doctors should not order tests with no clinical indication to reassure; document why further tests are not needed."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"These worries are real and exhausting, and you are not imagining how bad they feel. The good news is that this has a name, health anxiety, and a treatment that works well."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Each check gives you a few hours of relief, then the worry comes back stronger. CBT helps you break that loop, so the worry shrinks instead of the other way round."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: health anxiety. No psychosis or depressive episode. Please offer planned appointments every 2 to 4 weeks with one named GP and avoid new tests unless there are new objective findings. Referred to Talking Therapies for CBT."}],"write":"CMHT clinic. 52F. GP referral: persistent fears of serious illness; normal X-rays, USS, CT, LFTs.\nHx: ~6 yrs of illness fears, starting months after mother died of pancreatic cancer. Current fear: liver disease/cancer after noticing 'yellow' skin; normal LFTs not reassuring. Previous fears: bowel cancer, bone tumour.\nBehaviours: checks eyes and skin in mirror ~10x/day; searches symptoms online ~2 h most nights; GP ~weekly; A&E x3 this year; relief after normal results lasts hours.\nAccepts it 'might be worry' (6/10). No fixed belief. Mood: 'fed up' but enjoys family; sleep reduced by searching. No panic attacks. No obsessions outside health. No suicidal thoughts. Alcohol 2-3 units/week.\nImpression: health anxiety (hypochondriacal disorder). Ddx considered: delusional disorder (excluded, holds belief with doubt), depression (not met), bodily distress disorder, OCD.\nPlan: explained with her own cycle; leaflet; self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for health anxiety given; GP letter: one named GP, planned 2-4 weekly appts, no tests without new objective signs; husband to stop giving reassurance (consent given). Review 8 wks. Crisis: GP / NHS 111 option 2 if mood drops.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Keep the history tight: current fear, pattern, behaviours, onset, mood, certainty, risk.","Get numbers: checks a day, hours online, visits a year.","Use her symptoms in the explanation, not a textbook definition.","Draw the cycle: worry, check, relief, worry back.","Never say 'there is nothing wrong'; say 'your body is healthy, and the worry is the problem we can treat'.","End with something she does this week."],"next":"Mrs Kowalski self-refers to NHS Talking Therapies that afternoon. Dr Ellison books her fortnightly planned appointments. Four months into CBT she has cut her checking to once a day, stopped night-time searching, and has not been to A&E since.","marks":["Clarified the current fear, the earlier fears and her level of certainty.","Quantified checking, searching, reassurance and health-service use.","Linked onset to her mother's death.","Excluded depression, delusional belief and suicidal thoughts.","Explained health anxiety using her own cycle and checked understanding.","Agreed CBT, one named GP and limits on testing."],"pitfalls":["Offering another test 'to be sure'.","Giving a textbook speech that never mentions her checking or searching.","Missing the link to her mother's illness.","Not checking whether the belief is fixed.","Promising the worry will go without any work on her part."],"refs":["1. NICE CG123: common mental health problems: identification and pathways to care (2011; checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS website: health anxiety (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression manual (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS Talking Therapies accepts self-referral online in most areas. Give the patient the local link in clinic, so she can refer herself that day instead of waiting for a letter.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-03","n":1403,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"📁","title":"Demanding his records in A&E: a mental state assessment","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 15:10, mental health liaison","where":"A&E majors cubicle, general hospital","text":"Marcus Lindqvist, a 34-year-old man, walked into A&E asking to see his full medical records immediately. He is not known to mental health services. Nurses say he is loud, restless and behaving unusually, and they wonder whether he is mentally unwell. The A&E registrar, Dr Okafor, asks you to assess his mental state and tell her what you find."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess Marcus's mental state, describing what you observe and eliciting the key symptoms and risks, then present a structured summary and a safe next step to the A&E registrar.","first60":["Ask the nurse what she has seen: how long he's been here, whether he's eaten, any aggression, whether he arrived by car.","Check obs, capillary glucose and whether any bloods or a urine drug screen have been sent.","Pick a cubicle with space, the door open and a clear exit for both of you; tell security which cubicle you are in.","Start observing as you walk in: clothes, movement, speech, before he says a word.","Plan short, single questions: long sentences will lose him."],"ask":["'I'd like to help you with the records. Can we sit down for about ten minutes first?'","'Why do you need the records today?'","'How are you feeling in yourself, from 0 to 10?'","'Have you been chosen for something special? Tell me about the app.'","'Is anyone trying to stop you or take it from you?'","'How much sleep have you had this week?'","'How much money have you spent this week? On what?'","'Have you been drinking or taking anything, like cocaine or speed?'","'Has anyone made you so angry you felt like hurting them?'"],"recognise":["Bright suit, pacing, rapid pressured speech that is hard to interrupt and jumps between linked topics: psychomotor agitation, pressure of speech and flight of ideas.","Cheerful one moment and snapping the next: elated but irritable, labile affect.","A belief that he has been chosen to run a national health app, held with total conviction: a grandiose delusion, mood-congruent with mania.","Three nights without sleep but no tiredness, thousands spent this week: decreased need for sleep and reckless spending.","No prior history: exclude organic and drug causes (stimulants, steroids, thyroid, head injury) before calling it a first manic episode.","Risks: money, driving, sexual disinhibition, conflict when thwarted, and poor intake."],"decide":[{"c":"Present a probable manic episode with psychotic features, ask for bloods and a urine drug screen, ask him not to drive, brief the nurse in charge and your consultant, and request a Mental Health Act assessment if he tries to leave and risk is high.","r":"Right: a clear finding, organic causes checked, and a lawful plan for the moment he heads for the door.","best":true},{"c":"Explain that his records request must go in writing and let him go home, as he has no previous history.","r":"Wrong: no previous history does not mean low risk. He is not sleeping, spending heavily and may be driving.","best":false},{"c":"Detain him under Section 5(2) until a bed is found.","r":"Wrong: Section 5(2) only applies to admitted inpatients, not to people in A&E.","best":false}],"law":["He is free to leave A&E unless lawful grounds stop him. If he lacks capacity for the decision and is at serious risk, the MCA can allow brief proportionate restraint while you get help.","Section 5(2) and 5(4) do not apply in A&E. If risk is high and he refuses help, request a Mental Health Act assessment via the AMHP.","He has a right to his records through a subject access request: free, answered within one month, via the hospital records team. You do not hand over records on the spot.","Driving: advise him not to drive; DVLA guidance says acute mania or psychosis must be notified, and driving stops until stable."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how important this is to you, and I want to help. Sit with me for about ten minutes and I'll explain how you can get your records."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Marcus, you've had no sleep for three nights and you're spending a lot. I'm worried your mind is running too fast. I'd like to do some blood tests and get a senior doctor to see you."},{"to":"A&E registrar","s":"He's in a probable manic episode with grandiose delusions. Please send bloods and a urine drug screen. He must not drive. I'm calling my consultant; if he tries to leave, call me straight away."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 34M, self-presented asking for his full medical records. Not known to MH services.\nMSE. A&B: bright suit, dishevelled hair, pacing, overfamiliar, distractible by noises; sat after ~1 min with short questions. Speech: rapid, loud, pressured, hard to interrupt. Mood: 'brilliant, 10/10'; objectively elated and irritable, labile. Thought form: flight of ideas. Content: grandiose delusion of being chosen to run a national health app; believes records will prove it and that 'someone at the trust' is hiding them; no thoughts of harm to others. Perception: denies voices or visions. Cognition: oriented; attention poor. Insight: none ('never felt better').\nOther: ~3 nights no sleep, no tiredness; spent ~£4,000 this week; eaten little; drove here; denies drugs, alcohol 'a few beers'.\nRisk: financial, driving, conflict if thwarted, possible disinhibition; no suicidal thoughts.\nImpression: probable manic episode with psychotic features (first presentation). Ddx: stimulant intoxication, organic (thyroid, head injury, steroids), schizoaffective.\nPlan: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, UDS; advised not to drive (keys with partner); NIC and security aware; discussed with consultant; if leaves with high risk: MHA assessment via AMHP. SAR process explained. Partner Sofie to be called with consent.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Agree with what you can (the records) before you ask anything.","Short questions, one at a time; let him talk for a minute, then steer.","Describe, don't label: 'spoke rapidly and was hard to interrupt', not just 'manic'.","Always ask sleep, spending, driving, sex and drugs in mania.","Present in MSE order, then risk, then impression and plan.","Know what you will do if he walks out before you leave the cubicle."],"next":"Marcus's urine drug screen is negative and his TFTs are normal. His partner Sofie arrives and says he has been awake and spending for a week. After senior review he agrees to an informal admission. On the ward he starts olanzapine, and Sofie takes his car keys and bank card.","marks":["Stayed calm, aligned with his request and got him seated.","Described appearance, behaviour, speech and affect with examples.","Elicited the grandiose belief and checked for persecutory ideas and voices.","Asked about sleep, spending, driving, substances and sexual behaviour.","Assessed risk to others and to himself.","Presented a structured MSE with probable mania and a lawful plan."],"pitfalls":["Arguing about the records or the app.","Asking long, multi-part questions he cannot follow.","Forgetting drug and organic causes in a first presentation.","Missing driving and money as real risks.","Planning to hold him under Section 5(2) in A&E."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185: bipolar disorder (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015) (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. ICO: right of access (subject access requests) (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: when you present a mental state in the UK, use the standard order: appearance and behaviour, speech, mood and affect, thought form, thought content, perception, cognition, insight. Then risk, impression and plan.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-04","n":1404,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🪑","title":"Sitting in the armchair: mental state in psychotic depression","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:20, acute admissions ward","where":"Quiet room, acute adult psychiatric ward","text":"Stuart Halloran, a 54-year-old man, has just been admitted informally from the home treatment team, who could rarely get into his flat. He has been eating and drinking very little, stopped his medicines and spent most days in a soiled armchair. He has recurrent depression that has responded to ECT before, and hypertension. Medicines: sertraline 150 mg daily, mirtazapine 15 mg at night, ramipril 5 mg daily. Your consultant, Dr Rao, asks you to assess his mental state and report back before the ward round ends."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess Stuart's mental state at his pace, including depressive and psychotic symptoms, food and fluid intake and suicide risk, then present your findings and three same-day actions to your consultant.","first60":["Read the HTT handover: last known food and drink, weight, any falls, and what he has said about dying.","Ask the nurse for admission obs and NEWS2, and whether he has passed urine or drunk anything since arriving.","Bring a cup of water and sit at his level, angled, not opposite.","Plan to wait: count to ten in your head after each question before you rephrase.","Have the drug chart: ramipril plus little fluid intake is a kidney risk today."],"ask":["'Take as long as you need with each answer.'","'How's your mood been, from 0 to 10?'","'When did you last eat or drink anything?'","'What's been going through your mind, sitting in the chair?'","'Do you feel you've done something wrong? Do you deserve this?'","'Is something wrong with your body, do you think? Your insides?'","'Do you hear anyone talking when no one's there? What do they say?'","'Have you thought of ending your life? Do you want to stop eating so that you'll die?'"],"recognise":["Long response delay, quiet monosyllables, poor eye contact and little movement: psychomotor retardation, short of stupor but close.","Dry lips, unknown last drink, soiled clothes: dehydration and self-neglect are the immediate threat to life.","'My insides have rotted': a nihilistic delusion (towards Cotard's). Ruining his family and owing money: guilt and poverty delusions. All mood-congruent.","A voice calling him worthless: a second-person, derogatory, mood-congruent hallucination.","Severe depression with psychotic features, food and fluid refusal and past ECT response: a strong case for urgent ECT.","'No point eating' and 'already dead' can mean he is passively dying: treat as high suicide risk even if he denies a plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Present severe depression with psychotic features and high physical risk; today, send bloods including U&E and CK, start fluid and food charts with NEWS2, hold ramipril pending U&E and medical advice, set enhanced observations, and ask Dr Rao to consider urgent ECT and his capacity and legal status.","r":"Right: the physical danger is managed today, and the most effective treatment is put on the table.","best":true},{"c":"Restart sertraline and mirtazapine and review him in a week.","r":"Wrong: antidepressants take weeks, and he may not survive weeks of poor fluid intake. This needs a same-day physical plan and an ECT discussion.","best":false},{"c":"Accept 'no, I'm already dead' as a denial of suicidal ideation and set general observations.","r":"Wrong: that answer means he sees no reason to live and no point eating. Record it verbatim and treat the risk as high.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal. If he tries to leave and you think he needs detaining, Section 5(2) applies here on the ward; tell the nurse in charge Section 5(4) is available.","If he refuses food, fluids or treatment, assess capacity for each decision and discuss a Mental Health Act assessment with Dr Rao.","ECT for a detained patient who lacks capacity needs a SOAD certificate under Section 58A; in a life-threatening emergency, Section 62 allows urgent treatment.","With consent, contact his wife; if he cannot decide, share what is needed in his best interests and record why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"There's no rush, Stuart. I'll wait. Even a word or two helps me understand."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I know it feels hopeless, but this kind of depression does lift, and you've recovered from it before. Will you have a few sips of water with me?"},{"to":"Consultant","s":"He has severe depression with nihilistic, guilt and poverty delusions and a derogatory voice. He's dry and hasn't drunk for at least a day. I've sent bloods, started fluid charts and held ramipril. Can we discuss urgent ECT today?"}],"write":"Ward MSE. 54M, informal admission from HTT. Background recurrent depression, previous good response to ECT; HTN. Drugs: ramipril 5 mg od, sertraline 150 mg od, mirtazapine 15 mg nocte (adherence poor for ~2 weeks).\nA&B: unshaven, soiled clothes, dry lips, sitting motionless, minimal eye contact, stroking face; no catatonic posturing.\nSpeech: very quiet, monosyllabic, long latency (up to 20 s).\nMood: '0'; objectively depressed, flat. Anhedonia. Does not know when he last ate or drank. Sleep poor.\nThought content: nihilistic delusion ('my insides have rotted away'); guilt delusion (has ruined his family); poverty delusion (believes they have no money, wife says otherwise).\nPerception: second-person voice calling him 'worthless', mood-congruent. No command hallucinations described.\nCognition: oriented to place; slow; not formally tested.\nRisk: 'I'm already dead'; no point eating or drinking; stopped meds as 'nothing can help'. High risk of death through food/fluid refusal; high suicide risk. No harm to others.\nInsight: absent.\nImpression: severe depressive episode with psychotic features; high physical risk.\nPlan: U&E, creatinine, CK, FBC, LFT, glucose, TFT, calcium; ECG; NEWS2 4-hourly; fluid balance and food charts; daily weight; hold ramipril pending U&E (discuss with medics); enhanced observations (intermittent 15 min); consultant to review today re urgent ECT, capacity and MHA status.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Slow right down: one short question, then wait at least ten seconds.","Encourage every answer: 'Thank you, that helps.'","Look for nihilism, guilt, poverty and illness beliefs by name.","Ask about voices and what they say; record second or third person.","Ask specifically about food and fluids, not just 'appetite'.","Present the physical risk first: it is the thing that kills."],"next":"Stuart's urea and creatinine are raised; the medical team advise oral fluids with encouragement and an IV line if intake stays low, and ramipril stays on hold. Dr Rao assesses his capacity, finds he cannot weigh the decision about ECT, and he is detained under Section 3. With a SOAD certificate he starts ECT twice weekly. After six treatments he is eating, drinking and asking for his wife.","marks":["Approached gently, tolerated silence and encouraged answers.","Observed and described self-neglect, dehydration and psychomotor retardation.","Elicited nihilistic, guilt and poverty delusions.","Asked about voices and their content.","Assessed suicide, food and fluid refusal and medicine adherence.","Presented psychotic depression with high physical risk and same-day actions."],"pitfalls":["Firing questions before he has answered the last one.","Accepting 'I'm already dead' as 'no suicidal thoughts'.","Forgetting to ask about drinking, not just eating.","Missing ramipril in a dehydrated man.","Presenting the mood but no physical plan."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE TA59: electroconvulsive therapy (2003; checked Nov 2026)","3. MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015): consent to treatment, Sections 58A and 62 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: ECT Accreditation Service standards (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK wards, 'eating and drinking poorly' is a medical emergency in psychotic depression. Start a fluid balance chart and NEWS2 on day one, and call the on-call medical team if urine output falls or U&E worsen.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-05","n":1405,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Hostel worried he's relapsing after a switch: mental state and risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 19:15, liaison psychiatry on call","where":"A&E mental health assessment room","text":"Patrick Osei, a 38-year-old man with established paranoid schizophrenia, has been brought in by staff from his supported hostel. They say he has been hearing voices and acting strangely for about a week and fear a relapse. He is under the local CMHT, and three weeks ago his antipsychotic was changed from risperidone to aripiprazole 10 mg daily. The CMHT is closed. Assess his mental state, including risk, and phone your on-call consultant with your findings."},"task":"In about ten minutes, examine Patrick's mental state, characterise his voices and beliefs, check the medication switch, assess risk to himself and to the hostel manager, and present findings and a risk plan to your consultant.","first60":["Read the CMHT notes: why the switch, how it was done (cross-taper or straight swap), his relapse signature and his crisis plan.","Ask the hostel worker: what exactly has changed, has he threatened anyone, and is he taking his tablets?","Check the A&E obs and whether he has eaten; ask for a urine drug screen if he'll give one.","Use the assessment room with two exits; tell the nurse where you are.","Plan the ten minutes: observe, voices, beliefs, switch, risk, close, phone call."],"ask":["'The hostel staff were worried. How has this week been for you?'","'I noticed you looked over at the corner. Did you hear something?'","'How many voices? Are they speaking to you or about you? Do they tell you to do things?'","'Can other people read or take your thoughts? Does anything control your body?'","'What makes you think the manager is putting something in your food? How sure are you?'","'Since the new tablet, have you felt restless, like you can't sit still?'","'How many doses have you missed this week? Any cannabis or other drugs?'","'If the manager keeps doing this, what might you do?'"],"recognise":["Dishevelled, watchful, pausing and glancing at the corner mid-sentence: probably responding to voices. Name what you see and ask about it.","Two male voices discussing him in the third person and commenting on what he does: first-rank symptoms, consistent with his known illness relapsing.","Thoughts being taken out of his head: thought withdrawal, another first-rank symptom.","The poisoning belief targets a named person he lives with, and he is angry and refusing food: risk to that person and to his own nutrition.","Restlessness since the switch is likely akathisia from aripiprazole, which drives missed doses. A straight swap from risperidone, plus missed doses, invites relapse.","Previously well on risperidone: the history of what worked is the strongest guide to what to restart."],"decide":[{"c":"Present a psychotic relapse after the switch with akathisia and missed doses; phone the consultant about returning to risperidone, offer home treatment or admission by risk, and share the risk to the hostel manager with the hostel, telling Patrick first.","r":"Right: a clear cause, a fix for the medicine, and a named person protected.","best":true},{"c":"Increase aripiprazole to 30 mg tonight and send him back to the hostel.","r":"Wrong: a big jump worsens akathisia, which is already making him miss doses, and the risk to the manager is unmanaged.","best":false},{"c":"Keep the threat to the manager confidential because Patrick has not consented to sharing it.","r":"Wrong: GMC guidance permits sharing the minimum needed, even without consent, when a named person faces serious harm. Tell him what you will share and record why.","best":false}],"law":["He is in A&E, not an inpatient: Section 5(2) does not apply. If he refuses help and risk is high, request a Mental Health Act assessment through the AMHP.","Threat to a named person: disclose the minimum needed to the hostel to prevent serious harm, tell Patrick you are doing so if safe, and document the reasons (GMC confidentiality guidance).","Check for a Community Treatment Order or advance statement in the CMHT notes before deciding the plan.","Medication changes are made with his informed consent; record the discussion of options."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Patrick, it sounds like this week has been really hard: voices, and not trusting your food. I'd like to help get things settled again."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm going to let the hostel know you've been worried about the manager, so everyone stays safe. I won't share more than I need to."},{"to":"On-call consultant","s":"He's relapsed three weeks after a risperidone to aripiprazole switch, with akathisia and missed doses. Third-person voices, thought withdrawal, and a poisoning belief about the hostel manager, with anger. I'd like to discuss restarting risperidone and home treatment versus admission."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 38M, paranoid schizophrenia, known to CMHT. Brought by hostel staff: ~1 week of voices and odd behaviour. Switched risperidone to aripiprazole 10 mg od ~3 weeks ago (for weight gain).\nMSE. A&B: dishevelled, guarded, watchful, glancing at corner and pausing mid-sentence (?responding to voices); restless legs, shifting in chair. Speech: normal rate, some tangentiality. Mood: 'fine'; affect irritable when discussing manager. Thought content: persecutory delusion that hostel manager is poisoning his food (9/10); eating only sealed packets. Thought withdrawal ('they pull thoughts out'). Perception: two male voices, third person, discussing and commenting on his actions; no commands. No passivity. Cognition: oriented. Insight: partial (agrees he 'might be getting ill').\nMedication: missed ~4 doses this week; feels 'jittery, can't sit still' since switch (?akathisia). Cannabis: one joint last weekend. Alcohol nil.\nRisk: to others: angry with hostel manager, 'he'll be sorry if he carries on', no plan or weapon; to self: denies suicidal thoughts; self-neglect: poor intake.\nImpression: psychotic relapse following antipsychotic switch; akathisia; partial non-adherence.\nPlan: discussed with on-call consultant; UDS, bloods, ECG; restart risperidone per consultant; HTT referral vs admission per risk; hostel informed of risk to manager (Patrick told); CMHT care coordinator emailed for next working day.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Start open and let him talk; then use what you see (pauses, glances) as your way in.","Pin the voices down: number, gender, person, commentary, commands.","Ask about all three thought interferences and passivity by name.","Ask about the switch practically: when, how, doses missed, side effects.","Turn the named target into a specific risk question and a specific action.","Give the consultant one sentence of diagnosis before the detail."],"next":"The consultant agrees to restart risperidone at 2 mg, rising to his previous 4 mg, and to stop aripiprazole. Patrick accepts home treatment team visits with daily supervised doses; the hostel moves the manager off his corridor for a week. His care coordinator reviews him two days later, and the voices have quietened within three weeks.","marks":["Described appearance and behaviour, including signs of responding to voices.","Characterised the voices and asked about thought interference and passivity.","Explored the poisoning belief: evidence, certainty and actions.","Checked the switch: timing, method, adherence, akathisia and substances.","Assessed risk to the manager, to himself and from self-neglect.","Presented relapse after the switch with a named risk and medication plan."],"pitfalls":["Asking 'Are you hearing voices?' without using what you observed.","Not asking whether the voices give commands.","Missing akathisia as the reason for missed doses.","Keeping the threat to the manager to yourself.","Doubling aripiprazole without thinking about why he relapsed."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: aripiprazole, risperidone (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC: confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. MHA 1983 Code of Practice (2015) (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK practice, the CMHT notes usually include a crisis plan and a relapse signature. Read them before you see the patient; they tell you what worked last time and who to call out of hours.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-06","n":1406,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧂","title":"Lithium: should I switch?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:20, CMHT outpatient clinic","where":"Clinic room, community mental health team base","text":"Ewan Castellano, a 27-year-old man with bipolar affective disorder since 23, has had five admissions for mania. Quetiapine and then sodium valproate did not stop him relapsing. He left hospital six weeks ago after his latest manic episode and still takes olanzapine. He is well, takes every dose and keeps every appointment, but he has put on two stone and feels sluggish at work. He wants to talk about lithium. You have about ten minutes before your next patient."},"task":"In about ten minutes, explain lithium to Ewan: why it suits him, the checks before starting, dose and levels, side effects and toxicity, long-term monitoring and the dangers of stopping suddenly, and agree a next step.","first60":["Read the discharge summary: drug history, the last level of any mood stabiliser, and today's weight if recorded.","Check the latest bloods on the system: U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium. Note what is missing so you can request it today.","Scan his drug list and ask yourself: any NSAID, ACE inhibitor, ARB or diuretic? These change lithium levels.","Have the lithium patient pack ready: booklet, record book and alert card.","Plan your ten minutes: his view (2), why and how (3), side effects and toxicity (3), decision and close (2)."],"ask":["'What have you heard about lithium so far?'","'What matters most to you in choosing a medicine?'","'What worries you about it?'","'Do you take anything else, including ibuprofen or anything from the chemist?'","'Any kidney, thyroid or heart problems, or skin trouble like acne?'","'Are you and your partner planning a family?'","'Can you tell me in your words the signs that the level is too high?'"],"recognise":["Five manic admissions and two failed mood stabilisers: lithium is the obvious next step, and NICE names it first-line for long-term prevention.","He is well, insightful and reliable with tablets and appointments: exactly the person who can manage lithium safely.","Weight gain is his main grievance: say honestly that lithium can add some weight too, usually less than olanzapine.","He thinks lithium is 'toxic': the fear is half right. The answer is the monitoring and the warning signs, not reassurance alone.","Lithium in a man has no known effect on a baby he fathers; the pregnancy cautions apply to women taking it."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain why lithium suits him, the checks and level targets, side effects and toxicity, then agree baseline bloods today and a decision at a review in two weeks.","r":"Right: honest, specific and safe, and the choice stays his.","best":true},{"c":"Stop olanzapine today and start lithium at the same time to deal with the weight quickly.","r":"Wrong: he is six weeks out of a manic episode. Get lithium to a steady therapeutic level first, then taper olanzapine over weeks.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him lithium is perfectly safe as long as he takes his tablets.","r":"Wrong: it has a narrow range, real kidney and thyroid effects and dangerous interactions. False reassurance undermines consent.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity: starting lithium is his informed choice. Record the risks and benefits you discussed and his decision.","NHS patient safety alert on lithium: every patient gets the booklet, record book and alert card, and levels are shared between prescribers.","Shared care with the GP needs the GP's agreement in writing before monitoring is handed over.","DVLA: he must check the current rules for bipolar disorder and notify them if those rules require it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Two stone is a lot, and you've done everything right. Your wish to change makes complete sense, so let's look properly at lithium."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If you get sickness, diarrhoea, a shake that gets worse, feel unsteady or muddled, stop taking the lithium and get a level checked that day, through A&E if need be."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Please never stop it suddenly. Coming off quickly can bring on mania within weeks. If you want to stop, we'll reduce it slowly together."}],"write":"CMHT medication review. 27M, BPAD since 23, 5 admissions (mania). Failed: quetiapine, valproate (relapses). Discharged 6/52 ago, olanzapine since. Euthymic today; good insight, fully adherent.\nConcern: 2 st weight gain, lethargy on olanzapine. Priority: staying out of hospital. Worry: lithium 'toxic', blood tests; plans family with partner.\nDiscussed lithium: rationale (relapse prevention, lower suicide risk); baseline U&E/eGFR, TFTs, Ca, FBC, weight/BMI, ECG if CV risk; Priadel 400 mg nocte; 12-h level 1/52 after start and each change, target 0.6-0.8; weekly until stable, then 3-monthly yr 1; U&E, TFT, Ca, weight 6-monthly. SEs (thirst, polyuria, fine tremor, weight, metallic taste); long-term renal, thyroid, calcium. Toxicity signs and action. Interactions: NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, diuretics; hydration. Not to stop abruptly. Olanzapine taper only once lithium therapeutic. No known risk via fathers.\nTeach-back correct. Lithium pack given.\nPlan: baseline bloods today; review 2/52 to decide; GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask what he knows and what matters to him before you explain anything.","Give numbers: 400 mg at night, 12 hours after the dose, 0.6 to 0.8.","Be honest about weight: lithium can add some too.","Name toxicity signs and the action to take, not just 'side effects'.","Name the over-the-counter trap: ibuprofen and naproxen.","Finish with teach-back and a dated next step."],"next":"Ewan has his baseline bloods that afternoon; all are normal. At review two weeks later he chooses lithium. Priadel 400 mg at night gives a 12-hour level of 0.5, so it goes up to 600 mg; the next level is 0.7. Six weeks later olanzapine is tapered over a month. By spring he has lost a stone and still carries his alert card.","marks":["Explored his knowledge, worries and priority before explaining.","Explained why lithium suits him: relapse prevention and lower suicide risk.","Named baseline tests, start dose, 12-hour level and target range.","Described common side effects honestly, including weight.","Named toxicity signs, interacting drugs and dehydration, with clear action.","Covered long-term monitoring, the risk of stopping suddenly, teach-back and a dated next step."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing on lithium pharmacology without asking what he wants to know.","Promising he will lose weight on lithium.","Forgetting over-the-counter ibuprofen as a cause of toxicity.","Stopping olanzapine before lithium is at a therapeutic level.","Saying 'blood tests regularly' without giving the timing or the target."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185: bipolar disorder (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: lithium carbonate, monitoring and interactions (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS patient safety alert: safer lithium therapy, and the lithium patient pack (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: lithium (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK lithium is always prescribed by brand, and every patient gets a purple record book and alert card. Levels are taken 12 hours after the dose, so a bedtime dose means a morning blood test.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-07","n":1407,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🪞","title":"Nose surgery requests: what's going on?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00, CMHT new-patient clinic","where":"Clinic room, community mental health team base","text":"Adeel Rashid, a 22-year-old man who works in a phone shop, has been referred by Mr Hale, a private cosmetic surgeon. Adeel keeps asking for an operation to reshape his nose, yet there is no visible problem to correct. He has seen several surgeons and keeps coming back for another opinion. He has agreed to see a psychiatrist, though he isn't sure why. Your consultant wants a focused history, the risks and your likely diagnosis before she joins you in about ten minutes."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of Adeel's concern about his nose, test how firmly he holds it, assess the risks (suicide, self-surgery, risky procedures), screen the main differentials and name the likely diagnosis.","first60":["Read Mr Hale's letter: how many consultations, what was requested, any procedures done.","Check the GP summary for past mood problems, self-harm or antidepressants.","Decide your stance: take the distress seriously, but never agree that his nose is abnormal.","Sit side by side or at an angle: people with this worry often hate being looked at face on.","Plan your ten minutes: the concern (2), behaviours and impact (2), surgery (1), screen (1.5), risk (1.5), close (2)."],"ask":["'What is it about your nose that bothers you?'","'How many hours a day does it take up?'","'How sure are you, out of 100, that it looks the way you think?'","'What do you do to check it or hide it?'","'What has it stopped you doing?'","'What would change for you if the operation happened?'","'Have you ever thought of doing something to your nose yourself, or buying a treatment online?'","'Has it ever got so bad that you've felt life isn't worth it?'"],"recognise":["Hours of daily preoccupation with a defect others cannot see, plus checking, hiding and reassurance-seeking: body dysmorphic disorder.","Repeated surgical opinions are typical. Surgery rarely helps, and dissatisfaction afterwards often raises the risk.","Insight ranges from good to absent. Even near-total certainty is still BDD 'with poor to absent insight', not a separate psychosis.","Suicidal thinking is common in BDD. Ask directly, every time.","Watch for DIY risk: home 'filler pens', online kits, overseas clinics or attempts to reshape his nose himself.","Look for low mood, social anxiety, OCD and eating or muscle concerns: they often coexist."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused history with conviction and risk, name likely body dysmorphic disorder, advise against procedures for now, write to Mr Hale with consent and book follow-up for treatment.","r":"Right: covers the diagnosis, the specific BDD risks and a safe next step.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure him that his nose looks normal and send him back to the surgeon to discuss options.","r":"Wrong: reassurance feeds the cycle, and surgery is likely to leave him worse and at higher risk.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him his belief is delusional and start an antipsychotic.","r":"Wrong: poor insight is still BDD. First-line treatment is CBT with exposure and response prevention and an SSRI.","best":false}],"law":["He is an adult with capacity: share findings with Mr Hale only with his consent, and record that consent.","Private surgeons and clinics have their own duty to screen and decline procedures for people with likely BDD; your letter supports that.","If he discloses imminent suicidal intent, confidentiality can be broken to keep him safe; tell him first where possible.","Document the risk assessment and the plan the same day on the electronic record."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how much this hurts. I won't argue about how your nose looks; I'm interested in what this worry is doing to your life."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you describe has a name: body dysmorphic disorder. It's common, it's not vanity, and it responds well to the right treatment."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Adeel is 22, with four to five hours a day of nose preoccupation, camera checking and hiding, 90% conviction and passive suicidal thoughts. He has looked at a home filler pen. Likely BDD with poor insight and low mood."}],"write":"CMHT new assessment. 22M, phone shop sales. Referred by private cosmetic surgeon (Mr Hale): repeated rhinoplasty requests, no visible defect.\nConcern: nose 'too big, bent at bridge' since ~15 (teased 'beak' at school). Preoccupied 4-5 h/day. Conviction 90%; accepts others see it differently but 'they're being kind'. Ideas of reference at work, partly reality-tested.\nBehaviours: phone-camera checking, covers nose in photos, angles face, tapes nose at night, compares online, asks sister for reassurance.\nImpact: stopped gym, avoids dates, swapped to stockroom shifts. Seen 3 surgeons in 2 yrs; spent ~£600 on consultations.\nScreen: low mood and anhedonia ~3 months; no OCD outside appearance; no eating restriction or muscle concern; no psychotic symptoms beyond above; alcohol weekends, no drugs.\nRisk: passive SI ('pointless if not fixed'), no plan, no past attempts; has looked at buying a filler pen online, not bought; no anger or threats to surgeons. Protective: sister, job, agreed to help.\nImpression: BDD, poor insight, moderate-severe; depressive symptoms. Ddx: social anxiety, OCD, depression, delusional disorder (somatic).\nPlan: consent to write to Mr Hale and GP; advised no procedures; f/u 2 weeks with consultant for CBT/ERP referral and SSRI discussion; crisis: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask how sure he is, as a number out of 100.","Ask how many hours a day, not just 'does it bother you'.","List his checking and hiding behaviours: they are both the diagnosis and the treatment target.","Ask about DIY procedures and online kits by name.","Ask suicide directly; BDD carries a high risk.","Never agree the defect is real, and never call him vain."],"next":"With Adeel's consent you write to Mr Hale and his GP. At follow-up with your consultant he agrees to CBT with exposure and response prevention and starts fluoxetine. He deletes the filler pen bookmarks with his therapist in session four. Three months on he has been back to the gym twice.","marks":["Explored the perceived defect, onset and hours of preoccupation.","Tested conviction without confirming or dismissing the defect.","Identified checking, hiding, comparing and reassurance-seeking, and the effect on his life.","Screened for depression, OCD, eating disorder, psychosis and substance use.","Asked directly about suicide, self-surgery, DIY procedures and anger at surgeons.","Named likely body dysmorphic disorder and gave a safe next step with a crisis route."],"pitfalls":["Telling him his nose looks fine and expecting that to help.","Missing DIY or overseas cosmetic procedures as a risk.","Calling high conviction 'delusional disorder' and missing BDD.","Forgetting to screen for eating disorder or muscle dysmorphia.","Skipping a direct suicide question because he seems calm."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. ICD-11 6B21 body dysmorphic disorder, insight specifier (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: body dysmorphic disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. BDD Foundation: information for patients and clinicians (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK cosmetic surgeons often refer people with suspected BDD rather than operate. Your letter back, with his consent, helps stop another surgeon offering a procedure.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-08","n":1408,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💬","title":"Stopping sertraline: what's the real problem?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:40, CMHT outpatient clinic","where":"Clinic room, community mental health team base","text":"Shona Gallagher, a 28-year-old woman, was referred by her GP, Dr Kaur. She started sertraline a month ago for depression and now takes 100 mg once daily. Her mood scores have improved, so Dr Kaur was surprised when Shona asked to stop because she 'doesn't like the side effects'. Despite a good relationship, Shona would not say more and asked to see a psychiatrist. You have a ten-minute slot."},"task":"In about ten minutes, find out exactly which side effect is troubling Shona, check her mood and other causes, and agree a safe plan with her that does not involve stopping sertraline suddenly.","first60":["Read Dr Kaur's letter: start date, dose steps, the mood scores before and now.","Check the GP record for past episodes, contraception and recent bloods.","Expect a sexual side effect: it is the commonest reason people stop SSRIs and won't say why.","Make sure the door is closed and you won't be interrupted. Have the words ready: desire, arousal, orgasm.","Plan your ten minutes: the problem (3), mood and causes (3), options and plan (3), close (1)."],"ask":["'Some side effects are hard to mention. Is it something to do with sex, sleep or weight?'","'Is it your interest in sex, becoming aroused, or reaching orgasm?'","'How was that before you were depressed, and while you were depressed?'","'Is it the same when you're on your own?'","'How is this affecting you and your boyfriend?'","'What contraception do you use? Any plans for pregnancy?'","'Has anything sexual ever happened that you didn't want?'","'Any thoughts of hurting yourself since your mood dipped?'"],"recognise":["A good response on mood scores plus a vague 'side effects' and refusal to explain usually means a sexual side effect.","Separate the three phases: desire, arousal and orgasm. SSRIs most often delay or block orgasm.","Timeline matters: normal before depression, low desire during it, and new orgasm problems a week into sertraline point to the drug.","Depression itself, hormonal contraception, alcohol, relationship strain and physical illness can all cause similar problems: check, don't assume.","The real danger is that she stops suddenly at home: discontinuation symptoms and relapse of depression."],"decide":[{"c":"Explore the problem specifically, check mood and other causes, offer dose reduction or a switch, warn against stopping suddenly, and agree a written plan and GP letter.","r":"Right: keeps her well and treats the side effect she cares about.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her it will wear off and ask her to carry on at 100 mg.","r":"Wrong: sexual side effects often persist. Dismissing them is the fastest way to make her stop on her own.","best":false},{"c":"Agree to stop sertraline today, as she's feeling better.","r":"Wrong: one month in, stopping invites relapse and discontinuation symptoms. Continue treatment for at least six months after recovery.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: she decides what her boyfriend and her GP are told. Agree the GP letter's wording with her.","Shared decision-making: she has capacity, and the choice between options is hers after a balanced explanation.","If she discloses unwanted sexual contact, offer support and, with consent, a sexual assault referral centre or specialist service; safeguarding duties apply if others are at risk.","Record the options discussed and her choice; any dose change is copied to the GP the same day."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Sexual side effects are among the commonest with these tablets. You're not the first to tell me, and I'm glad you did, because we can sort it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Please don't stop suddenly. Coming off quickly can make you dizzy and irritable, and stopping this early risks the depression coming back."},{"to":"GP","s":"Shona has delayed orgasm and reduced arousal on sertraline 100 mg, with good mood response. She chose to reduce to 50 mg; please review mood and side effects in four weeks."}],"write":"CMHT review. 28F, 2nd depressive episode (1st at 22). Sertraline started 4/52 ago, now 100 mg OD. GP PHQ-9 19 -> 8.\nConcern: wants to stop due to 'side effects'. Disclosed: from ~1/52 on sertraline, much delayed or absent orgasm and reduced arousal, alone and with partner. Desire low during depression, now returning. Normal sexual function before depression. Mild nausea in wk 1, resolved.\nImpact: arguments with boyfriend (thinks she's gone off him); avoiding sex; low self-esteem about it.\nMood: brighter, sleeping, back at work. No SI now or this episode; no self-harm history.\nOther causes: COCP for 5 yrs (no change); alcohol 4 units/wk; no drugs; well, recent GP bloods normal; relationship otherwise supportive; no unwanted sexual experiences; no pregnancy plans this year.\nImpression: SSRI-induced sexual dysfunction (arousal and orgasm), good antidepressant response.\nPlan (shared): sertraline reduce to 50 mg OD; review 4/52 with PHQ-9 and sexual function; if mood slips, back to 100 mg; if side effect persists, switch (mirtazapine discussed). Do not stop abruptly; continue >= 6 months after remission. GP letter wording agreed: 'sexual side effect' named with consent. Crisis: GP same day, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Name the likely topic for her: 'Is it to do with sex?' makes it easy to say yes.","Ask about desire, arousal and orgasm separately.","Get the timeline: before depression, during depression, on sertraline.","Check other causes before blaming the drug.","Offer real choices with trade-offs, not just 'stick with it'.","Agree what the GP letter will say."],"next":"Shona drops to 50 mg. At four weeks her mood score holds at 7 and orgasm is easier, though not back to normal. She and her boyfriend talk openly for the first time. She stays on 50 mg and plans to discuss stopping slowly after six months well.","marks":["Normalised and named sexual side effects so she could disclose.","Separated desire, arousal and orgasm and set them against a timeline.","Checked current mood, past episodes and suicidal thoughts.","Screened other causes, including contraception, alcohol, physical health, relationship and unwanted experiences.","Offered dose reduction and switch options with trade-offs and warned against stopping suddenly.","Agreed a dated plan, the GP letter wording and a crisis route."],"pitfalls":["Looking embarrassed or using vague words, so she stays vague too.","Assuming sertraline is the only cause.","Telling her to just wait and see.","Agreeing to stop at once because her mood is better.","Writing to the GP with details she didn't agree to share."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: sertraline, mirtazapine, side effects and switching (checked Nov 2026)","3. NHS website: sertraline side effects and stopping (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: stopping antidepressants (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK clinics you are expected to ask about sexual function plainly and without embarrassment. A clear, matter-of-fact question earns trust; hesitation teaches the patient to stay silent.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-09","n":1409,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🗂️","title":"New editor missing deadlines: is it ADHD?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:10, adult outpatient clinic","where":"Outpatient clinic room, general adult psychiatry","text":"Imogen Faulkner, a 37-year-old woman, was a highly regarded features journalist until she was promoted to editor last year. Since then her output has dropped and she keeps missing deadlines. Her occupational health service has referred her. Your consultant asks for a focused history and an initial diagnosis in about ten minutes. You do not need to plan investigations or treatment, but Imogen will want to know what happens next."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history from Imogen to reach an initial diagnosis: current symptoms, why now, childhood onset, impact, the main alternative explanations and brief risk, then tell her what happens next.","first60":["Read the occupational health letter: what the employer has noticed and whether she is on a performance plan.","Check the GP summary for past mood, anxiety, alcohol or thyroid problems.","Hold the core triad in mind: inattention, hyperactivity, impulsivity, all present before 12 and in more than one setting.","Have the 6-item adult ADHD self-report screener ready for her to complete afterwards.","Plan your ten minutes: now (2.5), childhood (1.5), impact (0.75), other causes (1.5), risk and background (1.5), close (1.75)."],"ask":["'What's been happening at work, in your words?'","'Do you lose focus, lose things or leave tasks half-done?'","'Do you find it hard to sit through meetings, or catch yourself interrupting?'","'How did you cope as a writer that you can't as an editor?'","'What did your school reports say about you?'","'Is there anyone who knew you as a child I could speak to?'","'Have you ever had a spell of feeling unusually high or needing much less sleep?'","'Any speeding fines, accidents or impulsive spending?'"],"recognise":["A lifelong pattern of inattention, restlessness and impulsivity, unmasked when structure or demands change, is typical of adult ADHD.","Diagnosis needs symptoms before 12, in two or more settings, with real impairment: get school reports or a parent's account.","High intelligence and adrenaline-driven work often hide ADHD for years; managing others and long-term planning exposes it.","Rule out the mimics: depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety, burnout, poor sleep, thyroid disease, and alcohol or drug use.","Impulsivity carries real risks: driving offences, spending, substance use. Ask about each."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused developmental and current history, screen the mimics and risks, give adult ADHD as an initial impression, and explain the specialist assessment with collateral.","r":"Right: exactly the job asked, and she leaves knowing what comes next.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose ADHD today and offer to start lisdexamfetamine.","r":"Wrong: diagnosis needs a full specialist assessment with collateral, and stimulants need cardiovascular checks first.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her it is stress from the new role and suggest she ask to go back to writing.","r":"Wrong: misses a lifelong pattern with childhood onset and denies her an assessment that could save her job.","best":false}],"law":["She consents to the assessment; share findings with occupational health only with her consent, and agree what the letter says.","Equality Act 2010: ADHD can count as a disability; reasonable adjustments do not have to wait for the diagnosis.","DVLA: drivers must tell the DVLA if ADHD affects their ability to drive safely.","In England, patients may be able to choose their provider for a specialist ADHD assessment under NHS Right to Choose."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You were a successful writer for years, so this isn't about ability. Something changed when the job changed, and I'd like to understand what."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've told me, this fits adult ADHD. I can't confirm it today: that needs a fuller assessment and someone who knew you as a child."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Imogen, 37, has lifelong inattention, restlessness and impulsivity from primary school, unmasked by losing her flexible writing role. No mood episodes, little alcohol. Impression: likely adult ADHD; needs full assessment with collateral."}],"write":"Adult OPD, OH referral. 37F journalist, promoted to editor 1 yr ago; missed deadlines, low output; now on performance plan.\nNow: poor sustained attention (can't type >10 min), loses keys/phone daily, starts many tasks finishes few, forgets meetings; restless in meetings, interrupts colleagues, impulsive decisions.\nWhy now: as writer coped with flexible hours, pacing, dictation, deadline adrenaline; editor role needs planning, meetings, managing a team.\nChildhood: reports 'bright but daydreams', 'talks too much', detentions for calling out; symptoms at home and school before 12. Mother available for collateral; has old reports.\nImpact: work (probation), home (unpaid bills, late fees), relationship (partner frustrated), driving (2 speeding fines).\nOther causes: mood fine, no anhedonia; no episodes of elation or reduced sleep need; mild anxiety secondary to work; no ASD traits; sleep OK; alcohol ~6 u/wk (heavier in early 20s); no drugs; TFTs not done recently.\nRisk: no SI; driving and spending impulsivity.\nBackground: brother similar at school; no FHx sudden cardiac death; no personal cardiac history.\nImpression: likely adult ADHD (combined presentation). Ddx: bipolar, depression, anxiety, ASD, alcohol, thyroid - less likely.\nPlan: ASRS screener today; refer specialist adult ADHD assessment (structured interview + collateral from mother + school reports); TFTs via GP; OH letter (content agreed); DVLA advice given.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask for her own story first, then target the triad.","Get concrete examples, not yes or no answers.","Prove childhood onset: school reports and a parent.","Ask about mania as episodes; ADHD is constant, bipolar comes and goes.","Ask about driving and money, not just suicide.","Tell her exactly what happens next."],"next":"Imogen completes the screener in the waiting room and scores in the likely range. With her consent you write to occupational health, who agree written instructions and protected focus time. Six months later the specialist service confirms ADHD after speaking to her mother and seeing her school reports.","marks":["Elicited inattentive, hyperactive and impulsive symptoms with real examples.","Explained why problems surfaced now and how she coped before.","Established onset before 12 in more than one setting and named a collateral source.","Screened depression, mania, anxiety, autism traits, sleep, thyroid and substances.","Asked about suicidal thoughts, driving, spending, family and cardiac history.","Gave likely adult ADHD as an initial impression and clear next steps."],"pitfalls":["Taking only the current work story and missing the childhood history.","Labelling it stress or burnout without testing the pattern.","Not asking about episodes of elated mood.","Promising a diagnosis or medication today.","Forgetting the risks of impulsivity, such as driving."],"refs":["1. NICE NG87: ADHD diagnosis and management (2018, updated 2019; checked Nov 2026)","2. ICD-11 6A05 attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (checked Nov 2026)","3. GOV.UK: ADHD and driving, DVLA guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: ADHD in adults (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK only a specialist psychiatrist or suitably qualified clinician makes an adult ADHD diagnosis, after a structured interview and collateral. Your job in clinic is a sharp initial impression and a clean referral.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-10","n":1410,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚑","title":"Paracetamol overdose: assessing risk in A&E","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 13:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"A&E majors cubicle, general hospital","text":"Chloe Matthews, a 28-year-old woman who works as a receptionist, was brought in after a paracetamol overdose. She is on her acetylcysteine infusion and is now alert. The A&E doctor has asked liaison psychiatry to see her. You have about ten minutes before the next referral."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a history of the overdose (before, during, after), assess her current risk of further self-harm and suicide, and agree a safety plan and follow-up with her.","first60":["Read the A&E notes: time of ingestion, number of tablets, paracetamol level, LFTs, INR and the treatment plan.","Check the record for past self-harm, mental health contacts and safeguarding flags.","Ask the nurse: is she drowsy, has she tried to leave, has anyone visited?","Find privacy: close the curtain, sit at eye level, ask any visitor to step out.","Plan your ten minutes: before (1.25), during (1), after (0.75), mental state and history (1.5), risk (1.25), formulation (0.5), plan and close (3)."],"ask":["'Can you take me through what happened, from the start of the day?'","'When did you first think about taking tablets? Where did you get them?'","'Did you do anything to stop people finding you, or leave a note or message?'","'What did you think would happen when you took them?'","'How do you feel now about still being here?'","'Are you having thoughts about ending your life right now?'","'Have you hurt yourself before?'","'Do you feel safe at home?'"],"recognise":["Thirty-two tablets with alcohol after a row, then a text to her sister an hour later: an impulsive act with ambivalent intent and help-seeking.","Months of low mood with poor sleep and little enjoyment suggest an untreated depressive episode beneath the crisis.","Shame and embarrassment are common now; ask directly about current thoughts rather than reading relief as safety.","A row with a partner as the trigger: ask privately about domestic abuse.","NICE: do not use risk scales or a 'low/medium/high' label to decide care. Formulate her needs and agree a plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Take the overdose narrative, assess current thoughts, mental state and supports, agree a written safety plan with her sister's help, and arrange crisis team follow-up and a GP letter once she is medically fit.","r":"Right: thorough, needs-based and collaborative, and it keeps her safe after she leaves.","best":true},{"c":"Give her a risk score and discharge with a leaflet, as she says she regrets it.","r":"Wrong: NICE advises against risk scores to decide care, and regret now does not remove her underlying depression or risk.","best":false},{"c":"Detain her under section 5(2) in case she tries to leave before the drip finishes.","r":"Wrong: section 5(2) cannot be used in A&E. If she tries to leave the medical treatment, assess capacity under the Mental Capacity Act.","best":false}],"law":["If she wants to leave before acetylcysteine finishes: assess capacity to refuse treatment under the MCA 2005. Section 5(2) does not apply in A&E.","If mental disorder appears to drive an unsafe exit, an MHA assessment can be requested; police section 136 applies only outside a dwelling.","Confidentiality: involve her sister with her consent. Share without consent only if there is serious, imminent risk to life.","If domestic abuse is disclosed: safeguarding advice, a DASH risk check and an IDVA referral with consent."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for talking to me. It sounds like things have been really hard for a while. Nothing you say will shock me; I just want to understand."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Let's write your plan down together: the warning signs, what helps, who you'll call, and getting the extra tablets out of the house."},{"to":"A&E doctor","s":"Chloe took 32 paracetamol with wine after a row and texted her sister for help. She has a depressive episode, no current plans, and a safety plan with her sister. Psychiatrically fit for discharge once medically cleared; crisis team follow-up arranged."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 28F receptionist. Paracetamol OD last night, on acetylcysteine; alert, orientated.\nBefore: row with partner ~20:00; bought 2 x 16 paracetamol from two shops on the way home; no prior planning; no note; no final acts; partner out; didn't lock door.\nDuring: took all 32 over ~1 h with a bottle of wine; no other drugs; 'wanted everything to stop', thought she might die, unsure.\nAfter: texted sister ~1 h later; sister called 999. Now embarrassed, 'glad sister came', wants to live for her nephew.\nMental state: low mood ~4 months, poor sleep, low enjoyment, tearful at work; hopeless at times; no psychotic symptoms. Alcohol: ~3 bottles wine/wk. No drugs.\nHistory: superficial cutting at 16; no previous OD; no MH contact; not on medication.\nCurrent: no suicidal intent or plan; passive thoughts last week. Home: partner shouts in rows, denies violence or control, feels safe. No children; close to sister and nephew.\nFormulation: impulsive, ambivalent OD with help-seeking, on a background of untreated depression and alcohol use. Needs: treatment of depression, alcohol, relationship stress, means restriction, follow-up.\nPlan: complete acetylcysteine and repeat bloods (A&E); written safety plan with sister (consented); sister to remove tablets; crisis team contact within 72 h of discharge; GP letter today for review within a week; Talking Therapies; alcohol advice. Crisis: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Ask for the story first, in order: before, during, after.","Ask what she expected the tablets to do.","Ask about current thoughts directly; don't read embarrassment as safety.","Ask about home safety privately.","Write the safety plan with her, not for her.","Tell A&E and the GP what you found and what happens next."],"next":"Chloe finishes her infusion; her liver tests stay normal. Her sister clears the tablets from the flat and stays the weekend. The crisis team phones on Monday and visits on Tuesday. Her GP starts sertraline and refers her to Talking Therapies; she cuts her drinking to weekends.","marks":["Took a clear before, during and after narrative, including planning, precautions and intent.","Asked about current suicidal thoughts, hopelessness and feelings about surviving.","Screened depression, psychosis, alcohol and past self-harm.","Asked about home safety, dependants and protective factors.","Formulated needs rather than a risk score.","Agreed a written safety plan, means removal, follow-up and a GP letter."],"pitfalls":["Firing a checklist instead of hearing her story.","Labelling her 'low risk' because she seems embarrassed.","Forgetting to ask about domestic abuse after a row with a partner.","Planning discharge before she is medically fit.","Leaving tablets in the house with no plan to remove them."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225: self-harm assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","3. TOXBASE / BNF: paracetamol overdose and acetylcysteine (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: help for suicidal thoughts (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services no longer use risk scores to decide care after self-harm. Write a short formulation of her needs and a safety plan made with her, and copy it to the GP the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-12","n":1412,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚡","title":"Out-of-body spells and odd face movements: is it epilepsy?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20, neuropsychiatry outpatients","where":"Clinic room 4, general hospital outpatient block","text":"Funmilayo (Funmi) Bankole, 22, has been referred by her GP. For two years she has had brief spells of watching herself from above and feeling that nothing around her is real. Each lasts a few minutes, and people with her have noticed odd movements of her face. She has looked her symptoms up online and is frightened. She has come alone; her flatmate has seen several episodes."},"task":"Take a focused history of the episodes in about ten minutes, enough to name the most likely diagnosis, then explain it, the tests and the immediate safety advice to Funmi.","first60":["Read the GP letter: duration, frequency, any witness account, medicines, and whether she drives.","Check the system for previous A&E attendances, head scans, EEGs or neurology letters.","Decide your frame before she sits down: before, during, after, pattern, causes, impact.","Have a pen ready to note her exact words for the aura: they matter more than labels.","Know where the urgent neurology or epilepsy clinic referral form is, so you can promise a real next step."],"ask":["'Can you take me through the last one, from the very first thing you noticed?'","'Just before it starts, do you notice any feeling in your stomach, a smell or a taste?'","'Are you aware of what's going on around you during it? Can you hear and answer people?'","'What has your flatmate seen you do with your face or hands?'","'How do you feel afterwards, and how long until you're back to normal?'","'Have you ever had a fit as a child, a bad head injury or meningitis?'","'Do you drive, swim or work at heights?'","'How has all this affected your mood?'"],"recognise":["A rising feeling in the stomach and a strange smell before each spell are classic auras: a focal seizure starting in the temporal lobe.","Staring, lip smacking and not responding for a minute are automatisms with impaired awareness.","Confusion and tiredness for about 20 minutes afterwards point to a seizure, not panic or depersonalisation disorder.","Stereotyped, brief episodes that are worse with tiredness and alcohol fit epilepsy.","Febrile convulsions in early childhood are a recognised risk factor for temporal lobe epilepsy.","Six months of lower mood and a shrinking life are common in people with seizures: screen mood and risk."],"decide":[{"c":"Name likely focal seizures from the temporal lobe, explain in plain words, refer urgently to neurology for EEG and MRI, advise no driving and inform the DVLA, give water and height safety advice, and ask her flatmate to video an episode.","r":"Right: a confident working diagnosis with a clear route to confirm it, and the safety steps that cannot wait.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose depersonalisation disorder and refer her for CBT.","r":"Wrong: automatisms, a smell aura and post-episode confusion do not happen in depersonalisation disorder. Missing epilepsy leaves her driving and untreated.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her it is probably anxiety but book an EEG to be sure.","r":"Unsafe: a normal EEG between seizures does not rule out epilepsy, and she needs a neurologist and driving advice now.","best":false}],"law":["DVLA: a person with a suspected seizure must stop driving and tell the DVLA; doctors must advise this and record that they did.","GMC: if a patient keeps driving against advice, you may disclose to the DVLA after telling her you plan to do so.","MHRA: valproate must not be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists independently agree there is no alternative; in women, the pregnancy prevention programme applies.","She can consent to her own care; share details with her flatmate only with her agreement."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Funmi, from what you've told me, I don't think you're going mad, and nothing points to a tumour. These spells sound like small seizures starting in one part of the brain, the temporal lobe."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Until the neurologist has seen you, please don't drive, and let the DVLA know. Showers rather than baths, and no swimming on your own."},{"to":"GP and neurology","s":"Funmi Bankole, 22: two years of stereotyped episodes with epigastric and smell aura, depersonalisation, lip smacking, impaired awareness and 20 minutes' confusion. Febrile convulsions in infancy. Likely focal temporal lobe seizures. Please see urgently; driving advice given."}],"write":"Neuropsychiatry clinic. Funmi Bankole, 22F, GP referral.\nEpisodes for 2 years, now about twice a month. Aura: rising epigastric feeling, smell of burning rubber, déjà vu, fear; then detached, watching herself from above, surroundings unreal. Witness (flatmate): stares, lip smacking, picking at clothes, unresponsive about 1 minute. After: confused and tired about 20 minutes, patchy memory. No tongue biting, incontinence or generalised convulsions. Worse with poor sleep and alcohol.\nPMH: febrile convulsions age 2. No head injury, meningitis or encephalitis. No regular medicines, no drugs. FH: nil epilepsy.\nImpact: cut hours at pharmacy job, avoids going out; mood lower 6 months; no suicidal thoughts. Drives occasionally.\nImpression: likely focal impaired-awareness seizures of temporal lobe origin. Ddx: depersonalisation-derealisation disorder, panic attacks, migraine aura, dissociative seizures (less likely).\nPlan: urgent neurology referral; suggest EEG (sleep-deprived if routine normal), MRI brain epilepsy protocol, bloods (FBC, U&E, glucose, calcium, magnesium), 12-lead ECG via GP. Advised to stop driving and inform DVLA. Safety advice: showers, no lone swimming, no heights. Flatmate to video an episode. Leaflet: Epilepsy Action. Review after neurology.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Go through the spell in time order: before, during, after. Ask about each phase with specific questions.","Ask for the witness account: lip smacking and staring are the clinchers she cannot report herself.","Ask about febrile convulsions, head injury, infections and family history in one quick sweep.","Name the diagnosis in plain words and answer her fear of a tumour or madness directly.","Give the tests, the referral and the driving advice before she leaves the room.","Screen mood and suicidal thoughts briefly: seizures and low mood often travel together."],"next":"Funmi sees the epilepsy clinic within three weeks. Her flatmate's phone video shows lip smacking and staring, and a sleep-deprived EEG shows left temporal sharp waves. MRI is normal. She starts lamotrigine with a slow titration, stops driving and tells the DVLA, and returns to full-time work after four seizure-free months.","marks":["Built rapport and addressed her anxiety about seeing a psychiatrist.","Took a phase-by-phase account: aura, ictal features and post-ictal state.","Obtained the witness account and asked about pattern and triggers.","Covered febrile convulsions, head injury, infection and family history.","Considered differentials and screened mood and impact.","Explained likely temporal lobe epilepsy, the tests and safety advice."],"pitfalls":["Labelling it depersonalisation disorder or anxiety without asking about automatisms and confusion.","Forgetting to ask about driving and give DVLA advice.","Promising a normal EEG will settle the question.","Using jargon such as 'complex partial' or 'ictal' without explaining.","Ignoring her fear of a brain tumour or of 'going mad'."],"refs":["1. NICE NG217 (epilepsies in children, young people and adults, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, neurological disorders (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: valproate use in patients under 55 (2024; checked Nov 2026)","4. Epilepsy Action: information for people with focal seizures (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK doctors must advise patients with suspected seizures to stop driving and inform the DVLA, and document it. NICE NG217 expects suspected epilepsy to be seen by a specialist promptly, so refer the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-15","n":1415,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👁️","title":"Sudden loss of vision in one eye: history and fundoscopy","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:40, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical ward, general hospital, side room","text":"Leila Haddad, 28, was admitted two days ago after waking unable to see out of her left eye. The physicians have found nothing so far and think the cause may be psychological, so they have referred her to liaison psychiatry. Neurology and ophthalmology referrals have gone in, but no specialist has seen her yet, and the notes do not say whether anyone has looked at the back of her eyes."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of Leila's visual loss, including mood and risk, check acuity and pupils, carry out fundoscopy, and tell her and the medical team what you found.","first60":["Read the medical clerking: observations, bloods, any CT, and exactly what the referral asks.","Find an ophthalmoscope that works, a Snellen chart or near-vision card, and a pen torch; check the batteries.","Ask the nurse for a room you can dim, and a chaperone.","Check whether ophthalmology have a time to see her today; if not, plan to chase them after you finish.","Keep an open mind: 'probably psychological' is a hypothesis, not a finding."],"ask":["'Can you tell me exactly what happened when you woke up?'","'Is it completely dark in that eye, or blurry, patchy or like a curtain?'","'Does it hurt when you move your eye? Any flashes or floaters?'","'Any headache, weakness, numbness, tingling or problems speaking?'","'Has anything like this, or any odd numbness or blurred vision, happened before?'","'Do you wear contact lenses? Any diabetes, high blood pressure or medicines?'","'What's been happening in your life over the last few weeks?'","'And your mood? Any moments of feeling life isn't worth it?'"],"recognise":["Sudden painless monocular loss in a young woman: think optic neuritis, retinal detachment, retinal vessel occlusion and functional visual loss.","Pain on eye movement, colour desaturation and past tingling or numbness point to optic neuritis and possible MS.","No perception of light in one eye with normal, equal pupil reactions and no RAPD is very hard to explain by an optic nerve or retinal lesion: a positive sign of functional loss.","A normal disc does not exclude optic neuritis: in most cases the inflammation sits behind the eye.","A recent major stress, like losing her job, makes a functional disorder more likely but never proves it.","Low mood and passive death wishes after a loss need a brief risk screen, whatever the eye diagnosis."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused history including red flags and mood, check acuity, pupils and colour, perform fundoscopy, document normal findings precisely, and chase same-day ophthalmology without labelling it psychological.","r":"Right: you add hard data that helps the eye team, keep her safe, and avoid an early label that could miss optic neuritis or a vascular event.","best":true},{"c":"Confirm the medics' view that it is conversion disorder because her life is stressful.","r":"Wrong: stress is not a diagnosis. Functional loss rests on positive signs and specialist assessment, and organic causes have not been excluded.","best":false},{"c":"Instil dilating drops to get a better view.","r":"Not on your own: drops can mask pupil signs the neurologists need and carry a small risk of angle closure. Agree it with the eye team first.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity to consent to examination; explain each step, gain consent and offer a chaperone for close-up eye examination.","Share information with her husband only with her consent.","DVLA: advise no driving until the eye team confirm she meets the visual standard; she must tell the DVLA if the loss persists.","GMC: avoid a premature 'psychological' label in the notes; record findings and uncertainty precisely."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Leila, the back of your left eye looks healthy, and the way your pupils react is normal. That's reassuring. The eye specialists still need to see you today before anyone puts a name to this."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Losing your job and then your sight in the same week is a lot to carry. Whatever the eye team find, I'll come back to see how you're coping."},{"to":"Medical team","s":"Leila Haddad: left eye no perception of light since waking two days ago, painless, no other neurology. Pupils equal and reactive, no RAPD, normal red reflexes, discs, vessels and maculae both eyes. Low mood after job loss, passive thoughts, no plans. Please chase same-day ophthalmology."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry review. Leila Haddad, 28F, medical ward D2. Chaperone: SN Grace.\nHx: woke Monday with left eye 'completely dark'; no pain, pain on eye movement, flashes, floaters or curtain. No headache, weakness, numbness, speech change. No past visual or sensory episodes. No contact lenses, diabetes or hypertension; no regular meds. LMP 2 weeks ago.\nContext: lost job (office administrator) last week; low mood, poor sleep 1 week; fleeting thoughts she'd rather not wake up, no plans or intent; protective: husband, faith. No previous psychiatric history.\nO/E: VA R 6/6, L no perception of light. Pupils equal, direct and consensual reflexes normal, no RAPD on swinging light test. Ishihara R 17/17, L unable. Fundoscopy (undilated, dim room): red reflex present both eyes; discs pink, sharp margins, normal cup; vessels normal in all quadrants; maculae normal.\nImp: unexplained left monocular visual loss; normal pupils and fundi are positive features suggesting a functional cause, but organic causes not yet excluded (optic neuritis remains possible).\nPlan: same-day ophthalmology chased (bleep 4471, will see at 14:00); neurology to consider MRI brain and orbits. Advised not to drive. Mood follow-up by liaison tomorrow. Safety-netted: report pain, headache or any change in right eye. Medical team updated.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Get the onset in her words, then ask the red flags: pain on eye movement, flashes, floaters, curtain, headache, other neurology.","Check acuity in each eye and the pupils before the ophthalmoscope: the swinging light test is your most useful bedside sign.","Do fundoscopy in a set order: red reflex, disc, vessels in four quadrants, macula last.","Describe what you're doing as you go, in plain words.","Screen mood and risk briefly; don't make 'psychological' the headline."],"next":"Ophthalmology see Leila that afternoon: normal slit-lamp examination, visual fields and OCT. Neurology arrange an MRI of the brain and orbits, which is normal. The neurologist and the liaison team explain functional visual loss together. Her vision returns over the next fortnight, and she starts therapy for functional symptoms and help with job seeking.","marks":["Took a clear history of onset, nature and progression of the visual loss.","Asked red flags: pain on eye movement, flashes, floaters, headache, neurological symptoms.","Explored stresses, mood and suicidal thoughts sensitively.","Checked acuity, pupils with swinging light test and colour vision.","Performed fundoscopy correctly: red reflex, disc, vessels, macula, both eyes.","Explained findings without a premature label and arranged same-day ophthalmology."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'it's psychological' and skipping the red flags.","Going straight to the ophthalmoscope without checking acuity and pupils.","Using your right eye for her left eye, so you end up nose to nose.","Telling her 'there's nothing wrong with your eye'.","Forgetting a mood and suicide screen after a major loss."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Ophthalmologists: urgent eye referral guidance (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CKS: optic neuritis; and NICE NG127 (suspected neurological conditions, 2019; checked Nov 2026)","3. neurosymptoms.org: functional visual symptoms patient information (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, visual disorders (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK liaison work you are often the first to examine properly. Normal fundoscopy and pupils are useful findings for the eye team, but the diagnosis of functional visual loss belongs to neurology and ophthalmology.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-22","n":1422,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Memory clinic: a ten-minute cognitive check across every domain","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00, memory clinic","where":"Clinic room 3, memory assessment service","text":"Mr Kenneth Lau, 70, a retired restaurant owner, has been referred by his GP. For six months he has been forgetting friends' names and his grandchildren's birthdays, and he finds it embarrassing. His wife came with him and is in the waiting room. The nurse has already taken his history in detail; your job is a structured cognitive check before the team meets."},"task":"Explain the purpose, then in about ten minutes test each main cognitive domain with targeted items, record the results, and answer his questions honestly.","first60":["Read the referral and the nurse's history: onset, course, medicines, alcohol, mood score.","Set out a pen, blank paper, a printed clock circle and a watch with a second hand; turn your screen away.","Quiet room, phone on silent, wife waiting outside: one task each, with no prompting from the side.","Plan one or two items per domain, not a full ACE-III: orientation, attention, registration, language, visuospatial, fluency, then delayed recall.","Pick three recall words now and write them down so you don't vary them."],"ask":["'What have you noticed about your memory, and when did it start?'","'What about things from long ago, like your wedding or opening the restaurant?'","'How's your mood been? Are you worrying more than usual?'","'How much do you drink in a typical week?'","'Is English the language you use every day? How old were you when you left school?'","'Do you have your glasses and hearing aid with you?'","'How do you think you've done?' (after testing, before you give feedback)"],"recognise":["Six months of forgetting names and dates with clear long-ago memories fits a problem with new learning: the hippocampal pattern seen early in Alzheimer's disease.","Not knowing the building and zero delayed recall are the two most telling results here; writing difficulty adds a language or executive element.","Getting flustered under pressure can lower scores: anxiety and low mood can mimic dementia, so screen mood before you interpret.","Hearing loss, poor eyesight, limited schooling or testing outside a first language can all depress scores: check and record them.","A single screen is not a diagnosis: NICE expects bloods, an informant history and usually imaging before any label."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain, consent and check senses, then test one or two items per domain in ten minutes, record raw results, and give honest feedback with clear next steps.","r":"Right: covers every domain in the time a clinic allows, the results are interpretable, and he leaves knowing what happens next.","best":true},{"c":"Start straight into the questions to save time, and explain at the end.","r":"Wrong: testing without consent is disrespectful and leaves him anxious, which lowers his scores and his trust.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him 'You did really well, nothing to worry about' when he asks.","r":"Wrong: false reassurance. Be kind and honest: some parts were harder, and the next tests will explain why.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: cognitive testing needs his agreement; he has capacity to give it unless there is clear reason to doubt.","Speaking to his wife needs his permission. Ask him, and record his answer.","No diagnosis today means no DVLA duty yet; if dementia is later diagnosed he must tell the DVLA and his insurer.","Results belong to him: he decides who hears them and whether his wife joins the feedback appointment."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like to do some short memory and thinking tasks, about ten minutes. Some will feel easy and some harder; that's how they're designed. Is that all right?"},{"to":"Patient","s":"Some parts were harder for you, especially remembering the words and knowing where we are. That doesn't give us an answer by itself, so the next step is to find out why."},{"to":"Memory clinic team","s":"Mr Lau: disoriented to place, 0 of 3 on delayed recall even with cues, effortful writing; attention, clock and naming intact. Amnestic pattern. Needs bloods, MRI, wife's account and a mood screen."}],"write":"Memory clinic. 70M, retired restaurant owner. 6 months forgetting names and grandchildren's birthdays; long-ago memories intact. Mood: 'a bit fed up about it', no biological symptoms. Alcohol: 2 glasses wine/week. English daily since childhood; left school at 16. Glasses worn, hearing adequate.\nConsent: verbal, given.\nOrientation: time 5/5 (day, date, month, year, season); place: could not name building or floor.\nAttention: serial 7s 4/5 (one slip).\nRegistration: 3/3 (river, candle, garden), two trials.\nLanguage: named watch, pen, buckle; repeated 'no ifs, ands or buts'; followed 3-stage command; two sentences written slowly with one omitted word, flustered.\nVisuospatial: clock: numbers correctly placed, hands at ten past eleven correct.\nExecutive: F-words 8 in 60 s (no rule breaks).\nDelayed recall: 0/3 free; 1/3 with category cue.\nImpression: amnestic deficit with mild writing difficulty; attention and visuospatial preserved. Ddx: early Alzheimer's disease, vascular, mixed, depression-related.\nPlan: FBC, ESR or CRP, U&E, calcium, HbA1c, LFT, TFT, B12, folate; MRI brain; informant history from wife (consent given); GDS-15; results appointment with wife in 4-6 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Give the purpose and permission in two sentences, then check glasses and hearing before the first question.","Say 'some will feel hard' before you start: it protects his dignity when he struggles.","Use a fixed order so delayed recall comes after real distraction (language, clock, fluency).","Write raw answers, not just scores: 'could not name building' is more useful than '4/5'.","Stop prompting when he gets flustered: reassure once, move on, note it.","When he asks how he did, ask what he thinks first, then be honest and kind."],"next":"Bloods are normal apart from a borderline B12, which the GP replaces. MRI shows mild bilateral hippocampal atrophy. His wife describes repeated questions and a missed bill. At the results appointment, both together, he is told he has mild Alzheimer's disease and agrees to start donepezil.","marks":["Explained the purpose, gained consent and checked senses, language and schooling.","Took a brief history: onset, long-term memory, mood and alcohol.","Tested orientation, attention, registration and delayed recall.","Tested language, including writing, naming, repetition and a command.","Tested visuospatial and executive function: clock and letter fluency.","Gave honest, kind feedback with clear next steps and no premature diagnosis."],"pitfalls":["Starting tests with no explanation or consent.","Running a full ACE-III and running out of time for feedback.","Testing delayed recall straight after registration, with no distraction in between.","Not checking hearing, glasses, first language or schooling.","Saying 'it's probably Alzheimer's' or 'you did fine' at the end."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97: dementia assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. Addenbrooke's Cognitive Examination III and Mini-ACE: administration guides (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, dementia section (checked Nov 2026)","4. Alzheimer's Society: memory assessment information for patients (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK memory clinics expect you to name the domain you are testing and why. Say 'Now some questions on language' before each section; it keeps the patient oriented and shows you have a structure.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-23","n":1423,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Paracetamol overdose at 16: a ten-minute risk assessment in A&E","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 22:40, out of hours","where":"Paediatric side room, A&E","text":"Amira Hussain, 16, a Year 11 pupil, came in by ambulance with her parents after deliberately taking paracetamol this evening. Her 4-hour level is due at 23:00 and the paediatric team plan to keep her in overnight. She has already spoken to two doctors and a nurse and finds the department overwhelming. You are the liaison psychiatry doctor on call."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of the overdose and what led to it, assess her mood and current suicide risk, and agree tonight's plan with her.","first60":["Read the A&E notes: time and number of tablets, the 4-hour level time, obs, and what she has already told others.","Ask the nurse for a quiet side room and whether her parents are with her; plan to see her alone first, then with them.","Check the safeguarding flag on the record and whether there is a social worker or previous CAMHS contact.","Hold the order: before, during and after the overdose, the build-up, mood, self-harm and current risk, protective factors, plan.","Know who to tell tonight: paediatric registrar, on-call CAMHS consultant if needed, and the morning CAMHS liaison team."],"ask":["'What was happening just before you took the tablets?'","'Was it planned, or a spur-of-the-moment thing?'","'What did you want to happen when you took them?'","'Did you think that number could kill you?'","'What did you do after you'd taken them?'","'How do you feel now about still being here?'","'Have you ever hurt yourself in any other way?'","'Is anyone, at home, at school or online, hurting you or making you do things you don't want?'"],"recognise":["Intent to die, a belief that the dose would be fatal, going to bed hoping not to wake, and being alone all mark high intent, even though the act was unplanned.","Telling her parents when she felt sick and regretting it now are reassuring, but regret about upsetting others is not the same as no wish to die.","Three months of low mood, poor sleep, appetite and concentration, lost enjoyment and hopelessness: a likely moderate depressive episode.","Scratching her forearm on bad days is ongoing self-harm and a separate risk marker: ask, look, and treat the wounds.","The trigger (online bullying) is still active tonight: her phone is in her hand.","Risk is formulated, not scored: NICE advises against relying on risk scales or tools to forecast suicide or choose care."],"decide":[{"c":"See her alone with clear confidentiality limits, cover before, during and after, mood, self-harm and current risk, then agree a plan she and her parents understand, with overnight paediatric stay and CAMHS review.","r":"Right: you get the high-intent details she might hide with parents present, and the plan keeps her body and mind safe tonight.","best":true},{"c":"Interview her with her parents present the whole time to save repeating it.","r":"Wrong: she may not disclose intent, self-harm or abuse in front of them. See her alone first, then together.","best":false},{"c":"She regrets it and has no plan, so discharge home with a CAMHS letter once the level is back.","r":"Wrong: high intent, ongoing thoughts and active depression. She needs the medical treatment completed and a full CAMHS psychosocial assessment before discharge.","best":false}],"law":["At 16 she is presumed to have capacity under the MCA 2005 to consent to treatment; check it if she refuses acetylcysteine.","Confidentiality is hers, but limited: share with parents and services if there is a risk of serious harm, and tell her first.","If she lacks capacity and refuses urgent treatment, discuss with senior paediatric and psychiatric colleagues before using parental consent or the MCA; get legal advice early.","Safeguarding: online abuse and self-harm in a child need a check with the safeguarding team; refer to children's social care if harm at home is suspected."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Anything you say stays within the team caring for you. The only time I'd share more is if I'm worried you're not safe, and I'd tell you first."},{"to":"Parents","s":"Amira has spoken really openly. Tonight she stays on the children's ward for her treatment, and the CAMHS team will see her tomorrow. Please keep all medicines locked away at home from now on."},{"to":"Paediatric registrar","s":"High-intent overdose, now regretful, with three months of depression and arm scratching. No current plan but fleeting thoughts. Please keep her on the ward; general observations; CAMHS liaison will review tomorrow before any discharge."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E, 22:40. 16F, Year 11, lives with parents. Seen alone, then with parents (with her agreement).\nOverdose: 16 x paracetamol 500 mg at 19:00, bedroom, from kitchen first-aid box. Unplanned, after hurtful posts by girls from school. Intent to die; believed it would be fatal. Alone, no note. Went to bed hoping not to wake; woke ~21:00 nauseous, told parents. No alcohol or drugs.\nNow: regrets, mainly for parents; no current plan; fleeting thoughts of wishing you weren't here.\nBackground: online bullying 3 months. Low mood, initial insomnia, poor appetite and concentration, lost enjoyment (piano, sketching), hopeless about bullying stopping. Scratches L forearm with a pen on bad days for 1 month; superficial. No previous attempts. No psychotic symptoms. No abuse disclosed. No previous CAMHS contact.\nProtective: close to parents, wants to finish GCSEs, sought help herself, accepts support.\nFormulation: high-intent impulsive overdose in the context of a probable moderate depressive episode triggered by ongoing bullying; current risk raised but reduced by regret, help-seeking and supportive parents.\nPlan: complete medical treatment per paediatrics; admit to paediatric ward, general obs; CAMHS liaison psychosocial assessment tomorrow before discharge; parents to lock away medicines and sharps; school DSL to be contacted with consent; safeguarding team informed; arm dressed.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Name her tiredness of repeating herself in the first sentence; it earns you the rest.","Ask about intent with plain words: 'Did you want to die?' She will answer if you ask without flinching.","Go through before, during and after in time order; it is quicker than jumping about.","Ask about self-harm separately from the overdose; the arm scratching only comes out if you ask.","Ask what would help her stay safe tonight and tomorrow, then build the plan on her answer.","Close with what will happen in the next 24 hours, in that order."],"next":"Her level is above the treatment line and she completes acetylcysteine on the children's ward. CAMHS see her and her parents the next day. The school's safeguarding lead acts on the bullying, and she starts weekly CBT with CAMHS. Her parents buy a lockable medicine box.","marks":["Explained confidentiality and its limits and saw her alone.","Covered before, during and after the overdose, including intent and lethality belief.","Elicited the depressive symptoms and hopelessness.","Asked about other self-harm and found the arm scratching.","Assessed current thoughts, plans, access to means and protective factors.","Agreed a safe plan: medical treatment, overnight stay, CAMHS review, parents involved."],"pitfalls":["Seeing her only with her parents present.","Taking 'I regret it' as proof the risk is now low.","Missing the arm scratching because you never asked.","Using a risk score in place of a formulation.","Clearing her for discharge before the medical treatment and CAMHS review are complete."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225: self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG134: depression in children and young people (2019; checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA: intravenous acetylcysteine for paracetamol overdose, treatment line guidance (2012; checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC: 0-18 years guidance for all doctors (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, a 16-year-old is presumed to have capacity, and confidentiality is hers. Parents are usually involved with her agreement. Tell her clearly what you will share and why before you speak to them.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-24","n":1424,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"🦴","title":"Refusing hip surgery: a capacity assessment with a man with a learning disability","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Orthopaedic ward, general hospital","text":"Callum Ashworth-Nye, 35, has a mild learning disability (IQ around 63) and talks well. Yesterday he came off his bike and broke his right hip. The orthopaedic registrar wants to operate tonight: without surgery the bone may not join and its blood supply may fail. Callum has said no. His pain is now controlled, he is calmer and he has agreed to see you. He lives with his housebound elderly mother."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess whether Callum has capacity to refuse the hip operation, then explain the result to him in plain words.","first60":["Get the facts from the surgeon first: what the operation is, its main risks, what happens without it, and how long it can wait.","Ask the ward for easy-read pictures of the hip and the operation, and whether the LD liaison nurse is on site.","Check his pain score and last analgesia: assess when he is most able, not mid-wince or drowsy.","Ask the nurse what Callum has said about his mother and who is caring for her today.","Have the hospital capacity form ready: decision, information given, each functional test, the causal link, the outcome."],"ask":["'What have the bone doctors told you about your hip?'","'What would the operation do?'","'What might happen if you don't have it?'","'What makes you say no? What would make it easier to say yes?'","'Who's looking after your mum while you're here?'","'Can you tell me again what the doctors said happens without the operation?'","'Has anyone told you what you should decide?'"],"recognise":["Capacity is decision-specific and presumed: the question is only whether he can make this decision, now.","Telling the facts back straight after a picture shows understanding; losing them a few minutes later, even with the same picture, is a failure to retain.","Saying 'God will fix it, so nothing bad can happen' while the pain is eased by painkillers shows he cannot use the risks in his decision: a failure to weigh.","Religious belief by itself is not incapacity: many people with capacity refuse treatment for faith reasons. The failure here is not being able to hold the risks at all.","Worry about his mother is pressure, not incapacity: remove it by arranging her care, then reassess.","His learning disability is the impairment; the record must show how it causes the failure to retain and weigh."],"decide":[{"c":"Take all practicable steps (pictures, plain words, mum's care sorted), test each part, record that he cannot retain or weigh because of his learning disability, then explain kindly and pass to a best-interests decision led by the surgeon.","r":"Right: lawful, fair to him, and it gets an urgent decision made with his wishes, faith and mother's views on the table.","best":true},{"c":"He has a learning disability and is refusing a sensible operation, so he lacks capacity.","r":"Wrong: a diagnosis and an unwise choice never prove incapacity. You must show which part of the functional test he fails and why.","best":false},{"c":"He prays and his pain is better, so record a capacitous faith-based refusal and let him go home.","r":"Wrong: he cannot keep or use the key risks even with help, and believes nothing bad can happen. That is a failure to weigh, not a reasoned faith choice.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005 s1: presume capacity, take all practicable steps to help him decide, and don't treat an unwise decision as incapacity.","s3: he must understand, retain (even briefly), use or weigh, and communicate. Then show the impairment causes the failure (Supreme Court, A Local Authority v JB, 2021).","s4: the surgeon, as decision-maker, decides in his best interests, considering his wishes, beliefs and his mother's views. An IMCA is needed only if no one appropriate can be consulted.","s5-6: restraint only if necessary and proportionate; if he actively resists, get legal advice and consider the Court of Protection.","The Mental Health Act does not apply: this is treatment for a physical condition."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Callum, you told me what the operation does, and that's great. But a few minutes later it went out of your head, and you said nothing bad can happen. That tells me this decision is too hard to make on your own today."},{"to":"Orthopaedic registrar","s":"He lacks capacity for this decision: with pictures he can't retain the risks for more than a few minutes and can't weigh them, because of his learning disability. The best-interests decision is yours; please include his mother by phone and his faith."},{"to":"Ward social worker","s":"His mother is housebound and alone. Can we arrange emergency support for her today? Worry about her is weighing on his decisions."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry: capacity assessment. 35M, mild learning disability (IQ ~63), verbal. R NOF fracture after cycling accident. Decision: refusal of urgent surgical fixation of right hip.\nConditions: pain controlled (score 2/10), alert, quiet room, plain language, easy-read pictures, assessed at 15:30.\nInformation given: bone at top of thigh broken; operation joins it with metal; risks: pain, bleeding, infection, anaesthetic; without surgery: bone may not join, blood supply may fail, lasting pain and difficulty walking.\nUnderstand: repeated key facts with pictures immediately.\nRetain: 5 min later could not recall consequences of no surgery despite same pictures.\nUse/weigh: 'God will fix it, nothing bad can happen'; uses lower pain (on analgesia) as proof of healing; cannot consider risks.\nCommunicate: clear, consistent refusal.\nPressure: worried about housebound mother; social worker arranging her care.\nCausal link: inability to retain and weigh is due to his learning disability, not his faith alone.\nOutcome: lacks capacity for this decision at this time. Explained to him in plain words.\nPlan: best-interests decision by orthopaedic consultant today, consulting mother by phone; LD liaison nurse; chaplain offered; reassess if circumstances change.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Ask what he already knows before you tell him anything: it shows you where the gaps are.","Use one picture and short words; ask him to teach it back.","Come back to the key fact a few minutes later: that is your retention test.","Separate faith from failure to weigh: ask how he balances the risks, not whether he believes.","Take away pressure you can fix (his mum) before you decide.","Tell him the outcome plainly and say what he still gets a say in."],"next":"The orthopaedic consultant holds a best-interests discussion at 17:00 with Callum, his mother on the phone and the LD liaison nurse. The chaplain prays with him before theatre. A carer visits his mother that evening. He has the operation that night and is back on his feet with physiotherapy within two days.","marks":["Checked his understanding first, then gave the key information in plain words with a picture.","Tested understand, retain, use or weigh and communicate separately.","Separated faith from the failure to weigh.","Addressed his worry about his mother.","Linked the failed parts to his learning disability.","Explained the outcome kindly and named the best-interests process."],"pitfalls":["Deciding he lacks capacity because of the diagnosis or the unwise choice.","Calling a faith-based refusal incapacity without testing weighing.","Testing retention straight after explaining, then calling it retained.","Using medical words: 'avascular necrosis', 'non-union', 'fixation'.","Saying you, the psychiatrist, will decide on the operation."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005, ss1-6, and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52 (order of the capacity test)","3. NICE CG124: hip fracture management (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: reasonable adjustments for people with a learning disability (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England, the person proposing the treatment usually makes the best-interests decision. As the psychiatrist you assess capacity and advise; say so clearly to the surgeons and to the patient.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-25","n":1425,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🚪","title":"A man in A&E who won't say why: disclosure, risk to children and safeguarding","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 21:10, on call","where":"Mental health assessment room, A&E","text":"Stuart Pembridge, 30, a caretaker at a primary school, walked into A&E asking for a mental health professional. He would not tell the triage nurse why. He looks tense and keeps glancing at the door. He has never been in contact with psychiatric services. The nurse asks you to see him."},"task":"In about ten minutes, find out what is troubling him, assess the risk to children and to himself, and explain honestly what has to happen next.","first60":["Read the triage note and check the record: any previous contact, police alerts or safeguarding flags.","Use the assessment room with an alarm; tell the nurse in charge where you are and roughly how long.","Decide in advance what you will say about confidentiality, so you say it once, clearly, before he discloses.","Know your out-of-hours route: on-call consultant, police on 101 (999 if a child is in immediate danger), children's services emergency duty team.","Plan the order: why now, the nature of the urges, recent events, images, mental state, risk, safeguarding, close."],"ask":["'What made you come in tonight, rather than another night?'","'Who are these feelings directed towards: what ages, boys or girls?'","'How long have you been aware of them? Have you ever been attracted to adults?'","'Have you ever touched a child in a sexual way?'","'Do you look at images of children online, and do you keep any?'","'In your job or outside it, when are you alone with children?'","'Any thoughts of self-harm or of ending your life?'","'What has stopped you from acting on these feelings so far?'"],"recognise":["Lifelong sexual interest in pre-pubertal girls that distresses him and has been acted on (images) fits a paedophilic disorder; interest is not the same as a contact offence.","Arousal while alone babysitting an 8-year-old, with fantasies since, is an escalation: the child and others he has access to are at real risk.","Holding indecent images of children is a serious crime in its own right and fuels the abuse of other children.","Working at a primary school gives daily access to children: an allegation-management (LADO) matter.","Protective factors: he came forward, sees the urges as wrong, wants help, no alcohol or drugs.","After disclosure, and once police are involved, suicide risk rises sharply: assess it now and plan for it."],"decide":[{"c":"State confidentiality limits first, take a full history, assess suicide risk, then tell him honestly that police and children's services must be informed tonight, and offer support for himself.","r":"Right: protects children, is lawful under GMC guidance, keeps his trust as far as possible, and plans for his own safety.","best":true},{"c":"Promise complete confidentiality so he will open up, then decide what to share.","r":"Wrong: a promise you cannot keep. Explain the limits before he discloses; most people still talk.","best":false},{"c":"Since he did not touch the child, give him the Stop It Now number and discharge with a GP letter.","r":"Wrong: he holds indecent images and works with children. There is a duty to share with police and children's services, and he needs a suicide plan.","best":false}],"law":["GMC confidentiality guidance: you may share without consent where that protects others from serious harm, and to help prevent or detect a serious crime. Share only what is needed, with the right people.","Tell him before you share, unless that would put a child at greater risk or hinder the police; discuss with the on-call consultant if time allows.","Possessing indecent images of children is an offence (Protection of Children Act 1978 and Criminal Justice Act 1988).","An adult working with children who may pose a risk to them is referred to the Local Authority Designated Officer (LADO).","He is a voluntary patient and free to leave; the MHA does not apply unless a mental disorder needs detention in its own right."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you tell me is confidential, with one exception: if a child, or anyone, might be at risk of serious harm, I'd have to share it. I'd always tell you first."},{"to":"On-call consultant","s":"30-year-old school caretaker, self-presented: lifelong sexual interest in young girls, aroused while babysitting an 8-year-old last week, no touching, holds indecent images. No suicidal thoughts now. I intend to inform police and children's services tonight and the LADO tomorrow. Do you agree?"},{"to":"Police (101)","s":"I'm the psychiatry doctor in A&E. A man has disclosed holding indecent images of children and has access to children through work and babysitting. I'm sharing this to protect children; he knows I'm calling."}],"write":"On-call psychiatry, A&E, 21:10. 30M, primary school caretaker. Self-presented. No previous services contact. Seen alone, confidentiality and its limits explained before disclosure.\nHx: lifelong sexual interest in girls under 12; brief adult relationships ended through lack of interest. Last week babysat a friend's 8-year-old daughter alone for 4 hours; became aroused; states no touching; recurrent fantasies about her since. Holds indecent images of children downloaded online on a memory stick at home. Never arrested. Knows it is wrong; disgusted; wants help.\nMSE: anxious, tense, good eye contact once settled; mood 'anxious', poor sleep for fear of arrest; no suicidal thoughts, plans or past self-harm; no psychotic symptoms; no alcohol or drugs.\nRisk: to children: high (escalation, access through school and babysitting, images). To self: currently low but likely to rise after disclosure. Protective: self-referral, insight into wrongfulness, wants help.\nImpression: probable paedophilic disorder.\nActions: discussed with on-call consultant Dr X; police informed via 101 (ref ...); children's services emergency duty team informed; LADO referral next working day; trust safeguarding lead emailed. Patient told before each call. Agreed: no contact with children. Stop It Now and Samaritans numbers given. Safety plan agreed. GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Say the confidentiality limits once, before he discloses, in one sentence.","Thank him for coming: it is the strongest protective factor and keeps him talking.","Ask plain, specific questions about ages, touching and images: vague questions get vague answers.","Ask about access to children at work and outside it; both matter.","Assess suicide before you tell him about the police, and again after.","Be honest about what happens next; never imply you can keep it quiet."],"next":"Police attend A&E within the hour and arrange to collect the memory stick. Children's services contact the girl's parents. The LADO works with the school the next morning and he is suspended pending inquiries. He calls Stop It Now the next day, and his GP refers him to a specialist forensic service.","marks":["Explained confidentiality and its limits before disclosure.","Explored the nature, onset and focus of his sexual interest.","Established the babysitting event, the images and whether he has touched a child.","Identified access to children through work and outside it.","Assessed mood, suicide risk and substances, and named protective factors.","Explained honestly the duty to inform police, children's services and the LADO."],"pitfalls":["Promising total confidentiality.","Avoiding direct questions about touching and images out of embarrassment.","Forgetting his school job as a route of access to children.","Missing the suicide risk that follows disclosure.","Showing disgust or lecturing him; it shuts the conversation down."],"refs":["1. GMC: Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. Protection of Children Act 1978 and Criminal Justice Act 1988, s160","4. Stop It Now (Lucy Faithfull Foundation) helpline information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, you do not need the patient's consent to share information that protects a child, but you should usually tell them first and record exactly what you shared, with whom and why.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-26","n":1426,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💌","title":"A&E at night: he will only see Dr Fenwick","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 22:40, on call","where":"Emergency department, side room near the nurses' base","text":"Dominic Arrieta, 28, a professional online gamer, has waited in A&E for hours and is getting louder. He wants Dr Fenwick, a female A&E doctor who treated his asthma months ago, and he has come back with minor complaints several times since. Staff have refused to fetch her. The nurse in charge has asked you, the psychiatry doctor on call, to see him now."},"task":"In about ten minutes, settle him enough to talk, take a focused history that establishes the likely diagnosis, and grade the risk to Dr Fenwick, to staff and to himself, then hand over a safe plan.","first60":["Ask the nurse in charge: has he threatened anyone, does he have a weapon, is security on the floor, and is Dr Fenwick on shift tonight?","Choose a room with two exits or sit nearest the door; leave it ajar and tell a nurse you are going in.","Check the A&E record: how many attendances since the asthma visit, and who saw him each time.","Look for any prior police or mental health contact on the electronic record.","Decide now: you will not promise to fetch Dr Fenwick, and you will not argue with the belief."],"ask":["'Tell me about Dr Fenwick. How did you first meet?'","'What happened at that meeting that told you how she felt?'","'Nought to a hundred, how certain are you? Could anything else explain it?'","'She didn't accept your friend request. What did that mean to you?'","'Have you found out her address? Have you been there, written to her or followed her?'","'What were you planning to do if you saw her tonight?'","'If staff stop you seeing her, what would you do?'","'Have you ever felt this way about anyone else, and how did it end?'"],"recognise":["A fixed belief that a woman met once secretly loves him, built on tiny signals (a blink, raised eyebrows) and held for months: erotomania, a form of delusional disorder.","Contrary evidence is absorbed into the belief: an ignored friend request becomes 'she's keeping it secret'.","Repeat A&E visits to see her, online searching and a pre-booked trip for two are pursuit behaviours: risk is rising, not static.","A plan to take her away tonight plus stated willingness to use force, with mixed martial arts training, is a high, imminent risk to her and to anyone who stands in the way.","Normal mood, no hallucinations, no drugs and no other odd beliefs point away from mania, schizophrenia and intoxication, which you must still screen for.","Risk is highest at the point of rejection: the moment he is told she will not come."],"decide":[{"c":"Stay calm and seated, decline to fetch her, take the focused history and risk, then call the on-call consultant, keep security close, warn Dr Fenwick through her clinical lead and request a Mental Health Act assessment.","r":"Right: you get the facts that define the risk, protect the person he is fixed on and the staff, and use the right legal route.","best":true},{"c":"Page Dr Fenwick to come down for two minutes so he will calm down and go home.","r":"Dangerous: it feeds the delusion, puts her in front of a man who has talked about force, and teaches him that escalating works.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him firmly that she does not love him and that his belief is a delusion.","r":"Unhelpful at this point: arguing with a fixed belief raises agitation and closes the history. Acknowledge the feeling, not the content.","best":false}],"law":["GMC confidentiality: you may disclose without consent when it is needed to protect someone from serious harm. Warning Dr Fenwick, her clinical lead and security is justified here; record why.","Section 5(2) does not apply in A&E because he is not an inpatient. If he tries to leave intent on finding her, call the police.","Police can use Section 136 in A&E, which is not a private dwelling, and can act on any offence or threat of violence.","Request a Mental Health Act assessment (AMHP plus a Section 12 approved doctor) if he declines admission and the risk stays high.","Stalking Protection Orders are applied for by the police; Dr Fenwick can report the contact herself and should be supported to."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Dominic, I can see how much tonight matters to you. Dr Fenwick won't be coming down, and I won't pretend otherwise. I'd still like to understand this properly."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He believes Dr Fenwick secretly loves him and plans to take her abroad tomorrow. He says he'd use force if stopped and trains in martial arts. Keep security outside the room, no one alone with him, and call the police if he leaves."},{"to":"On-call consultant","s":"28-year-old man, likely erotomanic delusional disorder, months of pursuit of an A&E doctor, flights booked for tomorrow, will use force if stopped. I'd like a Mental Health Act assessment tonight and help warning her."}],"write":"Psychiatry on call, A&E, 22:40. Seen with nurse aware, door open, security outside.\n28M, professional online gamer. Demanding to see Dr F (A&E doctor, met once months ago for asthma).\nBelief: Dr F loves him; evidence = blinked twice and raised eyebrows; ignored friend request reinterpreted as secrecy. Conviction 100%, >3 months. No other delusions, no hallucinations.\nPursuit: multiple A&E attendances with minor complaints to see her; social media search; two flights to Venice tomorrow; intends to take her tonight. Home address: [state if known].\nMSE: agitated, irritable, rapid speech, no thought disorder, mood 'good', no biological change. No alcohol excess, no drugs. No self-harm or suicidal thoughts.\nRisk to others: HIGH, imminent. Would use force if stopped; MMA trained; no known weapon.\nForensic: no convictions, no police contact. No past psychiatric history.\nImpression: erotomania (delusional disorder). Ddx: mania, schizophrenia, substance-induced.\nPlan: not to see Dr F. Security present; no lone working. Discussed with on-call consultant. MHA assessment requested. Police if he leaves. Dr F, her clinical lead and trust security informed (public-interest disclosure, rationale recorded). Records flagged. Datix completed.\nDr Chinonso, psychiatry on call","make":["Name his frustration in the first breath; it buys you the next ten minutes.","Never offer or hint that she might come.","Ask what the signs were and what he plans next: the plan is the risk.","Ask the force question directly and calmly; he will often answer it.","Act before you leave the room: tell the nurse in charge and security what you heard.","Warn the person he is fixed on through her lead, and write down why."],"next":"Dominic stays in the side room with security outside. The consultant agrees, and an AMHP and a Section 12 doctor assess him at 01:30; he is detained under Section 2. Dr Fenwick's clinical lead tells her at home, trust security changes her rota, and the police open a stalking report.","marks":["De-escalated by naming the wait and the anger, and did not promise to fetch her.","Established the belief, its evidence, its fixity and its duration.","Asked about each pursuit behaviour, including the Venice plan.","Asked directly about force, weapons and martial arts, and screened self-harm.","Screened mood, psychosis and substances to support the diagnosis.","Named erotomania and acted: consultant, security, warning, Mental Health Act assessment."],"pitfalls":["Offering to see whether Dr Fenwick is free.","Challenging the belief head on in the first minute.","Forgetting to ask what he would do if stopped tonight.","Seeing him alone in a closed room with no one told.","Leaving without warning the person at risk or recording why you disclosed."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-11: delusional disorder 6A24 (checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC: Confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHA Code of Practice, ch.s on Sections 5 and 136 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG10: violence and aggression, short-term management (checked Nov 2026)","5. Stalking Protection Act 2019 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK you are expected to warn an identifiable person at serious risk, even without the patient's consent. Do it through her clinical lead or security, and write down exactly why you shared.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-27","n":1427,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🔥","title":"Burnt hands after a bin fire: why did she set it?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 19:15, liaison psychiatry","where":"Emergency department, minors cubicle","text":"Rhiannon Achebe, 20, a second-year university student, has minor burns to both hands. She told the triage nurse she got them lighting a fire in a recycling bin outside her student house, but would not say why. She has agreed to see the liaison team. She is not known to mental health services. Her hands have been dressed."},"task":"In about ten minutes, find out what happened and why, map the fire-setting before, during and after, rule out other causes, reach the likely diagnosis and grade the risk of further fires.","first60":["Read the triage note and the burns assessment so you can answer 'are my hands okay?' straight away.","Check whether the fire service attended and whether police have been involved yet.","Look for any previous attendances, self-harm or alcohol-related visits on the record.","Find a cubicle with the curtain closed and no housemates nearby: she is frightened of being reported.","Have one honest sentence ready on confidentiality and its limits."],"ask":["'What was going on for you in the hours before the fire?'","'How did you feel just before you lit it? And while it burned?'","'What did you do once it was alight? How did you feel watching the fire engines?'","'Have you set fires before? When did fire first interest you?'","'Do you carry a lighter or matches, even though you don't smoke?'","'Was it meant to hurt anyone, get back at anyone, or hurt yourself?'","'Any voices, very high moods, or drinking or drugs around the time?'","'If the urge came back tonight, what would you do?'"],"recognise":["Rising tension, then relief or pleasure while setting and watching the fire, with no gain or grudge behind it: the pattern of pyromania.","A lifelong fascination with fire, a lighter carried as a comfort, and several small past fires, each at a time of stress, make this a recurring pattern, not a one-off.","Excitement at the fire engines and the firefighting is typical of pyromania and is worth asking about gently.","Seeing her ex with someone new is a trigger, but the fire was not aimed at him: revenge fire-setting targets a person or their property.","Half a bottle of wine lowered her control tonight, but past fires happened sober, so intoxication does not explain the pattern.","She shut herself inside while the fire spread near a shared house: the risk to housemates is real and must be named."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain confidentiality and its limits, take the before-during-after history, check past fires and other causes, then agree a plan: lighter handed over, alcohol limited, forensic psychology referral, GP letter and a crisis route.","r":"Right: you find the meaning of the fire, the pattern and the risk, and leave her with a concrete plan that reduces the next fire.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her you have to inform the police because arson is a crime.","r":"Not automatically. No one was hurt and the fire is out. Disclosure without consent is for preventing serious harm; discuss ongoing risk with your consultant before sharing.","best":false},{"c":"Conclude it was a reaction to seeing her ex while drunk and discharge her.","r":"Misses the lifelong pattern, the relief, the lighter she carries and the risk to the people she lives with.","best":false}],"law":["Setting fire to property is arson, a criminal offence; the fire service may investigate. Be honest about that, and suggest she gets legal advice if contacted.","GMC confidentiality: you need not report a past crime. Disclosure without consent is justified to prevent serious harm to others; if risk of further fires near people is high, discuss with your consultant and record your reasoning.","She has capacity and is an informal patient; she can decline referral, but give her the safety plan in writing.","Sharing with her university needs her consent unless serious risk overrides it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you tell me stays within the health team. The only time I'd share is if someone were at serious risk, and I'd tell you first."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you describe, the tension building and then relief when you light a fire, has a name: pyromania. It's uncommon, and there's psychological treatment that helps."},{"to":"A&E doctor","s":"We've seen Ms Achebe. She has a long pattern of tension-relief fire-setting, likely pyromania, with no psychosis or mood disorder and no self-harm intent. She's handed over her lighter, has a safety plan, and we're referring to forensic psychology via her GP. Fine to discharge once burns are dressed."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E, 19:15. 20F, 2nd-year maths student, lives in shared student house. Minor burns both hands (dressed).\nToday: saw ex-partner kissing someone new; drank ~half bottle wine (unusual); overwhelming urge; set fire in recycling bin outside house; burnt hands trying to put it out; went inside, watched fire service attend.\nBefore: rising tension (coursework, father's cancer). During: relief. After: excitement watching fire engines, then fear and guilt.\nPast: fascinated by fire since childhood; carries lighter (non-smoker) for stress relief; several small previous fires, all at times of stress. No previous police contact.\nMeaning: tension relief. Not revenge, not self-harm, no gain.\nMSE: anxious, guarded then open, no psychotic features, mood 'stressed' not depressed, no manic features. Alcohol: social only. No drugs. No learning difficulty evident.\nRisk: further fire-setting moderate (pattern, stress ongoing); risk to housemates noted. No suicidal ideation or self-harm. Aware of consequences, remorseful.\nImpression: likely pyromania. Ddx: emotionally unstable personality traits, alcohol-related disinhibition.\nPlan: lighter handed in; no alcohol for now; safety plan given (NHS 111 option 2); GP letter requesting forensic psychology referral; university wellbeing with consent; GP review 1 week. Discussed with consultant: no disclosure to police at present.\nDr Chinonso, liaison psychiatry","make":["Answer her worry about her hands first; she will not talk until you do.","Give the confidentiality line before she discloses, not after.","Ask about feelings before, during and after the fire: that sequence is the diagnosis.","Ask about the fire engines and firefighting: excitement there is a key sign.","Ask what she carries in her pocket.","End with one concrete safety action she agrees to, such as handing over the lighter."],"next":"Rhiannon hands over the lighter before leaving and agrees to go back to her parents' house for the weekend. Her GP refers her to a forensic psychology service, and she starts weekly CBT on fire-setting six weeks later. The fire service close their enquiry with words of advice.","marks":["Dealt with her hands first and explained confidentiality and its limits.","Mapped tension before, relief during, and excitement then fear after.","Took a history of past fires, early interest and the lighter she carries.","Excluded revenge, self-harm, psychosis, mania, depression, intoxication and learning difficulty.","Named the risk of further fires and to housemates, and checked suicide and self-harm.","Explained likely pyromania and agreed a named safety plan and referral."],"pitfalls":["Promising total confidentiality, then having to break it.","Treating it as a drunken one-off and missing the lifelong pattern.","Never asking how she felt watching the fire service.","Forgetting the people asleep in the shared house.","Threatening police involvement and losing her trust."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-11: pyromania 6C70 (checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC: Confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG225: self-harm assessment and management (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: urgent mental health help, NHS 111 option 2 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK a doctor has no general duty to report a patient's past crime to the police. You share only to prevent serious harm, after discussion with a senior, and you tell the patient where you can.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-28","n":1428,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Bank holiday, no methadone: history and a safe plan","src":"","moment":{"when":"Bank holiday Monday 15:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Emergency department, majors cubicle","text":"Lee Gartside, 39, unemployed and living alone in a rented flat, has come to A&E saying he will not leave until he gets methadone. His pharmacy was shut for the bank holiday. He is restless, sweating and irritable, and the nurses have asked the liaison team to see him."},"task":"In about ten minutes, explore his concerns, take a full substance use history including his methadone details, gauge his withdrawal, and agree a safe plan for today that does not rely on his word alone for the dose.","first60":["Ask the nurse for his observations: pulse, blood pressure, temperature, pupils, and whether he has been yawning, sweating or vomiting.","Check the electronic record and NHS Summary Care Record for any prescription, previous attendances or overdoses.","Find out which local pharmacies are open today and whether the drug service has an out-of-hours number.","Have a COWS (Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale) chart ready.","Decide in advance: no methadone until the dose is confirmed or a senior agrees a small observed dose."],"ask":["'When did you last take your methadone, and what dose?'","'Who prescribes it, and which pharmacy do you collect from? Do they watch you take it?'","'How many doses have you missed?'","'Have you used any heroin or other opioids since your last dose?'","'How did your heroin use start, and how did you take it at its worst?'","'Have you ever shared needles? When were you last tested for hepatitis and HIV?'","'Have you ever overdosed, by accident or on purpose?'","'What do you think you'll do if you don't get methadone today?'"],"recognise":["Runny nose, sweating, cramps, nausea and restlessness over 24 hours after his last methadone: genuine opioid withdrawal. Methadone withdrawal starts later and lasts longer than heroin withdrawal.","Opioid withdrawal is miserable but rarely dangerous in itself; overdose is what kills.","Giving methadone on his word alone risks a fatal overdose if the real dose is lower or he has used on top.","His fear that he will buy heroin today is the main risk: tolerance may have dropped, and street heroin strength is unpredictable.","Two years stable on methadone, no heroin and no overdoses is real progress worth naming.","Past injecting and needle sharing mean blood-borne virus status and hepatitis B vaccination need checking."],"decide":[{"c":"Take the full history, score withdrawal, verify the dose through any open pharmacy or the drug service; give the confirmed dose observed, or, if not confirmable, a small observed dose under local policy with symptom relief, plus naloxone and a plan for tomorrow.","r":"Right: treats his withdrawal, guards against overdose, and protects his two years of recovery.","best":true},{"c":"Give him 60 mg now because he is clearly withdrawing and knows his dose.","r":"Unsafe: an unconfirmed dose risks overdose if his prescription is lower, he has missed more days than he says, or he has used heroin on top.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him A&E does not prescribe methadone and ask him to leave.","r":"Pushes him towards heroin today, with low tolerance and no naloxone. Symptomatic help and a plan are always possible.","best":false}],"law":["Methadone is a Schedule 2 controlled drug: any A&E dose must be prescribed, given observed, and recorded in the controlled drugs register.","Tell his pharmacy and prescriber what was given today so tomorrow's dose is not doubled.","Sharing his information with his drug service and pharmacy for safe prescribing is direct care; tell him you are doing it.","DVLA: drug dependence, including being on a methadone programme, must be declared; advise no driving while in withdrawal."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I want to get you through today safely. I can't give methadone on anyone's word alone, because the wrong dose can stop someone breathing. Let's confirm your script and sort the symptoms meanwhile."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If you do use heroin, your tolerance may be lower than you think. Don't use alone, and take this naloxone kit with you."},{"to":"A&E doctor","s":"Mr Gartside is on methadone 60 mg daily, unsupervised, for two years, last dose 26 hours ago. COWS is 14, moderate withdrawal. I'm trying the pharmacy's out-of-hours line; if we can't confirm, I suggest 10 mg observed per trust policy, symptom relief, and naloxone before he goes."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E, 15:30, bank holiday. 39M, lives alone, unemployed.\nPresenting: demanding methadone; pharmacy closed for bank holiday. Last dose Sun ~13:30 (26 h). Symptoms: rhinorrhoea, sweating, abdominal cramps, nausea, restless. COWS 14 (moderate). Pupils dilated.\nMethadone: 60 mg daily oral, 2 years, collected daily, unsupervised. Prescriber: community drug service. Pharmacy: [name]. Adherent; denies heroin on top.\nOpioids: smoked heroin from early 20s, then IV daily; up to £150/day funded by begging/theft. Shared needles in past; BBV tested on starting methadone (results to confirm). No overdoses.\nOther: alcohol 2 cans standard lager nightly (~4.5 units/day); tobacco 40/day; past daily cannabis, none now; denies benzodiazepines, crack, cocaine, other opioids, NPS, GHB.\nRisk: relapse to heroin today; overdose with reduced tolerance; no suicidal thoughts. Does not drive.\nPlan: dose verification attempted via [pharmacy out-of-hours / drug service]; [confirmed: 60 mg observed / not confirmed: 10 mg observed per policy, reassess at 1 h]; loperamide, metoclopramide, paracetamol; UDS. Naloxone kit given and demonstrated. Pharmacy and drug service informed of today's dose. Advised attend pharmacy tomorrow. GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, liaison psychiatry","make":["Name his distress and say you want to help before asking anything else.","Get the methadone facts in one block: dose, prescriber, pharmacy, supervised or not, last dose, missed doses, using on top.","For each substance: how much, how often, route, last use, how long.","Explain why you ask so much: it keeps him safe, not to judge him.","Say plainly why you won't prescribe on his word, and what you will do instead.","Hand him naloxone and show him how to use it."],"next":"The drug service's on-call nurse confirms 60 mg daily and no missed collections before Sunday. Lee takes 60 mg observed in A&E, his COWS falls to 3 within two hours, and he leaves with a naloxone kit. The pharmacy is told so tomorrow's dose is not doubled, and his keyworker plans holiday pick-ups for next time.","marks":["Explored his concerns and acknowledged his withdrawal without judgement.","Recorded methadone dose, prescriber, pharmacy, supervision, last dose and missed doses.","Took a heroin history: onset, route, cost, injecting, sharing.","Asked about every other substance group with amount, route and last use.","Asked about overdose, blood-borne viruses and current risk of using heroin.","Explained dose verification and agreed a plan: symptom relief, naloxone, tomorrow's dose."],"pitfalls":["Prescribing methadone on his word alone.","Sending him away with nothing, so he buys heroin.","A history that skips benzodiazepines, alcohol or other opioids.","Forgetting to tell the pharmacy what was given, so the dose is doubled tomorrow.","Lecturing about drugs and losing his cooperation."],"refs":["1. The Orange Book: UK clinical guidelines on drug misuse and dependence (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG52 and TA114: drug misuse, opioid detoxification and methadone (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: methadone hydrochloride (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, drug misuse and dependence (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK most methadone is prescribed by community drug services, not GPs, and collected daily from a pharmacy. Out of hours, the pharmacy and the drug service are your two sources of truth: call them before you prescribe.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-29","n":1429,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🤝","title":"After CBT didn't help: is IPT right for him?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team, clinic room","text":"Professor Rohan Iyer, 64, a retired engineering lecturer, self-referred to NHS Talking Therapies two months ago with mild to moderate depression. He did eight sessions of CBT, took part well, but felt little better. The therapist's discharge letter to his GP, not shared with him, suggested IPT might suit him. His GP has referred him to you on that basis. This is his first appointment."},"task":"In about ten minutes, check his mood and safety, find which IPT focus areas apply to him, explain what IPT is and why it might help, check his suitability, and agree a shared decision.","first60":["Read the Talking Therapies discharge letter: PHQ-9 scores session by session, what the CBT focused on, and why IPT was suggested.","Check his GP letter for physical health, medicines and any past episodes.","Have a PHQ-9 form ready to repeat today.","Know your local IPT route: who delivers it, how many sessions, and the current wait.","Prepare one sentence on why CBT came first that does not imply it was second-best."],"ask":["'How's your mood been over the last two weeks?'","'When did you first notice it, and what was happening in your life then?'","'What was CBT like for you? What helped and what didn't?'","'Have you lost anyone close to you in the last few years?'","'Who are the important people in your life now, and how are things with them?'","'Has life ever felt not worth carrying on with?'","'Could you come to weekly sessions for about four months?'","'If we focused on one area, which would make the biggest difference?'"],"recognise":["His mood dropped soon after retiring last spring, when he lost daily contact with colleagues and students: a role transition.","He and his wife now argue about how he spends his days: a role dispute, at the stage where both are still trying to change things.","Fewer social contacts since retiring may look like interpersonal deficits, but he had a full working life of relationships: this is loss of a role, not a lifelong difficulty.","No recent death: grief, in IPT, means bereavement, so that area does not apply.","He found CBT worksheets abstract but lights up talking about people: a sign he may connect with a relationship-focused therapy.","PHQ-9 of 14 with no suicidal thoughts and no alcohol or psychosis problems: safe to wait for outpatient therapy."],"decide":[{"c":"Recommend IPT with role transition as the main focus and the dispute with his wife as a second strand, 12-16 weekly sessions, with GP mood reviews while he waits and a clear crisis route.","r":"Right: matches the therapy to what drives his depression, keeps the focus narrow, and keeps him safe while waiting.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him CBT was the wrong therapy and IPT is better for people like him.","r":"Wrong message: NICE lists both as effective options. CBT is often offered first; a poor response to one therapy is a reason to try another, not proof the first was bad.","best":false},{"c":"Offer IPT targeting all four focus areas to be thorough.","r":"IPT works by keeping one or two areas in focus. Spreading across four dilutes it within 16 sessions.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity and the choice is his: offer therapy, medication, both, or watchful waiting, with balanced information.","He was not told about the therapist's IPT suggestion: be open that it was in the letter to his GP, and that he is entitled to see his records.","Inviting his wife to a session needs his agreement; she may also be offered a carer's conversation in her own right."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"IPT explores the two-way link between your mood and the people in your life, particularly when life changes a lot. For you, that's retiring and how things are at home."},{"to":"Patient","s":"CBT wasn't a wrong choice: it's a good therapy that helps many people, and it's usually offered first. It just didn't fit what's driving this for you. That tells us something useful."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Seen Professor Iyer. Moderate depression (PHQ-9 14), no suicidal ideation. Suitable for IPT, focus on retirement as role transition and a role dispute with his wife. Referred for 16 sessions; please review mood monthly while he waits."}],"write":"CMHT outpatient, first appointment. 64M, retired engineering lecturer, lives with wife.\nReferral: GP; mild-moderate depression; 8 sessions CBT (Talking Therapies), engaged, limited response; therapist suggested IPT.\nMood: low since retirement last spring; reduced enjoyment, poor sleep, low energy, poor concentration. PHQ-9 today 14.\nRisk: denies suicidal ideation and self-harm thoughts; alcohol minimal; no psychotic symptoms. Protective: wife, daughter, faith, wants help.\nInterpersonal inventory: lost daily contact with colleagues and students (role transition); arguments with wife about how he spends his days (role dispute, renegotiation stage); no recent bereavement; good lifelong relationships (not deficits).\nCBT: found worksheets abstract; preferred talking about people and events.\nSuitability for IPT: clear focus, links mood to events, motivated, can attend weekly, no contraindications.\nPlan: shared decision for IPT, 12-16 weekly sessions, focus role transition +/- role dispute. Referral sent. Leaflet given. GP to review mood monthly while waiting. Declines medication for now. Crisis: NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123.\nDr Chinonso, CMHT","make":["Ask about the CBT experience before explaining IPT: his answer tells you what to emphasise.","Ask about each IPT area by name in your head: grief, dispute, transition, isolation.","Pick one or two focus areas with him, not four.","Answer 'why wasn't this offered first?' without rubbishing CBT.","Give numbers: sessions, frequency, length, wait.","Close with a teach-back and a crisis route."],"next":"Rohan starts IPT eight weeks later with a therapist in the CMHT. By session six he has joined a local engineering mentoring scheme for school students, and he and his wife have agreed which days are 'his' and which are shared. His PHQ-9 is 7 at session 12.","marks":["Reviewed mood with a score and asked directly about suicidal thoughts.","Explored his CBT experience and acknowledged his disappointment.","Checked all four IPT areas and named which apply.","Explained IPT: mood and relationships, here and now, time-limited, three phases.","Answered why CBT came first without saying one therapy is better.","Checked suitability and agreed a focus, practical details and safety net."],"pitfalls":["Explaining IPT before hearing his story, so the explanation is generic.","Saying IPT is better than CBT.","Calling his retirement 'grief': in IPT, grief means a death.","Choosing all four areas as the focus.","Skipping the risk question because he seems 'only mild'."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS England: NHS Talking Therapies manual (checked Nov 2026)","3. IPT UK: about interpersonal psychotherapy (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: interpersonal therapy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in England, NHS Talking Therapies offers CBT first to most people with mild to moderate depression; IPT, counselling and other therapies are offered when CBT does not fit or does not work. Patients can self-refer.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-30","n":1430,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"BMI 14 on the medical ward: history and risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:00, liaison psychiatry","where":"General medical ward, bay 4","text":"Tamsin Bello, 19, was admitted with abdominal pain. Her BMI is 14. Her potassium was low on arrival and has been corrected; the medical team say everything else is unremarkable and have asked liaison psychiatry to see her. She is sitting up in bed under several blankets and has agreed to talk."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused eating disorder history, assess medical and suicide risk, and give the medical team a clear plan.","first60":["Check the observations chart: pulse, lying and standing blood pressure, temperature, and any falls.","Read the bloods: potassium, phosphate, magnesium, sodium, glucose, LFTs, FBC. Look for an ECG and the QTc.","Check her height, admission weight, and any previous weights in GP or clinic records.","Ask the nurse: is she eating, what is left on her tray, does she go to the toilet after meals, has anyone seen laxatives?","Draw the curtain and sit at eye level; she is likely to feel judged."],"ask":["'Can you take me through what you ate and drank yesterday?'","'What was your highest weight, and how fast has it come down?'","'Do you ever make yourself sick, or use laxatives, diet pills or water to control your weight?'","'How much exercise do you do in a day?'","'How do you feel about your body now? What would happen if you gained weight?'","'Have you felt dizzy, fainted, had a racing heart or chest pain?'","'Do you have trouble getting up from a chair or climbing stairs?'","'Have you had thoughts of wishing you weren't here, or of harming yourself?'"],"recognise":["BMI 14, an apple and black coffee a day, three hours' walking and a fear of looking 'huge': anorexia nervosa.","Nightly laxatives make it the binge-purge pattern in ICD-11 and explain the low potassium.","Low potassium from purging, plus a BMI of 14, puts her at real risk of arrhythmia and refeeding syndrome once she eats.","No periods for a year, feeling cold and covering up in blankets are signs of starvation.","A passive wish not to wake up is significant: suicide risk is raised in anorexia, so ask about plans and self-harm.","Wanting to go home now that her potassium is normal shows limited insight into medical risk."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history and risk, explain the danger plainly, ask her to stay, and give the medical team a plan: MEED-guided refeeding with daily bloods, ECG, laxatives stopped and searched for with consent, supervised meals, eating disorder service referral, and a capacity check if she tries to leave.","r":"Right: treats her as medically high risk, starts safe refeeding, involves the specialist service, and has a legal route ready.","best":true},{"c":"Advise discharge with an outpatient eating disorder referral, as her potassium is corrected.","r":"Unsafe: her potassium will fall again with ongoing purging, and BMI 14 with refeeding risk needs inpatient monitoring.","best":false},{"c":"Ask the ward to start a very low-calorie diet to avoid refeeding syndrome.","r":"Outdated: MEED warns that underfeeding is a risk too. Start at an adequate level with close electrolyte monitoring and replacement.","best":false}],"law":["She is an informal adult inpatient; assess capacity specifically for the decision to leave or refuse food, because anorexia can impair weighing up.","If she lacks capacity or refuses life-saving care because of the disorder, request a Mental Health Act assessment. Section 5(2) can be used on a medical ward by the doctor in charge or their nominated deputy.","Nasogastric feeding can be given under Section 63 as treatment for the mental disorder if she is detained.","Involving her family needs her consent at 19; ask, and record her answer."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see you want to get home, but your body is in a dangerous place right now. Your heart is at risk while your salts are unstable, so I'd like you to stay while we make eating safe."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Ms Bello has anorexia nervosa, binge-purge type, with nightly laxatives. BMI 14, high risk. Please start refeeding with the dietitian per MEED, thiamine, daily phosphate, potassium and magnesium, ECG, and stop laxatives. We'll refer to the eating disorder service today and review daily."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"She has a passive wish not to wake up, no plan. Please supervise meals and 30 minutes after, check for laxatives with her consent, and call us if she tries to leave."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, medical ward, 11:00. 19F, student. Admitted abdominal pain; BMI 14; K+ low on admission, corrected.\nIntake: apple and black coffee most days; calorie counting; no binges. Exercise: walks ~3 h/day. Compensatory: senna/bisacodyl nightly for months (number to confirm); no vomiting reported; no diet pills.\nWeight: highest ~[x] kg; lost [x] kg over [x] months.\nBeliefs: feels 'huge'; intense fear of gain; daily weighing.\nPhysical: amenorrhoea 1 year; cold; dizzy on standing; no faints, chest pain or palpitations reported; difficulty rising from chair.\nMood: low, anxious; passive death wish ('wish I wouldn't wake up'), no plan, no self-harm. No alcohol or drugs. FHx: [x].\nRisk: HIGH medical risk (BMI 14, purging, hypokalaemia, refeeding). Suicide risk: moderate (passive ideation, AN). Leaving: wants to go home.\nImpression: anorexia nervosa, binge-purge pattern. Limited insight.\nPlan: advised to stay; agrees today. To medics: MEED-guided refeeding with dietitian; thiamine; daily K, PO4, Mg, glucose, U&E for 1 week; ECG; lying/standing BP; stop laxatives. Supervised meals. Eating disorder service referral today. Family involvement with consent. Capacity assessment if she seeks to leave; MHA if needed. Daily liaison review.\nDr Chinonso, liaison psychiatry","make":["Ask for a full day's intake hour by hour; vague questions get vague answers.","Ask about each compensatory behaviour by name, including how many laxatives.","Respond to disclosures neutrally: acknowledge, don't praise or criticise.","Ask about faints, palpitations, chest pain and weakness: these drive medical risk.","Ask directly about wishing to be dead.","Leave the medical team a written, numbered plan."],"next":"Tamsin agrees to stay. The dietitian starts a meal plan with supplements, and her phosphate dips on day three and is replaced. The eating disorder team assess her on day five, and she is transferred to a specialist unit as an informal patient on day twelve.","marks":["Took a typical day's intake, rules and exercise in detail.","Asked about vomiting, laxatives with number, diet pills and water loading.","Explored weight history, fear of gain and body image.","Asked about physical red flags, periods and weakness.","Asked about mood, passive death wish and self-harm.","Explained the risk, asked her to stay, and gave the medical team a refeeding and referral plan."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'I eat healthily' without a detailed day.","Missing laxatives, so the potassium stays unexplained.","Advising discharge because the bloods are now normal.","Recommending very slow refeeding by habit.","Skipping the suicide question because the focus is food."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED), CR233 (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG69: eating disorders, recognition and treatment (checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: anorexia nervosa 6B80 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, MEED guidance replaced MARSIPAN in 2022. Medical teams will expect you to advise on refeeding, bloods and risk, not only the psychiatric diagnosis.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-31","n":1431,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍽️","title":"NG feeding and refeeding risk: briefing the nurse","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:40, liaison psychiatry","where":"Acute medical ward, general hospital","text":"You have just reviewed Imogen Darko, a 22-year-old woman with severe anorexia nervosa, BMI 13, moved here from the eating disorder unit for nasogastric feeding. Her last admission ended in intensive care with refeeding syndrome. She is detained under the Mental Health Act and lacks capacity to consent to treatment. The dietitian has written the feeding plan and the first feed is due at noon. Callum, her nurse today, has never nursed NG feeds in anorexia."},"task":"In about ten minutes, brief Callum on the plan: why refeeding is dangerous for Imogen, the feeding and monitoring regimen, the legal frame, the signs that mean call now, and what to do if she pulls the tube or tries to leave.","first60":["Read the dietitian's plan: the starting rate, how fast it rises, and the electrolyte replacement protocol it refers to.","Check the drug chart: thiamine and vitamin B compound strong prescribed and due before the first feed.","Look at this morning's bloods (phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose, U&E) and the ECG (rate, QTc).","Check the legal papers: which section, and whether she is here on Section 17 leave or transferred under Section 19.","Find out the agreed observation level, who the mental health nurse is, and the eating disorder unit's contact number."],"ask":["'What have you heard about refeeding syndrome so far?'","'Has her thiamine gone in yet, and when is the first feed?'","'Who is with her one-to-one today, and are they trained in safe holding?'","'How was she when the tube went in?'","'Tell me back: what would make you call the doctor straight away?'","'If she left the ward, who would you ring first?'"],"recognise":["BMI 13 plus a previous episode of refeeding syndrome puts her in the highest risk group: the dangerous window is the first few days of feeding.","The tiny starting rate is deliberate. Speeding it up because she looks so thin is the commonest well-meant mistake.","Phosphate is the key test: a falling level can cause heart failure, arrhythmia, weakness and confusion.","New ankle swelling, breathlessness or palpitations in the first week may be refeeding, not just 'fluid'.","Tube pulling, turning the pump down, hiding or vomiting feed, exercising and drinking water before weighing are part of the illness, not defiance.","Callum's unease about holding her is reasonable: he needs a clear, least restrictive plan and trained colleagues, not just permission."],"decide":[{"c":"Give Callum a short structured briefing: refeeding mechanism, low start, thiamine first, daily bloods and ECG, named call-now triggers, the legal frame and a tube-out plan; then check his teach-back.","r":"Right: he leaves knowing what to watch, when to call and what to do, and the plan is safe and lawful.","best":true},{"c":"Advise Callum to increase the feed faster because she is so underweight.","r":"Dangerous: rapid refeeding is exactly what put her in intensive care last time. The dietitian's rate stands unless the dietitian and medical team change it.","best":false},{"c":"Tell Callum that as she is detained and lacks capacity, staff can hold her for every feed without further thought.","r":"Wrong: restraint is a last resort, proportionate, done by trained staff and recorded every time. The aim is cooperation with explanation first.","best":false}],"law":["NG feeding for anorexia nervosa counts as treatment for her mental disorder under Section 63 of the Mental Health Act, so her consent is not required; staff still explain and seek her cooperation each time.","Check the papers: her section, and whether she is here on Section 17 leave (with its escort conditions) or transferred under Section 19.","If she leaves without permission she is absent without leave; Section 18 allows staff or police to return her.","Any holding for feeding: last resort, least restrictive, trained staff, recorded each time (MHA Code of Practice, chapter 26). She has a right to an independent mental health advocate (IMHA)."],"say":[{"to":"Ward nurse","s":"The feed starts tiny on purpose. Last time her phosphate crashed and she ended up in intensive care. Low and slow protects her heart."},{"to":"Ward nurse","s":"Bleep the medical doctor straight away for palpitations, chest pain, new confusion, a fit, sudden swelling or breathlessness, or a phosphate under 0.5. Then let me know."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Imogen needs one-to-one from a mental health nurse, especially during feeds and for an hour after. Can we agree who that is today and that they're trained in safe holding?"}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 22F, severe anorexia nervosa, BMI 13, transferred from ED unit for NG feeding. Detained under MHA; lacks capacity to consent to treatment. Previous refeeding syndrome requiring ICU.\nBriefed staff nurse Callum (10:45): refeeding mechanism; dietitian's low starting rate not to be increased without dietitian/medical review; thiamine 200-300 mg + vit B co strong + multivitamin before first feed and for 10 days; NG position by aspirate pH 5.5 or below before each feed and after vomiting/coughing.\nMonitoring: phosphate, K, Mg, glucose, U&E daily for first week; ECG baseline and if electrolytes fall; NEWS2 at least 4-hourly; capillary glucose; strict fluid balance; weight as per plan.\nCall medical team at once: phosphate <0.5, palpitations/arrhythmia, chest pain, new confusion, seizure, rapid oedema or breathlessness.\nLegal: NG feeding is treatment under MHA s63. Holding only as last resort, trained staff, recorded. If tube removed: stop feed, inform doctor, do not re-pass without plan. If leaves ward: AWOL procedure, inform nurse in charge, on-call psychiatrist and police if needed.\nObs: 1:1 RMN, especially during and 1 h after feeds. Callum repeated triggers correctly. Liaison review 16:00 today.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Use SBAR so he gets the whole picture in under a minute.","Explain the 'why' of refeeding in plain words: he will follow a rule better if he understands it.","Give numbers he can act on: thiamine before feed, pH 5.5, phosphate 0.5, four-hourly obs.","Name who to call, day and night, and what to say.","Respect his worry about restraint and give him a least restrictive plan, not just reassurance.","Finish with teach-back and a fixed review time."],"next":"Callum gives the thiamine at 11:30 and the first feed starts at noon at the dietitian's rate. On day 3 her phosphate falls to 0.6; he tells the medical doctor, it is replaced and the rate is held for a day. Imogen tugs at the tube once on day 2; the mental health nurse talks her down without holding. By day 7 she is on her target feed with stable bloods.","marks":["Introduced self, checked his experience and agreed a short structured briefing.","Explained refeeding syndrome and why the starting rate is low.","Gave the vitamin, tube-check and monitoring plan with timings.","Named specific escalation triggers and who to call.","Explained the legal frame and a least restrictive plan for tube pulling or leaving.","Checked understanding with teach-back and fixed a review time."],"pitfalls":["Talking in jargon ('hypophosphataemia') without saying what it means for her care.","Agreeing to speed up the feed because she looks so thin.","Forgetting thiamine before the first feed.","Saying 'call if worried' instead of naming the triggers.","Treating detention as permission for routine restraint, or skipping the legal frame altogether."],"refs":["1. NICE CG32: Nutrition support for adults (2006, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists CR233: Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters 24 and 26 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS Improvement Patient Safety Alert: nasogastric tube misplacement (2016; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK nurses expect doctors to brief them clearly and to listen. A short SBAR, a few numbers to act on and 'call me if…' with named triggers will earn more trust than a long lecture.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-32","n":1432,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Methadone and a new pregnancy","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00, perinatal mental health clinic","where":"Perinatal outpatient clinic, community hospital","text":"Jodie Pennington, a 31-year-old woman, is eight weeks pregnant. She stopped injecting heroin four years ago and has been stable since on methadone 30 mg daily, collected unsupervised from her pharmacy. She has stopped smoking and drinking. The pregnancy was unplanned but welcome. She wants to come off all medication for the baby's sake, but fears withdrawal and going back to heroin. Her GP has asked the perinatal team for advice."},"task":"In about ten minutes, check her drug use, health and mood, explain her options for methadone in pregnancy, answer her questions honestly (withdrawal in the baby, breastfeeding, social care), and agree a shared plan.","first60":["Read the referral and GP summary: methadone dose, who prescribes it, last urine drug screen, hepatitis C history, any HIV or hepatitis B results.","Check whether she has booked with a midwife yet, and whether there is a specialist substance misuse midwife locally.","Have the local leaflet on methadone in pregnancy ready to hand over.","Know your naloxone route: who supplies a kit locally (drug service or pharmacy).","See her on her own first, so you can ask about domestic abuse safely."],"ask":["'How long since you last used heroin, or anything on top of your methadone?'","'Do you get cravings, and what do you do when they come?'","'Have you injected at all since stopping? Have you had blood tests for hepatitis and HIV?'","'Have you booked with a midwife yet? Are you taking folic acid?'","'How's your mood been since you found out?'","'Does anyone at home ever hurt you, threaten you or make you feel afraid?'","'What would you most like to happen with your methadone?'"],"recognise":["Four years stable on 30 mg with no on-top use is a real achievement and the best predictor of a good outcome: say so.","Her wish to stop all medication comes from love and guilt, not from information: validate first, then inform.","Sudden withdrawal is riskier for the pregnancy than staying on a steady dose, and relapse after a gap carries overdose risk because tolerance drops.","Fear of social services can drive women away from antenatal care: an honest, supportive explanation keeps her engaged.","A past positive hepatitis C test needs a current result: treated and cleared, or still active?","A past depressive episode plus pregnancy and stopping smoking: screen mood now and plan to check again after birth."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise continuing methadone 30 mg, explain the risks of stopping, offer a slow specialist-led reduction later only if she still wants it, explain neonatal withdrawal and breastfeeding honestly, and link her with the specialist midwife and drug service.","r":"Right: safe for her and the baby, respects her choice, and keeps her engaged with every team she needs.","best":true},{"c":"Support her wish to stop methadone now, with a quick reduction over two weeks.","r":"Unsafe: rapid withdrawal in pregnancy risks relapse, overdose and harm to the pregnancy. Any reduction is slow, specialist-led and stops if cravings return.","best":false},{"c":"Advise switching to buprenorphine straight away because babies have milder withdrawal.","r":"Not now: a switch in a stable woman risks precipitated withdrawal and destabilising her. Methadone has the strongest evidence in pregnancy; stay on what works.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity: the decision about methadone is hers once she has balanced information. Record what was explained.","Consent to share: ask to write to her GP, prescriber and midwife; explain that safeguarding concerns can be shared without consent if a child is at risk.","A pre-birth plan with children's social care is common when a parent is on opioid treatment; it is about support, and being on methadone alone is not grounds to remove a baby.","Unsupervised doses: discuss safe storage now, as a young child in the home changes the risk."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Wanting the best for your baby is exactly right. For most women in your position, the safest thing is staying on a steady dose, because stopping risks withdrawal and going back to heroin."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Some babies do show withdrawal after birth: crying, poor feeding, sneezing. The team watch for it for a few days and it's very treatable. Breastfeeding actually helps."},{"to":"Drug service keyworker","s":"Jodie is eight weeks pregnant and stable on methadone 30 mg. Our advice is to continue at the same dose and review in the third trimester. Please share her last urine screen with the midwife."}],"write":"Perinatal psychiatry clinic. 31F, G1, 8/40. Methadone 30 mg daily, unsupervised pharmacy collection, 4 years stable. No heroin or on-top use since; last injected 4 years ago. Occasional cravings, managed. Stopped smoking and alcohol since positive test.\nPMH: hep C treated 2 years ago, told cleared. No other illness. Past depressive episode early 20s.\nMood: anxious, guilty about baby, no low mood, no suicidal thoughts. No domestic abuse (asked alone). Partner supportive.\nDiscussed: continuing methadone safest; risks of sudden withdrawal (relapse, overdose, harm to pregnancy); option of slow reduction in 2nd trimester with drug service if she wishes; possible dose increase in 3rd trimester; neonatal withdrawal (signs, observation, treatment); breastfeeding encouraged; pre-birth planning with social care as support.\nPlan: continue 30 mg; letter to GP, prescriber and midwife (consent given); specialist substance misuse midwife; booking bloods incl HCV RNA, HIV, hep B; folic acid 400 micrograms daily to 12/40; naloxone kit; locked box; leaflet given. Review 4 weeks. Crisis: 999 / 111 option 2.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 perinatal psychiatry","make":["Praise the four years and the changes she has already made before anything else.","Answer her real question early: 'Can I keep taking it?' Yes, and here's why.","Be honest about neonatal withdrawal; vague reassurance loses trust when the baby is watched after birth.","Offer a real choice: continue, or a slow, supervised reduction later, with clear conditions.","Ask about domestic abuse while she is alone.","Close with named people: specialist midwife, keyworker, GP, and when you will see her again."],"next":"Jodie stays on 30 mg. The specialist midwife books her the following week; her hepatitis C RNA is negative. At 32 weeks she feels shaky in the evenings and the dose is split. Her son is watched for five days, has mild withdrawal settled with skin-to-skin and breastfeeding, and goes home with her and Dan.","marks":["Built rapport and praised her achievements.","Took a focused drug, health and blood-borne virus history.","Screened mood, relapse risk and domestic abuse.","Explained why continuing methadone is safest and the risks of stopping.","Answered questions on neonatal withdrawal, breastfeeding and social care honestly.","Agreed a shared plan with named contacts and safety-netting."],"pitfalls":["Agreeing to a quick detox because she asks for it.","Promising the baby will have no withdrawal at all.","Telling her not to breastfeed because of methadone or hepatitis C.","Avoiding the social services question, or answering it vaguely.","Forgetting naloxone, safe storage and the third-trimester dose review."],"refs":["1. Department of Health 'Orange Book': UK clinical guidance for drug misuse and dependence (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG110: Pregnancy and complex social factors (2010; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG192: Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: methadone, pregnancy and breastfeeding (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, opioid substitution in pregnancy is usually continued, not stopped, and social care involvement is framed as support. Women on methadone are often frightened of losing their baby; saying this out loud early builds trust.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-33","n":1433,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍼","title":"Low after the baby: taking the history","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:00, adult psychiatry outpatients","where":"Outpatient clinic room, community mental health centre","text":"Ffion Dempsey, a 32-year-old teaching assistant, gave birth to her second son, Noah, six weeks ago. Her GP has referred her because she has felt low since, and is especially worried that she does not seem to be bonding with the baby. Her partner, Rhys, is in the waiting area with Noah and their three-year-old, Alfie. She comes in alone and apologises for being 'a mess'."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused psychiatric history to reach the most likely diagnosis, rule out the key differentials, and assess risk to Ffion and to both children.","first60":["Read the GP letter: delivery details, any blood loss, feeding, current medication, and any score the GP recorded.","Check the records for anything after her first baby, and for any family history of postnatal illness.","Have tissues within reach and a clock she can't see.","Know your same-day route: the consultant on duty and the perinatal team's urgent referral line.","She is alone with you now: this is your chance to ask about domestic abuse safely."],"ask":["'When did you first notice feeling this way, and has it been there most days since?'","'When Noah is asleep, can you sleep?'","'What is it like when you hold him?'","'Some mums get frightening thoughts that pop into their head about the baby being hurt. Has that happened to you?'","'Have you had any experiences that seemed strange, like hearing things others don't?'","'How were you after Alfie was born?'","'Have you felt life's not worth going on with, or that they'd manage better without you?'","'Have you ever had thoughts of harming Noah or Alfie?'"],"recognise":["Onset two weeks after birth, persisting a month: not baby blues, which start in the first days and settle by about two weeks.","Waking even when the baby sleeps, poor appetite and guilt are depressive features, not just a tired new mum.","'I feel nothing when I hold him' is common in postnatal depression and must be asked about without judgement; it is also a red flag for urgent specialist input.","Distressing unwanted thoughts of harm that she resists point to perinatal OCD; voices, odd beliefs or high mood point to psychosis, which is an emergency.","A previous low spell after her first baby and a mother with postnatal depression raise the chance of recurrence and change the urgency of referral.","Heavy blood loss at delivery: check for anaemia; thyroid problems after birth can mimic depression."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused history, name likely postnatal depression, discuss today with the consultant, make an urgent perinatal team referral, and share a crisis plan with Ffion and Rhys.","r":"Right: the diagnosis is clear, the red flags (detachment from the baby, thoughts of being better off gone) get a timely response, and the family has a safety net.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her it is the baby blues and review in three months.","r":"Wrong: a month of symptoms starting two weeks after birth is postnatal depression. Waiting risks her, the bond with Noah and Alfie's care.","best":false},{"c":"Refer to children's social care because she does not feel love for the baby.","r":"Not on these facts: she cares for Noah well, has no thoughts of harm and has support. Lack of feeling is a symptom to treat. Inform the health visitor, with consent, for extra support.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is seeking help: decisions about treatment are hers.","Ask her consent to share with her GP, health visitor and the perinatal team; explain that if a child were at risk, information would be shared without consent.","Safeguarding: no current concern on these facts, but record clearly that you asked about harm to both children and what she said.","Record risk to self and to children in a structured note in the electronic record the same day."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Lots of mums feel like this after a baby, and it isn't your fault. What you're describing sounds like postnatal depression, and it gets better with the right help."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Ffion is 32, six weeks after her second baby: a moderate depressive episode, onset at two weeks, detached from the baby, fleeting thoughts the family would be better off without her, no plan. Previous postnatal low mood. I'd like an urgent perinatal referral; can we discuss?"},{"to":"Health visitor","s":"With Ffion's consent: she has postnatal depression and is being referred to the perinatal team. No concerns about harm to the children. Could you offer extra visits?"}],"write":"Psychiatry OPD. 32F, teaching assistant. P2, 6/52 postnatal (Noah); son Alfie 3. Ref by GP: low mood, poor bonding.\nHPC: onset ~2 weeks after birth, worsening. Low most days, tearful daily, anhedonia (incl. with Alfie), initial and early waking even when baby sleeps, poor appetite, fatigue, poor concentration, guilt ('bad mother'). Feels 'nothing' holding Noah but meets all his care needs. Breastfeeding.\nNegatives: no psychotic symptoms, no elevated mood, no intrusive thoughts of harm or rituals (checks breathing 1-2x/night, proportionate).\nBirth: long labour, ventouse, PPH, on oral iron.\nPPH: low for ~2 months after Alfie, untreated. FH: mother postnatal depression. Alcohol/drugs: none. Social: partner Rhys supportive, works long shifts; mother 40 min away. DA: denied (asked alone).\nRisk: fleeting thoughts family better off without her in last week; no plan, intent or past self-harm; protective: children, partner. No thoughts of harming either child; no neglect.\nImpression: postnatal depression (moderate depressive episode, postpartum onset), recurrent pattern. Red flag: estrangement from infant.\nPlan: discussed with consultant today; urgent perinatal team referral; GP for FBC, ferritin, TFTs; health visitor informed (consent); crisis plan shared with Ffion and Rhys (111 option 2, 999).\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Normalise early: 'many mums feel like this' gives her permission to be honest.","Pin the timeline: when it started after the birth and how long it has lasted.","Ask about feelings for the baby and thoughts of harm in plain, calm words.","Separate depressive guilt, OCD-type unwanted thoughts and psychosis with one or two clear questions each.","Ask about the first baby and her mother: recurrence changes the plan.","Ask about domestic abuse while she is alone; ask about both children, not just the baby."],"next":"Ffion's bloods show a low ferritin; her GP increases her iron. The perinatal team see her within a week, start sertraline 50 mg (compatible with breastfeeding) and refer her for CBT and parent-infant work. Her health visitor visits weekly. By three months she says Noah 'feels like mine now'.","marks":["Built rapport and normalised her feelings.","Established timeline and core and biological symptoms.","Explored bonding and care of the baby without judgement.","Screened baby blues, OCD, psychosis, mania and physical causes.","Took past, family and social history, including domestic abuse.","Assessed risk to self and to both children, named the diagnosis and gave next steps."],"pitfalls":["Calling it the baby blues without checking the timeline.","Skipping the question about harming the baby because it feels awkward.","Asking only about Noah and forgetting Alfie.","Missing psychosis or OCD by not asking.","Not asking about the previous pregnancy or family history."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192: Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","3. MBRRACE-UK: Saving Lives, Improving Mothers' Care, maternal mental health red flags (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Postnatal depression (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, asking a new mother whether she has thoughts of harming her baby is routine and expected. Ask it calmly, after normalising, and explain why you ask everyone. It rarely offends, and missing it is a serious omission.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-34","n":1434,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"'That's not my baby': assessing the mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 16:00, liaison psychiatry","where":"Side room, postnatal ward, general hospital","text":"Deepa Raghunathan, a 31-year-old woman, is five days after an emergency Caesarean for fetal distress, complicated by heavy bleeding needing transfusion. Her daughter came back from the neonatal unit yesterday. Since then Mrs Raghunathan has grown agitated, keeps asking where her 'real' baby is and will not care for her daughter; her husband, Arjun, is doing the feeds. In her twenties she became manic on sertraline and was briefly admitted. The obstetric team ask you to assess her mental state and risk."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess her mental state and her risk to her daughter, other babies, staff and herself, screen for delirium, and explain the urgent plan to her.","first60":["Read the obstetric notes: Hb after transfusion, temperature, CRP, any infection, current drugs (opioids, antiemetics), sodium and calcium.","Ask the midwife in charge: what has she said and done, has she tried to leave, where is the baby now?","Check her past history: mania after sertraline, admission, antipsychotic, lost to follow-up.","Make sure the baby is with Arjun or staff, not alone with her, before you start.","Sit between her and the door, leave it ajar, and tell the midwife you are in there."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what's been happening since your daughter was born?'","'You seem very on edge. What's frightening you?'","'How do you know this baby isn't yours?'","'Have you heard people talking when nobody's there?'","'What's the date today? Can you go through the months in reverse order for me?'","'What do you plan to do about your real baby?'","'Could anyone get hurt?'","'Have you had thoughts of self-harm or the baby here with you?'"],"recognise":["Postpartum psychosis: sudden onset in the first days after birth, mixed mood and psychotic features, in a woman with a past manic episode. This is a psychiatric emergency.","The misidentification belief (baby swapped) is held with full conviction and explained by a persecutory story about the midwives.","Voices through the walls and a baby crying in the vent are hallucinations she experiences as completely real: don't call them that to her face.","Pressured speech, restlessness and terror suggest a mood component; past antidepressant-induced mania points to bipolar disorder.","Delirium must be excluded: recent haemorrhage, surgery, possible infection, opioids. Orientation and attention are the quick bedside tests.","The biggest risks: she plans to leave and take her 'real' baby by any means, so other babies, staff and her daughter are all at risk."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete a focused mental state and risk assessment, keep the baby safe with Arjun or staff, start constant observation, alert security and the neonatal unit, call the consultant, and request an urgent Mental Health Act assessment with a Mother and Baby Unit bed.","r":"Right: it treats postpartum psychosis as the emergency it is and protects her, her daughter and other babies.","best":true},{"c":"Advise the midwives to encourage bonding by leaving the baby with her overnight.","r":"Dangerous: she does not believe the baby is hers and is psychotic. The baby must not be left alone with her.","best":false},{"c":"Arrange outpatient follow-up with the community perinatal team next week.","r":"Unsafe: postpartum psychosis can escalate within hours, and she has stated intent to harm others. She needs same-day action.","best":false}],"law":["She is an informal inpatient. If she tries to leave before a Mental Health Act assessment, the doctor in charge of her care (or a nominated deputy) can use Section 5(2) to hold her for up to 72 hours. Midwives cannot use the nurse's holding power.","Urgent MHA assessment (AMHP and two doctors, one Section 12 approved); Section 2 is the likely outcome if she will not accept admission.","In an immediate emergency before that, if she lacks capacity, the MCA allows proportionate steps in her best interests; record them.","Safeguarding: the baby's safety comes first. Inform the named midwife for safeguarding and children's social care."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how frightened you are, and I want to help you be with your daughter. I'm worried you're very unwell after the birth, and I'd like you to have treatment today."},{"to":"Midwife in charge","s":"I think she has postpartum psychosis and she's said she'll leave to find her 'real' baby by any means. She needs one-to-one now, the baby stays with Arjun or staff, and please alert security and the neonatal unit."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Mrs Raghunathan, 31, day 5 after an emergency section with PPH, past antidepressant-induced mania: sudden psychosis, believes her baby was swapped, hears midwives through the walls, oriented, no insight, intends to take her 'real' baby by any means. I'm requesting an urgent MHA assessment and an MBU bed."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 31F, day 5 post emergency LSCS (fetal distress), PPH, transfused. Past: depression age ~22, sertraline-induced mania, brief admission, antipsychotic, full recovery; lost to follow-up. No current meds.\nMSE: hospital gown, dishevelled, restless, scanning door and vents, guarded. Speech rapid, pressured, whispered. Mood 'petrified'; affect anxious, labile. Thought content: fixed belief midwife swapped her baby (misidentification); persecutory ideas re midwives. Perception: hears midwives whispering 'bad mother' through walls; heard her 'real' baby crying in air vent. No passivity or thought interference. Cognition: oriented x3; months backwards correct. Insight: absent; declines treatment.\nRisk: to others HIGH - intends to leave and find 'real' baby 'by any means', would hurt someone if needed; abduction risk to other babies. To daughter: refuses care, believes not hers; neglect/harm risk. To self: denies suicidal thoughts.\nImpression: postpartum psychosis, likely bipolar spectrum. Delirium less likely but not excluded.\nPlan: 1:1 constant obs; baby with husband/staff, never alone with her; security and NNU alerted; consultant informed 16:15; urgent MHA assessment requested (AMHP); s5(2) if attempts to leave; MBU bed via perinatal team; bloods (FBC, CRP, U&E, Ca, glucose, TFTs, LFTs); safeguarding referral.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Win enough trust to talk: 'I'm here to help you, not to take anything away.'","Name what you see: 'You're speaking quite fast, and you seem very frightened.'","Follow her story with open questions, then pin details with closed ones.","Never argue with or agree to the swapped-baby belief: acknowledge her fear instead.","Test orientation and attention in under a minute to screen for delirium.","Ask the second and third risk question: 'And if someone stood in your way?'"],"next":"The consultant agrees; Mrs Raghunathan is detained under Section 2 that evening and moves to a Mother and Baby Unit two days later with her daughter. Olanzapine is started. After three weeks the voices stop, and she says she cannot believe she ever doubted her daughter. Lithium is started on the unit before discharge.","marks":["Built enough trust to engage a suspicious, frightened patient.","Described appearance, behaviour, speech and mood.","Elicited the misidentification and persecutory beliefs and the hallucinations.","Screened for delirium and assessed insight.","Asked directly about plans, harm to others, the baby and herself.","Explained the urgent plan and kept the baby and ward safe."],"pitfalls":["Arguing that the baby is definitely hers.","Focusing only on the delusion and missing mood, cognition and insight.","Accepting 'I just want my baby' without asking what she plans to do.","Forgetting other babies on the ward and staff.","Leaving without a same-day plan, or advising bonding time alone with the baby."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192: Antenatal and postnatal mental health (2014, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 18 (holding powers) (checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Postpartum psychosis information (checked Nov 2026)","4. MBRRACE-UK: Saving Lives, Improving Mothers' Care (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services treat postpartum psychosis as an emergency and aim to keep mother and baby together in a Mother and Baby Unit. Know your nearest MBU, and that Section 5(2) works on any hospital ward, not just psychiatric ones.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-35","n":1435,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"Jealous husband with a knife in custody","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 08:30, on call","where":"Interview room, police custody suite","text":"Gareth Pryce, a 36-year-old warehouse supervisor, was arrested late last night after police stopped him driving far over the limit. He told officers he was racing to the hospital where his wife, Kerry, is an emergency department nurse, to stop her affair with the senior charge nurse. A kitchen knife was under his seat. He has been agitated and preoccupied overnight, and the custody healthcare practitioner has asked you to assess his mental state and risk."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess his mental state, map the jealous belief and its origins, ask closely about the knife and his intentions, assess risk to his wife, the rival, others and himself, and tell him what happens next.","first60":["Ask the custody sergeant and healthcare practitioner: what he said on arrest, any intoxication, his last drink, any withdrawal signs, and whether he is to be interviewed.","Check police records available to you: previous call-outs to the address, domestic abuse reports, weapons or firearms licence.","Find out who is at home: children, and where they are now.","Check mental health records: any previous contact or diagnosis.","Sit nearest the door; agree with the officer how to call for help."],"ask":["'What made you set off at that moment last night?'","'How did you first come to believe Kerry was having an affair?'","'What have you done to find out for sure?'","'Have you heard voices or smelled things others don't?'","'What was the knife for?'","'And if he hadn't admitted it, what would you have done?'","'Have you ever hurt Kerry, or come close?'","'What will you do when you leave here?'"],"recognise":["Delusional (morbid) jealousy: a fixed belief in infidelity, built on trivial 'evidence' (new perfume, a parked car), with checking and following. It carries a real risk of serious violence, mostly towards the partner.","Voices that came before the suspicion, and commands last night, point to a psychotic illness rather than ordinary jealousy.","Heavy nightly drinking can cause or worsen jealous beliefs, and withdrawal may start in custody.","Taking a knife to a confrontation is planning. Not ruling out using it, with ongoing intent, makes the risk to the rival and anyone nearby high.","Denying any wish to hurt his wife does not make her safe: she would be present at any confrontation, and homicide-suicide is a recognised outcome.","A child at home makes this a safeguarding matter as well as a domestic abuse one."],"decide":[{"c":"Assess fully, advise police he is not safe to release, request an urgent Mental Health Act assessment in custody, share the risk so Kerry is warned, make safeguarding and MARAC referrals, and advise no driving.","r":"Right: it treats the risk as high, uses the law that fits, and protects his wife, the rival and the child.","best":true},{"c":"Advise release home if he promises not to go to the hospital.","r":"Unsafe: he has a fixed delusion, ongoing intent, no insight and recent weapon carrying. A promise does not reduce risk.","best":false},{"c":"Keep everything confidential because he has not consented to sharing.","r":"Wrong: GMC guidance permits sharing without consent where that is needed to stop someone being killed or seriously hurt. Share what is needed with police and so his wife is warned.","best":false}],"law":["Explain at the start that what he says may be shared if someone could be at serious risk; record that you did.","GMC confidentiality: sharing without consent is justified where it is needed to protect others from death or serious harm; share the minimum needed, with police and so Kerry can be warned. Record the reasons.","He is under arrest, so Section 136 does not apply. If he will not accept admission, request an urgent MHA assessment in custody (AMHP and two doctors, one Section 12 approved).","Advise the custody sergeant that he needs an appropriate adult for any interview and may not be fit for interview now. DVLA: he must stop driving and notify them of a psychotic illness."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I need to be open with you. Most of what we discuss stays within the care team. But if I think someone could be seriously hurt, I may have to pass that on."},{"to":"Custody sergeant","s":"In my opinion he has a psychotic illness with fixed jealous beliefs, took a knife to confront the man, can't rule out using it and still intends to. He should not be released. I'm requesting an urgent Mental Health Act assessment."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Gareth Pryce, 36, arrested with a knife en route to confront his wife's colleague. Eight months of delusional jealousy, voices including commands, heavy drinking, no insight, ongoing intent, an 8-year-old at home. I think he needs admission; I've asked for an MHA assessment and told police about the risk to his wife and the rival."}],"write":"Custody suite assessment, 08:30. 36M, warehouse supervisor. Arrested ~23:30 speeding, kitchen knife in car, said he was going to stop wife's affair. Confidentiality limits explained.\nHPC: ~9 months ago began hearing male voice at night (2nd person: 'she's laughing at you'); ~8 months fixed belief wife (Kerry, ED nurse) is having affair with senior charge nurse. 'Evidence': new perfume, silver car in street, late finishes; smells aftershave on her. Checks her phone nightly, her underwear, sits in hospital car park on her shifts. Found nothing; belief unshaken (100%). Last night voice said 'go now, catch them'. Voice quieter now.\nMood low, poor sleep, hopeless 'if she leaves there's nothing'; no suicidal plan. Alcohol 6-8 cans nightly x 6 months; 3 cans before driving; mild tremor this morning. Denies drugs.\nForensic: grabbed Kerry's wrist taking phone 1 month ago; no police contact; no firearms. Daughter Ellie, 8, at paternal grandmother's last night.\nRisk: knife 'to make him admit it'; cannot rule out using it; still intends to confront them. Denies intent to harm Kerry or self. HIGH risk to rival, wife and bystanders; risk to self moderate.\nInsight: none.\nImpression: psychotic disorder with delusional jealousy, alcohol contributing. Ddx alcohol-related psychosis, organic, depressive psychosis.\nPlan: not safe for release; urgent MHA assessment requested (AMHP); police informed of risk to wife and rival (public interest, GMC), so Kerry warned; safeguarding referral for Ellie; DASH/MARAC via police; CIWA-Ar monitoring by custody HCP; appropriate adult for any interview; no driving, DVLA advised. Consultant informed.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Say the limits of confidentiality before he says anything important.","Start with last night, then go back to how the belief began: the order of voices and jealousy matters.","Ask what he has done, not just what he believes: checking, following, confronting.","Ask about the knife at least twice, calmly: 'And if he'd said no?'","Ask about every person at risk: wife, rival, colleagues, child, himself.","Tell him plainly what you will share and with whom."],"next":"An AMHP and a Section 12 doctor assess him that afternoon; he is detained under Section 2 and treated on an acute ward with risperidone and an alcohol withdrawal plan. Police warn Kerry, who stays with her sister with Ellie. A MARAC meeting agrees a safety plan. After six weeks the voices have gone, but he still doubts her; the team advise continued separation and forensic input before any contact.","marks":["Explained the limits of confidentiality and built enough rapport to talk.","Mapped the jealous belief, its evidence, conviction and checking.","Elicited hallucinations, mood and alcohol, and the order of onset.","Pressed calmly about the knife and current intent.","Assessed risk to wife, rival, bystanders, child and himself.","Gave a clear plan with information sharing and no release."],"pitfalls":["Using up the ten minutes on the affair so the knife never gets asked about.","Accepting the first 'I don't know' about the knife.","Asking 'You weren't going to hurt anyone, were you?'","Promising confidentiality, then having to break it.","Forgetting the child, alcohol withdrawal in custody, or driving."],"refs":["1. GMC: Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 14 (assessment) (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG10: Violence and aggression (2015; checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK custody suites, a liaison and diversion team and a custody healthcare practitioner work alongside police. Your opinion on risk and need for hospital is what the custody sergeant acts on, so state it clearly, in writing.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-36","n":1436,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💉","title":"Refusing his depot and losing weight: a focused mental state at his door","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 11:00, community visit","where":"Supported accommodation, his bedroom doorway","text":"Kofi Mensah, 35, has paranoid schizophrenia and moved into supported accommodation eight weeks ago after an admission for relapse. For three weeks he has kept away from staff and residents, eaten only the odd takeaway and lost weight. He refused his last depot and will not let the CMHT nurse in. His care coordinator asks you to see him today and describe his psychotic symptoms and risk."},"task":"In about ten minutes, assess his mental state with a focus on psychotic symptoms and signs, grade the risk and his insight, and agree the next contact.","first60":["Read the last discharge summary: depot name, dose, due date, and whether he is on a CTO.","Ask the support worker what they have seen: meals, weight, sleep, talking to himself, who he avoids.","Check your own safety: a colleague nearby, the door clear, phone charged, someone knows where you are.","Look before you speak: clothes, weight, the room, food packaging, covered vents or windows.","Plan your ten minutes: rapport and food first, then beliefs, voices, mood, risk, insight, plan."],"ask":["'What changed for you about three weeks ago?'","'Staff say you only eat takeaways now. What's wrong with the food here?'","'What happened with the last injection?'","'How sure are you about that, out of a hundred?'","'Do you ever hear people talking when no one is there? What do they say?'","'Has food or drink tasted or smelt strange?'","'Can anyone put thoughts into your head, take them out, or know what you are thinking?'","'Have you thought of hurting yourself, or anyone here?'"],"recognise":["A fixed belief that staff contaminate shared food, held against the evidence of his weight loss: a persecutory delusion driving self-neglect.","The belief that the depot is used to control him, which began after a painful injection: possible delusional perception, a first-rank symptom.","A voice warning him off the nurse is a command-type hallucination linked to disengagement: ask what else it tells him.","Glancing at the vent and pausing mid-sentence suggest he is hallucinating during the interview; record it as an observation.","Feeling that people overhear his thoughts or talk about him: separate real sounds through walls from true hallucinations and from delusions of reference.","Absent insight with refusal of all treatment and weight loss: relapse with rising risk of self-neglect and of disengaging completely."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete a focused psychotic MSE and risk check, offer an oral option he chooses, and discuss today with the consultant and care coordinator, including whether the team should ask for a Mental Health Act assessment.","r":"Right: you capture the evidence of relapse, keep him engaged, and escalate on the same day with a lawful route ready.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him the depot is not optional and that he will be sectioned if he refuses again.","r":"Wrong: a threat confirms his fear that treatment is control, ends engagement and is not how the Act works.","best":false},{"c":"Accept that he has capacity to refuse and close the visit until his next depot date.","r":"Unsafe: capacity to refuse does not remove the risk from relapse and weight loss. Escalate and plan, even if he can refuse.","best":false}],"law":["Check whether he is on a Community Treatment Order. If so, the responsible clinician can recall him to hospital under Section 17E when treatment is needed and there is risk.","If he is not on a CTO, ongoing refusal with weight loss and absent insight may justify a Mental Health Act assessment, arranged through an AMHP.","Assess capacity for the depot decision and record it: refusal by a capacitous person still needs a risk plan.","Share information with his support staff only as far as needed for his safety, and tell him what you will share."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Kofi, it sounds frightening to think your food isn't safe. I'm not here to force anything. I want to understand what's been going on."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You don't have to agree with me that you're unwell. Could we agree on something that helps your sleep and keeps you eating?"},{"to":"Care coordinator","s":"He has a clear relapse: a persecutory delusion about food and the depot, a voice warning him off the nurse, weight loss and no insight. No current thoughts of harm. I'm calling Dr Baines now."}],"write":"Community visit, supported accommodation. 35M, paranoid schizophrenia, discharged 8/52 ago; depot refused 3/52.\nA&B: thin, unshaven, clothes loose; guarded, poor eye contact; repeatedly glances at ceiling vent and pauses as if listening.\nSpeech: slow, coherent; no formal thought disorder.\nMood: 'fine'; not objectively depressed; poor sleep.\nThought: persecutory delusion that staff contaminate shared food (eats sealed takeaways, ~1/day); believes depot used to control him, started after painful injection (?delusional perception); feels staff talk about him (reference); 95% convinced.\nPerception: one male voice, 2nd person, warns him not to trust the nurse; no commands to harm; no olfactory or gustatory hallucinations.\nThought interference/passivity: denies insertion, withdrawal, control; believes staff 'know what I'm thinking' (? broadcast).\nCognition: alert, orientated.\nInsight: absent; accepts poor sleep only.\nRisk: self-neglect (weight loss, low intake) moderate-high; disengagement high (plans to move out without telling staff); denies suicidal thoughts or harm to others; vulnerability to exploitation.\nPlan: discussed with Dr Baines (consultant) and care coordinator today; CTO status checked; offered oral aripiprazole; sealed food via staff; weight and bloods requested; daily visits by same nurse; MHA assessment if intake falls further or risk rises; crisis line given.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Start with food and safety, his concerns, not 'your illness'.","Ask about conviction and evidence for every belief, without arguing.","Screen all senses: taste and smell matter when he fears poisoning.","Comment on what you see: weight loss, self-care, eye contact, listening behaviour.","Ask directly about harm to self, harm to staff and plans to leave.","End with a concrete offer and a named next visit, not 'we'll be in touch'."],"next":"Dr Baines confirms Kofi is not on a CTO. Kofi agrees to try aripiprazole tablets in sealed packs and lets the same nurse visit daily. Staff give him sealed foods. His weight is checked weekly; if it falls further or he stops eating, the team will ask an AMHP to arrange a Mental Health Act assessment.","marks":["Opened calmly, explained why he came and checked consent to talk.","Commented on weight loss, self-care, eye contact and listening behaviour.","Elicited the food-poisoning and depot beliefs with conviction and evidence.","Screened reference, thought interference, passivity and hallucinations in several senses, including commands.","Assessed mood, sleep, self-harm, harm to staff and plans to leave.","Rated insight, offered a choice, and set a next contact and same-day escalation."],"pitfalls":["Arguing with the delusion or promising the food is safe.","Forgetting smell and taste hallucinations in someone afraid of poisoning.","Not asking what the voice says, or whether it tells him to act.","Missing his plan to leave the accommodation without telling anyone.","Leaving without a dated next contact or without telling the consultant today."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice: CTOs and recall (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: aripiprazole; zuclopenthixol decanoate (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG10 and RCPsych guidance on physical health in severe mental illness (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, community patients can refuse a depot unless a lawful route applies. The care coordinator is your first call, then the consultant. Know whether the patient is on a CTO before you knock.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-37","n":1437,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🩹","title":"Third overdose in six weeks: can she go home?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 09:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Side room, emergency department","text":"Leah Cartwright, 23, a retail assistant, came in last night after swallowing a handful of diazepam. It is her third overdose in six weeks. The emergency team have treated her, judged her medically fit, and ask whether discharge home is safe. You are the liaison psychiatry doctor."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of this overdose and the pattern before it, judge her current risk, and decide with her between admission, home treatment and discharge.","first60":["Confirm with the emergency team she is medically fit: paracetamol and salicylate levels done, no other co-ingestion, GCS normal.","Read the two earlier liaison notes: what was planned, and what happened to the follow-up.","Ask the nurse how she has been overnight, whether anyone has visited, and if she has tried to leave.","Book a side room with no ligature points, and tell security she is waiting for you, without posting a guard.","Plan ten minutes: this overdose, intent now, the pattern, mood and drink, supports, then the decision."],"ask":["'Take me back to yesterday evening. What was happening?'","'How many tablets, and whose were they?'","'Did you wait until you were alone? Did you leave a note or message?'","'When you took them, what did you hope would happen?'","'How do you feel about waking up here?'","'Are you having thoughts of dying right now? Any plan?'","'What happened the other two times?'","'Who could be with you if you went home today?'"],"recognise":["Three overdoses in six weeks: rising frequency is a risk in itself, whatever the intent of each one.","Planning, waiting to be alone and wishing she had not woken show high suicidal intent this time.","Ongoing thoughts of dying after waking signal continuing risk, not resolution.","Heavy nightly drinking, poor eating and poor sleep point to a depressive episode, alcohol misuse or both.","Self-cutting since her teens and overdoses after rows suggest emotional instability: screen for trauma, but do not label her in A&E.","Access to her mother's diazepam is a means that must be removed before any plan is safe."],"decide":[{"c":"Offer a short informal admission with a clear purpose, given escalating overdoses, high intent and ongoing thoughts of dying; ask for a Mental Health Act assessment if she refuses and the risk stays high.","r":"Right: the risk is high and current, home has no safe supervision, and admission has a defined goal and end.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge her home with a crisis card, as overdoses after rows are impulsive and attention-seeking.","r":"Wrong: this one was planned, she wishes she had died and still thinks of dying. Calling it attention-seeking is dismissive and unsafe.","best":false},{"c":"Complete a risk-scoring tool and admit only if the score is high.","r":"Wrong: NICE advises against using risk tools or scales to decide who is treated or discharged. Use a full psychosocial assessment.","best":false}],"law":["She is not an inpatient, so Section 5(2) does not apply in A&E. If she tries to leave before assessment and lacks capacity, staff may act under the Mental Capacity Act in her best interests.","If she refuses admission and the risk remains high, request a Mental Health Act assessment through the AMHP service.","Ask her consent to involve her mother; if she declines, you may still listen to family and share information if there is a serious risk to her life.","Ask whether she cares for any children: if so, consider a safeguarding referral."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"It sounds like everything fell apart when he left, and the tablets felt like the only way out. I'm really glad you're here to talk."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Leah, I'm worried about how unsafe you still feel. I think you need more help than going home alone tonight. Could we try a short stay on the ward?"},{"to":"A&E registrar","s":"She's medically fit but at high suicide risk: third overdose in six weeks, high intent, still thinking of dying. She's agreed to an informal admission. Please keep her here with 1:1 until the bed is ready."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, ED. 23F, retail assistant. 3rd OD in 6/52. Medically fit per ED (paracetamol/salicylate levels negative).\nIndex OD: ~20 x diazepam 5 mg (mother's supply) last night after partner left; waited until alone; no note; found by flatmate; wished she had not woken.\nCurrent: ongoing thoughts of dying, no specific plan today; regret at survival.\nPattern: 2 previous ODs in 6/52 after rows; self-cutting since age 14, last 2/52 ago.\nMood: low, poor sleep and appetite, empty; no psychotic features.\nAlcohol: ~1 bottle wine most nights; no withdrawal history. No drugs.\nSocial: lives with flatmate; mother nearby; no children; working but off sick.\nProtective: mother, job, cat; some future orientation.\nFormulation: high and current risk (escalating frequency, high intent, ongoing ideation, alcohol, recent loss, access to means).\nPlan: informal admission agreed; 1:1 nursing in ED until transfer; mother to remove diazepam with Leah's consent; CIWA-Ar on ward; GP informed within 24h; MHA assessment if she withdraws consent and risk persists.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Check medical fitness and paracetamol level before you start.","Get the facts of this overdose: tablets, source, planning, alone, note, how found.","Ask how she feels about surviving and whether thoughts of dying continue now.","Map the pattern: three overdoses, cutting, what triggers them, what helped.","Ask about alcohol, mood and trauma briefly but directly.","Make the decision with her, explain why, and name the next step and who you will tell."],"next":"Leah agrees to a short informal admission. Her mother removes the diazepam from the house. On the ward she is assessed for depression and alcohol use, starts weekly therapy contact and leaves after nine days with a safety plan, home treatment visits and a CMHT appointment.","marks":["Confirmed medical fitness and set a calm, private tone.","Took a clear account of this overdose: tablets, source, planning, isolation, note, discovery.","Assessed intent, regret and current thoughts of dying with plans.","Mapped the pattern of earlier overdoses and self-harm.","Screened mood, alcohol, drugs and trauma, and identified supports.","Made and explained a reasoned decision with her, with the legal fallback and who will be told."],"pitfalls":["Calling it attention-seeking or 'just impulsive'.","Not asking how she feels about waking up.","Relying on a risk score instead of the history.","Forgetting where the tablets came from and who will remove them.","Leaving the decision vague: 'we'll see how she is later'."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225: self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","4. Consensus statement on information sharing and suicide prevention, DHSC (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK A&E, a person who self-harms should get a psychosocial assessment, not a risk score. Write down what she did, what she meant, and what she thinks now. Senior support is expected for admission decisions.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-38","n":1438,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"🕷️","title":"Frightened and shaking two days after his fracture: delirium tremens","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 08:45, liaison psychiatry","where":"Bay on the orthopaedic ward","text":"Desmond Clarke, 70, a retired bus driver, fell two days ago and broke his wrist, which was fixed in theatre. His CT head was normal. He is waiting for an occupational therapy review. Overnight he became frightened, restless and shaky. Admission bloods: normal white cells, slightly raised CRP, raised MCV, GGT and ALP. The surgical team ask you to see him."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a short focused history, assess his mental state and physical signs, reach a working diagnosis, and hand over a clear plan to the surgical team.","first60":["Read the obs chart: pulse, temperature, BP, saturations, capillary glucose, and the trend since admission.","Check the drug chart for thiamine or Pabrinex, any benzodiazepine, opioids and anything anticholinergic.","Ask the nurse what happened overnight: hallucinations, pulling at lines, falls, seizure, any alcohol history on the clerking.","Look at the bloods again: MCV, GGT and ALP fit alcohol; check sodium, magnesium, phosphate and clotting if done.","Turn the lights up, lower the noise, and stand where he can see you."],"ask":["'What are you seeing on the wall? Can you feel anything on your skin?'","'Do you know where you are, and what day it is?'","'Could you go through the months backwards for me, starting at December?'","'On a normal day, what do you drink, and how much?'","'When did you last have a drink?'","'Have you ever had the shakes, seen things or had a fit when you stopped before?'","'Do you feel someone here wants to hurt you?'","'Did you hit your head when you fell?'"],"recognise":["Visual hallucinations of small creatures, fear and picking at sheets, with disorientation: a delirium.","Tremor, sweating, pulse 112 and temperature 37.8 two to three days after the last drink: autonomic overactivity of alcohol withdrawal.","Raised MCV, GGT and ALP support long-term heavy drinking.","Inattention (cannot do months backwards) and fluctuation separate delirium from a primary psychosis.","Confusion, unsteadiness or abnormal eye movements in a drinker mean presumed Wernicke's encephalopathy until treated.","A raised CRP and fever also need infection excluded (chest, urine, wound), and a fall in an older drinker keeps subdural haematoma on the list."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens), advise symptom-triggered benzodiazepine with CIWA-Ar, IV Pabrinex now, electrolyte correction, an infection screen and escalation criteria, and hand over to the surgical team.","r":"Right: treats the emergency, protects against Wernicke's, keeps other causes in view and gives the ward a clear plan.","best":true},{"c":"Give haloperidol 5 mg IM for agitation and review in the morning.","r":"Wrong: an antipsychotic alone does not treat withdrawal, lowers the seizure threshold and can prolong QT. Benzodiazepines are the treatment.","best":false},{"c":"Call it a late-onset psychosis and refer to the old age team.","r":"Wrong: inattention, disorientation, autonomic signs and timing point to withdrawal delirium, which can be fatal if missed.","best":false}],"law":["He is likely to lack capacity for treatment decisions while delirious: assess, record it, and give treatment as a best-interests decision under the MCA.","If he tries to leave and needs to be kept on the ward for more than a short time, the team should consider an urgent DoLS authorisation.","The Mental Health Act is rarely needed for delirium on a medical ward; physical treatment for a physical cause sits under the MCA.","DVLA: alcohol dependence must be reported, and he should not drive until cleared."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're safe, Desmond. You're in hospital after your fall. What you're seeing is frightening, and it's part of an illness we can treat."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Your body is reacting to suddenly going without alcohol. The medicine and the vitamins will help you feel calmer and safer."},{"to":"Surgical FY2","s":"This is delirium tremens: last drink about 60 hours ago, visual hallucinations, disorientated, pulse 112, temp 37.8. Please start CIWA-Ar with symptom-triggered lorazepam, IV Pabrinex before any glucose, and send magnesium, phosphate and a septic screen. Call your registrar and outreach if NEWS rises or he has a seizure."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 70M, D2 post ORIF L wrist after fall; CT head normal. Overnight: fearful, restless, tremulous.\nAlcohol (patient, partial; wife to confirm): whisky ~1 bottle every 2 days for years; last drink evening before fall (~60h); morning shakes relieved by drink; no known previous seizures.\nMSE: sweating, coarse tremor, picking at sheets; frightened; speech fragmented; visual hallucinations of spiders on wall and tactile sensations on arms; believes nurses whispering about him; disorientated to time and place; unable to do months backwards; fluctuating per nursing staff; no insight.\nObs: HR 112, T 37.8, BP 158/92; no nystagmus seen; gait not tested (bed rest).\nBloods: MCV, GGT, ALP raised; CRP mildly raised; WCC normal.\nImpression: alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens). Ddx: infection, subdural, hypoglycaemia, Wernicke's encephalopathy, hepatic encephalopathy.\nCapacity: lacks capacity for treatment decisions now; treat in best interests (MCA).\nPlan (handed over to surgical FY2): CIWA-Ar hourly; symptom-triggered lorazepam (age, LFTs); IV Pabrinex 2 pairs TDS before glucose; capillary glucose, U&E, Mg, phosphate, clotting; septic screen; ECG; repeat CT if focal signs or no improvement; quiet lit side room, 1:1 if needed; seizure and falls precautions; escalate to outreach if NEWS rises. Liaison review tomorrow. Wife to be contacted.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Reassure and orientate first, then ask what he is seeing.","Pin down the drink: what, how much, how long, and the time of the last one.","Test attention with one quick task, not a full cognitive battery.","Name the signs you see: tremor, sweating, picking, pulse and temperature.","Give the diagnosis and differentials in one line each.","Hand over a specific plan with escalation triggers."],"next":"Desmond's wife confirms a bottle of whisky every two days. He receives Pabrinex and symptom-triggered lorazepam, magnesium is replaced and his chest X-ray is clear. By day 4 he is orientated and calm. The alcohol care team see him before discharge and refer him to the community alcohol service.","marks":["Reassured and orientated him calmly before questioning.","Took a focused alcohol history including timing of the last drink and past withdrawal.","Tested orientation and attention with a brief task.","Elicited visual and tactile hallucinations and persecutory ideas.","Commented on tremor, sweating, tachycardia and fever.","Diagnosed delirium tremens with differentials and handed over a safe plan."],"pitfalls":["Calling it psychosis or dementia without testing attention.","Prescribing an antipsychotic alone for agitation.","Forgetting thiamine before glucose.","Missing infection or a subdural because the alcohol story fits.","Giving full-dose chlordiazepoxide to an older man with abnormal liver tests without thinking."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100: alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG103: delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Pabrinex, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, alcohol misuse and dependence (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK hospitals, alcohol withdrawal is usually managed with a symptom-triggered CIWA-Ar protocol and IV Pabrinex. Most trusts have an alcohol care team: bleep them early.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-39","n":1439,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🚓","title":"Exposing himself near a playground: causes and risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:20, duty psychiatry","where":"Quiet assessment room, emergency department","text":"Police have brought Kieran Holt, 38, a delivery driver, to A&E. He is alleged to have exposed his genitals to a group of primary school children near a park this afternoon. Officers want a psychiatric opinion before they decide what happens next. Two officers wait outside the room."},"task":"In about ten minutes, explore what led to this behaviour, screen for mental illness, substances and organic causes, assess the risk to children and to himself, and give the police a clear opinion.","first60":["Ask the officers what is alleged, whether he is under arrest, and what they need from you: mental disorder, risk, fitness for custody.","Check the record for any mental health contact, learning disability, head injury or neurological history.","Agree with the officers where they will wait and how you will alert them; sit nearest the door.","Prepare your confidentiality statement: what you will have to share, and why.","Plan ten minutes: the incident, sexual history, illness screen, substances, background, risk, then the opinion."],"ask":["'Talk me through today, from the start.'","'Had you planned to go there? Had you had any drink?'","'Were you sexually aroused? Did you touch yourself?'","'Has this happened before, caught or not?'","'Have you ever looked at sexual images of children online?'","'Have you ever touched a child sexually, or thought about it?'","'Do you have contact with children, at home or through work?'","'Since your arrest, has suicide crossed your mind?'"],"recognise":["Urges building for weeks, a planned trip to the park, arousal at the children's reaction and two earlier episodes: exhibitionism, a paraphilic pattern, not a one-off.","Viewing sexual images of under-age girls widens the concern: a sexual interest in children raises the risk of escalation.","No mania, psychosis, cognitive decline or new neurological signs makes illness-driven disinhibition unlikely.","Drinking before each episode is a disinhibiting factor and a treatment target, not an excuse.","Shame and suicidal thoughts after arrest for a sexual offence are a real and recognised risk.","A delivery job brings him near schools and homes: access to children matters for the risk plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise the police that there is no mental illness needing admission, share the risk to children and his suicide risk with custody staff, and recommend specialist forensic follow-up and a children's safeguarding referral.","r":"Right: an honest opinion, information shared lawfully for child protection, his safety covered, and a route to treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Keep his disclosures confidential because he is your patient and asked for help.","r":"Wrong: a serious risk to children overrides confidentiality. You told him the limits at the start; share what is needed.","best":false},{"c":"Admit him to a psychiatric ward to keep children safe until things settle.","r":"Wrong: there is no mental illness requiring hospital care. Admission would not be lawful or therapeutic, and it would delay the criminal process.","best":false}],"law":["Explain the limits of confidentiality at the start: risk to children or others will be shared.","GMC guidance allows disclosure without consent when it is needed to protect others from a risk of serious harm, such as abuse of children.","Indecent exposure is an offence under the Sexual Offences Act 2003; conviction can lead to notification requirements and MAPPA management.","Under PACE, a person in custody with a mental disorder or vulnerability needs an appropriate adult; say whether you think he does."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Before we start: most of what you tell me stays confidential, but if I'm worried about children or anyone's safety, I'll have to share that. Is that clear?"},{"to":"Patient","s":"Kieran, wanting help is important. There is treatment for urges like this, though the police will also need to be involved."},{"to":"Police officer","s":"I've found no mental illness needing hospital care, and he doesn't need an appropriate adult. He describes a pattern of urges and earlier episodes, so I'm making a children's safeguarding referral. He's had suicidal thoughts since arrest: please tell the custody nurse and keep him on enhanced observation."}],"write":"Duty psychiatry, ED. 38M delivery driver, brought by police; alleged exposure to primary school children near park today. Confidentiality limits explained and understood.\nIncident: urges building for weeks; drove to park deliberately; exposed himself; aroused by children's reaction; no masturbation in view; left when shouted at. Drank 4 cans beforehand.\nPrevious: 2 similar episodes in past year, not caught. Views sexual images of under-age girls online (months). Denies contact offending or contact urges toward children.\nPsychosexual: brief adult relationships; lives alone; no children; no work with children; delivers near schools.\nMental state: anxious, ashamed; no features of mania, psychosis or depression syndrome; no cognitive concerns; no head injury or epilepsy.\nSubstances: alcohol 4-6 cans most evenings; before each episode. No drugs.\nForensic: none known.\nRisk: further exposure high; escalation concern given images of children; risk to children via access through work. Suicide: thoughts since arrest, no plan; protective: sister.\nOpinion to police: no mental illness requiring admission; no appropriate adult needed; risk information shared (GMC public-interest disclosure); custody to be told of suicide risk.\nPlan: children's safeguarding referral today; GP letter; recommend specialist forensic psychosexual assessment; alcohol service; Stop It Now information.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 duty psychiatry","make":["State the limits of confidentiality before any detail.","Ask about arousal and masturbation plainly, without embarrassment.","Ask about earlier episodes and images of children directly.","Screen mania, psychosis, depression, learning disability and organic causes quickly.","Ask about access to children and suicide risk after arrest.","Give the police a clear opinion: illness, risk, appropriate adult, who will be told."],"next":"Kieran is returned to custody with a written opinion. The custody nurse places him on enhanced observation. A children's safeguarding referral is made, and the police search his devices. He is charged, bailed with conditions, and referred to a forensic psychosexual service and an alcohol service by his GP.","marks":["Explained the limits of confidentiality and took a non-judgemental stance.","Explored the incident: planning, arousal, masturbation, alcohol and aftermath.","Asked about earlier episodes, images of children and contact offending.","Screened for mental illness, learning disability, organic causes and substances.","Assessed risk of repetition, escalation, access to children and suicide.","Gave the police a clear opinion and named the safeguarding and follow-up steps."],"pitfalls":["Avoiding the arousal and images questions because they feel awkward.","Promising full confidentiality.","Missing suicide risk after an arrest for a sexual offence.","Treating it as a mental health crisis and suggesting admission.","Forgetting to ask about access to children through work or family."],"refs":["1. GMC: Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (checked Nov 2026)","2. Sexual Offences Act 2003 (checked Nov 2026)","3. Home Office: PACE Code C, appropriate adults (checked Nov 2026)","4. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK police often ask A&E psychiatrists whether a detained person has a mental disorder and needs an appropriate adult. Answer those questions directly and in writing, and share child-protection concerns without delay.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-40","n":1440,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏳️‍⚧️","title":"Asking to start oestrogen: a respectful gender history","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00, general adult outpatients","where":"Outpatient clinic room","text":"The referral is for Sam Kowalski, 29, a software tester registered male at birth, who has asked the GP to prescribe oestrogen. The GP would like a psychiatric opinion before referring on. In the waiting room the patient introduces herself as Sophie."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a history to reach a diagnosis, consider the important differentials, check mental health and safety, and explain the referral pathway. A formal mental state write-up is not needed.","first60":["Read the GP letter: what was asked, any past mental health history, any medicines or bloods.","Check the record shows the right name and title; if not, note it to update with her consent.","Remind yourself of the question: is there gender incongruence, and is anything else better explaining it or needing treatment?","Know the local pathway: GP referral to an NHS Gender Dysphoria Clinic, and current waits.","Plan ten minutes: name, story since childhood, social transition, goals, differentials, mood and risk, plan."],"ask":["'What name and pronouns would you like me to use?'","'When did you first feel your gender didn't match your body?'","'How did you play and dress as a child? What happened at puberty?'","'How do you feel about your body now?'","'Has wearing women's clothes ever been sexually exciting for you?'","'What do you hope hormones will do? Any thoughts about surgery or fertility?'","'Have you taken or bought any hormones yourself?'","'How's your mood been? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'"],"recognise":["A felt female identity since about age six, persisting through puberty to now, with distress about male features: a long-standing, consistent gender incongruence.","Living full time as a woman for two years shows a settled social transition.","No sexual arousal with clothing argues against fetishistic transvestism.","No psychotic beliefs about the body and no preoccupation with a single 'defect' argue against psychosis and body dysmorphic disorder.","Low mood linked to long waits and family rejection is common and treatable; it does not cast doubt on her identity.","Looking at online hormones is a safety issue: unmonitored oestrogen carries clot and dosing risks."],"decide":[{"c":"Record gender incongruence with no better explanation, support a GP referral to a Gender Dysphoria Clinic, address her low mood and the risks of online hormones, and offer support while she waits.","r":"Right: respectful, accurate, and focused on the real psychiatric role: excluding other causes and supporting mental health.","best":true},{"c":"Start oestradiol today to stop her buying hormones online.","r":"Wrong: gender-affirming hormones are initiated through the specialist pathway, not general psychiatry. Harm reduction means advice and GP safety bloods if she is self-medicating.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to see a therapist first to explore whether she really is transgender.","r":"Wrong: therapy aimed at changing gender identity is harmful and is not a gate to referral. Offer therapy for mood if she wants it.","best":false}],"law":["The Equality Act 2010 protects people on the grounds of gender reassignment; she needs no diagnosis or surgery to be covered.","She can change her name by deed poll or statutory declaration; NHS records can be updated at her request.","Consent: as an adult with capacity she makes her own decisions; confirm capacity is not in doubt, and record it.","Do not share her trans status with others, including family, without her consent."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you, Sophie. I'm not here to decide who you are. I want to understand your experience and make sure you get the right help."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I can't prescribe it myself today, Sophie, but I can explain the route and what can help while you wait."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Sophie has a long-standing, consistent gender incongruence with no evidence of psychosis, body dysmorphic disorder or fetishistic cross-dressing. Please refer to the Gender Dysphoria Clinic. Mild low mood: Talking Therapies offered."}],"write":"General adult OPD. Sophie Kowalski (registered Sam), 29, she/her, software tester. GP referral: requesting oestrogen.\nGender history: felt she was a girl from ~age 6; preferred girls' clothes and play, hid this at school; distressed at puberty (voice, body hair); persistent female identity since.\nSocial transition: living full time as a woman for 2 years; uses Sophie at work and socially; records still in old name.\nCross-dressing: never sexually arousing. Sexuality: attracted to men; current partner (Josh, supportive).\nBody: distress about facial hair, voice and genitals; wants breast development and softer skin; no fixed single 'defect'.\nGoals: oestrogen; considering vaginoplasty later; aware of effects on fertility; open to sperm storage.\nSelf-sourced hormones: researched online, none bought.\nMental health: low mood for 6 months related to waits and parents' rejection; sleep fair; no psychotic symptoms; no suicidal thoughts or plans; no self-harm; alcohol minimal; no drugs. Harassment on public transport.\nImpression: gender incongruence (ICD-11 HA60), long-standing and consistent; no better psychiatric explanation. Mild low mood.\nPlan: letter to GP supporting GDC referral; advised against unregulated hormones, GP safety bloods if this changes; Talking Therapies self-referral details; peer support and voice therapy information; records name update offered; crisis line given.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask name and pronouns first, and use them throughout.","Take the gender story in time order: childhood, puberty, adulthood.","Ask about arousal with cross-dressing directly but neutrally.","Check for psychosis and body dysmorphia without implying doubt.","Ask about self-sourced hormones, mood, self-harm and harassment.","Explain the pathway honestly: who prescribes, likely waits, what helps meanwhile."],"next":"The GP refers Sophie to the Gender Dysphoria Clinic. She self-refers to Talking Therapies for low mood, joins a local peer group, and updates her NHS records. She tells her GP if she starts any hormones so safety bloods can be done.","marks":["Asked and used her chosen name and pronouns.","Took a gender history from childhood through puberty to now.","Explored social transition, body feelings and goals, including fertility.","Excluded fetishistic cross-dressing, psychosis and body dysmorphic disorder.","Checked mood, suicidal thoughts, self-sourced hormones and harassment.","Explained the referral pathway and support while waiting."],"pitfalls":["Using the registered name or wrong pronouns after being told.","Treating the appointment as a test of whether she is 'really' trans.","Avoiding the arousal question, or asking it in a judgemental way.","Promising hormones or a short wait.","Forgetting to ask about hormones bought online."],"refs":["1. NHS England: service specification for adult gender dysphoria clinics (checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: gender incongruence of adolescence or adulthood, HA60 (checked Nov 2026)","3. Equality Act 2010 (checked Nov 2026)","4. RCPsych position statement on supporting transgender and gender-diverse people (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, gender incongruence is not treated as a mental illness, and psychiatry is not a gatekeeper for referral. Your role is to support mental health and exclude anything that better explains the presentation. Ask pronouns early.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-41","n":1441,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Coercive control at home: taking the history and naming it","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:40, general adult outpatients","where":"Community mental health clinic, interview room","text":"Natalia Kowalski, 37, has been referred by her GP. The practice noticed she has missed her asthma review, her contraception review and her cervical screening in a row. Her husband, Marek, is known at the surgery as angry and controlling, and the GP suspects abuse. The GP note says: no letters to the house, phone only on weekdays from a withheld number. She has come alone and has about ten minutes before she must collect her daughter from nursery."},"task":"Take a focused history of the abuse Natalia is experiencing, screen for risk to her and her children, and explain clearly what kind of abuse this is, in about ten minutes.","first60":["Read the GP letter for the safe-contact instruction: withheld number, weekdays, no letters home.","Check the record for children in the household and any previous safeguarding alerts.","See her alone. If anyone came with her, see her on her own for at least part of the visit, with a routine reason.","Have the National Domestic Abuse Helpline number (0808 2000 247) and your local IDVA referral route to hand.","Know who your trust's safeguarding lead is today, in case the risk is high."],"ask":["'Is it all right to talk here? Does anyone know you've come today?'","'What has made it hard to get to your appointments?'","'Is there anything he stops you doing, or makes you ask permission for?'","'Who looks after the money? Do you have your own bank card?'","'Has he ever pushed, hit or grabbed you, or put his hands round your neck?'","'Has he ever pressured or forced you into sex, or sexual things you didn't want?'","'Has it got worse recently? Are you frightened of what he might do?'","'How are the children? Have they seen or heard any of this?'"],"recognise":["A pattern of missed health appointments, a partner who answers for her, and anxiety about time are common signs of coercive control.","Coercive control is a pattern, not one incident: isolation, monitoring, rules, humiliation, threats and control of money.","No hitting does not mean low risk. Escalation, threats about the children, jealousy and drinking all raise risk.","Non-fatal strangulation is one of the strongest warning signs of future homicide: always ask about it directly.","Self-blame ('I'm stupid, it's my fault') is a typical effect of long-term coercion, not a sign she is exaggerating.","Children who see or hear domestic abuse are victims of it in their own right under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a gentle, specific history, ask the key risk questions, name it as coercive control and a crime, and agree a safe contact route, the helpline and an IDVA referral, telling her honestly about the children's referral.","r":"Right: she understands what is happening, knows it is not her fault, has a safe way forward, and the children are protected openly.","best":true},{"c":"Advise her to leave him today and go to a refuge.","r":"Wrong: leaving is the most dangerous time and must be planned with specialist support. Pushing her may stop her coming back.","best":false},{"c":"Write to her at home with the outcome and copy the letter to her GP.","r":"Dangerous: he may read it. Use only the safe contact route she agreed and mark the record as sensitive.","best":false}],"law":["Repeated controlling or coercive behaviour by a partner is a criminal offence under section 76 of the Serious Crime Act 2015.","The Domestic Abuse Act 2021 defines abuse to include coercive control and economic abuse, and treats children who see or hear it as victims.","Sharing information without her consent is justified to protect children or when risk to her life is high (MARAC); tell her when you do, unless that raises the danger.","Clare's Law (the Domestic Violence Disclosure Scheme) lets her ask the police about his history."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing has a name. It's called coercive control: a pattern of taking away your money, your friends and your freedom. It's a form of abuse, it's a crime, and it is not your fault."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Because the children hear the shouting, I'll need to share that with our safeguarding team. I'll do it with you, not behind your back, and the aim is support, not taking anyone away."},{"to":"Safeguarding lead","s":"37-year-old woman, coercive control by husband for six years, escalating, no physical violence, threats about the children, two children aged 7 and 4 exposed. DASH done with IDVA. Safe contact: withheld number, weekdays only."}],"write":"General adult OPD. Seen alone. 37F, referred by GP: missed asthma, contraception and cervical screening reviews.\nSAFE CONTACT: mobile, withheld number, Mon-Fri 10:00-14:00. NO letters to home address. Record marked sensitive.\nHistory: husband Marek; change after son's birth 7 yrs ago, worse past 6 months. Keeps her bank card, checks messages nightly, location app on phone, cancels her appointments, stopped contact with sister, rules on clothes and going out, insults her in front of others. Shouts in her face, punches doors. Denies hitting, strangulation, sexual coercion. Threatened she would 'never see the kids' if she left. Drinks more lately.\nChildren: Oskar 7, Zofia 4. Not harmed directly; hear shouting; Oskar bedwetting again.\nEffect: low mood, anxiety, poor sleep, weight loss, self-blame. No suicidal thoughts (children protective). No thoughts of harming husband.\nImpression: coercive control with emotional and economic abuse. Depressive and anxiety symptoms secondary; review.\nPlan: named and explained; helpline 0808 2000 247 and 999 then 55 given; IDVA referral with consent; DASH with IDVA; children's safeguarding referral discussed with her and made today; GP informed by secure email; review 2 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Say why you are asking ('we ask everyone about home'), then ask specific, behavioural questions.","Get at least four concrete examples of control before you name it.","Ask about strangulation and sexual abuse in plain words, every time.","Name it clearly: coercive control, emotional and economic abuse, a crime, not her fault.","Agree the safe contact route before she leaves, and write it at the top of your note.","Be open about any referral for the children, and frame it as support."],"next":"Natalia agrees to the IDVA referral. The IDVA completes the DASH by phone two days later from a withheld number: high risk on professional judgement, so a MARAC is held. Children's social care offer early help; Oskar's school starts weekly check-ins. The GP rebooks her asthma review on a nursery morning. At review she has reopened contact with her sister.","marks":["Checked she was alone and safe, and explained confidentiality and its limits.","Elicited specific controlling behaviours, including economic abuse and monitoring.","Screened for physical abuse, strangulation and sexual abuse.","Asked about the children and the key risk items, including her own suicide risk.","Named coercive control clearly and said it is a crime and not her fault.","Agreed safe contact, the helpline, 999 then 55, and an IDVA referral."],"pitfalls":["Asking only 'does he hit you?' and calling it low risk when the answer is no.","Telling her to leave today instead of planning safety with a specialist.","Sending a letter or appointment text to the home address or her monitored phone.","Hiding the children's referral from her, or not making it.","Running out of time before naming the abuse."],"refs":["1. NICE PH50: domestic violence and abuse, multi-agency working (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. Domestic Abuse Act 2021 and statutory guidance (checked Nov 2026)","3. Serious Crime Act 2015, section 76: controlling or coercive behaviour (checked Nov 2026)","4. SafeLives: DASH risk checklist and MARAC guidance (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, coercive control without violence is a crime, and children who hear abuse are victims too. Ask every patient about home, see them alone, and never send letters home without checking it is safe.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-42","n":1442,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"🔥","title":"Fire in the hostel: a fire-setting history in custody","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 13:20, liaison and diversion","where":"Police custody suite, consultation room","text":"Leah Brennan, 18, was arrested this morning after setting fire to her room at a supported hostel for young people. Nobody was hurt, but the building was evacuated. The custody sergeant thinks she is mentally unwell and has asked the liaison and diversion psychiatrist to see her before any interview. An appropriate adult from the local scheme sits with her. You have about ten minutes before the custody nurse needs your advice."},"task":"Take a history focused on the fire-setting that leads to a working diagnosis, assess the risk of further fires, and give clear advice to the custody team, in about ten minutes.","first60":["Ask the custody sergeant what she is arrested for, and read the custody record for her behaviour, observations and any drugs found.","Check the trust record and the leaving-care team: past contact, diagnoses, care history.","Confirm the appropriate adult is present and stays throughout.","Plan the ABC in order: the build-up, the act itself, the aftermath, then the reason.","Know the AMHP duty number in case she needs a Mental Health Act assessment from custody."],"ask":["'Can you take me through this morning, from when you woke up?'","'What did you set alight, and with what?'","'When you lit it, what did you want the fire to do?'","'Once it was burning, what did you do? Did you tell anyone?'","'How do you feel about it now?'","'Have you ever set a fire before, even as a child?'","'Have you been hearing voices or noticing odd things lately?'","'If you went back to the hostel tonight, would you light another fire?'"],"recognise":["An ABC analysis (antecedents, behaviour, consequences) is the backbone of any fire-setting history.","A fire lit to 'clean poison' on a voice's instruction points to psychosis, not to fire interest or revenge.","Pyromania is rare: repeated, planned fires for excitement or tension relief, with no other motive. Exclude it by asking.","Staying near the fire, raising the alarm and showing concern for others lower the immediate danger; fleeing or watching with excitement raise it.","Ongoing command voices about fire mean a high risk of repeat fire-setting until treated.","Care leavers have high rates of trauma, substance use and exploitation; screen these as part of the background."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the ABC and motive, screen for psychosis and other causes, ask about past fires, and advise that she is unwell, not fit for interview, and needs a Mental Health Act assessment if she will not accept admission.","r":"Right: the history shows why she lit the fire, the risk is clear, and she is diverted to treatment with the police kept informed.","best":true},{"c":"Stick to her mental state and leave questions about the fire to the police.","r":"Wrong: the motive and the details of the fire are the key to both the diagnosis and the risk of another fire.","best":false},{"c":"Tell the sergeant she is fine to be interviewed once she has slept.","r":"Unsafe: active command voices and persecutory beliefs make her answers unreliable. She is not fit for interview now.","best":false}],"law":["PACE Code C: a mentally vulnerable person must have an appropriate adult for interview and key custody procedures.","Fitness to be detained and interviewed is decided by the custody healthcare professional; your view informs it.","If she refuses admission, an AMHP and two doctors (one section 12 approved) can assess her in custody for section 2 of the MHA.","Tell her before you start that this is not confidential from the custody team where it bears on her safety, care or others' safety."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not the police. I'm a doctor, here to see how you are and whether you need help. Some of what you tell me I may share with the custody team, if it affects your safety or other people's."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've told me, I think you're unwell rather than bad. A voice and frightening beliefs pushed you to do this. The first step is getting you somewhere safe to be treated."},{"to":"Custody sergeant","s":"Leah has active psychotic symptoms, including a voice telling her to burn things. She isn't fit to be interviewed now. I'm asking the AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment here this afternoon."}],"write":"Liaison and diversion, custody suite. Seen with appropriate adult. 18F, arrested on suspicion of arson (own room, supported hostel, no injuries).\nConfidentiality limits explained.\nA: 3 weeks believes staff filming her, radio messages; voice (male, 2nd person) says room poisoned; sleepless 3 nights; stopped cannabis 1 month ago.\nB: lit mattress and clothes with lighter at 07:30, alone, unplanned beyond the voice's instruction.\nC: left room, shouted 'fire', waited outside; relieved then frightened; sorry others were scared.\nMotive: command hallucination and persecutory belief. No fire interest, excitement or revenge. No past fires.\nBackground: foster care from 13, left care at 18, hostel 3 months; mainstream school, no learning difficulty. Cannabis daily age 15-18; no alcohol or other drugs.\nMSE: guarded, perplexed, voice active now ('it's not finished'), no insight. Low mood, no suicidal intent.\nImpression: probable first-episode psychosis. Ddx: cannabis-induced psychosis, depression with psychotic features.\nRisk: repeat fire high while voice active; suicide low-moderate; vulnerable to exploitation.\nPlan: not fit for interview; declined informal admission, so AMHP contacted for MHA assessment in custody; UDS and bloods on admission; leaving-care personal adviser and hostel informed of outcome only; EIP referral.\nDr Chinonso, ST4 forensic, L&D","make":["Explain who you are and the limits of confidentiality in custody, before any questions.","Do the ABC in order: before, during, after.","Ask 'why' in an open way, then check each motive: voices, beliefs, revenge, excitement, tension relief, suicide, cry for help.","Ask whether she raised the alarm and how she feels now: concern for others matters.","Always ask about past fires and childhood fire play.","End with a clear message for the sergeant: unwell, fitness for interview, next step."],"next":"Leah declines informal admission. The AMHP and two doctors assess her in custody that evening and she is admitted under section 2. The CPS later agrees to take no further action in the public interest while she is treated. Her urine drug screen is negative. After four weeks on aripiprazole the voice fades, and the early intervention team begins work with her and her leaving-care personal adviser.","marks":["Explained role and confidentiality limits with the appropriate adult present.","Completed an ABC analysis of this fire.","Established the motive and linked it to psychotic symptoms.","Asked about past fires, fire interest and childhood fire play.","Screened mood, substances, trauma and care history; gave a working diagnosis.","Assessed repeat fire and suicide risk and gave clear advice to custody."],"pitfalls":["Treating it as a police matter and skipping the fire details.","Asking 'did the voices make you do it?' as a leading closed question.","Forgetting past fires and childhood fire play.","Not asking whether the voice is still telling her to burn things.","Leaving the sergeant without a view on fitness for interview."],"refs":["1. NHS England: Liaison and Diversion operating model (checked Nov 2026)","2. PACE Code C: detention, treatment and questioning, appropriate adults (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG155: psychosis and schizophrenia in children and young people (2013; checked Nov 2026)","4. MHA Code of Practice, ch.s 14 and 16 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: liaison and diversion teams work in UK custody suites to spot illness early and steer people to treatment. The custody healthcare professional decides fitness for interview, but your clear written view carries weight.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-43","n":1443,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🌊","title":"DBT: explaining it to Chloe and answering her questions","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00, CMHT outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team base, clinic room","text":"Chloe Mensah, 24, a retail supervisor, has been referred by her GP for long-standing mood swings that change within hours, angry outbursts, cutting at times of stress, low self-esteem and impulsive spending. Things are straining her relationship with her partner, Jordan. Two antidepressants have not helped; she still takes sertraline 100 mg. She has read about dialectical behaviour therapy online and has come wanting to know more. You have a ten-minute slot before the team meeting."},"task":"Briefly establish Chloe's difficulties, past help and current risk, then explain what DBT is, how it is structured and what it asks of her, give a clear recommendation and answer her questions, in about ten minutes.","first60":["Read the GP letter and any A&E records for the pattern and severity of self-harm.","Check her medication: sertraline 100 mg, and any past antipsychotic or mood stabiliser trials.","Know your local DBT route: who takes referrals, the waiting time and whether there is a pre-treatment group.","Have a DBT leaflet and the crisis line number ready.","Plan to spend no more than two minutes on history: she has come for an explanation."],"ask":["'In your words, what's been hardest for you?'","'When did you last hurt yourself, and how?'","'Has it ever got so bad you've thought about killing yourself?'","'What help have you had before, and what did or didn't work?'","'Why now? What's made you want help at this point?'","'What have you read about DBT so far?'","'How do you feel about being in a group?'","'Can you tell me back the four skills we talked about?'"],"recognise":["Rapid mood shifts linked to events, anger, self-harm to calm emotion, impulsive spending and unstable relationships fit an emotionally unstable (borderline) pattern.","Medicines do not treat the core difficulties; NICE does not recommend drugs specifically for this pattern.","DBT is the therapy with the strongest evidence for reducing repeated self-harm in this group.","Self-harm last week means a brief risk check now: method, wound care, suicidal intent and a crisis plan.","'Can I just have it one to one?' is common. The skills group is a core part of DBT, so be honest, not promising.","Motivation and readiness are key: she is asking, and that is a strength to name."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a brief history and risk check, say talking therapy is the recommended treatment, explain DBT's idea, four modules, structure and pre-treatment phase, recommend it clearly, be honest about the group, and agree a crisis plan and referral.","r":"Right: she gets accurate information, a clear recommendation, a realistic picture of what it asks of her, and safety in the meantime.","best":true},{"c":"Promise her individual DBT without a group, to keep her engaged.","r":"Wrong: the skills group is a core part of DBT and most services cannot offer it without one. A broken promise damages trust.","best":false},{"c":"Start a mood stabiliser to settle her mood swings while she waits.","r":"Not recommended: medication does not treat the core difficulties and adds overdose risk. Consider medicines only for a separate condition or short-term in a crisis.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is choosing a treatment: give balanced information and respect her decision.","Confidentiality: share with her partner only with her consent, unless there is a serious risk to life.","NICE self-harm guidance: do not use risk tools to predict outcomes; use a needs-based plan and a written safety plan.","Record her consent to referral and the information you gave."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"DBT is built on two ideas at once: accepting yourself as you are right now, and learning to change what isn't working. Both are true together."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd honestly recommend DBT for you. It was designed for exactly what you've described: big emotions that change fast, self-harm to cope, and strain on relationships."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I can't promise one-to-one only, because the skills group is a core part of DBT. But the group is more like a class than sharing your story, and people often find it's the part that helps most."}],"write":"CMHT clinic. 24F, retail supervisor. Referred by GP. Seen alone.\nDifficulties: mood shifts several times a day with events, anger outbursts at partner, low self-esteem, impulsive spending (credit card debt ~GBP 3,000). Self-harm: superficial cutting to thighs at times of distress, last 1 week ago, healed, no medical treatment. No current suicidal thoughts or plans; partner and job protective.\nPast help: fluoxetine, then sertraline 100 mg (current), no clear benefit. No therapy before.\nWhy now: relationship at risk; wants to change.\nImpression: emotional dysregulation consistent with an emotionally unstable (borderline) pattern; to be confirmed at DBT assessment.\nDiscussed: talking therapy first-line; DBT idea, four modules, structure (weekly individual, weekly skills group, phone coaching, diary card, ~12 months), pre-treatment assessment and commitment. Recommended DBT. She would prefer one-to-one only; explained group is core, cannot promise individual-only; she will consider it.\nPlan: referral to DBT service; crisis plan written; sertraline review with GP; debt advice; review 4 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT3","make":["Keep the history to two minutes and include a self-harm and suicide check.","Say clearly that therapy, not tablets, is the main treatment.","Explain acceptance plus change in one plain sentence.","Name all four modules and link each to one of her own difficulties.","Give numbers: weekly one-to-one, weekly group of about two hours, around a year.","Recommend clearly, and be honest about the group."],"next":"Chloe is referred to the DBT service and joins a four-week pre-treatment orientation group. She decides she can manage the skills group because it 'feels like a class'. Her diary card shows self-harm stops after three months, and she tapers sertraline with her GP. Jordan comes to one joint session.","marks":["Briefly established difficulties, past help and why now.","Checked current self-harm and suicide risk.","Said talking therapy is first-line and medication is not for the core problem.","Explained DBT's acceptance-and-change idea and the four modules.","Gave the structure, length, diary card and pre-treatment phase.","Recommended DBT and handled the group question honestly."],"pitfalls":["Spending five minutes on history and running out of time to explain DBT.","Skipping the self-harm risk check because she has 'come for information'.","Promising one-to-one DBT without a group.","Using jargon ('dialectics', 'validation strategies') without explaining it.","Not giving a clear recommendation when asked 'would you recommend it?'"],"refs":["1. NICE CG78: borderline personality disorder (2009; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG225: self-harm, assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE QS88: personality disorders, borderline and antisocial (2015; checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: dialectical behaviour therapy and borderline personality disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK services, DBT is usually delivered by a specialist team after a referral and assessment. Patients expect you to recommend clearly and to be honest about waiting lists and the group, rather than promise what the service cannot give.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-44","n":1444,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"👂","title":"Hearing the neighbour's voice: is it the drink?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:10, liaison psychiatry","where":"A&E majors, side cubicle","text":"Gareth Pryce, 43, a delivery driver, has come to A&E with his wife, Sian. For about a week he has heard his next-door neighbour's voice, and this afternoon he became very anxious and agitated in the supermarket during the weekly shop. The A&E registrar has refused to call it 'just anxiety' and has referred him to liaison psychiatry. Sian is in the relatives' room. You have about ten minutes before the A&E team needs a plan."},"task":"Assess the psychopathology of the voice and find its cause, check for withdrawal and other physical causes, and agree the immediate plan, in about ten minutes.","first60":["Read the A&E notes: observations, glucose, breath alcohol if done, and any head injury.","Ask the nurse to start CIWA-Ar scoring and hourly observations if not already running.","Check the summary care record for past alcohol contacts, LFTs and any seizures.","Plan the order: the voice in detail, other symptoms, then drink, then physical causes.","Know the alcohol care team's bleep for today."],"ask":["'Whose voice is it? Do you hear it through your ears, like me talking now?'","'What does it say? Is it speaking to you, or discussing you with someone else?'","'Does it ever tell you to do things?'","'Have you seen things that others can't, or felt things on your skin?'","'How much do you drink in a usual day, and what?'","'When was your last drink? Do you wake up shaky?'","'Have you ever had a fit, or a head injury?'","'Have you thought about having it out with your neighbour?'"],"recognise":["Alcoholic hallucinosis: vivid auditory hallucinations, often abusive and second person, in clear consciousness, in a heavy drinker during drinking or early withdrawal.","Clear consciousness separates it from delirium tremens: check orientation and attention, not just 'is he alert?'.","Visual or tactile hallucinations, fluctuating confusion or a racing pulse and fever point towards delirium tremens, an emergency.","Waking shaky and drinking in the morning to settle mean physical dependence and a real risk of withdrawal seizures.","Any heavy drinker going into withdrawal, or poorly nourished, should get parenteral thiamine before glucose.","Alcohol dependence and alcohol-related psychosis both affect fitness to drive: the DVLA must be told."],"decide":[{"c":"Characterise the voice, confirm clear consciousness, take a units-based alcohol history with last drink, screen withdrawal and Wernicke's, and admit for medically assisted withdrawal with Pabrinex, telling him to stop driving.","r":"Right: the cause is found, the dangerous complications are planned for, and the road risk is addressed.","best":true},{"c":"Start an antipsychotic and discharge him to the community team.","r":"Unsafe: he is physically dependent with his last drink this morning. Untreated withdrawal can cause seizures and delirium tremens.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose schizophrenia because he hears a voice and believes it is real.","r":"Wrong: new voices at 43 in a very heavy drinker, with nothing else psychotic, point to alcohol. Reassess once withdrawal is over.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity and agrees to admission; record it. If he lacked capacity and became delirious, the MCA would cover urgent treatment.","DVLA: alcohol dependence and psychosis must be notified by the driver. Tell him to stop driving now and record it.","If he keeps driving despite advice, the GMC allows you to tell the DVLA, after telling him you will.","Share information with Sian only with his consent."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You're not going mad, Gareth. Heavy drinking over years can make people hear voices like this, clearly, while they're fully awake. It's called alcoholic hallucinosis, and it usually settles with the right treatment."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Because you drive for work, I have to be straight with you: you mustn't drive until this is sorted, and you need to tell the DVLA. I'll write it down for you."},{"to":"A&E registrar","s":"Gareth has alcoholic hallucinosis in clear consciousness, with alcohol dependence, last drink 7 a.m. Please admit for medically assisted withdrawal: CIWA-Ar, chlordiazepoxide, Pabrinex before any glucose, and bloods. We'll review tomorrow."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 43M, delivery driver. Seen alone; wife in relatives' room.\nVoice: 1 week, neighbour's voice, heard through ears, as real as speech, 2nd person, abusive ('drunk', 'failure'), several times a day, worse in evening. No commands, no commentary. Heard it while neighbour away on holiday. Believed it real; now 'not sure'. Anxious in supermarket today.\nNo visual or tactile hallucinations, no delusions beyond the voice, no thought interference. Oriented x3, months backwards correct.\nAlcohol: 70 cl vodka daily (28 units/day) for 3 years; morning shakes, drinks on waking, failed attempts to cut down, drives to buy drink. Last drink 07:00. Tremor and sweating now. No seizures or DTs before. No head injury, drugs or epilepsy. Eating poorly.\nNo FH of psychosis, no past psychiatric history.\nRisk: had thought of confronting neighbour, agrees not to. No suicidal thoughts. Drove today? No (wife drove).\nImpression: alcohol-induced psychotic disorder (alcoholic hallucinosis); alcohol dependence. Ddx: early delirium tremens, schizophrenia (unlikely).\nPlan: admit; CIWA-Ar; chlordiazepoxide regimen; Pabrinex IV before glucose; FBC, LFT, GGT, U&E, Mg, phosphate, glucose, clotting; alcohol care team. Advised no driving, DVLA notification. Consent to speak to wife.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison","make":["Take the voice apart: source, through the ears, person, content, commands, frequency, onset.","Ask for any visual or tactile hallucinations and check orientation and attention.","Get the drink in units, the pattern, the last drink time and dependence features.","Screen withdrawal now and Wernicke's signs: confusion, unsteadiness, eye problems.","Ask about risk to the neighbour and on the road.","Explain the link to alcohol without blame, and give a clear plan."],"next":"Gareth is admitted to the acute medical unit and has a five-day chlordiazepoxide regimen with Pabrinex. The voice fades by day three and is gone by discharge. He tells the DVLA and starts with the community alcohol service on naltrexone. Sian joins a carers' group.","marks":["Characterised the hallucination fully: modality, source, person, content, commands.","Excluded other psychotic features and checked orientation and attention.","Quantified drinking, last drink and dependence features.","Screened withdrawal, Wernicke's signs, head injury and drugs.","Asked about risk to the neighbour, self and driving.","Explained the likely cause and agreed admission and no driving."],"pitfalls":["Stopping at 'he hears voices' without the person, content and commands.","Forgetting to ask about alcohol at all, or accepting 'I drink socially'.","Missing the last drink time and so missing the seizure risk window.","Calling him 'not confused' without testing attention.","Not mentioning driving to a delivery driver."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100: alcohol-use disorders, physical complications (2010; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG115: alcohol-use disorders, diagnosis and management of harmful drinking and dependence (2011; checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders and alcohol (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: chlordiazepoxide, thiamine and Pabrinex (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK drink is measured in units (10 ml of pure alcohol). A 70 cl bottle of 40% vodka is 28 units. Always write units per day and the last drink time: the A&E team will ask for both.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-45","n":1445,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🤝","title":"Is interpersonal therapy right for Fiona?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:20, general adult outpatients","where":"Outpatient clinic, consulting room","text":"Fiona MacLeod, 41, a part-time care assistant, married with two teenagers, has a depressive illness. Her GP started sertraline 50 mg six weeks ago, which has helped her sleep a little. She would like to try a talking therapy rather than rely on tablets alone, and her GP asked whether IPT might suit her. She filled in a PHQ-9 in the waiting room: score 14. You have about ten minutes."},"task":"Explore Fiona's depressive symptoms and risk, and assess whether she is suitable for interpersonal psychotherapy, then explain your view and agree next steps, in about ten minutes.","first60":["Read the GP letter: onset, sertraline start date and dose, any past episodes or highs.","Look at the PHQ-9 she completed: total 14, and her answer to item 9 (thoughts of death or self-harm).","Remind yourself of the four IPT areas: grief, role dispute, role transition, interpersonal deficits.","Know your local route: NHS Talking Therapies offers IPT for depression; she can self-refer.","Have an IPT leaflet and the crisis line number ready."],"ask":["'How's your mood been, and for how long?'","'What do you still enjoy? What have you stopped doing?'","'Have you had times of feeling unusually high, or needing little sleep?'","'Has it ever felt as if life isn't worth carrying on with?'","'Has anyone close to you died in the last few years?'","'Who are the important people in your life, and how are things with them?'","'What changed in your life around when this started?'","'Could you come every week for about four months?'"],"recognise":["IPT works on the link between mood and current relationships: the depression is understood in its interpersonal context.","Best fit: outpatient, mild to moderate depression, no psychosis or bipolar disorder, and a clear interpersonal trigger.","Becoming her mother's carer and cutting her work hours is a role transition.","Repeated arguments with her husband over the care load, with unmet expectations, is a role dispute.","A past death grieved normally does not make grief a focus; isolation that started with the depression is not an interpersonal deficit.","PHQ-9 of 14 is moderate depression, which suits IPT, with or without her sertraline."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm moderate depression with no psychosis, highs or suicidal plans, map all four IPT areas, identify role transition and role dispute, check motivation and weekly availability, and refer to Talking Therapies for IPT.","r":"Right: a clear, reasoned suitability decision with a specific focus she understands and a route to start.","best":true},{"c":"Refer for CBT because it is the most common therapy.","r":"Not the best fit: her depression is closely tied to a role change and a dispute, which is what IPT targets. CBT is a valid alternative, not the default.","best":false},{"c":"Stop the sertraline now that she will have therapy.","r":"Wrong: it has started to help. Continue, review dose, and keep it for at least six months after recovery.","best":false}],"law":["She is choosing a treatment with capacity: give balanced information about IPT, CBT and medication.","Under the Care Act 2014, as a carer she can ask the local authority for a carer's assessment.","Share information with her husband only with her consent.","Record the PHQ-9 score, the risk assessment and her choice in the clinic letter to the GP."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"IPT works on the two-way link between mood and the people in your life. For you, two areas stand out: becoming your mum's carer, which is a big change in your life, and the strain it's put between you and Iain."},{"to":"Patient","s":"It's usually about 12 to 16 weekly sessions of an hour. Early on you map your relationships, in the middle you work on the focus areas, and at the end you plan for staying well."},{"to":"GP","s":"Moderate depression (PHQ-9 14), no suicidal thoughts, no psychosis or past highs. Suitable for IPT: focus role transition into caring and role dispute with husband. Referred to Talking Therapies. Please continue sertraline 50 mg and review dose."}],"write":"General adult OPD. 41F, part-time care assistant, married, two children (16, 14).\nPHQ-9 14 (item 9 = 0).\nMood: 4 months low, anhedonia (stopped choir), early waking, reduced appetite, fatigue, guilt, poor concentration. No psychotic symptoms. No past highs. Alcohol minimal.\nRisk: no thoughts of death or self-harm; children and work protective.\nOnset: mother moved in after stroke 5 months ago; Fiona became main carer, cut hours from 25 to 12.\nIPT areas: Grief - father died 6 yrs ago, grieved and moved on: not a focus. Role dispute - repeated arguments with husband about sharing care; feels unsupported. Role transition - from worker and choir member to carer; resentful then guilty. Interpersonal deficits - good friendships before; withdrawal since onset only: not a focus.\nSuitability: outpatient, moderate, non-psychotic, non-bipolar, clear link mood-relationships, motivated, can attend weekly (sister covers Tuesdays).\nPlan: IPT via NHS Talking Therapies (focus: role transition, role dispute); continue sertraline 50 mg, GP to review; carer's assessment; crisis numbers given; review 8 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Cover core, biological and cognitive symptoms quickly, and use the PHQ-9 score.","Screen for psychosis and past highs: both change the plan.","Ask about all four IPT areas, even the ones you expect to be absent.","Link the onset of her low mood to a life change in her own words.","Check practical readiness: weekly attendance, childcare, care cover.","Agree one or two focus areas, not all four."],"next":"Fiona self-refers to NHS Talking Therapies that evening and starts IPT three weeks later, focusing on her new role as a carer and on the dispute with Iain. A carer's assessment gets her mother two days a week at a day centre. By session 12 her PHQ-9 is 6 and she is back at choir.","marks":["Assessed core, biological and cognitive symptoms and severity.","Screened psychosis, past highs and suicide risk.","Explored grief, role dispute, role transition and interpersonal deficits.","Linked her mood to her relationships and role change.","Checked motivation and practical readiness for weekly sessions.","Explained IPT and agreed a focus and referral route."],"pitfalls":["Spending the whole time on depressive symptoms and never reaching the four areas.","Forgetting to screen for bipolar disorder or psychosis.","Saying she has 'all four' problem areas instead of choosing a focus.","Skipping risk because she seems well.","Not explaining how IPT actually runs."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222: adult depression guideline (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS England: NHS Talking Therapies manual (checked Nov 2026)","3. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS Talking Therapies (formerly IAPT) offers IPT for depression, and patients can refer themselves online. A clear suitability letter naming the focus area helps the therapist start quickly.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-46","n":1446,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🪞","title":"Worried her eyes are too far apart: clarifying the diagnosis","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:20, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health clinic room","text":"Amira Haddad, a 27-year-old receptionist, has seen her GP four times in a year about her eyes, which she says sit too far apart and spoil her face. The GP checked her over, found nothing abnormal and referred her to you. She arrives in large tinted glasses and keeps them on. You have about ten minutes to find out what is going on and name it."},"task":"In about ten minutes, map her appearance concern, how firmly she holds it, her rituals and avoidance, any cosmetic or self-surgery plans and her suicide risk, screen the main alternatives, then name the likely diagnosis and agree the next step.","first60":["Read the GP letter: number of visits, anything already said about surgery, any past mental health contact.","Decide now: you will never argue about her eyes, and never reassure her that they look fine.","Plan the order: the concern, how sure she is, rituals, impact, surgery, other diagnoses, risk, close.","Leave her glasses on; asking her to remove them will end the rapport in the first minute.","Have the crisis line number and a BDD information leaflet ready for the close."],"ask":["'What is it about your eyes that bothers you most? Is any other part of your appearance a worry?'","'Adding it all up, how many hours a day are your eyes on your mind?'","'If someone tells you your eyes are fine, how do you explain that to yourself? Could you be seeing it differently?'","'How often do you check them, in mirrors, windows or your phone?'","'What do you do to hide them or make them look different?'","'Have you seen anyone about surgery? Have you ever thought about trying to change them yourself?'","'Has it ever got so bad you felt life wasn't worth living? Any plans?'","'How's your mood been? Do you worry about other things in social situations, apart from your eyes?'"],"recognise":["Hours a day of preoccupation with a defect others cannot see, plus repetitive checking, camouflage and comparing: body dysmorphic disorder until proven otherwise.","Insight lies on a spectrum. She accepts she might see it differently: fair insight, an overvalued idea. A belief she cannot doubt at all is BDD with absent insight, not a new diagnosis.","Saving for surgery and visiting several surgeons is a red flag: procedures rarely ease BDD and often shift the worry or deepen it.","Watching videos of people altering their own features, or 'I'll do it myself if no one will', is the most serious specific risk.","Suicidal thinking is common in BDD: always ask directly, even if she seems calm.","Screen the look-alikes: depression, social anxiety disorder, OCD, an eating disorder (weight and shape) and psychosis (other fixed beliefs, ideas of reference)."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused history of the concern, conviction, rituals, impact, surgery and risk; name body dysmorphic disorder with fair insight; offer CBT with exposure and response prevention and an SSRI discussion; advise against surgery for now.","r":"Right: it answers the GP's question, captures risk, and gives her a credible alternative to surgery without arguing about her face.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her firmly that her eyes are normal and that she has nothing to worry about.","r":"Wrong: reassurance does not shift BDD, makes her feel unheard and often ends engagement. Acknowledge the distress, not the defect.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose delusional disorder because she is so convinced, and start an antipsychotic.","r":"Wrong: she accepts others may see it differently, so insight is fair. Even with absent insight, ICD-11 keeps the diagnosis as BDD, and SSRIs remain first-line.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and attends voluntarily; treatment is her choice after a clear explanation.","Share the diagnosis with her GP in the clinic letter, copied to her, as standard NHS practice.","If she disclosed a concrete plan to operate on herself or to end her life, safety would come first: same-day crisis team review, and sharing information without consent if needed to prevent serious harm (GMC confidentiality guidance)."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not going to argue about how your eyes look. What I can see is how much time and pain this is costing you, and that's what I want to help with."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you're describing has a name: body dysmorphic disorder. It's common, it's treatable, and surgery is usually not the answer to it."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Impression: body dysmorphic disorder, fair insight, moderate impairment. Please avoid onward cosmetic referrals while she starts treatment; we have referred her for CBT with ERP and discussed fluoxetine."}],"write":"OPD. 27F receptionist. GP referral: concern eyes 'too far apart'; GP found no abnormality.\nConcern: eyes set too wide and 'uneven' since about 15 (teasing at school). Preoccupied ~4 h/day. Distress 8/10.\nConviction: 'I know they're wrong', but can accept others may see it differently = fair insight, overvalued idea. No ideas of reference beyond appearance; no other fixed beliefs.\nRituals: checks mirrors/phone camera ~30x/day, large tinted glasses outside, compares with colleagues, seeks reassurance from sister. Moved to back-office phone shifts. Stopped dating.\nCosmetic: 2 private surgical consultations; saving ~£6,000. Has watched online eyelid-taping/DIY videos; no intent or plan to self-operate.\nScreen: low mood secondary to concern, sleep and appetite OK; no panic, no other social fears, no weight/shape concern, no psychotic features. Alcohol ~6 units/wk. No drugs.\nRisk: passive thoughts life not worth living most weeks; no plan, intent or past attempts. Protective: sister, job. Risk to self: low-moderate, chronic.\nImpression: body dysmorphic disorder, fair insight. Ddx considered and not supported: depressive episode, social anxiety disorder, OCD, eating disorder, delusional disorder.\nPlan: explained BDD; leaflet; refer CBT with ERP for BDD; discuss fluoxetine at follow-up; advised no cosmetic procedures for now; GP letter; crisis plan given (NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123). Review 2 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask about every feature, not just the one in the letter.","Get a number: hours a day of preoccupation, checks a day, distress out of 10.","Test conviction gently with one question about other views; never with a mirror or a challenge.","Ask what she would do if no surgeon will operate: that answer is the key risk.","Screen the alternatives in one sweep, then name BDD in plain words.","End with a concrete next step and a crisis route, not just 'we'll be in touch'."],"next":"Amira takes the leaflet and agrees to CBT. At the two-week review she starts fluoxetine 20 mg. She postpones her surgical consultation and tells her sister about the diagnosis, which she says is the first time anyone has called it something other than vanity.","marks":["Explored every disliked feature, onset and hours of preoccupation.","Rated conviction and insight without arguing about her appearance.","Elicited checking, camouflage, comparing, reassurance-seeking and avoidance.","Explored cosmetic consultations, savings and any self-surgery thoughts.","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and plans.","Screened depression, social anxiety, OCD, eating disorder and psychosis, then named BDD."],"pitfalls":["Telling her she looks fine, or asking her to take her glasses off.","Stopping at 'she has seen surgeons' without asking what she will do if refused.","Labelling strong conviction as delusional disorder without testing insight.","Forgetting the suicide question because she seems composed.","Running out of time before naming the diagnosis and a next step."],"refs":["1. NICE CG31: OCD and BDD (2005; checked Nov 2026)","2. WHO ICD-11: 6B21 body dysmorphic disorder, insight specifiers (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: fluoxetine (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: body dysmorphic disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK clinics, patients with BDD often expect to be dismissed as vain. Saying the name of the condition, and that it is treatable, is often the moment they engage.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-48","n":1448,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Depression not lifting after two drugs: the next step","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:30, community mental health clinic","where":"Outpatient clinic room","text":"Jeremy (Jerry) Pearce, a 47-year-old surveyor, has had three past depressive episodes that each settled on fluoxetine 20 mg. Six months into this relapse he has had fluoxetine 20 mg then 40 mg, six weeks each, with no response, and is now on venlafaxine 150 mg. His mood is a little better; his energy, interest, concentration and appetite are not. The diagnosis is confirmed. His GP wants a plan."},"task":"In about ten minutes, check why he is not better (adherence, side effects, thyroid, alcohol, physical health, stress), ask about suicide, then recommend one clear next step with its monitoring, add talking therapy, and say what follows if it fails.","first60":["Check the GP letter: exact venlafaxine dose and formulation, start date, BP readings, any blood tests in the last six months.","Look for TFTs: tiredness and low energy that won't shift should make you think thyroid.","Check for heart disease, hypertension or epilepsy in the summary: they shape the venlafaxine decision.","Have a BP cuff ready in the room; you will want a reading today.","Decide your one recommended step before you go in, so you don't read out a list."],"ask":["'What's got better since the venlafaxine, and what hasn't?'","'How often do you manage to take it? Any side effects?'","'Have you noticed feeling the cold, constipation, dry skin or weight change?'","'Any heart problems, high blood pressure or fits, you or the family?'","'How much alcohol most weeks? Anything else?'","'Have things ever felt so bad that you've thought about suicide?'","'What matters most to you in treatment: work, side effects, weight?'","'Have you ever had talking therapy?'"],"recognise":["No full response after two antidepressants, from two different classes, each given properly: treatment-resistant depression is the usual working label.","Before adding drugs, look for the reasons: missed doses, alcohol, an underactive thyroid, other physical illness, ongoing stress.","Tiredness, weight gain, constipation, dry skin and feeling the cold point to hypothyroidism: check TFTs before anything else changes.","He has a partial response to venlafaxine 150 mg with few side effects: raising the dose usually beats switching.","He is worried about weight: mirtazapine augmentation would make that worse, so it is not the first add-on to offer him.","ECT is for severe or life-threatening depression or when other options have failed; it is not the next step for him."],"decide":[{"c":"Check TFTs, BP and adherence, then increase venlafaxine to 225 mg daily with BP checks, refer for CBT alongside, and outline augmentation if this fails.","r":"Right: looks for a treatable cause, builds on the partial response, adds therapy, and keeps the escalation clear and stepwise.","best":true},{"c":"Recommend ECT now because two antidepressants have failed.","r":"Wrong: he has a partial response, no psychotic features and no life-threatening features. Optimise medication and add therapy first.","best":false},{"c":"Read through every option for resistant depression and ask him to choose.","r":"Unhelpful: a list overwhelms him. Give one clear recommendation with reasons, then the fallback options.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity: the choice is his after a clear explanation of benefits and risks.","Ask his consent before involving his partner or contacting occupational health.","If you advise reduced hours or time off, the GP or hospital doctor can issue a fit note."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've done everything right, and the venlafaxine has started to help. My main recommendation is to raise it to 225 mg, check your blood pressure, and add talking therapy."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Before that, I'd like a blood test for your thyroid. Tiredness, feeling the cold and constipation can come from an underactive thyroid, and that would change the plan."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Partial response to venlafaxine 150 mg. Please check TFTs, U&E and BP; if normal, increase to 225 mg daily with BP at 2 weeks. Referred for CBT. Review in clinic in 4 weeks."}],"write":"OPD. 47M surveyor. Recurrent depressive disorder, current moderate episode; no response to fluoxetine 20 then 40 mg (6 wks each); partial response to venlafaxine 150 mg XL (8 wks).\nNow: mood slightly better; persistent fatigue, anhedonia (stopped cycling), poor concentration at work, reduced appetite yet weight up ~4 kg.\nAdherence: daily, no missed doses. SE: mild sweating.\nPhysical screen: feels the cold, constipated, dry skin - ?hypothyroidism. No known heart disease, HTN or epilepsy; father had hypothyroidism. Alcohol ~8 units/wk; no drugs.\nStress: big project at work since spring.\nRisk: no suicidal thoughts or plans; protective: partner, children, work.\nPlan: TFTs, U&E, FBC today; BP today 128/82. If TFTs normal: venlafaxine 225 mg XL mane; BP at 2 weeks; warn sweating, nausea, never stop suddenly. Refer CBT (Talking Therapies). If no response by 4-6 weeks at 225 mg: discuss augmentation (aripiprazole or lithium). ECT not indicated now. Review 4 weeks. Crisis: NHS 111 option 2.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask what is better and what is not before you advise.","Rule out the reasons for non-response: adherence, thyroid, alcohol, stress.","Recommend one step, with dose and monitoring, then pause.","Pair medication with a named talking therapy.","Give the fallback options briefly and put ECT in its proper place.","Check understanding and book a review date."],"next":"Jerry's TFTs come back normal. He goes up to venlafaxine 225 mg; his BP at two weeks is 132/84. He starts CBT through Talking Therapies and gets back on his bike for short rides. At six weeks he reports more energy and is concentrating better at work.","marks":["Asked what has and has not improved, and about adherence and side effects.","Screened for hypothyroidism and other physical causes, and for alcohol and drugs.","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts.","Recommended one clear next step: venlafaxine 225 mg with BP monitoring.","Combined medication with a named psychological therapy.","Gave augmentation as the next fallback and ECT only if all else fails."],"pitfalls":["Listing every possible treatment without a recommendation.","Jumping to ECT or a third switch while he has a partial response.","Missing the thyroid symptoms.","Raising venlafaxine without a BP check or a warning about stopping suddenly.","Forgetting his weight worry when choosing an add-on."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), further-line treatment (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: venlafaxine, dose and monitoring (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CKS: hypothyroidism (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE IPG542: rTMS for depression (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients expect one clear recommendation with reasons, not a menu. Say what you would do and why, then offer the alternatives.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-49","n":1449,"prog":"Forensic Psychiatry","icon":"💌","title":"In love with a nurse: how dangerous is he?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:40, on call","where":"General hospital main reception, then a side room","text":"The receptionist bleeps the duty psychiatry doctor. Lewis Hartley, 27, unemployed and living alone, is demanding the contact details of a staff nurse he says he loves. She looked after him in A&E three months ago when he thought he was having a heart attack. When refused, he shouted and became threatening. Security are on their way. You need to find out what he believes and how dangerous he is."},"task":"In about ten minutes, set up a safe interview, map his belief about the nurse and what he has done, ask what he will do if refused and about weapons, screen his mental state and past violence, then state a risk level and act on it.","first60":["Ask the receptionist exactly what he said and did; ask security to stay outside the door.","Choose a room with an alarm, you nearest the door, and a second staff member with you.","Find out who the nurse is and whether she is on shift now; ask the nurse in charge of A&E to keep her away from reception.","Check the electronic record: past psychiatric contact, A&E notes from three months ago, any alerts.","Decide your exit line before you start: if he escalates, you end the interview and leave."],"ask":["'Tell me about this nurse. How did you meet?'","'How do you know she feels that way about you?'","'Could there be another explanation for how she was with you?'","'What do you know about her: her full name, her shifts, where she lives, her phone or social media?'","'Have you tried to see her before, waited for her, or followed her?'","'What will you do if she doesn't want to see you?'","'Do you carry a knife or any weapon? Do you have one at home?'","'Any police record? Have you ever hurt someone?'"],"recognise":["A fixed belief that someone, usually of higher status and barely known, is secretly in love with him, based on trivial evidence such as a smile: erotomanic delusion until proven otherwise.","Dangerousness rises with knowledge of her (address, shifts), repeated contact attempts, waiting or following, anger at rejection, sexual preoccupation and weapon access.","'People will have to notice me' and 'whatever it takes' after rejection are threats until clarified: ask exactly what he means.","Static risk factors: young man, past violence, police record, previous fixations. Dynamic: anger now, substances, living alone, no treatment.","Screen for other psychotic symptoms, mood disorder and substances: erotomania can be part of schizophrenia or mania.","Your duty to the nurse and the receptionist overrides his confidentiality when there is a risk of serious harm."],"decide":[{"c":"Interview him with a colleague in a safe room, map the belief and his actions, ask about rejection and weapons, then inform the consultant, police, the nurse's manager and security and complete an incident report.","r":"Right: safe, thorough on the factors that predict harm, and acts proportionately to protect the nurse.","best":true},{"c":"Promise to pass a message to the nurse so that he leaves calmly.","r":"Wrong: it endorses the belief, encourages further contact and breaks the boundary. Be honest that you cannot arrange contact.","best":false},{"c":"Accept 'I'd never hurt her' and send him home with a GP letter.","r":"Unsafe: denial is common. His behaviour, his response to rejection and weapon access matter more than his reassurance.","best":false}],"law":["Tell him at the start that you cannot keep information secret if someone may be at risk of harm.","GMC confidentiality: disclose to the police and to the nurse what is necessary and proportionate to protect her from serious harm, and record why.","If he leaves with a threat and a possible weapon, the police lead; Section 136 can be used if they find him outside a private dwelling and he appears mentally disordered.","If a mental disorder is likely and he will not accept help, request a Mental Health Act assessment via the AMHP service."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I want to be honest with you, Lewis. I can't arrange for you to see her or give you her details. I do want to understand what's been happening for you."},{"to":"Consultant on call","s":"Twenty-seven-year-old man with a probable erotomanic delusion about an A&E nurse; has waited outside the unit twice, knows her shift, and hinted he'd 'do whatever it takes' with a knife at home. I've informed police and security. Please can you advise on an MHA assessment?"},{"to":"A&E nurse in charge","s":"A man who believes Hannah is in love with him has come to reception and made veiled threats. Police are aware. Please keep her away from the front of house and arrange for her to be escorted to her car tonight."}],"write":"On-call psychiatry, main reception. 27M, unemployed, lives alone. Seen with security nurse present, alarm room.\nBelief: convinced A&E staff nurse (Hannah) loves him after she smiled during A&E attendance 3 months ago (chest pain, no cardiac cause). Thinks of her 'all day'. Evidence: smile, 'the way she said my name'. No alternative explanation accepted: fixed.\nKnowledge/actions: knows first name, ward, believes her shift pattern; has waited outside the unit twice; searched social media; no address known; no messages sent.\nRejection: 'people will have to notice me'; 'whatever it takes'. Has a kitchen knife at home 'for protection'; denies carrying today. Sexual thoughts about her, denies intent to force.\nMSE screen: irritable, guarded; no AVH; no other delusions elicited; mood not elated or low; denies suicidal thoughts. Cannabis weekly. Past: caution for affray age 19; no MH history.\nImpression: probable erotomanic delusional disorder. Risk of harm to nurse: HIGH (fixation, approach behaviour, veiled threat, weapon access, anger at rejection).\nActions: consultant informed; police called (non-emergency then 999 if leaves); nurse's manager and A&E NIC informed, escort to car; security present; Datix completed; request MHA assessment via AMHP if he does not accept assessment. GP to be informed.\nDr Chinonso, CT1 on call","make":["Make the room safe before the first question, and say so to him calmly.","Stay curious about the belief; never argue with it, never endorse it.","Ask what he knows about her and what he has already done: actions predict risk better than feelings.","Ask the rejection question and the weapon question directly.","Screen psychosis, mood, substances and police history in one minute.","Act before you write: consultant, police, the nurse's manager, security."],"next":"Lewis waits with security while the police attend. The consultant arranges a Mental Health Act assessment that evening; he is admitted under Section 2. Hannah's manager arranges escorts and changes her shifts, and the police advise her on a stalking protection order.","marks":["Set up a safe interview and explained the limits of confidentiality.","Elicited how they met, the belief and his evidence, and tested its fixity.","Established what he knows about her and his contact attempts.","Asked what he will do if rejected, and about weapons and sexual thoughts.","Screened mental state, substances and forensic history.","Stated a risk level and named protective actions."],"pitfalls":["Seeing him alone, or in a room where he is between you and the door.","Arguing with the belief, or promising to pass on a message.","Stopping at 'I'd never hurt her' without asking about rejection and weapons.","Forgetting to warn the nurse and her manager.","Treating it as a security matter only and missing a treatable psychosis."],"refs":["1. GMC: Confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice: Section 136 and assessment (checked Nov 2026)","3. Stalking Protection Act 2019 (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG10: violence and aggression, short-term management (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, warning a person at risk is not a breach of confidentiality when the risk of serious harm is real. Share what is necessary, record why, and tell your consultant.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-50","n":1450,"prog":"Addiction Psychiatry","icon":"📉","title":"Lorazepam dependence: agreeing a safe reduction","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 09:30, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health clinic room","text":"Natalie Osei, 38, has just moved to the area. Her new GP found she has been prescribed lorazepam 4 mg a day for about four years, and she gets shaky if a dose is late. She does not want to change anything. The GP has asked for advice on managing the medicine. You have about ten minutes to understand her use and agree a plan with her."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a brief, focused history of her lorazepam use and dependence, screen mood, risk, alcohol, other drugs and physical health, explain why reducing helps her, and agree a written, gradual community taper she feels in control of.","first60":["Read the GP letter: exact dose and timing, who has been prescribing, any early requests or lost scripts.","Check the record for other sedatives, opioids, gabapentinoids or alcohol problems: they change the safety plan.","Have the equivalence ready: lorazepam 1 mg is roughly diazepam 10 mg, so 4 mg is about 40 mg.","Print or prepare a blank taper chart to fill in with her.","Plan your first line: nothing changes today without her agreement."],"ask":["'How did the lorazepam start, and how has it gone since?'","'How do you take it across the day? Has the dose crept up?'","'What happens if a dose is late? Do you find yourself craving it?'","'Does it work as well as it used to? Ever needed extra, or run out early?'","'How's your mood? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'Do you drink, or take anything else, including painkillers or sleeping tablets?'","'What worries you most about reducing?'","'What would make a reduction plan workable for you?'"],"recognise":["Shaking and sweating when a dose is late, craving, and needing more for the same effect: physical and psychological dependence.","Long-term benzodiazepines for anxiety are not recommended: tolerance, memory and concentration problems, falls, accidents and rebound anxiety.","Sudden stopping after years of use risks seizures and severe withdrawal: any change must be gradual.","Alcohol, opioids or gabapentinoids with a benzodiazepine raise the risk of breathing suppression and overdose: always ask.","An otherwise well, supported woman can taper safely in the community; hospital is not needed.","Her fear of anxiety returning is the main barrier: give her control of the pace and a plan for anxiety."],"decide":[{"c":"Confirm dependence, screen risk, explain the reasons to reduce, and agree a gradual taper (switching to diazepam, small steps every 2-4 weeks), with one prescriber, regular review and CBT for anxiety.","r":"Right: safe, evidence-based and negotiated, so she is far more likely to stick with it.","best":true},{"c":"Stop the lorazepam today because long-term use is inappropriate.","r":"Dangerous: abrupt withdrawal can cause seizures, confusion and severe anxiety. Always reduce gradually.","best":false},{"c":"Admit her to hospital for a rapid detox.","r":"Unnecessary: she is physically well, drinks little and has support. A slow community taper is safer and better tolerated.","best":false}],"law":["Lorazepam and diazepam are Schedule 4 controlled drugs: prescribe in small, regular quantities from a single prescriber.","Drug-driving law: it is an offence to drive if impaired; advise her not to drive if drowsy, especially during the switch.","Her consent is central: she has capacity and the plan must be agreed, not imposed."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Nothing changes today without your agreement. My aim is a slow plan you're in control of, not a sudden stop."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The shakiness when a dose is late tells me your body has got used to lorazepam. That's not your fault; it happens to most people who take it this long."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Lorazepam dependence. Plan agreed: switch to diazepam over a week to 10 mg four times daily, then reduce by about 10% every 2-4 weeks. Weekly scripts, single prescriber. CBT referral made. Review in 2 weeks."}],"write":"OPD. 38F, new to area. Lorazepam 1 mg QDS for ~4 years; started by previous GP during work-related anxiety; repeat prescribing since. No other treatments tried.\nDependence: tremor and sweating if dose late (withdrawal); craving; tolerance (less effect, once took extra and ran short); tiredness and poor concentration.\nMental state: mood OK, anxiety mostly controlled; no panic attacks recently; no suicidal thoughts or self-harm.\nAlcohol ~4 units/week; no drugs; no opioids, gabapentinoids or z-drugs. No epilepsy. Not pregnant; uses contraception. Supportive partner; works full time in admin. Drives.\nDiscussed: dependence; reasons to reduce; she prefers to stay on it but agrees to a slow trial.\nPlan agreed: switch to diazepam over about a week (one lorazepam dose at a time) to 10 mg QDS; then reduce ~10% every 2-4 weeks at her pace; hold if withdrawal marked; never stop suddenly. Avoid alcohol. Don't drive if drowsy. Weekly scripts from GP only. Refer CBT for GAD (Talking Therapies). Written schedule given. Review 2 weeks. Seek urgent help (999) if fit or confusion.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Open by taking away the fear: no sudden stop, nothing without her agreement.","Keep the history to two or three minutes, then move to the plan.","Name dependence with her own evidence: the shakiness, the craving, the tolerance.","Give reasons that matter to her life, not just 'guidelines say'.","Put numbers on the plan: drug, dose, step size, interval, review date.","Say clearly she can be treated at home, and how anxiety will be helped if it returns."],"next":"Natalie switches to diazepam over a week with little trouble. Six months later she is on 15 mg a day, attends CBT and has started going to the gym again. She asks to slow the steps at 10 mg, and the GP agrees.","marks":["Elicited why and when lorazepam started and how use has changed.","Identified withdrawal, craving and tolerance.","Screened mood, anxiety, suicide risk, alcohol, other drugs and physical health.","Explained dependence and gave personal reasons to reduce.","Agreed a gradual taper with numbers, a single prescriber and review.","Negotiated her fears, confirmed community treatment and gave a plan if anxiety returns."],"pitfalls":["Spending the whole time on history and never agreeing a plan.","Telling her she must stop, or stopping it suddenly.","Suggesting hospital admission for an otherwise well woman.","Forgetting to ask about alcohol, opioids or other sedatives.","Giving a vague plan with no doses, intervals or review date."],"refs":["1. NICE CKS: benzodiazepine and z-drug withdrawal (2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: benzodiazepines, approximate equivalent doses (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG113: GAD and panic disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: benzodiazepines and opioids, risk of respiratory depression (2020; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, long-term benzodiazepine prescribing is closely scrutinised, but abrupt stops are never acceptable. A negotiated, written taper with one prescriber is the standard.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-52","n":1452,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👂","title":"Voices back: why has he relapsed?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:20, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health team (CMHT) clinic room","text":"Callum Reid, 32, has paranoid schizophrenia and takes olanzapine 7.5 mg at night. He works part time in a warehouse and lives with his partner, Leah. Leah rang the team last week: over recent weeks he has been hearing voices again and seems guarded at home. His appointment has been brought forward, and he is with you now."},"task":"Take a focused history from Mr Reid, confirm what he is experiencing now and how risky it is, and work out the likely reasons for this relapse, then agree the next step with him.","first60":["Read the last clinic letter: his usual dose, his last relapse and what his early warning signs were.","Check the drug record: when was olanzapine last collected? Gaps in collection say more than 'yes, I take it'.","Note Leah's exact concern from the duty log, so you can reflect it accurately without making it sound like a complaint.","Have his weight, BMI and last bloods to hand: weight gain may be the reason he stopped.","Plan a non-judgemental way to ask about missed tablets and drugs."],"ask":["'When did the voices come back, and what do they say?'","'Do they ever tell you to do things? Do you feel you have to obey?'","'Have you felt watched, followed or that someone means you harm?'","'Most people miss tablets sometimes. How many nights a week would you say you miss?'","'What is it about the olanzapine that puts you off?'","'Have you been using cannabis or anything else? Has that changed lately?'","'What else has changed in the last couple of months: work, money, sleep, smoking?'","'Last time you were unwell, what were the first signs?'","'Have the voices or worries led to any thoughts of self-harm or someone else?'"],"recognise":["Return of mocking voices about a month ago, with ideas of being watched, in a man who was well: an early relapse of schizophrenia.","No command voices, no plans to harm, and he still works: risk is currently low but needs checking with Leah too.","Partial adherence (skipping olanzapine on work nights) is the commonest single cause of relapse; ask in a way that makes honesty easy.","Restarting skunk with a new workmate adds a second, strong trigger.","Tobacco smoking, including in joints, speeds olanzapine breakdown; stopping smoking raises levels. Any change matters.","Grogginess and weight gain are his reasons, so they must shape the plan, or he will stop again."],"decide":[{"c":"Name it as an early relapse linked to missed doses, skunk and work stress; restart olanzapine every night at 7 pm, discuss the dose and a switch if weight stays the barrier, and arrange weekly care coordinator contact and a crisis plan.","r":"Right: honest about the causes, uses his own reasons to shape treatment, and keeps him safe at home with clear follow-up.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him to take his tablets properly and stop smoking cannabis, and see him in three months.","r":"Wrong: lecturing without addressing grogginess and weight will not change what he does, and three months is too long during a relapse.","best":false},{"c":"Arrange admission today because the voices are back.","r":"Not needed: no command voices, no plans to harm and good support. Treatment at home with close review is the least restrictive option.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity to make decisions about his treatment; agree the plan with him, not for him.","Ask his consent before sharing details with Leah. You can always listen to her concerns without breaking confidentiality.","DVLA: he must not drive while his psychosis is active, and he must already have told DVLA about his diagnosis. GMC guidance explains when you must tell DVLA yourself.","No grounds for Mental Health Act assessment today; revisit if risk or refusal of care rises."],"say":[{"to":"Callum","s":"Lots of people skip tablets when they feel groggy or put on weight. It's really useful you've told me, because it helps us find a plan you can stick with."},{"to":"Callum","s":"I think the illness is starting to come back. Missing doses, the skunk and the early shifts have probably all added up. The good news is we've caught it early."},{"to":"Care coordinator","s":"Callum Reid has early relapse: mocking voices for a month, no commands, low risk. Causes: missed olanzapine, skunk, shift stress. Could you see him weekly from this week and share the crisis plan with Leah, with his consent?"}],"write":"Outpatient review, CMHT. Callum Reid, 32M, paranoid schizophrenia, olanzapine 7.5 mg nocte. Seen early at partner's request.\nPresenting: voices returned about 1 month ago; two male voices, outside his head, mocking him and saying he is watched. No commands. Feels watched at work; no passivity or thought interference. Mood 'fed up', sleep 4-5 h, appetite OK.\nCauses: missing olanzapine 3-4 nights/week for about 6 weeks (morning grogginess on early shifts, 8 kg weight gain); restarted skunk 2 months ago, most evenings; new 6 am shift pattern. Cigarettes unchanged. No new medicines or physical illness.\nPast: admitted 3 years ago after stopping medication with cannabis use. Early signs then: poor sleep, staying in room, checking windows.\nRisk: denies suicidal ideas and self-harm; no intent to harm others, no weapons; drives to work, advised not to drive while unwell.\nInsight: partial; accepts 'something has changed'.\nImpression: early relapse of paranoid schizophrenia; contributors: partial adherence, cannabis, shift stress.\nPlan: olanzapine every night at 7 pm from tonight; consultant to review dose (10 mg) and aripiprazole option re weight; bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, HbA1c, lipids), weight, UDS with consent; care coordinator weekly; drug service referral offered; crisis plan shared with Leah (consent given); review 2 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask about the voices in detail: when, how many, where, what they say, commands.","Normalise missed doses before you ask how many: you'll get the true answer.","Find his reasons for stopping and build the plan around them.","Cover drugs, stress, sleep, smoking and physical health as possible triggers.","Compare this with his last relapse and its early signs.","Name the relapse and the causes plainly, then agree one practical change."],"next":"Callum takes olanzapine at 7 pm every night and the morning grogginess eases. His care coordinator sees him weekly and refers him to the local drug service. At two weeks the voices are quieter. He and Leah write down his early warning signs together, and the consultant starts a discussion about switching to aripiprazole.","marks":["Explored the voices in detail, including commands and their onset.","Screened for delusions, passivity, mood and sleep.","Identified missed doses and his reasons without judgement.","Identified skunk use and other stresses as triggers.","Assessed risk to self and others, including driving.","Named the relapse, the likely causes and a practical, agreed next step."],"pitfalls":["Asking 'You are taking your tablets, aren't you?' and accepting 'yes'.","Lecturing about cannabis instead of exploring it.","Missing his reasons for skipping doses, so the plan repeats the problem.","Forgetting risk, especially command voices and driving.","Giving no follow-up or crisis plan."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: olanzapine; aripiprazole prolonged-release injection (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC: confidentiality, patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients often answer 'yes' to 'Do you take your tablets?' to avoid a telling-off. A normalising question ('Most people miss some; how many a week do you miss?') gets an honest answer and keeps the relationship.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-53","n":1453,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🚓","title":"Jealousy, a knife and a speeding car","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 19:10, liaison and diversion","where":"Police custody suite, interview room","text":"Gareth Holloway, 45, a plasterer, was stopped on the M6 driving at very high speed. He told officers he was going to confront his wife, Donna, whom he believes is having an affair with her manager. A kitchen knife was found in his car. He is under arrest in custody, and the liaison and diversion team has asked you to see him."},"task":"Take a focused history of his past and present risk factors: the jealous belief, why he took the knife and what for, past violence, other risks and protective factors. A detailed drug and alcohol history is not needed. End by telling him what happens next.","first60":["Read the custody record: arrest offence, breath test result, any self-harm on arrival, and whether an appropriate adult has been called.","Ask the custody sergeant where Donna is now and whether officers have spoken to her.","Check the room: you sit nearest the door, alarm in reach, an officer outside.","Look up any mental health record and previous police contact (the custody nurse or L&D practitioner can help).","Plan to ask the knife question directly; it is the centre of this assessment."],"ask":["'What were you planning to do when you got to Donna's work?'","'Why did you take the knife?'","'What would you have done if they had denied it?'","'How do you know it's true? What makes you sure?'","'Have you checked her phone, followed her or questioned her about it?'","'Have you ever pushed, hit or threatened Donna, or a previous partner?'","'Have the police ever been involved because of violence?'","'Who else lives at home? Are there children?'","'Have you had thoughts about ending your life?'"],"recognise":["Two years of certainty that Donna is unfaithful, built on trivial evidence and immune to argument: delusional (morbid) jealousy until shown otherwise.","Checking her phone, smelling clothes, timing journeys and following her are escalating, controlling behaviours and a domestic abuse concern in themselves.","Taking a knife, driving dangerously to the confrontation and being unable to say he would not use it: a very high, imminent risk to Donna and her manager.","Past physical violence to Donna (pushing her against a wall) and to a former partner in the same jealous context is the strongest historical predictor.","Low mood, poor sleep, more drinking, no insight and wanting to confront her as soon as he is out all raise risk now.","Remember suicide risk: a distressed man in custody facing charges and the loss of his marriage."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell him honestly you are worried about everyone's safety; inform the custody sergeant and officers of the risk to Donna, her manager and their daughter; request a Mental Health Act assessment in custody; ask for observations for suicide risk.","r":"Right: shares the risk with the people who can act on it, uses the right legal route, and keeps him safe in custody too.","best":true},{"c":"Keep the conversation confidential because he is your patient, and suggest GP follow-up.","r":"Wrong: a serious and specific risk of harm to identifiable people justifies disclosure in the public interest. GP follow-up does not protect Donna tonight.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest couples counselling once he is released.","r":"Unsafe: couples work with a partner at high risk puts her in danger. She needs protection and an IDVA, not joint therapy.","best":false}],"law":["Explain the limits of confidentiality at the start. GMC guidance permits sharing without his consent when that is needed to stop serious harm to others; tell him you are doing it unless that would raise the risk.","PACE Code C: as a mentally vulnerable adult he needs an appropriate adult for interview; advise the custody sergeant on fitness to be interviewed.","A Mental Health Act assessment (AMHP and two doctors, one Section 12 approved) can happen in custody; Section 2 would allow admission. The police and CPS then decide on charges.","Coercive control of a partner is a criminal offence under section 76 of the Serious Crime Act 2015.","A child in the home exposed to domestic abuse is a safeguarding concern: refer to children's social care (Working Together 2023)."],"say":[{"to":"Gareth","s":"I can hear how tormented you've been by this. I also have to be honest: with the knife and what you've told me, I'm worried someone could get badly hurt, and I need to share that."},{"to":"Custody sergeant","s":"He holds a fixed belief his wife is unfaithful, took the knife to confront her manager and cannot say he wouldn't use it. Please treat Donna and her manager as at high risk, and I'm requesting a Mental Health Act assessment."},{"to":"Custody nurse","s":"He has low mood, poor sleep and fleeting thoughts that his family would be better off without him. Please keep him on close observation and remove anything he could use to harm himself."}],"write":"Liaison and diversion, custody suite, 19:30. Gareth Holloway, 45M, plasterer. Arrested for dangerous driving (M6); kitchen knife in car. Seen with consent; limits of confidentiality explained.\nIndex: drove to confront wife Donna and her manager; took knife 'to scare him off'; cannot say what he would have done if they denied it; 'I don't know' when asked if he would have used it. Still intends to confront her on release.\nJealous belief: 2 years, stronger over 3 months. Evidence: manager's car in street, new phone passcode. Checks phone, smells clothes, times journeys, followed her twice, nightly questioning. Belief fixed; no insight.\nViolence: pushed Donna against wall 6 months ago (not reported). Grabbed ex-partner at 31 over suspected infidelity; caution. Pub fights in his 20s. No firearms; work blades at home.\nOther: no hallucinations; low mood, 3-4 h sleep, drinking more (4-5 cans nightly), none today (breath test negative). Passive thoughts family better off without him; no plan.\nHousehold: Donna and daughter Ellie, 14.\nImpression: probable delusional (morbid) jealousy; depressive symptoms; alcohol misuse. Risk to Donna, her manager: high and imminent. Risk to self: moderate in custody. Risk to Ellie: exposure to domestic abuse.\nPlan: custody sergeant informed 19:35; police to safeguard Donna and complete DASH (MARAC); MHA assessment requested via AMHP; appropriate adult for interview; close observation for self-harm; children's social care referral for Ellie; no contact with Donna. Consultant informed.\nDr Chinonso, ST4 liaison psychiatry","make":["Set out the limits of confidentiality before the hard questions.","Ask directly about the knife: why, what for, and what if they denied it.","Test the belief: evidence, duration, certainty, and the checking and following.","Get past violence in detail, especially to partners, and link it to jealousy and alcohol.","Ask about children at home, weapons and suicide.","Say plainly that you are worried and who you will tell."],"next":"The AMHP arranges an assessment that evening and Gareth is admitted under Section 2. Police speak to Donna, complete a DASH assessment and refer to MARAC; an IDVA contacts her the next day. Children's social care visits Ellie. On the ward, a diagnosis of delusional disorder, jealous type, is made.","marks":["Explained his role and the limits of confidentiality.","Asked what he intended with the knife and what he would have done if they denied it.","Explored the evidence, duration and fixity of the jealous belief, and checking behaviours.","Took a history of past violence to partners and police contact.","Asked about children, weapons, alcohol briefly and suicide.","Told him honestly about the risk and named who would be informed and why."],"pitfalls":["Never asking what the knife was for.","Arguing with the belief instead of exploring it.","Missing past violence to previous partners.","Forgetting children in the home and suicide risk.","Promising full confidentiality, then having to break it."],"refs":["1. GMC: confidentiality, disclosures to protect others (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Home Office: PACE Code C, appropriate adults (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 16: police powers and places of safety (checked Nov 2026)","4. SafeLives: DASH risk checklist and MARAC guidance (checked Nov 2026)","5. HM Government: Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK custody suites, the liaison and diversion team advises the custody sergeant, who is legally responsible for the detainee. Share the risk clearly with the sergeant; they and the officers in the case will act to protect the partner.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-54","n":1454,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🍼","title":"New mother not feeding baby: A&E","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 13:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"A&E, side room","text":"Amara Whitfield, 28, gave birth to her first baby, Leo, five weeks ago and has no known psychiatric history. Her health visitor called an ambulance after finding her agitated at home and hearing that Leo had barely been fed for a day. Leo is next door with the paediatric team. Her partner, Kwame, is on his way. A&E asks you to see her now."},"task":"Take a history from Ms Whitfield to reach a working diagnosis, screen for physical causes, and assess risk to her and to Leo. Then tell her what happens next.","first60":["Ask the A&E nurse for her observations: temperature, pulse, blood pressure, urine dip. Raised BP or fever changes everything.","Confirm with paediatrics that Leo is safe, being assessed and will not be left alone with her.","Read the health visitor's call note: exactly what she saw and what Amara said about feeding.","Ask security or a nurse to stay near: she may try to leave with Leo.","Phone the perinatal mental health team early; mother and baby unit beds take time to find."],"ask":["'How have things been since Leo was born? When did things start to feel different?'","'How much have you slept in the last few nights?'","'Is there a reason Leo hasn't needed feeding?'","'Have you heard anyone speaking when nobody else is around? What do they say?'","'Do you feel anyone is against you or Leo?'","'Have you had a temperature, pain, headaches or anything wrong with your vision?'","'Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby?'","'If the voice told you to do something else to Leo, would you do it?'","'Have any thoughts come into your head about hurting yourself, or Leo?'"],"recognise":["Well for about four weeks, then rapid onset over days of sleeplessness, racing ideas, a fixed belief that Leo is 'pure light' and a voice telling her to withhold milk: postpartum psychosis until proved otherwise.","Mood swings, perplexity and confusion about time are typical of postpartum psychosis and often have a manic flavour.","Her mother was admitted after giving birth: a strong family risk factor.","The risk to Leo comes from acting on the delusion (not feeding him), not from any wish to hurt him.","Fever, headache, high blood pressure or seizures point to infection, postpartum pre-eclampsia or a neurological cause: check them before calling it psychiatric.","No insight and a wish to go home with Leo: real risk of leaving A&E with him."],"decide":[{"c":"Treat as a psychiatric emergency: physical screen now, Leo stays with paediatrics and supervised, urgent mother and baby unit admission (informal if she agrees, Mental Health Act assessment if not), Kwame involved, safeguarding informed.","r":"Right: keeps both safe, rules out physical causes, and keeps mother and baby together in the right setting.","best":true},{"c":"Send her home with Kwame and arrange perinatal team follow-up tomorrow.","r":"Wrong: postpartum psychosis can worsen within hours, and she is already acting on a delusion that has stopped Leo being fed.","best":false},{"c":"Admit her to a general adult ward and place Leo with foster carers.","r":"Not first choice: NICE advises a mother and baby unit for women needing admission in the first year after birth, unless there is a specific reason against it.","best":false}],"law":["A&E is not a ward, so Section 5(2) cannot be used there. If she tries to leave and lacks capacity for that decision, staff can act briefly under the MCA in her best interests while an urgent Mental Health Act assessment is arranged.","Section 2 to a mother and baby unit if she refuses admission and the criteria are met; the AMHP coordinates.","Leo has come to harm through not being fed: discuss with the hospital safeguarding team and refer to children's social care.","Share information with Kwame with her consent; where there is serious risk to Leo, share what is needed to keep him safe."],"say":[{"to":"Amara","s":"I think you're unwell with an illness that can happen after having a baby, called postpartum psychosis. It's serious, but it's very treatable, and most women recover fully."},{"to":"Amara","s":"We want to keep you and Leo together and safe. The best place is a mother and baby unit, where you can be looked after with him."},{"to":"Perinatal on-call psychiatrist","s":"28-year-old, five weeks postnatal, first baby, four nights without sleep, believes her son doesn't need milk, a voice telling her to withhold feeds, mother had postnatal admission. Apyrexial, BP pending. Can we find a mother and baby unit bed today?"}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E, 13:50. Amara Whitfield, 28F, 5/52 postnatal (first baby, Leo; forceps delivery, no complications). Brought by ambulance after health visitor concern.\nHistory: well for 4 weeks. Last week: little sleep, now 4 nights almost none. Believes Leo is 'pure light' and does not need milk; stopped breastfeeding him yesterday. Hears a woman's voice telling her to keep him from food. Thinks health visitor wants to take him. Racing thoughts, elated then tearful, unsure of the day. Not eating herself.\nOrganic: no fever, pain, headache or visual change reported; no alcohol or drugs; paracetamol only. Obs and bloods pending.\nFamily: mother admitted after giving birth to her. No past psychiatric history.\nRisk: to Leo: high (delusional neglect; would follow voice if 'right'); denies wish to harm him. To self: self-neglect; denies suicidal thoughts. Absconding with Leo: high.\nImpression: postpartum psychosis.\nPlan: Leo with paediatrics, supervised contact only; 1:1 RMN; obs incl BP and urine dip; bloods FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT; perinatal team called for MBU bed; MHA assessment if she declines; safeguarding team and children's social care informed; Kwame updated on arrival with her consent; review 16:00.\nDr Chinonso, CT3","make":["Reassure her about Leo first; she cannot engage until you do.","Get the timeline: well, then a rapid change, and how fast.","Ask about the feeding gently and follow the belief to its end.","Screen for physical causes: fever, BP, headache, infection, drugs.","Ask about family history of postnatal illness or bipolar disorder.","Ask directly about harm to herself and to Leo, including following the voice."],"next":"Amara's blood pressure and bloods are normal. Leo is mildly dry and is fed by the paediatric team. She agrees to go with Leo to a mother and baby unit that evening, and Kwame goes with them. She starts olanzapine, sleeps for the first time in days, and the voice fades over two weeks.","marks":["Reassured her about Leo and established the timeline since the birth.","Elicited the belief about Leo, the voice and manic or perplexed features.","Screened for physical causes, including fever, blood pressure and substances.","Asked about family history of postnatal or bipolar illness.","Asked directly about harm to herself and to Leo, including acting on the voice.","Explained the likely diagnosis and the plan for a mother and baby unit."],"pitfalls":["Diving into risk questions before reassuring her about Leo.","Calling it postnatal depression and missing the psychosis.","Skipping the physical screen: fever, blood pressure, infection.","Not asking whether she would act on the voice.","Letting her leave A&E with Leo, or separating them without considering a mother and baby unit."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192: antenatal and postnatal mental health (updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists: postpartum psychosis information (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015) and Mental Capacity Act 2005 (checked Nov 2026)","4. Action on Postpartum Psychosis (APP): resources for women and families (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, women with severe illness in the first year after birth are admitted with their baby to a mother and baby unit, which may be in another county. Call the perinatal team early: the bed search takes hours.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-56","n":1456,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"👶","title":"Day three after birth: 'my baby is special'","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:40, liaison psychiatry bleep","where":"Postnatal ward, district general hospital","text":"Leanne Barker, 28, had her first baby, Ellis, by normal delivery three days ago. She has not fed him since the birth (the midwives are giving formula), barely sleeps, and tells everyone he is 'special'. This morning she asked to take him home now. Her midwife wants an urgent psychiatric review before anyone lets her go."},"task":"In about ten minutes, carry out a focused mental state assessment of Ms Barker and a brief risk assessment covering her, Ellis and others, then agree immediate safety steps.","first60":["Ask the midwife three things before you go in: has she fed or handled Ellis at all, has she slept, and has she tried to leave?","Check the maternity notes: delivery drugs, blood pressure, temperature, any infection, and today's bloods if done.","Look for past mental health history and family history of bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis in the booking notes.","Ask for Ellis to stay in the nursery or with a midwife while you talk, and for a midwife to sit in.","Sit between her and the door only if staff advise it; keep the room calm and quiet."],"ask":["'Tell me about Ellis. What's he like?'","'You said he's special. What makes him special?'","'How sure are you about that? Could there be another explanation?'","'Sometimes when people are under a lot of strain they hear voices others can't hear. Has that happened to you?'","'How have you been sleeping since he was born?'","'Has anyone given you reasons to worry about the staff here?'","'Have you thought about hurting yourself, or worried Ellis might come to harm?'","'How do you make sense of what's been happening to you?'"],"recognise":["Grandiose religious delusion (Ellis sent by God to heal the world) plus a voice confirming it, in the first week after birth: postpartum psychosis until proven otherwise.","Rapid, pressured speech, elation flipping to tears, and almost no sleep point to a manic or mixed picture.","Thought blocking and drifting off mid-sentence mean disordered thinking and poor attention.","She is oriented, which argues against delirium, but you still need obs and bloods to exclude infection or eclampsia.","No intent to harm Ellis does not mean low risk: refusing to feed him and wanting to leave are neglect risks now.","No insight: she sees no illness and no reason to stay."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise she stays on the ward with Ellis supervised at all times, call the perinatal consultant today for a mother and baby unit bed, and arrange a Mental Health Act assessment if she tries to leave.","r":"Right: postpartum psychosis is a psychiatric emergency; the baby is protected, her care is specialist, and the legal route is ready.","best":true},{"c":"Let her go home with Ellis if her partner promises to watch her, with a perinatal clinic appointment next week.","r":"Unsafe: psychotic, not sleeping and not feeding her baby. Postpartum psychosis can worsen within hours; a week is far too long.","best":false},{"c":"Separate her from Ellis completely and ask security to keep her on the ward.","r":"Wrong: separation is not the default and security has no power to detain. Supervised contact supports bonding; use the Mental Health Act if detention is needed.","best":false}],"law":["She is an informal in-patient. If she tries to leave and is a risk, the doctor in charge of her care (or their nominated deputy) can use Section 5(2) for up to 72 hours while a full Mental Health Act assessment is arranged.","A Mental Health Act assessment needs an AMHP and two doctors, one Section 12 approved; Section 2 allows admission to a mother and baby unit.","Ellis's welfare is paramount: a referral to children's social care is needed for a safety plan, and police can be called if she leaves with him and he is at risk.","Record her views and her capacity about treatment; consent still matters even when the Act may be used."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Leanne, I can see how much Ellis means to you. I'm worried you've had almost no sleep and your mind is racing. I'd like you both to stay here today while we help."},{"to":"Midwife in charge","s":"She has a postpartum psychosis. Please keep Ellis with a midwife at all times, one-to-one for Leanne, and call me straight away if she tries to leave. I'm ringing the perinatal consultant now."},{"to":"Perinatal consultant on call","s":"Day 3, first baby: grandiose delusions about the baby, a confirming voice, elated and pressured, no sleep, not feeding him, no insight, wants to leave. I'm asking for a mother and baby unit bed today."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, postnatal ward. 28F, P1, NVD day 3 (son Ellis). Referred: not feeding baby, says he is 'special', wants to leave.\nA&B: dishevelled, restless, poor eye contact, distractible; did not look at Ellis's cot.\nSpeech: rapid, pressured; thought blocking x2.\nMood: 'never better'; affect elated, labile (tearful briefly). Sleep ~2 h/night since delivery.\nThought: grandiose religious delusion that Ellis is sent by God to heal the world and needs no milk; feels staff may want to take him.\nPerception: second-person voice confirming his mission. No visual hallucinations.\nCognition: oriented to time, place, person; attention poor.\nInsight: none.\nRisk: to Ellis HIGH (neglect: refusing to feed; may leave with him); to self: denies suicidal thoughts; to others: low-moderate (suspicious of staff); absconding HIGH.\nImpression: postpartum psychosis (manic/mixed features). Ddx delirium (oriented; obs and bloods pending).\nPlan: Ellis supervised by midwives at all times; 1:1 nursing for Leanne; perinatal consultant called 11:55, MBU bed requested; MHA assessment if she tries to leave; obs, FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, Ca, glucose, TFT, urine; children's social care referral; partner informed with her knowledge. Review 16:00.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 liaison psychiatry","make":["Build rapport through the baby: ask his name and what makes him special before anything else.","Name each MSE domain in your head as you go and get one clear finding for each.","Ask about voices and suspicion of staff gently and directly; psychosis rarely volunteers itself.","Separate intent from risk: no wish to harm Ellis, yet high neglect risk.","Never agree to her leaving with or without Ellis; say why, kindly.","Make the three calls before you write: midwife in charge, perinatal consultant, children's social care."],"next":"The perinatal consultant agrees and a mother and baby unit bed is found for the evening. Leanne tries to leave at 14:00; the obstetric consultant uses Section 5(2) and she is assessed under Section 2 that afternoon. Ellis transfers with her, fed and supervised by unit staff.","marks":["Built rapport by showing interest in Ellis and her concerns.","Covered every MSE domain and gave a finding for each.","Elicited the grandiose delusion, the confirming voice and the suspicion of staff.","Found no insight and judged her poor judgement about feeding.","Assessed risk to Ellis, to herself and to others, including absconding.","Did not agree to her leaving and set out a safe immediate plan."],"pitfalls":["Taking a full life history instead of a focused MSE.","Accepting 'I'd never hurt him' as proof that Ellis is safe.","Missing delirium or eclampsia as causes of an acute change after birth.","Agreeing to discharge with a promise of outpatient review.","Arguing with the delusion instead of acknowledging her feelings."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192: antenatal and postnatal mental health (updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters 14 and 18 (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: olanzapine; lorazepam (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: postpartum psychosis information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK postpartum psychosis is treated in a mother and baby unit, where mother and baby are admitted together. Ask early for a bed through the perinatal team; they are few and may be far away.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-57","n":1457,"prog":"Child Psychiatry","icon":"📱","title":"Teenage overdose: bullying behind it","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 10:15, on call for the general hospital","where":"Children's ward, side room","text":"Chloe Nwosu, 15, came in two nights ago after taking about 80 paracetamol tablets. She has finished her acetylcysteine and the paediatricians say she is medically fit. Her mother is waiting in the parents' room and wants to take her home today. Nobody yet knows why she did it."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of the overdose and what led to it, and assess Chloe's current risk, so the team can decide on a safe discharge plan.","first60":["Read the A&E and paediatric notes: timing, number of tablets, paracetamol level, LFTs, and what she told the nurses.","Ask the nurse how Chloe has been on the ward: eating, sleeping, visitors, phone use, any tearfulness.","Check for previous attendances, CAMHS involvement or social care contact on the record.","Tell her mum you will see Chloe alone first, then both of them together.","Find a private room and a nurse who can sit nearby if Chloe wants."],"ask":["'Can you take me through the day you took the tablets, from the start?'","'Where did you get them, and how long had it been on your mind?'","'What did you think would happen when you took them?'","'How do you feel now about being alive?'","'What's been happening at school or online?'","'Has anyone ever hurt you, or asked you to do things you didn't want to?'","'Do you drink or use any drugs?'","'If those thoughts came back tonight, what would you do?'"],"recognise":["About 80 tablets bought from two shops, taken alone, with intent to die: a high-lethality, planned act, whatever she says today.","Messaging a friend an hour later shows ambivalence, not low intent.","Months of escalating bullying, including shared edited images online, means there is no escape at home.","Low mood, withdrawal, poor sleep and skipped lunches suggest a depressive episode.","Relief at surviving is protective, but ongoing low mood and fear of school keep the risk live.","Mum not knowing about the bullying is a gap in support and a target for the plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Discharge only with a written safety plan agreed with Chloe and mum, medicines locked away at home, school safeguarding lead contacted, and CAMHS follow-up within 48 hours.","r":"Right: risk is managed by a plan, not a score. Home is made safer, the bullying is addressed, and follow-up is fast.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge today because she says she is glad to be alive and has no plans.","r":"Unsafe: a high-lethality planned overdose with the trigger unchanged. Relief now does not settle the risk.","best":false},{"c":"Keep everything Chloe says confidential from her mum because she is 15.","r":"Wrong: confidentiality has limits when a child is at risk. Share what mum needs to keep her safe, ideally with Chloe's agreement.","best":false}],"law":["Explain confidentiality and its limits at the start: safety information will be shared with mum and the team.","Chloe can consent to assessment if she is Gillick competent; her mother holds parental responsibility.","Any abuse, exploitation or sexual images would need a safeguarding referral to children's social care and possibly the police the same day.","NICE NG225 advises against basing discharge, or any suicide prediction, on risk tools or scales."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Chloe, thank you for telling me. What happened to you at school and online is not your fault, and nobody should have to put up with it."},{"to":"Mother","s":"Chloe has been bullied for months, in school and online. She's agreed I can tell you. She's safer now, but we need a plan at home before she leaves."},{"to":"Paediatric registrar","s":"Planned overdose with intent, after months of bullying, plus depressive symptoms. Relieved to be alive, no current plan. Fit for discharge once the safety plan is done and CAMHS follow-up is booked."}],"write":"CAMHS on call, children's ward. 15F. Paracetamol OD ~80 tabs 2 nights ago; NAC complete; medically fit per paediatrics.\nOD: bought from two shops after school (avoided pack limits); thought about it for weeks; took alone in bedroom; no note; intended to die; messaged friend 1 h later, friend's parent called ambulance.\nNow: relieved to be alive; still low; occasional thoughts life is not worth it, no plan or intent today.\nBackground: months of bullying by group in Y10, edited (non-sexual) photos shared in group chat; stopped going to lunch; low mood, poor sleep, withdrawn ~3 months. No previous self-harm. No alcohol or drugs. No disclosure of abuse or exploitation.\nProtective: mum, best friend, wants to do GCSE art, relieved.\nRisk: to self raised (planned, high-lethality OD; unresolved trigger; depressive symptoms). Others: none.\nPlan: safety plan written with Chloe and mum; mum to lock away all medicines and remove sharps; school DSL contacted Monday; report images to platform and police advice; CAMHS follow-up within 48 h; Childline and crisis line given; GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT1 psychiatry","make":["Set out confidentiality and its limits in the first minute.","Walk through the overdose in order: before, during, after.","Ask what she expected to happen; intent matters more than the number.","Ask specifically about online abuse and whether any images were sexual.","Ask about thoughts now and what would make them stronger.","Finish with a plan that names who does what at home and at school."],"next":"Chloe agrees for her mum to hear about the bullying. Mum cries, then locks away the medicines that afternoon. The school safeguarding lead meets them on Monday, and CAMHS sees Chloe on Tuesday and starts weekly sessions.","marks":["Explained confidentiality and its limits.","Got a clear history of the overdose: planning, setting, concealment, help-seeking and intent.","Explored the bullying: nature, escalation, online spread and support.","Screened for depression, self-harm, substances and safeguarding.","Assessed current suicidal thoughts and protective factors.","Agreed a safety plan involving mum, school and CAMHS."],"pitfalls":["Letting mum sit in for the whole interview.","Judging intent from her calm manner today.","Not asking whether any images were sexual.","Using a risk score to justify discharge.","Forgetting means restriction at home."],"refs":["1. NICE NG225: self-harm assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG134: depression in children and young people (2019; checked Nov 2026)","3. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF for Children: fluoxetine (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, under-16s who self-harm are usually admitted to the children's ward and seen by CAMHS before discharge. Parents expect to be involved; explain what you will share before you start.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-58","n":1458,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏷️","title":"Barcode fears: belief or psychosis?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:30, CMHT new assessment","where":"Community mental health team clinic room","text":"Neil Corrigan, 45, a delivery driver, told his GP that the barcode on a blood tube carried 'the mark of the beast' and was part of a worldwide plot. He left without his bloods. The GP asks: is this psychosis? Neil has agreed to come in, a little reluctantly."},"task":"In about ten minutes, explore Mr Corrigan's belief (where it came from, how firmly it is held, how it affects his life), screen for psychotic illness, and tell him your diagnostic impression.","first60":["Read the GP letter: exactly what he said, why the bloods were needed, and any past mental health history.","Check the record for previous CMHT contact, substance use and any risk history.","Decide on your neutral opening line: curious, not challenging.","Know your definitions: delusion versus overvalued idea versus obsession.","Have the GP's number ready so the blood test plan can be agreed today."],"ask":["'Can you tell me how this started for you?'","'Where did you first come across the idea?'","'Do others in your family or church see it the same way?'","'Is there any chance it might be different from how you see it?'","'How does it affect your day, with barcodes everywhere?'","'Has it ever stopped you or your family getting medical care?'","'Do you ever get messages meant for you from the TV, radio or signs?'","'Have you heard voices or felt your thoughts were being controlled?'"],"recognise":["The belief came from years of Bible study with his church, and other members share it: culturally understandable.","He can consider that he might be wrong, so conviction is not absolute: against a delusion.","It is preoccupying and has made him miss a blood test: an overvalued idea with real costs.","No hallucinations, passivity, thought interference or ideas of reference: no psychotic features.","Normal mood, sleep and work, and no substances: no secondary cause.","Watch for risk: refusing care for himself or his children, or anger at people who disagree."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell him there is no sign of a psychotic illness; this is a strongly held belief that fits his faith. Agree a blood test plan with the GP and discharge with a clear route back.","r":"Right: an honest impression, respect for his faith, and the practical problem (the bloods) solved.","best":true},{"c":"Start a low-dose antipsychotic in case it is early psychosis.","r":"Wrong: there is no psychotic illness. Medication would cause harm and damage trust.","best":false},{"c":"Try to persuade him the barcode belief is false before he leaves.","r":"Unhelpful: arguing hardens beliefs and breaks rapport. Testing conviction gently is enough to tell you what you need to know.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity to decide about blood tests: he may refuse, after a clear explanation of what the test is for.","Under the Equality Act 2010, religion or belief is protected; assess with respect and without ridicule.","If the belief led him to refuse essential care for a child, that would be a safeguarding concern.","Share the outcome with the GP; he has agreed to the referral."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for explaining it so clearly. I don't see any sign of a mental illness. This is a strong belief that comes from your faith, and others in your church share it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"About the blood tests: they're there to check your health. I'll ask your GP whether the tube can be labelled out of your sight. Would that work for you?"},{"to":"GP","s":"No psychotic illness. The barcode belief is an overvalued idea rooted in his church's teaching. Please consider labelling the tubes out of his sight. Discharged, with a clear route back if things change."}],"write":"CMHT new assessment. 45M, delivery driver. GP referral: belief blood tube barcode is 'mark of the beast'; ?psychosis.\nBelief: from Bible study (Revelation) in his Baptist church since his teens; several members and his wife share it. Avoids barcodes when he can; declined GP bloods. Can accept he 'might be wrong about the tubes'; distressed by it at times; not preoccupied all day.\nPsychotic screen: no AH/VH, no passivity, no thought insertion, withdrawal or broadcast, no ideas of reference, no other delusions.\nMood normal; sleep normal; working full time. No alcohol excess or drugs. No previous mental illness; no family history.\nRisk: no harm to self or others; no anger at non-believers; daughter (14) fully vaccinated, had bloods last year. Missed own bloods: health risk.\nImpression: overvalued idea, culturally congruent. No psychotic illness.\nPlan: shared impression; GP letter suggesting tubes labelled out of sight; discharge; re-refer if new symptoms.\nDr Chinonso, ST4 general adult psychiatry","make":["Open with curiosity: 'How did this start for you?'","Find the source of the belief and whether his community shares it.","Test conviction gently by linking to what he has said, never by arguing.","Do a full psychotic screen: voices, passivity, thought interference, references.","Ask what the belief costs him: care refused, conflicts, children.","Give a clear impression in plain words and fix the practical problem."],"next":"The GP practice agrees that the nurse will label the tubes after Neil leaves the room. He has his bloods the following week. The HbA1c is borderline, and he joins the diabetes prevention programme.","marks":["Explored the origin and development of the belief.","Placed the belief within his faith, family and church.","Tested conviction gently and assessed its effect on his life.","Screened for hallucinations, passivity, thought interference and ideas of reference.","Checked mood, substances, past history and risk, including to his child.","Gave a clear impression: overvalued idea, not psychosis."],"pitfalls":["Calling a culturally shared belief a delusion.","Arguing with him about Revelation.","Skipping the psychotic screen because the belief sounds religious.","Forgetting to ask about refusal of care for his children.","Ending without a plan for the blood tests."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Recommendations for psychiatrists on spirituality and religion (PS03/2011; checked Nov 2026)","3. Equality Act 2010: religion or belief (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK patients come from many faiths. If you do not know a belief system, say so and ask. Saying 'Is this something your church teaches?' is respectful and gives you the key diagnostic fact.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-59","n":1459,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🤰","title":"Low mood: baby at home, pregnant again","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:00, perinatal clinic","where":"Perinatal mental health team, community hub","text":"Priya Lawson, 35, has a seven-month-old daughter, Isha, and has just found out she is 10 weeks pregnant. Her GP has referred her with persistent sadness. Isha is with Priya's mother in the waiting area. You have one ten-minute slot before the team meeting to find out how unwell she is and whether she and the children are safe."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of Mrs Lawson's low mood and assess risk to herself, to Isha and to the pregnancy.","first60":["Read the GP letter: duration of symptoms, any medication, the referral question.","Check the maternity record: booking visit done, Isha's birth (mode, complications, special care), any past postnatal illness.","Look for any past bipolar disorder or psychosis, in her or her family.","Note whether a health visitor or midwife has raised concerns.","Decide in advance to ask about harm to Isha plainly and kindly."],"ask":["'How's your mood been, and when did it start?'","'What's Isha like? What's it like being with her?'","'How did you feel when you found out you were pregnant?'","'Have you had times when you felt very high or needed little sleep?'","'Do you worry something is wrong with Isha?'","'Has it ever felt as if life isn't worth going on with?'","'Some mums have frightening thoughts about their baby coming to harm. Has that happened to you?'","'Who helps you at home?'"],"recognise":["Low mood since a few months after Isha's birth, worse with an unplanned pregnancy: a depressive episode across both perinatal periods.","Guilt ('bad mother'), tearfulness, poor sleep beyond the baby's waking and hopelessness: moderate severity.","Worry that Isha is ill despite normal weights is anxious, not delusional, if she accepts reassurance even briefly.","Passive wish not to wake, no plan: risk present, needs a crisis plan.","Her mother had postnatal depression: a family risk factor. No mania history, so an antidepressant is reasonable.","No thoughts of harming Isha and no neglect: no safeguarding referral, but the health visitor should know."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose a moderate perinatal depression, offer sertraline 50 mg after discussing risks in pregnancy plus CBT, and inform the midwife, health visitor and GP with a crisis plan.","r":"Right: treats a real illness in pregnancy with a balanced choice and wraps support around both children.","best":true},{"c":"Avoid all medication because she is pregnant and wait for therapy.","r":"Not by default: untreated moderate depression in pregnancy carries its own risks. Discuss both sides and let her choose.","best":false},{"c":"Refer Isha to children's social care because her mother is depressed.","r":"Wrong: depression alone is not a safeguarding concern. Refer only if there is harm or risk to the child.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity to make decisions about treatment in pregnancy; give balanced information and record her choice.","Share information with the midwife and health visitor with her consent, as part of her care.","If thoughts of harming Isha or serious neglect emerged, a safeguarding referral would override confidentiality.","Ask her consent before speaking to her husband."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Priya, you've been carrying a lot: a young baby, a surprise pregnancy, and feeling this low. That isn't you being a bad mother. It's depression, and it's treatable."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Sertraline is one of the medicines we use most often in pregnancy. There are small risks to talk through, and there are risks from untreated depression too. It's your choice, and I'll help you weigh it."},{"to":"Health visitor","s":"I've seen Priya Lawson in perinatal clinic. She has moderate depression; no risk to Isha identified. She's agreed I can share this. Could you increase your visits and let us know of any concerns?"}],"write":"Perinatal clinic. 35F, G2P1, 10/40 (unplanned). Daughter Isha 7/12 (EMCS, 24 h SCBU, well since). GP referral: low mood.\nHPC: low mood ~4 months, worse since positive test 2 weeks ago. Tearful most days, anhedonia, poor sleep (lies awake even when Isha sleeps), low appetite, poor concentration, guilt ('bad mother'), hopeless. Worries Isha is ill; HV weights normal; accepts reassurance briefly.\nBonding: loves Isha, feels flat with her at times; caring for her well (per mother).\nPregnancy: unplanned; booked with midwife; taking folic acid.\nPPH: none. No elation or reduced need for sleep. No psychotic symptoms. FH: mother had postnatal depression. Alcohol: none since pregnancy. Supportive husband (long hours) and mother.\nRisk: passive death wish ('wouldn't mind not waking'); no plan or intent; protective factors: children, mother, faith. No thoughts of harming Isha; no neglect. Pregnancy: attending antenatal care.\nImpression: moderate depressive episode, perinatal.\nPlan: discussed sertraline 50 mg OD (risks/benefits documented); CBT via perinatal psychology; TFT, FBC, ferritin; midwife, HV and GP informed with consent; crisis plan given; review 2 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, ST5 perinatal psychiatry","make":["Cover both perinatal periods: since Isha's birth and this pregnancy.","Ask about bonding and how she feels with Isha, not just whether she can cope.","Screen for mania and psychosis before you discuss antidepressants.","Ask about harm to Isha directly, normalising intrusive thoughts.","Separate risk to herself, to Isha and to the pregnancy, and grade each.","End with who will be told and a named crisis route."],"next":"Priya starts sertraline after talking it through with her husband. The perinatal psychologist begins CBT the following month. At 28 weeks her mood has lifted, and a pre-birth plan is written with the midwife.","marks":["Explored onset, duration and course of low mood.","Elicited core, biological and cognitive symptoms of depression.","Asked about Isha's birth, bonding and this pregnancy.","Screened for bipolar and psychotic features, including ideas about Isha.","Assessed suicide risk and risk to Isha and the pregnancy.","Identified her support network and agreed a clear plan."],"pitfalls":["Asking only 'Are you coping?' and missing bonding.","Avoiding the question about harm to the baby.","Forgetting the mania screen before an antidepressant.","Stopping or refusing all medication because she is pregnant.","Making a safeguarding referral without any risk to the child."],"refs":["1. NICE CG192: antenatal and postnatal mental health (updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: sertraline, pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)","4. UKTIS: use of SSRIs in pregnancy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, health visitors see every family with a young child and midwives every pregnant woman. They are your eyes at home; with consent, tell them your plan the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-60","n":1460,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"🧠","title":"CBT for depression: how it could help","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:00, outpatient clinic","where":"Community mental health clinic","text":"Martin Ellery, 43, an operations manager at an insurance firm, has been low since he had to tell team members they were being made redundant. Mirtazapine from his GP has fixed his sleep and appetite but not his mood, and he has been signed off for six weeks. His GP has asked you to explain CBT to him. No mental state or risk assessment is needed today."},"task":"In about ten minutes, explain CBT to Mr Ellery using his own story, cover how it would help and what it involves, and answer his questions.","first60":["Read the GP letter: the trigger, symptoms, mirtazapine dose and how long he's been on it.","Check the local NHS Talking Therapies route: self-referral link or clinic referral, and current wait.","Have a pen and paper ready to draw the CBT cycle with his own example.","Find a leaflet on CBT for depression (NHS or Mind) to hand over.","Plan to spend no more than two minutes on history: this is an explanation task."],"ask":["'What went through your mind when you had to tell your team?'","'What have you stopped doing since then?'","'Have you ever felt like this before?'","'What were things like growing up? What did your parents expect?'","'What do you already know about CBT?'","'What would you want to be different at the end of therapy?'","'In your words, how might CBT help you?'"],"recognise":["An excessive sense of responsibility ('I betrayed them') after redundancies he did not cause: a core belief CBT can target.","Withdrawal from friends, work and home keeps the low mood going: a clear behavioural target.","Perfectionism, demanding parents and protecting his younger brothers may explain why this hit so hard.","A low spell at university when he could not protect his brothers suggests a repeating pattern.","Mirtazapine helped sleep and appetite but not mood after two months at 30 mg: worth a medication review with the GP.","He is curious but sceptical about 'just talking': answer that directly."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain CBT using his own cycle of thoughts, feelings, body and behaviour; describe 12-20 weekly sessions with homework; advise staying on mirtazapine; refer to NHS Talking Therapies.","r":"Right: personal, practical and honest about the work involved, and medication continues while therapy starts.","best":true},{"c":"Give a general talk about CBT theory without linking it to his redundancy guilt.","r":"Weak: he won't see how it applies to him, and engagement drops. Use his own example.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him to stop mirtazapine as CBT will replace it.","r":"Wrong: stopping abruptly risks relapse and discontinuation symptoms. Combined treatment is often best for moderate to severe depression.","best":false}],"law":["Consent to therapy is his choice after balanced information.","Explain confidentiality in therapy and its limits, and what his employer would and would not be told.","Fit notes come from his GP; occupational health needs his consent to contact his employer."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"It's more than talking, Martin. CBT gives you practical tools to break the cycle you're stuck in, between the guilty thoughts and pulling away from people."},{"to":"Patient","s":"It's usually around 12 to 20 weekly sessions, about an hour each, with a trained therapist. Between sessions there's homework, like a thought diary."},{"to":"GP","s":"I've explained CBT to Mr Ellery and he has agreed to a Talking Therapies referral. His mood hasn't lifted on mirtazapine 30 mg after two months; please review the dose or consider a switch."}],"write":"Clinic. 43M, operations manager, off work 6/52. GP referral: explain CBT.\nBrief history: low ~3 months after informing team of redundancies; guilt ('I betrayed them'), anhedonia, low energy, poor concentration, withdrawal from friends and wife. Perfectionist; authoritarian parents; protected younger brothers; low episode at university. Mirtazapine 30 mg nocte 2/12: sleep and appetite better, mood unchanged.\nExplained: CBT model using his example (situation, guilt thoughts, low mood, fatigue, withdrawal); NATs and thought diary; behavioural activation and graded tasks; here-and-now focus; 12-20 weekly sessions, ~1 h, trained therapist; homework; may feel harder at first; evidence comparable to antidepressants with lower relapse.\nQuestions answered: continue mirtazapine; confidentiality.\nTeach-back correct. Leaflet given. Referral to NHS Talking Therapies (high intensity CBT) sent. GP letter re medication review.\nNo MSE or risk assessment requested; he volunteered no thoughts of self-harm.\nDr Chinonso, CT3 psychiatry","make":["Keep the history to two minutes and get one concrete example of a guilty thought.","Draw the cycle with his words in each box.","Name the two tools: catching and testing thoughts; scheduling activity.","Be specific on practicalities: who, how often, how long, homework.","Tell him early sessions can feel harder.","Finish with teach-back and a clear referral route."],"next":"Martin self-refers to NHS Talking Therapies that evening and starts high-intensity CBT four weeks later. His GP raises mirtazapine to 45 mg. By session eight he is seeing friends again and planning a phased return.","marks":["Took a brief, relevant history of the trigger and his response.","Linked his early experiences to his current difficulties.","Explained the CBT model using his own example.","Described core techniques: thought records, behavioural activation, graded tasks.","Gave practical details: number, length, therapist, homework.","Answered his questions, including medication, and checked understanding."],"pitfalls":["Spending the whole time on history and running out of time to explain.","Using jargon like 'cognitive distortions' without explaining.","Describing CBT in general terms only.","Promising a quick fix or that it will feel good straight away.","Advising him to stop his antidepressant."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026)","2. NHS website: cognitive behavioural therapy (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: mirtazapine (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: NHS Talking Therapies (formerly IAPT) takes self-referrals online. Telling patients they can refer themselves today is practical help and saves weeks.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-61","n":1461,"prog":"Psychotherapy","icon":"👨‍👩‍👧","title":"Family therapy: a father's doubts","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:30, early intervention clinic","where":"Early intervention in psychosis (EIP) team base, family room","text":"Ffion Pritchard, 15, was given a diagnosis of first-episode schizophrenia a fortnight ago and is now under the early intervention service. Her care coordinator has noticed frequent rows between her parents, and on those days Ffion often refuses her aripiprazole and stays in her room. Her father, Owen Pritchard, 44, thinks his wife is too protective and that Ffion should be pushed to go out and see friends. Ffion has agreed that the team can share information with him. You are seeing him alone to explain family therapy and hear his worries and hopes for it. Her diagnosis does not need to be revisited."},"task":"In about ten minutes, hear Mr Pritchard's worries, explain what family therapy involves and how it helps, address his fear of being blamed, and agree whether he will take part.","first60":["Check the consent-to-share note: Ffion agreed the team can talk with her father. Stay inside that.","Read the care coordinator's last entry: which days she refused aripiprazole, and what happened at home beforehand.","Find out who delivers family intervention in your EIP team and how referrals are made, so you can name a real next step.","Have the family intervention leaflet and the local carers' assessment referral route to hand.","Plan the ten minutes: about three on him and home, five on family therapy, two to agree and close."],"ask":["'What worries you most about Ffion right now?'","'What makes that feel so frightening for you?'","'What happens at home when you try to get her out of her room?'","'What does Ffion do on the days after a row?'","'When things get heated, what sort of things do you say to her?'","'Has anyone ever been pushed, hit or hurt during the arguments?'","'How do you think your wife sees what's going on?'","'Would you be willing to come along, and who else might come?'"],"recognise":["His drive is fear for her future, often rooted in a relative's illness: start with that, not with what he is doing wrong.","Rows followed by refused tablets and more withdrawal is the clearest sign the family pattern is affecting her recovery.","Calling her lazy usually means he is reading negative symptoms as a choice: this is what psychoeducation in family work targets.","Criticism from one parent and over-protection from the other are both forms of stress, and both come from love.","Fear of blame is the main barrier to engagement; say plainly that therapy is not about fault.","Any physical violence at home with a 15-year-old in the house is a safeguarding matter and changes the plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Hear his fear, explain family intervention (whole family, no blame, a minimum of 10 sessions across 3 to 12 months), link stress to relapse in plain words, ask directly if he will come, refer, and offer a carer's assessment.","r":"Right: he feels heard, understands what he is agreeing to, and leaves with a named next step and support for himself.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him his criticism is causing her relapses and that he needs to back off.","r":"Wrong: he will feel blamed and is likely to refuse therapy, and it ignores the over-protection side and the illness itself.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest he and his wife try couples counselling first and leave Ffion out of it.","r":"Wrong: the evidence is for family intervention that includes the young person where practical; couples work does not address her illness or relapse.","best":false}],"law":["Ffion is 15: she has agreed to sharing with her father. Share what helps him support her; keep details she has asked to stay private.","If any physical violence or a risk to Ffion emerges, follow the Children Act 1989 duty and make a safeguarding referral to children's social care the same day.","Under the Care Act 2014, the local authority must offer each parent a carer's assessment.","Record his consent to the family intervention referral and to contact about appointments."],"say":[{"to":"Father","s":"You clearly love Ffion and you're frightened for her. Family therapy isn't about finding who's to blame. It's about helping all of you pull in the same direction."},{"to":"Father","s":"It's usually ten or more sessions, spread across three months to a year. You, your wife and Ffion if she can manage it, with a trained therapist from our team."},{"to":"EIP care coordinator","s":"I've seen Mr Pritchard. He's agreed to family intervention and will ask his wife. Can you add them to the FI waiting list and refer him for a carer's assessment?"}],"write":"EIP clinic. Seen Mr Owen Pritchard (44), father of Ffion (15, FEP, under EIP), alone. Ffion consented to information sharing.\nConcerns: fears Ffion's future if out of school/socially isolated; brother has schizophrenia. Feels wife too protective. Rows when he pushes Ffion to go out; on those days she refuses aripiprazole and withdraws. Admits calling her lazy. No physical violence; no safeguarding concerns raised. Younger son keeping away from home.\nExplained family intervention: whole family, at least 10 sessions over 3-12 months, psychoeducation, communication, problem solving, early warning signs; stress (criticism and over-protection) and relapse explained without blame; evidence for fewer relapses.\nOutcome: agrees to attend; will discuss with wife tonight. Concern re blame addressed.\nPlan: refer to EIP family intervention (care coordinator); carer's assessment referral (local authority); FI leaflet and Rethink carers' info given. Safety net: EIP duty line in hours, NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 if immediate danger.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Start with his fear, not his behaviour: ask what drives it.","Find the pattern: what he does, what his wife does, what Ffion does next.","Explain format in numbers: who comes, how many sessions, how long, where.","Name both criticism and over-protection as stress, and both as love.","Ask the direct question: 'Will you come?' and name who else.","Leave him with something for himself: a carer's assessment."],"next":"Mr Pritchard brings his wife to the first family intervention session three weeks later. Ffion joins for the last ten minutes. By session five they have agreed one small weekly outing chosen by Ffion, and the rows about her room have stopped. She has not missed a dose since.","marks":["Heard his fear and what drives it before explaining anything.","Explored the pattern at home, including medication refusal after rows and safety.","Explained what family therapy is and how it runs, with numbers.","Explained stress and relapse without blaming him.","Asked directly about willingness and agreed a next step.","Offered a carer's assessment and a named crisis route."],"pitfalls":["Telling him his criticism causes relapse: he will walk away.","Lecturing on therapy models instead of format, aims and duration.","Re-explaining schizophrenia when the task says not to.","Never asking whether he will actually come.","Forgetting to check for violence at home with a 15-year-old present."],"refs":["1. NICE CG155: psychosis and schizophrenia in children and young people (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE QS102: psychosis and schizophrenia in children and young people, quality standard (checked Nov 2026)","3. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)","4. Rethink Mental Illness: support for carers (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, family intervention is a core NICE-recommended treatment in early psychosis, not an optional extra. Families expect to be told plainly how many sessions, who comes and what happens.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-62","n":1462,"prog":"Learning Disability Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"Can he decide about sertraline?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 11:00, psychiatry outpatients","where":"Community learning disability team clinic room","text":"Liam Fairweather, 27, has a mild intellectual disability and lives in supported accommodation. For several months he has been low, and he has had sessions with the psychology team with only a small improvement. The team is now thinking about sertraline. He has come to clinic with his support worker, Jade. You need to take a brief history, assess whether he can decide about sertraline, hear his worries and tell him the outcome."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a brief mood history, give sertraline information in plain words, test the four abilities, answer his worries, and tell him the outcome.","first60":["Read his health passport or communication profile: does he read, prefer pictures, need extra time?","Get the easy-read sertraline leaflet and a simple mood scale (faces) ready before he sits down.","Fix the decision in one sentence: 'whether to start sertraline for low mood now'. Capacity is about that decision only.","Check recent bloods (TFTs, FBC, U&E) and his current medicines for interactions.","Plan: two minutes history, two minutes information, four minutes testing and worries, two minutes outcome."],"ask":["'Is it okay if Jade stays with us?'","'How have you been feeling lately? What do you call that feeling?'","'How are you sleeping and eating?'","'What did you used to enjoy that you've stopped doing?'","'When did the sad feeling start? Did anything happen around then?'","'Do you ever think about hurting yourself?'","'What is the tablet for? Tell me your way.'","'What might happen if you don't take it?'"],"recognise":["An intellectual disability never decides capacity by itself: the test is about this decision, now, with support.","Capacity needs all four: understand, retain long enough to decide, use or weigh, and communicate a choice in any way.","Repeating your words back is not understanding: ask him to say it his way, or show you with the leaflet pictures.","Weighing means he links benefits, side effects and not taking it to his own life, not just lists them.","Low mood can colour a decision; check he is not refusing or agreeing out of hopelessness or to please.","Acquiescence is common: avoid yes/no questions when testing understanding."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a brief history, give the information in small chunks with the easy-read leaflet, test each of the four abilities with open questions, and tell him he can decide for himself.","r":"Right: capacity is presumed, all practicable steps have been taken to support him, and the evidence is in his own words.","best":true},{"c":"Ask Jade whether Liam would want the tablet and decide in his best interests.","r":"Wrong: best interests only applies if he lacks capacity after support. Skipping the assessment breaches the Mental Capacity Act.","best":false},{"c":"Read through the full patient leaflet with him, then ask 'Do you understand?'","r":"Wrong: too much information in one go and a yes/no check. It neither supports him nor tests understanding.","best":false}],"law":["Mental Capacity Act 2005: assume capacity; take all practicable steps to help him decide; an unwise decision is not proof of incapacity.","The question is about one decision at one time: starting sertraline, today.","Record the assessment on the trust's capacity form: the decision, the support given, each of the four abilities with his words, and the outcome.","If he had lacked capacity: best interests decision involving those who know him; an IMCA is needed only for serious medical treatment when nobody appropriate can be consulted."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"This tablet is called sertraline. It can help the sad feeling. You take one tablet each morning. It takes a few weeks to start working."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You understood it, remembered it and thought about the good and bad bits. That means you can make this choice yourself, Liam."},{"to":"GP","s":"Liam has capacity to consent to sertraline and has chosen to start. 25 mg each morning for a week, then 50 mg. Please see the attached capacity assessment."}],"write":"CLDT outpatients. Liam Fairweather, 27M, mild ID, supported accommodation. With support worker Jade (present with his agreement).\nHx: low mood ('the sad feeling') ~4 months, poor sleep, reduced appetite, stopped drama group. Onset after housemate moved out and nan's death. 6 psychology sessions (adapted CBT): helped a little. Denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts. Rarely drinks.\nCapacity assessment. Decision: whether to start sertraline for depression. Support given: plain words, short chunks, easy-read leaflet with pictures, extra time, Jade present.\nUnderstand: says 'it's for the sad feeling, one in the morning'. Retain: recalled name, purpose and two side effects after 3 min. Weigh: 'might feel sick at first but I want to go back to drama; if I don't take it I might stay sad'. Communicate: clear verbal choice to start.\nOutcome: has capacity to consent to sertraline. Consents.\nPlan: sertraline 25 mg mane for 7 days then 50 mg mane. Easy-read leaflet given. Continue psychology. Review 2 weeks (CLDT nurse). Staff to report worse mood, agitation or self-harm thoughts; NHS 111 option 2 out of hours. GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Ask him to explain in his own words, never 'do you understand?'","Give information in three small chunks and check after each one.","Leave a pause before testing retention: talk about something else first.","Test weighing by asking what happens if he doesn't take it.","Tell him the outcome directly, in a sentence he can repeat.","Document each of the four abilities with his own quotes."],"next":"Liam starts sertraline 25 mg. At two weeks his CLDT nurse finds mild nausea that has settled, and the dose goes up to 50 mg. By eight weeks he is back at drama group on Thursday evenings and rates his mood with a smiling face on the chart most days.","marks":["Took a brief history including trigger, psychology and self-harm thoughts.","Gave sertraline information in small, plain chunks with an easy-read leaflet.","Tested all four abilities with open questions.","Explored and answered his worries.","Told him the outcome directly and agreed next steps.","Documented the decision, support given and evidence for each ability."],"pitfalls":["Assuming the intellectual disability means he can't decide.","Testing capacity before giving him any information.","Asking yes/no questions and taking 'yes' as understanding.","Reeling off a long list of side effects at speed.","Talking to Jade instead of Liam."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005 and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG54: mental health problems in people with learning disabilities (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022) (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: sertraline (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS England: STOMP, stopping over-medication of people with a learning disability (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK law presumes capacity for every adult, including people with intellectual disability. Your job is to support the decision first, then test it, and write down exactly what he said.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-63","n":1463,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🩸","title":"Starting clozapine at home","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:00, CMHT clinic","where":"Community mental health team base, clinic room 2","text":"Aisha Rahman, 32, has schizophrenia that has not responded to two antipsychotics. On olanzapine 20 mg at night she still hears derogatory voices and believes her neighbours are plotting against her. At her last review the team suggested clozapine, started at home with daily Home Treatment Team (HTT) input. She said no then, but has told her care coordinator she is ready to try it. She has come in today. You need to explain clozapine, the home start and the side effects she must know, and answer her questions."},"task":"In about ten minutes, check how she is now, explain why clozapine, how it starts at home, the blood tests and the side effects she must act on, and answer her questions.","first60":["Confirm the two previous antipsychotic trials, doses and durations in the notes, and that adherence was good.","Check smoking status, weight, last bloods and ECG, and any history of bowel problems, fits or heart disease.","Have the clozapine patient leaflet, the HTT number and the monitoring service registration form ready.","Plan: one minute on now, then why, before, start, bloods, side effects, rules. Keep every answer short.","Agree with the HTT manager that daily visits can start on the chosen day 1."],"ask":["'What are the voices saying at the moment, and how often?'","'How often do you manage to take the olanzapine?'","'Have you had any thoughts of hurting yourself?'","'What have you heard about clozapine already?'","'What worries you most about it?'","'What would you hope to be different if it works?'","'Do you smoke? How many a day?'","'Is a pregnancy possible in the next year or so?'"],"recognise":["Two adequate antipsychotic trials with good adherence and persisting symptoms is treatment resistance: clozapine is the evidence-based next step.","Rule out the common causes of apparent non-response first: missed doses, cannabis or alcohol, too low a dose or too short a trial.","Neutropenia risk is highest in the first 18 weeks, which is why bloods are weekly then.","Myocarditis is most likely in the first 4 to 8 weeks: fever, chest pain, breathlessness or a fast pulse need same-day review.","Constipation on clozapine can progress to fatal bowel obstruction: ask about bowels at every contact.","Smoking induces clozapine metabolism; stopping can raise levels sharply within days."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain why clozapine, the baseline tests, a 12.5 mg start with slow rise and daily HTT checks, the blood schedule, common and serious side effects, and the missed-dose and smoking rules, then teach-back.","r":"Right: she gets consent-quality information in plain words and knows exactly what to act on.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her that clozapine is very safe and the blood tests are just routine.","r":"Wrong: she needs to know about neutropenia, myocarditis and constipation to recognise them. False reassurance undermines informed consent and safety.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest a third ordinary antipsychotic first because clozapine is a lot of work.","r":"Wrong: after two adequate trials, NICE recommends clozapine. Another standard drug is unlikely to work and delays recovery.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is consenting: record the information given, her questions, and her consent in the notes.","Clozapine can only be dispensed once she is registered with a clozapine monitoring service and has a 'green' blood result.","DVLA: she must already tell the DVLA about schizophrenia if she drives; advise no driving while sedated during titration.","Off-licence elements, such as hyoscine for drooling, need explaining and recording."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Clozapine is the medicine with the best chance of quietening voices that haven't settled on other tablets. Around half of people in your position improve, sometimes a lot."},{"to":"Patient","s":"If you get a sore throat, a temperature or flu-like feelings, ring the team that day for a blood test. And tell us if your bowels haven't opened for three days."},{"to":"HTT manager","s":"Aisha has consented to start clozapine at home. Baseline bloods and ECG are booked. Can you start daily pulse, BP and temperature checks from day 1?"}],"write":"CMHT clinic. Aisha Rahman, 32F, treatment-resistant schizophrenia (risperidone 6 mg x 8 wks, olanzapine 20 mg x 12 months, good adherence per care coordinator). Persisting derogatory voices most evenings, belief neighbours plotting. No thoughts of self-harm; no harm to others. Smokes 15 cigarettes/day. No alcohol or drugs. Not planning pregnancy; uses contraceptive implant.\nDiscussed clozapine: rationale, ~half improve, may take months; baseline FBC, U&E, LFT, lipids, HbA1c, troponin, CRP, ECG, weight/BMI/waist, BP; registration with monitoring service; start 12.5 mg with slow rise, olanzapine reduced gradually; HTT daily obs 2 weeks; FBC every week x18, two-weekly to 1 yr, then monthly; common SEs (sedation, drooling, constipation, weight, fast pulse, dizziness); serious (neutropenia, myocarditis, bowel obstruction, seizures) and red flags; 48 h missed rule; smoking interaction; no driving during titration; pregnancy planning with team.\nTeach-back correct. Leaflet given. Consents to start.\nPlan: baseline bloods/ECG today; register with monitoring service; start date agreed with HTT; senna 7.5 mg at night from day 1; consultant to prescribe titration chart.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Ask what she already knows and fears before explaining anything.","Keep each answer to a couple of short sentences, then check.","Give the blood schedule as numbers: 18 weeks weekly, two-weekly to a year, then monthly.","Name the three red flags out loud: infection signs, chest pain or breathlessness, no bowel opening for 3 days.","Never skip smoking and the 48-hour rule.","Close with teach-back of the red flags and the HTT number."],"next":"Aisha starts clozapine on a Monday with HTT visits each morning. On day 6 her pulse is 108 and she feels flushed; a same-day troponin and CRP are normal and it settles. By week 10 on 350 mg, the voices come only at night and are quieter. She is handed back to her care coordinator at week 4 and attends the clozapine clinic weekly.","marks":["Checked current symptoms, adherence, risk and what she knows and fears.","Explained why clozapine and a realistic chance of improvement.","Explained baseline tests, registration and the home titration with HTT checks.","Gave the blood monitoring schedule and the reason for it.","Covered common and serious side effects with clear red flags.","Covered missed doses, smoking and pregnancy, then used teach-back."],"pitfalls":["Lecturing for five minutes without letting her ask questions.","Saying 'it's very safe' and skipping the serious risks.","Forgetting constipation as a potentially fatal side effect.","Running out of time before smoking and the missed-dose rule.","Not giving her a number to ring for a sore throat or fever."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF and SmPC: clozapine (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and gastrointestinal hypomotility (checked Nov 2026)","4. Choice and Medication: clozapine patient information (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, clozapine is often started at home with the Home Treatment Team. The pharmacy will not release a supply without a current 'green' blood result from the monitoring service.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-65","n":1465,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🦵","title":"Leg weakness: neurological check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 10:30, acute adult ward","where":"Treatment room on an adult psychiatric ward","text":"Joanne Fletcher, 48, was admitted last week with a depressive episode. She drinks heavily at weekends. Over three weeks she has told nurses both legs feel heavy and numb, and she has tripped twice on the ward. The team wants to rule out a neurological cause such as a peripheral neuropathy or a spinal problem. A history is not needed: you are asked to carry out a sensory and motor assessment of her lower limbs and say whether there is a deficit."},"task":"In about ten minutes, examine tone, power, reflexes, coordination, gait and sensation in both legs, and state whether there is a neurological deficit and its pattern.","first60":["Read the falls entries and check her medicines and alcohol history in the notes; you won't take a history today.","Get a tendon hammer, a 128 Hz tuning fork, cotton wool and a disposable neurotip, and ask for a chaperone.","Check the room has space to walk 5 metres and a chair or wall nearby for Romberg's test.","Plan: one minute look, four minutes motor, two minutes coordination and gait, two minutes sensation, one minute close.","Have the falls care plan and the bloods request form ready for afterwards."],"ask":["'Is it alright if I examine both your legs? Would you like a chaperone?'","'Do you have any pain in your hips, knees or ankles before I start?'","'Lift your leg up off the bed and stop me pushing it down.'","'Can you feel this, and does it feel the same on both sides?'","'Close your eyes. Tell me when the buzzing stops.'","'Is your big toe moving up or down?'","'Run your heel down your shin from the knee to the ankle.'","'Stand with your feet together and close your eyes. I'm right here.'"],"recognise":["Symmetrical stocking sensory loss, reduced vibration and joint position at the toes, and absent ankle jerks point to a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy.","Alcohol, thiamine deficiency, B12 deficiency and diabetes are the common causes in this setting; check them all.","Brisk reflexes, raised tone, clonus, extensor plantars or a sensory level suggest a spinal cord problem and need urgent medical review and imaging.","Absent ankle jerks with extensor plantars is the classic mixed picture of B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration).","A positive Romberg's test means the sensory input from the feet is poor (sensory ataxia), explaining trips in the dark.","Unsteadiness, confusion or abnormal eye movements in a drinker raise Wernicke's encephalopathy: give parenteral thiamine without delay."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete a full lower limb sensory and motor assessment, report a symmetrical length-dependent sensorimotor neuropathy with no upper motor neurone signs, start thiamine, send bloods and refer to the medical team for nerve studies.","r":"Right: answers the question asked, rules out a cord problem at the bedside, and starts safe, specific next steps.","best":true},{"c":"Put the numbness down to anxiety and depression and ask the nurses to encourage her to walk more.","r":"Wrong: she has objective signs and has fallen twice. Missing a neuropathy or cord lesion is a serious error, and walking more increases fall risk.","best":false},{"c":"Test power and reflexes only, then refer to neurology.","r":"Wrong: without sensation, coordination and gait you cannot describe the pattern, and the referral will come back asking for it.","best":false}],"law":["Verbal consent for each part; offer a chaperone, record their name, and keep her covered except the part being examined.","Document the findings in a standard format (inspection, tone, power by MRC grade, reflexes, plantars, coordination, sensation) so others can compare.","Two falls on the ward: complete the falls risk assessment and inform the nurse in charge the same day.","If she declines parts of the assessment, record what was declined and offer again later."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"The nerves to your feet aren't carrying feeling as well as they should, and both ankles are a little weak. That explains the heaviness and the trips."},{"to":"Patient","s":"There's no sign of a problem in your spine. We'll do blood tests, give you vitamin B1 and get a nerve test arranged."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Jo has a symmetrical peripheral neuropathy with sensory ataxia: no cord signs. High falls risk, especially in the dark. Please update her falls plan and keep a night light on."}],"write":"Lower limb neurological assessment. Joanne Fletcher, 48F, inpatient (depressive episode), heavy weekend alcohol use. Reported bilateral leg heaviness and numbness 3 wks, 2 falls on ward. Chaperone: HCA present. Consent obtained.\nInspection: no wasting, fasciculation, deformity, scars or ulcers. Feet warm, DP pulses present bilaterally. Dry skin over both shins.\nTone: normal, no clonus.\nPower (MRC): hip flexion/extension 5/5, knee 5/5, ankle plantarflexion 5/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5 bilaterally, EHL 4/5 bilaterally.\nReflexes: knee 1+ both; ankle absent both, absent with reinforcement. Plantars flexor.\nCoordination: heel-shin normal. Gait broad-based; unable to heel-toe walk. Romberg positive.\nSensation: light touch and pin prick reduced to mid-shin bilaterally (stocking), no sensory level. Vibration absent at big toes and medial malleoli, present at tibial tuberosity. JPS impaired at big toes, intact at ankles.\nImp: symmetrical length-dependent sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy with sensory ataxia; no UMN or cord signs. Likely alcohol/nutritional; exclude B12, diabetes, thyroid.\nPlan: FBC, B12, folate, HbA1c, TFT, U&E, LFT, GGT, CK, serum protein electrophoresis. Thiamine 100 mg TDS oral. Refer medical team; request nerve conduction studies. Falls plan, physio/OT. Red flags discussed.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Ask about pain before you move any joint.","Compare left with right at every step.","Grade power out of 5 for each movement, both sides.","Use reinforcement before calling a reflex absent.","Test sensation from the toes upward until it becomes normal.","End with a one-line answer: deficit or not, and the pattern."],"next":"Bloods show a raised MCV and GGT with a low-normal B12. She starts thiamine and B12 replacement, and nerve conduction studies confirm an axonal sensorimotor neuropathy. With physiotherapy, a night light and good shoes she has no further falls, and she agrees to see the alcohol liaison nurse.","marks":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone and checked for pain.","Inspected and tested tone, power with MRC grades, and reflexes including plantars.","Tested coordination, gait and Romberg's test safely.","Tested light touch, pin prick, vibration and joint position distal to proximal.","Stated clearly whether there is a deficit and its pattern.","Named next steps: bloods, thiamine, falls plan, medical referral."],"pitfalls":["Taking a history when asked not to, and running out of time.","Calling ankle reflexes absent without reinforcement.","Testing sensation only at one point instead of mapping distal to proximal.","Doing Romberg's test without standing close enough to catch her.","Ending without a clear statement of whether there is a deficit."],"refs":["1. NICE CG100: alcohol-use disorders, physical complications (thiamine) (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CKS: neuropathic pain and peripheral neuropathy (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG249: falls assessment and prevention in older people and people 50 and over at higher risk (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: thiamine (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK psychiatric wards, the psychiatry resident does the first physical assessment. A clear written neuro assessment in the standard format is what the medical team will act on.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"}
);
